/* Generated by scripts/build-book-studies.py. */
const bibleBookStudies = [
  {"id": 92000, "title": "Hannah Pours Out Her Grief at Shiloh", "subtitle": "Bringing sorrow honestly before God", "text": "1 Samuel 1:1–18", "chapters": [[8, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Every year Elkanah travelled from his home in the hill country of Ephraim to worship and sacrifice at Shiloh. His household went with him. Peninnah had sons and daughters, while Hannah had no children. Elkanah loved Hannah and gave her a special portion when the family shared the sacrificial meal. Yet his affection could not take away the grief she carried. Peninnah repeatedly used Hannah's childlessness to hurt her. The journey to worship became another occasion for provocation. Year after year Hannah wept and lost her appetite. Elkanah asked why she was so sorrowful and whether his love meant more to her than ten sons. His questions show his concern, but Hannah still had something she needed to bring before God.", "After the meal at Shiloh, Hannah rose and prayed. Eli the priest was sitting beside a doorway of the sanctuary. Hannah wept bitterly as she addressed the Lord of hosts. She asked Him to look on her distress, remember her and give her a son. With that request she made a vow. If God gave her the child, she would give him to the Lord for his whole life, and no razor would come upon his head. Her prayer joined a deeply personal longing to a promise of lifelong dedication. The account lets us hear her grief and her request without pretending that the years of waiting had been easy.", "Hannah continued praying, and Eli watched her mouth. Her lips moved, but he heard no voice. He concluded that she was drunk and rebuked her, telling her to put away her wine. At the very place where she had come with her sorrow, Hannah now had to answer a mistaken accusation. She replied respectfully. She had drunk neither wine nor strong drink. She was a woman in deep distress, pouring out her soul before the Lord. Eli had seen the movement of her lips but had misunderstood the heart behind it. Hannah asked him to recognise the abundance of grief that had kept her praying there.", "Eli changed his response. He told her to go in peace and asked that the God of Israel grant what she had requested. Hannah received his words and went away. She ate, and her face was no longer downcast. At this point in the story she still had no child, and the narrator has announced no pregnancy. What has changed is the woman who has brought her burden before the Lord. The household's painful circumstances have been told plainly, the priest's mistake has been corrected, and Hannah has left the sanctuary able to eat again. The next morning will bring another act of worship and the journey home."], "lesson": "Hannah brings her grief to God in honest prayer and answers a mistaken accusation with restraint. Her story invites us to pray sincerely and to listen carefully before judging another person's distress.", "question": "What do Hannah's prayer and her answer to Eli teach you about responding when sorrow is misunderstood?"},
  {"id": 92001, "title": "The Child Hannah Brought Back to God", "subtitle": "Keeping a promise after prayer is answered", "text": "1 Samuel 1:19–2:11", "chapters": [[8, 1], [8, 2]], "start": 19, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Early the next morning Elkanah's household worshipped before the Lord and returned home to Ramah. In time Hannah conceived and gave birth to a son. She called him Samuel, connecting his name with the request she had made to the Lord. The account says that the Lord remembered her. The child whose absence had been the occasion of such grief now lay in her care. When Elkanah prepared for the next yearly journey to offer sacrifice and fulfil his vow, Hannah stayed home. She explained that she would bring Samuel after he was weaned, so that he could appear before the Lord and remain there. Elkanah agreed that she should care for him until that time.", "When Samuel was weaned, Hannah took him to Shiloh with offerings of animals, flour and wine. He was still young. The family offered a sacrifice and brought the child to Eli. Hannah reminded the priest who she was. She was the woman who had stood there beside him praying. This was the child for whom she had asked, and the Lord had granted her petition. She now gave him to the Lord for the whole of his life. The promise made in sorrow became an act of obedience after joy had come. The narrator records worship there, placing the child's arrival at Shiloh within the family's response to God.", "Hannah's prayer then rises beyond her own household. Her heart rejoices in the Lord and in His salvation. She declares that no one is holy like Him and that there is no rock like their God. Proud speech has no secure foundation before the God who knows and weighs human actions. She describes mighty warriors losing strength while those who stumbled are made strong. The well fed seek work for bread, while the hungry cease to hunger. Her words about the barren woman and the mother of many belong to a larger picture of God overturning circumstances that people assume will last. The prayer's picture of a barren woman bearing seven expresses fullness in its song of reversal. Samuel remains the one child whose birth has been narrated here.", "Hannah goes on to speak of the Lord bringing down and raising up, making poor and making rich. He lifts the needy from the dust and gives them a place of honour. He guards His faithful people, judges His enemies and gives strength to His king and His anointed. Her prayer looks beyond her present joy toward God's rule over the earth. Then the family returns to Ramah. Samuel stays behind and begins to minister before the Lord under Eli. Hannah has asked, received, returned and worshipped. Samuel's life of service begins where his mother once stood in tears."], "lesson": "Answered prayer calls for gratitude and faithfulness. Hannah keeps her vow and praises the God whose power reaches beyond her own changed circumstances.", "question": "How does Hannah's prayer move from her personal answer to the character and rule of God?"},
  {"id": 92002, "title": "The Priests Who Took What They Wanted", "subtitle": "Responsibility in the house of God", "text": "1 Samuel 2:12–36", "chapters": [[8, 2]], "start": 12, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Samuel's service at Shiloh takes place among men who misuse their sacred responsibility. Eli's sons, Hophni and Phinehas, serve as priests, yet the account says that they do not know the Lord. When worshippers prepare the sacrificial meat, a servant comes with a three pronged fork and takes whatever it brings out of the pot for the priest. Sometimes the servant demands raw meat before the fat has been offered to God. If a worshipper asks that God's portion be offered first, the servant threatens to take the meat by force. The priests' appetite has become an excuse to intimidate the people bringing sacrifices. Their sin is very great before the Lord, and people come to despise His offering.", "Alongside that conduct, the narrator shows Samuel serving as a child in a linen ephod. Hannah makes him a little coat and brings it each year when she comes with Elkanah to offer sacrifice. The small yearly gift connects his new life of service with the mother who brought him there. Eli blesses his parents and asks that the Lord give Hannah children in place of the child she has entrusted to His service. Hannah later bears three sons and two daughters. Samuel continues growing before the Lord. His growth and his mother's visits unfold while the older priests persist in conduct that harms the worshippers.", "Eli, now very old, hears what his sons are doing. He also hears that they are sleeping with the women who assemble at the entrance of the tabernacle. He challenges them about the reports and tells them they are causing the Lord's people to transgress. He warns of the seriousness of sinning against the Lord. They refuse to listen. The account connects their refusal with the judgment God intends to bring upon them. Meanwhile Samuel grows in favour with God and with people. The repeated notice of his growth places a faithful young servant within a household whose leaders are refusing correction.", "A man of God comes to Eli with a message. He recalls the privilege God gave Eli's ancestral house, choosing it for priestly service and providing offerings for its support. Why, then, has Eli honoured his sons above God and shared in the benefit of their greed? Judgment will fall upon his household. Its strength will be cut off, its descendants will suffer loss, and Hophni and Phinehas will die on the same day as a sign. God will raise up a faithful priest who acts according to His heart. The passage ends with descendants of Eli's house reduced to asking for food and a place of service. Sacred office has brought responsibility, and God has seen what its holders chose to do."], "lesson": "Spiritual responsibility requires reverence, integrity and action against known wrongdoing. Eli's spoken warnings do not remove his accountability for honouring his sons above God.", "question": "What does God's message to Eli reveal about the difference between noticing wrongdoing and taking responsibility for it?"},
  {"id": 92003, "title": "The Night Samuel Learned Who Was Calling", "subtitle": "Hearing and faithfully delivering God's word", "text": "1 Samuel 3:1–21", "chapters": [[8, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Samuel continues serving the Lord under Eli at a time when the word of the Lord is rare and visions are uncommon. Eli's sight has grown weak. One night he is lying in his place, and Samuel is also lying down in the sanctuary where the ark of God is kept. The lamp of God has not yet gone out. Into that ordinary night comes a call. The Lord calls Samuel, and the boy answers. Assuming Eli has summoned him, Samuel runs to the old priest and announces that he has come. Eli says he did not call and sends him back to bed. Samuel obeys, with no understanding yet of who has spoken.", "The call comes again. Samuel gets up and returns to Eli, still certain that the voice belongs to the man under whom he serves. Eli again tells him to lie down. The narrator explains that Samuel does not yet know the Lord in this way, because the word of the Lord has not yet been revealed to him. A third call brings him to Eli once more. This time Eli understands that the Lord is calling the child. He gives Samuel an answer to make if the voice comes again. Samuel is to say that the Lord should speak, because His servant is listening. He goes back to his place. When the Lord comes and calls his name again, Samuel gives the answer Eli has taught him.", "The message is severe. God is about to do something in Israel that will astonish those who hear it. Everything already spoken against Eli's household will be carried out. Eli knows the evil of his sons, and he has failed to restrain them. God declares that the iniquity of the house will not be dealt with by sacrifice or offering. Samuel's first recorded reception of God's word places a burden on him concerning the priest who has cared for him. The account gives no invented conversation to soften the message. Samuel lies until morning, then opens the doors of the Lord's house. He is afraid to tell Eli what he has seen and heard.", "Eli summons him and insists that he withhold nothing. Samuel tells him everything. Eli acknowledges that it is the Lord and submits to what He judges good. From that difficult beginning, Samuel grows with the Lord's presence upon his life. God lets none of his words fall to the ground. Across Israel, from Dan to Beersheba, people recognise that Samuel has been established as the Lord's prophet. God continues appearing at Shiloh and revealing Himself to Samuel through His word. The child who first mistook the call for Eli's voice has become a recognised bearer of a message he must deliver faithfully, even when it concerns someone close to him."], "lesson": "A willingness to hear God includes a willingness to receive correction and speak truth faithfully. Samuel's fear does not prevent him from telling Eli the whole message.", "question": "Why was telling Eli the whole message a necessary part of Samuel's response to God's call?"},
  {"id": 92004, "title": "The Ark Is Taken and Shiloh Hears the News", "subtitle": "Reverence beyond religious confidence", "text": "1 Samuel 4:1–22", "chapters": [[8, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Israel went out to fight the Philistines, camping near Ebenezer while the enemy gathered at Aphek. The first battle ended in defeat, with about four thousand Israelites killed. Back in camp the elders asked why the Lord had allowed them to be struck down. Their proposed answer was to fetch the ark of the covenant from Shiloh. They expected its arrival to save them from their enemies. The ark was brought into the camp accompanied by Hophni and Phinehas, the very priests whose wrongdoing had brought warnings of judgment upon Eli's house. When it arrived, Israel shouted so loudly that the ground seemed to ring with the sound.", "The Philistines heard the shouting and learned that the ark had come. They were frightened. Their own words about Israel's God recalled the defeat of Egypt, though their description confused the details and spoke of mighty gods. They urged one another to fight courageously rather than become servants of the Hebrews. The next battle brought an even greater disaster for Israel. Thirty thousand foot soldiers fell, the survivors fled to their homes, and the Philistines captured the ark. Hophni and Phinehas both died. The sign announced earlier against Eli's family had come to pass. The cheering in Israel's camp had given way to flight, death and the loss of the sacred object in which the elders had placed such confidence.", "A man from Benjamin escaped from the army and reached Shiloh that same day. His torn clothes and the earth upon his head showed that he carried news of disaster. Eli, ninety eight years old and nearly blind, sat watching beside the road, trembling with concern for the ark. The messenger's report set the city crying aloud. Eli heard the uproar and asked what had happened. The man told him of Israel's flight, the slaughter of the army and the deaths of his two sons. Last came the news that the ark had been captured. At that, Eli fell backward from his seat beside the gate. His neck broke, and he died. His forty years of judging Israel ended with the catastrophe he had been warned would fall upon his house.", "Phinehas's wife was close to giving birth when she heard that the ark was taken and that her husband and father in law were dead. Labour came upon her, and she gave birth to a son. The women attending her tried to comfort her with the news of his birth, but she gave no answering attention. As she died, she named him Ichabod, connecting the name with the departure of glory from Israel. The loss of the ark dominates her final words. A child has been born into a household shattered by death, and Shiloh's grief brings the story of Eli's unrestrained sons to its dreadful outcome."], "lesson": "Religious symbols and confident words cannot take the place of reverence and obedience. Read the elders' decision alongside the earlier warnings to Eli's household, and examine where you may be relying on outward religion while resisting God's correction.", "question": "What did Israel expect the ark's arrival to accomplish, and what had the earlier chapters already revealed about the nation's spiritual condition?"},
  {"id": 92005, "title": "Dagon Falls Before the Captured Ark", "subtitle": "God's honour in the land of the enemy", "text": "1 Samuel 5:1–12", "chapters": [[8, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The Philistines carried the ark from the battlefield to Ashdod and placed it in the temple of Dagon, beside the image of their god. They had won the battle and taken Israel's most sacred object. Inside Dagon's house, the ark stood in a setting that might have appeared to celebrate that victory. Yet the next morning the people of Ashdod found Dagon lying face down before the ark of the Lord. They lifted the image and put it back in its place. The account describes no Israelite army entering the temple and no priest of Israel acting to bring the idol down. The people who served Dagon had to raise him themselves.", "The following morning they found the image fallen again. This time Dagon's head and both hands were broken off upon the threshold, leaving only his trunk. The narrator connects that event with the later practice of Dagon's priests and worshippers avoiding the threshold when they entered his temple. The damage to the image became part of the place's remembered history. Israel's defeat had not made the Lord powerless in Philistine territory. The ark had been captured, but what happened beside it overturned the apparent meaning of its presence in the temple. The image set above the worshippers could neither remain upright nor preserve its own head and hands.", "The people of Ashdod also suffered beneath the heavy hand of the Lord. Destruction and painful swellings afflicted the city and its surrounding district. They recognised that the presence of the ark had become a danger to them and to Dagon. Gathering the Philistine rulers, they asked what should be done. The decision was to move the ark to Gath. There, too, disaster followed. People of every standing were struck, and great fear spread through the city. Sending the ark elsewhere had carried the crisis to another community. The narrative moves from the damaged idol to the suffering cities, keeping the Lord's power in view throughout.", "The next destination was Ekron. Its people cried out when the ark arrived, protesting that it had been brought there to kill them and their people. Again the rulers assembled. The demand was now to send the ark back to its own place. Death had spread through the city, and those who survived were afflicted with swellings. The chapter closes with their cry reaching heaven. The Philistines had begun by bringing a captured object into a temple. They now sought relief from the God whose ark they possessed. Their discussions would soon turn to how they could return it and acknowledge the judgment that had followed its journey through their land."], "lesson": "God's power and honour do not depend upon human victory or defeat. The fallen image and the suffering cities call for humility before the Lord and a willingness to acknowledge His authority.", "question": "How does the movement from Dagon's temple to the Philistine cities change the meaning of the ark's capture?"},
  {"id": 92006, "title": "The Cows Take the Road Back to Israel", "subtitle": "Receiving God's presence with reverence", "text": "1 Samuel 6:1–7:1", "chapters": [[8, 6], [8, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 1, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["After seven months with the ark in their country, the Philistines summoned priests and diviners to advise them. They wanted to know how to send it home. The advisers called for a guilt offering, consisting of five golden images of the swellings and five golden mice, corresponding to the five Philistine rulers and representing the affliction upon their people and land. They urged the rulers to give glory to Israel's God and recalled Pharaoh's stubbornness. The memory of Egypt now became a warning against continuing to harden their own hearts. They proposed a test to discover whether the disasters had truly come from the Lord.", "A new cart was prepared. Two cows that were nursing calves and had never worn a yoke were harnessed to it, while their calves were shut up at home. The ark was placed on the cart with a box containing the golden offerings beside it. The advisers said to watch its direction. If the cows went toward Beth shemesh, the Philistines would recognise that Israel's God had brought the disaster. If they went elsewhere, they would regard it as chance. The cows went straight along the road to Beth shemesh, lowing as they travelled and turning neither right nor left. The Philistine rulers followed as far as the border. The test they had chosen pointed to the answer they had feared.", "At Beth shemesh, people were harvesting wheat in the valley. Looking up, they saw the ark and rejoiced. The cart stopped beside a large stone in the field of a man named Joshua. The Levites took down the ark and the box of golden objects and set them upon the stone. The people split the wood of the cart and offered the cows as a burnt offering, together with other sacrifices that day. The five Philistine rulers saw what happened and returned to Ekron. The account records the cities represented by the golden offerings and recalls the great stone where the ark had been set down. Its return had been witnessed on both sides of the border.", "Joy at Beth shemesh was followed by mourning. The Lord struck people because they had looked into the ark, and the community lamented the great loss. They asked who could stand before the holy Lord God and where the ark should go next. Messengers went to Kiriath jearim, asking its inhabitants to fetch it. Those men came and brought it to Abinadab's house on the hill. They set apart his son Eleazar to keep it. The ark's journey had exposed both Philistine helplessness and Israel's need for reverence. Its return brought responsibility to the people who received it, and the story now turns toward the nation's relationship with the Lord."], "lesson": "Receiving what belongs to God calls for reverence as well as joy. The ark's return invites the people to honour the holy God who has made His power known among both nations.", "question": "Why must the rejoicing at Beth shemesh and the judgment that follows be read together?"},
  {"id": 92007, "title": "The Stone Samuel Raised After Mizpah", "subtitle": "Repentance and remembered help", "text": "1 Samuel 7:2–17", "chapters": [[8, 7]], "start": 2, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The ark remained at Kiriath jearim, and a long period passed. Twenty years went by, and the people of Israel lamented after the Lord. Samuel addressed the nation with a clear call. If they were returning to the Lord with all their hearts, they must put away their foreign gods and the Ashtaroth, direct their hearts toward Him and serve Him alone. He promised that the Lord would deliver them from the Philistines. Israel responded by removing the Baals and Ashtaroth and serving the Lord only. Their grief had to become a change in whom they worshipped. Samuel then called them to gather at Mizpah, where he would pray for them.", "The people assembled, drew water and poured it out before the Lord. They fasted and confessed that they had sinned against Him. Samuel judged Israel there. The gathering soon drew the attention of the Philistine rulers, who advanced against them. Israel heard of the approach and became afraid. This time their appeal was to Samuel to keep crying to the Lord for them, so that He would save them from their enemies. Earlier, the elders had brought the ark into battle and expected its presence to secure victory. Here the people had renounced their idols, confessed their sin and asked for prayer while facing the same powerful enemy.", "Samuel took a young lamb and offered it wholly as a burnt offering. He cried to the Lord on Israel's behalf, and the Lord heard him. As the sacrifice was being offered and the Philistines drew near, the Lord thundered with a mighty sound against them. Their forces fell into confusion and were defeated before Israel. The Israelites went out from Mizpah and pursued them as far as below Beth car. Samuel then took a stone and set it between Mizpah and Shen. He called it Ebenezer and explained that the Lord had helped them thus far. The stone gave a visible place to the memory of help they had received.", "The account describes the Philistines as subdued, with the Lord's hand against them throughout Samuel's days. Cities and surrounding territory previously taken by the Philistines were restored, and there was peace between Israel and the Amorites. Samuel continued judging Israel throughout his life. Each year he travelled a circuit through Bethel, Gilgal and Mizpah, administering justice in those places. He returned to Ramah, where he lived and also judged the people, and he built an altar to the Lord there. The story moves from the national crisis to the steady work that followed. Prayer, worship and justice belonged to Israel's continuing life after the battle, while the stone stood as a reminder of the One who had helped them."], "lesson": "Returning to God involves turning away from rival loyalties, confessing sin and depending upon His help. Remembering answered prayer should lead into continued worship and faithful conduct.", "question": "What actions show that Israel's return to the Lord at Mizpah went beyond feeling sorrow, and how can you practise that kind of repentance?"},
  {"id": 92008, "title": "Israel Asks for a King Like the Nations", "subtitle": "Taking our desires before God", "text": "1 Samuel 8:1–22", "chapters": [[8, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Samuel grew old and appointed his sons to judge Israel. Joel and Abiah served at Beersheba, but they departed from their father's ways. They pursued dishonest gain, accepted bribes and bent justice. Israel's elders gathered and came to Samuel at Ramah with a complaint that had a real foundation. He was old, and his sons were corrupt. Their proposed remedy was a king who would judge them as kings judged the surrounding nations. Samuel was displeased by the request. Before giving the people his answer, he prayed to the Lord. The conversation that followed showed that the elders' demand involved more than a practical response to Samuel's age and his sons' misconduct.", "The Lord told Samuel to listen to what the people were asking. Their rejection reached beyond Samuel to the Lord Himself, whose rule they were refusing. God connected their demand with their history since the exodus, when they repeatedly forsook Him and served other gods. Samuel was to grant their request while solemnly warning them about the kind of rule they were seeking. He therefore told the people all that the Lord had said. Their desire for a king would bring obligations upon the same households that hoped to find security under him. The warning made those obligations concrete, naming the people, work and possessions that royal power would claim.", "The king would take their sons for his chariots, cavalry and military service. Some would run before his chariots, while others would command troops or work his fields and make his weapons. Their daughters would become perfumers, cooks and bakers. Their best fields, vineyards and olive groves would pass to his servants. A tenth of their crops and produce would support his officers. Their servants, young men and donkeys would be taken for his work, and he would demand a tenth of their flocks. Samuel's repeated description of taking brought the cost of their request into the farms and families of the listeners. They would become his servants. A day would come when they cried out because of the king they had chosen, and the Lord would not answer that cry.", "The people heard the warning and still refused to change their demand. They insisted upon having a king so they could be like the nations. He would judge them, lead them and fight their battles. Samuel listened to their whole reply and repeated it before the Lord. God again instructed him to heed their request and make them a king. Samuel dismissed the men to their cities. No king has yet appeared in the account, but the decision has been made. The elders began with a justified complaint about corrupt judges. They ended by insisting upon their preferred answer even after hearing what it would cost and what their demand revealed about their trust in God."], "lesson": "A real grievance still requires a faithful response to God. Samuel's prayer and the people's refusal to heed the warning invite us to examine both the reasons for our requests and our willingness to receive correction.", "question": "Where do the people's stated reasons for wanting a king reveal a deeper desire to become like the surrounding nations?"},
  {"id": 92009, "title": "The Search for Donkeys Leads Saul to Samuel", "subtitle": "God at work beyond what we can see", "text": "1 Samuel 9:1–27", "chapters": [[8, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Kish, a man of standing in Benjamin, had a son named Saul. He was an impressive young man, taller than the rest of the people by head and shoulders. When Kish's donkeys went missing, he sent Saul with a servant to find them. They travelled through the hill country of Ephraim, Shalishah, Shaalim and Benjamin without success. By the time they reached the land of Zuph, Saul thought they should turn back. His father might stop worrying about the animals and begin worrying about the two travellers. The servant suggested one more step. A respected man of God lived in the nearby town, and what he said came true. Perhaps he could show them which way to go.", "Saul asked what gift they could bring, since their bread was gone. The servant had a quarter shekel of silver and offered it for the purpose. They agreed to go. As they climbed toward the town, they met young women coming out to draw water and asked about the seer. The women explained that he had arrived that day for a sacrifice at the high place. The guests would wait for him to bless the sacrifice before eating. If the travellers hurried, they could meet him. Saul and his servant entered the town just as Samuel came toward them on his way to the gathering. Saul's search had brought him to a meeting whose purpose he did not know.", "The day before, the Lord had told Samuel that a man from Benjamin would come at that time. Samuel was to anoint him as ruler over Israel, so that he could deliver the people from the Philistines. God had seen their distress and heard their cry. When Samuel saw Saul, the Lord identified him as the man. Saul approached with the simple question of where the seer's house was. Samuel answered that he was the seer and invited Saul to eat with him. He would send him away the next day and speak about what was in his heart. First he settled the matter that had brought Saul there. The missing donkeys had been found.", "Samuel then spoke of Israel's desire resting upon Saul and his father's house. Saul questioned why such words should be addressed to someone from Benjamin and from a family he considered insignificant. Samuel brought him and his servant into the dining room and seated them in the place of honour among about thirty guests. A portion of meat that Samuel had already reserved was served to Saul. Afterward Samuel spoke with him on the roof of the house. At dawn they prepared for his departure and walked together toward the edge of town. Samuel sent the servant ahead and asked Saul to remain, so that he could make known God's word to him. The search for his father's animals had reached a turning point that would change his life."], "lesson": "Saul knows the task in front of him while Samuel has received a message about what lies ahead. The account encourages humility about our limited understanding and careful attention when God's word brings new responsibility.", "question": "Which details show the difference between what Saul thought his journey was about and what God had already told Samuel?"},
  {"id": 92010, "title": "The Anointed Man Is Found Among the Baggage", "subtitle": "Receiving responsibility under God's authority", "text": "1 Samuel 10:1–27", "chapters": [[8, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["With the servant ahead of them, Samuel took a flask of oil and poured it upon Saul's head. He kissed him and declared that the Lord had anointed him as ruler over His inheritance. Samuel then described signs Saul would meet on his journey. Near Rachel's tomb, two men would tell him that the donkeys were found and that his father was now anxious about him. Farther on, three men going to worship at Bethel would meet him with goats, bread and wine. They would give him two loaves, which he was to accept. At the hill of God, where a Philistine garrison stood, Saul would encounter a company of prophets coming down from the high place with musical instruments.", "The Spirit of the Lord would come powerfully upon him, Samuel said, and he would prophesy with them and become a different man. When these signs occurred, he could act as the occasion required, because God was with him. Samuel also instructed him to go ahead to Gilgal and wait seven days for him to arrive, offer sacrifices and tell him what to do. Saul turned to leave, and God gave him another heart. All the signs took place that day. Those who had known him were astonished to see him prophesying among the prophets. Their question about Saul's presence there became a proverb. The journey was supplying confirmation of the word Samuel had spoken privately.", "When Saul's uncle asked where he and the servant had been, Saul told him about the search and their visit to Samuel. Asked what Samuel had said, he reported that the donkeys had been found. He kept the matter of the kingdom to himself. Later Samuel summoned Israel to Mizpah. Before proceeding, he reminded the people that the Lord had brought them out of Egypt and delivered them from their oppressors. Their demand for a king had rejected the God who had saved them. The tribes and families then presented themselves. Benjamin was selected, then the family of Matri, and finally Saul son of Kish. But Saul could not be found.", "They enquired further of the Lord and learned that Saul was hiding among the baggage. Men ran to bring him out. Standing among the people, he towered over them, and Samuel presented the man the Lord had chosen. The people acclaimed their king. Samuel explained the rights and duties of the kingship, wrote them in a book and placed it before the Lord. Then the assembly dispersed. Saul returned home to Gibeah with men whose hearts God had moved to accompany him. Others despised him, questioned his ability to save them and withheld gifts. Saul kept silent. His public selection had brought both support and contempt, while the word that established his responsibility continued to place him under God's authority."], "lesson": "God's calling brings responsibility and a need for obedience. Saul receives confirming signs, instruction, public recognition and mixed responses, all of which require more than an impressive appearance.", "question": "Why should Samuel's instructions and his account of the kingship matter more to Saul than either the crowd's praise or his critics' contempt?"},
  {"id": 92011, "title": "Saul Answers the Cry from Jabesh", "subtitle": "Receiving victory with humility", "text": "1 Samuel 11:1–15", "chapters": [[8, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Nahash the Ammonite surrounded Jabesh-gilead, and its people sought terms of surrender. They offered to serve him if he would make a treaty. His answer turned their desperation into a threat against the honour of all Israel. He would accept only if he could put out every man's right eye. The elders asked for seven days to send messengers through Israel. If no deliverer came, they would surrender. The messengers reached Saul's town of Gibeah and told the people what had happened. Those who heard began weeping. Saul was returning from the fields behind his cattle when he encountered the grief and asked its cause.", "When Saul heard the news, the Spirit of God came powerfully upon him, and he became intensely angry. He cut a pair of oxen into pieces and sent the pieces through Israel by messengers, warning that anyone who refused to follow Saul and Samuel would have the same done to his oxen. Fear of the Lord fell upon the people, and they assembled together. At Bezek Saul counted three hundred thousand Israelites and thirty thousand men of Judah. He sent the messengers back with a promise that Jabesh would receive help the next day, before the sun grew hot. The people of the besieged town rejoiced. They told their enemies that they would come out the following day and submit to whatever the Ammonites chose.", "Saul divided his force into three companies. During the morning watch they attacked the Ammonite camp, striking until the heat of the day. The survivors scattered so completely that no two remained together. The threatened mutilation of Jabesh's people had been averted, and the king whose ability some had doubted had led a successful rescue. In the excitement, people asked Samuel to identify those who had earlier opposed Saul's rule. They wanted the critics brought out and killed. Victory had created an opportunity for revenge against fellow Israelites. Saul refused it. No one was to be executed that day, he said, because the Lord had brought salvation to Israel.", "Samuel called the people to Gilgal to renew the kingship. There they confirmed Saul as king before the Lord and offered fellowship sacrifices. Saul and the people rejoiced greatly. The chapter carries him from the ordinary work of the field into the public exercise of royal responsibility. His anger at a cruel threat has led to united action, while his refusal to execute opponents has kept that action from ending in an internal purge. He openly credits the Lord for the deliverance. These are features of Saul's early leadership that the reader must remember as the story develops. The celebration at Gilgal is real, and the responsibilities that now rest upon the king will continue after the rejoicing has ended."], "lesson": "Leadership can protect those in danger and restrain revenge after victory. Saul's response gives reason to ask whether success makes us more thankful to God and more merciful toward others.", "question": "What do Saul's rescue of Jabesh and his refusal to kill his critics reveal about the way he used authority that day?"},
  {"id": 92012, "title": "Samuel Calls Israel to Answer Before God", "subtitle": "Faithfulness after a mistaken choice", "text": "1 Samuel 12:1–25", "chapters": [[8, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["With the king now standing before Israel, Samuel called the people to examine his own conduct. He had served among them from childhood and was now old and grey. Before the Lord and His anointed king, he invited anyone to name an ox or donkey he had taken, anyone he had defrauded or oppressed, or a bribe he had accepted. He offered restitution for any wrong. The people testified that he had neither cheated nor oppressed them nor taken their property. The Lord and the king stood as witnesses to that public acknowledgment. Samuel then turned from his own stewardship to the Lord's righteous dealings with their ancestors, asking the nation to listen to its history before God.", "He recalled how God sent Moses and Aaron when their ancestors cried from Egypt and brought them into their land. Later generations forgot Him and fell under foreign oppression. When they confessed their service to the Baals and Ashtaroth and cried for rescue, the Lord sent deliverers and gave them security. Samuel's account led to their recent fear of Nahash. They had insisted upon a human king when the Lord was already their king. Now the king they desired stood before them. Both ruler and people remained responsible to fear, serve and obey the Lord. Continued rebellion would still bring His hand against them. The new institution could not remove that obligation.", "Samuel called them to witness a sign. It was wheat harvest, yet he would ask the Lord to send thunder and rain so that they would recognise the evil of their demand. He prayed, and thunder and rain came that day. The people were deeply afraid and begged him to pray that they would not die. They admitted that asking for a king had added to their sins. Samuel told them not to surrender to fear, while plainly acknowledging their wrongdoing. They were to continue following the Lord and serve Him wholeheartedly. Turning to empty things would bring no help or deliverance. The Lord would not abandon His people for His great name's sake, because He had chosen to make them His own.", "Samuel also committed himself to their future. He would regard ceasing to pray for them as sin against the Lord, and he would keep teaching them the good and right way. Their failure had not ended his responsibility to intercede or instruct. His closing appeal joined reverence, sincerity and gratitude. They were to consider the great things God had done and serve Him with their whole hearts. Persisting in wickedness would bring ruin upon both them and their king. The address leaves Israel with neither an excuse for its earlier choice nor a reason to give up following God. The king is in place, the sin has been confessed, and the way forward requires the obedience Samuel has been urging throughout."], "lesson": "Acknowledging a wrong choice should lead to renewed obedience. Samuel joins truthful correction with prayer, teaching and confidence in God's faithfulness to His name.", "question": "How do Samuel's promises to keep praying and teaching shape the way you should respond to someone who has made a serious mistake?"},
  {"id": 92013, "title": "Saul Will Not Wait at Gilgal", "subtitle": "Obedience under growing pressure", "text": "1 Samuel 13:1–23", "chapters": [[8, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Saul selected three thousand men, keeping two thousand with himself near Michmash and Bethel and placing a thousand with Jonathan in Gibeah. Jonathan attacked a Philistine garrison at Geba, and news spread through the country. Saul sounded the trumpet, calling the Hebrews to hear, and the people assembled with him at Gilgal. The Philistines gathered a formidable force at Michmash. Their chariots, horsemen and numerous soldiers terrified Israel. Men hid in caves, thickets, rocks and pits. Some crossed the Jordan into Gad and Gilead. Those who remained with Saul trembled as they followed him. The king faced the prospect of battle while watching the strength of his own force diminish.", "At Gilgal he waited seven days according to the time Samuel had appointed, but Samuel had not arrived and the people were scattering. Saul called for the burnt offering and fellowship offerings, and he offered the burnt offering. As soon as he finished, Samuel appeared. Saul went out to greet him, but Samuel asked what he had done. Saul gave the circumstances that had driven his decision. His men were deserting, Samuel had not come within the appointed days, and the Philistines were gathering. He feared that they would attack before he had sought the Lord's favour. He said he had therefore forced himself to offer the sacrifice. His explanation shows how heavily the visible crisis weighed upon him.", "Samuel's answer addressed the command Saul had received. He had acted foolishly and failed to keep the Lord's instruction. Had he obeyed, the Lord would have established his kingdom over Israel, but his kingdom would now fail to continue. The Lord had sought a man after His own heart and appointed him to lead His people. Saul's royal responsibility had included submitting to the word that directed him. Neither a dangerous enemy nor a diminishing army could turn disobedience into faithfulness. Samuel left Gilgal for Gibeah, and Saul counted about six hundred men still with him. The military problem remained, now joined by a word of judgment concerning the future of his rule.", "The chapter closes by describing Israel's vulnerability. Philistine raiders moved out in three directions, threatening different parts of the country. The Philistines had also prevented Israel from having blacksmiths who could manufacture swords and spears. Israelites depended upon them even for sharpening agricultural tools. When battle approached, Saul and Jonathan were the only men with sword or spear among those with them. A Philistine detachment occupied the pass at Michmash. These details explain the pressure surrounding Saul's decision and prepare for Jonathan's next action. The narrator leaves the reader with the small force, the shortage of weapons and the enemy's commanding position all still in view. Deliverance would require more than the resources Saul could count."], "lesson": "Pressure can expose the reasons we give ourselves for disregarding an instruction from God. Saul's circumstances deserve careful attention alongside Samuel's clear account of his responsibility.", "question": "Which parts of Saul's explanation sound reasonable, and why does Samuel still describe his action as disobedience?"},
  {"id": 92014, "title": "Jonathan Climbs Toward the Enemy", "subtitle": "Trusting God's freedom to save", "text": "1 Samuel 14:1–23", "chapters": [[8, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Jonathan proposed to his young armour bearer that they cross to the Philistine outpost. He did not tell Saul, who remained near Gibeah with about six hundred men. Ahijah the priest, a descendant of Eli's house, was also there. No one in the camp knew Jonathan had gone. His route passed between two steep rocky crags, Bozez and Seneh, with the Philistines positioned above. Jonathan spoke of what the Lord might do for them. God's ability to save was not limited by the size of a force. His armour bearer offered wholehearted support, ready to follow where Jonathan led. Their exchange joined a willingness to act with an acknowledgment that the outcome belonged to God.", "Jonathan explained how they would approach. They would show themselves to the Philistines. If the soldiers told them to wait until they came down, the two would stay where they were. If the soldiers invited them to climb up, Jonathan would take that as the sign that the Lord had given the enemy into Israel's hand. They revealed themselves, and the Philistines mocked the Hebrews for emerging from their hiding places. Then they called them to come up. Jonathan told his armour bearer to follow and began climbing on his hands and feet. In the first encounter the two struck down about twenty men in a small area. Panic spread through the outpost, the camp and the raiding parties, and the ground shook.", "Saul's watchmen saw the Philistine force melting away in confusion. Saul ordered a count to discover who was missing and learned that Jonathan and his armour bearer were absent. He called to the priest, but as the uproar in the enemy camp increased, he interrupted the consultation. Saul and his men gathered and entered the battle. They found Philistines turning their swords upon one another. The disturbance begun around the outpost had become a rout within the larger force. Those who had seemed secure in their numbers and position could no longer maintain order. Israel's small company encountered an enemy already thrown into confusion.", "Other Hebrews joined the pursuit. Men who had previously been with the Philistines turned to Saul and Jonathan. Israelites who had hidden in the hill country of Ephraim heard that the enemy was fleeing and came out after them. The battle spread beyond its starting point toward Beth-aven. The narrator credits the Lord with saving Israel that day. Jonathan had acted without knowing how many others would join him or how the battle would develop. His confidence concerned God's ability to save through many or through few. The account places the steep climb, the faithful armour bearer, the panic and the returning Israelites within that larger declaration of divine deliverance. Another difficulty, arising within Israel's own ranks, would soon complicate the victory."], "lesson": "Faith acknowledges God's power while accepting that His action cannot be controlled. Jonathan's willingness and his armour bearer's support invite courage joined to humility before God.", "question": "How does Jonathan's statement that the Lord may work for them keep his courage connected to dependence?"},
  {"id": 92015, "title": "The Oath That Nearly Cost Jonathan His Life", "subtitle": "Authority accountable for its words", "text": "1 Samuel 14:24–52", "chapters": [[8, 14]], "start": 24, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["During the pursuit Saul bound the troops with an oath. Anyone who ate before evening, before he had taken vengeance on his enemies, would be cursed. The men grew distressed and weak. When they entered woodland where honey was available, no one dared eat. Jonathan, who had not heard his father's command, dipped the end of his staff into a honeycomb and tasted it. His strength revived. A soldier then told him of the oath. Jonathan said his father had brought trouble upon the land. His own renewed vigour showed what food could have done for the exhausted men. They might have inflicted a greater defeat if they had been free to eat the enemy's provisions.", "After fighting from Michmash to Aijalon, the hungry troops rushed upon the spoil. They slaughtered sheep, cattle and calves on the ground and ate meat with the blood still in it. Saul was told that the people were sinning against the Lord. He ordered a great stone brought and directed that the animals be slaughtered properly there. He also built an altar to the Lord. The king whose oath had increased the army's exhaustion now had to address a breach of God's law among the starving men. Saul then proposed continuing the pursuit by night, but the priest urged him to enquire of God. Saul asked whether he should go down after the Philistines and whether God would deliver them into Israel's hand. No answer came that day.", "Saul summoned the leaders to discover the sin and declared that even Jonathan would die if found responsible. The troops said nothing. Saul separated himself and Jonathan from the people, and the process of casting lots first identified the two of them, then Jonathan. Asked what he had done, Jonathan reported tasting a little honey and acknowledged the threatened consequence. Saul insisted that he must die. The people intervened. How could Jonathan, through whom God had brought such a great deliverance, be executed? They swore that not a hair of his head would fall, because he had worked with God that day. They rescued him from death. The pursuit ended, and the Philistines returned home.", "The remainder of the chapter surveys Saul's kingdom. He fought against Moab, Ammon, Edom, the kings of Zobah and the Philistines, and he delivered Israel from raiders. His campaigns included action against Amalek. The account names his sons and daughters, his wife Ahinoam and his commander Abner, a relative from his father's family. Warfare against the Philistines continued throughout his reign. Whenever Saul saw a strong or brave man, he recruited him. Those broader details place the troubling incident within his ongoing military rule. The king could gather soldiers and win battles, yet this day's oath had endangered his own son. The people had needed to protect the man through whom their deliverance began."], "lesson": "Words spoken from a position of authority can burden many people. Saul's oath calls leaders to consider consequences, receive correction and protect those entrusted to them.", "question": "What consequences followed Saul's oath, and what does the people's intervention teach about responding to a harmful decision?"},
  {"id": 92016, "title": "The Sheep Samuel Could Still Hear", "subtitle": "Owning disobedience without excuses", "text": "1 Samuel 15:1–35", "chapters": [[8, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Samuel brought Saul a command concerning Amalek, recalling the attack upon Israel after the exodus. The instruction was a severe judgment requiring the destruction of the people and their livestock. Saul gathered his army at Telaim and approached the Amalekite city. He first warned the Kenites to separate themselves, remembering the kindness they had shown Israel. The campaign then struck Amalek from Havilah toward Shur. Saul took King Agag alive, and he and the people kept the best sheep, cattle and other valuable animals. What they considered worthless they destroyed. Their decisions made a distinction the command had not authorised. The report of their victory therefore contained the evidence of their refusal to carry out the word they had received.", "The Lord told Samuel that Saul had turned away and failed to obey. Samuel grieved and cried to God through the night. The next morning he learned that Saul had erected a monument at Carmel and gone on to Gilgal. When they met, Saul greeted him with a claim that he had fulfilled the Lord's command. Samuel asked about the bleating and lowing he could hear. Saul said the people had saved the best animals for sacrifice and destroyed the rest. Samuel reminded him of the time when he had been small in his own estimation and God made him king. Why had he rushed upon the spoil and failed to obey? Saul repeated his claim of obedience while again attributing the retained animals to the people.", "Samuel declared that God values obedience above sacrifice. Rebellion and stubborn refusal were serious sins, and because Saul had rejected God's word, God had rejected him as king. Saul admitted sin, saying he had feared the people and listened to them. He asked Samuel to return so he could worship. When Samuel turned away, Saul seized his robe and tore it. Samuel made the torn garment a sign that the kingdom had been torn from Saul and given to a better neighbour. God's announced decision would stand. Saul again admitted his sin but also asked to be honoured before the elders and Israel. Samuel returned with him, and Saul worshipped. The account lets the request for public honour remain beside the confession.", "Samuel called for Agag. Speaking of the women made childless by Agag's sword, he pronounced judgment and put him to death before the Lord at Gilgal. Samuel returned to Ramah, and Saul went home to Gibeah. Their separation continued through Samuel's remaining life, though Samuel mourned for Saul. The chapter also speaks of the Lord's grief over making him king. Saul's failure is therefore surrounded by sorrow as well as judgment. His explanations about sacrifice, his shifting of responsibility and his concern for honour have all been exposed. The king remains alive and on the throne, but the word concerning his dynasty has become unmistakable. The story will now turn toward the neighbour God has chosen."], "lesson": "God's word calls for honest obedience and truthful repentance. Saul's explanations invite us to examine whether our admissions of wrong still protect our reputation or transfer responsibility to others.", "question": "How does Saul's response change during the conversation, and where do his excuses and desire for honour remain visible?"},
  {"id": 92017, "title": "The Shepherd Samuel Had to Send For", "subtitle": "God sees the heart", "text": "1 Samuel 16:1–23", "chapters": [[8, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The Lord asked Samuel how long he would mourn for Saul and sent him to Jesse of Bethlehem. A king had been chosen among Jesse's sons. Samuel feared that Saul would kill him if he learned of the journey. God directed him to take a heifer for sacrifice and invite Jesse, promising to identify the one to anoint. The elders of Bethlehem trembled when Samuel arrived and asked whether he came peacefully. He assured them that he did and called them to prepare for the sacrifice. Jesse and his sons were invited. Samuel saw Eliab and thought he must be the Lord's anointed. The Lord corrected his judgment, telling him that human beings look at outward appearance while God looks at the heart.", "Jesse presented Abinadab, Shammah and the other sons who were there. Seven passed before Samuel, and none was chosen. Samuel asked whether all the sons had come. The youngest was still tending sheep. Samuel insisted that he be fetched before they proceeded. David arrived, described as healthy and handsome, and the Lord told Samuel to anoint him. Samuel poured oil upon him among his brothers. From that day the Spirit of the Lord came powerfully upon David. Samuel then returned to Ramah. The family gathering had required the presence of the son left with the flock, and the prophet had needed God's correction to recognise him. The anointing did not immediately place David upon Saul's throne.", "The account next turns to Saul's distress. The Spirit of the Lord departed from him, and a harmful spirit from the Lord troubled him. His servants proposed finding a skilled harp player whose music would bring relief. Saul agreed and ordered them to seek someone who could play well. One servant recommended a son of Jesse, describing his musical skill, courage, sensible speech and appearance, and adding that the Lord was with him. Saul sent messengers to Jesse asking for David, the son who was with the sheep. Jesse sent him with bread, wine and a young goat. The young man recently anointed by Samuel now entered the service of the reigning king.", "Saul grew very fond of David, who became one of his armour bearers. He asked Jesse to let the young man remain, since he had found favour in his sight. When Saul suffered the troubling spirit, David played the harp, and Saul was refreshed and relieved. The chapter ends with service that benefits the man whose kingship David will eventually inherit. David has received an anointing, but his present work includes music and attendance upon Saul. The narrative brings together God's choice in Bethlehem and an invitation from the royal household without describing a public claim by David. For now, the shepherd's skill provides comfort at court, and the responsibilities of his future remain ahead."], "lesson": "God's knowledge reaches beyond the qualities that first attract human attention. David's anointing is followed by useful service, inviting faithfulness in the work currently entrusted to us.", "question": "How do Samuel's correction and David's service at court challenge the way you recognise readiness for responsibility?"},
  {"id": 92018, "title": "David Runs Toward Goliath", "subtitle": "The battle belongs to the Lord", "text": "1 Samuel 17:1–58", "chapters": [[8, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 58, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The Philistines and Israelites faced one another across the valley of Elah. From the Philistine ranks came Goliath of Gath, an enormous champion armed with heavy bronze protection and a massive spear, with a shield bearer ahead of him. He demanded that Israel choose a man to fight him. The outcome, he claimed, would decide which people served the other. Saul and his army were terrified. For forty days the challenge was repeated morning and evening. David's three oldest brothers were with Saul, while David travelled between the king's service and his father's sheep. Jesse sent him to the camp with grain and bread for his brothers and cheeses for their commander, asking him to bring news of their welfare. David left the flock with a keeper and reached the army as it moved into position.", "Leaving the provisions with the baggage keeper, David ran to greet his brothers and heard Goliath's challenge. The soldiers withdrew in fear. They spoke of the riches, royal marriage and freedom for the father's household promised to the man who killed him. David asked about that reward but also questioned how this Philistine dared defy the armies of the living God. Eliab accused him of pride and neglecting the sheep. David answered and continued asking others. His words reached Saul, who summoned him. David volunteered to fight. Saul objected that he was young and Goliath a warrior from youth. David recalled defending his father's flock against a lion and a bear. The Lord who rescued him then could deliver him now. Saul permitted him to go and put armour upon him. David removed it because he had no experience using it.", "With his staff, sling and five smooth stones chosen from the stream, David approached. Goliath despised his youthful appearance, cursed him by his gods and threatened to leave his body to birds and animals. David answered by naming the Lord of hosts, the God whose armies Goliath had defied. He declared that God would give him victory so the earth would know there was a God in Israel and the assembly would learn that deliverance did not depend upon sword or spear. The battle belonged to the Lord. When Goliath advanced, David ran toward him, took a stone and slung it. It struck the champion's forehead, and he fell face down. David had carried no sword. He ran to the fallen Philistine, drew Goliath's own sword and cut off his head.", "Seeing their champion dead, the Philistines fled. Israel and Judah rose with a shout and pursued them toward Gath and Ekron, leaving the wounded along the route. The Israelites then returned to plunder the abandoned camp. The narrator records David taking the head to Jerusalem and keeping the armour in his tent. It also recalls Saul asking Abner whose son the young man was as he went out to fight. Abner could not answer, and Saul ordered an enquiry. After the victory Abner brought David before him, still carrying the head. David identified himself as the son of Jesse of Bethlehem. The young shepherd sent to bring supplies and collect news has become the man through whom Israel's fear turns into pursuit. His public explanation of the victory points beyond his own courage to the God whose honour stood at the centre of his response."], "lesson": "David remembers earlier deliverance and entrusts the present danger to the Lord. His courage invites us to connect faith with God's character and honour while serving faithfully in ordinary responsibilities.", "question": "How do David's words before the fight explain what he wants both Israel and the wider world to learn from its outcome?"},
  {"id": 92019, "title": "The Song Saul Could Not Bear", "subtitle": "Jealousy within the royal household", "text": "1 Samuel 18:1–30", "chapters": [[8, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["After David spoke with Saul, Jonathan formed a deep attachment to him. He loved David as himself and made a covenant with him. He gave him his robe, weapons and belt, expressing that friendship through gifts associated with his own standing. Saul kept David in his service, and David succeeded in the assignments given him. The people and the king's servants accepted his appointment over soldiers. As the army returned from the victory over Goliath, women came out from Israel's towns with music and dancing. Their song credited Saul with thousands and David with tens of thousands. Saul heard a threatening comparison. He began asking what David could still lack except the kingdom, and from that time watched him with suspicion.", "The next day Saul was again under the influence of the troubling spirit while David played music. With a spear in his hand, Saul tried to pin David to the wall, and David escaped him twice. Saul feared him because the Lord was with David and had departed from Saul. He sent him away from his immediate presence and made him commander over a thousand. David continued leading the people successfully, and Israel and Judah loved him. Each new success increased Saul's fear. The king then offered his older daughter Merab in marriage, urging David to show courage and fight the Lord's battles. His hidden purpose was to let the Philistines kill David. David spoke humbly about his family's standing, and Merab was eventually given to another man.", "When Saul learned that his daughter Michal loved David, he saw another opportunity to endanger him. Through servants he encouraged David to become his son in law. David replied that marriage into the royal family was a serious matter for a poor man of little standing. Saul then named a marriage price of one hundred Philistine foreskins, presenting it as vengeance upon the king's enemies. He expected David to fall in the fighting required to obtain them. David welcomed the prospect of the marriage and went out with his men. They killed two hundred Philistines and brought the full number as proof. Saul gave Michal to David as his wife. A plan designed to remove him had instead brought him into the family.", "Saul could see that the Lord was with David and that his own daughter loved him. His fear deepened, and he became David's lasting enemy. Further Philistine campaigns brought David more success than the king's other officers, and his name became highly esteemed. The chapter places Jonathan's covenant loyalty and Michal's love beside Saul's jealousy. The same young man's achievements that encouraged the nation were interpreted by the king as threats to his position. David's growing reputation came through service, while Saul's schemes used military assignments and family promises to conceal murderous intent. The danger within the household was becoming as serious as the danger on the battlefield."], "lesson": "Jealousy can turn another person's faithful service into something we resent. Saul's response calls us to examine how we react when praise, opportunity or affection goes to someone else.", "question": "What changes in Saul after he hears the song, and how do those changes shape decisions that appear outwardly generous?"},
  {"id": 92020, "title": "David Escapes the House and the King's Messengers", "subtitle": "Protection amid broken promises", "text": "1 Samuel 19:1–24", "chapters": [[8, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Saul openly told Jonathan and his servants to kill David. Jonathan, who delighted in his friend, warned him to hide while he spoke to his father. He reminded Saul that David had done him good, risked his life against the Philistine and been used by the Lord to bring a great deliverance. Saul had seen it and rejoiced. Why shed innocent blood by killing him without cause? Saul listened and swore by the living Lord that David would not be put to death. Jonathan told David and brought him back into the king's presence. For a time the earlier relationship resumed. The attempt to reason from David's actual conduct had drawn a solemn promise from Saul.", "War returned, and David inflicted a heavy defeat upon the Philistines. Later Saul sat at home with his spear while David played music. The troubling spirit was upon the king, and he again tried to pin David to the wall. David slipped away, and the spear struck the wall instead. He fled that night. Saul sent men to watch his house and kill him in the morning. Michal warned her husband that remaining would cost his life and lowered him through a window. After he escaped, she placed a household image in his bed with goats' hair at its head and covered it. When the messengers came, she said he was ill. Saul ordered them to bring the bed itself so he could kill him.", "The deception was discovered. Saul challenged Michal for allowing his enemy to escape, and she claimed that David had threatened her if she refused to let him go. David reached Samuel at Ramah and reported all Saul had done. They went to Naioth and stayed there. When Saul learned their location, he sent men to capture David. They found a company of prophets prophesying with Samuel overseeing them. The Spirit of God came upon the messengers, and they too began prophesying. Saul sent a second group and then a third. Each met the same interruption. The king's repeated orders could not achieve the capture he demanded.", "At last Saul went himself. At a well in Secu he asked where Samuel and David were and was directed to Naioth. As he travelled, the Spirit of God came upon him also. He prophesied on his way and before Samuel, stripped off his clothes and lay exposed through that day and night. The old question about Saul being among the prophets was heard again. His experience now occurred within a pursuit intended to take David's life. The chapter has shown several means by which that pursuit was interrupted, from Jonathan's appeal and Michal's warning to the overpowering of the king's messengers and Saul himself. David remains a fugitive, but Saul's authority has repeatedly failed to place him within his grasp."], "lesson": "Promises require continuing faithfulness, and Saul's oath is exposed by his later conduct. Jonathan's intervention also shows the importance of speaking plainly when an innocent person is threatened.", "question": "How does Saul's renewed attempt to kill David expose the difference between a solemn promise and a changed course of action?"},
  {"id": 92021, "title": "The Arrow Beyond the Boy", "subtitle": "Covenant loyalty at a painful parting", "text": "1 Samuel 20:1–42", "chapters": [[8, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["David left Naioth and asked Jonathan what wrong he had committed that Saul should seek his life. Jonathan initially resisted the conclusion, believing that his father would reveal any serious plan to him. David answered that Saul knew of their friendship and would conceal his intention to spare Jonathan distress. Death, David said, was only a step away. Jonathan offered to do whatever his friend needed. They agreed that David would miss the approaching New Moon meal and hide in the field. If Saul asked, Jonathan would explain his absence by a family sacrifice at Bethlehem. Saul's response would reveal whether it was safe to return. David appealed to their covenant, asking Jonathan himself to judge him if he had done wrong.", "The friends went into the field and renewed their commitment before the Lord. Jonathan promised to discover his father's intention and warn David if danger remained. He asked that David show the Lord's kindness both to him and to his household in the future, even when David's enemies were removed. Their covenant reached beyond their own lives to their descendants. Jonathan arranged a signal with arrows near the stone Ezel. He would bring a boy to collect them. Words indicating that the arrows were near would mean safety. Words saying they were beyond the boy would mean David must leave. The Lord was witness between them. David remained hidden, and the meal began with his place empty.", "On the first day Saul said nothing, assuming David was ceremonially unclean. On the second he asked Jonathan about the continuing absence. Jonathan gave the agreed explanation. Saul erupted in anger, insulting him and declaring that David's survival threatened Jonathan's own future kingdom. He ordered David brought to die. Jonathan asked what David had done to deserve execution. Saul hurled a spear at his own son. Jonathan now knew the intention was settled. He left the table in anger and grief and ate nothing that day because of the shame and danger directed at David. Saul's argument about succession had failed to make Jonathan abandon the friend with whom he had pledged loyalty before God.", "In the morning Jonathan went out with the boy, shot an arrow beyond him and called that the arrow lay farther away. He urged the boy to hurry, then sent him back to town with the equipment. The boy knew nothing of the message he had carried. David emerged from hiding, bowed three times and embraced Jonathan. Both wept, David with particular intensity. Jonathan sent him away in peace, recalling the oath they had made in the Lord's name for themselves and their descendants. David departed, and Jonathan returned to the town. The signal has confirmed the danger, but their farewell preserves a commitment that Saul's anger could not break. David must now live beyond the protection of his former place at court."], "lesson": "Loyalty can require costly truthfulness and a willingness to surrender personal advantage. Jonathan protects David even when his father appeals to his prospect of inheriting the throne.", "question": "How does Jonathan's request for future kindness to his household show the seriousness and reach of his covenant with David?"},
  {"id": 92022, "title": "Bread at Nob and Fear in Gath", "subtitle": "Need and difficult choices on the road", "text": "1 Samuel 21:1–15", "chapters": [[8, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["David arrived at Nob and met Ahimelech the priest. His appearance caused alarm, and Ahimelech asked why he had come without a company. David claimed that Saul had entrusted him with a secret assignment and that his men were waiting elsewhere. The reader knows that he is fleeing Saul, so his explanation conceals his actual situation from the priest. He asked for five loaves of bread or whatever food was available. Ahimelech had no ordinary bread, only consecrated bread. He asked whether the young men had kept themselves from women. David assured him that they had and spoke of their ceremonial readiness. Ahimelech gave him the bread that had been removed from the Lord's presence when fresh loaves were put out.", "The narrative pauses to identify a witness. Doeg the Edomite, Saul's chief herdsman, was there that day, detained before the Lord. His presence will become significant, though the consequences have not yet unfolded. David then asked for a spear or sword, explaining his lack of weapons by the supposed urgency of the king's business. Ahimelech told him that Goliath's sword was wrapped in a cloth behind the ephod. No other weapon was available. David welcomed it, saying there was none like it. The sword from his great public victory now became the weapon of a man seeking food and protection while secretly escaping the king he had served.", "That day David fled to Achish, king of Gath, in Philistine territory. The choice brought him among people who recognised him. Achish's servants recalled the song that credited Saul with thousands and David with tens of thousands. They even described David as king of the land. The fame that had attracted Israel's admiration and Saul's jealousy now endangered him before a foreign ruler. David took their words seriously and became very afraid of Achish. Seeking shelter from one king had placed him under the scrutiny of another, in the very city associated with the champion he had killed. His movements show how narrow his options appeared to him.", "David changed his behaviour before them and pretended to be mad. He made marks upon the gate doors and let saliva run down his beard. Achish rebuked his servants for bringing such a man into his presence, asking whether he lacked madmen and whether this person should enter his house. The chapter ends with the performance producing a dismissive response from the ruler. It offers no claim that David's fear, false explanation at Nob or pretence in Gath provides a general pattern for faithful conduct. Those actions belong to the account of his escape, including his vulnerability and the risks that now surround other people who have helped him. The next stage will expose a terrible consequence of the meeting at Nob."], "lesson": "Scripture tells of David's fear and concealment without hiding their consequences. His circumstances invite honest examination of the choices we make under pressure and the people those choices may affect.", "question": "What does the presence of Doeg add to the apparent relief David receives at Nob?"},
  {"id": 92023, "title": "The Refuge at Adullam and the Bloodshed at Nob", "subtitle": "Power, responsibility and shelter", "text": "1 Samuel 22:1–23", "chapters": [[8, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["David escaped from Gath to the cave of Adullam. His brothers and other members of his father's household joined him. People who were distressed, indebted or bitter in spirit also gathered around him until about four hundred men looked to him as their leader. He went to Mizpeh in Moab and asked its king to shelter his parents while he waited to learn what God would do for him. They stayed there during his time in the stronghold. Then the prophet Gad told him to leave and enter Judah. David obeyed and moved into the forest of Hereth. His flight had begun to include responsibility for followers and concern for his family's safety.", "Saul heard that David and his men had been located. Sitting with spear in hand and his servants around him, he appealed to the Benjamites among them. Would Jesse's son give them fields, vineyards and military appointments? He accused them of conspiracy and complained that no one had told him of Jonathan's covenant or shown sympathy for him. He portrayed David as a servant stirred up to ambush his master. Doeg answered with his report of seeing David at Nob. He said Ahimelech had enquired of God for him, supplied food and given him Goliath's sword. Saul summoned Ahimelech and the priests of his father's house and charged them with helping a conspiracy.", "Ahimelech defended his actions. David was the king's faithful servant, his son in law and an honoured member of his household. Enquiring for him was nothing new, and the priest knew nothing of the alleged plot. Saul refused the defence and pronounced death upon him and his household. He commanded his guards to kill the Lord's priests, but they would not raise their hands against them. Saul then ordered Doeg to do it. Doeg killed eighty five men who wore the linen ephod. The violence spread to Nob itself, where men, women, children, infants and livestock were put to the sword. Saul's suspicion had become an attack upon an entire priestly community.", "Abiathar, one of Ahimelech's sons, escaped and reached David with the news. David recalled seeing Doeg and knowing that he would report to Saul. He acknowledged responsibility for bringing death upon Abiathar's household. He offered the survivor refuge, recognising that the same enemy sought both their lives. Abiathar could remain with him and be protected. The chapter sets that promise beside the massacre ordered by the king. It also preserves the guards' refusal to carry out the command, Ahimelech's truthful defence and David's admission of responsibility. Each response matters within a story where the abuse of royal authority has inflicted suffering far beyond the two men at the centre of the pursuit."], "lesson": "Authority must answer to justice, and obedience to a human ruler cannot justify murder. David's acknowledgment also calls us to face the consequences of our actions and care for those harmed.", "question": "What do the guards' refusal, Ahimelech's defence and David's admission each reveal about responsibility in a time of fear?"},
  {"id": 92024, "title": "David Saves a Town and Escapes a Siege", "subtitle": "Seeking direction through changing danger", "text": "1 Samuel 23:1–29", "chapters": [[8, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["David heard that Philistines were attacking Keilah and robbing its threshing floors. He asked the Lord whether he should intervene and received a command to save the town. His men were already afraid in Judah and feared facing Philistine forces. David enquired again and received assurance of victory. He led them to Keilah, struck the attackers, took their cattle and rescued the inhabitants. Abiathar had brought an ephod when he fled to David, and the account now shows David seeking the Lord's direction while protecting people beyond his own company. Saul, however, interpreted news of his arrival differently. A town with gates and bars seemed a trap into which God had delivered his enemy.", "Saul summoned forces to besiege Keilah. Knowing the danger, David asked Abiathar to bring the ephod and enquired whether Saul would come and whether the townspeople would surrender him. The Lord answered that Saul would come and that they would hand David and his men over. David therefore left with about six hundred followers and kept moving. Saul abandoned the planned expedition when he heard of the escape. David remained in the wilderness strongholds and hills around Ziph. Saul searched continually, but the narrator says God did not give David into his hand. The town David had saved could not be treated as a secure refuge merely because it had received his help.", "Jonathan visited him at Horesh and strengthened his trust in God. He urged him not to fear Saul, declaring that David would become king and expressing his own expectation of standing beside him. They renewed their covenant before the Lord, and Jonathan went home. The Ziphites then offered Saul very different assistance. They reported David's hiding places and promised to surrender him. Saul blessed them for their concern and asked for careful information about every place David used. The contrast is sharp within the events themselves. Saul's son goes into the wilderness to encourage the fugitive, while others seek royal favour by helping to locate him. David moves farther into the wilderness of Maon as the search tightens.", "Saul pursued him around a mountain, the two groups moving on opposite sides. David hurried to escape, but Saul's men were drawing around him. At that moment a messenger called Saul away because the Philistines had raided the land. Saul broke off the pursuit and went to meet the invasion. The place received a name recalling the division or escape, and David moved to the strongholds of En-gedi. The rescue from the closing circle came through a report from elsewhere in the kingdom. The chapter has shown guidance, encouragement and an interruption arriving at different stages of danger. David's company remains mobile and exposed, yet Saul has again failed to capture him."], "lesson": "Faithfulness includes seeking direction, listening to encouragement and acting upon what we learn. David's escape also warns against assuming that earlier kindness guarantees another person's loyalty.", "question": "How do David's repeated enquiries and Jonathan's visit sustain him when the people and places around him offer uncertain protection?"},
  {"id": 92025, "title": "The Piece of Robe in David's Hand", "subtitle": "Mercy when revenge is within reach", "text": "1 Samuel 24:1–22", "chapters": [[8, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["When Saul returned from fighting the Philistines, he learned that David was in the wilderness of En-gedi. He took three thousand chosen men to search among the rocky heights where wild goats lived. Along the way he entered a cave to relieve himself. Farther inside, David and his men were concealed. His followers interpreted the moment as a fulfilment of God's promise to put the enemy into his hand. Here seemed the chance to act as he wished. David approached secretly and cut a corner from Saul's robe. Even that action troubled his conscience. He spoke to his men of Saul as his master and the Lord's anointed and refused to raise his hand against him.", "David restrained his followers, allowing Saul to leave unharmed. Then he came out after him and called to the king. When Saul turned, David bowed low. He asked why Saul listened to claims that he intended harm. The cave had supplied visible evidence of the opposite. He had been urged to kill him and had spared him. Holding up the cut piece, David appealed to Saul as a father and asked him to recognise that he had neither rebelled nor sought his life. Saul was nevertheless hunting him down. David placed judgment and vindication in the Lord's hands, declaring that his own hand would not strike the king. He recalled the proverb that wickedness comes from the wicked.", "David asked whom Israel's king was pursuing, comparing himself to a dead dog or a flea. He appealed again for the Lord to judge, plead his cause and rescue him. Saul recognised his voice and began to weep. He admitted that David was more righteous, having returned good for evil. David's refusal to kill him when the opportunity arose showed the kindness of his conduct. Saul asked the Lord to reward him and acknowledged that David would surely become king and establish the kingdom. The hunted man has presented his evidence openly, and the pursuer has admitted the injustice. The conversation brings both the personal wrong and the future of the kingdom into the same encounter.", "Saul asked David to swear that he would preserve his descendants and the memory of his family. David gave the oath. Saul went home, while David and his men returned to their stronghold. The ending preserves a practical distance despite the tears and admissions. David has spared Saul, defended himself and promised future kindness, but he has not returned to court or abandoned the precautions of a fugitive. His refusal to avenge himself therefore belongs alongside his recognition that danger remains. A piece of cloth has served as proof of restraint in a moment when his followers offered a persuasive reason for violence. The narrative leaves the judgment of Saul in the hands to which David has appealed."], "lesson": "An opportunity to harm someone does not itself establish a right to do so. David shows mercy, speaks truth about the wrong and continues taking sensible steps for protection.", "question": "How does David combine mercy toward Saul with honesty about the danger he still faces?"},
  {"id": 92026, "title": "Abigail Meets David on the Mountain Path", "subtitle": "Receiving correction before anger becomes bloodshed", "text": "1 Samuel 25:1–44", "chapters": [[8, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["Samuel died, and Israel gathered to mourn and bury him at Ramah. David moved into the wilderness of Paran. Nearby, at Carmel, the wealthy Nabal was shearing his three thousand sheep. His wife Abigail was discerning, while he was harsh and badly behaved. David sent ten men with greetings of peace and a request for provisions during the shearing celebration. His company had protected Nabal's shepherds and taken nothing from them. Nabal answered with contempt, dismissing David as one of the servants who broke away from their masters and refusing to share his food. When the men reported back, David ordered swords strapped on. About four hundred accompanied him, while two hundred stayed with the baggage. He intended to destroy the males of Nabal's household by morning.", "One of Nabal's servants told Abigail what had happened. He confirmed that David's men had been good to them and had protected them like a wall by night and day. Disaster threatened the household, and Nabal was too unreasonable to hear advice. Abigail acted quickly without informing him. She loaded donkeys with bread, wine, prepared sheep, roasted grain, raisins and fig cakes and sent servants ahead. Riding behind them, she met David and his armed men as they descended the mountain. David was reflecting angrily on the insult and the apparent futility of protecting Nabal's property. Abigail dismounted, bowed at his feet and asked to be heard, taking the burden of the appeal upon herself.", "She acknowledged Nabal's folly and explained that she had not seen the earlier messengers. She urged David to receive her gift and forgive. Her argument reached beyond the immediate grievance to the future God had promised him. The Lord would establish his house and preserve his life. When he became ruler, he should have no burden of conscience from shedding innocent blood or avenging himself. She asked him to remember her when God dealt well with him. David blessed the Lord for sending her and thanked her for her good judgment. She had kept him from bloodshed and personal vengeance. He received the provisions and sent her home in peace, openly admitting that he had intended terrible harm if she had not intervened.", "Nabal was holding a feast like a king's and was very drunk when Abigail returned. She waited until morning to tell him. At her report his heart failed and he became like stone. About ten days later the Lord struck him, and he died. David thanked God for vindicating his cause and keeping him from evil. He then sent to ask Abigail to become his wife. She responded humbly and went with the messengers, accompanied by five attendants. The chapter also records David's marriage to Ahinoam of Jezreel and Saul's giving of Michal to another man. Abigail's intervention has exposed a dangerous failure in David himself. The man who spared Saul had nearly slaughtered a household over an insult, and he needed to receive another person's correction."], "lesson": "Wise intervention can prevent grievous harm, and accepting correction can be an act of strength. David's gratitude asks us to welcome those who challenge an angry course before we act upon it.", "question": "Which parts of Abigail's appeal help David see his intended revenge in the light of his responsibilities before God?"},
  {"id": 92027, "title": "The Spear Beside the Sleeping King", "subtitle": "Faithfulness tested a second time", "text": "1 Samuel 26:1–25", "chapters": [[8, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The Ziphites again went to Saul with information about David, reporting that he was hiding near the hill of Hachilah. Saul returned to the wilderness with three thousand chosen soldiers. David learned of the pursuit, sent scouts to confirm it and went to observe the camp. He could see where Saul lay, with Abner and the troops around him. He asked Ahimelech the Hittite and Abishai, Joab's brother, who would accompany him into the camp. Abishai volunteered. They entered at night and found Saul asleep with his spear planted beside his head. The commander and soldiers lay sleeping around their king. The man who had repeatedly used a spear to threaten David was now defenceless beside that same kind of weapon.", "Abishai interpreted the moment as God's delivery of the enemy and asked permission to kill Saul with a single thrust. David forbade it. Who could strike the Lord's anointed and remain guiltless? He entrusted Saul's end to the Lord, whether through divine judgment, the ordinary arrival of death or battle. They would take the spear and water jar and leave. David took them, and the two escaped unseen. No one woke because the Lord had caused a deep sleep to fall upon the camp. The opportunity to kill had been unmistakable, but David again refused to secure his future through the death of the king. Abishai's confidence about the occasion did not override the responsibility David believed he owed before God.", "From a hill at a safe distance, David called to Abner and challenged his failure to guard Saul. Someone had entered the camp, and the missing spear and jar proved how exposed the king had been. Saul recognised David's voice. David asked what evil he had done to deserve the pursuit. If God had stirred Saul against him, an offering could be sought, but if people had done so, they bore responsibility for driving him away from the Lord's inheritance. He spoke of being pressed toward service of other gods and compared the king's pursuit to hunting a flea or a partridge. Exile threatened his place among the people and worship of the Lord.", "Saul admitted that he had sinned and acted foolishly. Because David had valued his life, he promised not to harm him and invited him to return. David offered the spear back by means of a young man who could cross to collect it. He appealed to the Lord to reward righteousness and faithfulness, and to value his life as he had valued Saul's. Saul blessed him and predicted success. They parted, each going his own way. The episode resembles the earlier cave encounter but presents a fresh choice with another companion urging violence. David's restraint had to be practised again, and his separation from Saul continued despite the king's admission and invitation."], "lesson": "A conviction tested once may need to be obeyed repeatedly. David's restraint and careful distance invite mercy, consistency and continued dependence upon God when hostility returns.", "question": "Why does a second opportunity to kill Saul matter for understanding David's faithfulness?"},
  {"id": 92028, "title": "David Makes His Home in Ziklag", "subtitle": "Fear and the cost of concealment", "text": "1 Samuel 27:1–12", "chapters": [[8, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["After the encounters in which Saul had admitted wrongdoing, David still believed that one day the king would kill him. He concluded that his safest course was to escape into Philistine territory, where Saul would give up searching. He crossed to Achish king of Gath with his six hundred men and their households. His wives Ahinoam and Abigail went with him. The account records the result he expected: when Saul heard he had fled to Gath, he stopped pursuing him. David's fear had produced a practical decision that removed the immediate chase. Yet the new arrangement placed his company and their families under the protection of a Philistine ruler whose interests could differ sharply from Israel's.", "David asked Achish for a place in a country town, presenting continued residence in the royal city as unnecessary for a servant. Achish gave him Ziklag. The narrator explains that the town consequently belonged to Judah's kings in later days. David lived in Philistine country for a year and four months. Ziklag gave his company a base away from Achish's immediate presence, but his relationship with the king still required an account of his activities. The settled period therefore carried obligations as well as relief. His people had a home while their leader continued navigating the dangers of depending upon a foreign court.", "David and his men raided the Geshurites, Girzites and Amalekites, peoples occupying the region toward Shur and Egypt. They took sheep, cattle, donkeys, camels and clothing. The narrative also states that they killed the men and women, leaving no survivors to report their actions to Gath. When Achish asked where they had raided, David named areas associated with southern Judah, the Jerahmeelites and the Kenites. His answers gave Achish the impression that he was attacking people connected with Israel. The account describes killing that served to preserve this deception. Those details must remain part of the story, for the apparent safety of Ziklag had become entangled with acts of violence and false reporting.", "Achish believed David. He concluded that David had made himself hateful to his own people and would therefore remain his servant permanently. David knew the real targets of the raids, while the king trusted the version he was given. The arrangement seemed stable because those two understandings were kept apart. The chapter contains no enquiry of the Lord comparable to David's questions at Keilah. It recounts his private conclusion, his escape and the conduct that followed. Its ending prepares for a crisis when Achish will expect the loyalty he thinks David has demonstrated. The refuge that ended Saul's pursuit has created a different set of demands upon the man appointed to lead Israel."], "lesson": "Fear can narrow our view of the choices before us and draw us into further concealment. This account calls for honest attention to the consequences of David's decisions, including harm to others.", "question": "How does Achish's mistaken belief at the end of the chapter create a future danger for David?"},
  {"id": 92029, "title": "Saul Seeks Samuel at Endor", "subtitle": "Facing the word he had refused", "text": "1 Samuel 28:1–25", "chapters": [[8, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["The Philistines gathered for war against Israel, and Achish told David that he and his men must join the campaign. David answered that the king would see what his servant could do, and Achish promised to make him a permanent bodyguard. The narrative then turns to Saul, recalling Samuel's death and Saul's removal of mediums and spiritists from the land. Facing the Philistine army, Saul was terrified. He enquired of the Lord but received no answer through dreams, the Urim or prophets. He then ordered his servants to find a medium. They directed him to a woman at Endor. Disguised and accompanied by two men, he visited her at night.", "The woman feared a trap, since Saul himself had suppressed the practices she was being asked to perform. Saul swore in the Lord's name that she would suffer no punishment. Asked whom he wanted brought up, he named Samuel. The narrative says that when the woman saw Samuel she cried aloud and recognised the disguised king. Saul reassured her and asked what she saw. She described a figure rising, an old man wrapped in a robe, and Saul understood that it was Samuel. He bowed with his face to the ground. The account reports the encounter and the message that followed without giving readers a practice to imitate. The king was now seeking help through the very activity he had forbidden.", "Samuel asked why Saul had disturbed him. Saul described his distress, the Philistine threat and the absence of an answer from God. He wanted to know what to do. Samuel replied that asking him could not undo the Lord's departure and judgment. The kingdom had been torn from Saul and given to David, as previously announced. His failure to obey concerning Amalek stood behind the judgment now reaching him. Israel and its army would fall into Philistine hands, and the next day Saul and his sons would join Samuel in death. The message offered no escape from the word already spoken. Saul had sought a new answer, but the earlier judgment was confirmed.", "He collapsed to the ground in fear, weakened by having eaten nothing through the day and night. The woman approached and urged him to accept food, recalling the risk she had taken in responding to his request. At first he refused. With his servants she persuaded him to rise and eat. She prepared a calf and unleavened bread and served them. After the meal Saul and his companions departed into the night. The chapter ends with food restoring enough strength to travel while the announced defeat remains ahead. Saul's journey has led from unanswered enquiry into a forbidden consultation, and from there back to the judgment he had resisted. His fear has deepened, and the battle still waits."], "lesson": "Seeking reassurance while refusing God's known word leads into deeper trouble. Saul's night at Endor calls us to face correction and turn to God with obedience instead of pursuing forbidden means of control.", "question": "How does the message Saul receives repeat what he had already been told about his kingship?"},
  {"id": 92030, "title": "The Philistine Commanders Send David Back", "subtitle": "Release from a divided position", "text": "1 Samuel 29:1–11", "chapters": [[8, 29]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["At Aphek the Philistine forces assembled while Israel camped near the spring in Jezreel. The rulers passed in companies of hundreds and thousands. David and his men marched at the rear with Achish. Their presence raised an immediate objection from the other commanders. What were these Hebrews doing among them? Achish defended David as Saul's former servant who had lived with him for a considerable time. He claimed that he had found no fault in him since his arrival. The confidence established by David's reports from Ziklag now met the suspicion of men preparing to fight Israel. Achish's personal approval could not settle the question for the whole army.", "The commanders insisted that David return to the place allotted to him. They feared that he might become their enemy during battle and regain Saul's favour by turning upon them. They remembered the song about Saul's thousands and David's tens of thousands. David's reputation, celebrated in Israel, remained powerful in Philistine memory. Their objection concerned what could happen when the fighting began, regardless of how faithfully Achish believed David had served him. They were angry and would not accept the risk. The chapter therefore shows a limit to the protection and influence Achish could provide. He could house David and give him a town, but he could not compel the other rulers to welcome him into this campaign.", "Achish summoned David and praised his conduct, even swearing by the Lord that he regarded him as upright. He said he had found no evil in him but that the rulers did not approve. David must return peacefully and avoid provoking them. David asked what he had done and why he could not fight the enemies of his lord the king. The wording leaves the reader hearing a man whose allegiance has become difficult to express within this setting. Achish answered with further personal confidence, likening David's standing in his eyes to that of an angel of God. Nevertheless, the commanders had spoken clearly. David would not go into battle with them.", "Achish instructed him to rise early with the men who had come from Saul's service and depart as soon as there was light. David and his company did so, returning toward Philistine territory while the Philistine army advanced to Jezreel. The immediate prospect of marching against Israel had been removed by the objections of foreign commanders. The narrator gives no account of what David would have done if he had remained in the battle. Instead, it records the order and his departure. His refuge with Achish had placed him in a dangerous position, and an unwelcome dismissal now released him from it. The road home, however, would lead to an unexpected disaster at Ziklag."], "lesson": "A disappointment can interrupt a course that has become deeply compromised. David's dismissal invites humility about situations in which approval from one person conceals a wider conflict of loyalties.", "question": "Why does Achish's praise fail to make David's position within the Philistine army secure?"},
  {"id": 92031, "title": "David Returns to a Burned Ziklag", "subtitle": "Finding strength and sharing deliverance", "text": "1 Samuel 30:1–31", "chapters": [[8, 30]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["On the third day David and his men reached Ziklag and found it burned. Amalekite raiders had carried off the women, children and possessions, though they had killed none of the captives. The men wept until they had no strength left. David's wives were also taken. His own grief was joined by the threat of stoning, as the others' anguish turned against him. David strengthened himself in the Lord his God. He asked Abiathar to bring the ephod and enquired whether he should pursue the raiders and whether he would overtake them. The answer promised that he would overtake them and recover all. He set out with his six hundred men. At the Besor ravine, two hundred were too exhausted to continue, and four hundred went on.", "They found an Egyptian lying in a field and gave him bread, water, figs and raisins. He revived after three days without food or drink. He was the servant of an Amalekite who had abandoned him when he became ill. He described the raids, including the burning of Ziklag. David asked whether he could guide them to the raiding party. The young man requested an oath that he would neither be killed nor returned to his master. He then led them to a great company scattered over the ground, eating, drinking and celebrating the plunder taken from Judah and Philistine territory. The man left behind as useless had become the guide to the missing families.", "David attacked, and the fighting continued from twilight until the following evening. Four hundred young men escaped on camels. David recovered everything, including his wives, the sons and daughters of his followers and all their property. Additional flocks and herds became spoil. On returning to the Besor, David greeted the men who had been too weak to continue. Some who had fought wanted to give them only their wives and children and send them away without a share of the goods. David refused. The Lord had preserved them and given them victory, so they must share what He had given. Those who remained with the supplies would receive the same portion as those who entered the battle.", "David established that principle as a lasting rule. When he returned to Ziklag he also sent gifts from the spoil to the elders of Judah and his friends. The account names towns and districts, including Bethel, Jattir, Aroer, Eshtemoa, Hormah and Hebron, as well as places associated with the Jerahmeelites and Kenites. The gifts reached communities where he and his men had travelled. The chapter begins with a town in ashes and a leader under threat from his own followers. It ends with recovered families, a refusal to shame the exhausted and generosity extending beyond the company. David's acknowledgment of the Lord's help shapes the way he distributes the benefits of deliverance."], "lesson": "Strength sought in God should shape our treatment of the weak after a crisis. David's sharing challenges the claim that only the most visibly successful deserve a portion of what God has given.", "question": "How does David's understanding of the source of victory answer the men who want to exclude their exhausted companions?"},
  {"id": 92032, "title": "The Men of Jabesh Bring Saul Home", "subtitle": "Grief at the end of a reign", "text": "1 Samuel 31:1–13", "chapters": [[8, 31]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Samuel", "story": ["While David's story has followed the recovery from Ziklag, the final chapter returns to Israel's battle with the Philistines. The army fled, and men fell on Mount Gilboa. The Philistines pressed after Saul and his sons. Jonathan, Abinadab and Malchishua were killed. Saul came under heavy attack from archers and was badly wounded. He asked his armour bearer to kill him, fearing capture and abuse at the enemy's hands. The armour bearer was too afraid to comply. Saul then fell upon his own sword, and his attendant, seeing that he was dead, did the same. The account records these deaths as part of the terrible collapse of the king's position and the loss suffered by Israel that day.", "News of the rout and the deaths caused people in the valley and across the Jordan to abandon their towns. The Philistines occupied the deserted places. The defeat therefore reached beyond the soldiers on the mountain, displacing communities and changing control of the land. The next day Philistines came to strip the bodies and found Saul and his three sons. They cut off his head and removed his armour. Messengers carried news through their territory and into the houses of their idols. They displayed his armour in the temple of Ashtaroth and fastened his body to the wall of Beth-shan. Israel's national disaster became an occasion for public celebration among its enemies.", "At Jabesh-gilead the people heard what had been done to Saul. The town had once faced Nahash's threat to put out the right eyes of its men, and Saul's first great victory had rescued them. Now its courageous men travelled through the night to retrieve his body and those of his sons from the wall. They brought them back to Jabesh, burned the bodies and buried the bones beneath a tree. The community fasted for seven days. Their action could not reverse the battle, restore the lost towns or bring back the dead. It gave the bodies a burial and answered public humiliation with a final act of loyalty from a people who remembered their deliverance.", "The book closes in mourning. Samuel, who prayed for the people and warned their king, is already dead. Jonathan, whose friendship sustained David, has fallen beside his father. Saul's early courage, his growing jealousy and his repeated refusal of God's word have reached their final earthly outcome. David remains alive elsewhere, but the chapter offers no scene of his taking the throne. Its last attention belongs to the dead and to the men who honoured them. The grief at Jabesh leaves room to remember the rescue of chapter eleven while also facing everything that followed. The future of Israel and the response of David will open the next book, after this account of defeat, burial and fasting."], "lesson": "The end of Saul's reign calls for sober attention to a lifetime of choices. The people of Jabesh also show how gratitude can endure through another person's failure and find expression in a costly act of kindness.", "question": "Why is the final act of the people of Jabesh significant when read alongside Saul's earlier rescue of their town?"},
  {"id": 92500, "title": "David Receives the Crown and Sings a Lament", "subtitle": "Grieving without celebrating an enemy's fall", "text": "2 Samuel 1:1–27", "chapters": [[9, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["David had returned to Ziklag after defeating the Amalekite raiders. On the third day a man arrived from Israel's camp with torn clothes and earth upon his head. He bowed before David and reported the army's flight and the deaths of Saul and Jonathan. David asked how he knew. The messenger described finding Saul on Mount Gilboa, pursued by enemy forces, and claimed that Saul had asked him to end his life. He said he had done so and brought the crown and bracelet from the king's body. This is the messenger's account of his own action. The closing chapter of 1 Samuel has already described Saul falling upon his sword. The narrator now shows what David does with the claim made before him.", "David and his men tore their clothes. They mourned, wept and fasted until evening for Saul, Jonathan and the fallen people of Israel. The death of the king who had hunted him was received as a national sorrow. David questioned the messenger further and learned that he was an Amalekite resident among Israel. He asked why the man had not feared to strike the Lord's anointed. Calling an attendant, David ordered his execution. He held the man accountable by his own testimony that he had killed Saul. The crown delivered into David's hands brought no reward to the bearer. David continued the refusal to treat the destruction of Saul as a service he wished others to perform.", "He then composed a lament over Saul and Jonathan. The account connects its preservation with the Book of Jashar and its teaching among the people of Judah. David mourned Israel's fallen splendour and did not want Philistine towns to celebrate the news. His words turned toward Gilboa, where shields lay dishonoured, and remembered the courage of Saul and Jonathan in battle. He spoke of Jonathan's bow and Saul's sword, their strength and swiftness, and the unity of their deaths. He called the women of Israel to weep for the king under whom they had received prosperity. The lament remembers what the nation's loss includes, even after all that Saul's later conduct had done to David.", "The song becomes deeply personal as David addresses Jonathan. His friend had been dear to him, and the love shown in their covenant friendship had been extraordinary. The repeated cry over the fallen mighty men holds public grief and personal bereavement together. David offers no triumphant announcement of his own future in the poem. The book opens with a crown brought to Ziklag, but the first sustained response is mourning. Israel has lost its king, David has lost his faithful friend, and the weapons of its warriors lie useless. The question of who will rule next follows this act of remembrance, rather than erasing it."], "lesson": "Another person's downfall can bring relief and still call for grief. David's lament asks us to remember lives truthfully and resist making suffering an occasion for self advancement.", "question": "What does David choose to remember about Saul and Jonathan, and how does that shape his response to their deaths?"},
  {"id": 92501, "title": "Two Kingdoms and a Battle by the Pool", "subtitle": "The sorrow of a divided people", "text": "2 Samuel 2:1–32", "chapters": [[9, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["After mourning Saul, David asked the Lord whether he should enter one of Judah's towns. The answer directed him to Hebron. He went with Ahinoam, Abigail, his followers and their households, and they settled in its surrounding towns. The men of Judah anointed him king over their own people. Learning that Jabesh-gilead had buried Saul, David sent a message blessing them for their loyalty and promising kindness in return. He encouraged them to be strong, told them that Saul was dead and that Judah had made him king. His new position began with recognition from one part of the nation and an acknowledgment of those who had honoured his predecessor.", "Abner, Saul's commander, established Saul's son Ish-bosheth at Mahanaim as king over the remaining territories. Judah followed David, while the other side maintained Saul's house. The chapter records Ish-bosheth's age and reign and the seven and a half years David would rule Judah from Hebron. The division soon brought the two armies together at the pool of Gibeon. Abner's men sat on one side and David's men, led by Joab, on the other. Abner proposed a contest between young warriors, and Joab agreed. Twelve from each side advanced. Each seized an opponent and struck him, so that the whole group fell together. The encounter opened into fierce battle, and Abner's force was defeated.", "Among Joab's men was his swift brother Asahel. He pursued Abner without turning aside. Abner recognised him and urged him to pursue another soldier instead. Asahel refused. Abner warned him again, saying that killing him would make it impossible to face Joab. Still he came on. Abner struck him with the rear end of his spear, and Asahel fell dead. Men stopped when they reached the place where he lay. Joab and his other brother Abishai continued pursuing Abner until sunset, when the Benjamites rallied around their commander on a hill. A battle between rival royal houses had now created a personal loss that would continue shaping the conflict.", "Abner called to Joab and asked whether the sword must keep devouring, warning that its end would be bitterness. How long before he called men back from pursuing their brothers? Joab answered and sounded the trumpet. The fighting ceased. Abner's men travelled through the night, crossed the Jordan and returned to Mahanaim. Joab gathered his force and counted nineteen missing men besides Asahel, while Abner's side had lost three hundred and sixty. Asahel was buried in his father's tomb at Bethlehem. Joab and his men walked all night and reached Hebron at dawn. The kingdom has begun to change hands, but its opening stages have left graves, hostility and divided loyalties among people who belong to one nation."], "lesson": "Rivalry can turn neighbours and relatives into enemies and leave wounds that outlast a battle. The question about the sword's bitterness calls us to consider where escalating conflict is leading.", "question": "How does Asahel's death change the meaning of what began as a contest between the two forces?"},
  {"id": 92502, "title": "Abner Leaves in Peace and Returns to His Death", "subtitle": "Revenge threatens a path toward unity", "text": "2 Samuel 3:1–39", "chapters": [[9, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The war between Saul's house and David's continued. David grew stronger, and Saul's house weakened. Sons were born to David at Hebron, including Amnon, Chileab, Absalom and Adonijah, whose names would belong to later family struggles. Meanwhile Abner consolidated his influence under Ish-bosheth. When the king accused him concerning Saul's concubine Rizpah, Abner was furious. He recalled his loyalty to Saul's family and declared that he would now bring the kingdom to David, whose future rule he acknowledged the Lord had promised. Ish-bosheth was too afraid of him to answer. Abner sent David an offer to bring Israel over to him, but David required the return of Michal before they met.", "David also demanded Michal from Ish-bosheth, recalling their marriage. She was taken from Paltiel, who followed her weeping until Abner ordered him back. The political negotiation therefore carried a painful human separation within it. Abner spoke with Israel's elders and with Benjamin, urging them to make David king as they had previously desired and recalling the Lord's promise of deliverance through him. He arrived at Hebron with twenty men, and David gave them a feast. Abner undertook to gather the nation into a covenant with David. The king sent him away in peace. A long conflict appeared to be moving toward a negotiated settlement.", "Joab returned from a raid with plunder and learned what had happened. He accused Abner of spying and rebuked David for letting him leave. Without the king's knowledge, Joab sent messengers to bring Abner back. Taking him aside at Hebron's gate as though for a private conversation, he killed him in revenge for Asahel. The account names Joab and Abishai's family vengeance behind the act. David declared himself and his kingdom innocent of Abner's blood and pronounced a curse upon Joab's house. He ordered Joab and the people to put on mourning clothes. David himself walked behind the bier, wept at Abner's grave and lamented that an unbound man had fallen before wicked men.", "The people urged David to eat, but he vowed to take nothing until sunset. His public grief persuaded them that he had not ordered the killing. He spoke to his servants of a great leader fallen in Israel and admitted the difficulty of restraining the sons of Zeruiah, despite his own anointing. He appealed to the Lord to repay the evildoer. The chapter ends with the king mourning a man who had come to him in peace while acknowledging limits in his control over his own commanders. Abner's change of allegiance had opened a possibility of unity, but family revenge had cut through the settlement. David's lament and fasting addressed the murder publicly, yet Joab's power remained a troubling presence within his government."], "lesson": "Private revenge can destroy efforts toward peace and burden an entire community. David's grief calls attention to the difference between gaining advantage from an event and accepting responsibility for its justice.", "question": "How do Abner's peaceful departure and Joab's secret recall expose the nature of the killing?"},
  {"id": 92503, "title": "The Head Brought to Hebron", "subtitle": "Refusing profit from treachery", "text": "2 Samuel 4:1–12", "chapters": [[9, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["News of Abner's death weakened Ish-bosheth and troubled Israel. The commander who had sustained Saul's royal house was gone. Two of Ish-bosheth's raiding captains, Rechab and Baanah, sons of Rimmon from Beeroth, now enter the account. Their background is connected with Benjamin and with Beerothites who had fled to Gittaim. The chapter also pauses to mention Jonathan's son Mephibosheth. At five years old he had been injured when his nurse hurried to escape after hearing of Saul and Jonathan's deaths. He was left disabled in both feet. This notice preserves the presence of another vulnerable member of Saul's family while the struggle over its remaining power continues.", "Rechab and Baanah came to Ish-bosheth's house during the midday heat, when he was resting. Entering as though to obtain wheat, they reached his bedroom and murdered him upon his bed. They cut off his head and carried it away through the Arabah during the night. Their journey ended before David at Hebron. They presented the head as news of an enemy removed, describing Ish-bosheth as the son of Saul who had sought David's life. They even claimed that the Lord had avenged David against Saul and his descendants. Their account tried to turn a private murder into an act of service under a religious explanation.", "David answered by invoking the living Lord who had delivered him from every adversity. He recalled what had happened at Ziklag when a messenger expected a reward for announcing Saul's death and claiming to have killed him. David had ordered that man's execution. How much more, he asked, should he demand an account from men who had killed an innocent person in his own house upon his bed? The victim's political position did not justify the action. David would not accept their claim that the murder had become righteous because it removed a rival. Their expectation of favourable treatment met the same refusal that had greeted the earlier bearer of a crown.", "David ordered the brothers put to death. Their hands and feet were cut off, and their bodies were displayed beside the pool at Hebron. Ish-bosheth's head was buried in Abner's tomb. The chapter closes the remaining reign of Saul's son with both treachery and a severe public punishment of the perpetrators. David has benefited politically from the weakening of Saul's house, but the narrative records his insistence that this killing be judged. The earlier mention of Mephibosheth also keeps the reader from treating the family simply as a defeated institution. It includes people whose safety and future still matter. Their place in David's kingdom will return later in the story."], "lesson": "An outcome that benefits us does not make the means just. Rechab and Baanah's appeal to God's vengeance calls for careful judgment of claims used to excuse wrongdoing.", "question": "Why does David call Ish-bosheth innocent in this matter despite their rival claims to rule?"},
  {"id": 92504, "title": "David Takes Jerusalem and Seeks the Lord Again", "subtitle": "Established for the sake of God's people", "text": "2 Samuel 5:1–25", "chapters": [[9, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Representatives from every tribe came to David at Hebron. They spoke of their kinship with him, remembered his leadership even during Saul's reign and recalled the Lord's word that he would shepherd and govern Israel. David made a covenant with the elders before the Lord, and they anointed him king over the united nation. The chapter summarises his forty years of rule, beginning at age thirty, with seven and a half years in Hebron and thirty three in Jerusalem. The long wait that began with Samuel's anointing had passed through flight, war and division. Public recognition now extended beyond Judah to the whole people.", "David and his men advanced against the Jebusite stronghold at Jerusalem. Its defenders taunted him with the claim that even the blind and lame could keep him out. He nevertheless captured Zion and made it his city. The account recalls the water passage in connection with the assault and the bitter expressions associated with the siege. David built up the surrounding area, and his strength increased because the Lord was with him. Hiram of Tyre sent cedar, carpenters and stoneworkers to build him a palace. David recognised that God had established his kingship and exalted the kingdom for Israel's sake. The narrative also records additional wives, concubines, sons and daughters in Jerusalem, naming children who would form part of the royal household.", "The Philistines heard that David had become king over Israel and came to seek him. He withdrew to a stronghold while they spread through the valley of Rephaim. David enquired whether he should attack and whether God would give him victory. Receiving assurance, he went to Baal-perazim and defeated them. He described the Lord's action as a breaking through like water and named the place accordingly. The Philistines abandoned their images, which David and his men took away and burned. His military response followed an enquiry, and his explanation of the victory credited the Lord rather than treating the united throne as sufficient strength in itself.", "The Philistines returned to the same valley. David asked again and received a different instruction. He was to circle behind them and approach near the balsam trees. A sound of marching in their tops would signal the moment to act, for the Lord would go before him. David followed that direction and struck them from Geba toward Gezer. A repeated threat did not make renewed enquiry unnecessary. The chapter joins the establishment of a capital, the growth of a household and two campaigns under the account of God's presence with David. His own recognition that the kingdom had been exalted for the people's sake gives a purpose by which his later use of power will need to be assessed."], "lesson": "Responsibility is entrusted for the good of others. David's renewed enquiry also invites us to seek God's direction without assuming an earlier answer automatically settles a new occasion.", "question": "How do David's recognition of the kingdom's purpose and his second enquiry guide your understanding of faithful leadership?"},
  {"id": 92505, "title": "The Ark's Journey Stops and Begins Again", "subtitle": "Joy held together with reverence", "text": "2 Samuel 6:1–23", "chapters": [[9, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["David gathered thirty thousand chosen men to bring the ark of God from Abinadab's house into his city. A new cart carried it, guided by Uzzah and Ahio, while David and Israel celebrated with many instruments. At the threshing floor of Nacon the oxen stumbled, and Uzzah reached out to steady the ark. The Lord struck him down there for his irreverent act. The journey stopped in shock. David was angry and afraid and named the place Perez-uzzah, remembering the outbreak. He asked how the ark of the Lord could come to him. The sacred object whose arrival inspired music had also brought a reminder of the holiness of the God whose name it bore.", "David diverted the ark to the house of Obed-edom the Gittite. It remained there for three months, and the Lord blessed the household. When David heard of that blessing, he brought the ark toward Jerusalem with rejoicing. This time the account describes people carrying it. After they had taken six steps, he offered sacrifices. Wearing a linen ephod, David danced before the Lord with all his strength. The people brought the ark with shouting and trumpet sound. The interrupted journey resumed with sacrifice, movement and joy. Its earlier grief remained part of the story, and its completion could not be understood merely as a successful royal procession.", "Michal watched from a window as David leaped and danced and despised him in her heart. The ark was placed in the tent he had prepared. David offered burnt offerings and fellowship sacrifices, blessed the people in the Lord's name and distributed food to the assembled men and women. They returned home, and he went to bless his own household. Michal met him with a sarcastic accusation that he had exposed himself before servants' women like a shameless person. Her words raised the question of royal dignity at the very point where David had been rejoicing openly before the Lord. The public celebration was followed by a sharp disagreement within the home.", "David replied that his celebration had been before the Lord, who chose him over Saul's house to lead Israel. He was willing to appear lowly and humble himself further, and he said the women Michal mentioned would honour him. The chapter closes by recording that Michal had no child through the remainder of her life. The account therefore includes both the blessing shared by the gathered people and an unresolved breach within the royal household. The ark now stands in Jerusalem, but its journey has required a response to God's holiness, and its arrival has exposed differing judgments about honour. David's explanation locates his worship before God, while the wider narrative continues to hold the worshipper accountable for his conduct."], "lesson": "Worship calls for reverence and willing humility before God. The stopped procession and the later celebration invite us to hold joy, obedience and concern for God's honour together.", "question": "How does the first interruption change the way you understand the rejoicing when the ark finally enters Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 92506, "title": "The House God Promised to David", "subtitle": "Receiving a promise with humble prayer", "text": "2 Samuel 7:1–29", "chapters": [[9, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Settled in his cedar palace and enjoying rest from surrounding enemies, David spoke to Nathan about the ark's tent. He wanted a more lasting house for it. Nathan initially encouraged him to do what he intended, since the Lord was with him. That night God's word came to the prophet with a different message. He was to ask David whether he was the one to build a house for the Lord. Since the exodus God had accompanied His people in a tent and had not demanded a cedar building from their leaders. Nathan's first approval now had to give way to the word he had actually received. David's generous intention would be answered by a promise reaching beyond the building he had considered.", "The Lord recalled taking David from tending sheep and making him ruler. He had been with him, removed enemies and given him a great name. He would provide a secure place for Israel and give rest from oppression. Then the language of a house changed its meaning. God would establish David's house through his descendants. After David's death, a son would succeed him and build a house for God's name. God would establish his throne and relate to him as a father to a son. Wrongdoing would bring discipline, yet God's steadfast love would not be removed as it had been from Saul. The promise extended to the enduring establishment of David's house, kingdom and throne.", "Nathan faithfully delivered the whole message. David went in and sat before the Lord. He asked who he and his household were that God had brought them so far. Now God had spoken of a distant future as well. David recognised that the Lord knew His servant and had acted according to His own word and heart. His prayer praised God's greatness and uniqueness and remembered Israel's redemption from Egypt. The people had been made God's own, and He had become their God. The prayer draws the king's personal gratitude into the larger history of the people whose ruler he has become. His future is held within God's dealings with them.", "David then asked God to establish what He had promised, so that His name would be honoured and the house of His servant would endure. The promise itself had given him courage to pray. He confessed that God's words were true and asked for the blessing that would continue upon his household. The chapter begins with David considering what he might build for God and ends with him receiving and praying over what God has promised to establish. Its language includes discipline as well as enduring kindness. The dynasty's hope rests upon God's faithfulness, while the lives of David and his descendants remain accountable to the Father whose promise they have received."], "lesson": "God's promises invite gratitude, humility and prayer shaped by His word. David's response teaches us to receive His generosity without treating it as something we have earned.", "question": "How does God's promise change the meaning of the house David first wanted to build?"},
  {"id": 92507, "title": "Victories, Tribute and the Work of Justice", "subtitle": "Power entrusted for faithful rule", "text": "2 Samuel 8:1–18", "chapters": [[9, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The account next surveys David's campaigns and the widening reach of his kingdom. He subdued the Philistines and took a place of strategic control from them. He also defeated Moab. The narrative describes a severe treatment of the defeated people, measuring them with lines and putting two portions to death while sparing another. Moab became subject to him and paid tribute. These are events of ancient warfare with grave human consequences, and the story records them as part of the growth of David's power. It does not let the reader imagine that the expanding kingdom consisted only of peaceful ceremonies and celebrations. The territory beyond Israel's borders was being reshaped through military force.", "David also defeated Hadadezer of Zobah, capturing men, chariots and horsemen. He disabled most of the chariot horses while preserving enough for a limited force. When the Arameans of Damascus came to help Hadadezer, they too were defeated. David placed garrisons in their territory, and they brought tribute. The narrator repeatedly attributes his preservation to the Lord. Golden shields and large quantities of bronze were taken from the defeated king and his towns. These details give material form to the victories, showing how military success brought both security and wealth to Jerusalem. The power of the neighbouring rulers was reduced while David's resources grew.", "Toi king of Hamath heard that Hadadezer, his own enemy, had been defeated. He sent his son Joram to congratulate David, with objects of silver, gold and bronze. David dedicated these gifts to the Lord along with precious metals from other conquered peoples. The account recalls spoil and tribute from several nations, then describes his renown after the campaign in the Valley of Salt and his garrisons throughout Edom. The surrounding peoples had become subject to him. Once again the narrative states that the Lord preserved David wherever he went. The wealth received through conquest and diplomacy was connected with dedication to God, even as the chapter continued recording the exercise of royal dominion.", "The final verses turn from campaigns to government. David ruled all Israel and administered justice and righteousness to the people. Joab commanded the army, Jehoshaphat served as recorder, Zadok and Ahimelech held priestly responsibilities, and Seraiah was secretary. Benaiah oversaw the Cherethites and Pelethites, and David's sons occupied leading positions. The list shows a kingdom requiring ongoing civil, religious and military administration. Victory could enlarge its borders, but daily judgment still mattered within them. The chapter's movement toward justice gives another measure of the king's responsibility. The people needed fair government as well as protection, and those entrusted with office would influence how that responsibility was carried out."], "lesson": "Success and resources bring responsibility for justice. The chapter's closing account of government asks us to judge leadership by its care for people as well as its visible achievements.", "question": "Why is the statement about justice important at the end of a chapter largely concerned with campaigns and tribute?"},
  {"id": 92508, "title": "A Place at the King's Table", "subtitle": "Keeping kindness promised long ago", "text": "2 Samuel 9:1–13", "chapters": [[9, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["David asked whether anyone remained of Saul's family to whom he could show kindness for Jonathan's sake. His question reached back to the covenant made when Jonathan protected him from Saul. The friend who had helped the fugitive was dead, but the promise had a living claim upon the king. A servant of Saul's house named Ziba was summoned. Asked whether someone remained, he told David of Jonathan's son, who was disabled in both feet. The king asked where he lived. He was staying with Machir son of Ammiel at Lo-debar. David sent for him. The initiative came from the throne, before the young man had made any appeal for property or recognition.", "Mephibosheth arrived and bowed with his face to the ground. David addressed him by name and told him not to fear. He would show him kindness because of Jonathan, restore the land belonging to Saul and give him a continuing place at his own table. Mephibosheth bowed again and expressed astonishment that the king should notice someone he described as a dead dog. His own words show how low he regarded his position before David. The king's answer, however, had already named a relationship and a commitment that gave reason for welcome. Jonathan's son would receive both material provision and a place within the daily life of the royal household.", "David called Ziba and announced that everything belonging to Saul and his house had been given to Mephibosheth. Ziba, his sons and his servants were to work the land and bring in its produce. The estate required practical management so that the promise would become an enduring provision. Ziba had fifteen sons and twenty servants, making a substantial household available for the task. He agreed to carry out the king's command. David repeated that Mephibosheth would eat at his table like one of his sons. The repeated statement makes the invitation central to the account. Restoration of an inheritance was joined to a personal welcome that could be experienced day after day.", "The narrator adds that Mephibosheth had a young son named Mica and that Ziba's household served him. Mephibosheth lived in Jerusalem and regularly ate with the king. The final reminder that both his feet were disabled remains beside that picture of belonging. His circumstances have not all disappeared, but his place within David's care has changed. The chapter offers a quieter exercise of kingship after the campaigns and court appointments. David uses his authority to remember a covenant, locate a person who could easily remain overlooked and arrange continuing support. The story of Saul's family has included pursuit, rivalry and death. Here it also includes land restored and bread shared in honour of Jonathan."], "lesson": "Faithful kindness remembers commitments when the people who first received them can no longer ask for their fulfilment. David turns remembrance into practical support and continuing fellowship.", "question": "How do the land, the workers and the place at the table each contribute to the kindness David shows?"},
  {"id": 92509, "title": "The Messengers Sent Home in Shame", "subtitle": "Suspicion turns a gesture of kindness into war", "text": "2 Samuel 10:1–19", "chapters": [[9, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["When Nahash king of Ammon died, his son Hanun succeeded him. David decided to show kindness because Nahash had shown kindness to him. He sent servants to offer comfort in bereavement. Hanun's officials interpreted the visit as a threat. They asked whether David's men had really come to honour his father or to inspect the city as spies before an attack. Hanun accepted their suspicion. He shaved off half the men's beards, cut away their garments and sent them back publicly humiliated. When David heard, he arranged for them to remain at Jericho until their beards grew before returning. Their shame received consideration and time for recovery, rather than another public appearance at court.", "Realising they had made themselves offensive to David, the Ammonites hired Aramean forces from several regions. David sent Joab and his strongest troops. The Ammonites formed up at the city gate while the hired forces stood apart in the open country. Joab found enemies both ahead and behind. He chose an elite company to face the Arameans and put the rest under Abishai against Ammon. The brothers agreed to help whichever side came under greater pressure. Their plan depended upon mutual support while facing two forces. Joab urged courage for the people and God's towns and entrusted the outcome to whatever the Lord judged good.", "Joab advanced, and the Arameans fled. Seeing their allies retreat, the Ammonites also withdrew into the city. Joab returned to Jerusalem, but the conflict did not end. The defeated Arameans regrouped. Hadadezer called in forces from beyond the Euphrates, and they gathered at Helam under Shobach. David then assembled Israel, crossed the Jordan and faced them himself. The Arameans fled before him. Their chariot forces and horsemen suffered heavy losses, and Shobach was struck down and died. What began as a response to an insulting act had drawn several rulers and armies into a wider campaign, with casualties far beyond Hanun's original reception of the envoys.", "The kings subject to Hadadezer made peace with Israel and became its servants. The Arameans were afraid to help Ammon further. The chapter's opening kindness has therefore ended in a changed balance of power. It preserves several different uses of counsel and authority along the way. Hanun acts upon an untested accusation, David provides for his humiliated servants, and Joab arranges cooperation between threatened forces. The reader sees both the first assumption and its spreading consequences. The war against Ammon is still unfinished, and the next chapter will return to that campaign. Its battlefield, however, will become connected with a far more serious misuse of power within David's own household."], "lesson": "Suspicion can turn a good intention into an escalating conflict. The treatment of David's messengers calls for care in judging motives and for compassion toward those who have been humiliated.", "question": "Which decision first changes the direction of the story, and how far do its consequences spread?"},
  {"id": 92510, "title": "The Letter Uriah Carried", "subtitle": "Power used to conceal wrongdoing", "text": "2 Samuel 11:1–27", "chapters": [[9, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["When the season for campaigning returned, David sent Joab and the army against Ammon while he remained in Jerusalem. One evening, walking on his roof, he saw a beautiful woman bathing. His enquiry identified her as Bathsheba, daughter of Eliam and wife of Uriah the Hittite. The information named an existing marriage. David nevertheless sent messengers to bring her, slept with her and sent her home. The account notes her purification, then records her message to the king that she was pregnant. The orders of the ruler had reached into a soldier's household while that soldier was away at war. David now began trying to conceal what he had done.", "He summoned Uriah from Joab and asked about the army and the campaign. Then he told him to go home, sending food after him. Uriah instead slept with the king's servants at the palace entrance. Asked why he had not visited his house, he spoke of the ark, Israel, Judah, Joab and the soldiers living in tents and open fields. He would not enjoy food, drink and marriage comforts while they remained on campaign. David kept him another day and made him drunk, but he still did not go home. Uriah's loyalty frustrated the attempt to make the pregnancy appear to belong to an ordinary visit from her husband. His conduct also exposed the contrast between the soldier's concern and the king's private purpose.", "In the morning David wrote a letter to Joab and sent it by Uriah's own hand. It ordered that Uriah be placed where fighting was fiercest and then abandoned so that he would be killed. Joab assigned him to a dangerous position near the city. Enemy soldiers came out, and several of David's men died, including Uriah. Joab prepared the messenger to answer any objection about approaching the wall too closely. If David recalled the earlier death of Abimelech beneath a wall, the messenger was to add that Uriah was dead. The death had been arranged within a real battle, and other soldiers had died with the intended victim. Royal authority and military obedience had become instruments of concealment.", "When the report arrived, David sent back reassurance that the sword took one man as well as another and urged Joab to press the attack. His words presented the losses as the ordinary uncertainty of war, although the reader has seen his written instructions. Bathsheba mourned Uriah. After the mourning period, David brought her into his house, married her and received the son she bore. The sequence could appear outwardly settled: a dead soldier, a widow's mourning and a royal marriage. The final sentence gives the judgment that the arrangements could not remove. What David had done displeased the Lord. The king who had resisted killing Saul had now used his position to take Uriah's wife and order Uriah's death."], "lesson": "Concealed wrongdoing can draw other people into suffering and further sin. The Lord's judgment exposes the gap between David's public arrangements and what he knows he has done.", "question": "How does Uriah's behaviour make David's attempts at concealment more revealing?"},
  {"id": 92511, "title": "Nathan's Story Reaches the King", "subtitle": "Confession, forgiveness and consequences", "text": "2 Samuel 12:1–31", "chapters": [[9, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The Lord sent Nathan to David with a story about two men. One had many flocks; the other possessed a single lamb, raised among his children and cherished like a daughter. When a traveller visited, the rich man took the poor man's lamb for the meal rather than using his own. David was furious. He pronounced the act worthy of death and demanded fourfold restitution because the man had shown no pity. Nathan then identified the king as the man. God's message recalled David's anointing, his rescue from Saul and the kingdom given to him. He had despised the Lord's word by killing Uriah through Ammonite weapons and taking his wife.", "Nathan announced that the sword would remain within David's household and that evil would arise from his own family. The violation he had carried out secretly would be answered by public humiliation involving his wives. David confessed that he had sinned against the Lord. Nathan declared that the Lord had put away his sin and that he would not die, but the child born from the union would die. Forgiveness was announced without erasing the consequences. After Nathan left, the child became gravely ill. David pleaded with God, fasted and lay upon the ground. The elders tried to raise and feed him, but he refused. On the seventh day the child died, and the servants feared how he would respond.", "David noticed their whispering and asked directly whether the child was dead. Receiving the answer, he rose, washed, changed clothes and went to worship before returning to eat. His servants were puzzled by the change. He explained that while the child lived he hoped God might show mercy; now he could not bring him back. He would go to the child, but the child would not return. David comforted Bathsheba, and in time she bore Solomon. The Lord loved him, and through Nathan he also received the name Jedidiah. The account includes both the irretrievable loss of the first child and an expression of God's love toward the son born later. Neither event makes the other disappear.", "Meanwhile Joab captured an important part of Rabbah and called David to complete the conquest, lest the victory be associated with Joab's own name. David gathered the remaining force, took the city and received its heavy golden crown and abundant spoil. The passage also records severe treatment of the defeated people involving saws, iron implements and brick kilns, and says that other Ammonite towns were treated likewise. David and the army returned to Jerusalem. The campaign that formed the setting for Uriah's death is thus completed after Nathan's confrontation. Public victory continues, but the reader now knows the word spoken over David's household. His confession has been heard, forgiveness declared, and a future marked by painful consequences has begun."], "lesson": "Truthful repentance names sin without excuses. Nathan's message holds God's forgiveness together with consequences that David cannot control or reverse.", "question": "How does the chapter distinguish forgiveness from the removal of every consequence?"},
  {"id": 92512, "title": "Tamar's Cry Behind the Bolted Door", "subtitle": "Hearing the person who was harmed", "text": "2 Samuel 13:1–22", "chapters": [[9, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Amnon, David's son, became obsessed with his half sister Tamar, Absalom's sister. His distress was visible, and his cousin Jonadab asked its cause. When Amnon explained, Jonadab devised a plan. He should pretend to be ill and ask David to send Tamar to prepare food for him. Amnon followed the advice. David visited and received the request, then sent Tamar to her brother's house. She prepared the cakes in his sight, carrying out the task she had been given. Amnon refused to eat and ordered the attendants out. Then he asked her to bring the food into his bedroom. The care offered within a family had been manipulated to isolate her.", "When Tamar came near, Amnon seized her and demanded that she lie with him. She told him clearly not to force her. Such an outrage ought not to be done in Israel. She spoke of the shame and harm it would cause and the disgrace of his own action, urging him to speak to the king instead. He would not listen. Using his greater strength, he raped her. The narrative makes her refusal and his use of force explicit. The wrongdoing belongs to the man who ignored her voice and overpowered her. The account has shown how Jonadab's scheme, a false illness and the clearing of the room created the circumstances in which he attacked her.", "Amnon's desire then turned into intense hatred. He ordered Tamar out. She protested that dismissing her compounded the wrong, but he again refused to listen. He called a servant and commanded that she be put outside and the door bolted behind her. Tamar tore the special robe worn by unmarried royal daughters, put ashes on her head and went away crying with her hand upon her head. The story follows her distress beyond the room instead of ending with Amnon's order. The barred door belongs to his rejection, while the torn robe and public cry make visible the harm she carries. The name of love with which the chapter began cannot conceal the violence it has narrated.", "Absalom asked whether Amnon had been with her and told her to keep quiet, referring to him as her brother. Tamar remained desolate in Absalom's house. David heard and was very angry, but this passage records no act by him that brought justice or restored her security. Absalom said neither good nor bad to Amnon, for he hated him because of what he had done to Tamar. The household's silence therefore contains unresolved suffering and growing hostility. Tamar's own words have named the outrage most clearly. The closing notices about the men around her leave the reader facing a family in which royal power, anger and secrecy have failed to answer her cry."], "lesson": "Tamar's refusal and grief must be heard, and responsibility belongs to the one who used force. Anger or silence alone cannot provide the protection and justice a harmed person needs.", "question": "How does the narrator keep Tamar's words and suffering visible when the men around her fail to respond adequately?"},
  {"id": 92513, "title": "The Feast Absalom Prepared for Amnon", "subtitle": "Revenge kept alive in silence", "text": "2 Samuel 13:23–39", "chapters": [[9, 13]], "start": 23, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Two full years passed after Tamar's assault. Absalom arranged sheep shearing at Baal-hazor and invited the king's sons to a feast. He also asked David and the royal attendants to come. David declined, concerned that the whole party would be a burden, though he gave his blessing. Absalom then specifically requested that Amnon attend. David questioned why, but Absalom pressed until permission was given for Amnon and the other sons to go. A family celebration had become the setting for a plan concealed by the host. The silence between Absalom and Amnon recorded earlier had not meant that the matter was settled. The outrage against Tamar had remained the reason for a killing he now intended.", "Absalom instructed his servants to watch until Amnon was cheerful with wine. At his command they were to strike and kill him without fear, since he himself had ordered it. They obeyed. When Amnon was killed, the other princes mounted their mules and fled. News raced ahead of them in a distorted form, telling David that Absalom had killed every royal son and left none alive. David tore his clothes and lay upon the ground, while his servants stood with their garments torn. The death of one son had already created the terror of losing them all. Within the royal household, violent action was followed by confusion before reliable information arrived.", "Jonadab, who had first helped Amnon arrange Tamar's visit, corrected the report. Only Amnon was dead, he said, because Absalom had determined this from the day of the assault. The narrator places his explanation beside the earlier role he had played, without giving him credit for preventing either wrong. A watchman saw people coming along the hillside, and Jonadab pointed to the arriving princes as confirmation. They entered weeping, and David and his servants wept with them. The household had survived the complete destruction first reported, but the actual loss still filled it with grief. Tamar's injury had been followed by Amnon's murder, with no healing of the earlier wrong described.", "Absalom escaped to Talmai king of Geshur, his mother's family connection, and remained there for three years. David mourned his son and, as time passed and his grief concerning Amnon eased, longed for Absalom. The chapter ends with distance rather than reconciliation. One son is dead, another lives in exile, and Tamar remains within the story as the person whose violation set the hostility in motion. The years of waiting have not made revenge just or repaired what Amnon did. A public feast has served a private intention, and the surviving family now carries another lasting rupture. Bringing Absalom home will become a concern of the next chapter, but the moral and personal wounds cannot be settled merely by a change of residence."], "lesson": "Unanswered wrongdoing can become the ground for further violence. The story calls for justice and care that confront harm before silence hardens into plans for revenge.", "question": "What does the passage show about the limits of time, silence and retaliation as responses to Tamar's suffering?"},
  {"id": 92514, "title": "Absalom Comes Home but Cannot See the King", "subtitle": "A return that leaves the breach unresolved", "text": "2 Samuel 14:1–33", "chapters": [[9, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Joab recognised David's longing for Absalom and arranged an appeal through a wise woman from Tekoa. He instructed her to dress as a long grieving widow and supplied a story. Before David she described two sons quarrelling in a field, one killing the other, and relatives demanding the survivor's death. Killing him would extinguish her family's remaining hope. David promised protection, and she pressed until he swore that her son would be safe. She then turned the appeal toward the king's own situation. Why had he left his banished son away? Speaking of death as water spilled upon the ground, she urged him to consider a way for the exile to return.", "David detected Joab's involvement and asked directly. The woman admitted that Joab had arranged the conversation. David then told Joab to bring Absalom back. Joab thanked him, travelled to Geshur and escorted the young man to Jerusalem. The permission had a restriction: Absalom must live in his own house and could not see David. A geographical separation had ended, but father and son remained apart within the same city. The woman had used a constructed case to draw a promise from the king, yet the resulting arrangement did not include a narrated reckoning with Amnon's murder or Tamar's assault. The family problem had been moved closer without being clearly resolved.", "The account pauses over Absalom's admired appearance. He was praised throughout Israel for his beauty, and his exceptionally heavy hair was cut and weighed each year. It names three sons and a beautiful daughter called Tamar. Those notices place his public attractiveness and his own family within the period of waiting. For two years in Jerusalem he did not see the king. He sent for Joab to obtain an audience, but Joab would not come. After a second refusal, Absalom ordered servants to burn Joab's nearby barley field. The destruction forced the commander to respond. Absalom's impatience had again found expression through a plan that imposed loss upon someone else.", "When Joab demanded an explanation, Absalom said that living in Geshur had been as good as this half return. He wanted to see the king and said that if he was guilty he should be put to death. Joab reported the demand to David. Absalom was summoned, bowed with his face to the ground, and received the king's kiss. The chapter ends with that outward welcome. Its route has included an invented lawsuit, two years of exclusion and a burned field. The reader has seen the effort required to bring father and son into the same room, but has heard no confession from Absalom or account of justice for the earlier victims. The next chapter will show what the restored prince does with his renewed access and standing."], "lesson": "Reconciliation calls for more than proximity or an outward gesture. The events invite careful attention to the unresolved wrongs and habits that can remain beneath a public welcome.", "question": "Which details suggest that bringing Absalom back to Jerusalem has left serious matters within the family unaddressed?"},
  {"id": 92515, "title": "David Leaves Jerusalem Barefoot", "subtitle": "Loyalty and prayer in the hour of flight", "text": "2 Samuel 15:1–37", "chapters": [[9, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Absalom acquired a chariot, horses and fifty runners, presenting himself with royal display. He rose early to meet people bringing disputes to the gate. He affirmed their cases, complained that no representative of the king would hear them and wished aloud that he could judge their claims. When anyone bowed, he took the person by the hand and kissed him. Through this calculated attention he stole the people's hearts. Later he asked David's permission to fulfil a vow at Hebron, presenting the journey as worship. David sent him in peace. Absalom had meanwhile arranged signals throughout the tribes for announcing his kingship. Two hundred invited guests travelled innocently, while Ahithophel, David's counsellor, joined the conspiracy. Support increased around Absalom.", "A messenger told David that Israel's hearts had turned to his son. The king ordered an immediate departure to avoid being trapped and to spare Jerusalem an attack. His household and loyal servants went with him, while ten concubines remained to keep the palace. Among those leaving were the Cherethites, Pelethites and six hundred Gittites. David urged Ittai, a recent foreign arrival, to remain rather than share an uncertain exile. Ittai swore that he would stay with the king in life or death. He crossed with his men and their children. The countryside wept as the procession moved over the Kidron toward the wilderness. Loyalty was taking visible form at the moment when association with David carried risk.", "Zadok, Abiathar and the Levites also brought the ark, but David told them to take it back. If he found favour with the Lord, God could restore him to see it and its dwelling. If God judged otherwise, he submitted himself to that decision. He arranged for the priests and their sons Ahimaaz and Jonathan to keep him informed while he waited near the wilderness crossings. David climbed the Mount of Olives barefoot, head covered and weeping, with the people following in the same grief. On learning that Ahithophel had joined Absalom, he prayed that God would turn the counsellor's advice into foolishness. The king who once brought the ark into Jerusalem now refused to take it as a possession guaranteeing his return.", "At the summit, where he worshipped, Hushai the Arkite met him with torn clothes and earth upon his head. David sent his friend back to serve as a counter to Ahithophel's counsel. Hushai was to convey information through the priests and their sons. He entered Jerusalem as Absalom arrived. The chapter closes with David on the road and his son's faction taking the capital, while a network of loyal people remains within it. Prayer and practical arrangements appear together. David has not denied the danger or claimed control over the outcome. He has accepted Ittai's costly commitment, returned the ark to its place and asked for help while placing his own future before the Lord."], "lesson": "Crisis reveals loyalties and the basis of our trust. David's prayer and his willingness to return the ark invite dependence upon God alongside thoughtful action.", "question": "What does David's instruction concerning the ark reveal about the way he understands his possible return?"},
  {"id": 92516, "title": "Gifts on the Road and Betrayal on the Roof", "subtitle": "Judgment under pressure", "text": "2 Samuel 16:1–23", "chapters": [[9, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Beyond the summit, Ziba met David with saddled donkeys, bread, fruit and wine. He explained that the animals were for the royal household and the food for exhausted travellers. David asked where Mephibosheth was. Ziba claimed that Jonathan's son had remained in Jerusalem hoping that Saul's kingdom would be restored to him. David immediately transferred Mephibosheth's property to Ziba. The decision followed one servant's accusation at a moment of urgency. The reader has heard the charge but not the absent man's answer. A gift useful in a crisis has arrived together with a report that benefits its bearer, and the consequences will return when David comes back.", "At Bahurim, Shimei of Saul's family came out cursing and throwing stones and dust. He accused David of bloodshed against Saul's house and described Absalom's rebellion as judgment. Abishai asked permission to kill him. David refused. His own son was seeking his life, he said, so hostility from a Benjamite was hardly surprising. He was willing to endure the cursing under the Lord's hand and hoped God might see his distress and answer with good. David did not settle every accusation Shimei made. He restrained an immediate execution and continued walking while the abuse followed him along the hillside. The king and his company eventually stopped to refresh themselves, exhausted by the road.", "Meanwhile Absalom entered Jerusalem with Ahithophel. Hushai greeted him as king. Absalom questioned why a friend of David had abandoned him. Hushai answered with language of service to the one chosen by the Lord and the people, adding that he could serve the son as he had served the father. The reader knows David has sent him to frustrate Ahithophel's advice, while Absalom hears a profession of allegiance. Hushai's position depends upon being accepted within a court now occupied by the king's son. The conflicting loyalties outside the city have their counterpart within the palace, where words of loyalty can conceal another purpose.", "Absalom asked Ahithophel what he should do. The counsellor advised him to take David's concubines publicly so that everyone would know the breach with his father was complete and his followers would be strengthened. A tent was erected on the roof, and Absalom had sexual relations with the women David had left to keep the house. The women became the subjects of a public assertion of power within the revolt. The event also recalls Nathan's warning that David's secret sin would be answered openly within his own household. Ahithophel's counsel carried enormous prestige with both David and Absalom. The chapter ends showing how that admired intelligence could be used to deepen a family violation and make a political rupture harder to reverse."], "lesson": "Pressure can lead to hasty judgment, while gifted counsel can serve destructive ends. The chapter calls for attention to evidence, restraint and the people harmed by struggles for power.", "question": "What dangers appear when David acts upon Ziba's report and when Absalom follows Ahithophel's advice?"},
  {"id": 92517, "title": "The Message Hidden Beneath the Grain", "subtitle": "Counsel, courage and timely provision", "text": "2 Samuel 17:1–29", "chapters": [[9, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Ahithophel proposed an immediate pursuit with twelve thousand men. David was tired and vulnerable, he argued, and a sudden attack could scatter his company. Killing the king alone would end the struggle and bring the people back to Absalom. The proposal pleased the new court, but Absalom also called Hushai. David's friend challenged the timing. David and his men were experienced and enraged, like a bear robbed of her cubs, and the king would not sleep openly with the people. An early setback could frighten Absalom's followers. Hushai urged him to gather a vast army from all Israel and lead it personally, overwhelming David wherever he was found.", "Absalom and the elders preferred Hushai's advice. The narrator explains that the Lord had determined to frustrate Ahithophel's effective counsel and bring disaster upon Absalom. Hushai told Zadok and Abiathar what both men had advised and urged an immediate warning to David. He must cross the river that night. Jonathan and Ahimaaz waited at En-rogel, receiving messages through a servant woman because they could not openly enter the city. A young man saw them and reported them to Absalom. They hurried to a house at Bahurim and hid in a courtyard well. Its mistress covered the opening and spread grain over it, then directed the pursuers elsewhere. The hidden messengers escaped discovery.", "After the searchers left, the two climbed out and reached David. They told him to cross quickly, and by dawn his entire company was beyond the Jordan. The night of delay had given them time to reach safety. Ahithophel, seeing that his advice had been rejected, returned to his own town, put his affairs in order and took his life. He was buried in his father's tomb. The account records this death within the collapse of his counsel's influence, while Absalom continued preparing his army. A plan that had seemed capable of ending David's resistance swiftly had been set aside, and the consequences reached both the fugitive king and the counsellor who had turned against him.", "David went to Mahanaim. Absalom crossed the Jordan with Israel and appointed Amasa, a relative within the wider family of David's commanders, to lead in Joab's place. The opposing forces now stood in Gilead. At Mahanaim, Shobi, Machir and Barzillai brought beds, vessels, grain, flour, beans, lentils, honey, dairy provisions and sheep for David's company. They recognised that the travellers were hungry, weary and thirsty. The chapter moves from arguments about destroying an exhausted force to people providing for its actual needs. David's prayer concerning counsel has been followed by delay, messages, shelter and a safe crossing. On the far side of the river, help also arrives through food and rest, preparing the company for what remains ahead."], "lesson": "Faithful help can take the form of wise counsel, a dangerous message, shelter or ordinary provisions. This chapter asks us to recognise and answer real needs in a time of crisis.", "question": "How do the different people who help David contribute to a deliverance none of them could accomplish alone?"},
  {"id": 92518, "title": "The King Asks About His Son", "subtitle": "Victory shadowed by grief", "text": "2 Samuel 18:1–33", "chapters": [[9, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["David counted his force and divided it under Joab, Abishai and Ittai. He intended to go with them, but the men urged him to remain in the city because his life was the enemy's chief objective. He agreed and watched the companies leave. In everyone's hearing he instructed the commanders to deal gently with Absalom for his sake. The battle spread through the forest of Ephraim, where David's troops defeated Israel and twenty thousand died. The narrator describes the forest as claiming more lives than the sword. Absalom, riding his mule beneath an oak, caught his head in the branches and was left suspended when the animal went on.", "A soldier reported him to Joab. Asked why he had not killed him, the man recalled the king's public order and said that no reward would persuade him to strike the royal son. Joab would not delay. He drove three weapons into Absalom while he hung alive, and ten armour bearers surrounded and killed him. Then Joab sounded the trumpet and ended the pursuit. Absalom was cast into a pit and covered with a great heap of stones. The narrator recalls the monument he had erected for his own name, explaining his statement that he lacked a son to preserve his memory. That earlier monument stands beside the burial given him after the rebellion. The chapter does not explain every detail of his household's later circumstances.", "Ahimaaz wanted to carry the victory news, but Joab warned that this was a painful message because the king's son was dead. He sent a Cushite runner instead. Ahimaaz persisted and was allowed to run, overtaking the other messenger by the plain. David waited near the gate while a watchman announced the approaching men. Recognising Ahimaaz's running style, David expected good news. Ahimaaz bowed, praised the Lord for the defeat of the rebels and announced success. David immediately asked whether Absalom was safe. Ahimaaz spoke of confusion and did not give the answer. He was told to stand aside while the other runner approached.", "The Cushite also announced deliverance from the king's enemies. Asked the same question, he expressed the hope that all David's enemies would share the young man's fate. David understood. He went above the gate and wept, repeatedly calling Absalom his son and wishing he could have died in his place. The rebellion had been defeated, but the father's grief overwhelmed the king's reception of the victory. The reader knows both his instruction and Joab's deliberate action, as well as the suffering of the army and nation. The chapter closes with a cry that cannot restore the dead or settle the responsibilities of the living. Those who saved David's life will soon need him to acknowledge their costly loyalty."], "lesson": "Public deliverance and personal grief can arrive together. The chapter calls for attention to the lives lost, the responsibilities of authority and the people whose loyalty carries a heavy cost.", "question": "Why does David's repeated question make the messengers' news so difficult to deliver?"},
  {"id": 92519, "title": "David Returns to the Gate and the River", "subtitle": "Restoration without immediate revenge", "text": "2 Samuel 19:1–23", "chapters": [[9, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Joab heard that David was mourning Absalom. The day's victory became a time of shame among the troops, who slipped into the city as though returning from defeat. David covered his face and continued crying for his son. Joab confronted him sharply. The men had saved his life and the lives of his household, yet his response made them feel that their survival meant little beside Absalom's death. He warned that unless David appeared and encouraged them, their loyalty would collapse. David arose and sat at the gate. When the people heard, they came before him. His grief remained, but he resumed the visible responsibility his position required.", "Across Israel people argued about bringing the king back. They remembered his rescue of the nation and recognised that Absalom, whom they had anointed, was dead. David sent to Zadok and Abiathar, asking them to appeal to Judah's elders. Why should his own relatives be last to restore him? He also offered Amasa the command in Joab's place, appealing to their kinship. Judah's support came together, and they invited David and his servants to return. The movement toward restoration therefore included both the nation's discussion and the king's efforts to reconcile a significant group and a former rebel commander. Ending the battle did not by itself settle how the kingdom would be brought together again.", "David reached the Jordan, and Judah came to Gilgal to escort him across. Shimei, who had cursed and stoned him during the flight, hurried to meet him with a thousand Benjamites. Ziba also came with his sons and servants, helping the royal household at the crossing. Shimei fell before David and admitted his sin. He asked the king not to hold his earlier perversity against him, pointing to his prompt arrival as evidence of his present submission. The change in circumstances had brought the man who shouted from a hillside to the feet of the returning king. His plea placed the question of punishment directly before David while the journey home was under way.", "Abishai again asked whether Shimei should die for cursing the Lord's anointed. David refused to make the day an occasion for executions. He knew himself to be king over Israel again and would not let the sons of Zeruiah turn the return into immediate revenge. He swore that Shimei would not die. The passage ends at that assurance. It has moved from a grief that endangered the army's loyalty to a restoration requiring acknowledgment, negotiation and restraint. David has accepted a rebuke, resumed his place and offered mercy to a man who publicly insulted him. Other damaged relationships and regional resentments still await him on the road to Jerusalem."], "lesson": "Grief does not remove every responsibility toward those who depend upon us. David's return also asks whether restored power becomes an opportunity for restraint or retaliation.", "question": "How do David's appearance at the gate and his answer concerning Shimei each serve the fragile restoration of the kingdom?"},
  {"id": 92520, "title": "The People Who Meet the Returning King", "subtitle": "Loyalty heard and gratitude remembered", "text": "2 Samuel 19:24–43", "chapters": [[9, 19]], "start": 24, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["Mephibosheth came to meet David showing signs of prolonged mourning. Since the king's departure he had neglected his feet, beard and clothes. David asked why he had not gone with him. Mephibosheth said his servant had deceived him. Because of his disability he had intended to ride a donkey, but the servant had left him and then slandered him before the king. He appealed to David's judgment while recalling the kindness that had already given him a place at the table. David ended the dispute by declaring that he and Ziba should divide the land. Mephibosheth replied that Ziba could have it all, since the king had returned safely. His answer gives a very different account of his hopes from the accusation David had heard during the flight.", "Barzillai also came down to escort the king over the Jordan. He was eighty years old and had supplied David at Mahanaim from his substantial resources. David invited him to Jerusalem and offered continuing provision there. Barzillai declined with a frank account of age. He no longer had the same enjoyment of food, music and courtly life, and he did not want to burden the king. He preferred to return to his own town and be buried near his parents. He asked instead that Chimham accompany David and receive whatever kindness the king judged good. His assistance in the wilderness had not been offered as a means of obtaining a place at court.", "David agreed, promising care for Chimham and whatever Barzillai might further request. At their parting he kissed and blessed the older man, who returned home. Chimham continued with him toward Gilgal. Gratitude had been expressed in a way that respected the helper's own wishes. The return brought people with different needs before the king: a disabled member of Saul's house answering an accusation, an aged benefactor seeking a quiet homecoming and another person entrusted to royal kindness. The narrative lets these encounters interrupt the public movement of armies and delegations. The restoration of a throne has consequences in particular lives and promises, not only in national ceremonies.", "The public procession nevertheless reopened conflict. The men of Israel complained that Judah had taken the king across without including them properly. Judah answered that he was their close relative and denied receiving special payment or benefits. Israel argued that its ten parts in the kingdom gave it a greater claim and that it had first proposed bringing David back. Judah's response became harsher still. The chapter ends with competing claims to the same king rather than a settled celebration. Personal reconciliations and generous farewells have taken place, but regional jealousy remains active. The quarrel at the crossing will provide the setting for a fresh challenge to David's rule in the next chapter."], "lesson": "Faithful leadership listens to people affected by earlier decisions and remembers help received. The regional quarrel also warns how quickly rivalry can damage a shared restoration.", "question": "What do Mephibosheth and Barzillai reveal about loyalties that are not measured simply by property or position?"},
  {"id": 92521, "title": "The Woman Who Spoke from Abel's Wall", "subtitle": "A city threatened by another man's revolt", "text": "2 Samuel 20:1–26", "chapters": [[9, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["During the quarrel a Benjamite named Sheba sounded a trumpet and declared that Israel had no share in David. He called people back to their tents. Israel turned after him, while Judah remained with the king to Jerusalem. At home David placed the ten concubines left in the palace under guard and provided for them, but did not resume marital relations with them. They lived in seclusion as widows for the rest of their lives. The record preserves a lasting consequence for the women caught in the revolt. David then told Amasa to gather Judah's forces within three days. When he was delayed, David feared Sheba would secure fortified towns and become an even greater threat than Absalom.", "Abishai went out with the royal troops and Joab's men. Near the great stone at Gibeon they met Amasa. Joab approached with a greeting and took him by the beard as if to kiss him. Amasa did not guard against the sword in Joab's hand. Joab struck him fatally and continued the pursuit with Abishai. Men stopped at the body lying in the road until an attendant moved it aside and covered it, urging everyone loyal to David to follow Joab. Amasa's appointment in place of the commander has ended in another killing concealed beneath a friendly approach. The expedition proceeds, but the bloodshed within David's own leadership remains part of its story.", "Sheba reached Abel Beth-maacah. Joab's force raised a siege ramp and battered the wall. A wise woman called from within and asked to speak with him. She recalled the town's reputation as a place where disputes were settled and described herself among Israel's peaceful and faithful people. Why was he destroying a city belonging to the Lord's inheritance? Joab denied wanting its destruction and explained that he sought Sheba, who had rebelled against David. If the town surrendered that man, he would withdraw. The woman undertook to arrange it. She spoke with the people, and Sheba's head was thrown over the wall. Joab sounded the trumpet, and the siege ended.", "The soldiers dispersed, and Joab returned to Jerusalem. The final list of officials again places him over the army, with Benaiah over the royal guards, Adoram over forced labour or tribute, Jehoshaphat as recorder and Sheva as secretary. Zadok and Abiathar remained priests, and Ira held a leading position near David. The government continues, but the chapter has shown how its military commander recovered control through Amasa's death. It has also shown a woman using speech and negotiation to prevent a whole city's destruction. Her appeal forced the besieging general to name the actual object of his campaign. That clarification gave the townspeople a way to end the immediate danger."], "lesson": "Wise intervention can challenge the spread of violence and protect a community. The chapter also asks how power is gained and retained within institutions that claim to serve a rightful cause.", "question": "How does the woman's question change the course of the siege, and why does Amasa's death still matter after it ends?"},
  {"id": 92522, "title": "Rizpah Keeps Watch over the Dead", "subtitle": "Remembering covenants and human grief", "text": "2 Samuel 21:1–22", "chapters": [[9, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["A famine continued for three years, and David sought the Lord. The answer connected it with Saul's bloodshed against the Gibeonites, a people to whom Israel had sworn protection. Saul had sought their destruction in zeal for Israel and Judah. David summoned them and asked how the wrong could be addressed so that they might bless the Lord's inheritance. They refused silver and gold and asked for seven male descendants of Saul to be handed over for execution. David agreed. He spared Jonathan's son Mephibosheth because of his covenant with Jonathan. Two sons of Rizpah, Armoni and another Mephibosheth, and five grandsons connected with Adriel's household were delivered to the Gibeonites and put to death at the beginning of barley harvest.", "Rizpah spread sackcloth upon a rock and kept watch beside the bodies. From the start of harvest until rain fell, she prevented birds from settling on them by day and animals by night. Her action gives sustained attention to the grief within an account of royal decisions and national calamity. When David heard what she had done, he arranged to recover the bones of Saul and Jonathan from Jabesh-gilead, whose people had rescued them after Gilboa. The remains of the executed men were also gathered. Saul and Jonathan were buried in the tomb of Kish at Zela in Benjamin, and the king's orders were carried out. Afterward God answered prayer for the land.", "The chapter turns to further wars with the Philistines. David went into battle and grew exhausted. Ishbi-benob, a formidable warrior descended from the giants, intended to kill him, but Abishai came to his aid and struck the attacker down. David's men then bound him by an oath not to accompany them into battle again, lest Israel's lamp be extinguished. The king whose youthful courage had brought down Goliath now needed another man's protection. The passage records that dependence plainly. Preserving the leader's life required accepting that his strength and responsibilities had changed. The kingdom's defence continued through those who served beside him.", "Other encounters followed at Gob and Gath. Sibbecai killed Saph, Elhanan defeated a Gittite warrior associated with Goliath's family, and Jonathan son of David's brother Shimea killed a man of great stature who had defied Israel. The four formidable opponents fell through David and his servants. The closing accounts broaden the picture of courage beyond the king himself. Alongside them, Rizpah's vigil remains a different kind of endurance within the same chapter. The opening famine had exposed a violated national promise and led to a deeply painful reckoning. The narrated burial and renewed prayer for the land come after a woman has insisted through her watchfulness that the dead remain worthy of care."], "lesson": "Promises made by a community carry lasting responsibility, and public decisions must not hide personal suffering. Rizpah's vigil calls us to notice those whose grief can disappear from accounts of national events.", "question": "What changes when Rizpah's watchfulness is reported to David, and why is her action central to the story?"},
  {"id": 92523, "title": "David Sings of the God Who Rescued Him", "subtitle": "Remembering deliverance with praise", "text": "2 Samuel 22:1–51", "chapters": [[9, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The book places here a song David addressed to the Lord after deliverance from his enemies and from Saul. Its opening gathers images of safety: rock, fortress, shield, refuge and stronghold. David calls upon the Lord as worthy of praise and remembers dangers like waves and cords of death closing around him. He cried out in distress, and God heard. The song then portrays the answer through powerful poetic images. Earth and heaven shake, smoke and fire express divine anger, and God comes with darkness, cloud, wind and thunder. These are the language of praise describing His mighty intervention, rather than a new battle scene to be added to the historical narrative.", "The song pictures the Lord reaching from above and drawing David out of deep waters. His enemies were too strong, but God supported him and brought him into a broad place. David speaks of righteousness, clean hands and keeping God's ways, affirming that the Lord deals faithfully with the faithful and humbles the proud. Read within the whole book, the words must stand alongside the exposed sins and confessions that the narrator has also preserved. The song testifies to the justice of God's dealings and the deliverance of His servant. It gives no permission to erase Uriah, Bathsheba, Tamar or the painful consequences already narrated. Praise belongs within the truth of the story.", "David names the Lord as his lamp and the one who lights his darkness. God's way is perfect, His word proves true, and He shields those who take refuge in Him. The song asks who else is God or a rock. It moves into images of strength for action: sure feet on high ground, trained hands, a protective shield and a path wide enough to keep from slipping. David credits God's help with his ability to pursue and defeat enemies and to survive struggles among his own people. Foreign nations submit, and hostile strength fails. The military language recalls the setting of a warrior king whose survival and victories have been narrated across both books.", "The closing movement blesses the living Lord and exalts the rock of David's salvation. God brings peoples under him, lifts him above those who rise against him and delivers him from violence. David therefore promises thanks among the nations and songs to God's name. The final line speaks of enduring kindness to God's anointed, to David and his descendants. The song thus moves from personal danger through divine rescue to praise connected with the promised royal house. Placed near the end of a troubled reign, it gives the reader David's confession of dependence. His story includes courage, failure, judgment and mercy, and its hope remains bound to the Lord who hears, rescues and keeps His word."], "lesson": "Remembered deliverance should lead to praise grounded in truth. The song invites gratitude for God's help while the wider narrative keeps the worshipper humble and accountable.", "question": "Which images in the song best express David's dependence, and how does the wider account of his life guide your reading of them?"},
  {"id": 92524, "title": "The Morning Light of Just Rule", "subtitle": "Hope rests in God's enduring covenant", "text": "2 Samuel 23:1–7", "chapters": [[9, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The passage introduces David's final words with several descriptions of the speaker. He is Jesse's son, the man raised to a high place, the anointed of Jacob's God and Israel's beloved singer. Those titles hold his family origin, public responsibility and worship together. He speaks of the Spirit of the Lord speaking through him and of God's word upon his tongue. The declaration claims its authority from the God of Israel, called the Rock of Israel. At the centre stands an instruction concerning rule over human beings. The ruler must be just and govern in the fear of God. The closing account of David's reign therefore includes a standard before it turns to the names and exploits of his warriors.", "Just rule is compared with morning sunlight, a clear dawn and the fresh growth that follows rain. The picture makes the effects of authority visible through the life it encourages. People under a ruler need justice as growing things need light and water. The image also asks the reader to consider the record of the kingdom already told. David has administered justice, but he has also used power to take, conceal and destroy. The requirement that the ruler fear God applies to the anointed speaker himself. His office does not place him above the standard he announces. The beauty of the morning image deepens the seriousness of the responsibility.", "David then turns toward his own house and the everlasting covenant God has made with him. In the wording of the King James passage, the condition of his house does not provide a simple match for the ideal just described. Yet the covenant is ordered and secure, and David speaks of it as the ground of his salvation and desire. His hope rests upon the commitment of God. That hope belongs beside the promised discipline and steadfast love recorded in chapter seven. The final words do not offer a new account of how every family failure will be repaired. They locate the future of his house within a promise whose faithfulness exceeds his own record.", "The last image concerns the wicked, likened to thorns thrown aside. They cannot be grasped carelessly by an unprotected hand; iron and a spear shaft are required, and they are destined for burning. The picture of harmful growth answers the earlier image of life flourishing under just rule. The passage therefore holds accountable leadership, enduring promise and judgment within a short poetic declaration. It does not yet narrate David's death, which belongs to the following biblical book. Here it supplies words by which his reign can be considered as its closing material unfolds. The king's achievements and his household's future must both be read before the God whose word he has invoked."], "lesson": "Authority calls for justice and reverence toward God. Hope in His covenant should deepen accountability and humility in those entrusted with power.", "question": "How do the images of morning growth and discarded thorns illuminate the responsibility of a ruler?"},
  {"id": 92525, "title": "The Water David Would Not Drink", "subtitle": "Honouring costly service", "text": "2 Samuel 23:8–39", "chapters": [[9, 23]], "start": 8, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The account names David's mighty warriors and records deeds that helped sustain his kingdom. The leading warrior is remembered for an extraordinary victory against a great number. Eleazar stood against the Philistines when others withdrew, fighting until his hand was exhausted and clung to his sword. The Lord gave a great victory, and the people returned to gather spoil. Shammah defended a field of lentils after others fled, and again the narrator credits the Lord with deliverance. The stories give names and particular places to courage that could disappear behind a general statement about the king's success. David's strength depended in part upon men whose endurance held when others could no longer stand.", "Another memory returns to the days near Adullam, with Philistines in the valley of Rephaim and a garrison in Bethlehem. David longed for water from the well by Bethlehem's gate. Three warriors broke through the enemy lines, drew water and brought it to him. He refused to drink it and poured it out before the Lord. To take it as ordinary refreshment would ignore the lives endangered to obtain it. He described it as the blood of the men who had risked themselves. The action treats their costly service as something that cannot simply be consumed for a leader's private satisfaction. His longing had been answered by courage whose price demanded a different response.", "The record continues with Abishai's standing and his defeat of three hundred men. Benaiah's exploits include striking two formidable Moabite warriors, killing a lion in a pit on a snowy day and overcoming an armed Egyptian with a staff before taking the man's spear. He was honoured and appointed over David's guard. The account preserves distinctions in rank among the leading warriors and the larger company. These distinctions belong to its remembrance of service. Yet each named deed points beyond a single heroic king to people whose strength, risk and skill supported his rule. Their stories form part of Israel's memory of the kingdom.", "The longer list names men from different towns, families and peoples, including Asahel, Eliam son of Ahithophel, Zelek the Ammonite and Joab's armour bearer Naharai. It closes with Uriah the Hittite and a total of thirty seven. The final name matters deeply within the book. Uriah's loyalty has already been narrated, together with David's betrayal and arrangement of his death. His inclusion among the honoured warriors keeps his service visible in the record of the reign. The story of the water shows David recognising lives risked for him, while Uriah's name recalls the life he later treated as expendable. Both belong to the truth about the king and those who served him."], "lesson": "Leadership must honour the lives and service of others. The water offered to God and Uriah's final place in the list call for gratitude joined to an honest reckoning with harm.", "question": "How does the final mention of Uriah affect your reading of the courage and loyalty celebrated in this passage?"},
  {"id": 92526, "title": "The Altar David Would Pay For", "subtitle": "Confession and costly worship at the book's close", "text": "2 Samuel 24:1–25", "chapters": [[9, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Samuel", "story": ["The final chapter opens with the Lord's anger against Israel and with David being moved to order a census. He commanded Joab to count the people from Dan to Beersheba so he could know their number. Joab questioned why he delighted in this and wished the Lord would multiply them greatly, but the king's command prevailed. The officers crossed the Jordan, travelled through the northern regions and returned through the south. After nine months and twenty days they reported eight hundred thousand fighting men in Israel and five hundred thousand in Judah. The wide circuit and substantial totals brought the requested information, yet David's conscience then struck him. He confessed great sin and foolishness and asked God to remove his guilt.", "The next morning Gad brought a word from the Lord offering three judgments: years of famine, months of flight before enemies or three days of plague. David spoke of his great distress and chose to fall into the Lord's hands because His mercies were great, rather than into human hands. A plague came upon Israel, and seventy thousand died across the land. As the destroying angel stretched toward Jerusalem, the Lord commanded that the destruction stop. The angel stood beside the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite. The census had concerned the strength David could count, but the loss now fell upon the people whose number had been gathered. His authority and his responsibility remain linked in the account.", "Seeing the angel, David acknowledged his wrongdoing and asked what these sheep had done. He appealed for the hand of judgment to fall upon him and his father's house. Gad told him to build an altar at Araunah's threshing floor. David went in obedience. Araunah bowed and asked why the king had come. Learning the purpose, he offered the site, oxen and wooden equipment for the sacrifice, praying that the Lord would accept David. The king refused to offer what cost him nothing. He would buy it at a price. David paid fifty shekels of silver for the threshing floor and oxen. His confession moved into a concrete act of obedience and personal expense.", "David built the altar and offered burnt offerings and fellowship sacrifices. The Lord answered prayer for the land, and the plague ended. That is where 2 Samuel closes. Its final scene is neither a coronation nor an account of the king's death. A ruler has confessed, a people has suffered, mercy has restrained destruction, and an altar stands upon ground purchased for worship. The whole book has held God's enduring promise beside the serious consequences of David's choices. Its ending leaves him dependent upon the mercy he named when judgment was offered. The land's relief comes through God's answer, while the cost David accepts gives visible form to his responsibility before Him."], "lesson": "Confession should accept responsibility and lead to obedience. The book's closing scene directs hope toward God's mercy while refusing to hide the harm caused through a ruler's decisions.", "question": "How do David's confession, his appeal concerning the sheep and his payment for the altar belong together?"},
  {"id": 93000, "title": "Adonijah Holds a Feast Before the King Has Spoken", "subtitle": "Ambition beneath a father's silence", "text": "1 Kings 1:1–27", "chapters": [[10, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["David was old and could no longer keep warm despite the coverings placed upon him. His attendants found Abishag of Shunem to care for him. She served the king, but the account says he did not have sexual relations with her. Within that weakened royal household, Adonijah announced that he would be king. He acquired chariots, horsemen and fifty runners. The narrator notes his handsome appearance and David's failure to challenge his conduct. Adonijah consulted Joab and Abiathar, who supported him. Others, including Zadok, Nathan, Benaiah and David's mighty men, did not join him. A decision concerning Israel's future was being advanced through a son's ambition and the support of powerful officers while David remained unaware.", "Adonijah arranged sacrifices and a feast beside the stone Zoheleth near En-rogel. He invited royal brothers and officials from Judah but excluded Solomon, Nathan, Benaiah and the mighty men. The guest list showed the boundaries of his support. Nathan went to Bathsheba and warned that Adonijah's attempt endangered both her and Solomon. He advised her to remind David of his oath that Solomon would succeed him. While she spoke, Nathan would enter and confirm the report. Their response sought a decision from the living king before Adonijah's celebration could establish an accomplished fact. The danger required speech from people who understood what the gathering implied.", "Bathsheba entered David's chamber, where Abishag attended him, and bowed. She recalled the promise that her son would sit upon his throne and told him of Adonijah's feast. The king's sons, Joab and Abiathar had been invited; Solomon had been excluded. All Israel, she said, looked to David to declare his successor. Without that declaration, after his death she and Solomon could be treated as offenders. Her appeal joined the earlier oath to the immediate consequences of silence. The question concerned her son's safety as well as the transfer of authority. The king needed to hear what was being done in his name and during his remaining life.", "Nathan arrived before she finished. He bowed and asked whether David had authorised Adonijah to reign. The guests were already eating, drinking and acclaiming him as king, while Nathan and other loyal servants had received no invitation or explanation. Had David decided this without informing them? The passage ends with the question placed squarely before him. A feast outside the palace and a conversation inside his chamber now represent rival ways of settling the future. The narrator has named Adonijah's self exaltation, his father's earlier lack of correction and the courage of those willing to bring the concealed event into the open. David's answer will determine what happens next."], "lesson": "Unchallenged ambition can draw others into a dangerous course. Nathan and Bathsheba show the importance of bringing truthful information to those responsible for acting upon it.", "question": "How do the feast's invitations and exclusions reveal what Adonijah is trying to accomplish?"},
  {"id": 93001, "title": "The Trumpet That Ended Adonijah's Feast", "subtitle": "Authority made clear and mercy tested", "text": "1 Kings 1:28–53", "chapters": [[10, 1]], "start": 28, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["David called Bathsheba back before him and swore by the living Lord who had rescued him from every distress. He would fulfil his promise that very day: Solomon would succeed him. Bathsheba bowed in acknowledgment. David then summoned Zadok, Nathan and Benaiah. They were to place Solomon upon the king's own mule, escort him to Gihon, anoint him and sound the trumpet. Afterward he would sit upon David's throne as ruler of Israel and Judah. Benaiah affirmed the command and prayed that the Lord would be with Solomon and make his throne greater than David's. The private oath now became a set of public instructions with named witnesses and visible signs.", "The priests, prophet, commander and royal guards took Solomon to Gihon. Zadok brought oil from the sacred tent and anointed him. The trumpet sounded, the people acclaimed him and a joyful procession returned to the city with music. Its noise reached Adonijah's guests as their meal ended. Joab asked why the city was in uproar. Jonathan son of Abiathar arrived, and Adonijah welcomed him as a bearer of good news. His report overturned that expectation. David had made Solomon king, sent the recognised attendants with him and given him his own mule. Solomon had been anointed and was now seated on the royal throne. The celebration they heard belonged to another king.", "Jonathan also described the servants blessing David and the old king bowing upon his bed in gratitude. David had praised God for allowing him to see a successor upon the throne. Adonijah's guests became afraid and dispersed. The feast had depended upon their collective confidence, but the king's explicit action dissolved it. Adonijah fled to the altar and held its horns, seeking an oath that Solomon would spare him. The man who had announced his own kingship now called himself Solomon's servant. His danger was real, but the change also exposed how quickly public ambition could become an appeal for protection when its support failed.", "Solomon declared that Adonijah would be safe if he proved worthy, but wrongdoing would bring death. He had him brought from the altar. Adonijah bowed, and Solomon sent him home. The chapter ends with a conditional mercy rather than immediate execution. David's decision has been carried out without a battle between the factions, and the new king's first response gives his rival an opportunity to live under the changed order. The altar has become the place of a plea, but the promise of safety is tied to future conduct. The next events will test whether Adonijah accepts the settlement he now acknowledges. The throne is occupied, while the loyalties surrounding it remain to be examined."], "lesson": "Clear responsibility can prevent a crisis from spreading, and mercy calls for a changed response from the person who receives it. Adonijah's release carries an obligation he must now honour.", "question": "What do David's public instructions add to the earlier promise concerning Solomon?"},
  {"id": 93002, "title": "David's Last Charge to Solomon", "subtitle": "Obedience and unfinished responsibilities", "text": "1 Kings 2:1–12", "chapters": [[10, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["As David approached death, he charged Solomon to be strong and faithful. He spoke of going the way of all the earth and directed his son toward the Lord's requirements in the law of Moses. Solomon was to keep God's statutes, commands, judgments and testimonies in the places and work before him. David connected that obedience with the promise concerning his descendants. Their place upon Israel's throne required them to walk before God sincerely and wholeheartedly. The opening of the charge therefore placed the new king under a word beyond his father's wishes. His strength as ruler was to include submission to the God whose people he would govern.", "David then named unfinished matters from his reign. Joab had killed Abner and Amasa, treating times of peace as occasions for bloodshed. Their deaths remained a responsibility that royal success had never removed. David told Solomon to act wisely concerning him and not allow him to end his life in peace. He also remembered the sons of Barzillai, whose household had supported him during his flight from Absalom. They were to receive kindness and eat at Solomon's table. The charge carries both judgment and gratitude into the new reign. Those who had helped a displaced king and those whose violence had burdened his government were not to disappear from memory when power passed to another generation.", "Shimei was also named. David recalled his grievous cursing during the escape to Mahanaim and the oath he himself had given not to execute him when they met at the Jordan. He nevertheless instructed Solomon to consider his guilt and bring the matter to a severe conclusion through wisdom. The account preserves the tension between David's earlier promise and the responsibility he now gives his son. It reports his instructions without making every royal motive transparent. The reader has followed Shimei's conduct and David's restraint in the earlier book and must bring that history into this farewell. The words are spoken by a father whose reign included mercy, conflict and unresolved consequences.", "David died and was buried in the City of David. The narrator summarises forty years of rule, seven in Hebron and thirty three in Jerusalem. Solomon sat upon his father's throne, and his kingdom became firmly established. The brief death notice ends a life told across two books and carries the reader into the son's reign. David's final charge has included faithfulness to God, kindness toward remembered helpers and judgments concerning powerful or hostile men. These matters will soon take concrete form. The new king inherits more than a palace and a title. He receives promises, obligations and a history of decisions whose effects continue after his father's burial."], "lesson": "A faithful succession remembers obligations as well as achievements. The charge asks how reverence for God, gratitude and justice will shape the next use of authority.", "question": "Which responsibilities in David's charge reach beyond the immediate survival of Solomon's throne?"},
  {"id": 93003, "title": "The Rival's Request and the Commander at the Altar", "subtitle": "The kingdom takes a severe shape", "text": "1 Kings 2:13–46", "chapters": [[10, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Adonijah visited Bathsheba and asked her to request Abishag as his wife. He acknowledged that the kingdom had come to Solomon from the Lord while also saying that it had once seemed his own. Bathsheba agreed to speak. Solomon received his mother with honour and placed her beside him. But when she presented the request, he treated it as a renewed claim upon the kingdom, connecting Adonijah with Abiathar and Joab. The place of a woman from David's household gave the request political weight in the king's judgment. Solomon swore that Adonijah had spoken against his own life and sent Benaiah to put him to death. The conditional mercy at the altar had ended in a judgment upon a later action.", "Solomon removed Abiathar from priestly office and sent him to his land at Anathoth. He spared his life because he had carried the ark before David and shared his sufferings. The narrator connects his removal with the earlier word concerning Eli's house at Shiloh. Joab heard the news and fled to the sacred tent, grasping the altar's horns. When Benaiah ordered him out, he refused and said he would die there. Solomon directed Benaiah to carry out the sentence, recalling Joab's murders of Abner and Amasa without David's knowledge. Joab was killed and buried at his home in the wilderness. Benaiah became commander, and Zadok replaced Abiathar.", "Solomon next required Shimei to build a house in Jerusalem and remain there. Leaving would bring death, and Shimei accepted the condition. Three years later two of his slaves fled to Gath. Shimei saddled his donkey, pursued them to Achish and returned with them. Solomon summoned him and recalled the oath and warning he had accepted. He also spoke of Shimei's wrongdoing toward David. Benaiah was again ordered to carry out a death sentence. The last verse declares the kingdom established in Solomon's hands. The consolidation has involved removal from office, strict conditions, judgments over earlier bloodshed and the elimination of a rival claimant.", "The chapter does not hide the severity through which the new reign became secure. Its accounts connect particular decisions with the earlier stories of David's family and commanders. Adonijah's request follows an attempted succession, Joab's refuge follows the killings he committed, Abiathar's removal recalls both loyal service and support for Adonijah, and Shimei's journey breaches an agreed restriction. The repeated appearance of Benaiah shows how royal commands become actions upon human lives. Solomon now possesses the throne without those immediate challenges, but the rest of the book will judge his rule by more than his success in securing it. His need for wisdom and obedience remains before him."], "lesson": "Authority must be considered through its decisions and their consequences. This chapter asks readers to examine the claims of justice made by rulers alongside the actual conduct being judged.", "question": "How does the history of each person affect the way Solomon explains his decision?"},
  {"id": 93004, "title": "The King Asks for a Listening Heart", "subtitle": "Wisdom directed toward another person's life", "text": "1 Kings 3:1–28", "chapters": [[10, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Solomon formed an alliance with Egypt by marrying Pharaoh's daughter and brought her into the City of David while his major buildings were still unfinished. The people sacrificed at high places because the temple had not yet been built. The narrator says Solomon loved the Lord and walked in David's statutes, while noting his worship at those places. At Gibeon, where he offered many sacrifices, the Lord appeared in a dream and invited him to ask for a gift. Solomon remembered God's kindness to David and confessed his own lack of experience. He was responsible for a great people whom God had chosen. He asked for a listening, understanding heart to judge them and distinguish good from evil.", "The request pleased God. Solomon had sought discernment for justice rather than long life, wealth or the death of enemies. He would receive extraordinary wisdom, and also riches and honour he had not requested. Length of days remained connected with obedience. Solomon awoke, returned to Jerusalem, worshipped before the ark and held a feast. The account soon shows his wisdom tested in an ordinary household tragedy. Two women who worked as prostitutes came before him. They had given birth close together while living in the same house. One said the other's baby had died and that the living child had been exchanged for the dead one during the night. No outside witness had been there.", "The second woman denied the accusation, and both claimed the living baby. Solomon repeated the competing statements and ordered a sword brought. He announced that the child should be divided, half for each. The living child's mother pleaded that the baby be given to the other woman and kept alive. The other accepted the proposed division. Solomon then ordered the child handed to the woman who had begged for his life, identifying her as the mother. Her willingness to lose her claim rather than see the baby killed made the decisive difference. The threatened act served to expose the competing responses, and the final order preserved the child.", "Israel heard the judgment and recognised God's wisdom in Solomon for administering justice. The chapter's movement connects a request made in a dream with a decision affecting vulnerable people who came to court. The women had no status presented as grounds for special access, and the child could not speak. The gift Solomon sought is shown through the protection of a life and discernment beyond two incompatible statements. His early humility is therefore important to the meaning of his wisdom. He had admitted that he could not carry such responsibility unaided. Later achievements and temptations will need to be read against this beginning, when he asked for understanding in order to serve God's people."], "lesson": "Wisdom serves the people affected by a decision. Solomon's request invites humility about responsibility and a desire to discern what protects life and establishes justice.", "question": "How does the mother's response reveal something that the competing claims alone could not establish?"},
  {"id": 93005, "title": "A Kingdom Supplied and a Wisdom Heard Abroad", "subtitle": "Prosperity and the responsibilities beneath it", "text": "1 Kings 4:1–34", "chapters": [[10, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Solomon ruled all Israel through named officials. The account lists priests, secretaries, a recorder, a commander, a palace administrator and officers responsible for other work. Benaiah commanded the army, Jehoshaphat kept the record, and Adoniram oversaw the levy. Twelve district governors provided supplies for the royal household, each responsible for a month. Their areas stretched through the hills, plains and territory beyond the Jordan. Some were connected to the king by marriage. The list gives a practical shape to the united kingdom. The abundance later described depended upon organised labour, regional provision and people whose work supported the court throughout the year.", "Judah and Israel were numerous and enjoyed food, drink and celebration. Neighbouring kingdoms brought tribute, and Solomon had peace on his borders. The account pictures families living securely under their vines and fig trees from Dan to Beersheba. It also names the great daily requirements of the palace: fine flour, meal, cattle, sheep, game and poultry. Horses and chariot forces required their own supplies. Each governor delivered what was needed, including barley and straw for the animals. The peace experienced by households and the scale of the royal establishment are placed side by side. The machinery of provision remained part of the kingdom's prosperity and would matter when later generations judged its burdens.", "God gave Solomon wisdom, understanding and breadth of mind beyond the renowned wisdom of neighbouring lands. His reputation exceeded that of recognised sages, and his name spread among the nations. He spoke three thousand proverbs and composed a thousand and five songs. His attention extended across the created world, from Lebanon's cedar to the small hyssop growing from a wall. He spoke about animals, birds, reptiles and fish. The wisdom requested for judgment therefore appears as a wider capacity to observe, consider and express what he learned. The chapter remembers both the administrative structure of the state and the range of the mind at its centre.", "People came from many nations, sent by rulers who had heard of him, to listen to Solomon. The reign's influence reached beyond military tribute into conversation and learning. The narrative has moved from officers and monthly food supplies to a wisdom heard abroad, showing different forms of royal strength. None removes the need for faithful conduct. The security beneath the vine and fig tree gives a human purpose to the larger arrangements, while the named workers prevent the prosperity from appearing effortless. As the temple project begins, Solomon will draw upon these resources and relationships. The splendour ahead will require the same careful attention to the people and labour by which it is made possible."], "lesson": "Prosperity rests upon responsibilities and contributions that can easily be overlooked. Wisdom should help those who govern remember the households and workers beneath impressive achievements.", "question": "What does the chapter reveal about the ordinary work that makes a peaceful and prosperous kingdom possible?"},
  {"id": 93006, "title": "The Timber Floats Down from Lebanon", "subtitle": "Preparation shared across many hands", "text": "1 Kings 5:1–18", "chapters": [[10, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Hiram king of Tyre sent representatives when he heard that Solomon had succeeded David, whom he had long valued. Solomon responded by recalling that his father's wars had prevented him from building the Lord's house. Now God had given rest, and Solomon intended to carry out the promise that David's son would build it. He requested cedar from Lebanon and acknowledged the Sidonians' skill in cutting timber. His own workers would join theirs, and he would pay the wages Hiram named. The request connected a sacred purpose with a practical recognition of another people's expertise. Solomon's intention required cooperation beyond the workers already within his kingdom.", "Hiram welcomed the message and blessed the Lord for giving David a wise son to rule Israel. He agreed to supply cedar and cypress. His servants would bring the timber from Lebanon to the sea and float it to the place Solomon designated. In return Solomon would provide food for his household. The two kings maintained a peaceful agreement, and the narrator connects Solomon's wisdom with the Lord's promise. Timber and provisions moved in opposite directions under the arrangement. The house to be built in Jerusalem would carry within it the skill of Tyrian workers and the labour of people along the route from forest to sea to building site.", "Solomon raised a levy of thirty thousand Israelites, sending ten thousand to Lebanon each month. Each group spent a month there and two months at home, under Adoniram's supervision. Other great companies carried loads and quarried stone, while officers directed the work. The king ordered large, carefully cut and costly stones for the foundations. Solomon's builders worked alongside Hiram's builders and the stonecutters of Gebal. The passage describes preparation before the temple itself is raised. Timber must be selected and transported, rock extracted and shaped, food supplied and work coordinated. Its lists of workers give substance to the project that might otherwise be remembered only through the king's decision.", "The chapter ends with wood and stone being made ready. Solomon's stated purpose has become an undertaking spread across different lands and kinds of work. Hiram's response honours the God to whom the house is dedicated, while the builders' craft makes that dedication physically possible. The account offers no reason to treat the labour as unimportant beside the ceremony that will eventually open the temple. Preparation belongs to its story. The peaceful setting also marks a change from David's struggles, allowing resources and attention to be directed toward construction. In the next chapter, dates, measurements and materials will show what those efforts produce, alongside a word from God about the obedience that the finished house cannot replace."], "lesson": "Faithful work includes preparation, cooperation and recognition of others' skill. A purpose directed toward God still requires just and careful attention to the people who carry it out.", "question": "Why does the account give so much space to agreements, transport and workers before describing the completed temple?"},
  {"id": 93007, "title": "The House Rises in Prepared Stone", "subtitle": "God's word within a splendid building", "text": "1 Kings 6:1–38", "chapters": [[10, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["In the fourth year of Solomon's reign, in the month of Ziv, work began on the Lord's house. The account places it within Israel's history since the exodus. It gives the building's length, width and height, then describes the porch, windows and rooms around its sides. The surrounding chambers rose in three levels, with supporting ledges arranged so beams would not cut into the temple walls. Stones had been prepared before arrival, so no hammer or iron tool sounded at the site while the house was being built. The careful description invites attention to a project whose construction depended upon planning and workmanship already under way beyond Jerusalem.", "During the account of building, the Lord's word came to Solomon. He was to walk in God's statutes, perform His judgments and keep His commands. God would fulfil the promise made to David, dwell among Israel and not forsake His people. The message addresses the king's conduct while he is constructing a place of worship. Timber, stone and gold do not remove that requirement. The narrative then returns to the work: cedar covered the interior walls, cypress formed the floor and an inner chamber was prepared for the ark. The word about obedience stands inside the architectural account, giving the completed structure a context beyond its measurements.", "The inner sanctuary was a cube overlaid with gold, as were other parts of the interior and the altar. Two great cherubim made of olive wood stood within it, their extended wings reaching across the room and meeting in the centre. Their surfaces were also covered with gold. Carved cherubim, palm trees and open flowers decorated the walls, and gold covered the floors. Olive wood doors marked the inner entrance, while the main entrance had folding cypress doors. Their carvings were fitted with gold. The court was formed with courses of dressed stone and cedar beams. Each detail belongs to the provision of a distinct place for the ark and for the worship to be conducted around it.", "The foundation had been laid in Solomon's fourth year, and the house was completed in his eleventh, in the month of Bul. Seven years had passed in its construction. The last notice confirms that the work followed the full plan. The chapter has named the dimensions and craftsmanship while preserving the Lord's instruction at its centre. The building is beautiful and costly, but the king's responsibilities are expressed in verbs of faithful living. When later generations look upon the house, its very presence will recall both God's promise and the obligation attached to it. The completion of the walls begins a further task of furnishing, dedication and continuing worship that will require the same seriousness toward His word."], "lesson": "Care and beauty can honour a sacred purpose, while obedience gives the worshipper an ongoing responsibility before God. The message to Solomon directs attention to both building and conduct.", "question": "What does the Lord's message add to the detailed account of materials and measurements?"},
  {"id": 93008, "title": "The Palace, the Craftsman and the Two Pillars", "subtitle": "Craft and authority made visible", "text": "1 Kings 7:1–22", "chapters": [[10, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Solomon's own palace complex took thirteen years to complete. The account describes the House of the Forest of Lebanon with cedar columns, rows of windows and a great roofed interior. There were halls of pillars and a hall for the throne where he would judge. His private residence stood within another court, and Pharaoh's daughter had a house of similar construction. Costly dressed stones and cedar marked the buildings from their large foundations upward. The court arrangements are related to those of the Lord's house. Royal government and the king's household were being given a lasting architectural form beside the sacred building already described.", "The narrative then introduces Hiram, the bronze craftsman summoned from Tyre, a different role from the king who supplied Solomon's timber. He was the son of a widow connected with Naphtali, and his father had been a Tyrian metalworker. He possessed skill, understanding and experience in bronze work. Solomon brought him to carry out the needed castings. The text gives his background before describing his achievements, recognising the worker whose ability produced the visible objects. The temple's grandeur depended upon a craft learned and exercised by a named person. Its sacred purpose did not make the knowledge of materials and techniques incidental.", "Hiram cast two great bronze pillars, each with an elaborate capital. Networks, chains, pomegranates and lily shaped work decorated their upper parts. The description gives their dimensions and the repeated patterns worked around them. They were set at the temple porch and named Jachin and Boaz. Their place at the entrance made them part of the approach to the house, while their names and form gave them an identity within its remembered furnishings. The passage does not supply a story for every decorative feature. It allows the actual workmanship and the recorded names to carry the account without requiring an invented hidden meaning for each measurement or ornament.", "The section ends when the pillars' work is complete, before continuing to other bronze furnishings. It has moved from the large spaces of royal life to the particular skill of a craftsman and the objects at the sacred entrance. The hall for judgment recalls that the palace exists partly for the administration of justice. The temple porch recalls the purpose for which its materials were gathered. Both uses ask more of Solomon than the ability to commission impressive construction. The people who enter the spaces, the workers who make them and the decisions taken within them belong to the moral setting of their splendour. The next section will finish the equipment required for the temple's use."], "lesson": "Skilled work deserves recognition, and impressive settings carry responsibilities for those who use them. A hall of judgment must serve justice as well as display royal strength.", "question": "How does naming Hiram and describing his skill affect your view of the finished temple furnishings?"},
  {"id": 93009, "title": "The Bronze Sea and the Finished Furnishings", "subtitle": "Many kinds of work brought to completion", "text": "1 Kings 7:23–51", "chapters": [[10, 7]], "start": 23, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Hiram made a great round basin called the Sea, with a rim shaped like a cup and decorated with lily forms. It stood upon twelve bronze oxen arranged in four groups facing the directions of the compass. The account gives its breadth, height, thickness and capacity, making its size part of the reader's understanding of the court. This was one of the great castings prepared for the Lord's house. The workers' skill had to join strength and ornament in an object intended for use. The description now moves through the equipment that would fill the place whose walls and entrance had already been completed.", "Ten bronze stands supported smaller basins. Their panels carried lions, oxen and cherubim, and they had wheels, axles and supports cast as parts of the structure. Each followed the same design and dimensions. Five were placed on one side of the house and five on the other, while the Sea stood toward the south east. Pots, shovels and bowls were also made. The inventory brings large and small objects into the same account. Those handling the temple's work would need ordinary tools as well as striking vessels. The record honours both by naming them among what was made ready for service.", "The bronze objects were cast in clay ground in the Jordan valley between Succoth and Zarethan. Their quantity was so great that the total weight was not determined. The account then lists the gold furnishings: altar, table for the bread of the Presence, lampstands before the inner sanctuary, lamps, tongs, bowls, dishes, censers and even hinges for the doors. The materials differ, but the emphasis remains upon completing what belongs to the house. The light, offerings and other acts associated with worship would take place through the use and care of these provisions. Their value called for workmanship and stewardship, not simply admiration at the moment they were installed.", "Solomon finished the work for the Lord's house and brought in the silver, gold and vessels David had dedicated, placing them in its treasuries. The final act connects the new building with preparations made in the earlier reign. What David set apart has now been received within the place built by his son. The long construction account ends with the house and its equipment ready, yet the ark has still to be brought to its resting place. Completion therefore leads toward dedication. The inventory's many details record the contribution of planners, quarry workers, carpenters, metalworkers and those who supplied the resources. Their different efforts have become one provision for a purpose that will require continuing faithfulness from the whole people."], "lesson": "A shared purpose often depends upon many kinds of careful service. The inventory invites gratitude for work that is essential even when it receives less public attention.", "question": "What does the inclusion of small tools alongside the great vessels teach about the work of worship?"},
  {"id": 93010, "title": "The Cloud Fills the House", "subtitle": "Remembering the covenant at the dedication", "text": "1 Kings 8:1–21", "chapters": [[10, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Solomon gathered Israel's elders, tribal leaders and family heads to Jerusalem to bring the ark into the completed temple. The assembly met during the seventh month, at the time of the feast. Priests carried the ark, and priests and Levites brought the sacred tent and its vessels. Solomon and the gathered people offered sacrifices in such numbers that they were not counted. The ceremony joined the building just completed to the worship and covenant remembered from earlier generations. The ark's journey from David's city was the central action. The house had been made ready for it, and the congregation now accompanied its arrival with costly offerings and public attention.", "The priests placed the ark within the inner sanctuary beneath the wings of the great cherubim. The carrying poles extended toward the holy place, while the ark itself remained within. The narrator recalls its contents: the two stone tablets Moses placed there at Horeb, when the Lord made covenant with the people after bringing them from Egypt. That detail directs the reader's attention beyond the splendour of the room. The object at its centre bears the memory of God's word and Israel's relationship with Him. The new house is therefore connected to a history of deliverance and obligation already older than Solomon's reign.", "When the priests withdrew, a cloud filled the house. They could not continue their ministry because the Lord's glory filled it. Solomon spoke of the Lord dwelling in thick darkness and of the lasting house prepared for Him. Then he turned and blessed the standing assembly. He praised the God of Israel who had spoken to David and fulfilled the promise through His own action. David had desired to build a house for God's name. The Lord had approved the intention while appointing his son to carry out the work. Solomon now stood upon that son's throne and before the completed building. The ceremony became an occasion for acknowledging a word fulfilled across two generations.", "Solomon explained that a place had been prepared for the ark holding the covenant made at the exodus. His address returns to that covenant after speaking of his father's desire and his own achievement. The cloud has interrupted priestly work, and the king's blessing has credited the Lord for what the people see. The dedication cannot be reduced to the display of a successful builder. God has made His presence known, and the assembled nation is reminded of the word that defines its worship. Solomon will next pray for those who come toward this house in many kinds of need. The prayer will address the God whose glory fills it and whose greatness exceeds its walls."], "lesson": "Worship remembers God's word and faithfulness before celebrating human achievement. The ark's contents and the cloud direct attention to the One for whom the house was built.", "question": "Why does the account pause to recall the tablets within the ark during the temple's dedication?"},
  {"id": 93011, "title": "Solomon Prays for Those Who Will Come After Him", "subtitle": "A house of prayer for need and repentance", "text": "1 Kings 8:22–66", "chapters": [[10, 8]], "start": 22, "end": 66, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Standing before the altar, Solomon spread his hands toward heaven and praised the God who keeps covenant and steadfast love. He asked that the promise to David continue as his descendants walked faithfully. Yet he also acknowledged that even the highest heavens could not contain God, much less this house. He asked the Lord to hear prayer directed toward the place bearing His name, to hear in heaven and forgive. The opening of the prayer holds the temple's importance beside God's immeasurable greatness. The worshippers will have a place toward which to turn, but the One who hears is never contained by the structure the king has made.", "Solomon named situations that would bring people there. A disputed wrong might require an oath and a just decision. Defeat, drought, famine, disease, blight or siege might lead the people to recognise sin and seek forgiveness. He asked God to hear each person whose heart He alone fully knew and to teach the right way. His prayer also included the foreigner who came from a distant land because of God's name. He asked that such prayers be answered so every nation might know and fear the Lord. The temple's purpose thus reached beyond the king and beyond the boundaries of Israel, while the repeated requests for forgiveness recognised how deeply human need includes moral failure.", "The prayer looked toward troops sent into battle and toward the possibility of captivity after sin. Solomon said plainly that no one is without sin. If the exiles came to themselves, confessed and turned wholeheartedly toward God, he asked that the Lord hear, forgive and grant compassion before their captors. Israel belonged to the God who brought the people from Egypt. Solomon then rose from kneeling and blessed the assembly. He praised the Lord for fulfilling His promises and asked that He incline their hearts toward obedience. The people's hearts were to remain wholly devoted, keeping His commands. Prayer for mercy was joined to a call for a faithful life.", "Solomon and Israel dedicated the house with immense sacrifices. The middle of the court was used because the bronze altar could not hold all the offerings. A vast congregation celebrated from the north to the border of Egypt, and the account records the extended period of feasting. When dismissed, the people blessed the king and returned home glad for the goodness God had shown David and Israel. The public joy followed a prayer filled with future trouble, sin and exile. Those possibilities were not allowed to silence celebration, nor did celebration make them unthinkable. The house had been dedicated with hope that God would hear returning hearts when the people faced the consequences of their own choices."], "lesson": "Prayer can name failure honestly while trusting God's mercy. Solomon's concern for foreigners and future exiles also widens our sense of who should be remembered before God.", "question": "How do the repeated appeals for forgiveness shape the meaning of the dedication's joy?"},
  {"id": 93012, "title": "The Lord Answers and Warns the Builder", "subtitle": "A sacred house still requires faithfulness", "text": "1 Kings 9:1–28", "chapters": [[10, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["After Solomon completed the Lord's house, his palace and his other desired projects, the Lord appeared to him again as at Gibeon. He declared that He had heard the dedication prayer and consecrated the house for His name. His eyes and heart would be there. He also spoke of Solomon's conduct and the future of his descendants. If he walked uprightly and kept God's commands, the royal promise would stand. But if king or people turned to other gods, Israel could be removed from the land and the temple rejected. Passersby would ask why such ruin had come, and the answer would concern their abandonment of the God who redeemed them. The splendid house carried no exemption from covenant responsibility.", "The chapter then records dealings with Hiram after the twenty years of construction. Solomon gave him twenty towns in Galilee in return for the timber and gold supplied. Hiram inspected them and was displeased, giving the region the name Cabul. The exchange preserved a disagreement within a relationship that had helped make the building possible. The account also remembers Hiram's substantial contribution of gold. Success had involved transactions, expectations and land as well as friendship. Its material history stands immediately after the Lord's warning, keeping the reader among the practical choices through which a great reign was being exercised.", "Solomon's levy supported work on the temple, palace, Millo, Jerusalem's wall and fortified towns including Hazor, Megiddo and Gezer. Pharaoh had taken and burned Gezer and given it as a marriage gift to his daughter. Solomon rebuilt it and other places for storage, chariots and horses. The remaining peoples of the land were placed under forced labour, while Israelites served in military and official roles. Overseers directed the workforce. Pharaoh's daughter moved into her new house, and further work followed. These notices show both the reach of royal planning and the unequal obligations borne by different communities. The buildings and strongholds were supported by human labour under the king's command.", "The narrative recalls Solomon's regular sacrifices and incense at the temple. It also describes a fleet based at Ezion-geber near Elath on the Red Sea. Hiram sent experienced sailors to work with Solomon's men, and they returned from Ophir with gold. Worship, construction, trade and wealth continued together in the established reign. Yet the opening word of the chapter remains the measure for its future. God has heard Solomon's prayer, but the king must continue hearing God's command. Neither regular ceremony nor abundant resources can make the warning irrelevant. The account has set the possibility of ruin beside the evidence of prosperity before the later choices that will begin to bring that warning into view."], "lesson": "A good beginning and continuing religious activity still call for obedience. God's answer to Solomon joins welcome and warning within the same relationship.", "question": "Why does the warning about the temple matter at a point when Solomon's achievements appear secure?"},
  {"id": 93013, "title": "The Queen Hears More Than the Reports Had Told", "subtitle": "Wisdom, wealth and the purpose of a throne", "text": "1 Kings 10:1–29", "chapters": [[10, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["The queen of Sheba heard of Solomon's fame in connection with the Lord's name and came to test him with difficult questions. Her great company brought spices, gold and precious stones. She spoke openly of what was upon her mind, and Solomon answered everything she asked. She also observed his house, food, officials, attendants, clothing, cupbearers and the way he approached the Lord's house. The whole display astonished her. Reports she once doubted had proved inadequate to describe what she now saw. She praised the fortunate position of those who regularly heard his wisdom. Her visit gave the reputation of the reign a witness from beyond its own borders.", "The queen blessed the Lord for delighting in Solomon and placing him upon Israel's throne. She connected his appointment with God's love for the people and the purpose of administering justice and righteousness. Her words recall the responsibility beneath the magnificence. She gave extraordinary gifts, including a great quantity of spices, while Solomon granted her requests and offered royal gifts of his own. The account also mentions rare timber and stones brought by Hiram's ships and the use of the wood for temple and palace fittings and musical instruments. The queen then returned to her country. What she had heard and seen joined the outward splendour to a stated moral purpose for kingship.", "The remainder of the chapter surveys the scale of Solomon's wealth. Gold arrived through trade, gifts and regional rulers. Gold shields were placed in the House of the Forest of Lebanon. His ivory throne, overlaid with gold and flanked by lions upon its steps, had no equal among the kingdoms. Drinking vessels were gold, and silver counted for little amid the abundance. Ships returned with precious metals, ivory and unusual animals. People brought yearly gifts as they came to hear the wisdom God had placed in Solomon's heart. The repeated connection with wisdom sits beside an increasingly extensive record of goods and display.", "Solomon also gathered chariots and horsemen and maintained centres for them. Horses and chariots came through trade with Egypt and were supplied onward to other kings. Jerusalem's silver and cedar were described through images of extraordinary availability. The chapter has therefore moved from a visitor's questions to the institutions of wealth, transport and military power. The queen's statement about justice remains a searching word within it. A throne can impress through its material form while still needing to fulfil the reason for which it was given. The next chapter will turn from admiration abroad toward the heart of the king himself, where the greatest danger to the reign is already taking shape."], "lesson": "Gifts and reputation carry a purpose beyond admiration. The queen's words ask whether wisdom and resources are being used to establish justice for the people entrusted to a ruler.", "question": "Why is the queen's explanation of Solomon's kingship important within the account of his wealth?"},
  {"id": 93014, "title": "The New Robe Torn into Twelve Pieces", "subtitle": "A divided heart leads toward a divided kingdom", "text": "1 Kings 11:1–43", "chapters": [[10, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Solomon loved many women from nations whose gods Israel had been warned against. His seven hundred wives and three hundred concubines turned his heart as he grew old. He followed Ashtoreth and Milcom and built shrines for Chemosh and Molech near Jerusalem, providing places where his foreign wives offered incense and sacrifices. The narrator holds him responsible for failing to remain wholly faithful to the Lord, who had appeared to him twice and commanded him concerning other gods. God announced that the kingdom would be torn away and given to a servant. For David's sake the division would come in the son's reign, with a portion preserved for David's house and Jerusalem.", "Opposition arose through men whose histories reached back into David's wars. Hadad, an Edomite royal survivor taken to Egypt as a child, had received a house, land and marriage into Pharaoh's family. Hearing of David and Joab's deaths, he sought to return to his country. Rezon, who had escaped Hadadezer of Zobah, gathered a company and established himself at Damascus. Both became adversaries. The account links Solomon's increasing trouble with the Lord's judgment while also tracing the human histories of the men involved. Earlier conquests had left memories, survivors and rival ambitions that now stood around the prosperous kingdom.", "Jeroboam, an able worker from Ephraim whose mother was a widow, also entered the story. Solomon saw his industry and placed him over the labour of Joseph's house. Outside Jerusalem the prophet Ahijah met him in the open country wearing a new robe. He tore it into twelve pieces and gave Jeroboam ten, explaining the coming division because of the people's idolatry. David's line would retain a portion, and Solomon himself would finish his reign. Jeroboam was offered a lasting house if he obeyed God's commands. The sign named both judgment on the existing king and responsibility for the man who would receive the greater part of his kingdom.", "Solomon sought Jeroboam's death, and he fled to Shishak in Egypt until the king died. The narrator refers readers to further records of Solomon's deeds and wisdom, then records his forty years in Jerusalem, his burial in David's city and Rehoboam's succession. The reign that began with a request for an understanding heart has ended with a heart turned toward other gods. Its buildings and renown remain in the record, but the torn robe explains the future more decisively than its wealth. The promise offered to Jeroboam also prevents him from treating his own opportunity as permission to act without obedience. The next king will face a people already carrying the burdens and divisions of Solomon's later years."], "lesson": "Wisdom received must be joined to continuing obedience. The torn robe makes visible how choices at the centre of power can affect the future of an entire people.", "question": "How do the warnings given to Solomon and the promise offered to Jeroboam establish a responsibility for both men?"},
  {"id": 93015, "title": "The Heavy Yoke and the Golden Calves", "subtitle": "Two rulers choose how to hold power", "text": "1 Kings 12:1–33", "chapters": [[10, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Israel gathered at Shechem to make Rehoboam king. Jeroboam returned from Egypt and joined the people in asking that Solomon's heavy burden of service be lightened. They promised loyalty if the new king would hear them. Rehoboam asked for three days and consulted the elders who had served his father. They urged him to serve the people and answer kindly. He rejected that advice and turned to his own companions, who recommended threats of even harsher treatment. When the assembly returned, Rehoboam declared that his father's burden would become heavier. The narrator connects his refusal with the word Ahijah had spoken concerning the kingdom's division. A prophetic judgment was unfolding through a king's chosen answer.", "The northern tribes renounced their share in David's house and departed. Rehoboam still ruled those in Judah's towns. He sent Adoram, the levy officer, but the people stoned him, and the king fled to Jerusalem. Israel made Jeroboam king. Rehoboam gathered a great army from Judah and Benjamin to recover the kingdom, but God's word came through Shemaiah forbidding war against their brothers. The forces obeyed and went home. The refusal that broke the assembly at Shechem was followed by a command that stopped an immediate civil war. The two kingdoms now had to live within the division, each ruler responsible for his next response.", "Jeroboam strengthened Shechem and Penuel, then became afraid that worship in Jerusalem would draw the people's loyalty back to Rehoboam. He imagined them killing him and returning to David's house. He therefore sought counsel and made two golden calves, placing one at Bethel and the other at Dan. He presented them as Israel's gods associated with the exodus and told the people that the journey to Jerusalem was too much for them. The account calls the resulting worship sin. The man who had received a promise of lasting rule through obedience was arranging worship to answer his fear of losing power.", "Jeroboam also established shrines and appointed priests outside the appointed Levitical line. He devised a festival in the eighth month resembling Judah's feast and went to offer at Bethel's altar. The chapter ends with the king standing within the religious system he had designed. Rehoboam had tried to secure rule through heavier coercion; Jeroboam was seeking security through controlled worship. Both responses exposed the moral danger of treating the people chiefly as a kingdom to retain. The next chapter's prophetic interruption will take place at the altar where this one closes. Jeroboam's political success has not removed the claim of the God whose word first opened his way to the throne."], "lesson": "Fear of losing authority can corrupt both public speech and worship. The two rulers' decisions ask whether we are willing to serve and obey when control feels uncertain.", "question": "How does Jeroboam's private fear help explain the religious changes he presents as convenient for the people?"},
  {"id": 93016, "title": "The Prophet Who Turned Back", "subtitle": "Holding to the word already received", "text": "1 Kings 13:1–34", "chapters": [[10, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["A man of God came from Judah to Bethel as Jeroboam stood at his altar. He spoke against it in the Lord's name, announcing that a descendant of David named Josiah would one day defile it with the bones of the shrine's priests. The altar's immediate splitting would confirm the message. Jeroboam stretched out his hand and ordered the prophet seized, but the hand became withered and he could not draw it back. The altar broke and its ashes spilled. At the king's request the man prayed, and his hand was restored. Jeroboam invited him home for refreshment and a reward. He refused, explaining the command not to eat or drink there or return by the same road. He left by another route.", "An older prophet in Bethel heard the report from his sons, saddled his donkey and followed. He found the man resting beneath an oak and invited him home. The man repeated the instruction he had received. The older prophet then claimed equal prophetic standing and said that an angel had told him to bring the visitor back for food and drink. The narrative explicitly identifies that claim as a lie. The man of God accepted it and returned. The earlier refusal of royal hospitality had shown that he knew the command clearly. The second invitation succeeded by presenting itself as a message from the same God whose instruction he had obeyed at the altar.", "During the meal a word of judgment came through the older prophet. Because the visitor had disobeyed the Lord's instruction, he would not be buried in his ancestors' tomb. After he departed, a lion killed him on the road. The animal and the donkey remained beside the body, and passing travellers reported the sight. The older prophet went out and found that the lion had neither consumed the body nor attacked the donkey. He recovered the dead man, brought him back and buried him in his own tomb, mourning him as a brother. The unusual scene on the road confirmed the announced judgment within the story. The prophet who had deceived him now bore witness to the seriousness of the word.", "He told his sons that when he died they should bury him beside the man from Judah, because the declaration against Bethel and the other shrines would certainly be fulfilled. The chapter then returns to Jeroboam. Even after these events he did not abandon his course. He continued appointing shrine priests from those willing to serve, and this sin brought judgment upon his house. The narrative thus joins the warning to the king with the messenger's own responsibility. A true public ministry did not remove the need for personal obedience. Nor did another person's age, title or claim of an angel cancel the instruction already given. The original word remained decisive through the miracle, deception, death and renewed declaration of its fulfilment."], "lesson": "Spiritual claims must be tested by God's word. The older prophet's acknowledged lie warns us against surrendering a clear responsibility merely because another religious voice claims authority.", "question": "Why did the man refuse the king but accept the older prophet, and what should have guided both decisions?"},
  {"id": 93017, "title": "The Disguise Ahijah Saw Through", "subtitle": "Warnings heard in two kingdoms", "text": "1 Kings 14:1–31", "chapters": [[10, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Jeroboam's son Abijah became ill. The king sent his wife in disguise to Ahijah at Shiloh, the prophet who had announced his own rise. She carried bread, cakes and honey and was to ask what would happen to the child. Ahijah was old and blind, but the Lord had already told him who was coming and what to say. Hearing her footsteps, he called her Jeroboam's wife and questioned the pretence. The message recalled God's raising of her husband and the kingdom entrusted to him. He had answered with idolatry and rejection of the Lord. Judgment would remove his house, and its dead would be denied honourable burial.", "The child would die when she reached home. He alone would receive burial and national mourning because something pleasing toward the Lord had been found in him. Ahijah also announced a king who would end Jeroboam's dynasty and a further judgment uprooting Israel and scattering it beyond the river because of its idolatry. Jeroboam's wife returned to Tirzah, and as she crossed the threshold the child died. Israel mourned and buried him according to the word spoken. The account closes Jeroboam's reign by referring to its other records, naming his twenty two years and the succession of Nadab. The hidden visit had supplied no way to hide the king's conduct from God.", "The narrative then turns to Rehoboam in Judah. His mother was Naamah the Ammonite, and he ruled seventeen years in Jerusalem. Judah also provoked the Lord through worship at high places, pillars and sacred poles. The chapter records corrupt practices in the land associated with the peoples formerly driven out. In Rehoboam's fifth year Shishak of Egypt attacked Jerusalem and took the treasures of temple and palace, including Solomon's gold shields. Rehoboam replaced them with bronze shields entrusted to the guards. When he went to the Lord's house they carried them, then returned them to the guardroom. The repeated ceremony now carried a visible sign of diminished splendour.", "War continued between Rehoboam and Jeroboam. Rehoboam died and was buried in David's city, and Abijam succeeded him. The chapter has presented neither kingdom as safe merely because it possesses a different dynasty or place of worship. In the north a disguised queen hears judgment from a blind prophet whom God has informed. In the south gold accumulated during Solomon's glory is carried away, while bronze takes its place in royal routine. Both accounts direct attention from appearances to the people's relationship with the Lord. The death of a child and the loss of national treasures are narrated with their own gravity, while the kings' chosen courses continue shaping what their successors inherit."], "lesson": "A change of appearance cannot conceal wrongdoing from God. The chapter also warns against treating religious identity or continuing ceremony as proof of faithfulness.", "question": "How do the disguise in Shiloh and the replacement shields in Jerusalem each direct attention beneath appearances?"},
  {"id": 93018, "title": "Asa's Reforms and the End of Jeroboam's House", "subtitle": "Different courses within an unsettled land", "text": "1 Kings 15:1–34", "chapters": [[10, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Abijam succeeded Rehoboam and ruled Judah for three years. His heart was not wholly faithful, and the conflict with Jeroboam continued. Yet the Lord preserved a lamp for David in Jerusalem. The account remembers David's general faithfulness while explicitly retaining the grave exception concerning Uriah. Abijam died and was buried in David's city, and Asa followed him. Asa ruled forty one years and acted rightly before the Lord. He removed corrupt religious practices and idols, even deposing Maacah from her position as queen mother because of the image she had made. He destroyed it at the Kidron and brought dedicated silver, gold and vessels into the temple. High places remained, but the narrator recognises the direction of his heart.", "Baasha king of Israel built up Ramah to restrict movement to and from Judah. Asa took the remaining treasures from temple and palace and sent them to Ben-hadad of Damascus. He asked the Aramean ruler to break his agreement with Baasha and attack Israel. Ben-hadad agreed, striking northern towns and territory. Baasha abandoned Ramah, and Asa summoned Judah's people to carry away its timber and stones, using them to fortify Geba and Mizpah. The chapter records the practical success of the policy without adding the fuller prophetic discussion preserved elsewhere. It also notes Asa's later disease in his feet, his death and the succession of Jehoshaphat.", "The northern history moves back to Nadab, Jeroboam's son. He ruled two years and continued the sin of his father. While Israel besieged the Philistine town of Gibbethon, Baasha conspired against him and killed him. Taking the throne, Baasha destroyed Jeroboam's household, fulfilling Ahijah's earlier word. The narrator links the dynasty's fall with the sins through which Jeroboam had led Israel away. The transfer of power, however, did not produce a new course of worship. Baasha reigned twenty four years at Tirzah and walked in Jeroboam's way. A family had been removed while its harmful pattern remained in the government that replaced it.", "The chapter places reforms in Judah beside repeated violence and continuity of sin in Israel. Its dates join the histories of the two kingdoms, preventing each reign from becoming an isolated tale. Asa's removal of an image within his own family shows one kind of costly choice, while the conflict at Ramah displays the pressures of regional politics. Baasha's destruction of Jeroboam's house shows the fulfilment of a warning while exposing his own failure to learn from it. Kings inherit buildings, borders and struggles, but they also make decisions about the practices they continue. The next account will bring another prophetic word to the man who has taken Israel's throne."], "lesson": "Removing a failed leader does not by itself remove the practices that caused harm. Asa's reforms and Baasha's continued sin ask us to examine the direction of our own choices.", "question": "What remains unchanged after Baasha destroys Jeroboam's family, and why does that matter?"},
  {"id": 93019, "title": "The Throne Changes Hands but the Sin Remains", "subtitle": "Power cannot secure a disobedient house", "text": "1 Kings 16:1–34", "chapters": [[10, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["The Lord's word came through Jehu son of Hanani against Baasha. God had raised him from the dust and entrusted him with Israel, yet he had followed Jeroboam and led the people into sin. His household would therefore meet the same end. The account also holds him responsible for the bloodshed through which he had destroyed the earlier family. After his death and burial at Tirzah, Elah succeeded him. Elah's reign lasted two years. While he was drinking heavily in the house of his steward Arza, Zimri, commander of half the chariot force, entered and killed him. Zimri destroyed Baasha's household, including relatives and associates, fulfilling the warning against the dynasty.", "Zimri's own reign lasted only seven days. The army besieging Gibbethon heard of the murder and made its commander Omri king. Omri advanced upon Tirzah and captured it. Zimri retreated into the palace and burned it over himself, dying there. The narrative connects his end with the same evil course by which he had led Israel. The people then divided between Omri and Tibni. Omri's supporters prevailed, Tibni died and Omri secured the throne. The rapid sequence shows that the ability to seize a palace or eliminate a family could not guarantee a lasting rule. Each act of succession stood under the account's repeated judgment concerning worship and conduct.", "Omri ruled twelve years, first at Tirzah. He bought a hill from Shemer, built a city and named it Samaria after its former owner. The new capital became a lasting feature of Israel's history. Yet the narrator says Omri did greater evil than those before him, continuing Jeroboam's path. He died and was buried at Samaria, and Ahab succeeded him. Under Ahab the offence deepened further. He married Jezebel, daughter of Ethbaal king of the Sidonians, served Baal and built him a temple and altar in Samaria. He also made an Asherah. The account treats these acts as more than a continuation of inherited error, naming a further provocation under a newly established house.", "Ahab ruled twenty two years. During his days Hiel of Bethel rebuilt Jericho, losing his firstborn Abiram at the foundation and his youngest Segub at the setting of the gates, according to the word spoken through Joshua. That final notice reaches far back into Israel's memory and keeps the enduring claim of God's word before the reader. The chapter has followed military revolt, murder, civil division and the founding of a capital. Through every change the question of faithfulness remains. Ahab's kingdom now possesses political strength and a new centre of Baal worship. Into that setting the next chapter will introduce Elijah with a word that challenges the king's apparent control over the nation's life."], "lesson": "Changes of ruler and visible development do not settle the question of faithfulness. The repeated warnings ask whether we have learned from earlier failures or merely inherited their habits.", "question": "Why does the narrator return to Jeroboam's pattern when describing rulers who belong to entirely different families?"},
  {"id": 93020, "title": "The Brook, the Widow and the Child Restored", "subtitle": "God's word sustains a household", "text": "1 Kings 17:1–24", "chapters": [[10, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Elijah of Gilead appeared before Ahab and declared that no dew or rain would come except by his word, spoken before the living Lord whom he served. God then directed him to hide beside the brook Cherith. He drank its water while ravens brought bread and meat morning and evening. Elijah obeyed, but in time the brook dried because the land had received no rain. The prophet lived within the consequences of the drought he had announced. His provision had been real, yet its particular place did not remain permanent. A new instruction sent him to Zarephath near Sidon, where a widow would provide for him.", "At the town's gate he found her gathering sticks and asked for water. As she went to fetch it, he also asked for bread. She explained that she had only a handful of flour and a little oil, enough for a final meal for herself and her son before death. Elijah told her not to fear. She should prepare food, first bringing him a small loaf, because the Lord had promised that the flour and oil would last until rain returned. She acted upon the word, and the household ate through many days. The supplies did not fail. The account keeps her poverty visible while locating the continuing provision in God's specific promise through Elijah.", "Later her son became so ill that he stopped breathing. She cried out to Elijah, asking whether his coming had brought her sin to remembrance and caused her son's death. Elijah took the child from her arms to the upper room where he stayed. He laid him on the bed and cried to the Lord about the disaster in the home that had sheltered him. Stretching himself upon the child three times, he pleaded for life to return. The Lord heard, and the child revived. Elijah carried him downstairs and gave him back to his mother alive. The prophet's prayer had included distress and direct pleading, rather than an explanation offered at a safe distance from her grief.", "The woman then affirmed that Elijah was a man of God and that the Lord's word in his mouth was true. Her statement closes a chapter that began with that word before a king and followed it into places of hidden need. Water, bread, oil, illness and a mother's cry have become its setting. The account gives no general right to demand a poor person's last resources, nor a formula guaranteeing the same outcome in every bereavement. It tells what God promised and did within this household. The life of the prophet and the lives of those who received him have been sustained by the Lord whose authority Ahab would soon be called to confront publicly."], "lesson": "God's faithfulness is shown amid real need and grief. Elijah's response to the mother's distress invites compassionate involvement and prayer that does not dismiss the suffering before us.", "question": "How does the widow's final statement connect the provision of food with the restoration of her son?"},
  {"id": 93021, "title": "Obadiah Meets the Man Ahab Has Been Seeking", "subtitle": "Faithfulness in a dangerous household", "text": "1 Kings 18:1–19", "chapters": [[10, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["In the third year the Lord told Elijah to present himself to Ahab, promising rain. Famine was severe in Samaria. Ahab called Obadiah, the officer responsible for his household, and arranged a search through springs and streams for enough grass to keep the horses and mules alive. They divided the territory and went separate ways. The narrator identifies Obadiah as a man who greatly feared the Lord. When Jezebel had attacked the Lord's prophets, he had hidden a hundred of them in two caves and supplied bread and water. His service within the palace had therefore included a secret, costly protection of people whom the queen wanted destroyed.", "As Obadiah travelled, Elijah met him. He recognised the prophet, bowed and asked whether it was truly he. Elijah told him to announce his presence to Ahab. Obadiah feared the command would bring his death. The king had searched widely for Elijah and demanded oaths from kingdoms that said he was not there. If Obadiah reported finding him and the Spirit carried Elijah elsewhere, Ahab would kill the messenger who could not produce him. He recalled his lifelong fear of the Lord and his care for the hidden prophets. His protest revealed the danger under which faithful conduct had already been practised, rather than a simple refusal to acknowledge God.", "Elijah swore before the living Lord of hosts that he would show himself to Ahab that day. Obadiah then went to report, and the king came. Ahab greeted Elijah as the one troubling Israel. Elijah answered by locating the trouble in the king's abandonment of God's commands and his family's service of the Baals. The drought had brought a struggle over responsibility into public speech. Ahab blamed the messenger who announced it; Elijah named the disobedience against which the word had been spoken. Obadiah's feared meeting had become the point at which the ruler and prophet stood face to face.", "Elijah required the gathering of Israel at Mount Carmel together with the four hundred and fifty prophets of Baal and the four hundred associated with Asherah who ate at Jezebel's table. The instruction would move the confrontation beyond the two men. The people whose life had been affected by famine would hear the question of their allegiance. Before that public scene, the passage has given space to a servant whose hidden courage preserved a hundred lives. His experience also corrects any assumption that all faithfulness must look like Elijah's open challenge. The prophet stands before Ahab because God sent him, while Obadiah has already risked his position and safety to feed those who needed protection."], "lesson": "Faithfulness can involve public witness or quiet, sustained protection of others. Obadiah's fear deserves to be read alongside the costly service he had already given.", "question": "What do Obadiah's actions reveal about the risks faced by a person serving God within a hostile institution?"},
  {"id": 93022, "title": "The Fire Falls and the Rain Returns", "subtitle": "A people called to choose whom they will follow", "text": "1 Kings 18:20–46", "chapters": [[10, 18]], "start": 20, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Ahab assembled Israel and the prophets at Carmel. Elijah asked the people how long they would waver between two loyalties. If the Lord was God, they should follow Him; if Baal, they should follow him. They gave no answer. Elijah proposed two sacrifices without fire beneath either, one prepared by Baal's prophets and one by himself. The God who answered with fire would be acknowledged. The people agreed. Baal's prophets called from morning until noon, leaping around their altar. No answer came. Elijah mocked the silence, and their cries grew more intense. They injured themselves according to their practice and continued through the afternoon, but the narrator repeats that no voice or response was heard.", "Elijah called the people near and repaired the Lord's broken altar. He used twelve stones, recalling Jacob's sons and the name Israel given by God's word. He arranged wood and the sacrifice, dug a trench and ordered water poured over everything three times. At the time of the evening offering he prayed to the God of Abraham, Isaac and Israel. He asked that the people know the Lord was God, that he was His servant and that he had acted at His word. He also asked that their hearts be turned back. Fire fell, consuming sacrifice, wood, stones, dust and the water. The people fell down and confessed the Lord as God.", "Elijah ordered Baal's prophets seized, and they were taken to the Kishon and killed. The account includes that severe judgment within the confrontation, not merely the dramatic answer at the altar. Elijah then told Ahab to eat and drink because rain was coming. While the king did so, the prophet climbed Carmel, bent to the ground and placed his face between his knees. He sent his servant to look toward the sea. The first report was that nothing could be seen. Elijah sent him back repeatedly. On the seventh return the servant reported a cloud no larger than a man's hand rising from the sea.", "Elijah told him to warn Ahab to harness the chariot and leave before the rain stopped him. Soon the sky darkened with wind and cloud, and heavy rain fell. Ahab drove to Jezreel. The Lord's hand came upon Elijah, and he gathered his clothing and ran ahead to the city's entrance. The promised rain had followed the public confession, but the story's opening question still required a lasting response. A people who had remained silent had spoken before the fire. The continuing conduct of king, queen and nation would show how they received that revelation. Elijah's next experience would expose how little a single public victory guarantees an end to danger or discouragement."], "lesson": "A clear confession calls for continuing allegiance. The prayer at Carmel directs attention to God's truth and the turning of hearts, beyond the desire to witness an impressive event.", "question": "How does Elijah's prayer explain the purpose of the sign the people are about to see?"},
  {"id": 93023, "title": "Food in the Wilderness and a Voice at Horeb", "subtitle": "God meets a weary servant and gives him work", "text": "1 Kings 19:1–21", "chapters": [[10, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Ahab reported the events of Carmel to Jezebel, including the death of the prophets. She threatened Elijah with the same fate within a day. He fled to Beersheba, left his servant there and went a day's journey into the wilderness. Beneath a broom tree he asked the Lord to take his life, saying he had had enough and was no better than his ancestors. Then he slept. An angel woke him to eat bread baked on hot stones and drink water. He ate and lay down again. A second waking brought more food and the explanation that the journey was too great for him. Strengthened, he travelled forty days and nights to Horeb.", "In a cave there the Lord asked what he was doing. Elijah spoke of his zeal, Israel's broken covenant, destroyed altars and murdered prophets. He felt alone and pursued. God directed him to stand before Him. A great wind broke rocks, then came earthquake and fire, but the Lord was not in those manifestations. After the fire Elijah heard a quiet voice. He covered his face with his mantle and stood at the cave's entrance. The question was repeated, and he repeated his account. The narrative gives his complaint room to be heard after the care that restored his strength. His distress is not dismissed as though the earlier fire on Carmel had made it impossible.", "The Lord sent him back with instructions involving Hazael as king of Aram, Jehu as king of Israel and Elisha as his prophetic successor. Judgment would continue through agents beyond Elijah himself. God also told him that seven thousand in Israel had not bowed to Baal or kissed him. His sense of being the last faithful person did not describe all that God knew. A future and a people remained beyond his sight. The instructions did not require him to sustain the whole struggle alone or believe that the covenant's future rested upon his endurance. He had a task to carry out within work God would continue through others.", "Elijah found Elisha son of Shaphat ploughing with twelve teams of oxen and cast his mantle over him. Elisha left the animals and asked to farewell his parents before following. He then slaughtered a pair of oxen, used their equipment to cook the meat and shared it with the people. Afterward he went with Elijah and served him. The chapter ends with companionship and succession beginning. Its path has moved through threat, flight, a plea for death, sleep, food, revelation and renewed direction. The Lord's care addressed bodily exhaustion as well as Elijah's understanding of his situation. The prophet leaves with work still to do and a servant beside him, while the unseen faithful remain part of the nation's future."], "lesson": "Weariness calls for care as well as renewed understanding. Elijah's experience invites us to receive help and remember that God's work includes people and purposes beyond our own view.", "question": "How do the food, the word about seven thousand and Elisha's arrival each answer a different part of Elijah's distress?"},
  {"id": 93024, "title": "The Wounded Prophet Waits Beside the Road", "subtitle": "Deliverance brings responsibility", "text": "1 Kings 20:1–43", "chapters": [[10, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Ben-hadad of Aram besieged Samaria with thirty two allied kings. He first demanded Ahab's wealth and family, then insisted that servants search the houses and take whatever pleased them. Ahab consulted the elders and refused the expanded demand. Threats followed, answered by Ahab's warning against boasting before a battle was won. A prophet then promised that the Lord would deliver the great army so Ahab would know Him. Two hundred and thirty two young officers would lead, followed by seven thousand Israelites. While Ben-hadad and his allies drank, Israel attacked. The Arameans fled, and their king escaped. The prophet warned Ahab to prepare because the enemy would return the next year.", "Ben-hadad's advisers claimed that Israel's gods belonged to the hills and could be defeated on the plain. They replaced the allied kings with commanders and rebuilt the army. At Aphek Israel appeared like two small flocks beside a force filling the country. A man of God announced another victory because the enemy had denied the Lord's authority in the valleys. After seven days the battle brought a great slaughter, and a wall collapsed upon fugitives within Aphek. Ben-hadad hid in an inner room. His servants approached Ahab in signs of submission, hoping for mercy. Ahab called the defeated ruler his brother, received him into his chariot and agreed to a treaty promising restored towns and trading rights in Damascus. He let him go.", "A member of the prophetic company then asked another man, at the Lord's word, to strike him. When the man refused, the prophet announced that a lion would kill him; the event followed. A second man struck and wounded the prophet as requested. Disguising himself, he waited for Ahab and presented a case about a prisoner entrusted to him under penalty of death or a heavy payment if the man escaped. He had been busy elsewhere, he said, and the prisoner was gone. Ahab declared that the speaker had pronounced his own judgment. The prophet then removed the disguise, and the king recognised him. The staged case had drawn a verdict from the very ruler whose conduct was being challenged.", "The prophet announced that because Ahab had released a man appointed for destruction, his own life and people would answer for the other king's. Ahab returned to Samaria sullen and displeased. The chapter has shown repeated deliverance given so the king would know the Lord, followed by a treaty made as though the outcome belonged to his own discretion. Its warning concerns the responsibility attached to receiving God's help. The wounded messenger's case makes that responsibility personal by requiring Ahab to judge an entrusted task. Great victories have not made him willing to receive the word that exposes his later decision. The next story will again find him displeased, this time over a neighbour's refusal to surrender a vineyard."], "lesson": "Help received does not remove accountability for what follows. The prophet's case asks how we handle responsibilities and limits entrusted to us after success.", "question": "How does the story of the escaped prisoner bring Ahab's own decision before him for judgment?"},
  {"id": 93025, "title": "Naboth's Vineyard and the Letters with the King's Seal", "subtitle": "A ruler's desire becomes another man's death", "text": "1 Kings 21:1–29", "chapters": [[10, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["Naboth owned a vineyard beside Ahab's palace at Jezreel. The king wanted it for a vegetable garden and offered a better vineyard or payment. Naboth refused because it was his ancestral inheritance before the Lord. Ahab returned home resentful, turned his face away and would not eat. Jezebel asked why he was distressed. When he explained, she challenged his exercise of kingship and promised to obtain the vineyard. The account begins with a boundary that a neighbour's conviction has placed before a ruler's desire. Ahab has heard the refusal, but his response makes that neighbour's property into a grievance within the palace.", "Jezebel wrote letters in Ahab's name, sealed them with his seal and sent them to Naboth's elders and nobles. They were to proclaim a fast, place Naboth prominently and arrange two witnesses to accuse him of cursing God and the king. Then he was to be stoned. The local leaders obeyed. False witnesses gave the accusation publicly, and Naboth was taken outside and killed. The officials reported his death to Jezebel. She told Ahab to take possession, and he went down to the vineyard. Sacred language, civic office, legal appearance and royal authority had all been used to remove an innocent owner. The account lets the reader see the arrangement hidden behind the resulting possession.", "The Lord sent Elijah to meet Ahab there. The message asked whether he had killed and also taken possession. It announced that dogs would lick his blood as they had Naboth's. Ahab called Elijah his enemy. The prophet answered that he had found him because he had sold himself to do evil. Judgment would remove his house like those of Jeroboam and Baasha, and Jezebel herself would meet a shameful death. The narrative underscores the evil of Ahab's idolatry and Jezebel's influence while keeping him responsible for what had been done through his name and for his benefit. Standing in the acquired vineyard did not separate him from the murder that had made it available.", "When Ahab heard, he tore his clothes, put on sackcloth, fasted and moved in a humbled manner. God directed Elijah's attention to that response and said the full disaster upon the dynasty would come in his son's days rather than his own. The chapter ends with a merciful delay, while Naboth remains dead and the announced judgment remains real. Ahab's humbling is acknowledged without calling the earlier transaction innocent. The prophet had met him at the very place where desire had become possession. That meeting exposed a ruler's responsibility beneath the actions of queen, elders and witnesses, and it brought the Lord's word into a scene the king had expected to enjoy as a private gain."], "lesson": "Coveting can draw institutions and other people into grave injustice. The vineyard account calls us to respect another person's rightful limits and answer for wrongdoing carried out for our advantage.", "question": "Why does the Lord confront Ahab in the vineyard even though Jezebel wrote the letters?"},
  {"id": 93026, "title": "The Warning Ahab Put in Prison", "subtitle": "The word outlasts the king's disguise", "text": "1 Kings 22:1–53", "chapters": [[10, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Kings", "story": ["After three years without war, Jehoshaphat of Judah visited Ahab. Ahab proposed recovering Ramoth-gilead from Aram, and Jehoshaphat agreed to help while asking that they seek the Lord's word. About four hundred prophets promised victory. Jehoshaphat asked for another prophet of the Lord, and Ahab named Micaiah, whom he disliked because his messages were unfavourable. As the kings sat in royal robes, Zedekiah displayed iron horns and the prophets repeated their promises. The messenger urged Micaiah to agree with them. He answered that he would speak what the Lord told him. At first he repeated the favourable formula, but when pressed for truth he described Israel scattered like sheep without a shepherd. Ahab immediately treated the warning as proof of the prophet's hostility.", "Micaiah then told of a heavenly council in which a lying spirit would lead Ahab through his prophets toward defeat. He openly identified the assurances being heard as part of that judgment. Zedekiah struck him and mocked his claim to speak by the Spirit. Ahab ordered him imprisoned upon meagre food until the king returned safely. Micaiah answered that a safe return would prove the Lord had not spoken through him and called the people to witness. The warning was therefore delivered plainly before the campaign. The king chose confinement for its bearer while continuing toward the battle whose danger had just been named.", "Ahab disguised himself, leaving Jehoshaphat in royal clothing. Aram's chariot commanders had orders to attack Israel's king. They pursued Jehoshaphat until his cry and their recognition showed that he was the wrong man. Meanwhile an archer shot without selecting Ahab and struck him between the joints of his armour. Wounded, he asked his driver to take him out, but he remained supported in the chariot as the battle continued and died by evening. The army dispersed. His body was brought to Samaria and buried, and dogs licked his blood when the chariot was washed, fulfilling the word spoken. His ivory palace and other achievements receive mention, but they cannot change the outcome of the warning. Ahaziah succeeded him.", "The book closes with notices of both kingdoms. Jehoshaphat had followed Asa's generally faithful course, though the high places remained. He made peace with Israel and removed corrupt practices left from his father's days. Edom was governed by a deputy, and Jehoshaphat's ships intended for Ophir were wrecked at Ezion-geber. He declined Ahaziah's offer to share a later venture. After his death and burial, Jehoram succeeded him. In Israel Ahaziah ruled two years, continuing the ways of Ahab, Jezebel and Jeroboam and serving Baal. The closing notices carry the narrative beyond Ahab's death without resolving the nation's course. A warning could be imprisoned with its messenger, but the king could not make its truth depend upon whether he wished to hear it. The next book will continue among rulers still facing the word they inherit."], "lesson": "Truth must be received according to its faithfulness, even when it challenges a desired course. Ahab's disguise shows the limits of changing appearances while refusing the warning itself.", "question": "How do Micaiah's public warning and Ahab's attempted disguise together explain the outcome of the battle?"},
  {"id": 93500, "title": "The King Sends to Ekron", "subtitle": "A royal command meets God's word", "text": "2 Kings 1:1–18", "chapters": [[11, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["After Ahab's death, Moab rebelled against Israel. Ahaziah also suffered a serious fall through the lattice of an upper room in Samaria. Ill and anxious about recovery, he sent messengers to enquire of Baal-zebub, the god of Ekron. The angel of the Lord directed Elijah to intercept them. His question challenged the meaning of their journey: was there no God in Israel that they must seek this answer elsewhere? The message announced that Ahaziah would not leave his bed alive. The messengers returned before reaching their destination, and the surprised king asked why. Their report repeated the warning, and their description of the man in a rough garment and leather belt enabled Ahaziah to recognise Elijah.", "The king sent a commander with fifty men to bring the prophet down from a hill. The officer addressed him as a man of God while issuing the king's order. Elijah answered that if he was a man of God, fire should come down and consume the company. It did. Another commander arrived with the same demand, now urging speed, and the second company met the same end. The account presents a severe confrontation over authority. Ahaziah continued trying to summon the bearer of the word as though possession of the throne could place the message beneath his command. The two failed approaches did not bring the prophet into royal custody.", "A third commander came, knelt and pleaded that his own life and the lives of his men be valued. He recalled what had happened to the others. The angel then told Elijah to go with him without fear. Elijah entered the king's presence and repeated the same word concerning the enquiry at Ekron and Ahaziah's coming death. The manner of the third approach had changed, but the content of the divine answer had not. A humbled plea for lives brought a different meeting from the demands of the earlier officers, while the king still had to hear the warning originally sent to him.", "Ahaziah died according to the word spoken through Elijah. Because he had no son, Jehoram succeeded him, and the narrator directs attention to further records of his reign. The opening of the book therefore continues the struggle over God's word that closed the earlier history. A wounded king sought assurance from a foreign shrine, recognised the prophet who interrupted the journey and tried repeatedly to compel his attendance. The final audience supplied the answer already given. Its fulfilment brings the reign to an end, leaving another ruler upon the throne and the question of Israel's faithfulness still before the reader."], "lesson": "Seeking guidance calls for a willingness to hear the God whose word already addresses us. The third commander's plea also directs attention to humility when lives are at stake.", "question": "How do the three commanders' approaches illuminate the difference between a demand and a plea?"},
  {"id": 93501, "title": "Elisha Picks Up the Fallen Mantle", "subtitle": "The servant continues after his master's departure", "text": "2 Kings 2:1–25", "chapters": [[11, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Elijah travelled from Gilgal toward Bethel, and Elisha refused his repeated offers to remain behind. At Bethel and then Jericho, prophetic companions told Elisha that the Lord would take his master that day. He knew and asked them to be quiet. The two went on to the Jordan while fifty others watched from a distance. Elijah rolled his mantle and struck the river, and the waters parted so they crossed on dry ground. He then invited Elisha to ask what he might receive before their separation. Elisha requested a double portion of his spirit. Elijah called it a difficult request and connected its granting with Elisha seeing his departure.", "As they walked and talked, fiery horses and a chariot separated them, and Elijah went up into heaven in a whirlwind. Elisha saw and cried out to the man he called his father and Israel's chariot and horsemen. Then he tore his clothes in grief. He gathered Elijah's fallen mantle and returned to the Jordan. Asking where the Lord, the God of Elijah, was, he struck the water, which again divided. The watching prophets recognised that Elijah's spirit rested upon him. The master's departure had brought sorrow, but the returned crossing publicly marked the continuation of the work. The power did not belong to the discarded garment by itself; Elisha's cry named the God he sought.", "The companions urged a search in case the Spirit had set Elijah upon a mountain or in a valley. Elisha first refused, then yielded under their insistence. Fifty men searched for three days and found nothing. Back at Jericho, the townspeople brought another need: their location was pleasant, but the water was bad and the land unfruitful. Elisha requested a new bowl with salt, went to the spring and announced the Lord's healing of the waters. The account records their continuing restoration. The beginning of his recognised ministry thus includes both a correction of the companions' assumption and an act addressing the life of a town.", "On his way toward Bethel, a group of young people mocked him with taunts about his baldness and urged him to go up. He pronounced a curse in the Lord's name, and two bears came from the woods and mauled forty two of them. The narrative retains this severe judgment alongside the healed spring. Elisha continued to Carmel and then Samaria. The chapter ends with his movement into the places where his work will unfold. His succession has been witnessed, yet the stories that follow will continue revealing what it means to receive or reject the prophetic word. The recognition given at the river does not make every encounter gentle or every response faithful."], "lesson": "God's work continues through servants who depend upon Him. Elisha's grief and renewed crossing invite us to hold loss, responsibility and trust together during a change of leadership.", "question": "Why does Elisha's question at the Jordan matter more than the possession of Elijah's mantle alone?"},
  {"id": 93502, "title": "Water Appears in the Dry Valley", "subtitle": "Deliverance within a troubled campaign", "text": "2 Kings 3:1–27", "chapters": [[11, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Jehoram of Israel removed the image of Baal his father had made, yet continued Jeroboam's sins. Moab's king Mesha, formerly a major supplier of sheep and wool, rebelled after Ahab died. Jehoram gathered Israel and asked Jehoshaphat of Judah to join him. With the king of Edom they took a long route through the wilderness. After seven days there was no water for men or animals. Jehoram declared that the Lord had brought the three kings together for destruction. Jehoshaphat asked for a prophet through whom they could enquire, and a servant identified Elisha, remembered as the man who had attended Elijah. The kings went to him in their need.", "Elisha challenged Jehoram to consult his parents' prophets. He said that he would scarcely consider him were it not for Jehoshaphat's presence. After requesting a musician, Elisha received the Lord's word. The valley was to be prepared with ditches. Without visible wind or rain, it would fill with water sufficient for the company and livestock. Victory over Moab was also announced, together with the destruction of fortified towns, trees, wells and cultivated ground. In the morning, at the time of the offering, water came from the direction of Edom and filled the land. The exhausted force received what it could not provide for itself.", "The Moabites had assembled at their border. Morning sunlight made the water appear red, and they concluded that the allied kings had fought and killed one another. Expecting plunder, they rushed toward the camp and encountered an army ready to rise against them. Israel pursued them into Moab, destroying towns, stopping springs, felling trees and covering fields with stones. The fighting reached Kir-hareseth. Its walls remained under attack by slingers, and Mesha attempted to break through toward Edom with seven hundred swordsmen. The attempt failed. The campaign's promised victory unfolded through a terrible devastation of the country, which the narrative records together with the initial provision of water.", "In desperation Mesha offered his eldest son, the intended heir, as a burnt offering upon the wall. Great wrath arose against Israel, and the allies withdrew to their own land. The text does not fully explain the source or workings of that wrath, and the ending must be allowed its gravity. The chapter began with a mixed record of reform and continued sin, moved through military alliance and urgent enquiry, and closes with a child's death and an abandoned campaign. Receiving help had not turned the events into an uncomplicated celebration. The reader is left with the power of the Lord's word, the limits of the kings and the suffering through which their conflict has passed."], "lesson": "Need can reveal the limits of military confidence. The severe ending also calls us to read accounts of victory with attention to the human lives affected by war.", "question": "How do the opening crisis and the final withdrawal complicate the kings' expectations for the campaign?"},
  {"id": 93503, "title": "The Widow Borrows Empty Jars", "subtitle": "Provision that protects a household", "text": "2 Kings 4:1–7", "chapters": [[11, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["A widow from the prophetic community cried to Elisha. Her husband had died, and a creditor was coming to take her two sons into debt service. She reminded the prophet that her husband had feared the Lord. His death had left both grief and an obligation the household could not meet. The danger concerned the children's freedom as well as the loss of property. Her appeal named the need directly. The narrator gives no account of a hidden fault that produced it, and Elisha does not begin by accusing her. He asks what he can do and what she has in the house. She reports only a small jar of oil.", "Elisha tells her to borrow empty containers from her neighbours and gather many. Then she is to close the door with her sons and pour oil into the vessels, setting each full one aside. The instruction brings the neighbours' ordinary possessions into the response to her crisis. The household's small resource will be used within a task that also requires their cooperation. The sons endangered by the debt become active participants in carrying out the direction. The account remains close to these practical details, recording what the woman is told to gather and what she does once she returns home.", "She leaves, closes the door and begins pouring while her sons bring the containers. Each fills. When she asks for another, a son answers that none remain, and the oil stops. The narrative locates the provision within the home, away from a public display. It has become abundant enough to fill the borrowed vessels, although it began with the little she could name in answer to Elisha's question. The point at which the vessels are exhausted marks the completion of the act. Mother and sons can see the result together, and the woman returns to report what has happened. The urgent appeal has been answered through a provision she now has responsibility to use.", "Elisha tells her to sell the oil, pay the debt and support herself and her children with what remains. His final direction identifies the purpose of the abundance. The creditor's claim can be met, the sons can remain with their mother and the household can continue living. The passage is short, but its movement is complete: a threatened family cries for help, the existing resource is named, neighbours' jars are borrowed, the children assist and the resulting goods meet the obligation. The story's concern reaches beyond the wonder of oil flowing to the security of people who might otherwise be divided by debt. Provision takes the shape of freedom and continuing care within the family."], "lesson": "Compassion listens to a person's concrete need and seeks a response that protects life together. The widow's account invites attention to households whose obligations threaten their security.", "question": "Why is Elisha's direction to pay the debt and live on the remainder essential to understanding the provision?"},
  {"id": 93504, "title": "The Room at Shunem and the Son Returned", "subtitle": "Hospitality, promise and a mother's persistence", "text": "2 Kings 4:8–37", "chapters": [[11, 4]], "start": 8, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["A woman of standing in Shunem regularly invited Elisha to eat when he passed. Recognising him as a holy man of God, she proposed that her husband prepare an upper room with a bed, table, chair and lamp. Elisha stayed there and wanted to acknowledge her care. Through Gehazi he offered to speak for her to the king or army commander. She replied that she lived among her own people. Gehazi noted that she had no son and that her husband was old. Elisha called her and promised a child within a year. She asked him not to raise a false hope. The promised son was born at the appointed time.", "As the boy grew, he went to his father among the reapers and suddenly complained of his head. A servant carried him to his mother, and he died upon her lap at noon. She laid him on Elisha's bed, shut the door and arranged an immediate journey to Carmel. Her husband wondered why she was going outside the usual days of worship, but she pressed on. Elisha saw her approaching and sent Gehazi to ask after the family. She continued to the prophet and grasped his feet. When Gehazi tried to push her away, Elisha stopped him. Her distress was great, and the Lord had not disclosed its cause to him.", "She reminded him that she had not asked for a son and had warned against deception. Elisha sent Gehazi ahead with his staff and instructions for urgency. The mother refused to leave without Elisha, who rose and followed. Gehazi placed the staff upon the child, but there was no response. Meeting the others, he reported the failure. The account makes room for that result and for Elisha's earlier admission of limited knowledge. The mother's pain has brought a question to the promise she received, and the prophet must accompany her to the room she once prepared for his rest. The events cannot be settled merely by sending a familiar object ahead.", "Elisha entered, closed the door and prayed. He stretched himself upon the child, and warmth returned. After walking through the house, he did so again. The child sneezed seven times and opened his eyes. Elisha called the woman and told her to take her son. She bowed at his feet, lifted the child and went out. The story ends with the son in her care again. Its path has included generous hospitality, an unexpected promise, terrible grief, determined appeal and prayer in the same room. The woman's persistence has been heard rather than dismissed, and Elisha's dependence upon the Lord has remained visible through the uncertainty and the final restoration."], "lesson": "Care for those in distress includes listening, remaining present and praying without pretending to know everything. The mother's persistence calls attention to the promise and the pain she carries.", "question": "How do Elisha's admission and his willingness to follow the mother shape his response to her grief?"},
  {"id": 93505, "title": "Food Shared in a Time of Famine", "subtitle": "The community receives what sustains it", "text": "2 Kings 4:38–44", "chapters": [[11, 4]], "start": 38, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Elisha returned to Gilgal during a famine. The prophetic community sat before him, and he told his servant to put a large pot on the fire and prepare a meal. One member went into the fields to gather herbs. He found a wild vine, collected gourds and cut them into the stew without recognising what they were. The account places the mistake within an ordinary effort to feed people when food was scarce. Work that seemed helpful introduced a danger none of the group had intended. The meal was served, and those who tasted it cried that death was in the pot. They could not continue eating.", "Elisha asked for flour and put it into the pot, then directed that the food be served again. The narrator states that the harmful substance was gone. The threatened meal became usable for the community. The act is told as part of the Lord's provision through Elisha, with no general cooking method offered for making unknown plants safe. The people had a genuine need to eat, and a danger within their own preparation had brought it into sharper focus. The story remembers both the mistake and the help received. The meal could continue, and the gathered men were sustained in the famine.", "Another man came from Baal-shalishah bringing firstfruits: twenty barley loaves and fresh grain. Elisha told the attendant to give it to the people. The servant questioned how that amount could be set before a hundred men. His response counted the food against the number who needed it. Elisha repeated the instruction and declared the Lord's word that they would eat and have some left. The gift had already been brought; now the question concerned how it would be shared. The narrator directs attention to the promised sufficiency beyond the servant's first assessment. The food was not to remain with the prophet while the larger group went hungry.", "The attendant distributed it. The men ate, and food remained, according to the Lord's word. The chapter's closing accounts therefore bring provision into the life of a whole community after the earlier stories of individual households. In one scene an intended meal was dangerous; in the other a welcome gift appeared too small. Both end with people eating. Gatherers, donors, servants and the prophet each have a place within the events. Their ordinary acts of collecting, bringing, preparing and serving become part of the remembered help. The result is directed toward the needs of the gathered people, whose hunger gives the wonders their human setting."], "lesson": "A gift can become provision through a willingness to share and serve. These accounts invite gratitude for those who bring resources and those who distribute them among people in need.", "question": "What do the two food accounts reveal about the purpose of the help given through Elisha?"},
  {"id": 93506, "title": "Naaman Goes Down to the Jordan", "subtitle": "Receiving a gift and refusing a profit", "text": "2 Kings 5:1–27", "chapters": [[11, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Naaman was a respected commander through whom the Lord had given Aram victories, but he suffered from a serious skin disease. A young Israelite taken captive in a raid served his wife. She spoke of the prophet in Samaria who could restore him. Her words reached the king, who sent Naaman with a letter and rich gifts. Israel's king read the request for healing as a provocation and tore his clothes. Elisha heard and invited Naaman to come so he would know there was a prophet in Israel. The road toward help began with the testimony of a displaced servant whose position gave her little public importance.", "Naaman arrived with horses and chariot, expecting a personal appearance and an impressive act. Elisha instead sent a messenger telling him to wash seven times in the Jordan. Naaman was angry. He named the rivers of Damascus and objected to the simplicity of the direction. His servants approached respectfully and reasoned that he would have attempted something difficult if commanded. Why refuse the offer to wash and be clean? He listened, went down and immersed himself seven times. His skin became like a child's, and he was restored. The commander had needed to receive advice from people beneath his rank and act upon a word that offended his expectations.", "Returning, Naaman confessed that the God of Israel alone was God and offered a gift. Elisha refused payment despite his insistence. Naaman then requested earth to take home and declared that he would offer sacrifices only to the Lord. He also raised the difficulty of attending his master in Rimmon's temple and asked for pardon concerning that duty. Elisha sent him in peace. Gehazi, however, pursued him and falsely claimed that Elisha needed silver and clothing for visiting prophets. Naaman generously supplied more than requested. Gehazi hid the goods and returned, denying to Elisha that he had gone anywhere.", "Elisha revealed knowledge of the meeting and challenged whether this was a time for acquiring money, property and servants. Naaman's disease would cling to Gehazi and his descendants. Gehazi left diseased. The chapter therefore sets two responses to the same healing beside one another. Naaman moves through offended pride toward obedience and gratitude, while the prophet's servant turns an unpurchased gift into a chance for private gain. The young captive and the commander's attendants speak words that lead toward restoration; Gehazi speaks a lie that serves himself. Rank and proximity to a prophet do not determine who listens faithfully. The outcomes turn upon how the people receive and act upon the word before them."], "lesson": "Humility can make us willing to hear helpful truth from unexpected people. Gehazi's conduct also warns against using another person's gratitude as an opportunity for dishonest gain.", "question": "How do the different servants in the chapter use the influence available to them?"},
  {"id": 93507, "title": "The Lost Axe and the Army the Servant Could Not See", "subtitle": "Help for need and restraint toward enemies", "text": "2 Kings 6:1–23", "chapters": [[11, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["The prophetic community needed more room and asked to build near the Jordan, each member cutting timber. Elisha agreed and accompanied them. As one man worked, his borrowed axe head fell into the water. He cried out over the loss. Elisha asked where it fell, cut a stick and threw it there, and the iron floated. The man reached out and recovered it. The short account brings attention to a practical burden within shared work. The borrowed tool mattered to the person responsible for it. Help given through the prophet reached an ordinary loss before the narrative turned toward the movements of kings and armies.", "Aram's king planned attacks against Israel, but Elisha repeatedly warned Israel's ruler where danger lay. Suspecting a traitor, the Aramean king questioned his officers. One explained that Elisha disclosed even words spoken privately in the royal chamber. Learning that the prophet was at Dothan, the king sent a strong force by night to surround it. Elisha's servant rose and saw horses and chariots enclosing the town. He asked in alarm what they could do. Elisha told him that those with them were more numerous and prayed for his eyes to be opened. The servant then saw the surrounding hills filled with horses and chariots of fire.", "As the enemy advanced, Elisha prayed that they be struck with blindness. He led the affected force to Samaria and then prayed for their sight to return. They found themselves within the city they had threatened. Israel's king eagerly asked whether he should kill them. Elisha forbade it and directed that food and water be set before them so they could return to their master. A great meal was prepared. After eating and drinking, the men were released, and those raiding bands ceased entering Israel. The account ends this encounter with hospitality toward an enemy force whose capture could have become an occasion for slaughter.", "The two episodes differ greatly in scale, yet each exposes a limit in what the people first see. A worker sees a tool beyond recovery, and a servant sees an army with no apparent escape. Elisha's responses direct both situations toward the Lord's help. In Samaria that help also places responsibility upon the king. The enemy's helplessness becomes a reason for feeding and release. The narrative therefore connects deliverance with the way power is used afterward. The recovered axe returns a borrowed possession to its user's care, while the opened eyes reveal a danger transformed into a table. The next conflict with Aram will bring a different and much harsher situation to the city."], "lesson": "God's help can address both daily burdens and overwhelming danger. The meal at Samaria asks how we treat people when the balance of power turns in our favour.", "question": "Why is Elisha's instruction to feed the captives important to the meaning of their defeat?"},
  {"id": 93508, "title": "Four Men Find a Camp Without Soldiers", "subtitle": "Good news must be carried to the hungry", "text": "2 Kings 6:24–7:20", "chapters": [[11, 6], [11, 7]], "start": 24, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Ben-hadad gathered his army and besieged Samaria. Food became desperately costly, and the famine grew so terrible that a woman told the king of a child killed and eaten. Hearing her accusation against another mother, he tore his clothes, revealing sackcloth underneath. Yet he also swore to take Elisha's life. The prophet warned the elders sitting with him to bar the arriving messenger, with the king following. A complaint then questioned the value of waiting longer for the Lord. Elisha answered with a promise: by the next day flour and barley would sell cheaply at the city gate. A royal officer mocked the possibility, and Elisha told him he would see it but would not eat.", "Four men with skin disease sat outside the gate, reasoning that remaining or entering the starving city meant death. They decided to surrender to the Arameans, where some hope remained. At dusk they found the camp abandoned. The Lord had caused its soldiers to hear a mighty army, and they had fled, imagining allied forces approaching. The four entered tents, ate, drank and carried valuables away to hide. Then they challenged their own silence. This was a day of good news, and they ought to report it. They returned to the gate and told the keepers what they had found. Men excluded from the city's ordinary life became the bearers of news that could sustain it.", "The king suspected a trap. He thought the enemy had hidden nearby to seize the hungry people when they came out. A servant suggested sending the few remaining horses to investigate, since the situation already offered little security. Scouts followed the enemy's route toward the Jordan and found clothing and equipment discarded in flight. They returned with confirmation. The people rushed out and plundered the camp. Flour and barley became available at the prices Elisha had announced. Relief arrived through a fear among the besiegers, a discovery by four desperate men and a report tested by scouts. The king had imagined hidden danger, while the road held evidence of a completed flight.", "The sceptical officer was placed in charge of the gate. In the rush he was trampled to death, fulfilling the word that he would see the plenty but not share it. The account repeats the earlier exchange to connect the promise and its outcome. The famine's horror remains part of the story even after the camp is opened. The provision does not undo the lives already lost. It does, however, give the remaining people a future beyond the siege. The four men's decision to speak becomes central to that turning point. Food they could first enjoy privately had become news they were responsible to carry to others still facing hunger behind the walls."], "lesson": "Good received can create a responsibility to share it. The four men recognise that their relief belongs within the wider need of the city.", "question": "What changes when the men stop hiding valuables and decide to report what they have found?"},
  {"id": 93509, "title": "The Woman's Land and Hazael's Hidden Future", "subtitle": "God's word reaches property and kingdoms", "text": "2 Kings 8:1–15", "chapters": [[11, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Elisha had warned the Shunammite woman to leave with her household because a famine would last seven years. She followed the direction and lived among the Philistines. At its end she returned and appealed to the king for her house and land. He was then hearing Gehazi's account of Elisha's works. As the story of her son's restoration was being told, she arrived with the boy. Gehazi identified them, and the king asked her to speak. He appointed an officer to restore both her property and the income from the fields during her absence. The protection of a household continued beyond the earlier miracle into the practical matter of its livelihood.", "The narrative next follows Elisha to Damascus, where Ben-hadad was ill. The king sent Hazael with a great gift to enquire whether he would recover. Hazael stood before Elisha and presented the question. The prophet said the illness allowed recovery, yet the Lord had shown him that Ben-hadad would die. Elisha looked steadily at Hazael and began weeping. Asked why, he spoke of the violence Hazael would inflict upon Israel: strongholds burned and people of different ages cruelly killed. The knowledge of a future ruler brought grief to the prophet. Its meaning included the suffering of families who would have no place in the royal conversation taking place.", "Hazael questioned how someone of his low standing could accomplish such a thing. Elisha answered that the Lord had shown him as king of Aram. He returned to Ben-hadad and reported only the assurance about recovery. The next day he placed a wet cloth over the sick king's face and killed him. Hazael then took the throne. The prophecy named his coming position, while the narrative records the act through which he secured it. The report he gave his master concealed the darker word he had heard. A man entrusted with carrying a question about life had returned with information that became part of a royal murder.", "The two parts of the passage present very different uses of power and knowledge. One king hears a woman's account and orders restoration through an officer. Another royal servant receives a word concerning his future and then destroys the ruler who trusts him. Elisha's tears keep the human cost of the second movement before the reader. The events reach from a farm left during famine to the throne of a neighbouring state, and both remain within the account of the Lord's word. The woman's recovered livelihood and the violence foreseen under Hazael will continue to matter as the histories of Israel and its neighbours unfold."], "lesson": "Information can be used to restore another person's life or to advance a private ambition. The passage asks us to attend to the people affected by choices made around power.", "question": "Why are Elisha's tears important when the story announces Hazael's future kingship?"},
  {"id": 93510, "title": "Two Kings of Judah Follow Ahab's House", "subtitle": "Family alliances carry a direction", "text": "2 Kings 8:16–29", "chapters": [[11, 8]], "start": 16, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Jehoram son of Jehoshaphat began ruling Judah and reigned eight years in Jerusalem. The account connects his conduct with the kings of Israel because he had married a daughter of Ahab. He did evil before the Lord. Yet God preserved Judah for David's sake, recalling the promise that his descendants would retain a lamp. The notice joins a serious judgment upon the present king with the faithfulness of God to an earlier commitment. Jehoram had received a place within David's house, but his chosen pattern came from the royal family whose worship had provoked judgment in Israel. The marriage connection carried influence into the way Judah was governed.", "During his reign Edom rebelled and established its own king. Jehoram advanced with chariots and struck the Edomites who surrounded him, but his troops fled. Edom remained outside Judah's control. Libnah also revolted at that time. These losses give public shape to an unsettled reign. The narrator refers to further royal records, then tells of Jehoram's death and burial in David's city. The brief account does not allow the continuation of the dynasty to be mistaken for an untroubled kingdom. A king can inherit an enduring promise while decisions made during his rule still bring weakness and loss to the people.", "His son Ahaziah succeeded him at twenty two and ruled for one year. His mother Athaliah belonged to Omri's dynasty. He too followed Ahab's house and did evil, with family ties named as part of the explanation. He joined Joram of Israel in fighting Hazael at Ramoth-gilead. Joram was wounded and returned to Jezreel to recover. Ahaziah travelled there to visit him. The two kingdoms were therefore linked both through marriage and through a shared campaign, and their rulers came together in the place where the next chapter's upheaval would occur. The earlier account of Hazael's rise has already prepared the reader for the danger surrounding that war.", "The passage ends before the visitor knows how quickly his own reign will be brought to an end. Its notices have traced a pattern through two generations in Judah: allegiance shaped by Ahab's family, losses of territory and cooperation in a campaign against Aram. The promise to David still stands, but it does not make the kings' influences or choices harmless. The names can seem crowded because related men rule different kingdoms and share similar names. The narrator keeps their family lines and locations before the reader so that the approaching judgment can be followed as events involving actual households and political commitments. The visit to Jezreel will place both kings in Jehu's path."], "lesson": "Relationships can shape the course of a household or institution over generations. Faithfulness requires examining the direction an alliance gives, alongside its immediate advantages.", "question": "How do the family connections help explain the course taken by these two kings of Judah?"},
  {"id": 93511, "title": "Jehu Drives Toward Jezreel", "subtitle": "A judgment announced comes upon a dynasty", "text": "2 Kings 9:1–37", "chapters": [[11, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Elisha sent a young prophet to Ramoth-gilead with oil and instructions to anoint Jehu privately, then leave at once. The messenger drew him aside from the commanders and declared him king over Israel. He was to strike Ahab's house in judgment for the blood of the Lord's servants shed by Jezebel. The dynasty would meet the end previously announced, and Jezebel would have no honourable burial. The messenger fled. Pressed by his fellow officers, Jehu disclosed the anointing. They spread garments beneath him upon the steps, sounded a trumpet and acclaimed him. With their support secured, he ordered that no warning be allowed to reach Jezreel, where Joram was recovering.", "Jehu drove toward the city while Ahaziah of Judah was visiting the wounded king. A watchman saw the approaching company, and Joram sent horsemen to ask whether it came in peace. Neither returned, because Jehu ordered each to follow behind. The watchman recognised his furious driving. Joram and Ahaziah went out in separate chariots and met him upon Naboth's land. Asked about peace, Jehu named Jezebel's idolatry and practices. Joram turned to flee, warning Ahaziah of treachery. Jehu shot him through the body. He told Bidkar to cast the corpse upon Naboth's property, recalling the Lord's judgment concerning the blood of Naboth and his sons. The place seized through murder now received Ahab's dead son.", "Ahaziah also fled. Jehu's men wounded him, and he reached Megiddo, where he died. His attendants carried him to Jerusalem for burial in David's city. Jehu then entered Jezreel. Jezebel had prepared herself and looked from a window, challenging him with the memory of Zimri, who killed his master and scarcely held the throne. Jehu called for supporters above, and attendants looked out. At his order they threw her down. Her death became part of the violent fall of the house whose authority she had long exercised. The narrative keeps the killings within the remembered prophetic announcements while describing the human commands and actions that carried them out.", "After eating, Jehu ordered that Jezebel be buried because she was a king's daughter. The men found only a few remains. He identified the outcome with Elijah's word that dogs would consume her in Jezreel, leaving no recognisable monument to her body. The chapter ends with the earlier word fulfilled amid the collapse of two closely allied royal courts. Its return to Naboth's land keeps the victims of past royal violence within the story of succession. Jehu has gained support and acted decisively, but his own future conduct will also be examined. Being an agent within this judgment will not remove his responsibility to hear and obey the Lord himself."], "lesson": "A powerful household cannot make earlier bloodshed disappear. The return to Naboth's land asks readers to remember those harmed even when political events move on.", "question": "Why does the meeting on Naboth's property give Joram's death a meaning beyond a military revolt?"},
  {"id": 93512, "title": "Jehu Invites a Witness to His Zeal", "subtitle": "Public reform and the heart's continuing obligation", "text": "2 Kings 10:1–36", "chapters": [[11, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Ahab's descendants remained under guardians in Samaria. Jehu challenged their protectors to choose a king and resist him. Terrified by the deaths of the two rulers, they submitted. He then demanded the heads of the seventy sons entrusted to them. They killed the men and sent the heads in baskets to Jezreel. Jehu displayed them at the gate and spoke publicly of the Lord's word against Ahab's house being fulfilled. He killed remaining members and supporters of the family there. On the road to Samaria he also met forty two relatives of Ahaziah coming to greet the royal household. He had them captured and killed at the shearing place. The transfer of rule widened into destruction beyond the first royal chariot.", "Jehonadab son of Rechab met Jehu, who asked whether his heart was loyal. On receiving his hand, he brought him into the chariot and invited him to witness his zeal for the Lord. At Samaria Jehu finished destroying Ahab's remaining house. He then announced that he would serve Baal more greatly than Ahab and called all Baal's worshippers to a compulsory gathering. The narrator identifies the announcement as a deception intended to destroy them. Garments were distributed, and the sanctuary was checked to exclude servants of the Lord. Jehu stationed guards with strict orders, then commanded an attack after the gathering had begun its sacrifice.", "The worshippers were killed, the sacred objects burned and Baal's temple demolished and turned into a place of refuse. The account says Jehu removed Baal worship from Israel. Yet he retained the golden calves at Bethel and Dan and continued Jeroboam's sins. God acknowledged his execution of judgment upon Ahab's house and promised four generations upon the throne. The narrator then makes clear that Jehu did not follow the Lord's law wholeheartedly. The invitation to see his zeal is therefore read beside a judgment upon its limits. A dramatic campaign against one form of idolatry had left another established form serving the same kingdom.", "During those days Israel began losing territory. Hazael struck its land east of the Jordan, including Gilead, Bashan and regions belonging to Gad, Reuben and Manasseh. Jehu's reign lasted twenty eight years, and after his death and burial in Samaria his son Jehoahaz succeeded him. The chapter has shown an extraordinary concentration of violent action and public religious change. It ends by directing attention to the ruler's own obedience and to the weakening land. The destruction of Ahab's dynasty did not create a permanent exemption for the man who replaced it. The word assessing Jehu's heart remains beside the word granting his descendants a limited succession."], "lesson": "Visible zeal requires examination by the whole of God's word. Jehu's retained practices ask whether a reform also confronts the errors that remain convenient to its leader.", "question": "What does the narrator's assessment of Jehu add to his invitation to witness his zeal?"},
  {"id": 93513, "title": "The Child Hidden in the Lord's House", "subtitle": "A fragile life and a renewed covenant", "text": "2 Kings 11:1–21", "chapters": [[11, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["When Athaliah learned that her son Ahaziah was dead, she moved to destroy the royal family. Jehosheba, Ahaziah's sister, rescued the child Joash and his nurse from among those threatened. They were hidden, and Joash remained concealed in the Lord's house for six years while Athaliah ruled. The continuation of David's line rested in the survival of a child who could not defend himself. The account names the woman whose intervention preserved him before it describes the guards and public ceremonies that would later establish his rule. Years of hidden care preceded the day of recognition. Athaliah's apparent control had not included knowledge of every life she had sought to remove.", "In the seventh year Jehoiada summoned military officers and guards to the temple. He made a covenant with them, placed them under oath and showed them the king's son. He arranged their watches around the palace, gates and temple, including those coming on and going off duty on the Sabbath. They were to surround Joash and remain with him as he moved. David's stored weapons were issued to the captains. The guards took their positions, and Jehoiada brought the child out, crowned him, gave him the testimony and anointed him. The gathered people clapped and acclaimed the king. A concealed survivor had become a publicly recognised heir under careful protection.", "Athaliah heard the noise and entered the temple area. Seeing the king beside the pillar with officers, trumpeters and rejoicing people, she tore her clothes and cried treason. Jehoiada ordered her taken outside the ranks and forbade killing her within the Lord's house. She was escorted toward the palace by the horse entrance and put to death. The six year rule begun in the destruction of the royal family ended at the restoration of the child she had failed to kill. The chapter records the violence of that ending while keeping the place of worship distinct from the execution. The new kingship was then placed within a more explicit public commitment.", "Jehoiada made a covenant involving the Lord, king and people that they would be the Lord's people, and another between ruler and nation. The assembly destroyed Baal's temple, altars and images and killed its priest Mattan. Officers were appointed for the Lord's house. The guards and people escorted Joash from temple to palace, where he sat upon the royal throne. The city became quiet, and the people rejoiced. He was seven years old. The final age notice recalls the dependence still present beneath the crown. His survival has required courage, shelter and a community's action; his future rule will require instruction and faithfulness as well as a rightful descent."], "lesson": "Protecting a vulnerable life can preserve a future others cannot yet see. The covenant also gives the restored kingdom responsibilities beyond a successful transfer of power.", "question": "Why does the story begin with Jehosheba's hidden rescue before describing Jehoiada's public action?"},
  {"id": 93514, "title": "The Chest Beside the Altar", "subtitle": "A purpose needs honest arrangements", "text": "2 Kings 12:1–21", "chapters": [[11, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Joash ruled forty years in Jerusalem. He acted rightly while Jehoiada instructed him, though the high places remained in use. He directed that money brought into the Lord's house be used to repair its damage. The funds included required payments, assessed gifts and voluntary offerings. Priests were to collect and apply them, but by the twenty third year the repairs had not been carried out. Joash summoned Jehoiada and the priests and asked why. The original arrangement had failed to turn received resources into the work for which they were intended. The king required a change, and the priests agreed to cease that system of collection and responsibility for the repairs.", "Jehoiada made a hole in the lid of a chest and placed it beside the altar near the entrance. The doorkeepers put the offerings into it. When it held a substantial amount, the royal secretary and high priest counted and bagged the money together. It was then given to those supervising the work. They paid carpenters, builders, masons and stonecutters and purchased timber and dressed stone. The description follows the money from the donor's entrance to actual repairs. Oversight, shared counting and direct payment gave practical form to the purpose that the earlier instruction had named but not achieved. The house's condition now changed through work supported by these arrangements.", "The money was used for repairing the building rather than for additional precious metal vessels. The supervisors were trusted because they acted faithfully. Other payments belonging to the priests remained distinct. The account therefore recognises both honesty and an organised way of carrying out the work. Good intentions had required scrutiny when the years passed without results. Once the process was altered, the people responsible could apply the resources to the actual damage. The chapter gives space to such details because the maintenance of the Lord's house involved accountability among king, priests, donors and workers. Sacred purpose did not eliminate the need to ask what happened to the funds.", "Later Hazael captured Gath and threatened Jerusalem. Joash sent him treasures dedicated by earlier kings and by himself, together with gold from temple and palace, and Hazael withdrew. The account then records a conspiracy by Joash's own servants. They killed him at the house of Millo, and he was buried in David's city. Amaziah succeeded him. The reign remembered for temple repair therefore ends amid foreign pressure and violence within the royal establishment. Its earlier restoration remains an achievement, but it does not exhaust the truth about the king's life or the kingdom's condition. The same treasures gathered and guarded within sacred and royal houses had also become resources used to meet a military threat."], "lesson": "Resources entrusted for a good purpose need arrangements that bring the work to completion. The repair account invites honest questions, faithful oversight and respect for those doing the labour.", "question": "What changes between the initial instruction and the arrangement involving the chest?"},
  {"id": 93515, "title": "The Arrows Beside Elisha's Deathbed", "subtitle": "Mercy continues beyond the servant's life", "text": "2 Kings 13:1–25", "chapters": [[11, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Jehoahaz son of Jehu ruled Israel for seventeen years and continued Jeroboam's sins. Hazael and Ben-hadad of Aram oppressed the nation until its military strength was greatly reduced. Jehoahaz pleaded with the Lord, who heard because He saw the suffering. Deliverance was given, and people returned to a measure of settled life, though the corrupt worship continued. The account preserves both divine compassion and the people's incomplete response. Jehoahaz died and was succeeded by his son Jehoash, whose sixteen year reign also followed Jeroboam's pattern. The narrative then turns from royal notices to the prophet whose long ministry had accompanied many of these struggles.", "Elisha became ill with the sickness from which he would die. Israel's king came weeping and addressed him as his father and the chariot and horsemen of Israel, echoing Elisha's words at Elijah's departure. The prophet told him to take a bow and arrows. With his hands upon the king's, Elisha directed a shot through the east window and declared it the Lord's arrow of deliverance against Aram. The king would strike the enemy at Aphek. He was then told to strike the ground with the arrows. He did so three times and stopped. Elisha was angry, saying that five or six blows would have signified a complete victory, while the three would mean only three defeats of the enemy.", "Elisha died and was buried. During the season when Moabite raiders entered the land, another burial party saw a band approaching and hurriedly placed its dead man in Elisha's tomb. When the body touched the prophet's bones, the man revived and stood up. The event extends the witness to God's life giving power beyond the prophet's own lifespan. It does not make his death unreal or leave him available for further audiences with kings. His ministry has ended, while the Lord whose word he carried remains able to act. The striking notice stands within the history of a people still exposed to raids and foreign domination.", "Hazael continued oppressing Israel during Jehoahaz's days, but the Lord remained gracious because of His covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He did not yet destroy or remove them. After Hazael died, Jehoash recovered towns from his son Ben-hadad, defeating him three times in accordance with Elisha's word. The chapter closes with an outcome promised beside a deathbed. It has named persistent sin without withholding the account of compassion, and it has recorded a prophet's burial without making God's work end there. The restored towns gave practical relief to people whose lives had been worn down by years of war. Their recovery remained a gift calling for a more faithful response."], "lesson": "God's mercy can sustain people whose response remains incomplete. Elisha's final word asks readers to consider how seriously they receive what God has entrusted to them.", "question": "How do the recovered towns and the incident at Elisha's tomb show that the Lord's work continues after the prophet dies?"},
  {"id": 93516, "title": "Amaziah Challenges a Stronger King", "subtitle": "Victory, pride and unexpected mercy", "text": "2 Kings 14:1–29", "chapters": [[11, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Amaziah became king of Judah at twenty five and ruled twenty nine years. He followed the generally right course of his father Joash, though high places remained. Once established, he executed the servants who had murdered his father. He spared their children in obedience to the law of Moses, which held each person accountable for that person's own sin. The narrative names a limit upon royal revenge before turning to his military achievements. Amaziah defeated Edom in the Valley of Salt and captured Sela. His success then led him to challenge Jehoash of Israel to face him. Victory in one direction had become the occasion for a dangerous new ambition.", "Jehoash answered with a story of a thistle demanding a marriage alliance from a cedar, only to be trampled by a passing animal. He warned that Amaziah's success over Edom had lifted his heart and urged him to remain at home. Amaziah refused. At Beth-shemesh Israel defeated Judah and captured its king. Jehoash entered Jerusalem, broke down a long section of its wall, took treasures from temple and palace and removed hostages before returning to Samaria. The challenge had brought harm upon the city and people under Amaziah's care. The king's earlier victory could not make the warning unworthy of attention or guarantee the next battle's result.", "Jehoash died, and his son Jeroboam succeeded him. Amaziah lived fifteen years longer, but a conspiracy in Jerusalem drove him to Lachish, where pursuers killed him. His body was brought home for burial, and the people made his sixteen year old son Azariah king. Azariah restored Elath to Judah. The chapter then surveys Jeroboam's forty one years in Israel. He continued the sins of his namesake, the son of Nebat, yet recovered territory from the northern approaches toward the sea of the Arabah. This fulfilment came according to a word spoken through Jonah son of Amittai from Gath-hepher. The prophet's presence in this account connects his ministry with the nation's public history.", "The Lord had seen Israel's bitter affliction and lack of a helper and had not determined to blot out its name. He gave rescue through Jeroboam despite the king's evil course. After further notices of his achievements, the account records his death and the succession of Zechariah. Pride and mercy have both appeared in the chapter, but they must not be confused. Amaziah's confidence cost Judah dearly, while Israel's relief did not establish Jeroboam's faithfulness. The narrator gives the reason for that relief in God's compassion toward the afflicted. Military outcomes need to be read within that wider account, with attention both to the ruler's choices and to the suffering of the people."], "lesson": "An earlier success can tempt us to dismiss needed warning. The chapter also asks us to recognise mercy without treating it as approval of every choice made by its recipient.", "question": "How does Amaziah's refusal to listen affect people beyond himself?"},
  {"id": 93517, "title": "Short Reigns and a Kingdom Losing Its Land", "subtitle": "Repeated change without a faithful turn", "text": "2 Kings 15:1–38", "chapters": [[11, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Azariah, also called Uzziah, ruled Judah for fifty two years. He followed his father's generally right course, though the high places continued. He developed a serious skin disease and lived apart, with Jotham overseeing the palace and judging the people. The account does not here explain the fuller circumstances of his illness. After his death Jotham succeeded him. Meanwhile Israel entered a rapid succession of violent changes. Zechariah son of Jeroboam ruled six months before Shallum killed him. The narrator identifies his death with the fulfilment of the promise limiting Jehu's descendants to four generations. That prophetic word stands at the end of the dynasty's long presence upon Israel's throne.", "Shallum ruled one month before Menahem came from Tirzah, killed him and took power. Menahem attacked Tiphsah because it refused him entry and inflicted terrible violence, including upon pregnant women. His ten years continued Jeroboam's sins. When Pul of Assyria threatened the land, Menahem paid a large sum to secure support for his kingdom, collecting the silver from wealthy Israelites. The foreign army withdrew. His position had been strengthened through tribute, but the cost had been imposed upon the people, and the account's moral judgment remained unchanged. After his death his son Pekahiah ruled two years. Pekah, one of his officers, conspired with Gileadite soldiers and killed him in the palace.", "Pekah's reign likewise continued the established sin. During it Tiglath-pileser of Assyria captured northern towns, Galilee, Gilead and Naphtali's territory and deported their people. The losses reached beyond a royal court into the homes of whole regions. Hoshea then conspired against Pekah, killed him and took the throne. The sequence of names marks more than uncertainty about who ruled. It shows a kingdom whose internal violence and inherited idolatry continued while an empire steadily removed its land and population. A new ruler repeatedly appeared without bringing the faithful change that the narrative had been asking for through generations of warnings.", "The closing notice returns to Jotham in Judah. He ruled sixteen years from age twenty five, did what was right and built the temple's upper gate, though the high places remained. During his days the Lord began sending Rezin of Aram and Pekah of Israel against Judah. Jotham died and was buried in David's city, and Ahaz succeeded him. Judah's relative continuity therefore did not place it beyond coming danger. The chapter has moved between a long reign interrupted by illness, a series of assassinations, foreign tribute and deportation. Its repeated assessment of worship prevents political survival from becoming the only measure of a king. The next reign in Jerusalem will bring that question directly into the Lord's house."], "lesson": "Repeated changes of leadership can leave destructive habits intact. The deported towns remind us that political choices and conflicts fall heavily upon people outside the palace.", "question": "What pattern remains through Israel's many changes of ruler, and what happens to the land while it continues?"},
  {"id": 93518, "title": "Ahaz Sends a Pattern from Damascus", "subtitle": "Fear reshapes the place of worship", "text": "2 Kings 16:1–20", "chapters": [[11, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Ahaz became king of Judah at twenty and ruled sixteen years. He departed from David's example, followed Israel's kings and even offered his son through fire. He sacrificed at high places, hills and beneath spreading trees. Rezin of Aram and Pekah of Israel besieged Jerusalem without capturing him, while Elath was lost to Aram. Under that pressure Ahaz appealed to Tiglath-pileser of Assyria as a servant and son, asking for rescue. He sent silver and gold from temple and palace. The Assyrian king responded by taking Damascus, deporting its people and killing Rezin. The immediate threat had been answered through submission to a greater foreign power.", "Ahaz went to Damascus to meet his protector. There he saw an altar and sent its dimensions and design to Uriah the priest in Jerusalem. Uriah built it before the king returned. Ahaz approached the new structure and offered upon it. He then moved the original bronze altar from its position before the temple and placed it to the north. The arrangement of the Lord's house was being altered according to what the king had admired in the conquered city. His political journey had brought home a model for worship, carried through royal orders and a priest's willingness to execute them.", "Ahaz directed that the regular morning and evening offerings, his own sacrifices and the people's offerings be made upon the new large altar. He reserved the bronze altar for his own enquiry. Uriah complied. The king also dismantled parts of the bronze stands, removed their basins and lowered the great Sea from its bronze oxen to a stone pavement. Other changes affected the Sabbath structure and the royal entrance in connection with the Assyrian king. The narrative records the details because submission abroad had consequences within the sacred space. Objects prepared under Solomon were now moved and altered by a ruler following another set of influences.", "Ahaz died and was buried in David's city, and Hezekiah succeeded him. The chapter leaves the temple changed as well as the political situation. Its opening description of the king's worship prepares the reader to understand the significance of the orders from Damascus. The foreign design was not simply an isolated building preference. It belonged to a reign already following practices the Lord had forbidden, and the king's fear had deepened his dependence upon Assyria. Uriah's compliance also matters. A royal request can draw those with sacred responsibility into carrying out changes whose source and purpose deserve examination. The next king will inherit both the pressure of empire and the work of confronting worship reshaped by his father."], "lesson": "Fear can influence both alliances and worship. The account asks those entrusted with responsibility to consider the authority and purpose behind the instructions they carry out.", "question": "How does Ahaz's journey to meet the Assyrian king affect what happens afterward in Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 93519, "title": "Samaria Falls and New Peoples Enter Its Towns", "subtitle": "A long refusal reaches its consequence", "text": "2 Kings 17:1–41", "chapters": [[11, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Hoshea ruled Israel for nine years. He did evil, though the narrator distinguishes his course from some earlier kings. Shalmaneser of Assyria made him a tributary. When he sought Egyptian help and stopped the payments, the Assyrian king imprisoned him and besieged Samaria for three years. The city fell, and Israelites were deported to places in Assyria and the cities of the Medes. The northern kingdom's last political manoeuvre had ended in the removal of its people. The account then pauses at length to explain the fall through the history of Israel's relationship with the Lord who had brought the nation out of Egypt.", "The people had feared other gods, copied surrounding practices, built high places and images and served idols. The Lord repeatedly sent prophets calling them to turn and keep His commands. They refused, hardened themselves and rejected covenant and warning. Golden calves, worship of the heavenly bodies, Baal, divination and the sacrifice of children entered the account of their rebellion. Judah also followed corrupt practices. The narrator connects the northern course with Jeroboam's original separation from David's house and his leading the people away. Samaria's fall is therefore presented as the culmination of a long refusal rather than the result of one king's unsuccessful diplomacy alone.", "Assyria brought settlers from Babylon, Cuthah, Avva, Hamath and Sepharvaim into the emptied towns. When lions killed some of them, they reported that they did not know how to worship the land's God. A deported priest was sent back and settled at Bethel to instruct them. Yet the different communities continued making and serving their own gods. The account names the images and practices they brought, including further child sacrifice. They also appointed shrine priests and offered a form of reverence to the Lord. The resulting mixture receives repeated scrutiny. Instruction had been supplied, but the people continued combining it with the allegiances they already preferred.", "The closing verses recall the covenant's clear demand to worship the Lord who redeemed Israel, keep His written commands and refuse other gods. The people nevertheless continued their former ways, passing them to children and grandchildren. The chapter's repeated wording about fearing the Lord distinguishes outward recognition from the whole loyalty the covenant requires. It ends among a changed population in the northern towns, while the earlier inhabitants live in exile. The long explanation keeps the reader from viewing the event only through imperial armies and rulers. A people warned across generations had persisted in the same direction, and even the newcomers' experience repeated the problem of hearing instruction without surrendering rival worship."], "lesson": "Hearing instruction requires a response that reaches our actual loyalties. Samaria's history calls for attention to repeated warnings before a harmful course becomes inherited practice.", "question": "Why does the narrator devote so much space to Israel's earlier choices after describing the city's fall?"},
  {"id": 93520, "title": "Hezekiah Breaks the Bronze Serpent", "subtitle": "Faithfulness amid the pressure of empire", "text": "2 Kings 18:1–16", "chapters": [[11, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Hezekiah became king of Judah at twenty five and ruled twenty nine years. The narrator compares his conduct with David's and gives a particularly strong account of his trust in the Lord. He removed high places, broke sacred pillars and cut down the Asherah. He also destroyed the bronze serpent made by Moses because people had begun offering incense to it. He called it Nehushtan, treating the object according to its material nature. Something associated with an earlier act of divine rescue had become the focus of a corrupted devotion. Hezekiah's reform therefore reached beyond foreign images to a familiar object whose history could have been used to protect its continued misuse.", "The account says he held fast to the Lord and kept the commands given through Moses. God was with him, and he acted successfully. He refused continued service to Assyria and struck Philistine territory as far as Gaza. The narrator then places Samaria's fall within his reign, recalling the three year siege, the northern deportations and their cause in covenant disobedience. Judah's king is thus introduced beside the loss of the neighbouring kingdom. The contrast concerns their response to the same Lord and His word. Hezekiah's trust is described positively, while the removal of Israel supplies an immediate warning about the course from which he has turned.", "In his fourteenth year Sennacherib of Assyria attacked Judah's fortified towns and took them. Hezekiah sent to him at Lachish, admitting fault and offering whatever payment would secure withdrawal. The demand was three hundred talents of silver and thirty of gold. He emptied the available silver from temple and palace. He even removed gold from the temple doors and doorposts he had overlaid and gave it to the Assyrian ruler. The events show the heavy pressure upon a king already commended for trust. The book records the tribute and its cost plainly, without pretending that his faithful reforms meant his country would face no powerful threat.", "The passage ends with the temple's gold being used for an attempted settlement. The next scene will reveal that payment did not conclude the danger. Hezekiah's reign must therefore be read through both its clear direction toward the Lord and the decisions made under military fear. The bronze serpent's destruction had shown a willingness to challenge misplaced reliance. Assyria now confronted him with a different question of security, backed by captured towns and demands upon the treasury. The people living through that crisis had heard of Samaria's fate and could see foreign power advancing through their own land. The story will move from the surrendered treasures toward threatening words spoken outside Jerusalem's wall."], "lesson": "Faithful reform does not remove the need for trust under fresh pressure. The bronze serpent also asks whether a valued religious object has acquired a place that belongs to God.", "question": "Why is Hezekiah's treatment of the bronze serpent significant in a chapter concerned with trust?"},
  {"id": 93521, "title": "The Voice Outside Jerusalem's Wall", "subtitle": "Answering intimidation with prayer", "text": "2 Kings 18:17–19:7", "chapters": [[11, 18], [11, 19]], "start": 17, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Assyria sent senior officials with a large army to Jerusalem. They stood near the upper pool's water channel, and Eliakim, Shebna and Joah came out to represent Hezekiah. The spokesman challenged the basis of Judah's confidence. Egypt, he said, was a broken reed that would wound anyone leaning upon it. If the people trusted the Lord, had Hezekiah not removed His altars? The argument misrepresented the king's reforms as an offence against the God they honoured. The spokesman mocked Judah's military weakness and even claimed that the Lord had authorised the attack. The pressure of the army was joined by words designed to make every ground of resistance appear futile.", "Judah's officials asked him to use Aramaic, which they understood, rather than the language heard by the people upon the wall. He refused and spoke more loudly. The coming siege would make the listeners suffer, and he wanted them to hear. He urged them not to trust Hezekiah's assurances about the Lord. Surrender would allow a period of ordinary life before removal to another productive land. He named nations whose gods had not saved them and asked why Jerusalem's God should be different. The speech joined threat with a promise of manageable conditions, presenting deportation as a choice preferable to dependence upon the Lord. The people remained silent under the king's command.", "Eliakim, Shebna and Joah returned with torn clothes and reported the words. Hezekiah also tore his clothes, put on sackcloth and entered the Lord's house. He sent officials and senior priests to Isaiah, describing a day of trouble, rebuke and disgrace. They were like people reaching childbirth without strength to complete it. He asked that the prophet pray for the remaining people and appealed to the living God who had heard the insults. The king's response moved the speech from the wall into the place of worship and the prophetic enquiry. He did not deny the country's weakness. His request named it while placing the enemy's claim before the Lord.", "Isaiah answered that Hezekiah should not fear those words. The Lord would cause the Assyrian king to hear a report and return to his own country, where he would fall by the sword. The first answer therefore directly challenged the certainty of the threatening speech. A king who had demanded tribute and sent an overwhelming force did not possess the last word over Jerusalem's future. The narrative has allowed the enemy's reasoning to be heard in detail so that the response can be understood within its actual pressure. Prayer arises among people who know that their resources are small. The next exchange will bring another written threat and another appeal before God."], "lesson": "Intimidation often mixes true weakness with false conclusions about God. Hezekiah's response invites honest prayer that brings threatening words into the light of His word.", "question": "How does the Assyrian spokesman try to turn Hezekiah's reforms into a reason for distrust?"},
  {"id": 93522, "title": "Hezekiah Spreads the Letter Before the Lord", "subtitle": "The Holy One answers a ruler's boasts", "text": "2 Kings 19:8–37", "chapters": [[11, 19]], "start": 8, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["The Assyrian spokesman returned and found Sennacherib fighting at Libnah. News of Tirhakah's advance prompted another message to Hezekiah. The letter warned him against trusting a promise of safety and recalled nations Assyria had destroyed. Hezekiah read it, went into the Lord's house and spread it before Him. His prayer confessed the God of Israel as the one God over every kingdom and the maker of heaven and earth. He acknowledged that Assyria had truly devastated nations and burned their gods, explaining that these were human works of wood and stone. He asked for rescue so the kingdoms would know the Lord alone was God.", "Isaiah sent the answer that the prayer had been heard. In a poetic message Jerusalem's daughter was pictured scorning the invader. Sennacherib had lifted his eyes against Israel's Holy One and boasted that chariots, engineering and conquest could overcome every height and waterway. God's answer placed those achievements within His own purposes and declared that He knew the ruler's movements and rage. The one who treated nations as objects of command would himself be turned back, with the image of a hook and bridle describing the limit set upon him. The prophecy answered the arrogance beneath the military claims rather than simply matching the king's numbers with another visible army.", "A sign was given concerning the land's recovery. The people would first eat what grew without planting, then later sow, reap and plant vineyards again. Judah's surviving remnant would take root and bear fruit. Sennacherib would not enter Jerusalem, shoot an arrow there or raise a siege ramp against it. He would return by the road he had come. The Lord would defend the city for His own sake and for David's. The promise therefore included the life after the immediate crisis, when food and settled work could resume. Deliverance concerned both a city spared and people able to remain and grow within the land.", "That night the angel of the Lord struck the Assyrian camp, and one hundred and eighty five thousand died. Sennacherib withdrew to Nineveh. Later, while worshipping in Nisroch's temple, he was killed by two sons, who fled, and Esarhaddon succeeded him. The narrative draws the report of his end into the fulfilment of the earlier word. The letter spread in the temple had portrayed his power as irresistible. Its answer named the God whose authority reached beyond Judah to the ruler's movements and final return. Jerusalem's relief came without the victory scene its own limited army could scarcely have supplied. The people still faced the work of recovering their fields, but the remnant's future had been preserved."], "lesson": "Prayer can acknowledge an enemy's real strength while refusing its claim to final authority. The promise of rooted growth also directs gratitude toward the life restored after danger.", "question": "How does Hezekiah's prayer distinguish the Lord from the defeated images named in the letter?"},
  {"id": 93523, "title": "The Shadow Turns and Babylon's Visitors Depart", "subtitle": "Years received still carry responsibility", "text": "2 Kings 20:1–21", "chapters": [[11, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Hezekiah became gravely ill, and Isaiah told him to put his house in order because death was near. The king turned toward the wall, prayed and wept, asking God to remember his faithful course. Before Isaiah had left the middle court, the Lord sent him back. He had heard the prayer and seen the tears. Hezekiah would recover, go to the temple on the third day and receive fifteen more years. Jerusalem's protection against Assyria was also promised. A poultice of figs was applied to the boil, and the king recovered. The narrative holds the direct promise and the practical treatment together within the account of answered prayer.", "Hezekiah asked for a sign that the word would be fulfilled. Isaiah offered the movement of the shadow forward or backward by ten steps. The king chose the backward movement, and Isaiah cried to the Lord. The shadow retreated upon the steps associated with Ahaz. The extraordinary sign confirmed what had been spoken. The king whose life had seemed at its end could now continue. The account does not treat the added years as empty time. The next event will show decisions within them carrying consequences beyond his own lifespan. A mercy received in illness does not settle how the restored life will be used.", "Babylon's king sent letters and a gift after hearing of the illness. Hezekiah welcomed the envoys and displayed his wealth, precious substances, armour and storehouses. He withheld nothing from their inspection. Isaiah came and asked where the visitors were from and what they had seen. Hezekiah replied that they came from distant Babylon and had seen everything. Isaiah announced that a day would come when the accumulated treasures would be carried there, and descendants of Hezekiah would serve in the Babylonian palace as eunuchs. The visit that had seemed an occasion for presenting the kingdom's resources became the setting of a warning about its future loss.", "Hezekiah accepted the word as good and spoke of peace and security in his own days. The statement remains open to the reader's reflection about his response to consequences lying beyond him. The chapter closes by recalling his other achievements, including the pool and channel bringing water into the city, then his death and Manasseh's succession. His reign has included remarkable trust, deliverance and healing, together with a display of treasures that drew a severe prophecy. The added years did not remove the continuing need for humility. The coming generations named by Isaiah will live within the wider history the king can only partly see. Mercy in the present therefore carries a responsibility toward people whose days have not yet arrived."], "lesson": "Gratitude for mercy should deepen humility and concern for those who follow us. The envoys' visit asks how we use the life and resources newly placed in our care.", "question": "How does the warning about future descendants affect your reading of Hezekiah's response?"},
  {"id": 93524, "title": "Manasseh Restores What His Father Removed", "subtitle": "A long reign leaves a burden of bloodshed", "text": "2 Kings 21:1–26", "chapters": [[11, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Manasseh became king at twelve and ruled fifty five years in Jerusalem. He rebuilt the high places Hezekiah had removed, erected altars to Baal, made an Asherah and worshipped the heavenly bodies. His actions entered the Lord's house itself, where altars were placed in the courts and an image installed within the place bearing God's name. He offered his son through fire and practised divination, consulting mediums and spiritists. The narrator recalls the Lord's promise concerning His dwelling among Israel and the condition that the people obey the law given through Moses. Manasseh led them in the opposite direction, and the sacred building became part of the story of their rejection.", "The Lord spoke through prophets, announcing disaster upon Jerusalem and Judah. The judgment that had come upon Samaria and Ahab's house would become a measure laid against Jerusalem. The city would be wiped like a dish and turned upside down. The remaining people would be given to enemies because of their continuing provocation since the exodus. These images make the threatened loss more than a change of palace policy. A place that had been spared Assyria could still face destruction through its chosen course. The earlier rescue did not make the covenant's claims irrelevant to a later generation. The word given during Manasseh's rule therefore reaches toward the exile still ahead.", "Manasseh also shed innocent blood until Jerusalem was filled with it. The account names that violence alongside the idolatry through which he led Judah. His death brought burial in the garden of his house, and his son Amon succeeded him. This chapter does not narrate the later personal experiences recorded in Chronicles. Its purpose here is to establish the wrongdoing and warning that the following royal history will repeatedly recall. The length of his reign could not serve as proof of faithfulness, and the lives lost beneath his rule remained part of the nation's responsibility even when his own days ended.", "Amon ruled two years from age twenty two. He followed his father's idols and forsook the Lord. His servants conspired and killed him within his own house. The people of the land then killed the conspirators and made his son Josiah king. Amon was buried in the same royal garden. The chapter closes with another succession, but the burden of its earlier events remains. Within two generations, reforms have been reversed, the temple altered for other worship, bloodshed multiplied and assassination returned to the palace. Josiah will receive an opportunity to respond differently. His inherited position will include both the continuing promise to David and a warning whose seriousness the nation's recent history has made unmistakable."], "lesson": "Long tenure and sacred surroundings do not establish a leader's faithfulness. The chapter asks us to remember the people harmed by choices that later rulers may prefer to treat as finished history.", "question": "Why does the account keep innocent bloodshed alongside idolatry when explaining the coming judgment?"},
  {"id": 93525, "title": "The Book Found During the Repairs", "subtitle": "A king hears the word with a tender heart", "text": "2 Kings 22:1–20", "chapters": [[11, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Josiah became king at eight and ruled thirty one years. The account describes his course as faithful to David's example. In his eighteenth year he sent the secretary Shaphan to Hilkiah concerning money collected at the temple. The funds were to pass to the work supervisors for carpenters, builders, masons, timber and dressed stone. The workers were trusted because they dealt honestly. The restoration of the building therefore continued through practical arrangements like those remembered in an earlier reign. Then Hilkiah told Shaphan that he had found the Book of the Law in the Lord's house. Shaphan received and read it. A repair project had brought a more searching matter before the court.", "Shaphan reported the handling of the money to Josiah, then told him of the book and read it aloud. The king tore his clothes. He heard in its words a judgment upon his own people and their ancestors for failing to do what was written. He sent Hilkiah, Shaphan and other officials to enquire of the Lord for himself and Judah. The discovery had become more than an addition to the temple's possessions. Its content exposed a gap between the people's inherited practice and the covenant's demand. Josiah responded with grief and a request to hear what the Lord would say concerning the words now read before him.", "The delegation went to Huldah the prophetess, who lived in Jerusalem. She announced that the threatened disaster would come because the people had forsaken the Lord and burned incense to other gods. Their acts had provoked His anger, and the judgment against the place would stand. She also delivered a particular answer for Josiah. His heart had been responsive, and he had humbled himself, torn his clothes and wept when he heard. The Lord had heard him. He would be gathered to his ancestors without seeing the full disaster that would fall upon the place. The response distinguished the announced national consequence from the king's own reception of the word.", "The officials returned with the message. The chapter ends before Josiah's public actions, leaving the reader with his listening, grief and enquiry. Its movement gives a place to workers, a priest, a secretary, a king and a prophetess in bringing a found book into the nation's present responsibility. None can make the earlier disobedience disappear, but the newly heard word requires a response. Josiah has not defended inherited custom as sufficient or treated the temple's repair as the whole work required. His tender heart appears through what he does when truth becomes uncomfortable. The next chapter will bring that private hearing into a covenant assembly and widespread reform."], "lesson": "God's word calls for a response that reaches beyond possession or public honour. Josiah's humility invites willingness to hear what exposes the need for change.", "question": "What actions show that Josiah has received the found book as a word addressing him and his people?"},
  {"id": 93526, "title": "Josiah Reads, Removes and Keeps the Passover", "subtitle": "A covenant answered through practical change", "text": "2 Kings 23:1–25", "chapters": [[11, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Josiah gathered Judah's elders and led the people, priests and prophets to the Lord's house. Great and small heard the Book of the Covenant read aloud. Standing beside the pillar, he pledged to follow the Lord and keep His commands with the whole heart and soul, and the people joined the covenant. He then ordered the removal of objects made for Baal, Asherah and the heavenly bodies. They were burned outside Jerusalem, and corrupt officials and practices associated with the shrines were removed. The reading became public action within the very place where earlier kings had installed other worship. The people were being asked to recognise both the word and its claim upon familiar arrangements.", "The reform extended through Judah. Josiah defiled the high places and Topheth so children could no longer be sacrificed there. He removed horses dedicated to the sun, burned its chariots and destroyed altars associated with Ahaz and Manasseh. He also desecrated shrines Solomon had built for foreign gods near Jerusalem. The account reaches across generations of royal decisions, showing how practices introduced at different times had accumulated within the land. Josiah's response could not be limited to the newest image or a single ruler's error. It confronted things whose age and association with famous kings might otherwise have given them an appearance of permanence.", "At Bethel he destroyed Jeroboam's altar and shrine and burned human bones upon it, fulfilling the word spoken by the man of God from Judah. Seeing a marked tomb, he asked about it. The townspeople explained that it belonged to the prophet who had announced this very act. Josiah ordered that his bones remain undisturbed, together with those of the older prophet buried beside him. The story of the messenger deceived at Bethel thus returns through the place of his burial and the fulfilment of his warning. Josiah also removed shrines in Samaria's towns and put their priests to death upon the altars. The passage retains the severity of those acts within its account of reform.", "Returning to Jerusalem, Josiah commanded a Passover according to the covenant book. Its observance was remembered as exceptional since the days of the judges. He also removed mediums, spiritists, household gods and other corrupt objects so that the written law would be carried out. The narrator praises the wholehearted character of his turning to the Lord. The chapter has moved from hearing to removal and then to the shared remembrance of redemption. Reform included a worship to practise as well as places and objects to reject. The king's devotion receives clear recognition, even though the next passage will return to the judgment still resting upon Judah from its longer history."], "lesson": "A renewed commitment takes practical form in what we remove and what we faithfully practise. The Passover gives the reform a shared remembrance of God's deliverance.", "question": "How does the return to the prophet's tomb at Bethel connect Josiah's actions with the earlier history?"},
  {"id": 93527, "title": "Josiah Falls and Egypt Chooses a King", "subtitle": "A faithful reign ends within a troubled future", "text": "2 Kings 23:26–37", "chapters": [[11, 23]], "start": 26, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Despite Josiah's wholehearted reform, the Lord's anger against Judah was not turned away because of the provocations associated with Manasseh. Jerusalem and the temple would be rejected as Israel had been removed. The narrative places this declaration immediately after its strong praise of Josiah. His faithfulness mattered and was recognised, yet it did not erase the long history of idolatry and bloodshed carried by the nation. The reader is therefore asked to hold a good king's response beside the seriousness of earlier consequences. The final sequence of Judah's kings will unfold within the warning the found book and the prophets had brought into view.", "Pharaoh Neco marched toward the Euphrates in connection with Assyria. Josiah went out to meet him and was killed at Megiddo. His attendants brought his body to Jerusalem and buried him. The people chose his son Jehoahaz, anointing him as king. The reformer's death leaves no scene of an established future continuing under his own supervision. The account gives the place and circumstances of the loss briefly, allowing the abruptness to be felt. The king who heard the law and acted decisively has gone, and his people must face another transition under the pressure of powerful neighbours.", "Jehoahaz was twenty three and ruled three months. His conduct was evil. Neco detained him at Riblah and placed a heavy tribute upon Judah. He installed another of Josiah's sons, Eliakim, renamed him Jehoiakim and carried Jehoahaz to Egypt, where he died. The power to appoint Judah's ruler had shifted into foreign hands. The changing name made that control visible within the monarchy itself. Jehoiakim paid the demanded silver and gold by taxing the people according to their assessed means. A tribute imposed upon the land reached the households whose king had been chosen for them by the ruler who had killed his father.", "Jehoiakim began at twenty five and ruled eleven years, doing evil before the Lord. The chapter closes with that assessment, preparing for Babylon's rise in the next account. Within a short succession of notices, Judah has lost Josiah, seen one son deposed and watched another take office under a new name and financial obligation. The public covenant and Passover remain part of its recent history, but the kings who follow do not simply continue their father's response. The narrative refuses to make spiritual faithfulness an automatic inheritance. Each new ruler acts within received circumstances, and the people bear the burdens of choices made both in Jerusalem and in foreign royal courts."], "lesson": "A faithful example must be received through fresh obedience in the next generation. The rapid succession asks how convictions survive when leadership and circumstances change.", "question": "What does the contrast between Josiah's response and his sons' conduct reveal about inherited responsibility?"},
  {"id": 93528, "title": "The Treasures and the People Leave for Babylon", "subtitle": "A kingdom brought under another ruler's hand", "text": "2 Kings 24:1–20", "chapters": [[11, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Nebuchadnezzar of Babylon advanced during Jehoiakim's reign. Judah's king served him for three years and then rebelled. Raiding forces from Babylon and neighbouring peoples came against the land. The narrator connects their attacks with the word spoken through the prophets and with the innocent blood shed under Manasseh. That earlier violence remains an active part of the explanation rather than a closed account belonging only to a dead king. Jehoiakim died, and his son Jehoiachin succeeded him. Egypt no longer moved out to challenge Babylon, which had taken its former sphere from the Egyptian border toward the Euphrates. Judah's efforts to manoeuvre between powers had narrowed under the empire now dominating the region.", "Jehoiachin was eighteen and ruled three months. He continued his father's evil course. Babylonian forces besieged Jerusalem, and Nebuchadnezzar came personally. The king of Judah went out with his mother, servants and officials and surrendered. He was taken captive. Treasures from the Lord's house and palace were carried away, and Solomon's gold furnishings were broken apart. The narrative deliberately remembers the earlier builder as the objects leave. What had once displayed the kingdom's resources and the temple's glory now formed part of an imperial removal. The warnings spoken to Hezekiah about Babylon were finding a visible setting in the city's losses.", "The deportation included princes, warriors, craftsmen, smiths, royal family and officials. The account gives large numbers and emphasises the removal of people with military strength and specialised skills, while the poorest remained. Its impact reached far beyond the throne. Families, trades, administration and the means of resisting were all affected. Jehoiachin was carried to Babylon with those from his household and court. The king's surrender had not secured the ordinary continuation of life in the city. The exile was changing who could work, govern, defend and maintain the places left behind. Objects and people were both taken into another country's possession.", "Nebuchadnezzar made Mattaniah, Jehoiachin's uncle, king and renamed him Zedekiah. He was twenty one and ruled eleven years. The narrator assesses his course as evil like Jehoiakim's and relates Judah's approaching removal to the Lord's anger. Zedekiah then rebelled against Babylon. The final sentence opens the road to the last siege. The city still stood, a king still sat within it and some inhabitants remained upon their land. Yet its independence, treasures and leading population had already been deeply diminished. A changed royal name could not undo the warnings or heal the wrongdoing repeatedly named by the account. The next chapter will follow the consequences to the burning of temple and palace and the scattering of those still there."], "lesson": "A community's losses include people, skills and relationships as well as buildings and wealth. The repeated mention of innocent blood asks us to remember the human wrongs beneath national events.", "question": "Why does the narrator recall Solomon's furnishings and Manasseh's bloodshed while describing this deportation?"},
  {"id": 93529, "title": "The City Burns and a Captive Receives Bread", "subtitle": "Judgment, displacement and a final kindness", "text": "2 Kings 25:1–30", "chapters": [[11, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Kings", "story": ["Nebuchadnezzar surrounded Jerusalem in Zedekiah's ninth year and maintained the siege into his eleventh. Famine exhausted the city's food. When the wall was breached, the soldiers and king escaped by night toward the Arabah. Babylonian forces overtook Zedekiah near Jericho after his men scattered. At Riblah his sons were killed before him, then he was blinded, bound and taken to Babylon. The king who had rebelled lost both his family and his freedom. The account gives these consequences directly, before turning from the captured ruler to the city itself. Jerusalem's remaining defences had failed, and those who stayed within the walls faced the occupying army.", "Nebuzaradan, the Babylonian guard commander, burned the Lord's house, the palace and the city's important buildings. The walls were demolished. Remaining inhabitants and those who had gone over to Babylon were deported, though some poor people were left to tend fields and vineyards. The great bronze pillars, stands and Sea made for Solomon's temple were broken up and carried away, along with vessels of bronze, gold and silver. Their dimensions and ornament receive mention as they are dismantled. Objects whose making had once filled the dedication account now appear in the record of destruction. Priests, officers and other leading men were taken to Riblah and executed, and Judah was removed from its land.", "Babylon appointed Gedaliah over those who remained. Military leaders came to him at Mizpah, and he urged them to stay peacefully and serve the Babylonian king. A possible life within the devastated land was being proposed. Ishmael, who belonged to the royal line, arrived with ten men and murdered Gedaliah together with Judeans and Babylonians there. Fear then drove the remaining people and commanders to Egypt. The violence continued after the monarchy's defeat, breaking the fragile arrangement for those left behind. The chapter does not let the reader end with ruined stones alone. Displaced households still faced decisions, fears and further losses as they sought somewhere to live.", "Its final notice moves thirty seven years into Jehoiachin's captivity. A new Babylonian king, Evil-merodach, released him from prison, spoke kindly, gave him an honoured place among captive kings and changed his prison clothes. Jehoiachin received a continuing allowance and ate at the royal table for the rest of his life. The book closes upon that provision. Jerusalem remains destroyed, and the exile has not ended, but David's captive descendant is alive and treated with kindness. The final bread does not cancel the account of judgment. It leaves a small, concrete sign of continuing life beyond the collapse of the kingdom, after a history in which the Lord's warnings, compassion and promises have accompanied every generation."], "lesson": "The suffering of displacement deserves remembrance, and kindness within it still matters. The book's final provision invites hope that faces the losses honestly while attending to life that continues.", "question": "How does Jehoiachin's place at the table affect the ending without erasing what happened to Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 94000, "title": "The Family Record Begins with Adam", "subtitle": "Israel's story within the wider human family", "text": "1 Chronicles 1:1–54", "chapters": [[12, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 54, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["Chronicles begins with names rather than a royal event. Adam is followed through generations to Noah and his sons Shem, Ham and Japheth. The record then traces peoples associated with those three lines. Descendants of Japheth, Ham and Canaan appear before the account follows Shem toward Abraham. Nimrod receives a brief notice as a mighty man, and Peleg is remembered in connection with the division of the earth. The opening therefore gives Israel's later history a place within a much wider human record. Its first name is shared humanity's ancestor, and its movement gradually narrows toward the family whose descendants will occupy the rest of the book.", "The line through Shem leads by Eber, Peleg, Reu, Serug, Nahor and Terah to Abram, identified as Abraham. His family includes Isaac and Ishmael. Ishmael's sons are named, as are the descendants born through Keturah, including Midian and his branches. The account then follows Isaac to Esau and Israel. These related peoples remain present even as the record chooses the line it will follow more closely. A genealogy can distinguish families without making the surrounding households vanish. The names preserve relationships that political borders and later conflicts might otherwise lead readers to forget. Israel's ancestors lived among relatives whose own descendants also formed communities.", "Esau's descendants lead into the families of Seir and the history of Edom. The passage names sons, daughters, brothers and chiefs, moving through a record of households rather than presenting a single uninterrupted royal line. It then lists kings who ruled Edom before Israel had a king. Their cities and occasional deeds appear beside their succession. Hadad is remembered for defeating Midian, while another ruler's wife and her family are named. The repeated deaths and accessions show political life already unfolding beyond the people whose monarchy will later become central. The chapter closes with Edom's chiefs, giving their names a place in the same remembered world.", "The form of this opening calls for patient attention. It does not narrate an incident for every person or supply motives where none are given. It establishes continuity, kinship and a setting for what follows. The reader who has just finished the fall of Jerusalem in Kings now begins far earlier, before either temple or throne. The family's existence precedes its royal achievements and survives in a record that can be read after those institutions have suffered loss. As the next chapter turns specifically toward Israel and Judah, these names remain the wider frame. The people about to be remembered belong to generations whose lives connect them with others across the known world."], "lesson": "Remembering where we come from can encourage humility about status and nation. The opening record asks us to see a people's history within the larger human family before judging its later achievements.", "question": "Why might a book concerned with Israel's worship and kings begin with Adam and surrounding peoples?"},
  {"id": 94001, "title": "Judah's Families Lead Toward David", "subtitle": "Names preserve more than a royal line", "text": "1 Chronicles 2:1–55", "chapters": [[12, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 55, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The twelve sons of Israel are named before the record concentrates upon Judah. His sons include those born through Shua's daughter and through Tamar. Er's wrongdoing and death are mentioned, and Achar is remembered for the offence that troubled Israel. These notices prevent ancestry from becoming a simple celebration of unbroken honour. The family record includes moral failure alongside continuity. Through Perez and Hezron the account reaches Ram, Amminadab, Nahshon, Salmon, Boaz, Obed and Jesse. The names will bring the reader toward David, but the path passes through households whose lives belong to the story before his throne exists.", "Jesse's sons are listed, with David seventh in this record. His sisters Zeruiah and Abigail connect the family to Abishai, Joab, Asahel and Amasa. Men later encountered as commanders and rivals therefore enter first through kinship. The genealogies also follow Caleb son of Hezron, his marriages and descendants. Through Hur and Uri they reach Bezalel, a name associated with skilled work in the wider scriptural history. Other branches move into Gilead, settlements and the relationships of Machir's house. The record continues across family and place rather than abandoning every branch that does not lead directly to the king. Judah's life includes far more people than the dynasty at its best known point.", "Jerahmeel's descendants receive extended attention. Some men die without children, while other lines continue through sons and daughters. Sheshan has daughters but no sons and gives one daughter in marriage to Jarha, his Egyptian servant. Their descendants are traced through many generations. The passage preserves that connection within Judah's record. Its attention to interrupted and continuing households gives a human texture to the names. The reader is not given a complete biography of those involved, but the family relationships are treated as worth keeping. A line can continue through circumstances different from the simple pattern a reader might initially expect.", "The later notices connect Caleb's branches with towns such as Ziph, Hebron, Maon, Beth-zur, Kiriath-jearim and Bethlehem. Concubines and daughters are named, and the closing verse remembers groups of scribes associated with Jabez and Kenite families connected with Rechab's house. The chapter has moved from Judah's sons through David's immediate family into a broader network of settlements and occupations. That movement matters for reading Chronicles. Its concern with the king does not eliminate the communities among whom kingship existed. The preserved names can direct attention to those whose contribution was to maintain households, towns and the work of recording itself. The next chapter will focus upon David's descendants while this wider family setting remains behind them."], "lesson": "A truthful family memory includes failure, unexpected connections and people outside its most celebrated line. The chapter invites care for names and lives that public histories can easily pass over.", "question": "How do the branches that do not lead directly to David enrich the meaning of the genealogy?"},
  {"id": 94002, "title": "David's Descendants Beyond the Loss of the Throne", "subtitle": "Continuity through a broken royal history", "text": "1 Chronicles 3:1–24", "chapters": [[12, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The record names David's sons born at Hebron and identifies their mothers. Amnon, Daniel, Absalom, Adonijah, Shephatiah and Ithream belong to the years when he ruled Judah there. The account then moves to Jerusalem, recalling his thirty three years in the city and naming sons born there, including Solomon and Nathan. Tamar is also remembered among the family, together with the existence of children born to concubines. Familiar names bring memories from Samuel into the genealogy: rival claims, family violence and the suffering of a daughter. The list itself supplies no new scenes, but readers who know the earlier account carry those lives into the record.", "Solomon's line follows through the kings of Judah: Rehoboam, Abijah, Asa, Jehoshaphat and the generations that lead to Hezekiah, Manasseh and Josiah. The chapter gives the dynasty's continuity in a compressed form. Its names do not imply equal faithfulness. The histories already read contain reforms, idolatry, deliverance and judgment among them. Here the continuing line is the point of attention. A later reader can trace a relationship across reigns whose events often seemed capable of ending the whole family. The promise concerning David's house therefore stands within a real succession of people whose choices still required examination before God.", "Josiah's sons and the descendants associated with Jehoiakim and Jeconiah carry the record toward the exile. It then continues with names including Shealtiel, Pedaiah and Zerubbabel. Sons and a daughter of Zerubbabel are listed, followed by further generations. The genealogy does not stop where the royal throne in Jerusalem was lost. People continue to be born and remembered. That movement gives a quiet but important answer to any assumption that the burning of a palace made the family cease to exist. The record preserves continuity without claiming that every name represents a ruler who actually occupied the old throne.", "The chapter ends with later descendants rather than a new coronation. It gives no invented account of how each person lived or what every branch expected. Its form asks the reader to distinguish the survival of a family from the uninterrupted possession of royal power. David's descendants remain present within the community's memory beyond defeat and displacement. As Chronicles turns to other branches of Judah, this particular record has already established the line important to its later narrative of the kingdom and temple. The names locate hope within generations of actual people, while the earlier history keeps that hope joined to accountability, loss and God's continuing word."], "lesson": "A period of public loss does not make every relationship or promise disappear. The continuing family record invites patient hope that also remembers the responsibilities of those named.", "question": "Why is it significant that the genealogy continues after the kings associated with exile?"},
  {"id": 94003, "title": "Jabez Prays Among the Families and Workers", "subtitle": "God hears within ordinary family life", "text": "1 Chronicles 4:1–43", "chapters": [[12, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The account continues with Judah's families, tracing descendants through towns and related households. Within the sequence it pauses over Jabez, described as more honourable than his brothers. His mother had given him a name connected with the pain of his birth. Jabez called upon the God of Israel for blessing, enlarged territory, God's hand with him and protection from harm that would bring pain. The narrator states that God granted his request. The notice gives no long biography or method for obtaining every desired outcome. It preserves one man's prayer within a family record, showing that a life otherwise known through a name could be marked by dependence upon God.", "Other names follow, including Othniel and descendants linked with a valley of craftsmen. Caleb son of Jephunneh appears, as do families associated with towns, fine linen and pottery. Bithiah, identified as Pharaoh's daughter, is named within a marriage connection. Workers living among plantations and settlements served the king. The list places prayer, kinship and skilled labour within the same memory of Judah. It does not reserve attention only for those whose deeds would fill a royal chronicle. Crafts, marriage connections and the maintenance of towns are part of the people's life. The occupations matter because the kingdom and its worship depended upon work spread far beyond the palace.", "The chapter then turns to Simeon's descendants. Shimei's large family is noted, while other branches had not grown as numerous as Judah. Their towns and surrounding villages are listed, including Beersheba, Moladah, Hormah and Ziklag. Leaders are named before the account describes the search for pasture. Some families found broad, peaceful land with good grazing near Gedor. In Hezekiah's days they attacked its earlier inhabitants and settled there for their flocks. The record therefore includes movement and conflict as well as established residence. It tells how households and livelihoods became connected with particular places, keeping the costs and force of settlement within the account.", "Another five hundred Simeonites went to Seir under four leaders from Ishi's family and defeated a surviving Amalekite group. The chapter closes with their continued residence. Its movement has reached from a brief prayer to craftsmen's work, towns, pasture and armed migration. These are different aspects of a community whose memory cannot be reduced to a single famous person's request. Jabez's words remain within that setting. He called upon Israel's God while the surrounding names tell of people seeking a place, sustaining families and serving through their labour. Their preservation asks the reader to notice ordinary lives as well as the exceptional moment when a prayer receives an explicit answer."], "lesson": "God hears prayer within the real needs of family and work. The surrounding names also invite respect for the craftspeople and households whose service sustains a wider community.", "question": "How does reading Jabez's prayer within the surrounding family and work records affect your understanding of it?"},
  {"id": 94004, "title": "The Eastern Tribes Cry Out and Later Turn Away", "subtitle": "Past help still calls for continuing faithfulness", "text": "1 Chronicles 5:1–26", "chapters": [[12, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The record of Reuben begins with an explanation of his lost firstborn privilege. Because he violated his father's bed, the birthright passed to Joseph's sons. Judah supplied the leading ruler, while the birthright belonged to Joseph. The genealogy therefore does not simply follow rank by age. Conduct and the different place of each tribe matter to the account. Reuben's descendants and settlements east of the Jordan are named, including Beerah, a leader carried into exile by Assyria. The memory of removal appears early in a passage that will also remember strength and victory. Its later outcome is already part of the family's recorded history.", "Gad's families occupied Bashan and Gilead, with their leaders and neighbouring pasturelands named. Their registers were reckoned during the reigns of Jotham in Judah and Jeroboam in Israel. Reuben, Gad and half Manasseh together fielded a large force trained with shield, sword and bow. They fought the Hagrites and related groups. In battle they cried to God and were helped because they trusted Him. The narrative records the capture of people and great quantities of livestock and attributes the victory to God's action. It also says they occupied the conquered territory until the exile. Their remembered military success is therefore placed within a longer account that reaches beyond it.", "The half tribe of Manasseh spread through its eastern territory toward Hermon. Its household heads were courageous and well known. Yet these tribes became unfaithful to their fathers' God and followed the gods of the peoples previously removed from the land. God stirred the Assyrian rulers who carried them away to Halah, Habor, Hara and the river Gozan. The same passage that remembers their cry of dependence also names their later apostasy. It permits neither the earlier victory nor the leaders' reputation to stand as proof that the following generations remained faithful. The location of exile closes the record of people once settled confidently with their flocks and strongholds.", "The chapter's beginning and ending ask the reader to consider responsibility within inherited standing. Reuben's birthright was affected by wrongdoing, and the eastern tribes' later life in the land was affected by their chosen worship. Between those notices, genuine help came when they called upon God. The narrator does not cancel that help because of what followed, nor use it to excuse the later course. Memory must hold both. As the book turns next to Levi, the reader has already seen that lists of descent can carry searching judgments concerning trust, conduct and allegiance. A preserved family name is an invitation to remember faithfully what happened within the generations it connects."], "lesson": "An earlier answer to prayer calls for continuing trust rather than complacency. The chapter asks how faithfully we receive the help and responsibilities handed to us.", "question": "Why does the account keep the battle prayer and the later exile within the same family history?"},
  {"id": 94005, "title": "The Families Who Served in Song and Sanctuary", "subtitle": "Worship rests upon remembered responsibilities", "text": "1 Chronicles 6:1–81", "chapters": [[12, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 81, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The descendants of Levi are traced through Gershon, Kohath and Merari. From Kohath's family the record reaches Amram and his children Aaron, Moses and Miriam. Aaron's line continues through Eleazar and Phinehas toward the priests who served in Jerusalem. The names include Zadok, Ahimaaz, Hilkiah and Seraiah, and the sequence reaches Jehozadak, who went into captivity when Judah and Jerusalem were taken by Nebuchadnezzar. The priestly record therefore stretches from the early generations of Israel's worship to the exile. Office did not remove these families from the nation's losses. Their continuity is preserved through names even when the temple's public service could no longer continue as before.", "Other Levitical branches are listed, including the family connections of Samuel. The account then names those David appointed over the service of song after the ark came to rest. Heman, Asaph and Ethan stand within traced family lines, with their positions and ancestry remembered. They and their children ministered in music before the sacred tent and later in the temple Solomon built, serving according to their order. The genealogy gives a history to voices that might otherwise be remembered only as part of a ceremony. Their worship was sustained by families, instruction and repeated service. Other Levites carried out further responsibilities belonging to the sanctuary.", "Aaron and his sons had particular duties at the burnt offering and incense altars and in the most holy work, making atonement according to the command given through Moses. The record distinguishes those responsibilities from the wider service of the Levites. It then turns toward the towns assigned to their households. Aaron's descendants received places in Judah and Benjamin, including Hebron, Libnah, Beth-shemesh, Geba and Anathoth. The fields around Hebron remained associated with Caleb. Other Kohathites, Gershonites and Merarites received towns from different tribes, with pastureland for their livelihood. Their service had a place within Israel's shared provision of homes and resources.", "The list reaches through Ephraim, Manasseh, Issachar, Asher, Naphtali, Zebulun, Reuben and Gad, including refuge towns on both sides of the Jordan. Worship at a central sanctuary was thus supported by households spread through the land. The chapter has brought ancestry, appointment, sacrifice, music and dwelling places into one record. Those who sang or served needed continuing provision, and the wider nation had a part in supplying it. The exile named near the beginning gives these memories a further weight. The reader can see how deeply the sanctuary's life was woven into Israel's families and places before its disruption. Remembering the work also preserves an understanding of what faithful service involves when the community seeks to order its life again."], "lesson": "Public worship depends upon many distinct responsibilities and the provision that supports them. The names invite gratitude for faithful service continued across generations.", "question": "How do the towns and pasturelands help explain the practical life behind the sanctuary's worship?"},
  {"id": 94006, "title": "Families, Warriors and a House That Mourned", "subtitle": "Strength and sorrow belong to the same record", "text": "1 Chronicles 7:1–40", "chapters": [[12, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The genealogies turn to Issachar, naming Tola's descendants and the leaders and warriors counted in David's days. Numbers are connected with households, marriages and generations. Benjamin's branches also include capable fighters, while Naphtali receives a brief record through Bilhah's sons. The different lengths given to tribes show that the chapter preserves particular records rather than an identical form for every family. Its numbers concern actual people organised within kinship and military responsibility. The account does not explain an individual's character merely because he belongs to a large company. It remembers the strength available to communities whose later history will involve the gathering of Israel around David.", "Manasseh's record includes Machir and Gilead, the names of mothers and sisters and a notice that Zelophehad had daughters. The genealogy therefore carries women within the history of family continuity. The account moves through different branches before turning to Ephraim. Here it pauses over loss. Ezer and Elead were killed by men of Gath in a conflict involving livestock. Ephraim mourned for many days, and relatives came to comfort him. Another son was named Beriah in connection with the trouble that had entered the household. The grief is given space among the lists, interrupting any impression that a genealogy records only successful growth.", "Sherah is remembered for building Lower and Upper Beth-horon and Uzzen-sherah. The family line continues toward Nun and Joshua. Settlements of Joseph's descendants are then named, including Bethel, Gezer, Shechem, Beth-shean, Taanach, Megiddo and Dor. These details connect people with work, leadership and land. The account of mourning does not end the family's presence within the record, but its later continuity does not erase the deaths. The mention of a woman who built towns also widens the picture of contribution beyond the fighting men counted elsewhere. The chapter preserves more than one kind of strength within its remembered households.", "Asher's descendants close the passage, with sons, daughters, family heads and warriors recorded. Twenty six thousand men were reckoned fit for battle. The chapter has begun and ended with military capacity, yet the intervening notices require the reader to keep relationships, sorrow, comfort and building within that picture. Families could supply defenders and still need neighbours to sit with them in grief. They could inherit names and places while also making new contributions. These records prepare for a narrative concerned with David's gathered nation, but they ensure that the people being gathered already possess histories. The names are a memory of lives that did not begin when they first came into a king's service."], "lesson": "A community's strength includes care for the grieving and contributions beyond public power. The family record invites us to remember losses alongside achievements.", "question": "What does the account of Ephraim's mourning add to the surrounding notices of warriors and settlements?"},
  {"id": 94007, "title": "Benjamin's Families and Saul's Descendants", "subtitle": "A fallen king's house remains within the people", "text": "1 Chronicles 8:1–40", "chapters": [[12, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The record returns to Benjamin with a fuller account of his descendants. It follows branches through Bela and other sons, naming household heads and their movements between places. Some families were removed from Geba to Manahath. Shaharaim's children in Moab and his marriages are recorded, followed by other branches whose descendants built Ono and Lod. Beriah and Shema are connected with Aijalon and the driving out of Gath's inhabitants. The chapter therefore gives Benjamin a remembered life of migration, marriage, conflict and settlement before concentrating on its best known royal household. Its people are more than the tribe from which Saul happened to emerge.", "Several groups of family heads are listed as living in Jerusalem. The account then reaches Gibeon, naming its leading household and related descendants who also lived near their relatives in the capital. Ner, Kish and Saul enter within those family connections. Saul's sons include Jonathan, Malchishua, Abinadab and Esh-baal. Jonathan's line continues through Merib-baal and Micah. The genealogy remembers a family whose reign has already ended in the earlier biblical books. Its inclusion here keeps the royal defeat from becoming an excuse to erase the people who descended from that house. The coming narrative will judge Saul's conduct, while the family record still gives his descendants their names.", "Micah's descendants continue through several generations toward Azel and his six sons. Eshek's family and the sons of Ulam are also named. Ulam's sons are remembered as brave archers, and their descendants form a large company. The line therefore continues beyond Saul's sons and their immediate political importance. The text offers no suggestion that every later person inherited the same office or repeated the same choices. It preserves family continuity within Benjamin. The recorded archers belong to a community whose capacities and relationships remained part of Israel even when the throne passed to a different tribe and household.", "The chapter closes by identifying all these as Benjamin's descendants. Its repeated family heads and places can require patient reading, but that form carries a purpose. People who appear briefly in a conflict can have generations before and after their public moment. A king's failure is part of his nation's story, yet a genealogy can also remember those connected to him whose own lives continued. Chronicles will soon turn toward David with clear conviction about his kingship. Before doing so, it has supplied this substantial record of Benjamin and Saul's house. The reader is invited to keep judgment truthful without allowing political replacement to become the disappearance of a whole family from memory."], "lesson": "Truthful remembrance can acknowledge a leader's failure while still recognising the lives connected with his household. The names call for care in how we remember people after public defeat.", "question": "Why does the continuation of Saul's family matter in a book moving toward David's reign?"},
  {"id": 94008, "title": "The Gates Open Each Morning", "subtitle": "A community remembers the work that sustains worship", "text": "1 Chronicles 9:1–44", "chapters": [[12, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The chapter opens by recalling Israel's genealogical records and Judah's exile to Babylon because of unfaithfulness. It then identifies inhabitants in their towns and those living in Jerusalem from Judah, Benjamin, Ephraim and Manasseh. Priests, Levites and temple servants appear among them. Family heads and capable workers are counted. The record places the community's identity beside the history of judgment, keeping both ancestry and responsibility before the reader. The life of Jerusalem includes people who must carry out ordinary duties if worship is to continue. Their service becomes the chapter's particular concern before it returns again to Saul's family.", "Gatekeepers are named through their households and their connection with earlier service at the sacred tent. The account remembers Phinehas's leadership and the Lord's presence with him, and it associates the established offices with David and Samuel. Two hundred and twelve gatekeepers were reckoned through their villages. They watched entrances on all four sides, while relatives arrived in scheduled periods to share the work. Senior Levites had charge of rooms and treasuries. They lodged near the house because they were responsible for opening it each morning. The familiar beginning of a worship day depended upon people whose watch had continued before others arrived.", "Different workers cared for vessels, counting them in and out, and for flour, wine, oil, incense and spices. Priestly families prepared the blended substances, one Levite had responsibility for baked offerings and Kohathite members prepared the bread set out each Sabbath. Singers stayed available for work by day and night and were relieved from other duties. The details show a service requiring trust, knowledge, scheduling and repeated care. Sacred objects still had to be accounted for; supplies had to be ready. The chapter gives honour to work whose success might be noticed chiefly when something was missing or a gate remained closed.", "The closing verses repeat the line from Gibeon through Kish and Saul to Jonathan's descendants and Azel's sons. That return prepares for the next chapter's account of Saul's death. Before entering the royal narrative, the reader has been shown the people maintaining entrances, stores, bread and song. The book's concern with kingship and the ark will therefore unfold against an already established account of shared service. Memory can preserve a military disaster and a dynasty's change, but it can also preserve the person who opened the doors at dawn. The ordered life of worship rests upon such duties, carried through families and made visible here by the careful naming of those entrusted with them."], "lesson": "Faithfulness often appears through regular duties completed with care. The gatekeepers and supply keepers invite respect for work that supports others' worship without drawing attention to itself.", "question": "Which responsibility in this chapter helps you see a usually unnoticed form of service more clearly?"},
  {"id": 94009, "title": "Saul Falls on Mount Gilboa", "subtitle": "A kingdom changes hands under God's judgment", "text": "1 Chronicles 10:1–14", "chapters": [[12, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The family records have brought the reader to Saul's household. Now the account turns from names to the day that broke his reign. The Philistines fought against Israel, and the Israelite army fled on Mount Gilboa. Saul's sons Jonathan, Abinadab and Malchi-shua were killed. The king himself came under the archers' attack and was wounded. The royal family could not stand apart from the defeat spreading through the army. The men who had led Israel were falling, and the consequences would reach the towns whose families had trusted them for protection.", "Saul feared what the Philistines would do if they captured him alive. He asked his armourbearer to kill him, but the man was too afraid to obey. Saul then fell on his own sword. When the armourbearer saw that Saul was dead, he also took his own life. The account gives these deaths without treating them as an example to follow. They belong to the terrible collapse of Saul's kingship. When the Israelites in the valley saw that their army had fled and that Saul and his sons were dead, they abandoned their cities. The Philistines moved into the places they had left. Defeat on the mountain became displacement in ordinary homes.", "The following day the Philistines found Saul and his sons while stripping the slain. They took Saul's head and armour, spread news of their victory and placed the trophies in the houses of their gods. His head was fastened in the temple of Dagon. Israel's loss was being presented as a triumph for Philistine religion. Yet the men of Jabesh-gilead did not leave Saul's body dishonoured. Their valiant men recovered his body and the bodies of his sons, brought them to Jabesh and buried their bones beneath the oak. They fasted seven days. The burial preserved an act of loyalty within a chapter of judgment, although it could not restore the fallen kingdom.", "Chronicles closes the account by explaining Saul's death in relation to his unfaithfulness to God. He had failed to keep the Lord's word and had sought counsel through a medium. The writer describes his failure to seek the Lord as part of this disobedient course. The defeat therefore receives a deeper explanation than Philistine military strength. God brought Saul's reign to an end and turned the kingdom to David, Jesse's son. This short chapter carries great weight after the long genealogies. Family position, royal office and an army could not preserve a ruler who refused God's authority. The story also prepares for David without asking the reader to enjoy Saul's suffering. A new reign begins against the memory of real loss."], "lesson": "Authority must remain answerable to God. Saul's end warns against treating spiritual disobedience as a private matter that cannot affect others.", "question": "Where do I need to obey God's word instead of seeking an answer that excuses my own course?"},
  {"id": 94010, "title": "David Receives the Kingdom and Remembers His Mighty Men", "subtitle": "God's promise is fulfilled through shared courage", "text": "1 Chronicles 11:1–47", "chapters": [[12, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["All Israel gathered to David at Hebron. The elders acknowledged their kinship with him and remembered that he had led Israel even while Saul was king. They also recalled the Lord's word that David would shepherd His people and become their ruler. David made a covenant with them before God, and they anointed him king over Israel according to the word spoken through Samuel. The throne was therefore more than the reward of an ambitious soldier. The people were recognising a responsibility God had already announced. Their language of shepherding placed care for Israel beside the authority to rule it.", "David and all Israel then went against Jerusalem, still occupied by the Jebusites. Its inhabitants declared that he would not enter, but David captured the stronghold of Zion. He had promised command to the first man who struck the Jebusites, and Joab went up first and became chief. David settled in the fortress, which received the name City of David. He built around it, and Joab repaired the rest. His increasing strength is explained by the presence of the Lord of hosts with him. The growing city and the established throne were visible achievements, but the account refuses to make David's ability their final explanation.", "The chapter then names the mighty men who strengthened David's kingdom together with Israel. Jashobeam's courage and Eleazar's stand in a barley field receive special notice. When others fled, the field was defended, and the Lord brought a great deliverance. Another memory comes from David's days at the stronghold, when a Philistine garrison held Bethlehem. David longed for water from its well. Three men broke through the enemy lines, drew the water and carried it back. David would not drink it. He poured it out to the Lord because their lives had been placed in danger to obtain it. His desire did not give him the right to consume their costly devotion as though it were an ordinary convenience.", "Abishai and Benaiah also receive accounts of their courage. Benaiah's exploits include confronting a lion in a pit on a snowy day and overcoming a formidable Egyptian with the man's own spear. David placed him over his guard. The longer list then preserves men from many places and backgrounds, including Uriah the Hittite. These names keep the reader from imagining that one famous king carried the whole kingdom alone. Ranks and distinctions are recorded without erasing the others who served. David's establishment fulfilled God's promise, yet that fulfilment involved people who stood, fought, repaired and risked their lives. His refusal to drink the Bethlehem water gives a needed measure for leadership. Those who serve a ruler must never become objects for satisfying his wishes."], "lesson": "God's help does not make other people's service disposable. Good leadership receives loyalty with gratitude and respects the lives behind it.", "question": "Do I recognise the cost borne by people whose work supports my responsibilities?"},
  {"id": 94011, "title": "Helpers Gather Until There Is Joy in Israel", "subtitle": "Many tribes unite around the promised king", "text": "1 Chronicles 12:1–40", "chapters": [[12, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["Before David's public recognition at Hebron, men had joined him during the uncertainty of his earlier life. Some came to Ziklag while Saul still threatened him. Among them were Benjaminites from Saul's own tribe, skilled with bows and able to use either hand for stones and arrows. Their arrival showed that kinship with Saul did not determine every man's allegiance. Others came from Gad to the wilderness stronghold. They were trained warriors, ready with shield and spear, described through the strength of lions and the speed of mountain deer. Their crossing of the Jordan while it overflowed its banks marked the determination with which they reached David.", "When another company from Benjamin and Judah arrived, David went out to meet them. He did not assume that every visitor was trustworthy. If they came peaceably, he was ready to join his heart with theirs. If they intended betrayal, he appealed to the God of their fathers to judge it. Then the Spirit came upon Amasai, who declared their allegiance to David and wished peace to him and his helpers because God helped him. David received them and appointed them among his captains. Trust here developed through a clear declaration of purpose. Courage and discernment belonged together during days when betrayal could endanger many lives.", "Men from Manasseh also joined David around the time when the Philistine leaders sent him away from their campaign against Saul. They helped him against raiding bands and became part of the growing company around him. The account describes helpers arriving day after day until the gathering became a great host. It then turns to the armed contingents who came to Hebron to transfer Saul's kingdom to David according to God's word. Judah, Simeon, Levi, Benjamin, Ephraim, Manasseh and the other tribes appear with their numbers and responsibilities. Issachar's leaders understood the times and knew what Israel ought to do. Zebulun's men could keep their ranks and were not divided in heart. The qualities remembered include judgment and steadiness as well as military strength.", "The gathering at Hebron ended in shared celebration. The men stayed with David for three days, eating and drinking what their fellow Israelites had prepared. People from nearby regions and from as far as Issachar, Zebulun and Naphtali brought food on animals. Bread, flour, fruit, wine, oil, cattle and sheep supplied the gathering. The chapter ends by saying there was joy in Israel. That joy required the work of people who brought provisions as well as the presence of warriors and leaders. A nation was coming together around the king God had appointed. The account joins years of costly support to the public gladness of recognition. What appeared at Hebron had been growing through many earlier decisions to stand with David."], "lesson": "Unity requires faithful decisions, clear purpose and practical support. God remembers both visible courage and the work that feeds and sustains others.", "question": "What practical part can I take in supporting a responsibility that God has placed before our community?"},
  {"id": 94012, "title": "The Ark's Journey Stops at a Threshing Floor", "subtitle": "A willing crowd must still learn God's order", "text": "1 Chronicles 13:1–14", "chapters": [[12, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David consulted the commanders and the leaders of Israel about bringing the ark of God to them. He proposed sending throughout the land to gather their fellow Israelites, including the priests and Levites in their towns. He wanted the ark restored to its place in the nation's attention, acknowledging that they had not sought it during Saul's days. The assembly agreed. The proposal seemed right to all the people, and David gathered Israel from the southern region near Egypt to the entrance of Hamath. The decision carried national support and a stated desire to honour God. The coming disaster would therefore be especially disturbing. Enthusiasm and agreement had made the journey feel right from its beginning.", "David and the people went to Kirjath-jearim, also called Baalah, to bring the ark from Abinadab's house. The ark belonged to the Lord whose name was called upon it, the God enthroned above the cherubim. They placed it on a new cart, with Uzzah and Ahio driving. David and all Israel celebrated with their full strength. Singing and the sounds of harps, stringed instruments, tambourines, cymbals and trumpets accompanied the procession. The account lets the reader hear the joy before describing its interruption. No one could accuse the people of making the occasion small. Yet the ark's holiness was not determined by the size of their celebration or by the newness of the cart.", "At the threshing floor of Chidon, the oxen stumbled. Uzzah stretched out his hand to steady the ark. God's anger broke out against him because he touched it, and he died there before God. The procession stopped under a judgment it had not expected. David was distressed by what had happened and named the place Perez-uzzah in memory of the breach. He also became afraid of God that day. His question was now how he could bring the ark home to himself. The chapter does not resolve the difficulty by imagining Uzzah's private thoughts or by inventing words God did not speak. It records the act, the judgment and the fear that followed.", "David did not take the ark into his own city. He turned it aside to the house of Obed-edom the Gittite, where it remained for three months. During that time the Lord blessed Obed-edom's household and all that belonged to him. The same ark associated with the halted procession was present in a house that received blessing. The ending keeps the story from suggesting that God's presence was simply a danger to be avoided. What David and Israel needed was a right approach to the holy God they wished to honour. A later chapter will explain the neglected order for carrying the ark. For now the interrupted journey leaves room for fear, reflection and a change in practice."], "lesson": "A sincere desire to worship must be guided by God's word. Public approval and strong feeling cannot replace obedience to what He has commanded.", "question": "Where might I be relying on good intentions while neglecting instructions God has already given?"},
  {"id": 94013, "title": "David Asks Again Before the Second Battle", "subtitle": "Success does not remove the need for guidance", "text": "1 Chronicles 14:1–17", "chapters": [[12, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["Hiram king of Tyre sent messengers, cedar timber, masons and carpenters to build David a house. The support of another king helped make David's position visible beyond Israel. David understood his establishment as God's work, and he recognised its purpose in relation to God's people. His kingdom had been lifted up for Israel's sake. This understanding placed a duty beneath the honour of his throne. The chapter also records the growth of his household in Jerusalem and names the children born there, including Nathan and Solomon. His public strength and family were increasing, but another challenge soon showed that an established throne still needed the Lord's help.", "When the Philistines heard that David had been anointed king over all Israel, they came to seek him. David heard and went out against them. They spread themselves through the Valley of Rephaim. Before attacking, David asked God whether he should go and whether the enemy would be delivered into his hand. God told him to go and promised that deliverance. David obeyed and defeated them at Baal-perazim. He compared God's action to water breaking through a barrier. The victory received a name that directed attention to the Lord's power rather than allowing the king to claim that his growing reputation had secured it.", "The Philistines left their gods behind, and David ordered them burned. Their idols had not preserved their army. Yet the defeated enemy returned and spread out in the valley again. David could have assumed that the earlier instruction still supplied every detail for a second encounter. Instead, he asked God again. This time the command was different. He was not to advance directly behind them. He was to circle round and approach opposite the mulberry trees. A sound of marching in the treetops would tell him when to move, because God would have gone before him to strike the Philistine army. The second battle required fresh attention as well as remembered confidence.", "David did as God commanded. The Philistines were struck from Gibeon to Gezer, and David's fame spread through the surrounding lands. The Lord caused the nations to fear him. The chapter thus begins with recognition from Tyre and ends with a wider reputation following the defeat of a persistent enemy. Between those public signs stand two enquiries and two acts of obedience. David's strength did not make prayer unnecessary. Nor did the first answer permit him to stop listening. The story offers a practical picture of dependence under changing circumstances. Past help gave David grounds to trust God, while the new situation called him to seek and follow God's direction again."], "lesson": "Previous success should deepen dependence on God. A familiar problem may still require fresh prayer and careful obedience.", "question": "Am I assuming that yesterday's successful approach makes it unnecessary to seek God about today's decision?"},
  {"id": 94014, "title": "The Ark Is Carried on the Levites' Shoulders", "subtitle": "David corrects the earlier failure", "text": "1 Chronicles 15:1–29", "chapters": [[12, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David prepared a place for the ark in the City of David and pitched a tent for it. He also stated plainly who should carry it. God had chosen the Levites for this responsibility and for service before Him. Israel was gathered again, but the renewed effort included a correction that the first procession had lacked. David assembled the descendants of Aaron and the Levite families, naming their leaders and recording their numbers. Uriel, Asaiah, Joel and the other heads were called into the task. The interruption at Uzzah's death had not become an excuse to abandon worship. It had become a reason to learn how the duty should be performed.", "David summoned Zadok and Abiathar the priests together with the Levite leaders. He told them to consecrate themselves and their brothers for bringing the ark. He acknowledged that the earlier disaster had come because they had not sought God according to the required order. The king did not simply blame the man who had reached towards the ark. He recognised a failure in their approach. The priests and Levites consecrated themselves, and the Levites carried the ark on their shoulders with its poles, as Moses had commanded according to God's word. The corrected method was concrete. Repentance had reached the way the procession actually moved.", "Music also received thoughtful preparation. Levite leaders appointed singers and players, including Heman, Asaph and Ethan. Instruments had their places, priests sounded trumpets and doorkeepers attended the ark. Chenaniah instructed the singing because he understood the work. The record preserves both consecration and skill. People were not expected to choose between devotion and careful preparation. David, the elders and the commanders went to bring the ark from Obed-edom's house with joy. When God helped the Levites carrying it, they offered seven bulls and seven rams. The procession that once stopped in fear now continued under God's help, with responsibility placed where His word had directed.", "David wore a robe of fine linen and a linen ephod. Shouting, horns, trumpets, cymbals, harps and other instruments accompanied the ark into the city. The great sound of rejoicing had returned, now joined to the obedience missing before. Yet everyone did not receive the scene in the same spirit. Michal, Saul's daughter, looked through a window, saw David dancing and celebrating, and despised him in her heart. The chapter ends with that private response beside the public joy. It does not unfold their later conversation here. Its main movement has carried Israel from an acknowledged failure to corrected worship. David's willingness to learn did not make him less joyful. It made possible a procession that could reach its appointed place."], "lesson": "True correction changes practice. Reverence, preparation, skill and joy can belong together when God's word governs worship.", "question": "What specific change should follow from a mistake God has already helped me recognise?"},
  {"id": 94015, "title": "Praise Continues After the Celebration Ends", "subtitle": "The ark's arrival establishes a daily responsibility", "text": "1 Chronicles 16:1–43", "chapters": [[12, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The ark was placed inside the tent David had prepared. Burnt offerings and peace offerings were presented, and David blessed the people in the Lord's name. Every Israelite, both men and women, received a portion of food. The gathering was therefore remembered through shared provision as well as sacrifice and music. David then appointed Levites to minister before the ark, to remember, thank and praise the God of Israel. Asaph led, with other men assigned to instruments and trumpets. The day did not end with the ark simply being left in its new place. People received responsibility for worship that would continue after the crowd went home.", "David entrusted a song of thanksgiving to Asaph and his brothers. It called the people to thank God, call upon His name and make His deeds known. They were to sing, speak of His works and seek His strength continually. Remembering had a central place. Israel was to remember God's wonders, judgments and covenant with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. Their present celebration stood within a longer history of promise and preservation. When their ancestors were few and moved among nations, God had protected them. The song directed a settled people back to the days when their survival could not have been explained by national strength.", "The invitation then widened beyond Israel. All the earth was called to sing and announce God's salvation. The nations were to hear His glory and His works. Their gods were idols, but the Lord had made the heavens. Families of the peoples were summoned to bring Him honour, offer before Him and worship in holiness. Heaven, earth, sea, fields and trees were pictured joining the rejoicing because the Lord reigns and comes to judge. Israel's covenant memory did not make God's glory a private possession. The song joined thanksgiving to witness, and joy to the truth that the world belongs to its Creator. It also included a prayer for salvation, gathering and deliverance, so that the people could continue thanking and praising Him.", "The people answered with their agreement and praise. Then David established the continuing service. Asaph and his brothers remained before the ark to perform each day's work. Obed-edom and others served as gatekeepers. At Gibeon, Zadok and the priests attended the tabernacle and offered the morning and evening sacrifices according to the law. Heman, Jeduthun and other named servants took their places in thanksgiving and music. Finally, everyone returned home, and David went to bless his own household. The closing movement brings the great assembly into ordinary time. Daily duties, regular offerings and family life followed the public celebration. The praise was meant to endure because people had been appointed to carry it forward when the exceptional day was over."], "lesson": "Lasting worship needs both grateful remembrance and steady daily service. A moving occasion should lead into a life that continues to seek God.", "question": "What regular practice will help my thanksgiving continue after a special service or answered prayer?"},
  {"id": 94016, "title": "David Wants to Build a House and Receives a Promise", "subtitle": "God's purpose reaches beyond the king's plans", "text": "1 Chronicles 17:1–27", "chapters": [[12, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David sat in his cedar house and spoke to Nathan about the contrast before him. The king lived in a substantial building, while the ark of God's covenant remained beneath curtains. He wanted to do something about it. Nathan first encouraged him to carry out what was in his heart because God was with him. Yet that night God gave Nathan a different message. David was not to build the house. The prophet had to return with a word that corrected his earlier encouragement. Even a proposal that seemed honourable needed to remain subject to what God actually said.", "God reminded David that He had not lived in a permanent house since bringing Israel out of Egypt. He had moved with His people, and He had not demanded a cedar house from the judges appointed to shepherd them. The Lord then recalled David's own beginning. He had taken him from following sheep and made him ruler over Israel. He had been with him, defeated his enemies and given him a great name. David's present ability to consider a temple had itself come from gifts he had received. God also spoke of a secure place for Israel and the subduing of enemies. His care embraced the people, not only their king's desire to build.", "Then the meaning of a house changed within the promise. David had proposed a building for God, but God promised to establish David's house. After David's death, a son from his own family would succeed him. That son would build the house, and God would establish his kingdom. The Lord described a relationship of father and son and promised not to withdraw His mercy as He had from David's predecessor. The repeated language of enduring establishment carried the promise beyond the king's immediate project. Nathan delivered the whole message. David received both the boundary placed on his own work and the generosity of a future he could not have secured for himself.", "David went in and sat before the Lord. His prayer began with wonder at who he was and what his household was that God had brought him so far. The promise concerning the future deepened his gratitude. He praised God's uniqueness and remembered Israel's redemption from Egypt. Then he asked God to establish what He had spoken, so that God's name would be honoured and David's house would stand before Him. The promise gave him courage to pray. He did not argue that his rejected building plan deserved reconsideration. He rested in the goodness God had announced. The chapter ends with his request for blessing, confident that what the Lord blessed would remain blessed. David's intended gift had led him to recognise how much he still received."], "lesson": "God may redirect an honourable plan while giving a responsibility to someone else. Faith responds with grateful obedience and prayer grounded in His word.", "question": "Can I thank God for His purpose when my part in it differs from the part I wanted?"},
  {"id": 94017, "title": "Victories Bring Resources and New Responsibilities", "subtitle": "David's kingdom grows under God's preservation", "text": "1 Chronicles 18:1–17", "chapters": [[12, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["After the promise concerning his house, David's reign is shown extending through a series of victories. He subdued the Philistines and took Gath with its surrounding towns. Moab also came under his authority and brought tribute. He defeated Hadarezer king of Zobah as that ruler sought to establish his power towards the Euphrates. Chariots, horsemen and infantry fell into David's hands. He disabled most of the chariot horses while retaining enough for a hundred chariots. The account compresses major campaigns into a few sentences, but their place in the story is clear. The king whom God had taken from the sheepfold was becoming strong among the surrounding nations.", "When the Arameans of Damascus came to assist Hadarezer, David defeated them too. Garrisons were established, and the people brought tribute. The writer explains these results by saying that the Lord preserved David wherever he went. David brought the golden shields of Hadarezer's servants to Jerusalem. He also took a great quantity of bronze from Tibhath and Chun. Here the account looks forward to Solomon, who would use bronze for the great basin, the pillars and the temple vessels. Material taken during David's campaigns would eventually serve the building that David himself was not appointed to complete. The link between the two reigns is already being laid before the reader.", "Tou king of Hamath heard that David had defeated Hadarezer, with whom Tou had been at war. He sent his son Hadoram to congratulate David and enquire after him. Vessels of gold, silver and bronze accompanied the visit. David dedicated these gifts to the Lord along with the precious metals brought from other nations. The record gathers Edom, Moab, Ammon, Philistia and Amalek into that account of dedicated wealth. It then remembers Abishai's defeat of eighteen thousand Edomites in the Valley of Salt. Garrisons were placed in Edom, and the statement of God's preservation is repeated. Political success and accumulating resources are presented in relation to the Lord who had upheld David.", "The chapter closes inside the kingdom rather than on another battlefield. David ruled over all Israel and administered judgment and justice for his people. Joab commanded the army, Jehoshaphat served as recorder, priests attended their duties, Shavsha was scribe and Benaiah commanded the royal guard. David's sons held places near the king. These appointments show the work needed to govern what had been gained. A ruler's duty did not end when enemies submitted or valuable objects arrived in the capital. Justice had to reach the people under his care. The movement from campaigns to administration gives the victories a practical setting. God's preservation supported a kingdom whose ruler remained responsible for daily judgment as well as national strength."], "lesson": "Gifts, influence and success bring duties before God. Resources should be used in His service, and authority should produce justice for those entrusted to it.", "question": "How am I using increased resources or responsibility to serve God and deal fairly with others?"},
  {"id": 94018, "title": "An Act of Kindness Is Answered with Humiliation", "subtitle": "Suspicion turns a condolence visit into war", "text": "1 Chronicles 19:1–19", "chapters": [[12, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["Nahash king of Ammon died, and his son Hanun succeeded him. David remembered kindness that Nahash had shown him and sent messengers to comfort the new king concerning his father's death. The visitors came with a peaceful purpose. Hanun's princes, however, urged him to suspect them. They argued that David's men had come to inspect the land and prepare its overthrow. Nothing in the account supports that accusation. The advice transformed an expression of sympathy into an imagined threat, and Hanun acted on it. He had the men shaved, cut their garments shamefully short and sent them away in public humiliation.", "When David learned what had happened, he sent to meet his servants. He understood their shame and told them to remain at Jericho until their beards grew before returning. His response gave them time and shelter from further embarrassment. The Ammonites recognised that they had made themselves offensive to David, but they answered the danger by preparing for battle. Hanun and his people spent a thousand talents of silver to hire chariots and horsemen from Mesopotamia and the Aramean kingdoms of Maacah and Zobah. The forces gathered near Medeba, while Ammon assembled its own men from the cities. A choice founded on suspicion had now drawn other nations into its consequences.", "David sent Joab and the army. Joab found the Ammonites arrayed near the city gate and their hired allies in the open country. With enemies before and behind, he divided his forces. He took chosen men against the Arameans and placed the others under his brother Abishai against Ammon. The brothers agreed to help each other if either force proved too strong. Joab called for courage on behalf of their people and the cities of their God, while leaving the result to the Lord's judgment. Their plan required mutual support. As Joab advanced, the Arameans fled. Seeing their allies retreat, the Ammonites also withdrew into the city. Joab then returned to Jerusalem.", "The conflict did not end there. The defeated Arameans brought reinforcements from beyond the Euphrates under Shophach, Hadarezer's commander. David gathered Israel, crossed the Jordan and faced them. The Arameans fled again, many were killed and Shophach fell. Hadarezer's servants made peace with David and would no longer help Ammon. The chapter follows a painful chain from untested suspicion to insult, military expenditure, battle and loss. It also preserves two different ways of handling vulnerable people. Hanun exposed innocent messengers to shame, while David gave those same men a place to recover. Joab and Abishai's agreement offers another contrast in conduct within the story. Facing danger together, they prepared to support whichever brother needed help rather than treating his difficulty as his own concern alone."], "lesson": "Unfounded suspicion can cause harm far beyond the first accusation. Wise counsel tests claims, protects dignity and makes room for mutual help.", "question": "Do I check a troubling accusation before acting on it, and do I protect people who have been unfairly shamed?"},
  {"id": 94019, "title": "Rabbah Falls and David's Servants Face Giants", "subtitle": "The record preserves victory and its severity", "text": "1 Chronicles 20:1–8", "chapters": [[12, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["When the season for military campaigning returned, Joab led the army into Ammonite territory. He devastated the countryside and besieged Rabbah, while David remained in Jerusalem. Joab struck the city and brought it down. Chronicles tells this episode briefly, keeping its attention here on the Ammonite war rather than recounting every event associated with that season in Samuel. The reader should follow what this account actually records. The campaign that grew from Hanun's treatment of David's messengers had reached the Ammonite capital. Walls and royal wealth could no longer prevent the consequences of the conflict that had begun with a rejected act of kindness.", "David took the defeated king's crown. It contained a talent of gold and precious stones, and it was placed on David's head. A great amount of spoil was also brought from the city. The next notice is severe. The King James wording describes the conquered people being cut with saws, iron implements and axes, and it says David dealt similarly with other Ammonite cities. That troubling statement must not disappear behind the splendour of the crown. The account records harsh treatment of the defeated alongside the material gains of victory. It does not turn that treatment into a command for readers to imitate. David and the people then returned to Jerusalem, leaving the reader with both the triumph and the human weight of conquest.", "The narrative moves to further wars with the Philistines. At Gezer, Sibbecai the Hushathite killed Sippai, a descendant of the giant, and the Philistines were subdued. In another battle, Elhanan son of Jair killed Lahmi, the brother of Goliath the Gittite. The size of Lahmi's spear shaft recalled the formidable strength associated with that family. The chapter carefully identifies Lahmi as Goliath's brother. David's earlier victory over Goliath was therefore not the final occasion when Israel faced an imposing enemy of this kind. Other servants now had their own battles to fight, and the record gives them their names and their place in the story.", "At Gath, another unusually large warrior appeared with six fingers on each hand and six toes on each foot. He defied Israel, and Jonathan son of Shimea, David's brother, killed him. The concluding sentence places these victories under David and his servants together. The king's household and army included people who could face threats rather than expecting their leader to perform every act of courage. Even so, this short chapter should be read with care. It contains courageous service, defeated enemies, royal trophies and a grim notice about captives. Faithful reading receives the whole account. It can recognise courage without allowing success to make every action seem admirable, and it can remember that warfare leaves people beneath the record of a kingdom's expanding power."], "lesson": "Courage in serving others deserves recognition, but victory must not make us careless about human suffering. Read difficult events honestly rather than hiding them.", "question": "Do I allow a person's success to prevent me from considering the suffering connected with their actions?"},
  {"id": 94020, "title": "David's Census Ends at Ornan's Threshing Floor", "subtitle": "Confession and mercy meet beneath a drawn sword", "text": "1 Chronicles 21:1–30", "chapters": [[12, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["Satan rose against Israel and incited David to number the people. David ordered Joab and the leaders to count from Beersheba to Dan and bring him the total. Joab objected. He wished that God would multiply the people greatly, but asked why the king wanted to bring guilt upon Israel. David's command prevailed. Joab went through the land and returned with figures of one million one hundred thousand fighting men in Israel and four hundred seventy thousand in Judah. He did not count Levi and Benjamin because he found the command abhorrent. The chapter presents this numbering as a sinful act, with a warning rejected by the king before the consequences fell.", "God was displeased, and David confessed that he had sinned greatly and acted foolishly. Through Gad, the Lord placed three judgments before him: three years of famine, three months of destruction by enemies, or three days of plague under the Lord's sword. David was deeply distressed. He appealed to God's great mercy and asked to fall into His hand rather than into human hands. Plague came upon Israel, and seventy thousand died. When the destroying angel came towards Jerusalem, the Lord commanded him to stop. The angel stood by Ornan the Jebusite's threshing floor. Mercy interrupted the destruction before David could undo any part of the decision that had brought it.", "David saw the angel between earth and heaven with a sword stretched over Jerusalem. He and the elders fell before God in sackcloth. David acknowledged that he had ordered the census and done wrong. He called the people sheep and asked that judgment fall on him and his household rather than on them. Gad then directed him, at the angel's command, to build an altar on Ornan's threshing floor. Ornan had been threshing wheat, and his sons hid themselves when the angel appeared. When David approached, Ornan bowed and offered the place, the animals, the threshing equipment and the wheat. David refused to take another man's property as his own gift to God or to offer a sacrifice that cost him nothing.", "David paid six hundred shekels of gold for the place. He built the altar, offered burnt offerings and peace offerings, and called upon God. The Lord answered with fire from heaven upon the altar and commanded the angel to sheath his sword. David continued sacrificing there because God had answered him. The tabernacle and the established altar were then at Gibeon, but fear of the angel's sword prevented him from going there to enquire. This chapter therefore joins grave wrongdoing to the recognition of a place for worship. The location does not erase the seventy thousand deaths or make David's census useful in itself. It bears witness to mercy given after sin, confession and judgment. The king who insisted on counting his strength was brought low, and the place of answered prayer was purchased at a cost he refused to pass to someone else."], "lesson": "A leader must listen to warnings and own the consequences of wrongdoing. God's mercy invites honest confession and costly obedience rather than excuses.", "question": "Is there a warning I have overruled, or a responsibility I need to confess without shifting blame?"},
  {"id": 94021, "title": "David Prepares a Temple He Will Not Build", "subtitle": "A father equips the son appointed to finish the work", "text": "1 Chronicles 22:1–19", "chapters": [[12, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["After the sacrifice at Ornan's threshing floor, David identified the place as the site of God's house and Israel's altar of burnt offering. Preparation began with workers and materials. He gathered the foreigners living in the land and appointed stonecutters to prepare dressed stone. Iron was collected for nails and fittings, bronze was supplied in abundance, and cedar arrived from the Sidonians and Tyrians. David knew that Solomon was young and that the house was to bring honour to God among the nations. He therefore determined to prepare extensively before his own death. The work he could not complete still gave him much to do.", "David called Solomon and charged him to build the house of the Lord. He told his son that he had wanted to undertake it himself, but God had forbidden him because he had shed much blood and fought great wars. A son characterised by rest would perform the work. Solomon would receive peace from the enemies around him, build the house and stand in the promised relationship of son to God. David did not conceal the reason his own desire had been refused. Nor did he speak as though Solomon's task belonged to him only because David had grown old. The responsibility followed God's announced purpose, and David placed his preparation beneath that purpose.", "He prayed that the Lord would be with Solomon and give him wisdom and understanding. Success was connected to keeping God's law, including the statutes and judgments commanded through Moses. Solomon was to be strong, courageous and free from dismay. David then described the resources already gathered, including great stores of gold and silver, bronze and iron, timber and stone. He pointed to skilled workers who could carry out the necessary crafts and told Solomon to add to what was ready. The charge joined spiritual obedience with practical provision. Wisdom was needed for more than managing a building project. Solomon would govern Israel and needed a heart able to keep God's commands while carrying a great public duty.", "David also addressed Israel's leaders. They were to help Solomon, remembering the rest God had given them and the way the land had been brought under their authority. He urged them to set their hearts and souls on seeking the Lord, then rise and build the sanctuary so that the ark and holy vessels could be brought into it. The responsibility was shared beyond father and son. Leaders, craftsmen, labourers and the future king all had a part. David's refusal as builder did not leave him resentful or idle. He used his remaining years to make another man's obedience more possible. The chapter gives preparation its own dignity. A person may serve God's purpose fully while knowing that another generation will see the finished work."], "lesson": "Serving God can include preparing faithfully for work someone else will complete. Encouragement, resources and a clear charge to obey Him are valuable gifts.", "question": "What can I prepare now that will help someone else carry out their God-given responsibility?"},
  {"id": 94022, "title": "The Levites Prepare for Service in a Settled House", "subtitle": "Changed circumstances bring continuing duties", "text": "1 Chronicles 23:1–32", "chapters": [[12, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David was old when he made Solomon king and gathered Israel's leaders with the priests and Levites. The Levites were counted from thirty years of age and above, giving a total of thirty-eight thousand. Their duties were then distributed. Twenty-four thousand were assigned to oversee the work of God's house, six thousand to serve as officers and judges, four thousand as gatekeepers and four thousand to praise the Lord with the instruments David had provided. This was a considerable community of service. Temple preparation involved people as well as timber and treasure, and their responsibilities needed as much care as the materials already gathered.", "The account follows the three main branches of Levi: Gershon, Kohath and Merari. Families and household heads are named, sometimes with explanations about how smaller families were counted together. Within the Kohathite line, Aaron and Moses receive distinct notice. Aaron and his sons were set apart for the most holy duties, including incense, ministry before God and blessing in His name. Moses is called the man of God, but his sons are counted within the Levite tribe rather than receiving Aaron's priestly role. Honour given to Moses did not erase the particular responsibilities God had assigned. Other households appear with their sons and daughters, keeping the organisation grounded in actual family lines.", "A later part of the chapter records Levites from twenty years old and above for the service of the house. David explained that God had given His people rest and a dwelling in Jerusalem. The Levites would no longer need to carry the tabernacle and its vessels as they had during the people's journeys. Their work was changing with the settled setting, but it was not disappearing. They were to assist Aaron's descendants in the courts and rooms, help with purification and undertake the necessary work connected with God's house. Duties involving the sacred bread, flour, cakes, cooking and correct measures also belonged within this arrangement. Ordinary materials required careful handling in a holy responsibility.", "Morning and evening, Levites were to stand and thank and praise the Lord. Their work also supported the offerings on Sabbaths, new moons and appointed festivals, following the prescribed order. They kept charge of the meeting tent, the holy place and their service alongside the priests. The chapter therefore moves from a large census to repeated daily actions. No longer carrying the sanctuary did not mean that the Levites had outlived their calling. They needed to understand what faithfulness required in the conditions now before them. Their family records preserved continuity, while their assigned duties prepared them for change. The enduring purpose was service before God, expressed through the work the community actually needed in its new stage of life."], "lesson": "Faithfulness does not always mean repeating an old task. When circumstances change, God's servants should learn how their continuing responsibility is to be carried out.", "question": "Has a change in circumstances given me a new way to fulfil a responsibility I already have before God?"},
  {"id": 94023, "title": "The Priestly Families Receive Their Order of Service", "subtitle": "Publicly assigned duties preserve a shared responsibility", "text": "1 Chronicles 24:1–31", "chapters": [[12, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The organisation of worship now turns to Aaron's descendants. His sons had been Nadab, Abihu, Eleazar and Ithamar, but Nadab and Abihu died before their father without children. The priestly service therefore continued through Eleazar and Ithamar. David arranged their descendants with Zadok from Eleazar's line and Ahimelech from Ithamar's. The account begins with this family history because the responsibilities were not being created from nothing. Those appointed stood within a service handed down to them. At the same time, inherited responsibility still required an orderly arrangement for the people who would actually perform it in their own generation.", "There were more household leaders among Eleazar's descendants than among Ithamar's. Sixteen divisions came from the former and eight from the latter, making twenty-four. Both lines supplied leaders for the sanctuary and the house of God. Lots were used to assign their order. Shemaiah the Levite scribe recorded the results in the presence of the king, the leaders, the priests and the heads of the priestly and Levite families. The process was public and written down. An arrangement that affected many households did not depend on a private promise known only to a few. The people responsible for carrying it out could know the order in which service had been assigned.", "The names of the divisions follow, from Jehoiarib and Jedaiah to Delaiah and Maaziah. Abijah receives the eighth place, while other familiar priestly names appear throughout the list. The chapter does not attach a separate adventure to each household. Their importance here lies in their place within the whole service. First, eighth or twenty-fourth described an assigned turn, rather than a measure of how much God valued the people. Together the divisions provided an organised way to enter the Lord's house and carry out duties under the inherited commands given through Aaron. The list preserves the arrangement itself, allowing later readers to see that continuing worship involved a dependable order as well as willing individuals.", "Other Levite households are then named from the lines of Amram, Izhar, Hebron, Uzziel and Merari. Some family details receive notice, including Eleazar's lack of sons. These Levites also cast lots in the presence of David, Zadok, Ahimelech and the family heads. Senior and younger families participated in the same public procedure. The closing detail matters because status within a family could otherwise dominate access to responsibility. The chapter gives no warrant for inventing rivalries among them, but it does show an arrangement that did not leave every decision to personal influence. Service was distributed, witnessed and recorded. The temple being prepared would need people able to accept their own turn and recognise the legitimate responsibilities of others."], "lesson": "Shared service benefits from fair, clear arrangements. Faithfulness includes accepting an assigned duty without treating position or seniority as a claim to greater worth.", "question": "Do I carry out my own turn faithfully and respect the responsibilities assigned to others?"},
  {"id": 94024, "title": "Teachers and Learners Take Their Places in Song", "subtitle": "Music for God's house receives care and discipline", "text": "1 Chronicles 25:1–31", "chapters": [[12, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David and the army leaders set apart members of the families of Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun for the musical service. The chapter describes them as prophesying with harps, stringed instruments and cymbals. Their work belonged to the communication of praise before God, rather than being presented as entertainment detached from worship. The individual families are named, and the sons served under their fathers' direction. Asaph carried out his responsibility according to the king's order. Jeduthun's music is expressly connected with giving thanks and praising the Lord. Instruments, words and leadership therefore appear within one appointed service.", "Heman is called the king's seer in the words of God. The record names his sons and notes that God had given him fourteen sons and three daughters. This family notice places the musicians within a household that had received God's generosity. The children served under their father's direction for the music of God's house, alongside the arrangements given to Asaph and Jeduthun. The account does not supply private conversations or tell how every singer first learned an instrument. It preserves what is necessary for its purpose: the people, their leaders, the character of their work and the place that music held within preparation for the sanctuary.", "There were two hundred eighty-eight trained musicians together with their brothers, instructed in the songs of the Lord. Their skill is specifically recognised. Preparation mattered because a willing heart did not remove the need to learn the work. Yet when the order of service was assigned by lot, the procedure included small and great, teacher and pupil. Experience had a proper place in instruction, while participation in the service was not made the private possession of those who taught. The learner still belonged within an ordered community of worship. The chapter holds these truths together without suggesting that training created a higher class of person before God.", "Twenty-four groups follow, each consisting of twelve members from sons and brothers. Joseph receives the first lot, Gedaliah the second, and the list continues through Romamti-ezer in the twenty-fourth place. The repeated pattern can seem plain after accounts of battles and royal promises, but it records how praise was to continue reliably. A celebrated singer alone could not provide the whole future service. Families, teachers, learners and groups would share the task. In this way the preparation for God's house reached beyond valuable materials and impressive plans. It included patient instruction, practiced ability and a willingness to work within an arrangement with others. The songs that the congregation would hear rested on this quieter discipline. Their beauty was supported by people who learned, taught and took their appointed places."], "lesson": "Offering a gift to God includes developing it and using it cooperatively. Skill should serve worship, while experience should make room for teaching and shared service.", "question": "How can I improve a gift I use for God while helping someone else learn to serve?"},
  {"id": 94025, "title": "Gatekeepers and Treasurers Guard What Has Been Entrusted", "subtitle": "Strength and honesty serve beyond the public platform", "text": "1 Chronicles 26:1–32", "chapters": [[12, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The gatekeepers receive their own place in the preparations. Meshelemiah's family, Obed-edom's descendants and Hosah's household are named with the men available for service. Obed-edom's large family is associated with God's blessing, and its members are described as able and strong. Hosah had appointed Simri as chief although he was not the firstborn, a small detail showing that the record attends to actual household arrangements. These men were not incidental attendants. Guarding entrances to God's house required dependable people, and the account gives their families and abilities the same careful notice already given to other kinds of service.", "Lots assigned the gates to small and great alike. Shelemiah received the east, his son Zechariah the north, Obed-edom the south and a responsibility for his sons in the storehouse area. Other men received the western approaches. Numbers are supplied for the daily stations, including the gates, the causeway and the area called Parbar. Zechariah is remembered as a wise counsellor, so the record values judgment along with physical strength. The arrangement made care for the house specific. Entrances and approaches were not left to whoever happened to be available. Named people knew the place they were to guard, and the shared pattern supported continuing watchfulness.", "The chapter then turns to the treasures of God's house and the dedicated gifts. Levite families held these responsibilities, including descendants connected with Moses through Gershom and Eliezer. Shelomith and his brothers cared for offerings dedicated by David, family leaders and military commanders. Some came from spoils of war and were set aside to maintain God's house. The account also remembers gifts dedicated by Samuel, Saul, Abner and Joab. These objects remained entrusted property, whatever the wider histories of the people who had given them. Their custody required more than admiration for a generous offering on the day it was received. Someone had to preserve and administer what had been committed to a holy use.", "Other Levites served in work beyond the sanctuary. Chenaniah and his sons were assigned as officers and judges. Hebronite families handled responsibilities west of the Jordan, while men found at Jazer in Gilead were appointed over Reuben, Gad and the half tribe of Manasseh in matters concerning God and the king. The chapter thus reaches from a guarded doorway to distant tribal administration. Worship, money, judgment and public responsibility each required trustworthy service. Most of these men receive no dramatic personal story, yet their work helped sustain the life of the people. The reader is invited to recognise the value of duties that become most noticeable when they are neglected. What has been entrusted must be guarded, accounted for and handled with care wherever the responsibility is carried out."], "lesson": "Practical stewardship is part of serving God. Entrances, money, records and public judgments all need people who are dependable when attention is elsewhere.", "question": "What has been entrusted to me that needs more careful protection, record keeping or oversight?"},
  {"id": 94026, "title": "The Kingdom Depends on Many Kinds of Work", "subtitle": "Armies, farms and counsel all require responsible people", "text": "1 Chronicles 27:1–34", "chapters": [[12, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["The preparations described in Chronicles extend into the administration of David's kingdom. Military divisions entered and left royal service month by month through the year. Each division numbered twenty-four thousand and had its appointed commander. Jashobeam led the first, Dodai the second and Benaiah the third. The remaining months receive their leaders in turn. Some names are familiar from the accounts of David's mighty men, including Sibbecai and Maharai. Asahel is named in connection with the fourth division, followed by his son Zebadiah. The list preserves an established pattern of service without requiring every man named to have held his place at precisely the same moment throughout the reign.", "Leaders over the tribes are then recorded, including responsibilities among the Levites and Aaron's descendants. The account also returns briefly to the census. David had not counted those twenty years old and younger, remembering God's promise to multiply Israel like the stars. Joab had begun the numbering but had not completed it because wrath fell upon Israel, and that number was not entered in the account of David's chronicles. This reminder stands within a chapter full of legitimate organisation and numbers. The earlier census cannot therefore be dismissed as a harmless piece of administration. The writer keeps its guilt visible even while recording the orderly structures of the kingdom.", "The next appointments concern the king's property and its produce. Azmaveth had charge of the royal treasures, while Jehonathan oversaw storehouses in fields, towns, villages and fortresses. Ezri supervised cultivation. Others cared for vineyards, wine supplies, olive and sycamore trees, and oil stores. Herds in Sharon and the valleys had their own overseers. Obil the Ishmaelite cared for camels, Jehdeiah for donkeys and Jaziz the Hagerite for flocks. These details bring the kingdom down to fields, animals and stored food. Royal life could not continue on military courage alone. It relied on land being worked, produce being kept and animals being tended by people whose names seldom appear in accounts of battle.", "Finally, counsellors and close attendants are named. Jonathan is described as wise and as a scribe. Jehiel attended the king's sons. Ahithophel and his successors appear in the counsel given to the king, while Hushai is remembered as the king's companion and Joab as army commander. The chapter gathers a wide range of work without pretending that every servant performed the same task. Its order depends on distinct responsibilities carried out together. The reader sees an administration held up by courage, knowledge, agriculture, storage and personal counsel. Such a list gives dignity to practical competence. It also raises the question of whether influence and resources are being handled faithfully, since a large system can suffer greatly when the people within it neglect the part placed in their hands."], "lesson": "A community needs more than its most visible work. Careful administration, practical skill and wise counsel are responsibilities that should be carried out before God.", "question": "Which ordinary duty of mine helps other people live or work, and am I giving it proper attention?"},
  {"id": 94027, "title": "David Gives Solomon the Plans and a Charge to Know God", "subtitle": "The building must begin with a willing heart", "text": "1 Chronicles 28:1–21", "chapters": [[12, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David gathered Israel's leaders in Jerusalem. Tribal heads, military commanders, stewards of property, officers and valiant men were present. The aged king stood and addressed them as his brothers and his people. He explained his desire to build a resting place for the ark and the preparations he had made. He also repeated God's refusal: he had been a man of war and had shed blood. His own election as king remained a gift, from God's choice of Judah and Jesse's household to His choice of David among his brothers. Now Solomon had been chosen from David's many sons to occupy the throne and build God's house.", "David placed obedience before the assembly. They were to keep and seek all God's commands so that they could possess the good land and leave it to their children. Then he addressed Solomon directly. He was to know his father's God and serve Him with an undivided heart and willing mind, because the Lord searches every heart and understands the thoughts within it. Seeking God carried a promise of finding Him, while forsaking Him carried a grave warning. The temple commission did not excuse Solomon from personal faithfulness. The son chosen for an extraordinary public work still needed to know and serve the God for whom the house would be built.", "David handed over the plans. They included the porch, buildings, treasuries, upper rooms, inner rooms and the place of the mercy seat. He supplied arrangements for the courts, surrounding rooms, priestly and Levite divisions, and the vessels needed for service. Weights of gold and silver were set out for lampstands, tables, utensils, bowls and other objects. Refined gold was provided for the incense altar and for the cherubim spreading their wings above the ark. David said that the Lord had given him understanding of the design in writing. The record presents a prepared responsibility being passed from father to son, with detail sufficient to guide work beyond the moment of public encouragement.", "David again told Solomon to be strong, courageous and active. He was not to give way to fear or dismay, because the Lord would be with him and would not abandon him before the work was finished. Priests, Levites, willing skilled workers, leaders and people were available to assist. Solomon did not receive the charge as an isolated person expected to invent every detail and carry every burden. He received plans, resources, helpers and the assurance of God's presence. Yet all these supports followed the deeper appeal to know God and serve Him willingly. The building's splendour could never replace that relationship. David's final encouragement joined the heart of the worker to the actual work, calling Solomon to rise and do what God had entrusted to him."], "lesson": "Preparation and help are valuable, but public service must rest on personally knowing and obeying God. Courage grows as duty is received in dependence on Him.", "question": "Am I giving attention to my own heart before God as well as to the visible work I hope to complete?"},
  {"id": 94028, "title": "The People Give Gladly and David's Reign Ends", "subtitle": "Everything offered to God has first come from Him", "text": "1 Chronicles 29:1–30", "chapters": [[12, 29]], "start": 1, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Chronicles", "story": ["David reminded the assembly that Solomon was young and that the work was great because the house was for the Lord God. He described the materials already prepared with all his strength: precious metals, iron, wood and stones of many kinds. Then he added a personal gift from his own possessions because his affection was set on God's house. Three thousand talents of gold and seven thousand talents of refined silver were offered beyond the earlier provision. David asked who else would willingly dedicate themselves to the Lord that day. Leaders, commanders and overseers responded with substantial gifts of metals and precious stones. The people rejoiced because the offerings were willing and wholehearted, and David shared their joy.", "Before the whole assembly, David blessed God. Greatness, power, glory, victory and majesty belonged to the Lord, together with everything in heaven and earth. Riches and honour came from Him, and the strength to become great rested in His hand. David then asked who he and his people were that they could give so willingly. All things had come from God, and what they offered was already His. Their days were passing like a shadow, as those of their ancestors had passed. The enormous provision did not make its donors self-sufficient. David's prayer placed their temporary lives and borrowed possessions beneath the enduring rule of the One they worshipped.", "He acknowledged that God tests the heart and delights in uprightness. He prayed that the willing spirit seen that day would remain in the people's thoughts and that their hearts would be directed towards God. He also asked for Solomon to receive a wholehearted desire to keep God's commands and complete the house. At David's call the assembly blessed the Lord, bowed and paid their reverence. Sacrifices followed in abundance, and they ate and drank before God with great gladness. Solomon was made king a second time in this public account and anointed for rule, while Zadok was anointed as priest. The leaders, mighty men and David's sons submitted to Solomon, and God gave him great majesty before Israel.", "The book ends by looking back over David's reign. He had ruled forty years, seven in Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem. He died at a good old age, full of days, riches and honour, and Solomon succeeded him. The closing notice points to records associated with Samuel, Nathan and Gad, covering David's deeds and the times through which he and Israel had passed. The story that began with Adam and moved through families now leaves a prepared temple project and an established successor. David would not see the building finished. His last great act of preparation belonged to a future carried by others. His prayer supplies the right measure for all the wealth, work and honour described: everything received had come from God, and everything offered was being returned to Him."], "lesson": "Generosity grows through recognising God's ownership. A faithful ending includes preparing others, praying for their obedience and giving without claiming the future as our own.", "question": "Can I offer my possessions and service gladly, remembering that both the gift and the ability to give have come from God?"},
  {"id": 94500, "title": "Solomon Asks for Wisdom to Lead God's People", "subtitle": "A new king recognises the weight of his responsibility", "text": "2 Chronicles 1:1–17", "chapters": [[13, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Solomon's kingdom grew strong because the Lord was with him. Yet the son who had inherited David's throne still needed help to carry its responsibility. He summoned Israel's commanders, judges, governors and family leaders and went with the assembly to Gibeon. The sacred tent made in Moses' time was there, together with the bronze altar made by Bezalel. David had brought the ark to Jerusalem, where it rested in a tent prepared for it. Gibeon therefore remained a place where the congregation could gather before the altar. Solomon offered a thousand burnt offerings there, beginning this account of his reign with worship shared by the leaders of the people.", "That night God appeared to Solomon and invited him to ask what he should receive. Solomon began by acknowledging the mercy shown to David and the fact that God had made him king in his father's place. He asked that the promise to David be established. Before him stood a people too numerous for him to regard lightly, and he understood that they belonged to God. He requested wisdom and knowledge to lead them and judge their affairs. His answer brought the practical burden of kingship into his prayer. Receiving the title had not made him able to decide every case or guide a nation by himself.", "God recognised what Solomon had asked and what he had left unasked. He had not requested wealth, honour, the death of enemies or long life. Wisdom for the care of God's people had occupied his heart. God granted wisdom and knowledge and also promised extraordinary riches and honour. Solomon returned from Gibeon to Jerusalem and ruled over Israel. The promise of added wealth followed the request for understanding, but the order matters. The king's petition had placed the welfare of the people before the advantages he might personally gain from his office. The answer was a gift to equip him for service, not a reason to forget why he needed wisdom.", "The chapter closes with visible signs of Solomon's growing power. He gathered fourteen hundred chariots and twelve thousand horsemen, stationed in chariot cities and at Jerusalem. Gold and silver became abundant, as did cedar timber. Royal merchants traded in horses and chariots from Egypt and supplied other kings. These details introduce the splendour that will mark his reign. They also leave his opening prayer before the reader as the right measure of that splendour. A large treasury and a powerful force could not themselves provide the judgment required to lead God's people. The king who possessed so much had begun by admitting that the responsibility exceeded his own understanding."], "lesson": "Ask God for the understanding needed to serve people faithfully. Honour and resources do not remove our need for wisdom.", "question": "Which responsibility should lead me to ask God for wisdom before asking for easier circumstances?"},
  {"id": 94501, "title": "Solomon Seeks Workers and Timber for the Temple", "subtitle": "A great building cannot contain the greater God", "text": "2 Chronicles 2:1–18", "chapters": [[13, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Solomon resolved to build a house for the Lord's name and a palace for his kingdom. He assigned seventy thousand men to carry burdens, eighty thousand to cut stone in the hills and thirty-six hundred to oversee the work. Then he sent to Huram king of Tyre, recalling the cedar supplied for David's house. The new request concerned a place dedicated to God's worship. Solomon explained the continuing service it would support: incense, the sacred bread, daily offerings, Sabbaths, new moons and appointed feasts. The project was meant to serve an established relationship between God and His people, with duties that would continue long after the builders finished.", "Solomon spoke of the greatness of the house because the God he worshipped was greater than all gods. Yet he immediately acknowledged a limit to every building. Heaven itself could not contain the Lord, so no king could construct a house large enough to hold Him. Solomon's task was to prepare a place for sacrifice before Him. That confession kept the proposed splendour in its proper place. The king could provide costly materials and organise thousands of workers, but he could not make God dependent on their work. Reverence included recognising that the One they intended to honour remained beyond everything their hands could produce.", "He requested an expert craftsman able to work with metals, dyed fabrics and engraving alongside the workers David had prepared. He also asked for cedar, fir and algum timber from Lebanon, where Huram's servants knew the work. Solomon offered wheat, barley, wine and oil for the woodcutters. Huram answered in writing, blessing the God who made heaven and earth and had given David a wise son. He supplied a skilled man whose mother came from Dan and whose father was Tyrian. This craftsman's abilities included metal, stone, timber, fabric and design. The timber would be floated by sea to Joppa, and Solomon would arrange its transport up to Jerusalem.", "The chapter finishes by identifying the labour force among the foreigners living in Israel. The total was one hundred fifty-three thousand six hundred, distributed among carrying, cutting and supervision. These figures belong to the actual organisation of a demanding royal project. The splendour ahead depended on burdens borne and stone shaped by many people whose individual stories are not recorded here. The account brings planning, payment, transport and skill into the service of worship. Solomon's confession about God's greatness remains essential beneath those arrangements. Careful work could honour Him, while no finished structure would put Him within the king's control or replace the obedience that worship required."], "lesson": "God deserves careful preparation and our best work. Remembering His greatness keeps skill, expense and achievement from becoming objects of pride.", "question": "How can I prepare carefully for God's service while remembering that He is greater than my work?"},
  {"id": 94502, "title": "The Temple Rises on Mount Moriah", "subtitle": "David's prepared place receives Solomon's building", "text": "2 Chronicles 3:1–17", "chapters": [[13, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Solomon began building the Lord's house in Jerusalem on Mount Moriah. The site was the threshing floor of Ornan the Jebusite, where God had appeared to David and where David had prepared for the future house. The location carried the memory of judgment halted and prayer answered. Now, in the second month of Solomon's fourth year, the work began to rise there. What David had identified and prepared was becoming a building under his son. The account gives the place and time before describing the beauty. The temple belonged to a history of God's mercy and promise before it became a remarkable royal achievement.", "The main house was sixty cubits long and twenty cubits wide according to the measure recorded. A porch stood before it. Solomon overlaid the interior with gold and covered the larger room with timber decorated with palm trees and chains. Precious stones added beauty, and the gold included that of Parvaim. Beams, doorposts, walls and doors received their covering, while cherubim were carved upon the walls. The description moves patiently over the work because the appearance was intentional. Materials had been gathered, designs provided and craftsmen engaged. Their labour was now taking a visible form through spaces and surfaces prepared for the worship of God.", "The most holy place measured twenty cubits by twenty and was overlaid with six hundred talents of fine gold. Two great cherubim stood within it, covered with gold. Their wings extended across the room, reaching the walls and meeting one another. The account gives their position and span rather than inviting the reader to imagine them as independent objects of devotion. They belonged to the furnishing of the inner sanctuary. A curtain of blue, purple, crimson and fine linen, worked with cherubim, marked the separation within the house. Beauty and boundary stood together. The building did not present every space as open to casual approach simply because the king had paid for its construction.", "Two pillars were set before the temple, one on each side. Their capitals carried chains and pomegranate ornaments. Solomon named the right pillar Jachin and the left Boaz. The chapter ends at this entrance, leaving the reader with an ordered house whose materials and craftsmanship were meant to express honour. Yet the beginning of the account must not be forgotten amid the gold. This was the place connected with David's humbled prayer, and Solomon was carrying forward a responsibility received from God. The sanctuary's splendour would never give later kings permission to treat its Lord lightly. A beautiful house still called for reverence from the people who approached it."], "lesson": "Care and beauty can honour God, but sacred surroundings never replace humility before Him. Remember the mercy beneath what others can see.", "question": "Does attention to outward preparation also lead me to approach God with inward reverence?"},
  {"id": 94503, "title": "Altars, Basins and Lamps Are Made Ready", "subtitle": "The house is equipped for continuing worship", "text": "2 Chronicles 4:1–22", "chapters": [[13, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["With the structure taking shape, Solomon provided the equipment for its service. A great bronze altar stood twenty cubits long and wide and ten cubits high. A large round basin, called the sea, was cast in bronze and set upon twelve oxen facing the four directions. Its rim was shaped like a cup with lily flowers. The detailed measurements and decoration show that these objects were carefully designed rather than improvised when worship began. The house needed provision for the work that would be carried out there. Solomon's task included preparing a functioning sanctuary, with suitable places and vessels for the duties assigned to the priests.", "Ten smaller basins were placed in two groups, five on each side. They were used for washing what belonged to the burnt offerings, while the great basin supplied washing for the priests. The distinction connects the vessels with purification and sacrificial service. Ten golden lampstands and ten tables were likewise arranged on either side within the house, and a hundred golden bowls were made. Solomon provided the priests' court and the larger court and covered their doors with bronze. These arrangements gave particular objects particular purposes. Their value lay in serving the worship God had appointed, not merely in the impression produced by their number or cost.", "Huram completed his assigned metalwork. The record gathers pillars, capitals, networks, pomegranates, stands, basins, pots, shovels and forks into its account of his achievement. The bronze was cast in the Jordan plain between Succoth and Zeredathah. Its quantity was so great that the total weight was not determined. This brief notice carries the reader away from Jerusalem to the place where the demanding production occurred. The finished sanctuary depended on skilled work done elsewhere as well as on objects finally positioned within it. Huram's name is preserved beside that labour, giving the craftsman a recognised place in the account of Solomon's temple.", "Gold furnished the inner service as bronze furnished much of the outer work. The altar, tables for the sacred bread, lampstands and lamps, flowers, tongs, bowls, spoons and censers are listed. Even the doors associated with the most holy place and the main house received gold. By the end of the chapter, the reader has seen preparation extend to the small utensils handled during repeated duties. Great visions are carried into practice through such details. The magnificence would be visible on the day of dedication, but the tools were intended for many ordinary days afterward. They remind us that worship includes tasks requiring readiness, cleanliness, light, order and faithful people to use what has been provided."], "lesson": "Practical preparation matters in God's service. Honour is shown through care for necessary details as well as through large and visible gifts.", "question": "Which small preparation could help me or someone else carry out a responsibility more faithfully?"},
  {"id": 94504, "title": "The Ark Enters and the Glory Fills the House", "subtitle": "The completed temple receives the covenant ark", "text": "2 Chronicles 5:1–14", "chapters": [[13, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["The work Solomon had undertaken for God's house was finished. He brought in the silver, gold and vessels David had dedicated and placed them among the temple treasures. Then he assembled the elders, tribal heads and family leaders in Jerusalem to bring the ark from the City of David to its new place. The gathering took place during the festival in the seventh month. Israel's representatives stood within a moment linking generations. David had gathered and prepared, Solomon had built, and the ark connected them to the covenant made long before either king was born. The house's completion served that continuing relationship rather than beginning a religion of Solomon's invention.", "The Levites took up the ark. Priests and Levites also brought the sacred tent and its vessels. Solomon and the assembled congregation offered so many sheep and cattle that their number was beyond counting. The priests carried the ark into the most holy place beneath the wings of the great cherubim. Its poles extended so that their ends could be seen from before the inner sanctuary, though not from outside. The account pauses over what the ark contained: the two stone tablets Moses had placed there at Horeb when the Lord made His covenant with Israel after bringing them from Egypt. Amid abundant treasure, these tablets kept God's word at the centre of the house.", "The priests present had consecrated themselves, without limiting the occasion to the ordinary divisions of service. The Levite singers from the families of Asaph, Heman and Jeduthun stood in white linen at the east side of the altar with cymbals, harps and other instruments. A hundred twenty priests sounded trumpets beside them. The carefully arranged duties described in the earlier book now became a gathered act of praise. Voices and instruments joined as one sound in thanking the Lord for His goodness and enduring mercy. The central words directed attention to God's character. Their united sound served a truth larger than the skill or standing of the musicians themselves.", "As the praise rose, a cloud filled the house. The priests could no longer stand to carry out their ministry because the Lord's glory had filled it. The climax did not come through a speech praising Solomon's achievement or through admiration of the furnishings. God made His presence known, and the servants' activity yielded before it. Everything had been prepared for ministry, yet the Lord remained sovereign over the house and those serving within it. The chapter leaves the people before a completed building filled with glory they had not manufactured. Their work could prepare a place of worship, but the presence that gave the occasion its deepest significance came from God."], "lesson": "God's word and character belong at the centre of worship. Skilled service should lead us to honour His presence rather than admire ourselves.", "question": "What helps me keep God's goodness and word central when worship involves much visible preparation?"},
  {"id": 94505, "title": "Solomon Kneels and Asks God to Hear", "subtitle": "A dedication prayer makes room for repentance and mercy", "text": "2 Chronicles 6:1–42", "chapters": [[13, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Solomon spoke to the congregation after the cloud filled the house. He blessed the Lord for fulfilling what He had promised David. His father had desired to build the temple, and God had approved the desire while assigning the work to his son. Solomon now occupied the throne and had placed the covenant ark within the finished house. He then stood on a bronze platform in the court, knelt before the whole assembly and lifted his hands towards heaven. The king who had supervised such an impressive project took the position of a petitioner. He needed the Lord to hear, keep His word and show mercy to the people.", "His prayer acknowledged that no God in heaven or earth was like the Lord, who kept covenant with wholehearted servants. He asked for the promise to David's house to continue, together with the obedience required of David's descendants. Then he recognised that heaven itself could not contain God, much less this building. He asked for God's attentive care towards the place bearing His name, but described heaven as His dwelling. Those praying towards the house would need forgiveness from the God who ruled beyond it. Solomon's language therefore guarded against treating the temple as a container for God or a means by which Israel could compel His favour.", "The prayer brought many possible troubles before Him. When neighbours disputed and an oath came before the altar, Solomon asked for righteous judgment. When sin led to defeat, drought or other calamity, he asked that confession and turning should receive forgiveness. Famine, disease, damaged crops, locusts, siege and personal grief all had a place. God alone knew every heart and could judge each person rightly. Solomon also prayed for the foreigner who came because of God's great name, asking that the nations might know and fear Him. He prayed for Israel in a campaign God had directed, and for captives carried away after sin. Even in a distant land, wholehearted repentance and prayer were to look towards the Lord who could hear.", "The closing appeals asked God to attend to prayer, receive His resting place with the ark, clothe the priests with salvation and let His faithful people rejoice in goodness. Solomon appealed to the mercies promised to David. The prayer had moved far beyond the successful dedication into failures and sorrows that might lie ahead. It recognised that no one was without sin, while holding open the way of confession and return. The temple's future mattered because a needy people would need to seek their God. Solomon asked for more than a memorable celebration. He asked that judgment, mercy, justice and forgiveness should meet people through the years when their present gladness had given way to difficult circumstances."], "lesson": "Prayer should include confession, concern for others and trust in God's knowledge of every heart. No distance or outward circumstance makes sincere repentance useless.", "question": "Which part of Solomon's prayer speaks most directly to a need I should bring honestly before God?"},
  {"id": 94506, "title": "God Answers with Fire and a Call to Turn", "subtitle": "The dedication's joy is joined to a solemn warning", "text": "2 Chronicles 7:1–22", "chapters": [[13, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["When Solomon finished praying, fire came from heaven and consumed the burnt offering and sacrifices. God's glory filled the house, preventing the priests from entering. The people saw and bowed with their faces to the pavement, worshipping and praising the Lord for His goodness and enduring mercy. The answer met the prayer publicly, giving the assembly a reason to humble themselves. Their finished house had not made them masters of God's presence. They were worshippers receiving His response. The scene joined awe with thanksgiving, and the words of praise remembered the mercy upon which Israel's relationship with Him depended.", "The king and people continued offering sacrifices. Solomon presented twenty-two thousand cattle and a hundred twenty thousand sheep. Priests and Levites served with trumpets and the instruments associated with David's provision for praise. The middle of the court was consecrated for offerings because the bronze altar could not accommodate them all. Israel gathered from the entrance of Hamath to the river of Egypt. Dedication and festival days were observed, followed by a solemn assembly. On the twenty-third day of the seventh month, Solomon sent the people home rejoicing in the goodness God had shown to David, Solomon and Israel. The celebration ended in households carrying the memory of shared worship.", "After the building work was completed, the Lord appeared to Solomon by night. He had heard the prayer and chosen the place for sacrifice. He then spoke of the very troubles Solomon had named: withheld rain, devouring locusts and disease. If His people humbled themselves, prayed, sought His face and turned from wickedness, He would hear from heaven, forgive and heal their land. This was a covenant promise addressed to Israel in its land. Its call to repentance included actual turning, not words alone. The Lord declared His attentive care for the house and called Solomon to walk obediently before Him, joining the promise about David's throne to continued faithfulness.", "A warning followed. If the people abandoned God's commands and served other gods, they would be uprooted from the land, and the very house dedicated to His name would be cast from His sight. Passersby would be astonished by the ruin and ask why it had happened. The answer would identify Israel's abandonment of the God who redeemed them from Egypt. The chapter therefore places fire, glory and rejoicing beside the possibility of desolation. A magnificent beginning would not grant immunity from later unfaithfulness. The God who heard repentance also took disobedience seriously. The people were called to remember both truths after returning from the festival to the choices of daily life."], "lesson": "God welcomes humble repentance that turns from sin. Past blessings and sacred buildings do not excuse continued disobedience.", "question": "Does my prayer for God's help include a willingness to turn from the conduct He has shown to be wrong?"},
  {"id": 94507, "title": "The King's Projects Continue and Worship Keeps Its Order", "subtitle": "Prosperity brings daily responsibilities", "text": "2 Chronicles 8:1–18", "chapters": [[13, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After twenty years of work on the temple and palace, Solomon continued building throughout his kingdom. He restored towns received from Huram and settled Israelites there. He gained control in Hamath-zobah, built Tadmor in the wilderness and provided store cities in Hamath. Upper and Lower Beth-horon received walls, gates and bars. Other towns supplied storage, chariots and horsemen. His projects reached Jerusalem, Lebanon and the lands under his authority. The account gives a picture of a kingdom able to build and organise widely. The same reign that produced the temple also required roads of supply, defended settlements and practical arrangements beyond the capital.", "The work rested on different obligations among the inhabitants. Descendants of the Canaanite peoples remaining in the land were subjected to labour, while Israelites held military and commanding responsibilities. Two hundred fifty chief officers supervised the people. These notices belong to the social arrangements of Solomon's rule and keep the labour beneath his achievements visible. The chapter also records his moving Pharaoh's daughter from the City of David to a house built for her. He explained that places associated with the ark were holy and should not be her dwelling. The account records his concern about those places without inviting the reader to imagine that every aspect of his household was therefore beyond question.", "Solomon offered burnt offerings on the altar before the temple porch. Daily requirements, Sabbaths, new moons and the three annual festivals followed Moses' command. The priests served in their divisions, and the Levites praised and assisted according to each day's need. Gatekeepers took their turns at the entrances. These arrangements followed David's instructions, and the priests and Levites kept the commands concerning both service and treasures. The building's completion was therefore followed by the ordered work for which it had been prepared. The temple did not exist simply to mark a successful reign. Its rooms, gates, altar and stores supported recurring acts of worship requiring people to be present and faithful.", "The chapter ends at the sea. Solomon travelled to Ezion-geber and Eloth in Edom, where Huram supplied ships and experienced sailors. They joined Solomon's servants in a voyage to Ophir and brought back four hundred fifty talents of gold. Trade extended the kingdom's resources and depended on knowledge Solomon's own people received through cooperation. Across the chapter, many forms of competence appear: builders, officers, priests, gatekeepers and sailors. Their work made a prosperous reign function. Yet recurring worship remains within that activity as a responsibility that could not be replaced by further wealth. The days after dedication still required the daily offering, the appointed song and the guarded gate."], "lesson": "Faithful worship continues through ordinary schedules while other work expands. Prosperity should strengthen our responsibility to God rather than crowd it out.", "question": "What regular duty before God needs protection when my other activities become busy or successful?"},
  {"id": 94508, "title": "The Queen of Sheba Hears Solomon's Wisdom", "subtitle": "Royal splendour ends beneath the measure of a lifetime", "text": "2 Chronicles 9:1–31", "chapters": [[13, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["The queen of Sheba heard of Solomon's fame and travelled to Jerusalem with a great company. Camels carried spices, abundant gold and precious stones. She came with difficult questions and spoke with him about what occupied her mind. Solomon answered her, leaving no question beyond his ability to explain. She also observed his palace, table, attendants, clothing and the way connected with his approach to God's house. The combined sight of wisdom and ordered splendour overwhelmed her. Reports had reached her own land, but seeing and hearing for herself showed that the greatness exceeded what she had previously believed.", "She praised the happiness of those who continually heard his wisdom and blessed the Lord who had made him king. Her words linked his throne to God's love for Israel and to the duty of judgment and justice. She gave a hundred twenty talents of gold, precious stones and an extraordinary quantity of spices. Other imported treasures included algum timber used for the temple and palace and for musical instruments. Solomon gave the queen what she requested, and she returned to her country with her servants. The encounter made wisdom visible beyond Israel. Yet her recognition of justice also supplied a reminder that a ruler's greatness was meant to serve the people under him.", "The account then describes Solomon's wealth. Six hundred sixty-six talents of gold came in during one year apart from other trade and tribute. He made gold shields, possessed an ivory throne overlaid with gold and used golden drinking vessels. Lions decorated the throne and its six steps. Ships brought further riches and unusual goods, while rulers sought the wisdom God had placed in his heart. Horses, chariots, valuable metals and cedar were abundant. His authority extended across surrounding territories. These details give the reader the scale of a reign marked by resources and recognition. They also keep identifying the source of the wisdom: it was something God had given, not an achievement Solomon could claim as self-created.", "The chapter closes by referring to records connected with Nathan, Ahijah and Iddo. Solomon had ruled all Israel for forty years. Then he died and was buried in David's city, and Rehoboam his son succeeded him. The plain notice of death follows the extraordinary catalogue of splendour. None of the gold, influence or admiration made his reign endless. Chronicles has concentrated here on the wisdom and temple-building years, and its next movement will show the kingdom facing a painful division. The reader leaves Solomon with gratitude for what God supplied and with a sober understanding of human limits. Even the most celebrated ruler receives his years, carries his duty and must eventually leave the throne to another."], "lesson": "Wisdom and resources are gifts to be used for justice and service. Recognition should deepen gratitude, since neither wealth nor reputation can lengthen our appointed lifetime.", "question": "How can the gifts I have received become a blessing to others while I still have opportunity to use them?"},
  {"id": 94509, "title": "Rehoboam Rejects the Counsel to Be Kind", "subtitle": "A harsh answer tears the kingdom apart", "text": "2 Chronicles 10:1–19", "chapters": [[13, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Rehoboam went to Shechem, where Israel had gathered to make him king. Jeroboam returned from Egypt, where he had taken refuge from Solomon, and joined the people in bringing their request. They spoke of the heavy burden Solomon had placed upon them. If the new king would lighten the harsh service and yoke, they would serve him. The appeal gave Rehoboam an opportunity to begin by listening. His father's wealth had been impressive, but those who carried its demands had their own account of the reign. Rehoboam told them to return in three days while he considered his answer.", "First he consulted the older men who had served Solomon. They advised kindness, a favourable response and good words. If he treated the people well, they said, they would remain his servants. Their counsel offered a way for authority to gain willing loyalty through care. Rehoboam rejected it and consulted the men who had grown up with him. They urged him to boast of being stronger than his father and to threaten a heavier yoke. Where Solomon had used whips, he was to promise scorpions. These advisers interpreted the people's appeal as an occasion for displaying superior force. Their words allowed the new king to imagine that harshness would prove his ability to rule.", "When Jeroboam and the assembly returned, Rehoboam answered roughly according to the younger men's advice. He would increase their burden rather than reduce it. Chronicles places this refusal within God's fulfilment of the word spoken through Ahijah to Jeroboam. That larger purpose did not make Rehoboam's conduct wise or remove the choices the narrative has just exposed. The people heard a ruler who would not listen. They asked what share they had in David's house and called Israel back to its tents. The claim of a continuing united kingdom broke under the weight of the king's own answer. Rehoboam retained authority over the Israelites living in Judah's towns.", "He then sent Hadoram, the official over compulsory labour, but the Israelites stoned him to death. Rehoboam hurried to his chariot and fled to Jerusalem. The chapter ends with Israel's rebellion against David's house continuing. A consultation that could have produced relief had ended in division, death and the king's frightened departure. The story gives no reason to blame youth alone, since Rehoboam chose the counsel that suited his desire to appear strong. Nor does it make listening a weakness. The unaccepted advice had offered a durable kind of authority rooted in service. The throne Solomon left behind required wisdom, and Rehoboam's opening decision showed how quickly inherited position could be damaged by refusing it."], "lesson": "Authority becomes destructive when it treats a plea for relief as an insult. Seek counsel that helps you serve, and listen before answering firmly.", "question": "Do I choose advice because it is wise or because it tells me what I already want to hear?"},
  {"id": 94510, "title": "Rehoboam Stops the War and Strengthens Judah", "subtitle": "Obedience preserves lives after a disastrous decision", "text": "2 Chronicles 11:1–23", "chapters": [[13, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Rehoboam reached Jerusalem and gathered a hundred eighty thousand chosen warriors from Judah and Benjamin. He intended to recover the kingdom by force. The division at Shechem had cost him authority, and an army seemed a way to reverse the loss. Before the attack, God's word came through Shemaiah. The king and his people were not to fight their fellow Israelites. They were to return home because the turn of events stood within God's purpose. This time they obeyed. The expedition stopped, and the men went back instead of advancing against Jeroboam. After a chapter dominated by refusal to listen, this response prevented another stage of immediate bloodshed.", "Rehoboam remained in Jerusalem and strengthened the territory he still governed. Bethlehem, Tekoa, Beth-zur, Adullam, Lachish, Hebron and other towns became part of a network of fortified places. He supplied them with commanders, food, oil, wine, shields and spears. His attention moved from trying to reclaim everything by war to preparing Judah and Benjamin for defence. The record recognises the practical work without making fortifications a substitute for faithfulness. Walls and provisions could serve legitimate needs, but the following chapters would show that their effectiveness could not be separated from the people's relationship with God.", "Meanwhile priests and Levites came from the northern territories. Jeroboam and his sons had excluded them from serving the Lord and appointed priests for the high places and calf worship. The Levites left their pasturelands and possessions and moved to Judah and Jerusalem. Other Israelites whose hearts were set on seeking the Lord followed them and came to sacrifice. Their movement strengthened Judah during the first three years, when the people followed the ways associated with David and Solomon. These families accepted material loss to continue worshipping faithfully. The political frontier did not prevent people from making a spiritual choice about the service to which they belonged.", "The account then describes Rehoboam's household, including his wives, sons and daughters. He especially favoured Maachah and designated her son Abijah as chief among his brothers, intending him to succeed. Other sons were placed throughout fortified towns and supplied generously. The writer recognises prudence in distributing them, while also recording the extent of the royal household. The chapter offers a mixed picture rather than a simple recovery from Shechem. Rehoboam could obey a warning and organise his land well, yet those actions did not guarantee a settled heart. The arrival of people determined to seek God supplied a deeper strength than the arrangement of cities alone. Their costly devotion helped sustain a kingdom still learning the limits of its ruler's power."], "lesson": "A bad decision need not lead to another if we will heed correction. Faithful worship may also require costly changes in where and how we live.", "question": "Where should I stop a course of action because God has already made the right direction clear?"},
  {"id": 94511, "title": "Gold Shields Are Replaced with Bronze", "subtitle": "Strength without a seeking heart cannot preserve Judah", "text": "2 Chronicles 12:1–16", "chapters": [[13, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["When Rehoboam's kingdom was established and he had grown strong, he abandoned the Lord's law, and the people followed him. The danger came after the early strengthening, when success might have seemed to promise safety. In his fifth year, Shishak king of Egypt invaded with a vast force of chariots, horsemen and allied peoples. He captured the fortified towns and advanced towards Jerusalem. The defences prepared in the preceding chapter did not keep the enemy away. Chronicles identifies the invasion with Judah's unfaithfulness. The king's outward preparations had not supplied the obedience his people needed to continue before God.", "Shemaiah came to Rehoboam and the leaders gathered in Jerusalem. God's message was direct: because they had abandoned Him, He had left them in Shishak's hand. The king and leaders humbled themselves and acknowledged the Lord's righteousness. God saw that response and sent another word through the prophet. They would receive some deliverance, and Jerusalem would not suffer complete destruction at Shishak's hand. Yet they would become his servants, learning the difference between serving God and serving earthly kingdoms. Mercy limited the judgment without removing all its consequences. Their confession mattered, even though it could not make the invasion disappear as though nothing had happened.", "Shishak took the treasures of the temple and palace, including Solomon's golden shields. Rehoboam made bronze shields to replace them and entrusted them to the royal guard. When he entered the Lord's house, the guards brought them out and later returned them to storage. The ceremonies could continue, and the shields could still accompany the king, but the material had changed. A simple detail made the loss visible within royal routine. Solomon's splendour had not proved permanent. The account also notes that Rehoboam's humility turned away complete destruction and that good things remained in Judah. Judgment was real, but it did not erase every sign of God's restraint.", "Rehoboam ruled seventeen years in Jerusalem, the city God had chosen for His name. His mother was Naamah the Ammonitess. The final assessment explains his evil by saying that he had not set his heart to seek the Lord. This reaches beneath particular policies to an unresolved direction of life. He could humble himself in a crisis without having cultivated the continuing devotion that would govern his choices. Records connected with Shemaiah and Iddo preserved his deeds, while conflict with Jeroboam remained part of his reign. He died and was buried in David's city, and Abijah succeeded him. His story leaves a warning about depending on establishment, inherited privilege and occasional religious response while neglecting a heart steadily directed towards God."], "lesson": "Seek God during strength as well as trouble. Humility can receive mercy, but it must become a continuing way of life.", "question": "Am I preparing my heart to seek God regularly, or turning to Him only when my security is threatened?"},
  {"id": 94512, "title": "Judah Cries to God When the Battle Closes Around Them", "subtitle": "A greater army cannot decide the outcome by numbers alone", "text": "2 Chronicles 13:1–22", "chapters": [[13, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Abijah began his short reign over Judah while Jeroboam still ruled Israel. War brought four hundred thousand chosen men of Judah against eight hundred thousand from the north. Before the fighting, Abijah stood on Mount Zemaraim and addressed Jeroboam and Israel. He appealed to God's enduring covenant with David and challenged the rebellion against David's house. His speech presented Rehoboam as unable to withstand the men who had gathered against him. The reader has already heard the fuller account of Rehoboam's own harsh answer, so Abijah's royal defence should be recognised as part of his address rather than allowed to erase that earlier responsibility.", "Abijah also challenged the northern kingdom's worship. Jeroboam's golden calves could not give it the Lord's authority. The priests descended from Aaron and the Levites had been expelled, and replacements admitted according to arrangements resembling those of the surrounding nations. Abijah contrasted this with the continuing service in Judah: morning and evening offerings, incense, the sacred bread and the lamps. He declared that God was their leader and that the priests stood ready to sound the alarm. His warning urged Israel not to fight against the God of their fathers. The speech placed the conflict in relation to the covenant and the sanctuary, not merely a rivalry between two ambitious kings.", "While Abijah spoke, Jeroboam arranged an ambush behind Judah. The men of Judah looked around and saw enemies before and behind. Their position had become dangerous regardless of the confidence of the speech. They cried to the Lord, and the priests sounded their trumpets. When Judah shouted, God struck Jeroboam and Israel before them. The northern army fled, and Judah pursued with a terrible loss of life among their fellow Israelites. Chronicles records five hundred thousand chosen men falling. The victory is explicitly explained by Judah's reliance upon the Lord, rather than by their smaller army being naturally stronger or by their king's words defeating an ambush.", "Abijah took towns including Bethel, Jeshanah and Ephrain with their surrounding settlements. Jeroboam did not recover strength during Abijah's days, and the account records the Lord striking him and his death. Abijah grew strong, and his family increased. The chapter refers readers to Iddo's record for more of his deeds and sayings. Its central movement has taken Judah from public claims about God to actual dependence when surrounded. The outcome cannot make the civil war a light matter, for the dead belonged to Israel. It does show that neither a numerical advantage nor a concealed strategy places events outside God's rule. The cry that rose in danger mattered more than an appearance of military certainty."], "lesson": "Confidence in religious identity must become actual reliance on God. Bring real danger to Him rather than trusting words, numbers or position alone.", "question": "When a situation exposes my weakness, do I turn to God as readily as I speak about trusting Him?"},
  {"id": 94513, "title": "Asa Prays Before an Overwhelming Army", "subtitle": "Rest and preparation do not remove dependence on God", "text": "2 Chronicles 14:1–15", "chapters": [[13, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After Abijah's death, Asa became king and the land enjoyed ten quiet years. He did what was good and right before the Lord. Foreign altars, sacred pillars and objects of idolatry were removed, and he commanded Judah to seek the God of their fathers and obey His law. The quiet of these years is presented as God's gift. Asa used it to strengthen towns with walls, towers, gates and bars. He told the people that they still had opportunity to build because they had sought the Lord and received rest. Preparation was a response to peace, not an announcement that they could now manage without its Giver.", "Judah and Benjamin supplied a substantial army. Three hundred thousand from Judah carried large shields and spears, and two hundred eighty thousand Benjaminites carried shields and bows. Their courage and equipment are recorded, yet the force that came against them was greater still. Zerah the Ethiopian advanced with a million men and three hundred chariots to Mareshah. Asa went out, and the armies faced one another in the Valley of Zephathah. The earlier years of building could not spare him this confrontation. A faithful beginning and wise preparation had brought Judah to the day with resources, but the enemy still exceeded what those resources could confidently answer.", "Asa cried to the Lord his God. He confessed that God could help whether those needing help were many or powerless. He asked for aid because Judah relied on Him and went against the great company in His name. The prayer identified the Lord as their God and appealed that a human enemy should not prevail against Him. Asa did not deny the enemy's size or pretend that Judah lacked soldiers. He put both realities beneath God's ability to act. His kingship included leading the army, but at this point his most important act was to acknowledge openly the source of the help they needed.", "The Lord struck Zerah's army before Asa and Judah, and the invaders fled. Judah pursued towards Gerar, taking extensive spoil. The surrounding towns were struck as fear of the Lord fell upon them, and the army brought back sheep and camels from the livestock encampments. They returned to Jerusalem after a deliverance that their own numbers had not guaranteed. The chapter links the removal of idols, the use of quiet years and the prayer in battle through a consistent dependence on God. Asa's story will later reveal a painful change in how he handles danger. Here, however, his appeal stands as a clear witness: the ruler who prepared responsibly still knew that victory rested with the Lord."], "lesson": "Prepare responsibly during peaceful times and depend on God when trouble comes. Having resources is no reason to stop praying.", "question": "How can I use present opportunities wisely without making my preparation the foundation of my trust?"},
  {"id": 94514, "title": "Asa Takes Courage and Renews the Covenant", "subtitle": "Seeking God reaches both public worship and the royal family", "text": "2 Chronicles 15:1–19", "chapters": [[13, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["The Spirit of God came upon Azariah son of Oded, and he went to meet Asa. His message addressed the king with Judah and Benjamin. The Lord was with them as they remained with Him, and those who sought Him would find Him. Forsaking Him would bring abandonment. Azariah recalled troubled times without the true God, a teaching priest or the law, when violence and insecurity disturbed ordinary travel and public life. Yet turning to God in distress had brought an answer. The warning also carried encouragement. They were to be strong and keep working, because their labour would receive its reward.", "Asa heard and took courage. He removed detestable idols from Judah, Benjamin and the towns taken in the hill country of Ephraim. He restored the Lord's altar before the temple porch. Then he gathered the people, including those from Ephraim, Manasseh and Simeon who had joined Judah after recognising God's presence with him. In the third month of his fifteenth year, the assembly offered seven hundred cattle and seven thousand sheep from the spoil already received. Deliverance was followed by renewed attention to worship. The prophetic word did not merely comfort Asa about the past. It moved him towards further action that would shape the people's future.", "The assembly entered a covenant to seek the Lord wholeheartedly. Their oath included the severe covenant penalty of death for refusing Him, without distinction of rank or sex. That provision belongs to this historical national arrangement and must be read as part of the account rather than turned into permission for religious coercion today. Trumpets, horns and voices accompanied the oath. Judah rejoiced because they had sought God with their full desire, and He was found by them and gave rest. The joy arose from a serious commitment, not from treating allegiance as an empty ceremony. Their promise called the direction of their lives into question.", "Asa's reform reached his own family. He removed Maachah from her royal position because she had made an idolatrous image. He cut it down, crushed it and burned it at the Kidron. The account also acknowledges that high places remained in Israel, keeping the reform's limits visible. Asa brought dedicated silver, gold and vessels into God's house, and peace continued through the period recorded. His heart is described as devoted in its general direction, though the next chapter will expose later failure. Here his courage included confronting privileged wrongdoing close to him. A reform that stopped at other people's idols would have left an important part of the responsibility untouched. Seeking God required action inside the household as well as before the national assembly."], "lesson": "Receive God's encouragement as a call to act. Genuine reform must include cherished habits and protected wrongdoing close to home.", "question": "Is there something I condemn at a distance while allowing it to remain within my own conduct or household?"},
  {"id": 94515, "title": "Asa Imprisons the Prophet Who Corrects Him", "subtitle": "A successful policy exposes a failure of trust", "text": "2 Chronicles 16:1–14", "chapters": [[13, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Baasha king of Israel fortified Ramah to restrict movement into and out of Judah. Asa faced the pressure by taking silver and gold from the temple and palace treasuries and sending them to Ben-hadad in Damascus. He appealed to an existing relationship and asked the Aramean king to break his treaty with Baasha. Ben-hadad accepted and attacked northern towns, including places in Naphtali. When Baasha heard, he stopped building Ramah. Asa brought Judah to carry away its stone and timber and used them to strengthen Geba and Mizpah. The immediate political objective had been achieved. An enemy's project had become material for Judah's own defences.", "Hanani the seer then came with a judgment that challenged the appearance of success. Asa had relied on the king of Aram rather than on the Lord, and the Aramean army had escaped from his hand. Hanani reminded him of the huge Ethiopian and Libyan force with its chariots and horsemen. When Asa relied on God, that enemy had been delivered to him. The Lord's eyes searched the whole earth to show His strength for those whose hearts were wholly directed towards Him. Asa had acted foolishly, and wars would follow. The prophet measured the policy by its dependence, not simply by whether Ramah's building work had stopped.", "Asa became furious. He imprisoned Hanani rather than receiving the correction, and at the same period oppressed some of the people. The king who had once taken courage from a prophet's words now used power against the man who spoke them. This response made the failure deeper than a single diplomatic decision. Correction had revealed a heart unwilling to be challenged. The account does not suggest that Hanani's imprisonment changed God's assessment or prevented the consequences announced. It shows what can happen when a ruler treats an uncomfortable message as an offence against his authority rather than an opportunity to return to God.", "In Asa's thirty-ninth year, he developed a severe disease in his feet. Even in this trouble, he did not seek the Lord but relied on physicians. The issue in the account is his failure to seek God, not a command to refuse medical care. He died in the forty-first year of his reign and received an honourable burial with spices and a great ceremonial burning. Public honour at his death could not remove the final chapter's sadness. Earlier trust had been real, but it did not make later self-reliance harmless. The story asks the reader to continue depending on God and to remain teachable, especially when an apparently effective decision receives unwelcome correction."], "lesson": "A result that looks successful does not prove that the path was faithful. Receive correction without punishing the person who brings it.", "question": "How do I respond when someone questions a decision I am pleased to call successful?"},
  {"id": 94516, "title": "Jehoshaphat Sends Teachers Through Judah", "subtitle": "A kingdom is strengthened by learning God's law", "text": "2 Chronicles 17:1–19", "chapters": [[13, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Jehoshaphat succeeded Asa and strengthened Judah against Israel. He stationed troops in fortified towns and garrisons in the territory, including the towns in Ephraim his father had taken. Yet the account first explains God's presence with him through the direction of his life. He followed the earlier ways of David, rejected the Baals and sought the God of his father. He walked in God's commands rather than following northern Israel's practices. The Lord established the kingdom under him, and Judah brought gifts. Wealth and honour increased, but his first distinction was the One he sought. The chapter connects his strength with a spiritual direction before presenting the scale of his resources.", "His heart took courage in the Lord's ways, and he removed high places and sacred poles from Judah. In the third year of his reign, he sent a group of officials, Levites and priests to teach in the towns. They carried the book of the Lord's law and went throughout Judah instructing the people. Reform therefore included more than destroying objects of false worship. The people needed to learn what faithfulness required. The royal messengers did not take their own standing as sufficient authority for whatever they wished to teach. The book they carried gave substance to their instruction and brought God's commands into towns beyond the capital.", "Fear of the Lord fell on the neighbouring kingdoms, and they did not make war against Jehoshaphat. Some Philistines brought gifts and silver tribute, while Arabians brought great flocks of rams and male goats. The king continued to grow strong, building fortresses and store cities. Judah's towns had extensive work, and capable soldiers were stationed in Jerusalem. These notices show the practical results of a secure reign, with provisions, building and defence all receiving attention. The teaching mission belonged within this same period of strength. Jehoshaphat did not regard knowing God's law as an unnecessary activity once the borders were guarded and resources were flowing into his kingdom.", "The closing lists name commanders from Judah and Benjamin with the men under them. Adnah, Jehohanan, Amasiah, Eliada and Jehozabad appear beside large forces, apart from those posted throughout the fortified towns. Amasiah receives a particular notice: he had willingly offered himself to the Lord. That brief phrase gives an inward quality to a list otherwise concerned with numbers and readiness. Behind a strong public arrangement stood individuals whose service could be personally devoted to God. The chapter thus holds teachers and soldiers, the law book and the fortified city together. Defence helped preserve the people, while instruction showed them the way in which their preserved lives should be lived."], "lesson": "People need instruction in God's word as well as protection and provision. Willing service grows stronger when it is guided by understanding.", "question": "How can I help someone understand God's word rather than merely expecting them to know how to live?"},
  {"id": 94517, "title": "Micaiah Speaks Despite the Agreement of Four Hundred", "subtitle": "Ahab cannot disguise himself beyond God's word", "text": "2 Chronicles 18:1–34", "chapters": [[13, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Jehoshaphat's prosperity was followed by an alliance with Ahab through marriage. During a visit to Samaria, Ahab honoured him with generous provision and persuaded him to join a campaign for Ramoth-gilead. Jehoshaphat agreed to share his cause but asked that they first enquire of the Lord. Ahab assembled four hundred prophets, who promised success. Jehoshaphat still asked for a prophet of the Lord. Ahab named Micaiah son of Imla but admitted that he hated him because his messages were unwelcome. The messenger who fetched Micaiah urged him to agree with the others. Micaiah answered that he would speak what his God said.", "The kings sat in royal robes while the prophets performed before them. Zedekiah had made iron horns to illustrate victory over Aram. Micaiah initially echoed the promise of success, but Ahab demanded a truthful answer. Then Micaiah described Israel scattered like sheep without a shepherd and dismissed in peace because their master was gone. He also told of a heavenly vision in which a spirit offered to entice Ahab through lying words in the prophets' mouths. Their reassuring unanimity belonged to judgment upon the king, and the Lord had announced disaster. Micaiah exposed the deception in the kings' hearing. Ahab was not left without a clear warning merely because many other voices pleased him.", "Zedekiah struck Micaiah on the cheek and challenged his claim to speak by God's Spirit. Micaiah said the truth would become clear when Zedekiah hid in an inner room. Ahab ordered Micaiah imprisoned on meagre bread and water until his safe return. The prophet answered that if Ahab returned safely, the Lord had not spoken through him, and called the people to witness. The kings nevertheless went to battle. Ahab disguised himself while Jehoshaphat wore royal robes. The Aramean chariot commanders had been ordered to concentrate on Israel's king. Mistaking Jehoshaphat for him, they surrounded Judah's ruler. He cried out, and the Lord helped him by turning them away once they recognised their mistake.", "An archer drew his bow without selecting Ahab and struck him between the pieces of his armour. Wounded, the king asked to be taken from the fighting, but remained propped in his chariot as the battle intensified. He died near sunset. His disguise had not placed him beyond the word he rejected. Jehoshaphat's rescue also did not make the alliance wise, as the next chapter will show. This account brings flattery, pressure and punishment around one truthful witness, then lets the outcome establish whose message came from God. Agreement among many speakers could not make a false assurance reliable. The solitary warning was the one the kings most needed to hear."], "lesson": "Truth must be tested by God's word rather than popularity or convenience. Hearing a warning is useful only when we are willing to respond.", "question": "Whose honest correction am I tempted to reject because more agreeable voices surround me?"},
  {"id": 94518, "title": "Jehoshaphat Returns and Appoints Judges", "subtitle": "Correction leads into a renewed duty to serve justly", "text": "2 Chronicles 19:1–11", "chapters": [[13, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Jehoshaphat returned safely to Jerusalem from the battle in which Ahab died. His escape had been God's help, but it did not mean that his participation had been approved. Jehu son of Hanani went to meet him and asked why he should assist the wicked and love those who hated the Lord. Wrath stood against him because of that choice. The prophet also recognised good in the king: he had removed idolatrous objects and set his heart to seek God. The message was specific and balanced. It neither concealed his wrongdoing beneath his earlier reforms nor declared that the failure made every faithful act meaningless.", "Jehoshaphat remained in Jerusalem and then went among the people again, from Beersheba to the hill country of Ephraim, bringing them back to the God of their fathers. The account gives no response of imprisonment or angry retaliation like Asa's treatment of Hanani. Instead, it describes renewed public responsibility. Jehoshaphat appointed judges in the fortified towns throughout Judah and charged them to consider carefully what they were doing. Their judgments were rendered before God, who was present with them in that work. A local dispute might appear to concern only the people standing before the court, but the judge remained accountable to a greater authority than the king who appointed him.", "The fear of the Lord was to govern their decisions. God had no injustice, favouritism or acceptance of bribes, and those judging under His authority were to act accordingly. Wealth, influence and personal connection could not be allowed to determine a verdict. The charge gave the judges a moral standard rather than merely a title. Appointing officials would accomplish little if their conduct reproduced the corruption they were meant to restrain. Each town needed people willing to apply the right judgment when doing so proved inconvenient. Reverence for God belonged in the handling of evidence, the treatment of neighbours and the refusal of gifts meant to buy a favourable answer.", "In Jerusalem, Jehoshaphat appointed Levites, priests and family heads for difficult cases and disputes. They were to serve faithfully and wholeheartedly and warn the people against offending God. Amariah the chief priest held responsibility for matters concerning the Lord, while Zebadiah handled the king's affairs, with Levites assisting as officers. The closing encouragement called them to act courageously. Justice required more than knowing a rule. Those entrusted with it needed the strength to carry it out. Jehoshaphat's own correction now stood beside a system intended to correct and guide others. His safe return became an opportunity to renew service, and the chapter directs that service towards the daily fairness on which the lives of ordinary people depended."], "lesson": "Accepting correction should lead to renewed faithfulness in practical duties. Reverence for God requires justice without favouritism or bribery.", "question": "Where does my responsibility require a fair decision that personal influence or convenience might distort?"},
  {"id": 94519, "title": "Judah Waits Before God and Goes Out Singing", "subtitle": "Fear is brought into prayer and answered with deliverance", "text": "2 Chronicles 20:1–37", "chapters": [[13, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["A great force from Moab, Ammon and Mount Seir approached Judah. When Jehoshaphat heard that it had reached En-gedi, he was afraid. He set himself to seek the Lord and proclaimed a fast. People came from Judah's towns, standing before God with their wives and children. The king remembered God's rule over nations, His gift of the land and the sanctuary where they had been taught to cry for help. The invading peoples now sought to drive them from their inheritance. Jehoshaphat confessed that they lacked strength and did not know what to do, but their eyes were upon God. His public prayer made uncertainty a reason to seek the Lord rather than conceal their need.", "The Spirit came upon Jahaziel, a Levite from Asaph's family. He told the assembly not to fear because this battle belonged to God. They were to go out the next day to the named place, take their position and see the Lord's salvation without fighting. Jehoshaphat and the people bowed in worship, and Levites rose to praise loudly. Early in the morning they went towards the wilderness of Tekoa. The king urged them to trust God and His prophetic word. Singers were appointed before the army to praise His holiness and enduring mercy. Their song answered a particular promise already given, rather than attempting to force a victory through sound.", "As they began to sing, the Lord set the invaders against one another. Ammon and Moab attacked Mount Seir, then turned upon each other. Judah reached the lookout and found the ground covered with bodies. They spent three days gathering the abundant spoil. On the fourth day they blessed God in the valley called Berachah, then returned joyfully to Jerusalem with instruments and trumpets. Fear of God spread among the neighbouring kingdoms, and Judah received rest. The people who had stood helpless with their children now came back in thanksgiving. Their deliverance directed attention to the Lord who had heard and helped them.", "The closing assessment records Jehoshaphat's generally faithful reign while acknowledging that the people had not fully prepared their hearts and that high places remained. It also records another harmful alliance. He joined wicked King Ahaziah of Israel in building trading ships at Ezion-geber. Eliezer announced that God would break the undertaking because of that partnership, and the ships were wrecked before sailing. The chapter therefore refuses to let the great deliverance make every later decision appear wise. Jehoshaphat could trust God wonderfully in one crisis and still repeat an earlier weakness in choosing allies. His story calls for continuing discernment after prayer has been answered, as well as gratitude for the mercy received."], "lesson": "Bring fear honestly to God and follow His word. An answered prayer should lead to continued obedience in later decisions.", "question": "After receiving God's help, where do I still need to guard against repeating a familiar mistake?"},
  {"id": 94520, "title": "Jehoram Secures His Throne by Killing His Brothers", "subtitle": "Cruel power cannot preserve a kingdom", "text": "2 Chronicles 21:1–20", "chapters": [[13, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Jehoshaphat died and was buried in David's city. His firstborn son Jehoram received the kingdom, while the other sons had been given silver, gold, valuable gifts and fortified towns. Their father's provision had given them places within Judah. Once Jehoram established himself, however, he killed his brothers and several leaders. The throne was made secure in his own eyes through the removal of people closest to him. Chronicles presents the killings plainly before describing the rest of his reign. Royal succession did not excuse the use of murder, and inherited position could not turn violence against his family into faithful government.", "Jehoram followed the ways of Israel's kings and Ahab's household, into which he had married. He did evil before God. Yet the Lord would not destroy David's house because of His covenant and the promise of a continuing lamp for David and his descendants. That restraint was mercy grounded in God's word, not approval of Jehoram. Edom rebelled and appointed its own king. Although Jehoram struck his way through an encircling force by night, Edom remained outside Judah's control. Libnah also revolted. The loss of authority is connected with his abandonment of the God of his fathers. He had made his household less secure spiritually even while killing to protect his political power.", "He established high places and led Jerusalem and Judah into unfaithfulness. A written message from Elijah confronted him. It named his rejection of Jehoshaphat's and Asa's faithful ways, his imitation of Ahab's house and the murder of brothers better than himself. Judgment would strike his people, family and possessions, and he would suffer a severe disease of the bowels. The message reached into conduct he might have hoped to hide behind royal authority. His wrongdoing involved those he had destroyed and those he was persuading to follow him. The king's personal choices had become suffering and corruption within the community entrusted to his care.", "The Lord stirred the Philistines and neighbouring Arabians against Judah. They invaded and carried away royal property, wives and sons, leaving only the youngest son, Jehoahaz. Jehoram then suffered the announced incurable illness and died after two painful years. His people did not honour him with the ceremonial burning given to earlier kings. He was buried in David's city but outside the royal tombs, and the account says he departed without being desired. The end answers his opening violence with terrible emptiness. The power for which he killed could neither preserve his household nor win the affection of his people. God's covenant continued, but Jehoram's own use of the throne left a memory of harm."], "lesson": "Position cannot justify cruelty or protect wrongdoing from God's judgment. Care for those entrusted to us matters more than securing our own control.", "question": "Where am I tempted to protect my position at the expense of another person's safety or dignity?"},
  {"id": 94521, "title": "Joash Is Hidden While Athaliah Seizes Power", "subtitle": "A threatened royal line survives through one courageous rescue", "text": "2 Chronicles 22:1–12", "chapters": [[13, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After Jehoram's death, Jerusalem made his youngest surviving son Ahaziah king. The invading bands had killed the older sons, leaving a household already wounded by violence. Ahaziah's reign lasted only one year. His mother Athaliah counselled him towards wickedness, and advisers associated with Ahab's house helped shape his decisions. The account identifies their influence as destructive. Becoming king had not freed him from the need to examine the people guiding him. His chosen counsel drew him further into the very pattern that had damaged his father's reign, and its consequences soon reached beyond the palace into the survival of David's family.", "Ahaziah joined Joram of Israel in war against Hazael of Aram at Ramoth-gilead. Joram was wounded and returned to Jezreel to recover. Ahaziah went to visit him there. At this moment Jehu was carrying out the judgment appointed against Ahab's house. Ahaziah went with Joram to meet him and became caught within that overthrow. Princes of Judah and relatives serving Ahaziah were killed. Ahaziah himself was found hiding in Samaria, brought to Jehu and put to death. He received burial because of his connection with Jehoshaphat, remembered as a man who sought the Lord wholeheartedly. That honour towards his ancestor could not give Ahaziah's weakened household the power to hold the kingdom.", "When Athaliah learned that her son was dead, she began destroying Judah's royal heirs. The earlier influence through counsel became an open attempt to take the throne for herself. The violence threatened to extinguish the line whose preservation Chronicles has connected with God's promise. Yet within the slaughter, Jehoshabeath acted. She was a daughter of Jehoram, Ahaziah's sister and the wife of Jehoiada the priest. She took Ahaziah's son Joash from among the children marked for death and hid him with his nurse in a bedroom. Athaliah did not kill him because she did not have him in her hands.", "Joash remained hidden with them in God's house for six years while Athaliah ruled. The chapter ends without a public rescue or immediate reversal. A child survived through the courage of a woman, the care of a nurse and a household willing to shelter him. Their work required secrecy and patience during years in which the wrong ruler appeared secure. God's promise did not depend on the threatened child possessing any power to defend himself. The account gives the rescue its human details so that preservation is seen through faithful action. Jehoshabeath could not repair every loss, but the life she protected mattered to the future of the people."], "lesson": "Faithfulness may require protecting someone vulnerable when wrongdoing holds public power. A quiet act of courage can have consequences far beyond what we can see.", "question": "Who needs practical protection or support that I am in a position to provide?"},
  {"id": 94522, "title": "Jehoiada Brings the Hidden King Before the People", "subtitle": "A crown is joined to a renewed covenant", "text": "2 Chronicles 23:1–21", "chapters": [[13, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["In the seventh year Jehoiada acted to bring Joash out of hiding. He made an agreement with trusted commanders, who gathered Levites and family heads from Judah's towns to Jerusalem. The assembly entered a covenant with the king in God's house. Jehoiada declared that the king's son should reign according to the Lord's word concerning David's descendants. The claim rested on more than opposition to Athaliah. It appealed to the promised royal line whose surviving child had been protected through the previous six years. The rescue now moved from hidden care towards public responsibility, requiring careful cooperation among those willing to support it.", "Jehoiada arranged the guards and their stations. Priests and Levites took responsibility at the doors, the palace and the foundation gate, while the people remained in the courts. Access to the sanctuary was restricted to those appointed to serve there. Levites surrounded the boy with weapons, and the arriving and departing divisions remained on duty together. David's stored weapons were distributed to the commanders. Then Joash was brought out, crowned and given the testimony. Jehoiada and his sons anointed him, and the people acclaimed the king. The crown was accompanied by the witness of God's law. The boy was receiving a duty under divine authority, not an unrestricted right to follow his wishes.", "Athaliah heard the noise and came to the temple. She saw the king at his place, commanders and trumpeters beside him and the people rejoicing with singers and instruments. She tore her clothes and cried treason. Jehoiada ordered the commanders to take her outside the ranks and not kill her within God's house. She was taken towards the palace and put to death at the entrance of the horse gate. The account records the violent end of her rule without making private revenge its lesson. Its larger movement concerns the restoration of David's line and the removal of a regime established through the killing of children.", "Jehoiada made a covenant involving himself, the people and the king, that they should belong to the Lord. The people demolished Baal's temple and its images and killed Mattan its priest. Duties in God's house were restored according to Moses' law and David's arrangements, with offerings, singing and guarded entrances. Joash was escorted to the palace and placed upon the royal throne. The people rejoiced, and the city became quiet. The new beginning therefore included worship and covenant as well as a change of ruler. Its future would depend on whether the restored king and people actually lived according to the commitment they had made together."], "lesson": "A good beginning must be joined to a willingness to live under God's word. Position brings responsibility, and public promises need continuing obedience.", "question": "What commitment have I celebrated that now needs to be carried faithfully into ordinary practice?"},
  {"id": 94523, "title": "Joash Repairs the Temple and Later Kills His Benefactor's Son", "subtitle": "A borrowed faith fails when faithful guidance is gone", "text": "2 Chronicles 24:1–27", "chapters": [[13, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Joash began reigning at seven and ruled forty years. While Jehoiada lived, he did what was right. He later determined to repair the temple, damaged during Athaliah's time, when dedicated things had been diverted to Baal worship. The first effort to collect money through the Levites moved too slowly. Joash called attention to the delay, and a chest was placed outside the temple gate. People and leaders brought the required contributions gladly. The king's scribe and the high priest's officer emptied the chest when it filled, returning it to receive more. Funds paid masons, carpenters and metalworkers, and the house was restored.", "The workers completed the repairs and brought the remaining money to Joash and Jehoiada. It supplied vessels for the temple's ministry. Burnt offerings continued during Jehoiada's lifetime. When the priest died at a hundred thirty, he was buried among the kings because of the good he had done for God and Israel. His faithful care had preserved Joash's life, supported his throne and helped restore worship. The honour at his burial recognised a service that had reached far beyond his own household. Yet the removal of his influence soon revealed how little of that faithfulness the king had made his own.", "Judah's leaders came and bowed before Joash, and he listened to them. The king and people abandoned God's house and turned to idols. God sent prophets to bring them back, but they would not listen. The Spirit came upon Zechariah, Jehoiada's son, who confronted their disobedience and warned that abandoning God had brought abandonment. Joash ordered him stoned in the temple court. The king who had been protected by Jehoiada now killed the faithful man's son. As Zechariah died, he called upon the Lord to see and require an answer. The restored stones of the temple surrounded an act that exposed the terrible disorder of the king's heart.", "An Aramean force came against Judah and Jerusalem, killed the leaders and sent spoil to Damascus. Though its numbers were small, the Lord delivered Judah's larger army into its hand because the people had forsaken Him. Joash was left severely afflicted. His own servants then conspired against him because of the blood shed from Jehoiada's family and killed him on his bed. He was buried in David's city but outside the royal tombs, and Amaziah succeeded him. The contrast with Jehoiada's honoured burial is painful. Joash had helped repair a building while guidance remained beside him, but refused the word that could have corrected his later course. His story calls for a faith that remains personally rooted in God when a trusted guide is no longer present."], "lesson": "Gratitude and faithfulness must outlast the people who first taught us. Outward religious work cannot replace a heart willing to receive God's correction.", "question": "Would my obedience continue if the person who encourages or supervises me were no longer present?"},
  {"id": 94524, "title": "Amaziah Wins a Battle and Rejects the Warning Afterward", "subtitle": "Pride turns a partial obedience towards ruin", "text": "2 Chronicles 25:1–28", "chapters": [[13, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Amaziah became king at twenty-five. He did right, but his heart was not wholly devoted. When secure on the throne, he executed the servants who had killed his father, yet spared their children according to Moses' law that each person should answer for their own sin. He assembled three hundred thousand soldiers from Judah and Benjamin and hired a hundred thousand northern Israelites for a hundred talents of silver. A man of God warned him not to take those hired troops, because the Lord was not with northern Israel. When Amaziah asked about the money already paid, the answer was that God could give him much more.", "Amaziah dismissed the hired soldiers, who returned in anger. He led Judah to the Valley of Salt and defeated Edom. The victory included a grim act: ten thousand captives were taken to a cliff and thrown down. That cruelty must be kept within the account rather than lost behind the king's military success. Meanwhile, the dismissed troops raided Judah's towns, killed three thousand people and carried off spoil. Amaziah had accepted one costly correction, but obedience in that decision did not make every subsequent act admirable or spare the land every consequence of the wider undertaking.", "After defeating Edom, Amaziah brought its gods home and worshipped them. A prophet asked why he sought gods unable to save their own people from his hand. The king interrupted and threatened him. The prophet stopped, but declared that God had determined to destroy him because he had done this and rejected counsel. Amaziah then challenged Joash king of Israel to a confrontation. Joash answered with a story of a thistle making a grand request of a cedar, only to be trampled by a passing animal. He warned Amaziah that victory over Edom had made him boastful and urged him to stay home rather than bring disaster on Judah.", "Amaziah refused. At Beth-shemesh, Judah was defeated and he was captured. Joash brought him to Jerusalem, demolished a large section of wall, took temple and palace treasures and carried hostages back to Samaria. The king who had feared losing his payment to hired soldiers now lost much more through pride and idolatry. He survived Joash by fifteen years, but a conspiracy eventually drove him to Lachish, where he was killed. His body was returned for burial. The chapter traces a divided heart through genuine restraint, partial obedience, cruelty, false worship and rejected warnings. Its final lesson cannot be reduced to the first victory. The condition of the heart is disclosed by what a person does when correction stands against a new desire."], "lesson": "Partial obedience must grow into a wholehearted response to God. Success makes humility and willingness to hear correction more necessary.", "question": "Has a recent success made me less willing to listen to a warning?"},
  {"id": 94525, "title": "Uzziah Becomes Strong and Crosses a Forbidden Boundary", "subtitle": "A king's success does not give him every responsibility", "text": "2 Chronicles 26:1–23", "chapters": [[13, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Judah made Uzziah king at sixteen, and his reign lasted fifty-two years. He restored Eloth and began by doing what was right. During the days of Zechariah, who instructed him in reverence for God, he sought the Lord and prospered. He defeated Philistine towns, received help against other enemies and gained recognition towards Egypt. The Ammonites brought tribute. His early strength is explained as God's help, not simply the natural result of a gifted young ruler. The account makes that explanation clear before describing the many achievements that might later tempt him to think of himself as their sole author.", "Uzziah fortified Jerusalem with towers, built in the wilderness and dug many wells. He possessed large herds and took an interest in farming, with workers and vineyards in the hills. His organised army received shields, spears, helmets, armour, bows and slings. Skilled men devised equipment for towers and battlements to shoot arrows and large stones. The combination of agriculture, defence and invention made his name widely known. Chronicles says he was wonderfully helped until he became strong. That sentence places all the useful activity beneath a gift. The next movement shows what happened when the strength produced through help became an occasion for pride.", "Uzziah entered the temple to burn incense on the altar. Azariah the priest followed with eighty courageous priests and confronted him. Burning incense belonged to Aaron's consecrated descendants, not to the king. They ordered him to leave the sanctuary because he had trespassed and would receive no honour from God for the act. Uzziah became angry while holding the censer. As he raged against the priests, leprosy appeared on his forehead beside the incense altar. The priests saw it and hurried him out, and he himself hastened to leave because the Lord had struck him. His royal power could neither erase the boundary nor prevent the judgment.", "He remained afflicted until his death and lived in a separate house, excluded from the temple. Jotham his son managed the palace and judged the people. Uzziah's deeds were recorded by Isaiah, and he was eventually buried in the field belonging to the royal burial ground because of his condition. The reign that had extended for decades ended under a lasting sign of the boundary he had refused. His earlier accomplishments were real, but they did not entitle him to take another person's sacred duty. The priests' courage also deserves attention. They served the king best by resisting him when he crossed God's command. Faithful leadership must be able to hear that a responsibility belongs to someone else."], "lesson": "God's help should produce gratitude rather than a claim to unlimited authority. Respect the responsibilities and boundaries He has given.", "question": "Where do I need to accept a limit instead of assuming that success gives me the right to take control?"},
  {"id": 94526, "title": "Jotham Orders His Ways Before the Lord", "subtitle": "A brief reign shows the value and limits of a faithful example", "text": "2 Chronicles 27:1–9", "chapters": [[13, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Jotham became king at twenty-five and ruled sixteen years in Jerusalem. His mother was Jerushah daughter of Zadok. He did what was right before the Lord, following the good in his father Uzziah's reign while avoiding the act that had brought his father low. He did not enter the temple to seize the priestly duty. The comparison matters because learning from a parent does not require repeating every choice. Jotham could carry forward what was faithful and refuse the proud intrusion that had ended Uzziah's public service. His own reign began with a responsibility to distinguish between the two.", "He built the upper gate of the Lord's house and carried out extensive work on the wall of Ophel. He also built towns in Judah's hill country and fortified places and towers in the wooded areas. The short account gives no invented speeches for these undertakings. It preserves the work itself as part of his government. Care for Jerusalem, the temple approach and the wider territory belonged within one reign. His refusal to cross the sanctuary's boundary did not mean indifference to the house of God. He could contribute appropriately without claiming an office he had not received. That distinction gave a practical form to the lesson visible in his father's life.", "Jotham fought the Ammonite king and prevailed. For three years the Ammonites supplied substantial silver, wheat and barley. His strength is then explained by the statement that he ordered his ways before the Lord his God. The emphasis falls on a settled direction rather than on a single crisis prayer or spectacular achievement. His conduct was arranged in God's sight. Chronicles does not expand that statement into details it has not recorded, but leaves it as the explanation readers should carry into the material signs of success. Military results and tribute were visible, while the ordering of his life before God was the deeper matter.", "The chapter also states that the people continued acting corruptly. A faithful king could offer direction without automatically transforming every heart in the land. This sober notice prevents the account from treating good leadership as a mechanical guarantee of communal obedience. Jotham's other wars and deeds were preserved in the royal records. He died, was buried in David's city and was succeeded by Ahaz. His brief account leaves no dramatic personal downfall, yet it carries an important measure of faithfulness. He respected a boundary his father had crossed and directed his own life before God. At the same time, the people remained responsible for whether they would follow the good example before them."], "lesson": "Learn from both the strengths and failures of those before you. Order your own life before God without assuming that another person's example can replace your personal obedience.", "question": "What good example should I follow, and what inherited mistake should I deliberately avoid?"},
  {"id": 94527, "title": "Oded Calls for Mercy While Ahaz Turns Further Away", "subtitle": "Distress leads some to repentance and others to deeper sin", "text": "2 Chronicles 28:1–27", "chapters": [[13, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Ahaz became king at twenty and followed the northern kings rather than David's faithful example. He made images for the Baals, worshipped throughout the land and burned his children in the Valley of Hinnom. His wrongdoing brought terrible suffering upon others. Aram defeated Judah and carried captives to Damascus. Pekah of Israel also struck Judah, killing a hundred twenty thousand in one day, while royal officials and a son of the king fell. Northern Israel carried away two hundred thousand women and children with much spoil. The account connects Judah's humiliation with abandoning God, but it does not give the victors permission to treat their fellow Israelites without restraint.", "Oded the prophet met the returning army at Samaria. He declared that God had given Judah into their hands in anger, yet their own rage had reached heaven. They intended to enslave captives while having sins of their own before the Lord. He ordered them to return their brothers and sisters. Four Ephraimite leaders supported the warning and refused to allow the captives to be brought in. The armed men yielded. Named men then used the spoil to clothe the naked, provide shoes, food and drink, and tend the captives. The weak rode on donkeys to Jericho, where they were returned to their people. Receiving correction became visible through practical mercy.", "Ahaz responded differently to distress. Edom attacked and carried off captives, while Philistines occupied towns in Judah's lowlands and south. He asked Assyria for help and gave treasure from the temple, palace and leaders to Tiglath-pileser. The Assyrian ruler troubled him rather than strengthening him. Ahaz's payment could not buy the security he wanted. Instead of turning to the Lord, he increased his unfaithfulness. He reasoned that the gods of Damascus must have helped the Arameans defeat him and began sacrificing to them. The account identifies those gods as his ruin. He read defeat as an argument for imitating false worship rather than a warning about abandoning the true God.", "He gathered and cut up temple vessels, shut the temple doors and established altars at Jerusalem's corners. High places for other gods spread through Judah's towns. Ahaz died and was buried in Jerusalem but outside the royal tombs, and Hezekiah succeeded him. Within one chapter, two responses to a divine warning have been displayed. Northern leaders acknowledged their guilt and restored vulnerable captives with care, while Judah's king used his distress to justify further rebellion. Suffering alone does not guarantee repentance. The difference appears in whether a person will hear God's word and change. The captives' restored dignity gives the reader a concrete picture of the mercy that such a change can produce."], "lesson": "Receiving correction should lead to practical mercy. Trouble calls us to seek God rather than imitate the wrongdoing of those who seem stronger.", "question": "What act of restoration could show that I have truly accepted a correction?"},
  {"id": 94528, "title": "Hezekiah Opens the Doors His Father Closed", "subtitle": "Renewal begins with cleansing and restored worship", "text": "2 Chronicles 29:1–36", "chapters": [[13, 29]], "start": 1, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Hezekiah became king at twenty-five and did what was right before the Lord. In the first month of his first year, he opened and repaired the temple doors. Ahaz had shut them, but his son made restoring worship an early priority. He gathered priests and Levites and called them to consecrate themselves and cleanse God's house. Their fathers had turned their backs on the Lord, closed the porch, extinguished the lamps and stopped the appointed offerings. The suffering around them bore witness to the consequences. Hezekiah desired a covenant with God and urged the servants not to remain negligent in the work for which they had been chosen.", "Levite leaders gathered their brothers and prepared themselves according to the Lord's word. Priests removed uncleanness from the inner house into the court, and Levites carried it away to the Kidron. Beginning on the first day of the month, they reached the porch on the eighth and completed the cleansing on the sixteenth. They reported that the house, altar, sacred table and vessels were ready. Objects Ahaz had discarded were prepared and consecrated again. The renewal involved sustained work by named people. Opening the doors was an important beginning, but the rooms and equipment behind them also needed attention before service could properly resume.", "Hezekiah rose early and brought the city leaders to God's house. Animals were presented for offerings concerning the kingdom, sanctuary and Judah. The leaders laid hands upon the goats for the sin offering, and the priests made atonement for all Israel, as the king had commanded. Levites stood with instruments according to the arrangements of David, Gad and Nathan, and priests sounded trumpets. When the burnt offering began, song began with it. The congregation worshipped while singers and trumpeters continued until the offering was finished. Then king and people bowed, and praise in the words of David and Asaph rose with gladness. The reopened house received confession and thanksgiving together.", "The people brought further sacrifices and thank offerings freely. Their generosity exceeded the number of priests ready to prepare everything, so Levites assisted until the work was done and more priests were consecrated. The account notes the Levites' greater readiness in this matter. Abundant offerings, drink offerings and peace offerings filled the service, which was set in order again. Hezekiah and the people rejoiced that God had prepared the people, for the change had come quickly. Its speed did not remove the need for cleansing, sacrifice, cooperation and willing hearts. The chapter shows renewal reaching both a neglected building and the people responsible for serving within it. Their gladness followed an honest reckoning with what had gone wrong."], "lesson": "Renewal requires more than reopening an old activity. Remove what has defiled it, receive God's mercy and prepare to serve faithfully again.", "question": "What neglected responsibility needs both a fresh beginning and the patient work that makes the beginning real?"},
  {"id": 94529, "title": "Some Mock the Invitation and Others Come to Passover", "subtitle": "A scattered people are called to return to a merciful God", "text": "2 Chronicles 30:1–27", "chapters": [[13, 30]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Hezekiah sent letters throughout Judah and Israel, including Ephraim and Manasseh, inviting people to Jerusalem for Passover. The king and assembly agreed to keep it in the second month because the priests and people had not been ready at the usual time. Messengers carried an appeal from Beersheba to Dan. The surviving people were to return to the God of Abraham, Isaac and Israel rather than repeat their ancestors' stubbornness. They were urged to enter His sanctuary and serve Him. The invitation held out hope for relatives in captivity because the Lord was gracious and merciful and would not turn away those who returned.", "The messengers met different responses as they passed through northern towns. Many laughed at them and mocked the appeal. Yet some from Asher, Manasseh and Zebulun humbled themselves and came. In Judah, God's hand gave the people a united purpose to carry out the command grounded in His word. A large congregation gathered in Jerusalem and removed the city's false altars, throwing them into the Kidron. The invitation did not require every neighbour's approval before a person could respond. Those who came made a choice that others around them had ridiculed. Their journey joined people across political and regional divisions in the worship of their fathers' God.", "Passover animals were killed on the fourteenth day of the second month. Priests and Levites consecrated themselves and performed their duties according to Moses' law. Many worshippers, however, had not completed the required purification. Levites helped with the slaughter, and Hezekiah prayed for those whose hearts were set on seeking God despite their lack of ceremonial readiness. He appealed to the good Lord to pardon them. God heard and healed the people. The account takes the prescribed order seriously while also showing mercy towards those genuinely turning to Him. The king's intercession sought forgiveness rather than pretending that the irregularity did not exist.", "Seven days of Unleavened Bread followed with daily praise, offerings and confession. Hezekiah encouraged the Levites who taught the knowledge of the Lord. The assembly then agreed to remain another seven days, supported by generous gifts of animals from the king and leaders. More priests prepared themselves, and the congregation, including foreigners, rejoiced. Such joy had not been seen in Jerusalem since Solomon's time. The priests and Levites blessed the people, and their prayer reached God's holy dwelling in heaven. The chapter ends with an answered appeal where it began with an invitation to return. Mockery had not stopped God's mercy from meeting those who humbled themselves and came."], "lesson": "Do not let other people's mockery prevent a sincere return to God. His mercy meets those who seek Him with an honest heart.", "question": "Is fear of being laughed at keeping me from responding to something God is calling me to do?"},
  {"id": 94530, "title": "The Offerings Are Gathered and Faithfully Distributed", "subtitle": "The joy of renewal becomes organised support for daily service", "text": "2 Chronicles 31:1–21", "chapters": [[13, 31]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After the festival, the Israelites who had gathered went through Judah, Benjamin, Ephraim and Manasseh removing images, sacred poles, high places and false altars. Then they returned to their towns and possessions. Their worship in Jerusalem had led to changes where they lived. Hezekiah arranged the priests and Levites in divisions for offerings, ministry, thanksgiving and praise. He also provided from his own resources for the regular sacrifices on ordinary days, Sabbaths, new moons and festivals according to the law. The king's example accompanied his instructions. Restored worship would need continuing provision after the visitors returned home and the extraordinary gathering ended.", "He commanded Jerusalem's people to supply the priests' and Levites' portion so that they could give themselves to the law's service. Firstfruits of grain, wine, oil, honey and other produce arrived abundantly. People from Judah's towns brought tithes of cattle, sheep and dedicated things. Heaps accumulated from the third month to the seventh. When Hezekiah and the leaders saw them, they blessed the Lord and His people. The king asked about the supplies, and Azariah the chief priest explained that the servants had eaten sufficiently and still had a great surplus because God had blessed the people. The visible abundance became an occasion for thanksgiving rather than a reason for careless handling.", "Hezekiah ordered rooms prepared in God's house for storage. Cononiah the Levite, his brother Shimei and appointed overseers received the offerings faithfully. Kore and other named men handled freewill gifts and distribution in the priests' towns. Portions were supplied according to the divisions, without neglecting either greater or smaller households. The records included those actively serving, together with the wider families, wives, sons and daughters who depended on the provision. Men were also appointed for priests and Levites living in the surrounding pasturelands. The account gives space to these arrangements because generosity only fulfilled its purpose when the entrusted goods actually reached those for whom they were intended.", "The chapter concludes that Hezekiah did what was good, right and faithful before the Lord throughout Judah. In service connected with God's house, the law and the commands, he sought God wholeheartedly and prospered. That assessment follows storage rooms, named overseers and fair distribution. The practical administration was part of the faithfulness, not a distraction from it. A joyful assembly could begin a renewal, but dependable arrangements helped sustain the life that followed. The people gave, the king provided, and appointed servants received and shared the resources. Together these duties supported continuing worship and the families whose work made it possible. Their careful service gave lasting shape to the willingness expressed during the festival."], "lesson": "Generosity needs trustworthy administration. Faithfulness includes making sure that what people give is cared for and reaches its proper purpose.", "question": "How can I handle shared resources so that generosity becomes dependable help rather than a neglected store?"},
  {"id": 94531, "title": "Hezekiah Prays Under Threat and Is Tested After Deliverance", "subtitle": "God's help calls for courage and continued humility", "text": "2 Chronicles 32:1–33", "chapters": [[13, 32]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After Hezekiah's reforms, Sennacherib invaded Judah and besieged its fortified towns. The king prepared Jerusalem by stopping water sources outside the city, repairing walls, strengthening towers and making weapons. He appointed commanders and encouraged the people. Assyria had human strength, but Judah had the Lord to help and fight for them. The people took confidence from his words. Faithful reform had not prevented an invasion, and trusting God did not prevent Hezekiah from taking practical precautions. He placed their preparations beneath the greater assurance that the enemy's visible force was not the final measure of their situation.", "From Lachish, Sennacherib sent servants to challenge that trust. They accused Hezekiah of leading the people towards starvation and argued that removing local altars had weakened rather than honoured their worship. They listed nations whose gods had not saved them and treated the Lord as another powerless object made by human hands. Letters repeated the insults. The messengers called out in the people's own language to frighten those on the wall and make the city easier to capture. The pressure targeted confidence as well as fortifications. Hezekiah and Isaiah answered by praying and crying to heaven, bringing the threats against God and His people before Him.", "The Lord sent an angel who destroyed mighty men and commanders in the Assyrian camp. Sennacherib returned home in shame and was later killed by his own sons in the house of his god. The Lord saved Hezekiah and Jerusalem and guided them on every side. Gifts came to God's house and to the king, whose honour increased. The chapter also records Hezekiah's grave illness, his prayer and God's answer with a sign. Yet the benefits he received were followed by pride rather than fitting gratitude. Wrath stood against him and Judah, but he and Jerusalem humbled themselves, and the announced judgment did not fall during his lifetime.", "Hezekiah possessed great wealth, stores, herds and buildings because God had given him much. He redirected the upper waters of Gihon towards the City of David and prospered in his work. When Babylonian envoys came to enquire about the wonder in the land, God left him to a test that exposed his heart. Chronicles gives that meaning without retelling every detail found in Kings. Hezekiah eventually died with honour and was succeeded by Manasseh. His story places prayer under danger beside humility after blessing. The threat he could plainly see was not his only spiritual test. Recognition and prosperity also required a faithful response from the heart that had cried to God in weakness."], "lesson": "Seek God in danger and remain humble after rescue. Gratitude must continue when the attention of others turns towards our success.", "question": "Am I as careful about pride after a blessing as I am about fear during a crisis?"},
  {"id": 94532, "title": "Manasseh Humbles Himself in Captivity", "subtitle": "A deeply sinful king finds mercy and begins to undo his wrong", "text": "2 Chronicles 33:1–25", "chapters": [[13, 33]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Manasseh began reigning at twelve and ruled fifty-five years. He reversed Hezekiah's reforms, rebuilt high places, established Baal worship and honoured the heavenly bodies. Altars entered the temple courts, and an image was placed within God's house. He sacrificed his children, practised divination and sought mediums. The king led Judah into conduct worse than that of the nations removed before Israel. God's promises about Jerusalem and the land had called for obedience, yet Manasseh used his position to spread the very practices those commands opposed. The Lord spoke to him and the people, but they refused to listen.", "Assyrian commanders captured Manasseh, bound him and took him to Babylon. There, in affliction, he sought the Lord and humbled himself greatly before the God of his fathers. The account gives no invented wording for his prayer. It tells what he did and how God responded. The Lord heard his appeal and returned him to Jerusalem and his kingdom. Manasseh then knew that the Lord was God. The change did not make his earlier cruelty small or remove the harm from Judah's history. It displayed mercy reaching a man whose conduct had given others strong reason to expect only judgment.", "Back in Jerusalem, he strengthened the city wall and appointed commanders in fortified towns. More importantly, he removed the foreign gods and the image from the temple, taking away the altars he had built in Jerusalem and casting them outside the city. He restored the Lord's altar, offered peace and thank offerings and commanded Judah to serve Israel's God. The people continued using high places, though now sacrificing to the Lord. That remaining practice shows the limits of the repair. Manasseh's repentance included efforts to undo his work, while the community he had misled did not simply return to an untouched beginning. Restoration was real and still bore the marks of earlier damage.", "The records preserved both his prayer and the sins committed before his humiliation. He died and was buried in his own house. Amon his son then ruled for two years and followed the earlier evil, worshipping Manasseh's images. Unlike his father, he did not humble himself but increased his guilt. Servants killed him, and the people killed the conspirators and made Josiah king. The paired accounts prevent an easy assumption that a father's late repentance automatically directs his son's choices. Manasseh had responded to mercy by changing his conduct. Amon chose the inherited wrong and refused the humility that had opened a different path. Each remained responsible for his own response to God."], "lesson": "No record of sin makes sincere repentance pointless. Mercy should lead us to remove the wrong we helped establish and accept responsibility for the harm done.", "question": "What practical change would show that my request for forgiveness includes a real turning from sin?"},
  {"id": 94533, "title": "Josiah Hears the Law and Humbles Himself", "subtitle": "The recovered book calls king and people to renewed obedience", "text": "2 Chronicles 34:1–33", "chapters": [[13, 34]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Josiah became king at eight. While still young, in his eighth year, he began seeking the God of David. In his twelfth year, he began purging Judah and Jerusalem of false worship. Baal altars were torn down before him, images crushed and sacred poles removed. The work extended through northern territories, including Manasseh, Ephraim, Simeon and Naphtali. His actions confronted a legacy older than his own reign. In his eighteenth year he sent officials to repair God's house. Money gathered from Judah and the remnant of Israel paid builders, stonecutters and carpenters. Levites supervised, and the workmen carried out their duties faithfully.", "During the handling of the temple funds, Hilkiah found the book of the Lord's law given through Moses. He gave it to Shaphan, who brought it to Josiah after reporting on the repairs. Shaphan read before the king. Josiah tore his clothes when he heard the words. The reading did more than confirm that his repairs were proceeding well. It exposed the nation's guilt under commands their fathers had not kept. He sent Hilkiah and other officers to enquire of the Lord for himself and the surviving people, recognising the greatness of the wrath announced. A king already engaged in reform still needed to submit his understanding to God's written word.", "The delegation went to Huldah the prophetess in Jerusalem. She announced that judgment would come upon the place and its people because they had abandoned God and provoked Him through their idols. The written warnings were not empty. She also gave a distinct message for Josiah. Because his heart was tender and he had humbled himself, torn his clothes and wept before God, the Lord had heard him. He would be gathered before seeing the coming devastation. The answer recognised his personal response without pretending that generations of public unfaithfulness had no consequences. He received mercy within a history already moving towards severe judgment.", "Josiah assembled the elders and went to God's house with priests, Levites and people great and small. He read the covenant book publicly and committed himself to follow the Lord wholeheartedly and keep its commands. Those present stood with the covenant. He removed detestable practices throughout the territory and directed the people to serve the Lord. During his lifetime they did not turn away from following Him. The chapter moves from a hidden book to a hearing king and then a listening assembly. Repairing walls and floors had become the setting for a deeper reckoning. Josiah's strongest response was not pride that he had already improved matters, but humility before the word that showed what still needed to change."], "lesson": "Even sincere reform must remain open to God's word. A tender heart receives its correction and helps others hear it too.", "question": "When Scripture challenges my conduct, do I defend what I have already done or ask what must now change?"},
  {"id": 94534, "title": "Josiah Keeps a Great Passover and Later Falls at Megiddo", "subtitle": "Faithful worship does not remove the need to heed a warning", "text": "2 Chronicles 35:1–27", "chapters": [[13, 35]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["Josiah kept Passover in Jerusalem on the fourteenth day of the first month. He placed priests in their duties and encouraged their service. Levites were told to put the ark in Solomon's house and prepare according to the arrangements of David and Solomon. Their work was to serve God and His people under the word given through Moses. The king provided thirty thousand lambs and young goats and three thousand cattle from his own possessions. Leaders added generous gifts for the people, priests and Levites. The reform begun with removing idols now supplied a gathering centred on remembering God's deliverance.", "Priests and Levites took their places. Passover animals were killed, blood was handled according to the prescribed service and offerings were distributed among the families. The Passover meat was roasted, and other holy offerings were prepared in pots and pans. Levites supplied portions for priests whose work continued until night. Singers and gatekeepers remained at their duties because their brothers prepared food for them. These practical details show care for those who could not leave their posts. The seven-day feast was kept, and the account declares that no comparable Passover had been observed since Samuel's days. Its greatness included order, provision, shared service and attentive obedience.", "Later, Pharaoh Necho advanced towards Carchemish on the Euphrates, and Josiah went out to confront him. Necho sent messengers declaring that he was not attacking Judah and that God had commanded him to hurry towards his own conflict. He warned Josiah not to interfere. Chronicles identifies these words as coming from God's mouth, but Josiah did not heed them. He disguised himself and entered the battle in the Valley of Megiddo. Archers wounded him severely. He asked his servants to take him away, and they transferred him to another chariot and brought him to Jerusalem. The king who had received the recovered law with tears now failed to receive a warning through an unexpected speaker.", "Josiah died and was buried among his fathers. Judah and Jerusalem mourned, and Jeremiah lamented for him. Male and female singers continued to remember him in their lamentations. The records preserved his goodness according to God's law together with his other deeds. This ending does not erase the reality of his reforms or turn his faithful Passover into hypocrisy. It does show that a good history did not make a later refusal harmless. The same king could listen humbly in the palace and act stubbornly before a battle. His death leaves grief among the people he had guided and a sober call to remain attentive. Faithfulness must continue to receive God's word rather than rely on the strength of an earlier response."], "lesson": "Past obedience does not remove the need to listen today. A warning should be weighed by its truth even when it comes through an unexpected person.", "question": "Am I dismissing a needed warning because I dislike or do not expect the person delivering it?"},
  {"id": 94535, "title": "Jerusalem Burns and Cyrus Opens a Way Home", "subtitle": "Repeatedly rejected mercy is followed by judgment and a new summons", "text": "2 Chronicles 36:1–23", "chapters": [[13, 36]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Chronicles", "story": ["After Josiah's death, the people made Jehoahaz king, but his reign lasted only three months. Necho removed him, imposed tribute and placed his brother Eliakim on the throne under the name Jehoiakim. Jehoahaz was carried to Egypt. Jehoiakim ruled wickedly for eleven years. Nebuchadnezzar came from Babylon, bound him and carried temple vessels away. Jehoiachin succeeded him briefly before he too was taken to Babylon with valuable vessels. Zedekiah was appointed over Judah. The rapid changes show a kingdom increasingly governed by foreign power. The throne remained in Jerusalem for a time, but the independence and treasures associated with earlier reigns were being stripped away.", "Zedekiah ruled eleven years and refused to humble himself before Jeremiah's word from the Lord. He rebelled against Nebuchadnezzar despite the oath made in God's name and hardened himself against turning back. Priestly leaders and people also increased their unfaithfulness and polluted the house God had made holy. The Lord repeatedly sent messengers because He had compassion on His people and His dwelling. They mocked those messengers, despised His words and mistreated His prophets until judgment could no longer be averted. Chronicles explains the fall through this long rejection of mercy. The disaster did not follow a lack of warning or indifference on God's part.", "The Babylonian king came, and people of every age suffered. Young men were killed in the sanctuary, and neither youth nor old age received protection from the invaders. Temple vessels, royal treasures and the possessions of leaders were taken to Babylon. God's house was burned, Jerusalem's wall broken down and palaces destroyed. Survivors were carried into exile and served Babylon until Persia rose. The land lay desolate and received its neglected Sabbath rest, fulfilling the word associated with Jeremiah and the seventy years. The house that had opened the book with glory and praise now stood within an account of fire and loss. Its sacred history had not made persistent disobedience safe.", "Yet the book does not end with burning ruins. In Cyrus's first year, the Lord stirred the Persian king to issue a written proclamation, fulfilling His word through Jeremiah. Cyrus acknowledged the God of heaven and announced a commission to build His house in Jerusalem. Whoever belonged to God's people was invited to go up with the Lord's presence. Judgment had been carried out, but God's purpose had not ceased. The last words point towards a return and a house to be rebuilt. Readers leave Chronicles with the need to answer that summons. The history of kings and temple has exposed both the seriousness of rejecting God's word and the mercy that opens a way forward after profound loss."], "lesson": "Do not despise repeated calls to return to God. His faithfulness makes repentance urgent and gives hope beyond judgment and loss.", "question": "What call from God should I answer now rather than continue postponing?"},
  {"id": 95000, "title": "Cyrus Opens the Way Back to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "God's word outlasts the years of exile", "text": "Ezra 1:1–11", "chapters": [[14, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["Ezra begins where Chronicles ended. In Cyrus's first year as king of Persia, the Lord stirred him to issue a proclamation, fulfilling the word spoken through Jeremiah. The message was announced throughout the kingdom and recorded in writing. Cyrus acknowledged the God of heaven as the source of his dominion and declared that he had been charged to build God's house in Jerusalem. Those belonging to God's people were invited to return and undertake the work. The ruined city was still a real place requiring labour, yet its future was no longer described only through the defeat that had emptied it.", "The proclamation also addressed those who would remain where they lived. They were to assist returning neighbours with silver, gold, goods and animals, in addition to voluntary offerings for God's house. Restoration would involve both travelling and supplying. Family heads from Judah and Benjamin, priests, Levites and others whose spirits God stirred prepared to go. The same chapter that describes God moving a king also describes Him moving His people. Royal permission opened an opportunity, but willing households had to act upon it. Their neighbours strengthened their hands with valuable objects, supplies and animals. The return became a shared undertaking carried through practical gifts as well as a public decree.", "Cyrus brought out the temple vessels that Nebuchadnezzar had removed from Jerusalem and placed in the house of his gods. Mithredath the treasurer counted them out to Sheshbazzar, the prince of Judah. The inventory names gold and silver dishes, bowls and other utensils. Objects associated with conquest were now entrusted to a journey of restoration. Their value made careful accounting necessary, but their significance went beyond the precious metal. They belonged to the worship that had been violently interrupted. Returning them testified that Babylon's seizure was not the final event in their history or in the history of the people who would use them.", "The chapter gives a total of five thousand four hundred gold and silver vessels brought by Sheshbazzar with the returning exiles. It ends with movement towards Jerusalem rather than a finished temple. The people had received permission, resources and entrusted property, but the work still lay ahead. God's faithfulness did not remove the need to leave familiar surroundings, organise a journey and begin building among ruins. The opening therefore joins promise to response. A foreign king's decree, a family's willingness and a neighbour's gift all belong within the Lord's fulfilment of His word. The reader is prepared to follow that fulfilment through delay and difficulty, without mistaking those later troubles for proof that the beginning was empty."], "lesson": "God's faithfulness can open a new beginning after long loss. Respond through the practical obedience and support available to you.", "question": "What opportunity to rebuild something good calls for action from me rather than admiration from a distance?"},
  {"id": 95001, "title": "The Returning Families Are Counted by Name", "subtitle": "Restoration belongs to a whole community", "text": "Ezra 2:1–70", "chapters": [[14, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 70, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["The return from Babylon is recorded through families, towns and responsibilities. People carried away under Nebuchadnezzar now came again to Jerusalem and Judah, each to their own town. Zerubbabel and Jeshua appear among the leaders, followed by the names and numbers of households. Some groups number in thousands and others only dozens. The long list gives the return a human shape. It was not simply a change in imperial policy or the movement of two famous leaders. Descendants of particular families made the journey, bringing their remembered places and relationships into a land where a new life would have to be established.", "The record includes people associated with Bethlehem, Anathoth, Bethel, Ai, Jericho and other settlements. Priestly families follow, together with Levites, Asaph's singers and gatekeepers. Temple servants and descendants of Solomon's servants also receive their place. These groups help explain what a restored community would need. A building alone would not provide worship, guarding, song or daily assistance. The return included people connected with all those duties, alongside the households whose fields and towns would sustain the wider life of Judah. Preserving their names recognises service that could otherwise disappear behind the better known story of rebuilding the sanctuary.", "Some returnees could not demonstrate their family descent through the available records. Certain men claiming priestly ancestry searched for their registrations but did not find them. They were excluded from the priestly service and from the most holy food until the question could receive an authorised resolution. The governor did not simply declare the missing evidence irrelevant because workers were needed. Nor does the chapter supply a final verdict that it has not recorded. The restriction concerned a particular sacred responsibility. The account places hope and careful discernment together: the community could welcome a return from exile while still taking seriously the duties for which clear family qualification was required.", "The whole assembly numbered forty-two thousand three hundred sixty, besides servants and the additional singers noted in the record. Horses, mules, camels and donkeys are also counted, reminding the reader of the practical scale of relocation. On reaching the site of God's house, some family heads gave freely according to their ability for its rebuilding. Gold, silver and priestly garments were added to the work's resources. Then people settled in their towns. The list ends in places of ordinary life rather than leaving everyone permanently gathered at the temple site. Worship would rise within a people who needed homes, livelihood, shared service and a continuing memory of who had come. Their restoration was made up of many lives, and the record treated those lives as worth remembering."], "lesson": "God's work includes households and varied forms of service. Give according to your ability and value contributions that receive little public attention.", "question": "Whose quiet contribution helps sustain our community, and how can I acknowledge or support it?"},
  {"id": 95002, "title": "The Altar Stands Before the Temple Is Built", "subtitle": "Joy and weeping rise over a new foundation", "text": "Ezra 3:1–13", "chapters": [[14, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["When the seventh month arrived, the people gathered in Jerusalem with a shared purpose. Jeshua and the priests, together with Zerubbabel and his fellow leaders, rebuilt the altar of Israel's God. Their aim was to offer the sacrifices prescribed through Moses. Fear of surrounding peoples remained, but it did not postpone worship until every danger had gone. They set the altar on its base and began morning and evening burnt offerings. The people who had returned to a broken city could not yet point to a restored temple, but they could begin obeying what God had already made clear.", "They kept the Feast of Tabernacles according to the written directions, supplying the daily offerings in their proper numbers. Regular sacrifices, new moons, appointed festivals and voluntary gifts followed. The first day of the seventh month marked this renewed service, even though the temple foundation had not been laid. The sequence matters. They did not possess every structure they hoped to build before drawing near through the worship appointed to them. Meanwhile, masons and carpenters received money, and food, drink and oil went to workers in Sidon and Tyre. Cedar would be brought from Lebanon by sea to Joppa under Cyrus's permission. Present worship and future preparation proceeded together.", "In the second month of the second year after their arrival, leaders, priests, Levites and the returning people began work on the house. Levites from twenty years old supervised. When the builders laid the foundation, the priests stood with trumpets and Asaph's descendants with cymbals, following David's arrangements. They sang in response, praising the Lord for His goodness and enduring mercy towards Israel. The people shouted because the foundation was laid. Their rejoicing concerned a beginning rather than a finished achievement. After the loss and interruption of exile, even the first visible stage of rebuilding gave them reason to thank God.", "Among the older priests, Levites and family heads were people who remembered the former temple. Seeing the new foundation, they wept loudly while others shouted for joy. The sounds mingled until listeners could not distinguish the weeping from the celebration, and the noise carried far. The chapter does not require the reader to invent one motive for every tear. It records memory, grief and hope meeting within the same assembly. Those starting again could receive the present gift while still feeling the weight of what had been lost. The new foundation did not erase the old sorrow. It gave a community carrying that sorrow a place from which to continue worshipping and working together."], "lesson": "Begin the obedience already within reach, even while much remains unfinished. Gratitude for renewal can share a heart with grief over real loss.", "question": "What faithful beginning can I make now without waiting for every difficulty or sorrow to disappear?"},
  {"id": 95003, "title": "Opposition Grows from Discouragement to Official Accusation", "subtitle": "The rebuilding faces pressure across successive reigns", "text": "Ezra 4:1–24", "chapters": [[14, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["When the adversaries of Judah and Benjamin heard that the returned exiles were building the temple, they approached Zerubbabel and the family heads. They asked to share the work, claiming to seek the same God and recalling their settlement under Esarhaddon of Assyria. Zerubbabel, Jeshua and the other leaders refused the proposed partnership. They would build together under the charge given by Cyrus. The account identifies the approaching group as adversaries before recording their offer. Its concern is the responsibility for rebuilding God's house, and the leaders would not surrender that responsibility merely because the proposal sounded cooperative.", "The surrounding people then weakened the builders' hands, frightened them and hired advisers to frustrate the undertaking. Pressure continued through the days of Cyrus towards Darius's reign. The chapter broadens its account by including accusations from later reigns, under Ahasuerus and Artaxerxes, showing the continuing pattern of opposition to Jerusalem. In the letter associated with Artaxerxes, Rehum, Shimshai and their companions accused the Jews of rebuilding a rebellious city and its walls. These city accusations should be distinguished from the temple work with which the chapter begins. The collected correspondence shows how opponents used political suspicion across time, before the narrative returns to the earlier interruption of the temple project.", "The writers warned that a restored city would stop paying taxes and damage the king's revenue. They professed concern for royal honour and asked for a search of earlier records. Jerusalem's past rebellions, they argued, proved that rebuilding threatened imperial control beyond the Euphrates. Their letter took facts about a troubled history and used them to frame the present people as an inevitable danger. The king answered that records confirmed former resistance and powerful rulers in Jerusalem. He ordered the building of the city stopped until a further command. Rehum and his companions hurried to enforce the instruction, using force and power to make the Jews cease.", "The final verse returns to the temple work, which remained stopped until Darius's second year. This ending leaves the reader facing a real delay. Cyrus's decree had been genuine, the people had returned and the altar stood, yet progress was not uninterrupted. Opposition used fear, discouragement, hired influence and official procedure. Faithfulness would require more than the enthusiasm of laying the first foundation. The chapter prepares for the renewed prophetic call that follows, while warning against a simple expectation that God's work never meets obstruction. It also asks careful readers to follow the different subjects and periods in the correspondence rather than flatten every accusation into one moment or one building project."], "lesson": "Discouragement and misrepresentation can obstruct good work. Remain truthful and discerning when pressure tries to replace a clear responsibility with fear.", "question": "Which pressure is weakening my willingness to continue a responsibility I know to be right?"},
  {"id": 95004, "title": "The Builders Resume and Answer the Governor", "subtitle": "God's care sustains work under examination", "text": "Ezra 5:1–17", "chapters": [[14, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["Haggai and Zechariah spoke to the Jews in Judah and Jerusalem in the name of Israel's God. Their ministry led into action. Zerubbabel and Jeshua rose and began rebuilding the house, with the prophets beside them in support. The long interruption did not have to become the permanent condition of the work. God's word called the leaders back to their duty, and the prophets' help was more than distant encouragement. The account places them with the builders as the project resumed. Renewed obedience did not begin because every political question had already been settled. It began with a fresh response to what God had said.", "Tattenai, governor of the province beyond the Euphrates, came with Shethar-boznai and other officials. They asked who had authorised the building and requested the names of those responsible. The questions could have led to another immediate halt. Instead, the elders continued while the matter was referred to Darius, because God's eye was upon them. The writer does not claim that the governor never examined their work. Divine care is shown within an examination that was allowed to proceed without stopping it. The builders had to answer real officials and trust God through the period before the royal response arrived.", "Tattenai's report described a house being built with large stones and timber, progressing diligently. It included the elders' answer. They called themselves servants of the God of heaven and earth and explained that they were rebuilding a house originally erected by a great king of Israel. They also acknowledged why it had been destroyed. Their ancestors had provoked God, who gave them into Nebuchadnezzar's hand, leading to the temple's destruction and the people's exile. Their defence did not rest on pretending that Judah had an entirely innocent history. They confessed the judgment while distinguishing it from the present royal permission to rebuild.", "They explained that Cyrus had ordered the house rebuilt and returned its gold and silver vessels through Sheshbazzar, whom he appointed governor. Sheshbazzar had laid the foundation, and the house remained unfinished. Tattenai asked Darius to search the archives to establish whether the decree existed and then send his ruling. The chapter ends with that request still before the king. Honest testimony, careful records and continuing work appear together under God's watch. The elders did not need to manufacture a more impressive past or threaten the official who questioned them. They could state both their failure and the mercy that had opened a new beginning. Their answer grounded present obedience in a history they were willing to tell truthfully."], "lesson": "Trust in God's care is consistent with answering questions honestly. Admit past failure without allowing it to erase a genuine opportunity to obey now.", "question": "Can I explain my responsibility truthfully, including the failures and mercy that form part of its history?"},
  {"id": 95005, "title": "The Decree Is Found and the Temple Is Finished", "subtitle": "An enquiry becomes provision for the work", "text": "Ezra 6:1–22", "chapters": [[14, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["Darius ordered a search of the archives. At Ecbatana in Media, a scroll was found preserving Cyrus's decree concerning God's house in Jerusalem. It authorised the rebuilding, described the construction and directed that royal funds meet the cost. It also required the temple vessels taken by Nebuchadnezzar to be returned to their places. The elders' answer had been supported by the written record. What their opponents or officials might have treated as a doubtful claim now stood before the king as an earlier royal instruction. The search did not invent permission. It recovered a command already given and still relevant to the unfinished house.", "Darius instructed Tattenai and the other officials to leave the builders alone. He went further, ordering that the provincial revenue provide the expenses without delay. Animals, grain, salt, wine and oil needed by the priests were to be supplied daily so that sacrifices could be offered and prayers made for the king and his sons. The decree carried a severe imperial penalty against anyone who altered it. Those who had asked about authority now received orders to support the work. Tattenai and his companions complied promptly. The account shows a remarkable reversal through ordinary instruments of government: an archive, a recovered scroll and an enforceable letter.", "The elders continued building under the encouragement of Haggai and Zechariah. The writer gathers God's command and the supporting decrees of Persian kings into the account of completion. The temple was finished on the third day of Adar in Darius's sixth year. Priests, Levites and returned exiles dedicated it with joy. They offered cattle, rams and lambs, together with twelve goats as a sin offering for all Israel, according to the number of the tribes. Their thanksgiving did not claim that rebuilding had made atonement unnecessary. They came as a restored people still needing forgiveness. Priests and Levites were arranged in their divisions for service according to Moses' book.", "Passover followed on the fourteenth day of the first month. Priests and Levites had purified themselves, and the returning exiles shared the meal with others who had separated themselves from the surrounding people's impurity to seek Israel's God. This detail shows that belonging in the worshipping assembly involved allegiance to the Lord, not a simple claim that no outsider could ever approach. They kept Unleavened Bread for seven days with joy. God had turned the imperial ruler's heart towards them and strengthened the building of His house. The work begun amid tears and then delayed now supported gathered worship again. Completion became a reason to remember the Lord's help and continue the service for which the house had been rebuilt."], "lesson": "God can provide through decisions and records beyond our control. Receive completed work with gratitude and continue the obedience it was meant to support.", "question": "How can I make thanksgiving for a completed task lead into faithful use of what has been provided?"},
  {"id": 95006, "title": "Ezra Sets His Heart to Study, Practise and Teach", "subtitle": "Learning God's law prepares a leader for service", "text": "Ezra 7:1–28", "chapters": [[14, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["After the temple's completion, the narrative moves to Artaxerxes's reign and introduces Ezra. His priestly line is traced through ancestors including Hilkiah, Zadok, Phinehas and Aaron. He was a skilled scribe in Moses' law, which God had given Israel. The king granted his request because the Lord's hand was upon him. Other Israelites, priests, Levites, singers, gatekeepers and temple servants went with him towards Jerusalem. The journey began in the first month and reached its destination in the fifth. The account explains Ezra's readiness through a settled purpose: he had prepared his heart to seek God's law, practise it and teach its commands in Israel.", "Artaxerxes gave him a letter permitting willing Israelites and their ministers to join the journey. Ezra was commissioned to enquire into Judah and Jerusalem according to God's law. He carried gold and silver freely offered by the king, counsellors and people for the temple service. Animals, grain and drink offerings were to be purchased, and remaining resources used according to God's will. Vessels were to be delivered to the house, with further needs supplied from the treasury. The letter gave Ezra substantial opportunity, but his competence could not be reduced to possessing royal authority. He carried a law he had determined to obey before he required others to learn it.", "Provincial treasurers were instructed to supply specified amounts of silver, wheat, wine and oil, with salt as needed. Temple servants received exemption from certain taxes. Ezra was also authorised to appoint magistrates and judges for those governed by God's law and to teach those who did not know it. The decree included civil penalties for disobedience to divine and royal law. These powers belonged to the Persian administration described in the account. They show the breadth of Ezra's commission, extending beyond the delivery of donations to instruction and judgment within the restored community. Such authority made personal wisdom and faithfulness more necessary, not less.", "Ezra answered the opportunity with praise. He blessed the God of his fathers for placing the desire to honour the Jerusalem temple in the king's heart and for granting him mercy before the court. He was strengthened because God's hand was upon him and gathered leading Israelites to accompany him. His thanksgiving did not pretend that the king and counsellors had played no part. It recognised God's kindness through their decisions. The chapter joins ancestry, study, obedience, teaching and gratitude in one servant's preparation. Ezra's heart had been directed towards the word before his influence expanded. The restored house now needed a people learning to live before the God whose name it bore."], "lesson": "Study and teaching should be joined to personal obedience. Opportunities for influence call for gratitude and a heart already willing to submit to God's word.", "question": "Am I practising what I am preparing to teach or ask of someone else?"},
  {"id": 95007, "title": "Ezra Fasts for the Journey and Weighs the Treasures", "subtitle": "Dependence on God goes with careful stewardship", "text": "Ezra 8:1–36", "chapters": [[14, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["Ezra records the families and leaders who travelled with him from Babylon. Descendants connected with priests and David's house appear alongside other households and their numbers. At the river near Ahava, the company camped for three days. Ezra inspected the people and priests and found no Levites among them. He sent leaders and men of understanding to Iddo at Casiphia with a request for servants for God's house. Sherebiah and other Levites came, together with two hundred twenty temple servants. Ezra attributed their arrival to God's good hand. Careful preparation had exposed a real absence, and prayerful dependence included taking steps to supply the missing people.", "At Ahava he proclaimed a fast so that the travellers could humble themselves and seek a safe journey for their children, possessions and company. Ezra had told the king that God's good hand rested on those who sought Him, and he felt ashamed to request a military escort against enemies along the road. The account describes his particular decision and its reason, rather than giving every later traveller a command to refuse protection. The company fasted and prayed, and God heard them. Their trust included concern for little ones who depended on decisions others made. Faith was expressed through openly acknowledging their need before setting out.", "Ezra then entrusted the precious metals and vessels to appointed priests and Levites. Silver, gold and costly bronze were weighed into their hands. He reminded them that both they and the vessels were set apart to the Lord and that the wealth had been freely offered to Israel's God. They were to guard it until it could be weighed again before the responsible leaders in Jerusalem. Trust did not remove accountability. The gifts came from the king, counsellors, officials and Israelites, and their intended purpose required protection through the journey. Naming custodians and recording weights served that purpose without contradicting the prayer for God's care.", "They left Ahava on the twelfth day of the first month. The Lord delivered them from enemies and ambush along the way, and they reached Jerusalem. After three days, the treasures were weighed into the hands of temple officials, with their numbers and weights written down. The travellers offered sacrifices for all Israel and presented the royal commissions to the provincial authorities, who supported the people and God's house. The journey ends with worship and completed accountability. The company did not merely arrive safely with an inspiring story. What had been entrusted was handed over properly. Ezra's account holds spiritual dependence and practical care within one faithful undertaking, giving neither permission to neglect the other."], "lesson": "Prayer and careful stewardship belong together. Protect what others entrust to you and make its handling clear and accountable.", "question": "What needs both earnest prayer and a more careful practical arrangement in my present responsibilities?"},
  {"id": 95008, "title": "Ezra Hears of Unfaithfulness and Kneels in Confession", "subtitle": "Restored buildings cannot excuse a returning pattern of sin", "text": "Ezra 9:1–15", "chapters": [[14, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["After the journey and delivery of gifts, leaders brought Ezra disturbing news. Israelites, including priests and Levites, had entered marriages joining them to the surrounding peoples and their detestable practices. Leaders had taken a prominent part. The report concerns the restored covenant community's disobedience and the danger of renewed idolatry. Ezra's response was grief, not satisfaction that he could expose other people's failure. He tore his garments, pulled hair from his head and beard and sat appalled. Those who trembled at God's words gathered around him. Until the evening sacrifice he remained overwhelmed, recognising how deeply the report challenged the meaning of the restoration.", "At the evening offering Ezra rose, knelt in his torn garments and spread his hands before God. He confessed shame at the people's guilt, which had risen beyond them. His prayer repeatedly used the language of shared responsibility. From their ancestors' days, sin had brought rulers, priests and people under foreign powers, leading to sword, captivity, plunder and humiliation. Ezra did not describe the exile as an unfortunate event without a moral history. He remembered the very lesson the community was in danger of forgetting. His grief joined the present report to the long record of unfaithfulness that had already cost Israel so much.", "He then recalled the mercy they had received. For a little time God had granted grace, leaving a remnant and giving them a firm place in His holy dwelling. Though still under foreign rule, they had not been abandoned. Persian kings had shown favour, allowing the temple to rise and providing renewed life and protection in Judah and Jerusalem. Ezra's words about a wall belong within this language of restored security and should not be made into a claim that every city defence had already been rebuilt. The central confession is clear: God's generosity had given them room to live again, and they were answering that mercy with renewed disobedience.", "Ezra asked what they could say after abandoning commands given through God's servants. He recalled the warning against alliances with peoples whose practices had defiled the land and endangered Israel's faithful life across generations. God had punished them less than their guilt deserved and allowed this remnant to escape. To repeat the old course after such deliverance threatened the very community mercy had preserved. The prayer ends with God's righteousness and the people's guilt still before Him. There is no excuse, demand or confident claim that their restored temple guarantees safety. Ezra's grief prepares the next chapter's difficult response by making repentance a matter of returning to God rather than simply managing an embarrassing public report."], "lesson": "Mercy should make us more serious about obedience. Honest confession acknowledges shared responsibility and refuses to use past deliverance as permission to repeat sin.", "question": "What kindness from God should lead me to confess and change a pattern I am tempted to excuse?"},
  {"id": 95009, "title": "The Assembly Faces the Marriages and Their Consequences", "subtitle": "Repentance brings a painful investigation", "text": "Ezra 10:1–44", "chapters": [[14, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Ezra", "story": ["As Ezra wept and confessed before God's house, a great assembly of men, women and children gathered and wept with him. Shechaniah acknowledged their unfaithfulness and said that hope still remained. He proposed a covenant to put away the foreign wives and children according to the counsel of Ezra and those who feared God's command. He urged Ezra to act and promised support. The account records a grave proposal affecting whole households. Its seriousness must not disappear behind a general lesson about making a fresh start. The wrongdoing had already involved families, and the chosen response would involve wives and children whose lives were bound to those decisions.", "Ezra required the leaders, priests, Levites and people to swear to carry out the undertaking. He then withdrew and continued mourning without food or drink. A proclamation required the returned community to assemble in Jerusalem within three days, under threat of forfeiture and exclusion for failure to appear. On the twentieth day of the ninth month, the men of Judah and Benjamin gathered in the temple square. They trembled because of the matter and the heavy rain. Ezra called them to confess and separate from the relationships identified as unfaithful. The assembly agreed, while recognising that the number of cases and the weather made an immediate settlement impossible.", "They proposed appointed hearings, with local elders and judges accompanying those involved. Ezra and designated family heads began examining the matter on the first day of the tenth month and completed the investigation by the first day of the first month. The record then lists priests, Levites, a singer, gatekeepers and other Israelites implicated. Certain priests pledged to dismiss their wives and offered a ram for guilt. Naming the men placed responsibility on actual participants rather than leaving the problem as an accusation against unnamed outsiders. The closing statement notes that some marriages included children. The book ends without supplying detailed accounts of the families' later lives or resolving every question a modern reader may ask.", "This difficult ending belongs to Israel's covenant crisis after exile. It should be read with the rest of Scripture, including Paul's instruction in 1 Corinthians 7 that a believer should not dismiss an unbelieving spouse willing to remain. Ezra's account cannot therefore be lifted into a general command to dissolve marriages across ethnic or national lines. Its continuing spiritual challenge concerns covenant faithfulness, serious confession and the consequences wrongdoing brings into relationships. Hope had been named at the beginning, but hope did not make the process painless. The restored community had to face the fact that rebuilding God's house and transporting sacred gifts could not replace obedience in the intimate decisions shaping its future."], "lesson": "Take disobedience and its effects on other people seriously. Read difficult historical responses within the whole counsel of Scripture and seek a repentance that is truthful and responsible.", "question": "Where have my choices affected other people, and what responsible response is now required of me?"},
  {"id": 95500, "title": "News from Jerusalem Brings Nehemiah to Prayer", "subtitle": "A burden becomes confession and a request for help", "text": "Nehemiah 1:1–11", "chapters": [[15, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["Nehemiah was serving in the palace at Susa when Hanani and other men arrived from Judah. He asked about the people who had survived the exile and about Jerusalem. Their answer brought the city's need close to a man living far away. The remaining community faced distress and disgrace, the wall was broken and the gates had been burned. The report described more than damaged stonework. An inhabited city lacked the protection and dignity its people needed. Nehemiah sat down and wept. For several days he mourned, fasted and prayed before the God of heaven. He did not allow distance or his own place at court to make the suffering someone else's concern.", "His prayer began with God's greatness, covenant and mercy. He asked the Lord to attend to the prayers he offered day and night for Israel. Then he confessed the people's sins, including those of himself and his father's household. He did not put himself outside the history that had brought them to this condition. They had acted corruptly and failed to keep the commands given through Moses. The man who would later confront opponents and organise builders first stood among the guilty. His grief did not become an accusation that God had neglected an innocent people. He acknowledged their disobedience before asking for restoration.", "Nehemiah appealed to the word God had given concerning scattering and return. Unfaithfulness would disperse the people among nations, but turning back and obeying would be met by God's gathering mercy, even from distant places. He remembered that these were the people the Lord had redeemed by His great power. The prayer joined confession to a promise rather than leaving the community's future dependent on its present strength. Broken gates were real, but they were not the only reality. God's word provided a reason to pray that something could change, and Nehemiah brought that word honestly into the circumstances before him.", "He asked God to hear all His servants who delighted to fear His name and to grant him success and mercy before the man he would approach. Only at the close does the chapter identify Nehemiah's position: he was the king's cupbearer. His daily service gave him proximity to royal power, but he knew that proximity did not guarantee a favourable answer. The chapter ends before his request is spoken at court. What has changed already is his own response. A report has become a burden, the burden has become prayer, and prayer is preparing him to act. He will need both God's mercy and a willingness to place himself within the work he hopes to see accomplished."], "lesson": "Let concern lead to confession, prayer and readiness to serve. God's promises encourage action grounded in dependence rather than self-confidence.", "question": "What need have I heard about that should move me beyond sympathy into prayer and responsible action?"},
  {"id": 95501, "title": "Nehemiah Receives Permission and Inspects the Ruins", "subtitle": "Prayer is joined to a clear request and careful preparation", "text": "Nehemiah 2:1–20", "chapters": [[15, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["In the month of Nisan, Nehemiah served wine before Artaxerxes. The king noticed his sadness and asked why he looked troubled when he was not ill. Nehemiah was deeply afraid. He explained that the city of his ancestors' graves lay ruined and its gates had been consumed by fire. When the king asked what he wanted, Nehemiah prayed to the God of heaven and then made his request. He asked to be sent to Judah to rebuild the city. The moment of opportunity met a burden already carried in prayer. Fear remained, but it did not prevent a clear answer about the work he desired to undertake.", "With the queen beside him, the king asked how long the journey would take and when Nehemiah would return. Nehemiah named a time and requested letters for safe passage through the provinces. He also asked for timber from the royal forest for gates, the wall and his residence. Artaxerxes granted the requests, and Nehemiah attributed the favour to God's good hand. Military officers and horsemen accompanied him. When he delivered the letters, Sanballat and Tobiah were distressed that someone had come seeking Israel's welfare. Royal approval had opened the way, but it had not removed everyone who opposed the city's recovery.", "After three days in Jerusalem, Nehemiah went out by night with a few men. He had not yet told the leaders what God had placed in his heart. Riding past the Valley Gate, the Dragon Well and the refuse gate, he examined broken walls and burned entrances. At the fountain gate and the king's pool, the rubble blocked his animal's passage. He continued along the valley, inspected the wall and returned. The quiet examination gave substance to his concern. He did not invite people into a plan based only on a distant report. He looked at the actual condition they would face and kept his early movements limited until he was ready to speak.", "Then he called the people to consider their shared distress and rebuild so that Jerusalem would no longer remain a disgrace. He told them about God's favour and the king's words. They answered that they would rise and build and strengthened themselves for the work. Sanballat, Tobiah and Geshem mocked them and accused them of rebellion. Nehemiah answered that the God of heaven would grant success and that His servants would build. The chapter joins brief prayer, detailed preparation, personal inspection and public encouragement. Confidence in God did not eliminate practical questions. It helped Nehemiah answer them and call others into a responsibility he had first been willing to examine himself."], "lesson": "Pray when opportunity comes, prepare clear requests and understand a task before inviting others to share it. Opposition need not cancel a faithful beginning.", "question": "What practical preparation should accompany my prayer about a responsibility before me?"},
  {"id": 95502, "title": "Families and Tradesmen Build Beside One Another", "subtitle": "A city's wall is repaired through shared responsibility", "text": "Nehemiah 3:1–32", "chapters": [[15, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["Eliashib the high priest and his fellow priests rose to rebuild the Sheep Gate. They set its doors and consecrated their work towards the nearby towers. Men from Jericho and other workers repaired adjoining sections. The chapter then follows the wall through gates, turns, towers and houses, repeatedly placing one group beside another. No single worker could restore the whole boundary. The strength of the undertaking depended on connected sections, each receiving the attention of people willing to carry its particular burden. Priests, townspeople and household heads appear as fellow labourers rather than leaving the work entirely to one profession or one celebrated leader.", "The Fish Gate received beams, doors, bolts and bars. The Old Gate, Valley Gate, refuse gate and Fountain Gate each had named workers. Goldsmiths and makers of perfumes joined the repairs, and rulers took their places among them. Shallum worked with his daughters. Some repaired directly opposite their own houses, while others undertook another section after an earlier assignment. The record preserves both the places and the people because the work involved real commitments. A gate required more than a general wish for Jerusalem's safety. Someone had to provide the labour that would make an opening secure and connect it to the walls on either side.", "The Tekoites repaired a section, but their nobles refused to put their shoulders to the work. Their failure did not prevent the townspeople from serving, and the Tekoites later appear repairing another portion. Baruch is remembered for working earnestly. These small notices reveal different responses within the same undertaking. Rank did not ensure willingness, and an ordinary worker's zeal was worth recording. The account does not tell the private motives of those who refused, but it does preserve the refusal. A shared project can expose both faithful effort and the reluctance of people who possess the standing to help but choose to remain apart.", "The list continues past the areas near David's tombs, the king's garden, the armoury and the temple servants' dwellings. Priests repaired near their houses, a gatekeeper worked by his assigned area, and merchants and goldsmiths completed the circuit towards the Sheep Gate. The chapter closes where it began, giving the reader a sense of joined labour around the city. Nehemiah's leadership was important, yet the wall rose through many named contributions. Their differences of trade, family and position did not make them independent of one another. The record honours a willingness to take a definite place and finish the task there. The repeated words about the next workers become a testimony to service carried out in cooperation."], "lesson": "Shared work depends on people accepting specific duties and valuing the labour beside them. Position matters less than a willing and faithful response.", "question": "What definite part of our shared work should I take instead of waiting for someone else to carry it?"},
  {"id": 95503, "title": "The Builders Pray, Keep Watch and Continue Working", "subtitle": "Courage is sustained through shared vigilance", "text": "Nehemiah 4:1–23", "chapters": [[15, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["Sanballat grew furious when he heard that the wall was being rebuilt. Before his associates and the Samaritan force, he mocked the Jews as weak people trying to restore burned stones from rubbish. Tobiah added that even a fox would break their wall. Nehemiah brought the contempt before God in a severe appeal for judgment against those who opposed the builders. He entrusted their reproach to the Lord rather than stopping the work to argue over every insult. The wall continued rising and reached half its height because the people were determined to labour. Mockery had not removed the need or made faithful hands useless.", "As the gaps began closing, opposition became a conspiracy. Sanballat, Tobiah and neighbouring groups planned an attack to create confusion and halt progress. The workers prayed and posted guards day and night. Meanwhile Judah's burden bearers were becoming exhausted, and the quantity of rubble made the task seem impossible. Reports of danger arrived repeatedly from Jews living near the opponents. The pressure now included physical weariness, unfinished work and fear for families. Nehemiah did not dismiss those realities as a lack of faith. He positioned people in exposed places, grouped by households, with swords, spears and bows.", "He urged the leaders and people to remember the great and awesome Lord and be ready to defend their relatives, children, wives and homes. When the enemies knew their plan had been discovered, the attempt was frustrated and the people returned to their work. New arrangements remained in place. Half of Nehemiah's servants worked while the others held weapons and armour. Burden carriers were prepared to defend themselves, and builders wore swords. Nehemiah kept a trumpeter beside him because the work spread across a wide area. At the trumpet's signal, people would gather where support was needed. Their confidence that God would fight for them was joined to a practical means of helping one another.", "From dawn until the stars appeared, labour and guard duty continued. Workers and their servants were told to remain within Jerusalem at night, sharing the watch rather than dispersing beyond its protection. Nehemiah and those around him stayed ready through the demanding period. The chapter offers no picture of faith as carefree ease. It records prayer, difficult judgment, tired bodies and changed arrangements. What prevented fear from governing the whole enterprise was remembering God while responding wisely to the danger. Each person's task remained connected with the safety of others. Building the wall and guarding the people belonged to the same responsibility, and perseverance required both the courage to continue and the humility to accept help."], "lesson": "Prayer and practical vigilance belong together. Take weariness and danger seriously while remembering God and supporting those who work beside you.", "question": "What change in our arrangements could help people continue safely and faithfully under pressure?"},
  {"id": 95504, "title": "Nehemiah Confronts the Debt Crushing His Own People", "subtitle": "Rebuilding must include justice within the community", "text": "Nehemiah 5:1–19", "chapters": [[15, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["A great outcry rose from the people and their wives against fellow Jews. Some needed grain to feed their children. Others had mortgaged fields, vineyards and houses during scarcity, while still others borrowed to pay the king's tax. Their sons and daughters were entering bondage, and they lacked the means to redeem them because others held their land. The wall's progress could not hide this distress. These were members of the same community, with children no less human than those of their wealthy neighbours. External enemies were not the only danger to Jerusalem's restored life. Exploitation within it was destroying households.", "Nehemiah became angry when he heard, but first considered the matter before rebuking the nobles and officials. They were exacting interest from their own people. He called a public assembly and challenged the contradiction. Jews had been redeemed from foreign slavery where possible, yet fellow Jews were now reducing them to bondage again. The leaders had no answer. Nehemiah appealed to the fear of God and the disgrace their conduct brought before surrounding enemies. He and his own associates were also lending money and grain, and he called for the exaction to stop. The correction reached into the conduct of those sharing his work, not only people safely outside his circle.", "He required the immediate return of fields, vineyards, olive groves and houses, together with the portion being charged on money and produce. The leaders promised restoration and agreed to demand no more. Priests witnessed their oath. Nehemiah shook out the fold of his garment as a sign of judgment upon anyone who refused to keep the promise. The assembly answered with agreement and praised God, and the people acted accordingly. Repentance was therefore measured through restored property and changed treatment of vulnerable neighbours. A solemn promise mattered because it was carried into the economic relationships that had caused the cry in the first place.", "Nehemiah then described his own conduct during twelve years as governor. He had not taken the usual food allowance, unlike earlier governors who burdened the people and whose servants dominated them. Reverence for God restrained him. He continued working on the wall, acquired no land through the situation and supplied a large table for officials and visitors without demanding the governor's provision from the people. Their burden was already heavy. He ended by asking God to remember his service for their good. The chapter places public reform beside personal restraint. A leader's words about justice gain substance when his own use of privilege helps relieve the people rather than adding another weight to their lives."], "lesson": "Faithful leadership confronts exploitation and accepts limits on personal advantage. Genuine correction includes restitution where others have suffered loss.", "question": "Does any benefit I receive depend on a burden I should help lift from someone else?"},
  {"id": 95505, "title": "Nehemiah Refuses the Trap and the Wall Is Finished", "subtitle": "Fear and false reports fail to stop the work", "text": "Nehemiah 6:1–19", "chapters": [[15, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["The wall had no remaining gaps, though its gate doors were not yet installed. Sanballat and Geshem invited Nehemiah to meet them in the plain of Ono. He understood that they intended harm and sent word that he was doing an important work and could not leave it. Four invitations received the same answer. Opposition now approached through an apparently reasonable meeting rather than open mockery. Nehemiah's refusal concerned this particular threat and duty. He did not need to abandon unfinished responsibilities simply because opponents repeatedly demanded his presence. Steadiness protected the people whose work depended on his remaining attentive.", "A fifth message arrived as an open letter. It claimed that surrounding nations were reporting a planned Jewish rebellion, with Nehemiah intending to become king and prophets announcing his rule. Sanballat urged consultation before the report reached Artaxerxes. Nehemiah denied the accusation directly, saying it had been invented. He recognised its purpose as frightening the workers so that their hands would weaken and the work remain incomplete. His response included a brief prayer for strengthened hands. He did not attempt to answer a fabricated charge by surrendering his duty to the people spreading it. The threat to his reputation became another reason to depend on God.", "Shemaiah then urged him to hide within the temple because assassins would come at night. Nehemiah refused to flee in that manner or enter where he had no right to seek safety. He discerned that the message did not come from God. Tobiah and Sanballat had paid Shemaiah to provoke fear, lead him into sin and supply material for disgrace. Nehemiah appealed to God concerning them, the prophetess Noadiah and the other prophets trying to frighten him. Religious language did not make the proposed action obedient. His discernment required examining what he was being urged to do, even while the warning claimed concern for his life.", "The wall was completed on the twenty-fifth of Elul, after fifty-two days. Their enemies lost confidence, recognising that the work had been accomplished with God's help. Yet completion did not end every pressure. Judah's nobles exchanged letters with Tobiah, whose marriage ties created loyal supporters. They praised him before Nehemiah, passed Nehemiah's words to him and received letters intended to intimidate. The chapter therefore ends with a finished wall and unresolved relationships. God's help had made real progress possible without making every influential person trustworthy. Nehemiah's example joins perseverance to discernment, prayer and refusal to sin for the sake of appearing safe. The work reached its goal because fear was repeatedly denied the authority to set its course."], "lesson": "Test proposed action by God's commands, even when advice sounds protective or religious. Stand steadily against pressure that would lead you from a clear duty into wrongdoing.", "question": "What fear or accusation is tempting me to abandon a responsibility or excuse a wrong action?"},
  {"id": 95506, "title": "The Gates Are Guarded and the Returning Families Remembered", "subtitle": "A completed wall needs a living community", "text": "Nehemiah 7:1–73", "chapters": [[15, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 73, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["With the wall built and the doors installed, Nehemiah appointed gatekeepers, singers and Levites. He entrusted Jerusalem to Hanani and Hananiah, the fortress commander, commending faithfulness and reverence for God. Instructions regulated when the gates should open and how they should be secured. Residents were to take their turns on watch, including positions near their own houses. The physical achievement now required dependable care. A gate left without trustworthy oversight could compromise what many hands had built. Completing a project did not remove responsibility. It changed the kind of service the community needed to provide.", "Jerusalem was spacious, but few people lived within it and houses were not yet rebuilt. God placed in Nehemiah's heart the gathering of nobles, officials and people for registration by family. He found the record of those who had first returned from exile. The account reaches back to the company led by Zerubbabel and Jeshua, preserving the families and towns whose people had come again from Babylon. Nehemiah's work belonged within that earlier restoration. The wall was not an isolated achievement beginning a new people's history. It protected a community whose relationships and responsibilities had already been carried through displacement and return.", "The register names households associated with Parosh, Shephatiah and many others, together with people from Bethlehem, Anathoth, Jericho and further settlements. Priests, Levites, Asaph's singers, gatekeepers, temple servants and descendants of Solomon's servants receive their place. Some people could not establish their ancestry through the available records. Men claiming priestly descent without the necessary registration were excluded from the most holy service until an authorised decision could resolve it. The list remembers both welcome into a returning community and care about particular sacred duties. It does not supply biographies for every name, but it gives the workers and households a continuing place in the people's memory.", "The total congregation is recorded as forty-two thousand three hundred sixty, besides servants and additional singers, with transport animals also counted. Gifts of gold, silver, bowls and priestly garments came from the governor, family heads and people. They settled in their towns, and the seventh month found Israel in its places. The chapter has moved from newly guarded entrances to an older record of shared identity and provision. That movement helps the reader understand why the wall mattered. Jerusalem needed families, worshippers, servants and livelihoods within the protection of its stones. A secure boundary was part of restoration, while a faithful community was the life meant to grow behind it. Remembering names prepared the people to consider how that life should now be ordered."], "lesson": "Preserving good work requires trustworthy people and care for the community it serves. Remember earlier contributions instead of treating the present achievement as the whole story.", "question": "What continuing responsibility follows the completion of a task I have been working towards?"},
  {"id": 95507, "title": "Ezra Reads and the People Learn to Rejoice", "subtitle": "Understanding God's word leads to worship and obedience", "text": "Nehemiah 8:1–18", "chapters": [[15, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["On the first day of the seventh month, the people gathered in the square before the Water Gate and asked Ezra to bring Moses' law. Men, women and all who could understand listened from morning until midday. Ezra stood on a wooden platform prepared for the occasion, with other leaders beside him. When he opened the book, the assembly stood. He blessed the great God, and the people answered with lifted hands before bowing their faces to the ground. The restored city was now gathering around more than its new defences. Its people wanted to hear the word that should govern the life being rebuilt within them.", "Levites helped the people understand the reading. The text was read clearly and its sense explained. Attention to understanding mattered because the law was meant to direct conduct, not merely supply a solemn sound. As the people heard, they began to weep. Nehemiah, Ezra and the teaching Levites told them that the day was holy and should not be spent in mourning. They were to eat good food, drink what was sweet and send portions to those for whom nothing had been prepared. The Lord's joy would be their strength. Their response to God's word needed room for received mercy and shared gladness as well as sorrow over failure.", "The Levites quieted the assembly and repeated the instruction not to grieve. The people went to eat, drink, share food and rejoice because they had understood the words explained to them. Understanding had changed what they should do that day. On the following day, family heads, priests and Levites gathered around Ezra for further study. They found the command concerning the seventh-month festival and dwelling in temporary shelters. The discovery led to a proclamation through Jerusalem and the towns. People were to gather branches from the hills and make the shelters required for the feast. The reading continued to produce definite actions rather than ending with the emotion of the first assembly.", "Shelters appeared on roofs, in courtyards, in the temple courts and in the squares near the Water Gate and Ephraim Gate. The returned community kept the feast with extraordinary gladness, described as a celebration unlike any since Joshua's days in this manner. Each day, from the first to the last, the law was read. Seven festival days were followed by the prescribed solemn assembly. The chapter joins reverence, explanation, grief, generosity and joy within a single response to God. The people did not have to choose between taking His word seriously and receiving a reason to celebrate. As they understood, they were learning both how to obey and how to share the goodness of the holy day with neighbours who otherwise might have been left without provision."], "lesson": "Seek understanding of Scripture that leads to a faithful response. God's word can call us to repentance and also teach us to receive and share His joy.", "question": "What does a passage I have recently understood ask me to do, rather than merely feel?"},
  {"id": 95508, "title": "The People Confess Their History Before a Merciful God", "subtitle": "Remembered grace exposes repeated rebellion", "text": "Nehemiah 9:1–38", "chapters": [[15, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["On the twenty-fourth day of the month, Israel gathered in fasting, sackcloth and dust. They separated themselves for the assembly and confessed their own sins and those of their ancestors. Part of the day was given to reading God's law and another part to confession and worship. Levites called the people to stand and bless the Lord, whose glorious name surpassed every blessing they could offer. Their prayer began with the Creator who made and preserves heaven, earth and sea. It then remembered His choice of Abram, the covenant concerning the land and the righteousness shown in keeping that promise. Confession stood within a history of God's faithful initiative.", "The prayer followed the ancestors into Egypt, where God saw oppression and heard their cry. He judged Pharaoh's pride, divided the sea, defeated pursuers and led His people by cloud and fire. At Sinai He gave good commands through Moses. In the wilderness He supplied bread and water and promised the land. Yet the people became proud, refused to remember and wanted to return to bondage. Even the golden calf did not exhaust God's mercy. He continued guiding them, gave His good Spirit to instruct, withheld neither food nor water and sustained them forty years. The prayer placed persistent provision beside repeated provocation, so that no one could honestly explain Israel's survival through its own consistent obedience.", "God gave kingdoms, multiplied their descendants and brought them into a fertile land. They received strong towns, full houses, wells, vineyards and fruit trees and enjoyed abundant goodness. Again they rebelled, rejected the law and killed prophets sent to turn them back. Enemies gained power over them, and in distress they cried to God. He repeatedly delivered them, yet rest was followed by further evil. His patience continued through years of warning by the prophets until foreign nations overcame them. Still, He had not consumed or utterly abandoned them. Their continuing existence bore witness to the gracious and merciful God they had so often refused.", "The prayer finally brought that history into the assembly's own circumstances. They asked God not to regard as small the trouble suffered by kings, leaders, priests, prophets and people since Assyria's days. They acknowledged His justice and their wrongdoing. Even now, in the good land given to their ancestors, they lived as servants under rulers who claimed its produce and controlled their bodies and livestock. Restoration had not removed every consequence of the past. In this distress they made a firm written covenant, to be sealed by leaders, priests and Levites. The long confession therefore ended in a present commitment. Remembering mercy honestly had exposed the need for a different response from the people now standing before Him."], "lesson": "A truthful account of the past recognises God's mercy and our responsibility. Let remembered grace move you towards definite obedience now.", "question": "What repeated pattern does God's patience call me to confess and change?"},
  {"id": 95509, "title": "The Covenant Reaches Marriages, Markets and Offerings", "subtitle": "A written promise receives practical form", "text": "Nehemiah 10:1–39", "chapters": [[15, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["Nehemiah's name appears among those sealing the covenant, followed by priests, Levites and community leaders. The list records particular people accepting responsibility rather than leaving the agreement as an unnamed wish. The wider assembly joined them: men and women, sons and daughters able to understand, gatekeepers, singers, temple servants and others who had separated themselves to God's law. They bound themselves by a solemn oath to walk in the commands given through Moses. The words followed public hearing and confession. Their seriousness lay in carrying those responses into the ordinary relationships and duties through which faithfulness would be tested after the assembly dispersed.", "They promised to avoid marriages joining their households to the surrounding peoples in violation of their covenant obligations. They also committed themselves not to buy goods or food brought for sale on Sabbaths and holy days. Economic opportunity would not be allowed to erase the appointed rest. The seventh year's release and the remission of debts were included. These promises concerned the life of this restored covenant community under Moses' law. The reader should understand that setting while recognising the underlying challenge: devotion to God reaches decisions that affect family, profit, rest and the treatment of people who owe us something. A promise to obey becomes meaningful where convenience offers reasons to avoid it.", "The people undertook an annual contribution for God's house. The sacred bread, regular grain and burnt offerings, festivals, sin offerings and other necessary work required continuing support. Lots assigned households their times for bringing wood to the altar. Firstfruits of crops and trees, offerings concerning firstborn children and animals according to the law, and gifts of dough, wine and oil were also included. The record does not picture worship continuing through goodwill without supplies. Their dedication needed a practical arrangement for providing what the service required. Different resources and responsibilities would come from households throughout the land, joining daily life to the sanctuary.", "Tithes were to reach the Levites in the farming towns, with a priest present when they were received. The Levites in turn would bring their portion into the temple storerooms. Grain, wine and oil supported those serving near the sanctuary vessels, including priests, singers and gatekeepers. The chapter ends with the promise not to neglect God's house. That sentence gathers the detailed commitments into a shared purpose. The later narrative will show how easily solemn promises can be broken, but here the people have stated clearly what faithfulness should require. Their agreement concerns more than maintaining an important building. It concerns an ordered life in which worship, resources and the care of those entrusted with service remain connected through responsibilities willingly acknowledged before God."], "lesson": "Promises to God need practical arrangements. Consider how time, money, relationships and shared responsibilities should reflect the obedience you profess.", "question": "Which commitment needs a clear arrangement if it is to remain more than good intentions?"},
  {"id": 95510, "title": "Households Take Their Places in Jerusalem and the Towns", "subtitle": "Restoration requires people willing to live within it", "text": "Nehemiah 11:1–36", "chapters": [[15, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["The leaders lived in Jerusalem, and lots were cast to bring one household in ten into the holy city while the others remained in their towns. People also blessed those who volunteered to settle there. The wall had made the city's outline secure, but households were needed to fill its spaces with life. Moving affected possessions, livelihood and relationships. The brief blessing recognises a willingness to accept those changes for the community's good. The text gives no private speeches from the volunteers or claims that every family faced the same difficulty. It records their offering of themselves as a contribution deserving the people's gratitude.", "Families from Judah and Benjamin are named among the residents. Descendants associated with Perez stand beside Benjaminite households, with overseers appointed for the city. Priests and their relatives carried out the work of God's house. Levites held responsibilities both within its worship and in its outward business. Mattaniah led thanksgiving in prayer, while others served alongside him. Gatekeepers watched the entrances, and temple servants lived in Ophel under their own leaders. These notices show the range of people needed in a functioning city. Jerusalem's security and sacred calling depended on more than the presence of a governor. Households and servants carried duties that returned every day.", "The record also notes the continuing provision for singers under the king's command. Their portion was assigned daily. Pethahiah handled matters concerning the people in relation to the king. Worship, maintenance and public administration therefore appear together in this account of settlement. Naming those involved gives the practical life behind the restoration a recognised place. A song heard in the sanctuary required someone to be available to sing, and availability needed provision. The people who gave, the workers who received and the officials who maintained order all belonged within the same shared responsibility. The chapter invites attention to that dependence without making every role identical.", "Many Israelites, priests and Levites remained on their own inheritances throughout Judah. Villages and surrounding fields are named from Hebron and Beersheba through places such as Ziklag, Adullam and Lachish. Benjaminite settlements included Geba, Michmash, Bethel, Anathoth, Ramah, Lod and Ono. Levite divisions also lived among Judah and Benjamin. The chapter therefore does not make Jerusalem the only place where faithful life could continue. The city and countryside needed one another. Those who moved helped strengthen the centre, while those remaining sustained farms, villages and other responsibilities. The remembered map is a picture of a people being established again, with different households receiving different places in a restoration larger than any one home."], "lesson": "A community is strengthened by people who accept the place and duty needed of them. Value both those who move to serve and those who faithfully remain.", "question": "Does serving the common good require a change I have been reluctant to consider?"},
  {"id": 95511, "title": "Two Companies Give Thanks Upon the Wall", "subtitle": "The sound of Jerusalem's joy carries far", "text": "Nehemiah 12:1–47", "chapters": [[15, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["The chapter first remembers priests and Levites who returned with Zerubbabel and Jeshua, then follows family heads through succeeding generations. Priestly lines and Levite responsibilities are recorded across the years associated with later leaders. Those who led thanksgiving and kept watch stood within an established service rather than beginning everything anew. The lists connect the coming celebration with earlier returnees and with David's arrangements for praise. They preserve continuity beside the fresh achievement. The wall belonged to the present workers, but worship drew upon responsibilities and songs handed down through generations whose names remained part of Israel's memory.", "For the dedication, Levites were gathered from their settlements to Jerusalem. Singers came from villages around the city and from further districts, bringing cymbals, harps and other instruments. Priests and Levites purified themselves, the people, the gates and the wall. Nehemiah then brought Judah's leaders onto the wall and arranged two large companies giving thanks. One travelled in one direction, with Ezra before it, while the other proceeded the opposite way with Nehemiah following. The routes passed gates, towers, stairs and familiar places named in the building account. Spaces once inspected as ruins now carried people praising God for what had been accomplished.", "The two companies stood in God's house with leaders and priests sounding trumpets. Singers raised their voices under their overseer. Great sacrifices were offered, and the people rejoiced because God had given them great joy. Women and children shared it, and Jerusalem's gladness could be heard far away. The account does not reduce the dedication to the success of a single man. The celebration gathered families, singers, priests, officials and workers before God. Their voices answered earlier scenes of mockery, alarm and weary labour. They could now stand upon the completed wall and recognise a mercy that had met them through demanding work and persistent opposition.", "At that time, people were appointed over storerooms for contributions, firstfruits and tithes. The portions required by the law were gathered for priests and Levites, whose service Judah valued. Singers and gatekeepers kept their responsibilities according to the arrangements associated with David and Solomon. Daily provision is remembered from Zerubbabel's days through Nehemiah's. The chapter therefore ends by returning from exceptional rejoicing to continuing support. A dedication could fill a city with song, but ordinary service needed resources afterward. Gratitude became durable when the people provided for those carrying its daily expression. The great sound heard afar off mattered, and so did the faithful portion delivered when the gathering was over and each worker resumed an appointed place."], "lesson": "Celebrate God's help together and support the continuing duties that follow. Lasting gratitude includes dependable provision as well as a joyful occasion.", "question": "How can thanksgiving for a shared achievement become continuing support for the people who maintain it?"},
  {"id": 95512, "title": "Nehemiah Returns to Find Promises Neglected", "subtitle": "A restored city still needs continuing faithfulness", "text": "Nehemiah 13:1–31", "chapters": [[15, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Nehemiah", "story": ["Reading Moses' law renewed attention to Israel's obligations concerning the assembly and the history of Ammon and Moab, including the hiring of Balaam. The people acted upon the reading. The narrative then explains what happened during Nehemiah's absence at the Persian court. Eliashib had given Tobiah a large temple room previously used for offerings, vessels and the portions belonging to the servants. When Nehemiah returned and learned of it, he threw out Tobiah's belongings and ordered the rooms cleansed and restored to their proper use. An opponent's influence had entered through a relationship within the leadership, and dedicated space had been diverted from the responsibility it was meant to serve.", "The Levites' portions had also been withheld. Singers and other workers had gone back to their fields to sustain themselves. Nehemiah challenged the officials for neglecting God's house, gathered the workers and restored the arrangement. Grain, wine and oil again reached the stores, and faithful men were appointed to distribute them. He asked God to remember his work for the house and its service. These failures directly contradicted the recent covenant. A promise made before the people had not maintained itself. Continuing oversight and willingness to correct influential wrongdoing remained necessary after the wall's completion and the dedication's joy.", "Nehemiah also found work and trading taking place on the Sabbath. Judah's produce and goods brought by Tyrian merchants entered Jerusalem. He rebuked the nobles, recalling the judgment their ancestors had faced, and ordered the gates shut before the Sabbath until it ended. Guards prevented loads entering. When merchants camped outside, he warned them to stop, and the Levites were charged with keeping the gates for the holy day. His prayer again appealed to God's mercy. The city's restored entrances now served the rest its people had promised to honour. Economic pressure was being answered through the use of the same gates that earlier chapters celebrated.", "Marriages with women of Ashdod, Ammon and Moab raised further concern about covenant loyalty and children losing the community's language. Nehemiah confronted the men harshly, including physical treatment the narrative records without making it a model for pastoral care. He recalled Solomon's fall and expelled a priestly relative allied by marriage with Sanballat. The account concerns this community's covenant crisis and must not become a general licence for hostility across ethnic lines. Nehemiah restored priestly and Levite duties, wood offerings and firstfruits and ended by asking God to remember him for good. The book closes with renewed correction rather than a claim that everything was permanently settled. Rebuilding had brought real hope, while faithfulness still needed to be practised when public celebration and close supervision were no longer enough."], "lesson": "Good beginnings need continued obedience, fair oversight and correction. Take seriously the commitments that can quietly fail once attention moves elsewhere.", "question": "Which responsibility have I allowed to weaken since a time when I promised to take it seriously?"},
  {"id": 96500, "title": "Vashti Refuses the King's Summons", "subtitle": "Royal power cannot turn every demand into wisdom", "text": "Esther 1:1–22", "chapters": [[16, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["Ahasuerus ruled a vast Persian kingdom of a hundred twenty-seven provinces, stretching from India to Ethiopia. In his third year he gathered officials, nobles and provincial leaders at Susa and displayed his wealth and royal splendour over many days. A further feast welcomed the people present in the citadel, great and small, into the palace garden. Fine hangings, marble pillars, costly couches and golden drinking vessels made the setting an exhibition of abundance. Wine was supplied generously, while the stated arrangement allowed each guest to drink according to preference. Within the royal house, Queen Vashti held her own feast for the women.", "On the seventh day, with his heart merry from wine, the king ordered seven eunuchs to bring Vashti wearing her royal crown. He wanted the assembled people and officials to see her beauty. Vashti refused to come. The account does not explain her private reasoning or describe details beyond the summons itself, so the reader should not invent a scene to make the refusal easier to judge. What it does show plainly is the king's burning anger. A celebration designed to display the completeness of his power had met a boundary he could not simply remove by making the same demand.", "He consulted the seven leading princes who knew law and judgment and had access to his presence. Memucan answered as though the queen's refusal threatened every household in the empire. Women would hear and despise their husbands, he argued, while noblewomen would imitate Vashti and spread contempt. He proposed an unalterable decree excluding her from the king's presence and giving her royal position to another woman. A personal humiliation was being expanded into a public emergency. The advice placed the protection of male prestige at the centre of imperial concern, with no recorded enquiry into why Vashti had refused or whether the summons itself had been wise.", "The proposal pleased the king and his advisers. Letters went throughout the provinces in their different scripts and languages, declaring that each man should rule his own household. The vast machinery of government now carried a response to an argument at a feast. The chapter opens with the display of royal greatness and ends by exposing the insecurity beneath it. An emperor could command messengers across a continent while lacking the restraint to receive a refusal without turning it into a law for everyone. Vashti disappears from the story, but her removal prepares the place Esther will later occupy. The beginning asks readers to notice the difference between possessing authority and using it with justice, understanding and self-control."], "lesson": "Power needs restraint and wise counsel. Anger and wounded pride can turn a private conflict into harm affecting many others.", "question": "When my authority or pride is challenged, do I seek understanding before demanding a stronger public response?"},
  {"id": 96501, "title": "Esther Receives the Crown and Mordecai's Warning Is Recorded", "subtitle": "An orphan's rise takes place within a powerful court", "text": "Esther 2:1–23", "chapters": [[16, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["After his anger subsided, Ahasuerus remembered Vashti and the decree against her. His attendants proposed gathering beautiful young women from the provinces so that he could choose another queen. Esther, also called Hadassah, was among those taken into the royal household. She was a Jewish orphan whom her cousin Mordecai had raised as his own daughter. Their family belonged to the history of exile from Jerusalem. The court's search placed her within a system she had not created, under officials controlling the women's preparation and access to the king. The story should not be reduced to a simple account of winning a voluntary contest.", "Esther gained the favour of Hegai, the keeper of the women, who supplied her preparation, food, attendants and a good place within the household. At Mordecai's instruction she did not disclose her Jewish identity. He walked near the women's court daily to learn how she was and what would happen to her. His care continued across the separation imposed by palace life. The women underwent twelve months of preparation before their turn with the king. Afterward they entered another household under the keeper of the concubines and could return only if summoned by name. The details reveal how much their future depended on decisions made beyond their control.", "When Esther's turn came, she asked only for what Hegai advised. She found favour with those who saw her and was brought to Ahasuerus in the tenth month of his seventh year. The king preferred her to the others, placed the crown upon her and made her queen. A feast, gifts and a release to the provinces marked the occasion. Esther still kept her ancestry private and continued respecting Mordecai's guidance. The crown changed her position, but it did not erase the family relationship through which she had first received protection. Nor did it make her Jewish identity cease to matter, though the court did not yet know it.", "While sitting at the king's gate, Mordecai learned that Bigthan and Teresh, two doorkeepers, planned to attack the king. He told Esther, and she reported the plot in his name. An investigation confirmed it, the men were executed and the matter entered the royal records. No reward to Mordecai is mentioned at this point. His faithful action was written down without immediately changing his circumstances. The chapter has placed both Esther and a remembered act of service within the court's life. Their importance will unfold later. For now, care for an orphan, a concealed identity and a truthful warning are parts of a story whose larger danger has not yet been revealed."], "lesson": "Faithful care and truthful service can matter before their results are visible. Honour those who have cared for you and act responsibly with what you learn.", "question": "What faithful action should I take even if recognition or reward does not follow immediately?"},
  {"id": 96502, "title": "Haman Turns One Refusal into a Decree Against a People", "subtitle": "Wounded pride joins prejudice and imperial power", "text": "Esther 3:1–15", "chapters": [[16, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["Ahasuerus promoted Haman above the other officials. At the king's command, those serving at the gate bowed before him, but Mordecai did not. Other servants questioned Mordecai repeatedly and eventually reported him to Haman, explaining that he was Jewish. The text connects his refusal with his declared identity without supplying a complete explanation of his reasoning. Haman saw the lack of reverence and became furious. Yet he considered an attack upon Mordecai alone beneath him. Learning the identity of Mordecai's people, he sought the destruction of Jews throughout the empire. An injury to one man's pride became a plan against families who had done him no personal wrong.", "In the first month of the king's twelfth year, lots called Pur were cast to determine the appointed time. The selected date fell in Adar, the twelfth month. Haman then spoke to the king about a people scattered throughout the provinces. He accused them of having different laws and failing to keep royal laws, presenting their continued existence as contrary to the king's interest. He did not openly describe his anger over Mordecai. Prejudice appeared in the language of public advantage, allowing a private hatred to seek the authority of government. A great payment of silver was offered in support of the proposed destruction.", "The king gave Haman his signet ring and authority concerning the people. Royal scribes were summoned, and letters were prepared for officials throughout the provinces in the scripts and languages of their inhabitants. Written in Ahasuerus's name and sealed with his ring, the decree ordered the killing of all Jews on the thirteenth of Adar. Young and old, women and children were included, and their possessions were offered as plunder. The scale and cruelty must remain clear in the retelling. This was a plan for the destruction of a people, with no distinction between the man Haman resented and an infant whose family happened to share his ancestry.", "Couriers hurried the command throughout the kingdom. At Susa, the decree was announced while the king and Haman sat drinking. The city was bewildered. The final contrast gives the chapter its terrible weight: those authorising the violence could enjoy a table while others faced the announcement of their deaths. Authority, administrative skill and multilingual communication had become instruments of injustice. The story leaves a date fixed in the future and a threatened community spread across a vast territory. Esther's crown and Mordecai's recorded service have not yet answered this danger. The reader must now follow what happens when those near the palace learn that privilege cannot be separated from the suffering the palace has authorised."], "lesson": "Resentment becomes especially dangerous when it can enlist prejudice and authority. Test accusations about whole communities instead of letting fear or personal grievance decide their worth.", "question": "Am I accepting an accusation against a group without examining its truth and the harm it could produce?"},
  {"id": 96503, "title": "Esther Chooses to Approach the King", "subtitle": "A protected position becomes a costly responsibility", "text": "Esther 4:1–17", "chapters": [[16, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["Mordecai learned of the decree, tore his clothes and put on sackcloth and ashes. He went through Susa crying bitterly and came as far as the king's gate, where sackcloth could not be worn within. Across the provinces, Jews mourned, fasted and wept as the order reached them. Esther first heard through her attendants about Mordecai's condition. She sent clothes, hoping he would remove the sackcloth, but he refused. The visible grief could not be settled by changing his appearance. She needed to understand the threat beneath it and sent Hathach to ask what had happened.", "Mordecai explained the plot and the money Haman had offered. He supplied a copy of the decree so that Esther could see its actual terms. His request was that she approach the king and plead for her people. Hathach carried the message back. Esther answered with the court's law: anyone entering the inner court without being summoned faced death unless the king extended his golden sceptre. She had not been called for thirty days. The crown therefore did not provide unrestricted access or make the request safe. She faced a real danger, not simply an uncomfortable conversation she had been unwilling to attempt.", "Mordecai warned her not to assume that the palace would preserve her when other Jews perished. If she remained silent, he expressed confidence that relief would arise elsewhere, while she and her father's household would not escape. He asked whether her place in the kingdom might have come for this very time. The question did not give her complete knowledge of the future. It called her to consider the responsibility attached to her position. Palace privilege could either become a means of service or encourage a silence that separated her imagined safety from her people's danger. She could not treat the decree as someone else's crisis.", "Esther told Mordecai to gather Susa's Jews to fast for her for three days, with neither food nor drink. She and her attendants would do the same. Then she would approach the king despite the law, accepting the possibility of death. The account records the fast without setting it out as a general pattern that others must reproduce to secure an answer. Its force lies in the community's shared dependence and Esther's decision to act. Mordecai followed her instructions. The chapter ends before the king's response, leaving courage defined through a step taken without a guarantee of personal safety. Esther's words commit her to the people whose grief had finally entered the protected spaces of her life."], "lesson": "Consider what responsibility accompanies your place and opportunity. Courage can involve acting for others while acknowledging genuine risk and seeking shared support.", "question": "Whose need requires me to use an opportunity or influence I have been tempted to preserve only for myself?"},
  {"id": 96504, "title": "Esther Invites Haman While He Plans Mordecai's Death", "subtitle": "Patience and pride move towards an unseen reversal", "text": "Esther 5:1–14", "chapters": [[16, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["On the third day, Esther put on her royal garments and stood in the inner court. Ahasuerus sat upon his throne opposite the entrance. When he saw her, she found favour, and he extended the golden sceptre. She approached and touched its tip. The danger named in her message had not been imaginary, and this act allowed her to live and speak. The king asked her request, using the generous language of offering as much as half the kingdom. Esther invited him and Haman to a banquet she had prepared. He ordered Haman summoned quickly, and both attended.", "At the banquet, Ahasuerus again asked what she wanted. Esther replied by inviting the two men to another banquet the following day, promising then to make her request. The text does not disclose every reason for her timing, and the retelling need not fill the silence with an invented revelation or conversation. What is clear is that she did not immediately accuse Haman before the first table was cleared. The invitation carried the story into another day, allowing events outside her banquet room to develop. Her approach combined a courageous entrance with a measured use of the opportunity the king had given.", "Haman left delighted. The honour of being the only guest invited with the king seemed to confirm his extraordinary standing. Then he saw Mordecai at the gate, neither rising nor trembling before him, and anger returned. At home he assembled his friends and his wife Zeresh. He listed his wealth, many sons, promotions and advancement above other officials. Esther's exclusive invitation was added to the catalogue, including the further banquet awaiting him. Yet he declared that all of it meant nothing while Mordecai remained at the gate. The scene exposes a heart unable to enjoy great privilege because one person would not supply the recognition it demanded.", "Zeresh and the friends advised building an execution structure fifty cubits high and asking the king in the morning to hang Mordecai upon it. After that, Haman could go happily to Esther's feast. The suggestion pleased him, and he had it made. His anger was being encouraged rather than corrected by those closest to him. The chapter closes with preparations for a public death standing beside preparations for a royal meal. Haman thinks both will serve his satisfaction, while Esther is seeking an opening to expose his plot. The reader sees how pride narrows judgment until even the removal of an innocent life appears a reasonable condition for enjoying an invitation. The next movement will show how little control Haman actually possesses over the honour he assumes is already his."], "lesson": "Pride can make abundance seem worthless when one desire remains unsatisfied. Seek counsel that restrains resentment rather than helping it become destructive action.", "question": "What unmet desire is making me overlook blessings or justify an unkind response towards someone else?"},
  {"id": 96505, "title": "Haman Must Proclaim Honour for Mordecai", "subtitle": "A sleepless night interrupts a murderous plan", "text": "Esther 6:1–14", "chapters": [[16, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["That night Ahasuerus could not sleep. He ordered the royal records brought and read before him. Among the entries was the account of Mordecai exposing Bigthana and Teresh's plot against his life. The king asked what honour had been given in return, and the servants answered that nothing had been done. An act recorded years earlier now came into view at precisely the moment when Mordecai's enemy wanted him killed. The chapter does not narrate a new public miracle or explain the cause of the king's sleeplessness. It lets the convergence of timing and remembered service carry the movement of the story.", "The king asked who was in the court. Haman had arrived to request Mordecai's death on the structure prepared at his house. Before he could present the request, he was brought in and asked what should be done for the man the king delighted to honour. Haman assumed that the description could concern no one more suitably than himself. He proposed royal garments, the king's horse and a procession led by a leading prince announcing the king's pleasure. His answer revealed the kind of recognition he desired. The question had not named its subject, but his imagination supplied himself without considering that another person might be meant.", "Ahasuerus told him to take the garments and horse immediately and do exactly what he had proposed for Mordecai the Jew at the king's gate. Nothing was to be omitted. Haman now had to arrange the honour he had designed for himself and proclaim it before the man whose refusal to bow had consumed him. Mordecai was led through Susa in royal attire, while Haman announced the king's delight in honouring him. The reversal was public, not merely an inward disappointment. The official who had arrived seeking permission to destroy Mordecai became the person required to acknowledge his service before the city.", "Afterward Mordecai returned to the gate, and Haman hurried home mourning with his head covered. He told Zeresh and his advisers what had happened. The household that had encouraged his confidence now predicted that he would not prevail against Mordecai but would fall before him. Their words are reported as their assessment, not a general rule that every later conflict will repeat the same outcome. Before the conversation ended, royal attendants arrived to hurry Haman to Esther's banquet. The chapter has transformed the meaning of that invitation. He had expected to attend after securing Mordecai's death. Instead, he goes after proclaiming Mordecai's honour, carrying a humiliation he has no time to repair and moving towards a request whose real purpose he still does not know."], "lesson": "Pride makes us misread opportunities as promises of our own advancement. Faithful service may be remembered in ways and at times we cannot arrange.", "question": "Do I hear a possible opportunity fairly, or immediately assume that its honour and benefit should belong to me?"},
  {"id": 96506, "title": "Esther Names Haman Before the King", "subtitle": "The hidden threat becomes a personal appeal for life", "text": "Esther 7:1–10", "chapters": [[16, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["Ahasuerus and Haman came to Esther's second banquet. Again the king asked what she wanted and promised a generous answer. This time Esther spoke the petition for which she had risked entering his presence. She asked that her own life be granted and her people spared. They had been sold to destruction, slaughter and death. Her words joined the threatened community to the queen sitting at his table. A decree treated as a matter of imperial advantage now concerned someone whose favour the king had publicly recognised. Esther did not begin with a demand for wealth or further privilege. She asked for lives.", "She said that if the matter had concerned sale into bondage, she would have remained silent, though the loss could not be adequately compensated. The statement expresses the urgency of the actual threat rather than making slavery harmless. Ahasuerus asked who had dared undertake such a thing. Esther answered that the adversary and enemy was the wicked Haman. She named the powerful man present in the room rather than leaving the accusation vague. Haman became terrified before the king and queen. The banquet he had treated as proof of his security had become the setting in which his plan was exposed by the person he had counted as another source of honour.", "The king rose in anger and went into the garden. Haman remained to plead with Esther because he understood that judgment was turning against him. When Ahasuerus returned, Haman had fallen upon the couch where she was reclining. The king took the scene as a further assault upon the queen within his own house. Attendants covered Haman's face. The narrative records the king's interpretation without requiring the retelling to invent another intention beyond Haman's plea for life. His collapse made the moment worse, and the authority he had relied upon was no longer available to protect him from its master's anger.", "Harbonah then mentioned the fifty-cubit structure Haman had made for Mordecai, the man whose warning had served the king. Ahasuerus ordered Haman executed upon it. The device prepared for Mordecai became the place of Haman's death, and the king's anger subsided. The reversal is striking, but the chapter's end does not yet mean that the Jews throughout the empire are safe. Haman is dead while the letters bearing the royal seal remain in force. Esther's courage has exposed the plot and removed its architect. Further action will be needed to protect the people whose danger first brought her to the inner court. The reader is left with a delivered queen still responsible to remember those beyond the palace walls."], "lesson": "Use opportunity to speak truth clearly on behalf of those in danger. Removing one wrongdoer does not always remove the consequences of the wrong.", "question": "What unresolved harm still needs attention even after the person responsible has been confronted?"},
  {"id": 96507, "title": "A New Decree Gives the Jews Leave to Defend Their Lives", "subtitle": "Esther continues pleading after her own danger has changed", "text": "Esther 8:1–17", "chapters": [[16, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["On the day of Haman's death, Ahasuerus gave his property to Esther. She explained her relationship with Mordecai, who was brought before the king. The signet ring taken from Haman was given to him, and Esther placed him over Haman's estate. The reversal brought wealth and influence to people previously threatened. Yet Esther did not treat her own improved circumstances as the end of the matter. She fell before the king again, weeping and asking him to stop the evil Haman had planned against the Jews. Her appeal reached beyond the palace's transfer of possessions to the families still facing an appointed day of death.", "The king extended the sceptre, and Esther asked for the letters ordering destruction to be reversed. She could not endure seeing her people's calamity or her relatives destroyed. Ahasuerus answered that a writing sealed in his name could not simply be revoked. He authorised Esther and Mordecai to write another decree for the Jews and seal it with his ring. The legal rigidity first used against Vashti and later employed in Haman's plan now complicated rescue. A change in royal feeling alone was insufficient. The danger had been sent into the empire through formal orders and needed an answer reaching the same officials and communities.", "On the twenty-third of Sivan, scribes prepared Mordecai's instructions for the hundred twenty-seven provinces. The letters addressed rulers and inhabitants in their scripts and languages, including the Jews in theirs. Couriers carried a decree permitting Jews in every city to gather and stand for their lives on the thirteenth of Adar. Its severe wording authorised destruction of attacking forces and their households and the taking of spoil. This was a grim legal answer to a grim earlier command, not a bloodless administrative correction. The story is concerned with a threatened people receiving permission to resist those who would attack them on the appointed day.", "Mordecai left the king's presence wearing royal blue and white, fine linen and purple and a great golden crown. Susa, bewildered by the earlier decree, now rejoiced. Wherever the new letters arrived, Jews celebrated with gladness, honour and feasting. Many others joined themselves to the Jews as fear of them spread. The account records that changed public response without pretending every heart had the same motive. For the threatened families, the message brought light where the earlier letters had brought mourning. Esther's continued pleading had helped turn personal access into protection beyond herself. The chapter ends with preparation still required, but the Jews no longer face the appointed date under a command that leaves them without an authorised defence."], "lesson": "Let another person's continuing need remain important when your own position improves. Responsible help follows a problem far enough to address its practical consequences.", "question": "Have I mistaken my own relief for the resolution of a difficulty that still affects other people?"},
  {"id": 96508, "title": "The Appointed Day Brings a Reversal", "subtitle": "Deliverance is remembered through a costly conflict", "text": "Esther 9:1–19", "chapters": [[16, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["The thirteenth of Adar arrived, the day on which the enemies of the Jews had expected to overpower them. Instead, the situation was reversed. Jewish communities assembled in their cities against those seeking their harm. Provincial officials supported them because Mordecai's influence had grown and his reputation had spread. The authority once placed in Haman's hand now stood behind the people's defence. Months of fear reached a decisive day, but the outcome did not take the form of every opponent peacefully abandoning the plan. The account records violent conflict and many deaths. A truthful retelling must allow the severity to remain visible within the story of rescue.", "In Susa, five hundred men were killed, including Haman's ten sons. Their names are preserved rather than leaving them as an anonymous part of the total. The Jews did not take the spoil. When the report reached Ahasuerus, he asked Esther what further request she had. She asked that the Jews in Susa be permitted another day under the decree and that Haman's sons be publicly displayed on the execution structure. Since their deaths have already been recorded, this further act concerns their bodies. The king granted the request. The text reports it without explaining every calculation behind Esther's decision or inviting the reader to invent a cleaner version of what occurred.", "On the fourteenth, another three hundred men were killed in Susa, and again no plunder was taken. Elsewhere, Jews gathered, stood for their lives and gained rest from their enemies. The recorded total of their slain foes was seventy-five thousand. They too refrained from spoil. The repeated detail distinguishes the action from using the decree as an opportunity to enrich themselves, though it cannot make the loss of life small. The passage concerns an ancient community under a particular threat and royal authorisation. Its violence must not be turned into permission for a modern reader to attack people identified as personal or religious enemies.", "The Jews in the provinces rested on the fourteenth and marked it with feasting and gladness. Those in Susa rested on the fifteenth because their conflict had continued another day. The difference explains the dates that would become part of the remembrance. In villages and unwalled towns, people celebrated and sent food to one another. The narrative begins moving from danger into a shared practice of remembering relief. Its joy is the joy of people who had been marked for destruction and survived. That perspective helps the reader understand the celebration while still reading the deaths with seriousness. Rescue and grief are not abstractions here. They concern households whose future had been placed under an order written months before."], "lesson": "Receive deliverance with gratitude without becoming careless about the human cost of conflict. Use remembered mercy to strengthen care within the community.", "question": "How can remembrance of a difficult rescue lead me towards gratitude and care rather than delight in another person's suffering?"},
  {"id": 96509, "title": "Purim Preserves the Memory of Rescue", "subtitle": "Joy is shared through food, generosity and public service", "text": "Esther 9:20–10:3", "chapters": [[16, 9], [16, 10]], "start": 20, "end": 3, "kind": "story", "category": "Esther", "story": ["Mordecai wrote to Jews throughout the provinces, near and far, to establish an annual remembrance on the fourteenth and fifteenth of Adar. These were the days when they had gained rest from their enemies, and the month in which sorrow had become joy and mourning a day of celebration. The remembrance included feasting, gifts of food to neighbours and gifts to the poor. The rescued community was to share its gladness rather than leave households without resources outside the celebration. Remembering survival would take a practical form through people receiving something good from one another. The account therefore joins a date in the calendar to a continuing duty of generosity.", "The name Purim came from Pur, the lot Haman had cast when devising destruction. The word connected the celebration with the plot that had failed. What was intended to determine an appointed day for killing became part of the name by which rescue would be remembered. The people accepted Mordecai's written instruction for themselves, their descendants and those joining them. Families, towns, provinces and future generations were to keep the memory alive. Their children would not need to have stood under the original threat to learn why the days mattered. The written record and repeated observance would carry an experience beyond the lives of its first witnesses.", "Esther, identified as Abihail's daughter, joined Mordecai in confirming the instructions with authority. Letters went through the empire with words of peace and truth, confirming the appointed times and the memory of fasting and lamentation. The account includes both the earlier cry and the later joy. Purim did not ask people to pretend that courage had been effortless or that fear had never entered their households. The queen who once received a decree through a messenger now helped establish a different message across the same wide territory. Her authority was used to preserve the people's memory and the practices through which they would share their gratitude.", "The book closes with Ahasuerus's tribute and the royal records of his power and Mordecai's advancement. Mordecai stood next to the king and was held in high regard among many of his people because he sought their welfare and spoke peace for their descendants. The final measure of his greatness is therefore not only the ring, clothing or rank. It includes the good he pursued for others. The narrative has followed an orphan, her guardian, a threatened people and a series of remarkable reversals within imperial life. It ends by directing remembered rescue towards generous celebration and responsible influence. Those who survived are called to remember, share and seek the peace of the community whose existence had once been treated as disposable."], "lesson": "Let remembered mercy produce shared joy, generosity to the poor and influence used for others' welfare. Honour received is best carried through continued service.", "question": "What experience of help should I remember through a practical gift or act of service rather than words alone?"},
  {"id": 97000, "title": "Job Loses His Wealth and His Children", "subtitle": "A faithful life is struck by grief it did not foresee", "text": "Job 1:1–22", "chapters": [[17, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job lived in Uz, a man described as upright, reverent towards God and determined to turn from evil. He had seven sons, three daughters, extensive flocks and herds and many servants. His children shared feasts in their homes, and Job regularly offered sacrifices out of concern for their standing before God. The opening gives the reader this account before any calamity arrives. His later suffering must therefore not be explained by assuming the narrator has concealed a life of obvious wickedness. Wealth and worship formed part of his ordinary life, and the coming loss would strike someone whose faithfulness had already been affirmed.", "The scene moves to an assembly before the Lord, where Satan questions Job's devotion. Does he fear God freely, or only because his household and possessions are protected? Satan argues that loss would turn his blessing into cursing. Permission is given concerning what Job possesses, with a limit protecting his person. Job does not hear this conversation. The reader receives an explanation of the challenge that the sufferer himself will lack. The difference matters throughout the book. Those who later speak confidently about his suffering do not possess the knowledge given in these opening scenes.", "Messengers arrive one after another. Sabean raiders have taken oxen and donkeys and killed servants. A devastating fire has consumed sheep and more servants. Chaldean bands have stolen the camels and killed those tending them. Before the reports have ended, another messenger tells of a great wind striking the house where Job's children were feasting. The building collapsed, and all died. The repeated arrival of survivors gives Job no pause to absorb one loss before the next. His wealth, working household and children have been torn from the life described at the chapter's beginning. The suffering is personal and communal, involving many dead beyond the man who hears the reports.", "Job rises, tears his robe, shaves his head and falls to the ground in worship. He remembers coming naked into life and returning without possessions, blessing the Lord's name amid what he cannot understand. The narrator says that in this he does not sin or charge God wrongly. His response does not mean he feels no grief. The torn clothing and shaved head give that grief an outward form. Worship and mourning stand together before the reader. The chapter introduces a faith that cannot be reduced to a bargain for uninterrupted prosperity. Job has not been told why these things happened, yet his first answer refuses to make the gifts he has lost the only reason to honour their Giver."], "lesson": "Do not assume that suffering proves hidden wrongdoing. Faith may hold to God while honestly mourning a loss it cannot explain.", "question": "How can I give a grieving person room for both sorrow and faith without demanding an explanation for the loss?"},
  {"id": 97001, "title": "Job Sits Among Ashes and His Friends Sit Beside Him", "subtitle": "Pain reaches his body while companionship begins in silence", "text": "Job 2:1–13", "chapters": [[17, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Another assembly before the Lord brings a renewed challenge. Job still holds his integrity, despite the devastation that has come without the wrongdoing his later friends will assume. Satan now argues that bodily suffering will change him. Permission is given to afflict his body, with his life preserved. Again the conversation is outside Job's knowledge. The reader sees limits upon the adversary's action while the sufferer receives no explanation of those limits. The book does not present him as someone who knows a hidden cause and merely needs patience until an agreed test ends. He must live through the pain without that account.", "Job is struck with painful sores from the soles of his feet to his head. He takes a broken piece of pottery to scrape himself and sits among ashes. The wealthy man's former standing offers no protection from the humiliating condition. His wife asks whether he still holds his integrity and urges him to curse God and die. Her words arise within the same household bereavement, though the narrator does not describe her private thoughts. Job answers that her proposal is foolish and asks whether they should receive good from God while refusing adversity. The account again says he does not sin with his lips. The reply rejects cursing without pretending that pain has become pleasant.", "Three friends hear what has happened: Eliphaz from Teman, Bildad from Shuah and Zophar from Naamah. They arrange to come together, intending to mourn with Job and comfort him. Seeing him from a distance, they scarcely recognise him. They weep, tear their robes and throw dust upon their heads. His suffering has changed his outward appearance so profoundly that friendship first answers through shared mourning rather than an explanation. Their arrival begins with compassion. The later disputes should not make readers forget that they originally travelled to be beside a friend in need.", "For seven days and nights they sit with him on the ground without speaking because they see the greatness of his pain. Their silence becomes one of the book's strongest pictures of companionship. No confident diagnosis has yet been placed between Job and his visitors. They recognise a grief too large for a quick answer and accept the discomfort of staying. What follows will test whether they can continue that humility once words begin. The chapter ends with four men seated near the ashes and a sufferer whose integrity has not stopped his distress. The scene gives readers a practical beginning for care: come, notice, mourn and remain present before presuming that another person's trouble is understood."], "lesson": "Presence and shared mourning can offer care before words are possible. Do not let the desire to explain pain outrun the willingness to sit beside it.", "question": "Who needs patient companionship from me more than an immediate answer?"},
  {"id": 97002, "title": "Job Curses the Day of His Birth", "subtitle": "Lament gives words to suffering without relief", "text": "Job 3:1–26", "chapters": [[17, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["After the days of silence, Job opens his mouth and curses the day of his birth. The distinction matters: he does not begin by doing what the adversary predicted and cursing God. He turns his grief towards the day and night through which his life began. He wishes darkness would claim them and erase their celebration from the calendar. The speech is poetry shaped by anguish, not a set of instructions about changing time. Its images express a desire that the beginning which led to this suffering had never come. The reader hears how far pain has entered the way Job regards his own existence.", "He asks why he did not die at birth or remain without seeing light. He imagines death as rest beside kings, counsellors and princes, beyond the disturbances of ordinary power. There the wicked stop troubling, the weary rest, prisoners no longer hear an oppressor and servants are free from masters. These pictures arise within his lament. They reveal what the sufferer longs to escape and should not be treated as a complete account of everything Scripture teaches about death. Job sees relief where the present offers none, and his language gathers people of different standing into that desired quiet.", "He then asks why light is given to someone in misery and life to a person bitter in soul. He pictures people searching for death more eagerly than hidden treasure and rejoicing at finding a grave. Such words show the depth of despair, but the account does not make despair a command to follow or a final judgment upon life's value. It gives a suffering person room to speak what pain has made hard to contain. Job feels his way is hidden and that the God whose protection once surrounded him has now hemmed him into distress. The same image of enclosure that earlier signified safety has become, in his experience, confinement.", "Sighing comes before his food, and groaning pours out like water. What he feared has arrived, and he cannot find ease, quiet or rest. The speech ends without a comforting resolution. That unfinished quality is part of its truth within the story. The book will continue to question Job's understanding, but it first allows his anguish to be heard at length. His friends must decide how to receive it. Readers who care for others face the same choice: whether to hear the misery beneath difficult words or hurry towards a correction that leaves the sufferer more alone. Lament is brought into Scripture's account of faith, reminding us that honest distress can be voiced before every question has an answer."], "lesson": "Listen carefully to lament instead of demanding an immediate return to cheerful words. Deep distress calls for compassionate presence and appropriate help.", "question": "Can I hear the pain beneath another person's difficult words without making their grief an occasion for judgment?"},
  {"id": 97003, "title": "Eliphaz Explains What He Thinks the Suffering Means", "subtitle": "True sayings can become harmful when applied without knowledge", "text": "Job 4:1–5:27", "chapters": [[17, 4], [17, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Eliphaz is the first friend to answer. He remembers Job instructing others and strengthening those whose hands and knees were weak. Now trouble has reached Job himself, and Eliphaz asks why he has become dismayed. He directs him towards reverence and integrity, then presents a principle he regards as decisive: innocent people are not cut off, while those who sow wrongdoing reap its trouble. Images of lions losing their strength illustrate the downfall of the powerful. The speech begins with remembered good but moves towards an explanation that assumes suffering can be read as evidence of moral failure. The opening narrative has already given the reader reason to question that assumption.", "Eliphaz recalls a frightening night experience, a spirit passing before his face and a voice asking whether a mortal could be more righteous than God. Human frailty is compared with a house of clay, easily crushed and passing without lasting notice. The experience gives his words an imposing atmosphere, but an impressive account does not establish that every application is correct. He is speaking within a conversation the book will eventually judge. Readers must keep the speaker and setting visible rather than turn every statement from a friend into God's own explanation of Job's condition.", "Eliphaz warns that anger destroys a fool and pictures a seemingly secure household suddenly broken, with children left without protection. In Job's circumstances, those words are especially painful. He then urges Job to seek God, describing His wonders, rain, help for the lowly and defeat of the crafty. God can preserve the poor and silence injustice. These are substantial truths about divine power and care. Yet the speech places them inside a diagnosis that has not been proved. A true description of God can be joined to an untrue explanation of what has happened to a particular person.", "He calls the person corrected by God blessed and urges acceptance of discipline. The God who wounds can also bind up, deliver from repeated troubles, protect in danger and give peace, descendants and a full old age. Eliphaz closes by presenting the conclusion as something carefully examined for Job's good. His intention to help does not resolve the danger in his confidence. He has moved from general convictions to assurances about Job's future without knowing the reason for the loss. The passage asks readers to discern between wisdom and overreach. Comfort becomes harmful when it demands that a sufferer accept an accusation merely because the adviser can express it beautifully and surround it with familiar truths."], "lesson": "Speak truth with humility about what you do not know. General principles do not give us authority to diagnose every sufferer's guilt or promise a particular outcome.", "question": "Am I applying a true statement in a way that assumes facts about another person's suffering I cannot know?"},
  {"id": 97004, "title": "Job Asks His Friends for Kindness and God for Relief", "subtitle": "A wounded man refuses an explanation that does not fit", "text": "Job 6:1–7:21", "chapters": [[17, 6], [17, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job asks that his anguish and calamity be weighed together. Their weight would exceed the sand of the sea, and his wild words belong to that overwhelming burden. An animal does not cry without need when food is present. His lament likewise has a cause. He feels struck by the Almighty's arrows and longs for an end to suffering he has no strength to sustain. His flesh is not bronze or stone. He asks for kindness from friends and compares their help to seasonal streams that look promising but disappear when travellers desperately need water. Their arrival created hope, but their answer has left him disappointed.", "He has not demanded that they pay a ransom or rescue him through wealth. He asks them to teach him where he has actually gone wrong. Honest words can carry force, but their argument has not identified the offence it assumes. Will they treat the words of a desperate man as something merely blown by the wind and then use them against him? He urges them to look at him and reconsider. Job is not claiming that correction is always unwelcome. He is refusing to exchange his integrity for an accusation unsupported by what his friends know. Compassion should not require an innocent person to confess a fabricated cause for grief.", "His speech then turns towards God. Human life seems like forced labour or a hired worker waiting for evening. Job's nights bring tossing rather than rest, while his damaged skin makes his bodily misery inescapable. His days pass quickly without hope. He speaks of life as a breath and of the grave cutting a person off from their familiar home. These words arise from his present horizon of pain. The reader should hear the lament without making it the book's last word about what God can do. Job wants the One who preserves life to remember how little strength and time he possesses.", "Even sleep does not comfort him because dreams terrify him. He asks why God watches him so intensely and why, if he has sinned, forgiveness does not bring relief. He feels made into a target and longs to be left alone before he lies in the dust. The speech contains severe and unsettling language, showing how bodily torment and unanswered questions press upon his mind. The narrative gives no suggestion that the friends improve matters by insisting their theory must be accepted. Job needs care, truthful listening and an answer beyond the formula already offered. His complaint remains directed towards God, even while he struggles with the thought that God's attention itself has become unbearable."], "lesson": "Do not confuse a desperate cry with proof of hidden wrongdoing. Offer kindness, examine evidence and let honest questions be spoken without adding blame.", "question": "How can I make my care more dependable for someone whose pain has lasted longer than I expected?"},
  {"id": 97005, "title": "Bildad Defends a Formula of Justice", "subtitle": "An appeal to tradition becomes an accusation without evidence", "text": "Job 8:1–22", "chapters": [[17, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Bildad answers Job's words as though they were a strong, disorderly wind. His starting question is whether God perverts justice. The answer is clear to him, but he uses it to suggest that Job's children may have been delivered to death because of their sin. Nothing in the conversation has established that claim. The reader has heard of their father's care but has not been given Bildad's proposed explanation. His defence of God's righteousness therefore begins by placing an unsupported judgment upon the dead. To a bereaved parent, the argument brings another wound under the appearance of protecting divine honour.", "He urges Job to seek God and plead for mercy. If Job is pure and upright, Bildad says, God will restore his righteous dwelling and make the latter state greater than its small beginning. The promise assumes that an improved outward condition must follow the right moral response. He appeals to earlier generations, saying that people living now know little and their days are only a shadow. Tradition can supply wisdom, but his use of it does not answer the particular facts he lacks. The inherited principle is being treated as a complete explanation of a life before him, leaving no room for suffering whose cause remains unknown.", "His images come from plants needing moisture, a web too weak to bear weight and a green growth eventually torn from its place. Those who forget God, he argues, have a hope that cannot endure. Their apparent security disappears as quickly as the plants' life when their support is gone. The poetry makes the argument memorable. Its danger lies in assuming that Job's broken security proves he belongs among those who have forgotten God. The opening chapters have described precisely the opposite. Beautiful imagery can clarify a truth, yet it can also conceal the gap between a general statement and the accusation being made through it.", "Bildad concludes that God will not reject an upright man or strengthen evildoers. Job's mouth will again be filled with laughter, while his enemies will face shame. The words offer hope, but on terms that require Job to accept Bildad's reading of his present state. The book asks readers to resist that easy trade. God's justice need not be denied in order to admit that we do not know why a righteous person suffers. The friend who insists otherwise makes comfort conditional upon a theory. Bildad's speech becomes a warning about the use of tradition without patient attention. Faithful counsel should honour God's righteousness while refusing to invent guilt where the evidence has not supplied it."], "lesson": "Defending God's justice does not require accusing a sufferer or explaining every loss. Tradition must be applied with truthfulness and compassion.", "question": "Have I used a familiar religious explanation to avoid admitting that I do not know why someone is suffering?"},
  {"id": 97006, "title": "Job Longs for Someone to Hear His Case", "subtitle": "The Creator's greatness makes his unanswered questions more painful", "text": "Job 9:1–10:22", "chapters": [[17, 9], [17, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job agrees that God is powerful and wise, but asks how a human being can be in the right before Him. No one could answer even a small portion of the questions such a contest might raise. He describes the Maker moving mountains, commanding the sun, spreading the heavens and establishing the stars. God's action exceeds human perception and cannot be restrained by a challenge. Yet those convictions bring Job little comfort while he feels condemned without a hearing. Knowledge of divine greatness deepens his question: if the One whose hand sustains everything stands against him, where can he bring an honest account of his life?", "He fears that even truthful words would be turned against him and that an attempt to establish his innocence would end in condemnation. He sees disasters striking both righteous and wicked people and struggles with the injustice permitted in the world. His language becomes an accusation born of anguish, not a settled explanation the reader must accept as God's character. He cannot simply smile and forget. His days pass swiftly, and even an effort to wash himself clean seems unable to remove the condition in which he feels trapped. He longs for an arbiter who could stand between himself and God, remove terror and allow him to speak.", "Turning directly to God, Job asks not to be condemned without being told the reason. Does the Creator delight in oppressing the work of His own hands? Does He see with the limited eyes of a human being or need to hurry an investigation within a short lifetime? Job appeals to God's knowledge that he is not the wicked person his friends imply. He remembers the care through which his body was formed, clothed with skin and flesh and sustained with life and kindness. The memory makes the present agony harder to understand. Why would such patient making be followed by what feels like relentless destruction?", "He fears that every movement is watched for guilt and that even righteousness would not let him lift his head. The old question returns: why was he brought into life if this would be its end? He asks for a little respite before entering the darkness of death. The two chapters bring together reverence for God's greatness and a sufferer's longing to be heard by Him. Job has not abandoned the relationship, but he can no longer make its present experience fit the care he remembers. His appeal warns counsellors against assuming that reciting God's power is sufficient comfort. A person may believe that power deeply and still need a patient hearing of the wounds that make it difficult to trust its purpose."], "lesson": "Let sufferers bring hard questions before God without pretending those questions give a complete account of Him. Listening can accompany faith in His greatness.", "question": "Can I make room for another person's unanswered questions without either condemning them or treating every anguished claim as final truth?"},
  {"id": 97007, "title": "Zophar Calls Job Guilty Before Hearing an Answer", "subtitle": "Certainty grows while compassion diminishes", "text": "Job 11:1–20", "chapters": [[17, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Zophar enters the conversation by attacking the quantity and character of Job's words. He calls them empty talk and false claims, asking whether no one will answer or shame him. He understands Job's defence as an assertion of perfect purity and wishes God would speak against him. Then he claims that Job is receiving less punishment than his wrongdoing deserves. The accusation goes beyond anything established in the discussion. The reader knows that the opening account affirms Job's integrity. Zophar's confidence therefore exposes how quickly the desire to defend a theory can become a readiness to condemn the person before us.", "He speaks of God's wisdom reaching beyond human discovery, higher than heaven and deeper than the grave, longer than earth and broader than sea. No human being can restrain God's action or hide wrongdoing from Him. These claims emphasise a genuine limit on human knowledge. Yet Zophar does not allow that limit to restrain his own explanation of Job's trouble. If God's ways exceed his understanding, he should be cautious about announcing precisely what a sufferer deserves. Instead, the mystery becomes a weapon: Job cannot comprehend God fully, so he must accept Zophar's verdict. The speech illustrates the difference between confessing a limit and using a limit to protect one's certainty.", "Zophar urges Job to prepare his heart, stretch out his hands and put away wrongdoing. If he does so, his face will be lifted without shame, fear will end and misery will fade like water that has passed. Darkness will become morning, hope will bring security and he will rest without being frightened. The invitation sounds generous, but it depends on the assumption that Job has hidden wickedness producing his condition. A future of relief is offered in exchange for a confession the evidence has not justified. Zophar has not asked which wrong he actually knows Job to have committed.", "The speech ends with the failure and hopelessness of the wicked. By placing that image against the promised safety of repentance, Zophar leaves little room for Job to answer without appearing to choose wickedness. His words deepen the pressure upon the sufferer to accept a false explanation for the sake of ending the argument. The book does not deny that repentance matters. It challenges the use of repentance as a demanded admission to an invented charge. Careful counsel would hold moral seriousness together with truthfulness about what is known. Zophar's first speech instead shows how an adviser can speak much about God's inaccessible wisdom while behaving as though he personally holds the key to another person's pain."], "lesson": "Humility about God's wisdom must restrain our own judgments. Do not demand confession to an accusation you have not established.", "question": "Am I offering help only on the condition that someone accepts my explanation of their trouble?"},
  {"id": 97008, "title": "Job Challenges Counsel That Claims to Defend God", "subtitle": "He longs to bring his case before the One who made him", "text": "Job 12:1–14:22", "chapters": [[17, 12], [17, 13], [17, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job answers with bitter irony that wisdom will surely die with his friends. He knows the truths they recite and is not inferior in understanding. Yet an upright man has become an object of laughter, while robbers and those provoking God can remain secure. Animals and earth testify that life is in God's hand, but that truth does not make the friends' simple explanation complete. God can overturn judges, rulers, nations and the confidence of the aged. His wisdom exceeds the respected voices assembled beside Job. Neither public standing nor inherited sayings give a person control over the meaning of another life.", "Job calls his friends unreliable physicians and asks whether they intend to defend God through falsehood and partiality. Their silence would be wiser than an accusation made in His name without truth. He wants to speak to the Almighty and arrange his case. In the KJV's wording, he declares trust even if God slays him, while still insisting on presenting his ways before Him. The speech holds resolve and protest together. Job believes that honestly approaching God is different from hypocrisy. He asks for the terror and pressure to be removed so that he can speak and receive an answer rather than be silenced by fear.", "He asks God to show his offences and explain why he is treated as an enemy. He feels like a driven leaf or dry straw pursued beyond all proportion. Human life is short, like a flower or passing shadow, and the Creator already knows its appointed boundaries. A felled tree may sprout again at water's scent, but a person dies and disappears from ordinary life. Job longs to be hidden until anger has passed, then remembered and called again by the Maker who would desire His own handiwork. The question of living after death appears within this searching lament rather than as a complete, settled explanation of resurrection.", "The hope flickers beside images of mountains falling, rocks moving and water wearing away stone. Job fears that human hope is similarly eroded and that the dead no longer share their children's honour or sorrow. He ends with bodily pain and inward mourning still present. The three chapters carry the reader through sarcasm, moral argument, prayer and a fragile longing to be remembered beyond present anguish. Job has not solved the mystery, and the friends have not answered his appeal. What he refuses is the idea that God needs their dishonesty. The passage gives counsellors a searching task: speak about Him in ways that are truthful both to His greatness and to the actual person suffering before them, leaving room for questions their knowledge cannot settle."], "lesson": "God does not need us to support Him with false accusations. Honest prayer and humble listening are better than confident words detached from truth.", "question": "Where might admitting the limits of my understanding be more faithful than continuing an argument?"},
  {"id": 97009, "title": "Eliphaz Treats Job's Protest as Evidence Against Him", "subtitle": "The second round hardens into a portrait of the wicked", "text": "Job 15:1–35", "chapters": [[17, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Eliphaz returns to the conversation with sharper accusations. A wise man, he says, should not fill himself with empty wind or offer speech that helps no one. He accuses Job of undermining reverence and prayer, declaring that his own mouth condemns him. The disagreement has changed its shape. Instead of hearing Job's protest as a request to examine the friends' assumptions, Eliphaz treats the protest itself as proof of guilt. That move makes the theory difficult to question: silence could be taken as admission, while resistance becomes another offence. The sufferer's words no longer receive a fair hearing.", "Eliphaz asks whether Job was the first person born or had listened in God's council. Does he possess wisdom unknown to them? Older men stand among the friends, and he appeals again to their experience. He asks why God's consolations are not enough and why Job's heart turns against Him. The difficulty is that Eliphaz has identified his own counsel with divine consolation before establishing that the counsel fits. Respect for age and experience matters, but it cannot turn an unsupported judgment into God's voice. The speech shows how easily a respected adviser can confuse disagreement with himself and rejection of the Lord.", "He stresses human uncleanness and then describes the wicked man's life as full of pain, fear and approaching destruction. Terror sounds in his ears, the sword waits, hunger follows and darkness closes in. The man has stretched out his hand against God and charged proudly against the Almighty. Images of ruined houses, drying branches, fallen fruit and consuming fire make his downfall vivid. Eliphaz is presenting an account of arrogant wrongdoing, but the conversation makes clear that Job is expected to see himself within it. No new evidence of such acts has been supplied. The portrait's detail increases the force of the accusation without increasing its truth.", "His conclusion joins hypocrisy, bribery, mischief and deception within a barren future. The speech offers little of the first answer's promise of restored peace. As the discussion continues, the friends' confidence becomes more severe while their knowledge of Job's actual conduct remains unchanged. The reader must keep the opening testimony in view. Job's suffering has not authorised Eliphaz to rewrite his life as a catalogue of concealed cruelty. This chapter warns about a familiar failure in correction: when a person's refusal to accept our explanation makes us intensify the charge rather than reconsider it. Faithful counsel should remain able to ask whether its own argument has been wrong. Eliphaz instead continues speaking as though greater severity can supply what missing evidence has not provided."], "lesson": "Do not treat resistance to your opinion as automatic proof of rebellion against God. Correction must remain truthful and open to reconsideration.", "question": "Have I hardened my judgment because someone challenged my advice rather than because new evidence showed wrongdoing?"},
  {"id": 97010, "title": "Job Calls His Friends Miserable Comforters", "subtitle": "He seeks a witness beyond the voices accusing him", "text": "Job 16:1–17:16", "chapters": [[17, 16], [17, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job says that he has heard many such things and calls the friends miserable comforters. If their positions were reversed, he too could heap words upon them and shake his head. Yet he says he would use his mouth to strengthen and relieve them. The contrast exposes the choice before anyone offering care. Words can display an adviser's ability or serve the sufferer's need. Job finds that neither speaking nor silence removes his grief. His companions' certainty has added a further burden to an affliction they originally came to share. Their presence is no longer experienced as the comfort their journey intended.", "He describes himself as worn out, his household desolate and his wasted body treated as a witness against him. Images of tearing, assault, archers and repeated breaches express how relentless the suffering feels. He sees God handing him to the wicked and making him a target. These are the anguished perceptions of the speaker, not an invitation to accept every image as the complete explanation of God's purpose. Job weeps and wears mourning clothes, yet maintains that his hands are not violent and his prayer is sincere. He refuses to let the appearance of his body replace the truth about his conduct.", "He appeals for his cry not to be buried and declares that his witness is in heaven. While friends mock, he pours tears before God and longs for someone to plead for a person as one neighbour pleads for another. The hope of a hearing rises inside the complaint. In the next chapter he asks God for a pledge and finds no other person able to provide it. His eyesight is dim with grief and he has become a public saying, yet he insists that the righteous can continue holding their way. His own longing for vindication exists beside a wider conviction that integrity must not be abandoned merely because suffering has made it costly.", "He sees his plans broken and hears promises of light that do not reach his darkness. If the grave is the only household awaiting him, where is the hope his friends keep describing? The speech ends with the dust, refusing an easy recovery produced by rhetoric. Job is not helped by being told that the future must match the friends' preferred pattern. He needs his life to be known truthfully before God. The passage challenges a reader to offer comfort that strengthens rather than pressures, and to recognise a reaching faith even when it appears within broken words. His tears are still poured towards the One he cannot presently understand, while human witnesses have failed to honour the facts before them."], "lesson": "Comfort should strengthen a sufferer rather than make them defend themselves against imagined guilt. Let truthful care accompany a person's longing to be heard by God.", "question": "Are my words helping someone endure, or requiring them to protect themselves from my assumptions?"},
  {"id": 97011, "title": "Bildad Describes a House Going Dark", "subtitle": "The picture of judgment is made to resemble Job's losses", "text": "Job 18:1–21", "chapters": [[17, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Bildad asks when the words will end and complains that the friends are being treated like unintelligent creatures. He regards Job as tearing himself in anger and asks whether the world's order should be changed for him. The opening reveals how personally the disagreement has become. Bildad hears Job's objection as an insult to the friends and to the structure of justice they defend. He therefore begins another account of the wicked person's fate, expecting its certainty to settle what Job's protest has left unresolved. The question of whether the description actually belongs to Job receives no fresh examination.", "He pictures light extinguished within the wicked man's tent. Strong steps become cramped, and the person's own counsel brings him down. Nets, snares, traps and hidden cords surround the path. Terror pursues from every side, hunger consumes strength and destruction waits nearby. The images turn the world into a place from which the guilty cannot escape. Such language can speak truthfully about the insecurity of evil, yet in this conversation it is being directed towards a particular sufferer. Bildad does not merely warn wrongdoing in the abstract. The repeated appeal assumes that Job's present condition can be read through this portrait.", "Disease consumes the skin, confidence is torn from the dwelling and the sufferer is brought before the king of terrors. Roots dry, branches are cut, remembrance disappears and no descendants remain. The details echo losses the reader has already seen in Job's life. That resemblance gives the speech a cruel force. A father who has buried all his children hears their absence used as part of the description of a man who does not know God. Nothing Bildad has learned justifies that verdict. Matching an outward calamity to an established image of judgment cannot establish the moral cause of the calamity.", "The speech ends by declaring that such are the dwellings of the wicked. Its firmness provides no new comfort and no evidence beyond the suffering itself. The reader is being trained to distinguish the reality of divine judgment from the friends' claim to identify it in every broken home. Job's case has already challenged that identification. The chapter also reveals how an offended counsellor can choose language that answers his own hurt while deepening another person's wound. Careful listening would require Bildad to set aside the need to be vindicated and ask what his words are doing to the bereaved man. Instead, a catalogue of terrors is offered where compassionate uncertainty might have made room for truth and continued friendship."], "lesson": "Outward similarity does not prove that two sufferings have the same cause. Guard against using a person's deepest losses as evidence for an accusation.", "question": "Have I mistaken a resemblance to a familiar pattern for knowledge of what has actually happened?"},
  {"id": 97012, "title": "Job Appeals to His Living Redeemer", "subtitle": "Hope rises while isolation remains severe", "text": "Job 19:1–29", "chapters": [[17, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job asks how long the friends will break him with words. Their repeated reproaches have made them strangers to him. Even if he has erred, he challenges their use of his disgrace to magnify themselves. He describes God as enclosing his path, stripping away honour and uprooting hope like a tree. He cries about wrong without receiving the judgment he seeks. The images show a man unable to make sense of his suffering, and they should be heard as his appeal rather than detached into a complete doctrine about God's character. His need is not another polished accusation but an honest hearing.", "He then describes the social isolation surrounding the physical pain. Relatives stay away, acquaintances forget him, servants treat him as a stranger and a call for help goes unanswered. His condition is repellent even within close household relationships. Children despise him, and people he loved turn against him. His body has wasted to skin and bones. He begs his friends for pity because he has already been afflicted so deeply. Their continuing pursuit seems to him like joining the force against which he has no defence. The losses have reached beyond possessions and health into the human relationships through which a person ordinarily receives reassurance of belonging.", "Job wishes his words could be written permanently, engraved with iron and lead in rock. He wants a witness that will outlast his weakening body and the friends' accusations. Then he declares that his redeemer lives and will at last stand upon the earth. Despite the destruction of his skin, he expects to see God for himself. The passage has received sustained Christian attention as an expression of hope reaching beyond present suffering towards vindication and life. Its place within Job's speech must also remain visible: he is appealing for the living defender who can establish the truth when human testimony has failed, not explaining every detail of the future.", "The hope does not immediately restore his circumstances. It rises within the same anguish and longing described around it. Job ends by warning those pursuing him that judgment is real for them as well. Their ability to speak about God's justice does not place their treatment of a sufferer beyond examination. The chapter gives a striking movement from social abandonment to a defender who lives. Readers should not use its famous words to silence the distress of the preceding verses. Their force belongs precisely to a man who has not yet received relief. His longing to see God and be known truthfully becomes a reason to keep hearing him, while the warning asks every accuser to remember that the judge of another person's life will also judge the use of their own words."], "lesson": "Hope in God's final vindication can coexist with profound pain. Do not let a statement of hope become an excuse to ignore a person's continuing need for care.", "question": "How can I honour someone's hope in God while taking seriously the loneliness they are still experiencing?"},
  {"id": 97013, "title": "Zophar Predicts Sudden Ruin and Job Points to What He Sees", "subtitle": "A theory of prosperity meets the evidence of ordinary life", "text": "Job 20:1–21:34", "chapters": [[17, 20], [17, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Zophar answers quickly because he feels reproached. He insists that the wicked person's triumph is brief, however high the apparent success. It vanishes like a dream. Evil may taste sweet, but it becomes poison within; wealth swallowed through oppression must be disgorged. He pictures a man who seized the poor person's house and finds no rest, with weapons, fire and darkness closing upon him. Heaven and earth reveal his guilt, and his household's increase disappears. Zophar calls this the portion God assigns the wicked. The moral seriousness of exploitation is real, but his rigid timetable is being used to explain Job's losses without proving Job committed the offences described.", "The speech again makes the sufferer listen to a detailed picture of judgment resembling his own ruin. Zophar offers neither evidence nor a question that might let Job's actual history correct the argument. His certainty grows from an established view of the world. If wickedness always rises briefly and collapses visibly, a collapsed life seems to confirm wickedness. The reader knows that the inference is precisely what the opening account denies in Job's case. The friend's anger at being challenged has not supplied the knowledge his earlier words lacked. The next response therefore turns towards observations that the formula cannot easily contain.", "Job asks them to listen carefully and regard that listening as their comfort to him. Why do wicked people live long, grow powerful, watch descendants flourish and keep secure households? Their livestock prospers, children dance and music accompanies their ease, even while they reject the need to know or serve God. Job does not endorse that rejection. He expressly distances himself from their counsel. His point is that outward prosperity cannot be used as a reliable public certificate of righteousness, just as calamity cannot automatically prove an individual's secret guilt. The friends must reckon with what other people can plainly see.", "He considers the claim that punishment falls upon descendants and asks why the offender should not face it personally. People die in different conditions, one well supplied and another bitter, then lie in the same dust. Travellers can tell of wicked people carried with honour to guarded tombs. Final judgment is not denied by recognising those present facts, but the friends' immediate explanation fails. Job concludes that their comfort is empty because falsehood remains within the answers. The two speeches ask readers to let truth challenge a convenient system. Compassion and reverence do not require pretending that life's visible outcomes always disclose God's complete moral accounting. Honest counsel must make room for the evidence before announcing a reason for another person's grief."], "lesson": "Do not use prosperity or disaster as a simple measure of a person's standing before God. Let observed truth correct an explanation you have become attached to.", "question": "What evidence am I overlooking because it does not fit the explanation I prefer?"},
  {"id": 97014, "title": "Eliphaz Names Crimes and Job Asks Where Justice Can Be Found", "subtitle": "The debate turns from inference to unsupported particulars", "text": "Job 22:1–24:25", "chapters": [[17, 22], [17, 23], [17, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Eliphaz asks whether human righteousness adds anything God needs, then directly accuses Job of extensive wickedness. He names taking pledges without cause, stripping people, refusing water and bread, sending widows away and crushing the fatherless. These are serious offences, but the speech has provided no testimony that Job committed them. The accusation has grown more detailed as the argument has failed to persuade him. Eliphaz says that this wrongdoing explains the snares and darkness surrounding Job and attributes to him a belief that God cannot see through the clouds. He has replaced a difficult person with a constructed offender who fits the theory.", "He again calls Job to submit to God, receive instruction and return. Precious metals should not become his confidence, and delight in the Almighty would bring a restored life. Prayer would be heard, plans established and light return. Those promises must be read as part of Eliphaz's speech rather than detached into guarantees that any believer can secure a chosen outcome by declaring it. The book is testing the friend's application. An appeal containing good counsel about devotion does not make its underlying charges true. The reader must keep the unsupported crimes and the comforting conclusion within the same conversation.", "Job answers that he wishes he knew where to find God and present his case. He believes a fair hearing would bring understanding rather than sheer force. Yet wherever he turns, he cannot perceive the One he seeks. God knows his way, and Job expects to emerge like gold from testing because he has treasured God's words and kept His path. Confidence and fear remain together, for he also knows that divine purpose is not something he can simply redirect. He then widens his lament beyond his own situation. Why do those who know God fail to see a clear time when judgment answers the wrong around them?", "He names moved boundary stones, stolen flocks, a widow's animal taken as security and poor people driven from the road. People work hungry and thirsty while producing another person's grain, oil and wine. The wounded cry, while murderers, thieves and adulterers use darkness for their acts. Job recognises that the wicked are not beyond God's ultimate sight or the grave's reach. His question concerns the delay and unevenness that the friends' formula ignores. The chapters place two uses of moral language beside one another: Eliphaz invents particular crimes for Job, while Job points to actual patterns of oppression as a reason for humility about easy explanations. A truthful defence of justice must be willing to hear those facts rather than use the word justice to silence them."], "lesson": "Serious moral claims require evidence. Seek justice for those actually harmed instead of inventing guilt to preserve an explanation.", "question": "Am I listening to concrete suffering around me, or using general convictions to avoid its difficult questions?"},
  {"id": 97015, "title": "Job Holds His Integrity Before the Creator's Power", "subtitle": "He refuses both false confession and the praise of wickedness", "text": "Job 25:1–27:23", "chapters": [[17, 25], [17, 26], [17, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Bildad gives a brief final speech about God's dominion and the awe belonging to Him. His armies are beyond numbering, His light reaches all and no human being can claim purity beside His greatness. Moon and stars are not pure in His sight, and frail humanity is compared with a worm. These words return to divine majesty without answering Job's questions about the timing and experience of justice. The speech is short, but its effect continues the earlier pressure: Job should accept the friends' account because a human being cannot stand above God. The unanswered issue is whether the friends have spoken for Him truthfully.", "Job responds by asking how these words have helped someone without strength. Whose wisdom has been supplied, and whose spirit has spoken through the advice? He then gives his own account of God's power. The depths lie open before Him, the earth hangs under His rule, waters are held within clouds and the sea has its appointed boundary. Heaven trembles and the great waters yield. Yet these are only the edges of His ways, a faint whisper beside the thunder of His power. Job's protest therefore cannot fairly be reduced to ignorance that God is great. He knows that greatness while still seeking an explanation for his affliction.", "He continues with an oath that, while breath remains, he will not speak falsely or surrender his integrity merely to declare the friends right. God has left him feeling denied a hearing, but that experience will not justify lying about his life. His conscience cannot consent to wrongdoing he has not committed. The resolve is not a claim that he possesses God's complete knowledge or has never needed mercy. It is a refusal to let pressure dictate a confession without truth. He also asks what hope a godless person has when life is taken and whether that person continues delighting in God through trouble.", "Job describes the downfall appointed to oppression: wealth passing to others, children facing hunger or violence, an unstable house and terrors sweeping away its apparent security. This recognition keeps his earlier observations of prosperous wickedness from being mistaken for approval of evil or denial that judgment exists. The dispute concerns the right to use suffering as a complete moral explanation, not whether oppression can stand forever before God. The passage joins integrity to reverence and moral seriousness. Job will neither flatter wickedness to win his argument nor invent wickedness in himself to satisfy his friends. He remains a sufferer seeking the truth, while the human conversation has almost exhausted its power to supply the answer he longs to hear."], "lesson": "Refuse falsehood even when it would end a difficult argument. Humility before God and integrity about known facts belong together.", "question": "What pressure might tempt me to say something untrue about myself or another person for the sake of agreement?"},
  {"id": 97016, "title": "Where Can Wisdom Be Found?", "subtitle": "The search reaches beyond wealth and human skill", "text": "Job 28:1–28", "chapters": [[17, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["The poem turns from argument to the search for wisdom. Human beings know how to find silver, refine gold, extract iron and smelt copper from stone. Miners enter darkness, cut hidden passages and expose treasure no bird of prey has seen and no lion has walked over. They work beneath the earth that produces food, overturn rock and restrain underground waters. Their effort brings what was concealed into light. The opening honours remarkable human skill. People can reach places inaccessible to strong animals and sharp eyes, yet the question about wisdom remains. The ability to uncover precious material is not the same as understanding the meaning of life before God.", "Where is wisdom found, and where does understanding dwell? The depths and sea cannot claim to contain it. It cannot be purchased with gold, measured against silver or exchanged for jewels, crystal, coral or fine stones. The repeated comparisons set its value beyond the resources people most readily use to secure what they desire. Wealth can fund an expedition and expertise can discover a vein, but neither gives mastery over the questions troubling Job. The friends' arguments have displayed confident skill with words. This poem asks whether confidence and inherited knowledge have actually reached the wisdom they appear to possess.", "Even destruction and death have only heard a report of it. God understands the way to wisdom and knows its place because His sight reaches the ends of the earth. He establishes the weight of the wind, measures the waters and orders rain and lightning. The Creator's understanding embraces relationships and purposes no single observer can survey. The argument does not make human inquiry useless. It places that inquiry within its proper limits. A person may discover much and still need to acknowledge that the full order of creation and providence remains beyond a human grasp. That admission gives humility a rightful place beside learning.", "The poem concludes with what God has made known for human life: reverence for the Lord is wisdom, and turning from evil is understanding. The ending gives a clear duty without pretending that every hidden cause has been disclosed. Job's opening description already joined fear of God with departure from evil, connecting the search to the character the narrator affirmed before his losses. Readers are not asked to buy secret knowledge or claim control through an explanation of every event. They are directed towards a reverent and obedient life. The poem therefore offers a foundation deeper than the friends' attempt to identify guilt from calamity: the person who cannot explain all God's ways can still honour Him through truthfulness, humility and a real refusal of wrongdoing."], "lesson": "Value wisdom above wealth and confidence. Reverence and departure from evil remain clear responsibilities when the causes of suffering are hidden.", "question": "What obedient response is already clear even though I do not yet understand the situation fully?"},
  {"id": 97017, "title": "Job Remembers His Former Life and Gives His Final Defence", "subtitle": "Integrity is described through the treatment of vulnerable people", "text": "Job 29:1–31:40", "chapters": [[17, 29], [17, 30], [17, 31]], "start": 1, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job remembers the days when God's lamp seemed to shine over him, his children surrounded him and his household knew abundance. At the city gate, young men stepped aside, elders stood and leaders listened to his counsel. He explains that honour through service: he rescued the poor, cared for the fatherless, brought joy to widows and acted as eyes and feet for people needing help. He investigated unfamiliar cases and restrained the powerful who seized another's property. He had expected to grow old securely. His memory therefore includes both a cherished relationship with God and the public usefulness through which his former standing had been expressed.", "Now he is mocked by younger people whom he describes harshly through the outcast lives of their fathers. His speech preserves the wounded pride and social assumptions of the speaker rather than making every judgment within it a pattern to imitate. He has become a song of ridicule, is spat upon and feels swept away by terror. Nights bring bone-deep pain, clothing binds his diseased body and cries to God seem unanswered. He remembers weeping for others in distress and cannot reconcile that care with his own present darkness. Music has become mourning. The contrast between former respect and current contempt gives the lament its human force.", "Job then sets out an oath concerning his conduct. He has guarded his eyes against lust and rejected deceit and adultery. He asks for a just weighing of his integrity. Servants' complaints mattered because their Maker was also his Maker; authority did not place him beyond accountability. He denies withholding food or clothing from the poor, exploiting the fatherless or using influence against those unable to defend themselves. He rejects trusting gold as his security, secret worship of sun and moon, delight in an enemy's ruin and refusal of hospitality. His righteousness is being described through costly responsibilities, not simply a claim to possess respectable religious language.", "He also denies concealing wrongdoing from fear of the crowd and calls for a hearing before God. If an accusation were supplied, he would carry it openly and answer for his steps. His final appeal includes the land itself: if its produce came through injustice or its owners suffered through his conduct, let weeds replace the harvest. The text then says his words are ended. The three chapters provide an extended answer to the particular crimes Eliphaz had asserted without evidence. Job's defence will not prevent God from correcting his limited understanding, but neither should that later correction erase the integrity of the life described. Readers are left with a searching account of moral responsibility reaching desire, wealth, labour, courts, hospitality and the dignity of those beneath one's power. The final human plea remains that the truth should be heard rather than replaced by a theory."], "lesson": "Integrity includes the way we treat people affected by our power and resources. Be willing to examine your life through concrete duties rather than reputation alone.", "question": "Which part of Job's defence most closely examines the way I use money, influence or authority?"},
  {"id": 97018, "title": "Elihu Waits, Then Enters the Debate", "subtitle": "A younger voice offers another account of suffering and justice", "text": "Job 32:1–34:37", "chapters": [[17, 32], [17, 33], [17, 34]], "start": 1, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["The three friends stop answering because Job remains righteous in his own eyes. Elihu, a younger man who has waited out of respect, becomes angry with both sides. He believes Job has justified himself rather than God, while the friends have condemned Job without answering him. Age, he now says, does not automatically provide wisdom; understanding comes through the Almighty's gift. He feels pressed to speak and promises not to flatter. The introduction makes his difference clear without declaring that every later statement is God's own verdict. Another human voice has entered a discussion whose greatest answer is still to come.", "Elihu asks Job to hear him as someone formed from clay like himself, without an overwhelming power that should make him afraid. He repeats Job's claim to innocence and his complaint that God treats him as an enemy. God is greater than humanity, he answers, and can speak in ways a person does not recognise. Dreams may warn against pride, and bodily suffering may become a means of instruction rather than merely a punishment exposing an earlier crime. He pictures a messenger explaining the right way, a ransom and a restored person confessing wrongdoing and seeing light again. The emphasis broadens the discussion towards discipline and rescue, though it does not give Elihu access to every hidden cause in Job's own case.", "He then appeals to the hearers to test words as the mouth tastes food. God cannot act wickedly or pervert justice. All life depends upon His sustaining breath, and rulers and poor people stand alike as the work of His hands. Nothing can conceal wrongdoing from Him, and the cries of oppressed people reach His attention. Elihu challenges the implication that God's rule could be unjust simply because Job has not received the hearing he seeks. These convictions about God's character matter within the book. They also require the reader to distinguish defending divine righteousness from assuming that a human account of every event is therefore complete.", "Elihu calls for a teachable response: ask God to show what is unseen and undertake not to continue any wrongdoing disclosed. Yet he also judges Job's words harshly and wishes him tested further because his answers add rebellion. His attempt to correct can therefore carry its own pressure upon a sufferer already exhausted. The passage introduces a serious distinction: pain may have an instructive purpose without proving the specific guilt the earlier friends alleged. It leaves readers with a need for discernment rather than a new infallible human counsellor. Listen to what rightly honours God, examine the speaker's claims and remember that Job's original suffering began before the arguments now being criticised. A call to humility belongs to every participant, including the person confident enough to announce that he can supply wisdom where others failed."], "lesson": "Remain teachable and recognise that suffering cannot be reduced to one simple explanation. Test human counsel without confusing its confidence with God's final word.", "question": "How can I receive a true correction while still examining assumptions the adviser may not be entitled to make?"},
  {"id": 97019, "title": "Elihu Calls Job to Consider the Works of God", "subtitle": "The speech moves from moral argument towards the coming storm", "text": "Job 35:1–37:24", "chapters": [[17, 35], [17, 36], [17, 37]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Elihu continues by challenging the question of what advantage righteousness brings. God's greatness is not dependent on gifts people can supply, while human good and evil have real effects upon neighbours. He speaks of oppressed people crying under the strong and asks whether they seek the Maker who gives songs in the night. His argument urges trust even when God cannot be seen and judgment seems delayed. The words should be read as Elihu's response to Job's speech, not a reason to dismiss every unanswered cry as insincere. The book has already shown a faithful sufferer whose pain cannot be explained by an absence of reverence.", "He again undertakes to speak for God's righteousness, describing the Almighty as powerful without despising people. God can watch the righteous and use affliction to open their ears to instruction. Elihu urges a response to correction and warns against choosing wrongdoing as an escape from pain. He also recognises that wealth cannot purchase rescue or give a person authority to command God. His confidence about prosperity following obedience echoes concerns already tested in the wider debate, so readers must keep the whole book's account in view. A particular statement's devotional appeal does not make it a universal timetable for relief in every suffering life.", "The speech turns to God's work in rain and clouds. Water is drawn up and poured down, lightning spreads and thunder announces a power people cannot comprehend. Snow, showers, frost and storms interrupt human labour and send animals to shelter. Elihu describes these events as subject to God's counsel, whether their effect is correction, provision for the land or mercy. The movement carries attention beyond the disputants' attempts to account for one person's circumstances. Creation operates with purposes and relationships no observer controls completely. Human vulnerability before weather becomes an image of a broader need for humility before the Creator.", "Elihu asks Job to stop and consider those wonders. Does he know how clouds are balanced, lightning appointed or the skies spread? What words can limited people arrange adequately before such majesty? The conclusion affirms the Almighty's surpassing power and justice and calls for reverence rather than self-satisfied wisdom. No response from Job is recorded before the Lord begins to speak. Elihu's final questions prepare the setting, but the coming divine speech must still be heard in its own right. The reader has listened to people defend God, accuse a sufferer and reach towards explanations. Now the discussion approaches a different encounter. The greatest need is no longer another person confidently claiming the last word, but a willingness to listen when the One whose works exceed all of them answers for Himself."], "lesson": "Let the works of God cultivate reverence and restrain self-satisfied explanations. Being able to describe a truth does not mean we control all its applications.", "question": "What would it mean for me to pause an argument and listen more carefully before claiming the final answer?"},
  {"id": 97020, "title": "The Lord Answers from the Whirlwind", "subtitle": "The sufferer is addressed by the Creator whose world exceeds his view", "text": "Job 38:1–39:30", "chapters": [[17, 38], [17, 39]], "start": 1, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["The Lord answers Job from the whirlwind. He asks who has darkened counsel with words lacking knowledge and calls Job to prepare for questions. The answer does not begin by telling him the heavenly conversations recorded at the book's opening. Instead, God brings the sufferer before the scope of creation. Where was he when earth's foundations were laid and the morning stars sang? Who bounded the sea, clothed it with clouds and set a limit to its waves? The questions expose a distance between living within God's world and possessing the knowledge needed to govern the whole of it. Job is addressed directly, but not confirmed as the judge of every purpose.", "The speech moves through dawn, the sea's depths, the gates of death, light and darkness, snow, hail, rain and lightning. God sends rain even upon uninhabited land, bringing growth where human usefulness cannot be the only explanation. Stars and seasons follow ordinances Job did not establish. He cannot command clouds or dispatch lightning to report for duty. The questions do not make his pain unreal. They enlarge the frame within which he has tried to account for it, showing a world sustained through wisdom and care that extend beyond the boundaries of his experience. Human life is important without being the measure of every created relationship.", "God then speaks of lions and ravens receiving food, mountain goats giving birth and young animals growing beyond their parents' care. The wild donkey lives free of the driver's command, and the powerful wild ox cannot simply be recruited to gather Job's harvest. The ostrich's strange behaviour and remarkable speed stand beside the horse's strength and courage in battle. The catalogue includes creatures whose lives are useful, untamed, puzzling and beautiful in different ways. They do not all fit into one simple pattern designed for human control. Their Maker's knowledge embraces what Job can observe only in fragments, and His care is active beyond the places where people supervise it.", "The hawk's flight and the eagle's high nest bring the speech to a close, including their search for prey. The created world is not presented as a tame garden without danger or mystery. Its order and vitality nevertheless belong to the One answering Job. The passage gives the reader more than an instruction to stop asking questions. God has entered the conversation and shown that wisdom includes a care and power far greater than the sufferer's present view. Job's friends could not supply that encounter through accusation. The Lord's own questions call him towards humility without rewriting the opening testimony that his losses were not the punishment the friends assumed. He must now respond to the Creator who knows both him and the world he cannot fully comprehend."], "lesson": "Let God's wisdom correct the assumption that your view contains the whole story. Reverence can grow through attending to His care beyond what you control.", "question": "What part of my judgment needs to be humbled by remembering how much lies outside my knowledge?"},
  {"id": 97021, "title": "Job Grows Silent Before Behemoth and Leviathan", "subtitle": "The power to question is not the power to govern creation", "text": "Job 40:1–41:34", "chapters": [[17, 40], [17, 41]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["The Lord asks whether the person contending with the Almighty can now instruct Him. Job answers by acknowledging his smallness and laying his hand upon his mouth. He will not continue as before. Yet the divine address continues with a sharper moral question: will Job condemn God's judgment in order to establish his own righteousness? Does he possess an arm and voice like God's? If he can clothe himself with majesty, bring every proud person low and overcome wickedness, then he could claim the power to save himself. The questions reach beyond the truth of Job's particular integrity to the danger of making his limited understanding the measure of all divine government.", "God directs attention to behemoth, a creature made alongside Job yet possessing strength he cannot readily master. It eats grass, has powerful limbs and bones described through metal, rests among reeds and shade and remains unalarmed by a surging river. The language draws the reader into the creature's formidable life without requiring certainty about every modern identification. Its role in the speech is clear: something within creation already exceeds the ordinary reach of human command. Job need not be able to capture or explain every detail for the question to expose the difference between observing power and possessing it.", "The address then turns to leviathan. Can Job draw it out with a hook, secure it, make it a servant or treat it as a child's pet? Weapons fail against the creature's armour, and an attempt to subdue it would not be lightly repeated. Its poetic description includes terrifying jaws, closely joined scales, fiery breath and a wake that makes the waters boil. Readers have understood its imagery in different ways, but the speech does not give a practical manual for naming a spirit or diagnosing a person's condition. Its purpose here is to display a fearsome power that belongs within God's creation and remains beyond Job's control.", "If no ordinary person can confidently stand before such a creature, who can claim superiority over its Maker? Everything beneath heaven belongs to the Lord, who owes no creature repayment for having first supplied Him. The section ends with leviathan's fearless strength and its rule over the proud. The questions have not told Job that oppression is good or that his friends' accusations were true. They have challenged the reach of his claims about how God must govern. His need for a hearing has met a Lord whose knowledge includes powers he cannot manage. The remaining response will require humility, but it will also lead to a public correction of those who thought they had explained his pain so completely."], "lesson": "Recognise the difference between honestly appealing to God and assuming the authority to judge all His government. Humility need not require a false confession to other people's accusations.", "question": "Where am I treating my inability to understand or control something as proof that God's wisdom must have failed?"},
  {"id": 97022, "title": "Job Prays for the Friends and Receives a New Beginning", "subtitle": "God corrects the counsellors and restores the sufferer", "text": "Job 42:1–17", "chapters": [[17, 42]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Job", "story": ["Job answers that God can do all things and no purpose of His can be thwarted. He recognises that he has spoken about matters beyond his understanding. The Lord's questions have brought him from what he had heard to a direct encounter he describes as seeing. He responds in the dust and ashes with repentance. This admission concerns the limits and overreach exposed by God's answer. It does not require the reader to accept the catalogue of hidden crimes the friends invented. The book will immediately distinguish Job's humbled response from the confident explanations they offered in God's name.", "The Lord addresses Eliphaz and expresses anger against him and his two companions. They have not spoken rightly concerning Him as Job has. They are to bring seven bulls and seven rams to Job, offer sacrifice and receive the prayer of the man they accused. God will accept Job rather than deal with them according to their folly. Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar obey. The reversal reaches the moral centre of the conversation. The sufferer is no longer standing alone beneath their judgment. Those who claimed to defend God must receive correction from Him and approach the person they failed to comfort. Job's prayer becomes part of their restoration.", "The Lord restores Job's fortunes as he prays for his friends and gives him twice his former possessions. Relatives and former acquaintances come, share a meal, comfort him and bring gifts. The losses are not retrospectively made imaginary. Their renewed presence answers a real loneliness, while the new blessing opens a future that grief had made difficult to imagine. Job receives fourteen thousand sheep, six thousand camels, a thousand yoke of oxen and a thousand female donkeys. He also has seven sons and three daughters. The children who died are not treated as objects whose value can be cancelled by new children. The narrative records continued family life after devastating bereavement.", "His daughters, Jemimah, Keziah and Keren-happuch, are named and receive an inheritance alongside their brothers. Job lives a further hundred forty years and sees four generations before dying old and full of days. The ending gives this particular man restoration without promising that every righteous sufferer will receive the same timetable or material increase. Its deeper correction has already been spoken: suffering did not prove the friends' accusations, and God's wisdom exceeded the explanations of every disputant. Job has been humbled, heard and publicly accepted. His story calls readers towards reverence, integrity and compassion, refusing both a faith reduced to prosperity and a counsel that protects its own certainty by blaming those in pain."], "lesson": "Receive God's correction without surrendering truth, and let mercy extend towards those who have failed you. Never turn Job's restoration into a formula for judging another sufferer's future.", "question": "How can the ending of Job change the way I listen, advise or pray for someone living with unanswered suffering?"},
  {"id": 97500, "title": "Wisdom Calls Above the Crowd", "subtitle": "Learning begins with the fear of the Lord", "text": "Proverbs 1:1–33", "chapters": [[19, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["Proverbs opens by naming Solomon, David's son and king of Israel, and explaining why these sayings have been gathered. They teach wisdom, justice, fair judgement and discretion. Young people need this instruction, and people already considered wise must keep listening. The foundation is the fear of the Lord. Knowledge begins with reverence for God and a willingness to receive correction. A son is therefore urged to hear both his father's teaching and his mother's instruction. Their counsel is pictured as an ornament of honour, something worth carrying into public life rather than leaving behind at home.", "The first temptation comes through companions who offer belonging and quick wealth. They propose an attack on innocent people, confident that their victims can be swallowed up and their possessions divided among the group. Their invitation to share one purse hides the violence required to fill it. The father tells his son to keep his feet away from their path. Greed has made these men unable to see the trap they are preparing for themselves. They expect to seize another person's life, yet their own lives are endangered by the course they have chosen.", "Another voice then rises in the streets. Wisdom is presented as a woman calling openly at busy places and city gates. Her instruction is available where ordinary choices are made. She asks how long the inexperienced will cling to their inexperience, mockers enjoy scorn and fools hate knowledge. She offers understanding to those who turn at her rebuke. The seriousness of the warning comes from repeated refusal. People have ignored her outstretched hand, dismissed her counsel and rejected correction until the consequences of their choices arrive like a storm.", "Wisdom describes a time when a late appeal cannot undo the harvest already growing from those refusals. The laughter in this poetic warning expresses the terrible reversal of people who mocked counsel and then discover that their confidence was empty. It gives readers no permission to enjoy another person's distress. Those who despised the fear of the Lord eat the fruit of their own way. Those who listen are invited into a different security. The chapter places the young listener between two calls, the companions promising stolen gain and wisdom offering a teachable life. The decision begins before the violent plan succeeds or disaster becomes visible. It begins with whose voice is allowed to guide the next step, and whose correction is welcomed along the way."], "lesson": "Reverence for God makes correction welcome and exposes the cost hidden inside an attractive invitation. Choices about companions and money belong to spiritual obedience.", "question": "Which voice are you allowing to shape a decision that promises quick gain or acceptance?"},
  {"id": 97501, "title": "Searching for Wisdom as for Hidden Treasure", "subtitle": "Understanding becomes a guard for the path", "text": "Proverbs 2:1–22", "chapters": [[19, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The father continues with an invitation that requires an answer. His son must receive the words, store the commandments, incline his ear and apply his heart. Wisdom is sought with the seriousness of someone searching for silver or hidden treasure. The picture rules out casual interest. A person may admire wise sayings yet never let them interrupt an appetite or correct a plan. This search involves prayer, attention and effort together. Its promised discovery is the fear of the Lord and the knowledge of God. The treasure is more than a clever technique for avoiding embarrassment. It is a life learning to see its way under God's authority.", "The Lord is the giver of wisdom. Knowledge and understanding come from him, and he guards the paths of justice and the way of his faithful people. Human effort therefore remains dependent on divine generosity. The learner does not dig wisdom out of himself by becoming increasingly confident in his own opinions. As understanding enters the heart and knowledge becomes welcome, discretion begins to preserve him. He learns to recognise righteousness, justice and fairness. The change reaches his judgement and his desires, giving him reasons to prefer a good path when a crooked one appears profitable.", "One danger is the company of people whose speech is twisted and whose pleasure is doing evil. They have left upright paths for darkness and now celebrate the very wrong that should trouble them. Their companionship can make wrongdoing feel ordinary. Another danger is the flattering invitation of a woman who has abandoned the companion of her youth and forgotten her covenant before God. The warning concerns covenant betrayal and sexual unfaithfulness. It should not become suspicion of women generally or hostility to people from another nation. The danger described is an invitation to participate in faithlessness, supported by words designed to quiet the conscience.", "Her house is described as descending towards death. The language makes the direction of the path plain before the learner takes it. Wisdom guards him by helping him see where an invitation leads, especially when its immediate attraction hides that end. The chapter closes by setting the path of good people beside the removal of the wicked from the land. These covenant-shaped pictures of life and inheritance give moral weight to the choice. The listener is being taught where he belongs and what sort of company he should seek. Wisdom becomes protective as he receives it, prays for it and walks according to it, rather than merely knowing that protection would be useful."], "lesson": "Ask God for understanding and give his instruction sustained attention. Discernment helps a believer recognise the direction of a tempting path before taking it.", "question": "What habit would help you seek wisdom with the seriousness you give to something valuable?"},
  {"id": 97502, "title": "Trusting God in the Ways of Ordinary Life", "subtitle": "Faithfulness, correction and generosity belong together", "text": "Proverbs 3:1–35", "chapters": [[19, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["A son is asked to keep his father's teaching within his heart. Mercy and truth are to remain with him like a visible ornament and a lasting inscription. The chapter connects these qualities with peace, favour and a life ordered well before God and other people. It then addresses the source of confidence beneath daily choices. The listener must trust the Lord with his whole heart, acknowledge him in every way and resist making his own understanding the final authority. Reverence for God leads him away from evil. This trust can be practised in decisions that seem too ordinary to require prayer, including work, possessions and the treatment of a neighbour.", "Honouring the Lord with wealth and the first of the harvest makes that trust concrete. The picture of full barns expresses the blessing of life under God's care. It is wisdom for faithful living, not a contract by which a payment forces God to make someone wealthy. The next instruction concerns correction, showing that a loved child may also experience painful training. The son must neither despise the Lord's discipline nor become weary under it. Correction can belong to a father's delight in his child. The passage does not authorise calling every illness or bereavement a punishment for a hidden offence.", "Wisdom is praised as more valuable than silver, gold or precious stones. Her ways lead towards peace, and she is pictured as a tree of life to those who hold her fast. The Lord's own work in establishing the earth and heavens displays wisdom, understanding and knowledge. A person receiving wisdom is learning to live in keeping with the Creator's order. Sound judgement and discretion help him walk steadily and rest without needless dread. The confidence described is rooted in the Lord, whose care gives a firmer foundation than the pretence that danger can never arise.", "The chapter then tests that confidence in relationships. A person able to give help must not withhold what is due or send a neighbour away with an unnecessary promise of tomorrow. Someone living trustfully nearby must not become the target of a secret harmful plan. Quarrels without cause and envy of violent people both contradict the path being commended. Oppressors may appear powerful, but their conduct cannot be adopted without sharing its wrong. The closing contrast concerns the Lord's opposition to perverseness and pride, and his favour towards the upright and humble. Trust grows visible through honest worship, teachable endurance, prompt generosity and the refusal to make another person's vulnerability an opportunity for gain."], "lesson": "Trust in the Lord changes how we receive correction, handle possessions and respond when a neighbour needs help we can give.", "question": "Where does trust in God need to become a practical act of faithfulness today?"},
  {"id": 97503, "title": "Keeping the Heart and Watching the Path", "subtitle": "Wisdom is received, treasured and followed", "text": "Proverbs 4:1–27", "chapters": [[19, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The speaker calls children to listen and then remembers being a son himself. His father taught him to hold instruction within his heart, keep the commandments and seek wisdom. The counsel has passed through generations, but each learner must receive it personally. Family connection cannot do the listening on a child's behalf. Wisdom must be acquired, loved and retained. The language of honour, a garland and a crown shows the worth of what is being offered. The father wants his children to value understanding enough to keep it when another course would win easier approval or quicker satisfaction.", "He describes himself as a guide who has shown a good way. Instruction provides room for steady walking and running, while the path of the wicked must be avoided deliberately. The warning becomes urgent because wrongdoing has become habitual among those companions. They cannot rest unless they have harmed someone, and violence has become like their daily food and drink. Their way is not a harmless alternative that a young person can visit without being affected. It trains the desires. Avoiding it may require turning away before a conversation, arrangement or friendship carries the listener further than he intended.", "The two paths are pictured through light and darkness. The way of the righteous is like the growing light of dawn, moving towards the full day. The wicked stumble in darkness without understanding what trips them. These images describe the direction and character of the paths. They do not imply that a faithful person understands every difficulty immediately or that suffering proves someone has chosen darkness. The father is teaching the value of a course governed by instruction. As light increases, a person can see and walk with greater steadiness. Refusal of wisdom makes the way increasingly difficult to judge.", "The final appeal gathers the whole person into this learning. The ear attends, the eyes keep the words in view, and the heart stores them. Guarding the heart is essential because the course of life flows from it. The mouth must reject crooked speech, the eyes look straight ahead, and the feet take a considered path. Inner intention and outward behaviour belong together. A guarded heart is therefore more than private sincerity while conduct remains careless. It is a heart that receives God's instruction and directs words, attention and movement accordingly. The father ends with the practical demand to turn the foot away from evil. Wisdom is cherished within and then followed in the next visible step."], "lesson": "Guarding the heart includes what we hear, desire, say and repeatedly choose. Faithfulness grows through deliberate attention to the path before us.", "question": "Which part of your daily path needs a deliberate turn away from something that is training your heart badly?"},
  {"id": 97504, "title": "Faithfulness to the Wife of Your Youth", "subtitle": "Seeing beyond the first sweetness of temptation", "text": "Proverbs 5:1–23", "chapters": [[19, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The father asks his son to attend closely because the attraction he describes works through words and desire. The speech of an unfaithful woman seems sweet and smooth, yet its end is bitter and destructive. The warning looks beyond the first welcome to the direction of the relationship. The son is told to stay far from her house. The responsibility to keep his way faithful rests with him. This teaching cannot excuse a man by blaming a woman's appearance, nor can it make every relationship between men and women suspicious. It addresses the deliberate crossing of a covenant boundary and the refusal to consider where it leads.", "The father imagines the losses that may follow. Honour, years of labour, strength and possessions pass into other hands. The person who once dismissed instruction finally looks back in grief and recognises how persistently he rejected teachers and correction. The warning brings that later perspective into the present, while a choice can still be made. It exposes a particular deception of temptation. Pleasure appears immediate and private, while its costs seem distant and unlikely. The passage makes those costs visible. Betrayal reaches the body, household, community and conscience, even when those involved initially expect to keep it contained.", "A different picture follows, drawn from water belonging to one's own household. The son is called to delight in his own wife and rejoice in the wife of his youth. The language speaks warmly of marital affection and bodily intimacy. Faithfulness is presented as a relationship to cherish, with desire directed towards the spouse to whom one is joined. The picture refuses the idea that marriage should receive only duty while excitement is sought elsewhere. Mutual affection, gratitude and a sustained willingness to enjoy life together form part of the positive calling behind the prohibition of adultery.", "The closing reason reaches beyond whether another person discovers the affair. The Lord sees human paths and weighs their course. Secret wrongdoing has never been hidden from him. The image of a sinner caught in the cords of his own sins shows how repeated choices can become captivity. The person who refused instruction is carried further by the folly he welcomed. This warning is meant to awaken the learner before that pattern is established. The chapter asks him to choose distance from the tempting door, honesty before God and active delight in a faithful marriage. Its severity serves a good gift, the preservation of trust and love rather than the pursuit of pleasure at someone else's expense."], "lesson": "Faithfulness involves avoiding a destructive invitation and tending the love and trust of marriage. God's knowledge of our paths gives secrecy no refuge.", "question": "What boundary or act of care would strengthen faithfulness in your present circumstances?"},
  {"id": 97505, "title": "The Ant, the False Witness and the Burning Coal", "subtitle": "Wisdom attends to promises, work and hidden desires", "text": "Proverbs 6:1–35", "chapters": [[19, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The chapter begins with a person trapped by a financial promise. He has pledged himself for someone else and now stands bound by his own words. The father's counsel is urgent. He must humble himself and seek release rather than sleep as though the obligation will disappear. The images of a hunted animal and a bird in a snare show the seriousness of an unwise commitment. Help for another person should be thoughtful and honest about what can actually be carried. A generous intention does not remove the consequences of a promise, especially when it places a household's basic security beyond the promiser's control.", "The lazy person is sent to observe an ant. Without an overseer standing above it, the ant prepares food in the proper season. Its example challenges repeated postponement and the desire for a little more sleep while necessary work remains undone. Poverty may arrive with the force of a robber. This is a warning about neglect, not a claim that every poor person has been idle. The next portrait concerns someone actively devising harm. Crooked speech, secret signals and a heart planning mischief all serve the sowing of discord. The harm is organised, even when the outward gestures appear small.", "A list then names what the Lord hates. Pride, lying, the shedding of innocent blood, a scheming heart, feet hurrying towards evil, false testimony and the spreading of strife belong together. Religious language cannot make these things acceptable. The father's instruction and the mother's teaching are therefore to be kept close, guiding the listener when he walks, rests and wakes. Commandment is pictured as a lamp, and correction as a path towards life. Instruction protects by exposing wrong before it becomes an action someone later tries to excuse or hide.", "The closing warning returns to adultery. Desire must be confronted within the heart, and seductive appearance cannot be allowed to cancel the demands of faithfulness. Carrying fire against one's clothes or walking on hot coals will burn. The comparison exposes the claim that betrayal can be managed without damage. A hungry thief may be understood, yet still owes restitution. Adultery brings another kind of wound, violating a neighbour's marriage and provoking grief, dishonour and anger that money cannot simply remove. The passage describes the seriousness of jealousy without authorising revenge. Across its varied subjects, the chapter joins wisdom to accountable promises, timely labour, truthful relationships and the refusal to treat a destructive desire as harmless."], "lesson": "Small choices about obligations, diligence, truth and desire shape the security of a whole life. Wise correction deserves attention before damage is done.", "question": "Which warning here calls for prompt action rather than another delay?"},
  {"id": 97506, "title": "The Young Man Near the Wrong Door", "subtitle": "A flattering invitation hides the end of the road", "text": "Proverbs 7:1–27", "chapters": [[19, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The father asks his son to treasure instruction as carefully as the pupil of his eye. The commandments are to be bound upon his fingers and written upon his heart. Wisdom should be as familiar as a sister and understanding as a close relation. This closeness matters because another kind of intimacy is about to be offered. The chapter does not begin at the moment of wrongdoing. It begins with what has been allowed to occupy the inner life. A person who keeps good instruction distant becomes more vulnerable when a persuasive voice comes near and offers an apparently private pleasure.", "The speaker describes looking from a window and seeing a young man among the inexperienced. He lacks judgement and walks near a woman's corner, taking the road towards her house as daylight fades. She meets him with boldness and calculated speech. Her restless movements have brought her into the street, where she seizes and kisses him. She mentions peace offerings and completed vows, then claims to have come especially to find him. Religious language and personal flattery stand beside the temptation. Neither her talk of worship nor her insistence that he is uniquely wanted makes the invitation faithful.", "She describes a carefully prepared bed, fine coverings and fragrances, then invites him to a night of pleasure. Her husband is away on a long journey, she says, and will return at an appointed time. The argument rests on opportunity and concealment. Someone else's absence is made to sound like permission. The young man yields under repeated persuasion and follows. The description then changes abruptly. He is like an ox moving towards slaughter, a creature caught in a trap, or a bird hurrying to a snare. The comparison reveals a danger that the attractive speech has kept out of sight. His own life is involved in the choice.", "The father returns to his children and urges them to listen. They must keep the heart from turning towards her ways and avoid wandering onto her paths. Many have been wounded through this course, including those who seemed strong enough to resist. The story therefore gives no grounds for pride in one's supposed invulnerability. It also leaves the young man's responsibility clear. He chose the road, entertained the invitation and followed. The lesson concerns the combined work of direction, attention and desire. Wisdom is received before dusk, boundaries are kept before the door is reached, and flattering speech is tested by the faithfulness of what it asks a person to do."], "lesson": "Temptation often makes opportunity sound like permission. Remembering the end of a path helps us refuse the first steps towards it.", "question": "What invitation should you judge by its direction rather than by how flattering it sounds?"},
  {"id": 97507, "title": "Wisdom Speaks at the Gates", "subtitle": "The Creator's wisdom calls people towards life", "text": "Proverbs 8:1–36", "chapters": [[19, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["Wisdom again appears as a woman speaking openly, standing on high places, beside roads and at city entrances. Her call addresses ordinary people, including those who lack experience or understanding. What she offers is righteous speech, truth and instruction worth more than silver, gold or jewels. There is no deceit hidden within her words. The public setting matters. Wisdom belongs in decisions about work, government, money and relationships. It is not a secret reserved for a small circle claiming unusual access to spiritual mysteries. Those who hear are asked to prefer understanding over possessions that might otherwise dominate their search.", "Wisdom describes her companions and her opposition. Prudence, knowledge, sound counsel and strength belong with her. Pride, arrogance, evil conduct and a twisted mouth are hated. Through wisdom, rulers govern and judges make just decisions. The claim measures leadership by justice rather than merely admiring the possession of power. Kings need wisdom as surely as inexperienced listeners do. Those who love and seek her are promised a good beyond passing wealth. Her fruit is better than fine gold because she leads in righteousness and justice. The way in which something is gained matters as much as the appearance of success.", "The speech reaches back before the earth's formation. Wisdom is pictured as present before the depths, springs, mountains and fields, and beside God as the heavens and the boundaries of the sea were established. She rejoices before him and delights in the inhabited world and its people. This is poetic personification within a chapter where wisdom speaks as a woman. It displays the deep connection between wisdom and the Creator's work. The passage should not be turned into a simple account of a separate created deity or used carelessly to settle every question about Christ's eternal nature. Its immediate invitation is to recognise the worth of the wisdom by which God orders his world.", "The speech returns from creation to the children listening now. They are blessed if they keep wisdom's ways and refuse to reject instruction. The eager listener is pictured watching daily at her gates and waiting beside her doors. Learning is sustained attention rather than a single moment of enthusiasm. Finding wisdom means finding life and receiving favour from the Lord, while rejecting her damages one's own soul. The chapter joins a great vision of creation to the next human decision. The wisdom celebrated before mountains and seas calls a person to abandon pride, tell the truth, judge fairly and listen again today."], "lesson": "God's wisdom gives moral direction to ordinary life and public responsibility. Its value becomes clear when we prefer righteousness to wealth and pride.", "question": "Where do you need to value truthful, just counsel more than an impressive result?"},
  {"id": 97508, "title": "Two Houses and Two Invitations", "subtitle": "The response to correction reveals the listener", "text": "Proverbs 9:1–18", "chapters": [[19, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["Wisdom has built a house and prepared a generous meal. Seven pillars, a furnished table and servants sent to call guests picture a settled and abundant welcome. Her invitation reaches people who lack understanding. They need not pretend to be accomplished before entering. They must, however, leave their foolish course and begin walking in the way of understanding. The meal represents instruction that is meant to sustain life. Wisdom offers more than a view to admire from the road. The hearer is called to come, receive and allow the invitation to change the direction in which he has been travelling.", "The chapter pauses to consider correction. A mocker may respond to rebuke by insulting the person who gives it. A wise person receives correction with love and becomes wiser through teaching. The distinction concerns teachability. Someone with great knowledge can behave as a mocker when a particular failure is named, while someone inexperienced can begin wisely by listening. The warning also requires judgement from the teacher. Repeating a rebuke to a person bent on contempt may achieve only further abuse. Wisdom involves considering the hearer and the moment, rather than treating every argument as an obligation to keep speaking indefinitely.", "The fear of the Lord is again named as the beginning of wisdom, and knowledge of the Holy One as understanding. Learning is rooted in a relationship of reverence and accountability. Each listener bears the consequences of receiving or mocking the instruction. The promise of multiplied days describes wisdom's life-giving course, while the warning makes personal responsibility plain. No companion can do one's listening in one's place. The house stands open, but an invitation received by the ear must also be answered by the way the person chooses to live. Wisdom's benefits cannot be separated from her teaching.", "Another woman now calls from a seat at her own door. Folly is noisy, ignorant and confident in what she does not understand. She repeats a welcome to the inexperienced, but her meal is described as stolen water and secret bread. Its appeal rests on what is forbidden and concealed. The traveller is encouraged to imagine that secrecy adds sweetness. He does not see that her guests are among the dead. The two houses therefore offer similar sounding invitations with opposite destinations. The chapter asks readers to judge the character of the welcome, the willingness to receive correction and the end of the path. A voice can be loud, hospitable and attractive while leading away from life."], "lesson": "A welcome should be tested by what it invites us to become. Receiving correction is one practical sign that we have chosen wisdom's table.", "question": "How do you respond when good correction interrupts something you want to enjoy?"},
  {"id": 97509, "title": "Words, Honest Weights and a Generous Hand", "subtitle": "Righteousness strengthens the life of a community", "text": "Proverbs 10:1–11:31", "chapters": [[19, 10], [19, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The collection of short sayings associated with Solomon begins by placing a child's conduct within family life. Wisdom brings joy, while folly brings grief. The sayings then move between work, wealth, speech and character, showing how closely these matters are connected. Diligent hands gather in the season of harvest, while laziness wastes an opportunity. Wealth obtained through wickedness cannot provide lasting rescue. These sayings describe the moral direction and ordinary consequences of conduct. They are not a licence to assume that every rich person has been diligent or that every poor person has been unfaithful. Proverbs will itself name injustice as a cause of deprivation.", "The mouth receives repeated attention. Righteous speech is compared to a well of life and to valuable silver, while destructive words hide violence or spread slander. Hatred keeps stirring conflict, but love can cover offences instead of continually reopening them. This covering must be understood alongside the condemnation of lies and false testimony. Love does not require concealing abuse or preventing a harmed person from receiving protection and justice. Restraint in speech, willingness to receive instruction and truthful testimony all contribute to a trustworthy community. The lazy messenger, by contrast, is like smoke in the eyes of the person who depends on him.", "The next chapter opens at the marketplace. False balances are hateful to the Lord, while accurate weights delight him. Worship cannot be detached from a transaction in which someone deliberately gives less than has been paid for. Pride brings shame, and integrity guides. Riches cannot settle the account when judgement comes. The upright person contributes to the well-being of a city, while a hypocritical mouth can destroy a neighbour. Keeping confidence, avoiding contempt and seeking wise counsel all protect relationships. Even a promise to guarantee another person's debt requires care, because a careless undertaking may bring suffering to those expected to carry it.", "Generosity is then placed beside grasping self-interest. One person gives and increases, while another withholds what ought to be shared and becomes poorer. Someone who refreshes others is refreshed. Hoarding grain when neighbours need food draws condemnation, while making it available brings blessing. The chapter also contrasts outward beauty with discretion, and trust in riches with a flourishing righteous life. Its closing images include a tree of life and the wisdom of winning people. Together the sayings ask what a life produces around it. Honest trade, measured speech, mercy and generosity make righteousness visible in households, friendships and public life, where its fruit can nourish people who might otherwise be harmed or neglected."], "lesson": "Integrity includes truthful speech, fair business and generosity. A righteous life should bring practical good to the people who meet it.", "question": "Who would benefit if you became more careful with your words or more open with what you can share?"},
  {"id": 97510, "title": "Correction, Patient Work and Faithful Companions", "subtitle": "Wisdom is learned through habits that bear fruit", "text": "Proverbs 12:1–13:25", "chapters": [[19, 12], [19, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The person who loves instruction loves knowledge, while the rejection of correction reveals a stubborn refusal to learn. This contrast runs through sayings about households, work and speech. A capable wife strengthens her husband, and the root of the righteous endures beyond the apparent success of wicked schemes. Outward display is questioned. It is better to live modestly with actual provision than to advertise importance while lacking bread. The righteous person even cares for an animal's life. Wisdom reaches the treatment of a creature that cannot repay kindness or argue its case, exposing how empty a claim to goodness becomes when it leaves cruelty undisturbed.", "Diligent cultivation is set beside chasing empty pursuits. A person's hands and words both bear fruit. A fool assumes his own way is right, while a wise person listens to counsel. Sudden anger quickly displays itself, but prudence can pass over an insult. Speech may pierce like a sword or bring healing, and truth stands in contrast with the short life of a lie. The Lord delights in faithful dealing. An anxious heart can be weighed down, and a good word can make it glad. The sayings give the listener responsibility for the effect of words spoken into another person's difficulty.", "The next chapter continues with a son who hears instruction and a mocker who rejects rebuke. Guarding the mouth guards life. Wishing alone does not accomplish the work that diligence undertakes. Wealth gathered gradually is contrasted with wealth obtained through empty or dishonest means. Another saying recognises the pain of hope deferred and the joy of a desire fulfilled. These observations allow room for real human disappointment. Wisdom does not demand that a faithful person deny the ache of waiting. It asks the learner to keep receiving instruction, speaking truth and making careful choices while the outcome remains incomplete.", "Companionship gives the teaching a practical direction. Walking with wise people helps a person grow wise, while the company of fools brings harm. The good person considers future generations, yet the text also acknowledges food lost from the fields of the poor through injustice. This prevents an easy equation between poverty and laziness. Parental discipline is described through the ancient image of the rod, stressing the responsibility to correct a child rather than abandon him to folly. It must not become permission for cruelty, injury or uncontrolled anger. Across these sayings, wisdom appears in patient work, teachable relationships, honest provision and care strong enough to guide without becoming harsh."], "lesson": "Receiving correction and choosing wise companions help good intentions become steady habits. The suffering of the poor must also be considered in the light of injustice.", "question": "Which relationship helps you receive truthful counsel, and how can you give that relationship proper attention?"},
  {"id": 97511, "title": "The Quiet Answer and the Listening Heart", "subtitle": "Reverence for God changes how neighbours are treated", "text": "Proverbs 14:1–15:33", "chapters": [[19, 14], [19, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["A wise woman builds her household, while folly can tear it down through the very hands that should sustain it. The sayings move from home to field, courtroom and public life. An empty stall is easy to keep clean, but an ox supplies the strength needed for a harvest. Wisdom therefore accepts necessary effort and inconvenience rather than mistaking the absence of work for success. Faithful witnesses tell the truth. The prudent consider their steps instead of believing every claim. A path may seem right and still lead towards death. Confidence needs examination when the direction of life contradicts God's instruction.", "The heart's hidden experience also receives attention. A person knows his own bitterness, and laughter may coexist with sorrow. This calls for humility when judging someone else's condition from appearance alone. The poor may be deserted while the wealthy attract friends, but despising a needy neighbour is called sin. To oppress the poor is to insult their Maker, while mercy honours him. The fear of the Lord provides refuge and is pictured as a fountain of life. Its public fruit includes patience, truthful testimony and righteousness that benefits a nation. A society's moral condition matters beyond the reputation of its rulers.", "The next chapter begins with the effect of an answer. Gentle speech can turn anger aside, while a harsh word adds fuel. The Lord sees both evil and good, including motives concealed beneath respectable behaviour. The sacrifice of the wicked cannot substitute for an upright life, and prayer is not detached from conduct. Wise lips share knowledge. A wholesome tongue gives life, while damaging speech can crush the spirit. The contrast between a small meal with love and a feast with hatred brings the teaching home. Plenty loses its attractiveness when those sharing a table fear or despise one another.", "The remainder joins teachability to humility. Counsel helps plans become established, and a timely word gives joy. Greed and bribery trouble a household, while the Lord upholds the vulnerable widow against the proud. A righteous person considers an answer instead of pouring out whatever anger supplies. Good news can refresh, and correction can increase understanding when the ear is willing to hear it. The final saying places humility before honour. These chapters do not describe a weak or careless gentleness. They describe strength that listens, speaks truth, restrains anger and recognises another person's dignity. The ordinary conversation becomes a place where reverence for God is either practised or contradicted."], "lesson": "A gentle answer, fair testimony and mercy towards the poor express the same reverence for God. Wisdom listens before it speaks and considers the heart it may wound.", "question": "Where could a patient, truthful answer bring peace without avoiding an issue that needs attention?"},
  {"id": 97512, "title": "The Lord Weighs the Heart", "subtitle": "Humble plans, faithful friendship and a restrained tongue", "text": "Proverbs 16:1–17:28", "chapters": [[19, 16], [19, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["People form plans and often regard their own motives as sound, but the Lord weighs the heart. The sayings invite the learner to commit work to him and recognise his authority over the course of events. Planning remains necessary, yet it must be offered with humility. A little gained righteously is better than great revenue obtained through wrong. Kings also stand under this standard. A throne should be established by justice, and honest lips should be welcome before it. References to royal authority describe its proper task and its serious power, not a promise that every ruler's decision is righteous or beyond question.", "Wisdom is again valued above gold. Pride goes before destruction, while a humble place among the lowly is better than sharing the plunder of the proud. Understanding shapes persuasive, gracious speech, and pleasant words are compared to honey that refreshes. The sayings nevertheless refuse to make sweetness alone a test of truth. A way can seem right while ending in death. A whisperer divides close friends, a violent person draws a neighbour towards harm, and a schemer uses even small gestures to advance evil. Discernment must consider what a speaker is leading someone to do.", "The chapter's closing contrasts honourable age with uncontrolled force. Restraining anger is greater than conquering a city. Human choices and apparent chance remain under the Lord's knowledge and rule. The next chapter then brings wisdom into a house where a dry morsel with peace is preferable to abundance with strife. God tests hearts as a refiner tests precious metal. Mocking the poor insults their Maker, and enjoying another person's disaster is condemned. Faithfulness within a household can be displayed even by a servant whose wisdom exceeds that of an irresponsible son. Character matters more than inherited position.", "Friendship and judgement occupy much of what follows. Love may pass over an offence, while repeated retelling separates friends. A true friend remains loving in adversity. Early restraint can stop a quarrel before it spreads like released water. The text condemns both acquitting the wicked and condemning the innocent, and specifically names the bribe that corrupts justice. Its observation that gifts can open doors is therefore not permission to purchase a dishonest verdict. A cheerful heart is compared to medicine, while a crushed spirit drains strength. Finally, restraint in speech appears again. Even a fool can seem wise by keeping silent. The learner is called to seek actual understanding, whose presence is shown through faithful company, honest judgement and words measured before they leave the mouth."], "lesson": "Humility places plans before God and leaves room for correction. Friendship, leadership and speech all need the same commitment to truth and self-control.", "question": "Which plan or conversation needs you to examine your motives before asking God to bless the outcome?"},
  {"id": 97513, "title": "Listening Before Giving an Answer", "subtitle": "Wisdom considers the lonely, the accused and the poor", "text": "Proverbs 18:1–19:29", "chapters": [[19, 18], [19, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The opening sayings expose a person who pursues his own desire in isolation and a fool more interested in displaying his opinion than in understanding. Speech can be a deep and useful source, but it can also trap its speaker or wound a neighbour through gossip. In judgement, favouring the wicked at the expense of the righteous is condemned. Carelessness at work is placed beside destruction because neglected duties can cause real loss. The name of the Lord is pictured as a strong tower, while wealth is a high wall in the rich person's imagination. These forms of confidence are not equally secure.", "Answering before listening brings folly and shame. The first person to present a case may sound convincing until another examines it. These sayings require patience from anyone asked to settle a disagreement. Sympathy for a speaker should not remove the need for fair inquiry. The chapter also recognises the difficulty of a wounded spirit and the strength of barriers created by offence. Words have the power to sustain or damage lives, which explains the image of death and life within the tongue's power. This concerns the serious fruit of speech, not a claim that every sentence spoken creates a future by an automatic spiritual mechanism.", "The next chapter honours the poor person who walks in integrity above the dishonest fool. Hastiness without knowledge brings trouble, and human folly may ruin a path before its owner blames the Lord. Wealth attracts people who disappear when poverty arrives. These observations uncover shallow loyalty rather than approving it. False witnesses face accountability, while discretion can delay anger and overlook an insult. A prudent wife is received as a gift from the Lord. The sayings about family grief name conduct that damages a household. They should never be turned into contempt for women or a refusal to hear an actual complaint.", "Kindness to the poor is described as lending to the Lord, who regards what is given. Instruction should be received while there is hope, and parental correction must serve a child's good. Rescuing a habitually angry person from consequences may simply prepare for another repetition if nothing changes. Many plans arise within the human heart, but the Lord's purpose stands. The closing warnings return to false testimony, scorn and the shame of mistreating parents. Across both chapters, wisdom asks the hearer to slow down enough to know what is true, see who has been overlooked and distinguish loyal love from the company purchased by money. Understanding grows through listening, and its worth becomes visible in a fair and merciful response."], "lesson": "Listening carefully is an act of justice. Trust in God should lead to patient inquiry, steady friendship and practical concern for people without influence.", "question": "Whose account or difficulty do you need to hear more fully before reaching a judgement?"},
  {"id": 97514, "title": "Justice Matters More Than a Public Offering", "subtitle": "Integrity in trade, power and private intention", "text": "Proverbs 20:1–21:31", "chapters": [[19, 20], [19, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["Wine is described as a mocker and strong drink as a source of turmoil, warning against a confidence that leaves judgement impaired. Honour is found in stepping away from needless strife. Diligence requires working in the proper season rather than allowing discomfort to become a standing excuse. The sayings then ask a searching question about human character. Many people announce their own goodness, but actual faithfulness is harder to find. No one can honestly claim to have purified his own heart by self-congratulation. The Lord made the hearing ear and seeing eye, and the learner is called to use both with humility.", "Dishonest weights and measures receive repeated condemnation. A buyer who belittles goods and then boasts about the bargain illustrates the gap between words and intention. Bread obtained deceitfully may taste sweet at first and become like gravel afterwards. Wise counsel should guide serious plans, and gossiping company cannot be trusted with confidence. Rash vows bring a snare when a person investigates the cost only after promising. The son is told to avoid taking vengeance into his own hands and wait for the Lord. Mercy and truth sustain good rule, while youth's strength and age's experience both have their proper honour.", "The next chapter places even the king's heart under God's authority. People justify their own ways, but the Lord examines motives. Doing justice and righteousness is more acceptable than offering a sacrifice while refusing to obey. The diligent plan carefully, while haste and deceit bring ruin. A household filled with quarrelling is shown as a miserable place despite its space and comfort. The emphasis is the destructive conduct of contention, not a judgement that women as a whole are its source. Closing one's ears to the poor also has spiritual seriousness. A person who refuses another's cry should not imagine that his own cry can be detached from that refusal.", "The remaining sayings contrast restraint, generosity and truthful listening with greed, anger and a hardened face. Wisdom may overcome a strength that looks greater, while guarding the tongue protects against needless trouble. Religious activity offered with an evil intention is especially offensive because it attempts to bring wrongdoing into worship. A false witness is condemned, and the person who truly listens is commended. The final picture joins preparation and dependence. A horse may be made ready for battle, but deliverance belongs to the Lord. Faith does not remove responsible work. It removes the claim that preparation, influence or public offerings can make obedience unnecessary or place the outcome wholly within human control."], "lesson": "God examines the motives behind trade, worship and leadership. Responsible preparation should be joined to justice, mercy and dependence on him.", "question": "Is there a public act of religion or generosity that needs to be matched by private honesty and fair dealing?"},
  {"id": 97515, "title": "A Good Name and a Heart That Can Be Taught", "subtitle": "Resisting greed, harmful company and the deceptive cup", "text": "Proverbs 22:1–23:35", "chapters": [[19, 22], [19, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["A good name and loving favour are placed above great wealth. Rich and poor share the same Maker, so possessions cannot establish a different human worth. Prudence recognises danger and takes shelter, while inexperience walks on without considering the cost. Training a child is presented as a serious, lasting responsibility. The saying commends the formative power of guidance without making parents sovereign over every later choice. Debt, greed and careless pledges can bring bondage, while generosity shares bread with the poor. The repeated call is to receive instruction inwardly until truthful words are ready when they are needed.", "The words of the wise forbid robbing the poor precisely because they are vulnerable. The Lord will plead their cause. Angry companions can train a learner into their own habits, and guaranteeing debts without means to pay may cost even his bed. Moving a boundary marker steals an inheritance under the appearance of a small adjustment. Diligence and skill, by contrast, prepare a person for responsible service. These counsels unite religious trust with honest property dealings, measured promises and careful friendship. They expose the temptation to gain an advantage from someone who lacks the power to resist.", "A table with a ruler introduces another danger. Appetite must not surrender judgement to impressive food or influence. The sharp image of a knife at the throat calls for restraint, not self-injury. Riches can fly away, and a grudging host may speak warmly while inwardly resenting every portion. The saying about what such a person thinks in his heart belongs to this picture of false hospitality. It is not a formula that thoughts alone create material success. The learner is again told to protect the fatherless, value truth and instruction, listen to ageing parents and refuse envy of sinners. Discipline of children is described in ancient physical terms, whose concern for correction cannot justify violence that injures or terrifies a child.", "The chapter ends with an extended portrait of drunkenness. Sorrow, quarrels, wounds and distorted sight accompany the cup whose appearance first attracts. The drinker is compared to someone lying in the sea or high upon a mast, exposed and unsteady. Even after being struck, he expects to seek the drink again. This is a sobering picture of captivity within repeated desire. It stands beside warnings about sexual betrayal, gluttony and the heart's need for direction. The father asks for the son's heart because external respectability alone cannot govern an appetite. Wisdom is received as a beloved guide, shaping what the learner values, whom he trusts and what he is willing to refuse."], "lesson": "A teachable heart values truth above appetite, money and influence. Protecting the vulnerable and refusing harmful company both belong to that learning.", "question": "Which appetite or ambition most needs to be brought under truthful instruction?"},
  {"id": 97516, "title": "The Field Covered with Thorns", "subtitle": "Wisdom acts when help and honest work are needed", "text": "Proverbs 24:1–34", "chapters": [[19, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The learner is told to resist envy of evil people. Their apparent success cannot make destructive intentions worth copying. A house is built through wisdom and established through understanding, while knowledge fills its rooms with what is valuable. Strength includes wise counsel, especially when a decision carries danger for others. The chapter then challenges weakness in adversity and the refusal to rescue people being led towards death. Claiming not to have known cannot deceive the God who weighs hearts. Wisdom includes responsibility for a neighbour in peril. It is diminished when a person uses uncertainty or inconvenience as a comfortable reason never to help.", "Wisdom is compared to honey, whose sweetness makes its goodness immediately understandable. The learner is encouraged to value understanding with the same readiness. An evil person must not prey upon the dwelling of the righteous. Though the just fall repeatedly, they rise again, while wickedness leads towards calamity. In this setting the falling concerns adversity and attack, so the saying should not be reduced to permission for deliberate, repeated sin. The command that follows also matters. The listener must not rejoice when an enemy stumbles. Another person's suffering is not made righteous entertainment by the existence of a quarrel.", "The closing collection names additional sayings of the wise. Partiality in judgement is wrong, and declaring a wicked person righteous damages public confidence. Honest rebuke serves the community, while a truthful answer has the warmth of a sincere greeting. Practical order matters too. A person should prepare the necessary work in the field before building the household that will depend on it. Testimony against a neighbour must have a truthful basis. The learner must refuse the plan to repay someone simply by doing the same harm in return. Justice and revenge differ in their purpose, evidence and restraint.", "The speaker finally passes the field and vineyard of a lazy man. Thorns have spread, nettles cover the ground and the stone wall is broken. No single spectacular act has caused the ruin described. The result has grown through a repeated preference for a little more sleep and delay. The observer looks carefully and receives instruction. This willingness to learn from what he sees is itself an example of wisdom. The chapter has moved from rescue and public justice to an overgrown field, joining urgent moral responsibility with steady ordinary work. Both require a person to respond while action is possible. Neglect can injure through what is left undone as surely as malice injures through what is deliberately planned."], "lesson": "Wisdom responds to danger, judges fairly and does necessary work in its proper time. Repeated neglect can become a serious form of harm.", "question": "What responsibility is becoming more difficult because you keep leaving it for later?"},
  {"id": 97517, "title": "A Word Fitly Spoken and a Fire Left Unfed", "subtitle": "Judgement is needed in speech, company and restraint", "text": "Proverbs 25:1–26:28", "chapters": [[19, 25], [19, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["A new section identifies proverbs of Solomon copied by the men of Hezekiah, king of Judah. The opening sayings consider the searching responsibility of rulers. Removing corrupt people from a king's presence is compared to refining silver, so that righteous government can become established. The learner must not push himself into a place of honour. An invitation to come nearer is better than public humiliation after self-promotion. Disputes also require restraint. A person should address the neighbour concerned rather than rush to a wider quarrel and disclose private matters that may never be gathered back into confidence.", "Well-timed words are compared to gold set in silver. Wise correction received by a willing ear is an ornament, while a faithful messenger refreshes the person who depends on him. Empty promises resemble clouds without rain. Patience and gentle speech can influence a ruler, and even good things require measure. Too much honey sickens, and constant visits can exhaust hospitality. False witness is compared to a weapon. Trusting an unreliable person in trouble is like relying on a damaged limb, and cheerful songs offered without regard for a grieving heart can feel painfully out of place. Wise kindness attends to what the other person actually needs.", "An enemy who is hungry or thirsty should receive food and water. The image of coals upon his head must not become an excuse to disguise revenge as generosity. The immediate command is practical kindness, with reward left to the Lord. A righteous person yielding to wickedness is like a polluted spring, and a person without self-control resembles a city with broken walls. The following chapter examines folly through vivid comparisons. Giving honour to a fool can be as unsuitable as snow in summer. An undeserved curse is compared to a wandering bird, challenging fear of a baseless accusation as though it automatically determined someone's future.", "Two neighbouring sayings advise against answering a fool according to his folly and then advise answering him so that he does not remain wise in his own eyes. They require discernment. Sometimes imitation draws the responder into foolishness, while sometimes a careful answer exposes it. Pride that refuses correction is especially dangerous. Laziness invents excuses and congratulates itself, while interfering in someone else's quarrel is compared to grabbing a dog by its ears. A harmful trick is not excused by calling it a joke. Finally, gossip and contention feed a quarrel as wood feeds fire. The learner can help a conflict die by refusing to supply more fuel, while remembering that flattering speech may hide real hatred."], "lesson": "Wise speech considers truth, timing and the listener. Refusing gossip and needless provocation can prevent a quarrel from becoming a lasting fire.", "question": "In a present disagreement, would wisdom call you to answer carefully, remain silent or stop repeating the matter?"},
  {"id": 97518, "title": "Faithful Wounds and Mercy for the Confessing", "subtitle": "Care for friendships, work and an honest conscience", "text": "Proverbs 27:1–28:28", "chapters": [[19, 27], [19, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The learner is told not to boast about tomorrow, because a day may bring what he cannot foresee. Praise should come from another person's mouth rather than his own. Anger is heavy, and envy can be especially destructive. Friendship then receives a searching test. Open rebuke is better than a love that remains silent when truth is needed. A friend's faithful wounds differ from an enemy's flattering kisses. These sayings commend honest care, not cruelty dressed as correction. Counsel can refresh, and a nearby faithful neighbour may be more useful in a crisis than a relative who is far away.", "The chapter considers attention within relationships and work. A loud blessing given at the wrong time can be unwelcome, showing that goodwill needs consideration. Iron sharpens iron, as people affect one another through contact and counsel. Praise tests character, and desire can remain unsatisfied despite repeated gains. The closing section turns to flocks, herds and seasonal provision. A person must know their condition because riches and crowns do not last indefinitely. Careful tending provides clothing, food and support for a household. Wisdom here looks like continuing attention to the sources of daily life, rather than assuming that yesterday's provision guarantees tomorrow's.", "The next chapter contrasts the confidence of righteousness with the fear attending wickedness. It again honours a poor person with integrity above a rich person following a crooked path. Oppression can be committed by someone poor as well as by someone wealthy, and its harm is compared to rain that destroys rather than nourishes a harvest. The learner must keep the law, reject dishonest gain and resist leaders whose power consumes the vulnerable. Prayer cannot be used to compensate for a deliberate refusal to hear God's instruction. Seeking the Lord should make judgement clearer and conduct more accountable.", "Confession is then placed beside concealment. A person who hides sin will not prosper in that course, while one who confesses and forsakes it receives mercy. The call includes turning away, so confession is more than admitting something while keeping a settled intention to repeat it. Fear of the Lord keeps the heart responsive, while hardness brings danger. Faithful work is contrasted with chasing empty ventures and hurrying after wealth. Partiality, theft from parents and flattery receive condemnation. Generosity to the poor and truthful rebuke express a better way. Across both chapters, wisdom exposes false security in money, praise or concealment. A life can stand more honestly through faithful friendship, attentive labour and the humility to bring wrongdoing into the light."], "lesson": "Honest friendship and confession both require a willingness to hear unwelcome truth. Mercy is received with a turning from the wrong we acknowledge.", "question": "Is there a truth you need to receive from a faithful friend or confess before God?"},
  {"id": 97519, "title": "When the People Mourn Under Wicked Rule", "subtitle": "Justice, correction and the fear of man", "text": "Proverbs 29:1–27", "chapters": [[19, 29]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The chapter opens with a severe warning about a person repeatedly corrected who continues hardening his neck. Ruin can arrive suddenly after a long history of refusal. The issue is not the absence of warning, but the settled determination not to hear it. Public life then comes into view. Righteous authority brings gladness, while wicked rule causes people to mourn. A king establishes a country through justice, but one who accepts corrupt gifts undermines it. The moral character of leadership has consequences for people with little access to power. Their suffering must not be concealed beneath admiration for a ruler's position or outward achievements.", "The righteous person takes an interest in the cause of the poor, while the wicked refuses even to understand it. This is more demanding than a passing feeling of pity. A just response requires attention to what has happened and what protection or judgement is needed. Mockers can inflame a whole city, while wise people turn anger aside. A ruler who listens readily to lies trains a corrupt court around himself. Even a ruler's own servants become affected by the kind of report he welcomes. The poor and the oppressor both receive their sight from the Lord, which places them together under their Maker's authority.", "Correction within a household is treated as a responsibility whose neglect brings shame. The ancient sayings use the language of the rod and rebuke, expressing the need for active moral guidance. They do not give parents freedom to injure a child or pour anger upon the weak. Instruction must pursue wisdom and the child's good. Another saying warns that where there is no vision, people cast off restraint, while the keeper of the law is blessed. Its concern is the absence of divine revelation and instruction. It should not be reduced to a slogan that any ambitious personal plan guarantees success.", "The closing sayings return to speech, anger and fear. Hasty words can be more dangerous than obvious folly, and a furious person multiplies wrongdoing. Pride brings a person low, while humility receives honour. Partnership with a thief damages the partner's own life and conscience. Fear of other people becomes a snare when it governs the choices that should be made before God. Seeking a ruler's favour may seem decisive, but judgement ultimately belongs to the Lord. The chapter therefore asks the learner to remain teachable and just in both private relationships and public responsibility. Courage grows through trusting God enough to hear correction, refuse corruption and care about the person whose cause powerful people would prefer to ignore."], "lesson": "The fear of the Lord frees us to receive correction and pursue justice. Fear of people can trap a person into silence, flattery or participation in wrong.", "question": "Where is fear of someone's reaction making it harder for you to do what is right?"},
  {"id": 97520, "title": "Agur Asks for Enough", "subtitle": "Humility before God and wisdom seen in small creatures", "text": "Proverbs 30:1–33", "chapters": [[19, 30]], "start": 1, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["Agur's words begin with a confession of limited understanding. He refuses the pose of someone who has mastered the knowledge of the Holy One. Questions about ascending to heaven, gathering the wind, binding the waters and establishing the earth expose the distance between human claims and divine greatness. God's word is pure, and he is a shield to those who trust him. The listener must not add to his words and then present the addition as truth from God. This is a fitting foundation for the observations that follow. Wisdom grows where a person is willing to acknowledge what he does not know.", "Agur asks for two things. He wants falsehood removed from him, and he asks for neither poverty nor riches, but the food that is needed. He recognises danger at both extremes. Fullness might encourage him to deny his dependence on the Lord, while desperate need might tempt him to steal and dishonour God's name. The prayer examines the heart rather than treating wealth as the highest possible blessing. A warning against slandering a servant follows, then portraits of people who curse parents, imagine themselves clean, look down proudly and devour the poor. Their confidence hides a serious moral uncleanness.", "The sayings move through numbered groups drawn from human life and the natural world. The grave, the barren womb, dry earth and fire picture desires that do not easily say enough. The sorrow of childlessness appears within this image of longing, not as an accusation of guilt. Other observations express wonder at an eagle's movement, a serpent on rock, a ship at sea and the way of a man with a woman. Adultery is then exposed through the image of someone wiping her mouth and denying wrongdoing. Further sayings describe disruptive reversals within an ancient household and society. They call for attention to conduct and order without making social rank the measure of a person's worth.", "Small creatures provide positive lessons. Ants prepare food, rock-dwelling creatures find secure homes, locusts move together without a king, and a small creature can enter even royal palaces. Size is no barrier to the kind of wisdom displayed in preparation, shelter, cooperation and persistence. Other images picture a confident and orderly bearing. The final counsel returns the observer to his own conduct. If pride or evil intention has risen within him, he should cover his mouth and stop. Just as churning produces butter and pressure on the nose produces blood, forcing anger produces strife. The person who admires wisdom in creation must also learn the restraint of his own tongue."], "lesson": "Humility receives God's word without adding to it and asks for provision without making abundance an idol. Wisdom can be learned from small, ordinary things.", "question": "How could Agur's prayer for truth and enough reshape what you are asking God to give you?"},
  {"id": 97521, "title": "The Mother's Counsel and the Woman Worthy of Praise", "subtitle": "Wisdom protects justice and honours faithful work", "text": "Proverbs 31:1–31", "chapters": [[19, 31]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Proverbs", "story": ["The final chapter begins with the words of King Lemuel, taught to him by his mother. Her repeated address to her son expresses both love and the seriousness of his responsibility. He must not give his strength to relationships and conduct that destroy kings. Rulers are warned against drink that clouds judgement, because forgetting the law can mean denying justice to the afflicted. The remarks about drink for the dying and distressed recognise the setting of deep suffering. They do not cancel the wider warnings against drunkenness or make intoxication a wise answer to ordinary trouble. The ruler's immediate responsibility is to keep judgement clear.", "His mother commands him to speak for those unable to speak for themselves. He must judge righteously and defend the cause of the poor and needy. Authority is therefore measured by protection and fair judgement. Silence may preserve a ruler's convenience while abandoning the people his office should serve. The chapter's opening gives a mother's teaching a place in the formation of public leadership. Her son needs counsel that reaches beyond his own comfort, appetite and reputation. Power becomes accountable when he remembers the people whose cries might otherwise never enter the room where decisions are made.", "The poem that follows praises a woman of noble character, whose worth exceeds precious stones. Her husband trusts her, and she works for the household's good. She obtains materials, prepares food, organises provision and considers a field before purchasing it. She plants a vineyard from the fruit of her labour and recognises the value of her goods. The portrait includes judgement, enterprise, skill and physical effort. She also opens her hand to the poor and prepares her household for the coming season. The poem honours a full life of responsible work. It should not be turned into a daily checklist by which an exhausted woman is condemned for lacking the resources or circumstances of this household.", "Strength and dignity mark her bearing, and wisdom and kindness shape her speech. Her care includes the condition of the household and the needs of those who depend on it. Her children and husband praise her, while the closing verses turn attention beyond charm and beauty to the fear of the Lord. She is to receive the fruit of her labour, and her works are to speak for her in the public gates. The book ends with wisdom embodied in faithful conduct that others are called to recognise. The ruler must defend the vulnerable, and the household must honour the woman whose work sustains it. Reverence for God gives both responsibilities their lasting measure."], "lesson": "Wisdom uses authority to defend the vulnerable and gives honest recognition to faithful labour. The fear of the Lord gives character a worth that appearance cannot supply.", "question": "Whose faithful work needs your practical support and proper recognition, and whose cause needs your voice?"},
  {"id": 91000, "title": "The Family Into Which Jesus Came", "subtitle": "God's promise through generations", "text": "Matthew 1:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Matthew begins by naming Jesus Christ as the son of David and the son of Abraham. Then he traces the family line forward from Abraham. Isaac is followed by Jacob, then Judah and his brothers. At several points Matthew mentions women as well as fathers and sons. Perez and Zerah are named with Tamar. Rahab is named with Boaz, and Ruth with Obed. Ruth's son Obed is followed by Jesse, the father of David.", "From David the record goes through the house of Judah's kings. Solomon is named as the son David had by Uriah's wife. The names that follow include Hezekiah, Manasseh and Josiah. Matthew brings this part of the record to the carrying away to Babylon. A family whose members had held a throne is now marked by a national loss.", "The record continues after the exile. Jeconiah is followed by Shealtiel and Zerubbabel, and more names carry the family line towards Joseph. When Matthew comes to Joseph, the form of the record changes. He calls Joseph the husband of Mary and says that Jesus, who is called Christ, was born of her.", "Matthew groups the names into three stretches of fourteen generations: Abraham to David, David to the exile, and the exile to Christ. The grouping helps the reader remember the turns in the family's history. The Gospel will now move from the names of generations to what took place in the house of Joseph and Mary."], "lesson": "The exile could seem to mark the end of David's house. Matthew keeps tracing the line beyond it. God's faithfulness can outlast the losses that make his promises seem remote.", "question": "What does Matthew gain by keeping the exile in the family record?"},
  {"id": 91001, "title": "Joseph Takes Mary Home", "subtitle": "Obedience when plans change", "text": "Matthew 1:18–25", "chapters": [[39, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Mary was pledged to marry Joseph. Before they came together, she was found to be expecting a child. Matthew tells the reader from the beginning that the child was conceived by the Holy Spirit. Joseph did not yet have that explanation. He had to decide what to do with the news as he understood it.", "Joseph was a righteous man, and he did not want to make Mary a public example. He intended to put her away privately. While he was considering what to do, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream and addressed him as a son of David. The angel told him not to be afraid to take Mary as his wife, because the child she carried was from the Holy Spirit.", "The instruction also concerned what would happen after the birth. Mary would have a son, and Joseph was to name him Jesus, because he would save his people from their sins. Matthew connects the birth with the prophet's words about a child called Emmanuel, which means God with us. The promise gives the child's coming a significance beyond the immediate trouble in Joseph's household.", "When Joseph woke, he followed the angel's instruction and took Mary home as his wife. He did not have relations with her before she gave birth. When the son was born, Joseph gave him the name Jesus. The man who had been preparing to end the engagement now took responsibility for carrying out what the Lord had entrusted to him."], "lesson": "Joseph was prepared to act without making Mary a public example. When God gave him a different course, he acted on what he had heard.", "question": "Which instructions did Joseph carry out, and what do they show about his trust?"},
  {"id": 91002, "title": "The Visitors From the East", "subtitle": "Two responses to a king", "text": "Matthew 2:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After Jesus was born at Bethlehem, wise men from the East arrived in Jerusalem asking where the King of the Jews had been born. They had seen his star and had come to worship him. The question troubled Herod and, Matthew says, all Jerusalem with him. Herod gathered the chief priests and scribes and asked where the Christ was to be born. They named Bethlehem and gave him the words of the prophet about a ruler who would shepherd Israel.", "Herod then met the wise men privately and asked when the star had appeared. He sent them to Bethlehem to search carefully. When they had found the child, he said, they should report back so that he too might go and worship. The visitors departed, and the star went before them until it stood over the place where the child was. Seeing it, they rejoiced greatly.", "Inside the house they found the child with Mary his mother. They fell down, worshipped him and opened their treasures, offering gold, frankincense and myrrh. The journey that had begun with a question in the capital ended with them bowing before a child in a house.", "Herod had asked them to return, but God warned them in a dream against doing so. They went back to their own country along another road. Herod had obtained an answer about where the ruler would come from. The visitors had gone there, given their gifts and left by the way God directed them."], "lesson": "Herod learned where the Christ would be born and used that knowledge to protect his power. The visitors used what they learned to seek and worship Jesus. Consider where your knowledge of Scripture is taking you.", "question": "What differences do you see between Herod's questions and the visitors' actions?"},
  {"id": 91003, "title": "The Flight to Egypt and the Return", "subtitle": "God's care amid danger and grief", "text": "Matthew 2:13–23", "chapters": [[39, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the visitors had gone, an angel appeared to Joseph in a dream. He was to take the child and his mother to Egypt and stay there until he received further word. Herod would search for the child to kill him. Joseph got up, took them that night and left. They remained in Egypt until Herod died. Matthew connects their time there with the prophet's words about God calling his son out of Egypt.", "Herod realised the visitors had departed without reporting back. Enraged, he ordered the killing of the boys in Bethlehem and the surrounding district who were two years old or younger. He used the time he had learned from the visitors to set the age. Matthew recalls Jeremiah's picture of Rachel weeping for her children and refusing comfort because they were gone. The escape of one family sits alongside the grief of others.", "After Herod's death, an angel told Joseph in Egypt that those seeking the child's life were dead. Joseph took the child and his mother back towards Israel. On hearing that Archelaus ruled Judea in his father's place, he was afraid to go there. Another warning in a dream directed him away, and he withdrew into Galilee.", "The family settled in Nazareth. Matthew associates that place with the words of the prophets about Jesus being called a Nazarene. The chapter ends with the family finding a place to live after leaving at night and changing course again on the way home."], "lesson": "Matthew records both the child's deliverance and the grief of the bereaved families. God's work in history gives room for lament as well as gratitude.", "question": "Why does Matthew include the cries of the grieving families in the account of Jesus' escape?"},
  {"id": 91004, "title": "A Voice in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Repentance that bears fruit", "text": "Matthew 3:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea. He called people to repent because the kingdom of heaven was near. Matthew presents him as the voice Isaiah spoke of, calling for the Lord's way to be prepared. John's clothing was made of camel's hair, and he wore a leather belt. His food was locusts and wild honey.", "People left Jerusalem, Judea and the country around the Jordan to hear him. In the river they were baptised by him while confessing their sins. The wilderness became a place to speak openly about conduct people might otherwise have kept to themselves.", "When many Pharisees and Sadducees came, John asked who had warned them to flee from coming wrath. He called for fruit consistent with repentance. They should not rely on saying Abraham was their father. God could raise children to Abraham from stones. John spoke of an axe already laid at the root of trees that failed to bear good fruit.", "John then directed their attention to someone who would come after him. John used water and called for repentance. The coming one was mightier, and John said he was unworthy even to carry his sandals. He would baptise with the Holy Spirit and with fire. John closed with the image of someone clearing a threshing floor, gathering the wheat and burning the chaff. His preaching prepared the crowd for a person with authority beyond his own."], "lesson": "John's warning reaches people who rely on their religious ancestry. Repentance becomes visible in the life that follows confession.", "question": "What would count as fruit of repentance in a situation you are facing?"},
  {"id": 91005, "title": "Jesus Comes to the Jordan", "subtitle": "The Father's beloved Son", "text": "Matthew 3:13–17", "chapters": [[39, 3]], "start": 13, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The crowds had come to John at the Jordan confessing their sins. John had told them that someone greater was coming after him. He had spoken of being unworthy to carry that person's sandals. Matthew now records who came to the river. Jesus travelled from Galilee to John and asked to be baptised.", "John resisted. He told Jesus that he himself needed baptism from him and asked why Jesus was coming to him. That question brings the earlier preaching into the encounter. The man who had baptised the crowds now spoke of his own need before the one standing in front of him.", "Jesus told him to allow it for the present because it was fitting for them to fulfil all righteousness. John consented. Matthew leaves the exchange there and turns to what happened as Jesus came up from the water. The heavens opened, and Jesus saw the Spirit of God coming down like a dove and alighting on him.", "A voice from heaven declared that this was God's beloved Son, in whom he was well pleased. John had questioned whether he should baptise Jesus, and Jesus had answered by speaking of righteousness. The voice from heaven says who Jesus is as he begins the work Matthew will go on to recount. The next account takes him from the water into the wilderness, carrying that declaration into the time of testing."], "lesson": "Jesus willingly took the course that fulfilled righteousness. The Father's declaration identifies the one whose life and teaching Matthew now follows.", "question": "What does the Father's declaration tell you about the person John baptised?"},
  {"id": 91006, "title": "The Test in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Trusting God under pressure", "text": "Matthew 4:1–11", "chapters": [[39, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After his baptism, Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil. He fasted for forty days and forty nights and was hungry. The tempter approached him at the point of that hunger. If he was the Son of God, the tempter suggested, he could command the stones to become bread. Jesus answered from Scripture that a person lives by every word that comes from God, as well as needing bread.", "The devil then took him to the holy city and set him on a high point of the temple. Again he challenged Jesus to act if he was God's Son. This time he used words from Scripture, appealing to a promise that God's angels would bear him up. Jesus brought another command to bear on the proposal. God must not be put to the test.", "On a very high mountain the devil showed him the kingdoms of the world and their glory. He offered them in exchange for Jesus falling down and worshipping him. Jesus told Satan to go away and replied from Scripture that worship and service belong to the Lord God alone.", "The devil left, and angels came and ministered to Jesus. Matthew records their arrival after Jesus had refused to force a display of their protection. Through hunger, an appeal to a biblical promise and an offer of power, Jesus continued to answer from what God had said."], "lesson": "Each offer invited Jesus to take something by abandoning obedience. His answers show that a verse must be read in keeping with what God has commanded.", "question": "How does Jesus' second reply help you recognise a misuse of Scripture?"},
  {"id": 91007, "title": "The First Followers by the Lake", "subtitle": "The call to follow Jesus", "text": "Matthew 4:12–25", "chapters": [[39, 4]], "start": 12, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus heard that John had been put in prison and withdrew into Galilee. He left Nazareth and settled in Capernaum beside the sea, in the region associated with Zebulun and Naphtali. Matthew remembers Isaiah's words about light coming to people who sat in darkness. Jesus began to preach, calling for repentance because the kingdom of heaven was near.", "Walking beside the Sea of Galilee, Jesus saw two brothers, Simon Peter and Andrew. They were casting a net into the water because that was their work. He called them to follow him and said he would make them fishers of people. They left their nets at once and followed.", "A little farther on he saw James and John, sons of Zebedee. They were in a boat with their father, mending their nets. Jesus called them as well. They left the boat and their father and followed him. Those first calls came in the middle of work the four men already knew how to do. The promise Jesus made would take them into work they still had to learn.", "Jesus travelled throughout Galilee, teaching in synagogues, preaching the good news of the kingdom and healing diseases. People brought sufferers to him from as far away as Syria. Crowds followed from Galilee, the Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea and beyond the Jordan. The men who had left their nets now accompanied Jesus as his teaching and healing drew people from many places."], "lesson": "The fishermen's response had an immediate cost. Jesus gave them a new purpose whose full meaning they would learn by accompanying him.", "question": "What did the four men leave, and what did Jesus say he would make of them?"},
  {"id": 91008, "title": "The People Jesus Calls Blessed", "subtitle": "A visible life of faith", "text": "Matthew 5:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus saw the crowds, he went up the mountain and sat down. His disciples came to him, and he began to teach. He called the poor in spirit blessed because the kingdom of heaven belonged to them. He spoke of people who mourn and will be comforted, of the meek who will inherit the earth, and of those who hunger for righteousness and will be filled.", "Jesus continued with the merciful, the pure in heart and the peacemakers. He attached promises to those ways of life: mercy, sight of God and the name of God's children. He included people persecuted for righteousness and spoke directly to his hearers about what to remember when people reviled them because of him. The prophets before them had also faced persecution.", "Then Jesus compared his hearers to salt. Salt that has lost its savour can no longer do its work. He gave a second picture, a city on a hill which cannot be hidden. People light a lamp to put it on a stand where it gives light to the house. Covering it with a measure would defeat the purpose of lighting it.", "Jesus applied the lamp to the lives of his disciples. Their good works were to be visible enough that others would glorify their Father in heaven. The people he had called blessed were now asked to let their conduct be seen for what it showed about God."], "lesson": "The blessings correct the way we measure a good life. The light Jesus describes draws attention to the Father through the conduct of his people.", "question": "Which of the blessings would change the way you judge a difficult situation?"},
  {"id": 91009, "title": "Before You Bring Your Gift", "subtitle": "Reconciliation and the heart", "text": "Matthew 5:17–26", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After speaking of visible good works, Jesus addressed how his teaching related to the law and the prophets. He had come to fulfil them. He gave weight even to the smallest details and spoke of the responsibility of people who both do and teach God's commands. The righteousness of his hearers must go beyond that of the scribes and Pharisees.", "Jesus began with the command against murder. He brought anger and words of contempt into the discussion. A person might never have struck a fatal blow and still need to answer for how he regarded and addressed another person. Jesus' examples take the hearer from an outward offence towards conduct that can help a quarrel grow.", "He pictured someone bringing a gift to the altar who remembers a brother has something against him. The worshipper should leave the gift there and go first to be reconciled. Then he can return and make the offering. What he remembers at the altar gives him something to attend to before completing his act of worship.", "Jesus gave a second picture of two people on their way to court. He urged agreement while they were still on the road, before the matter passed through the judge and the officer and ended in prison. Both examples identify a moment when a hearer still has room to act. One is the moment of remembering a grievance. The other is the remaining distance before reaching the judge."], "lesson": "Jesus connects worship with how we treat people. A serious attempt to make peace can reveal whether we have taken his words about anger to heart.", "question": "What unfinished matter would Jesus' picture of the worshipper bring to your attention?"},
  {"id": 91010, "title": "Faithfulness in Desire and Speech", "subtitle": "Integrity without loopholes", "text": "Matthew 5:27–37", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 27, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus next took up the command against adultery. He spoke of the look that is directed towards lust and brought the heart into view. What another person could see of someone's conduct was only part of the matter. Jesus asked his hearers to reckon with what they were allowing themselves to desire.", "He used severe images of an eye or a hand that causes a person to stumble. He pictured giving up something precious rather than letting it lead the whole person to ruin. The force of the illustration is in the seriousness of what is at stake. A person who sees a source of temptation has a decision to make about what place to give it.", "Jesus then spoke of putting away a wife. His hearers knew the provision for a written notice of divorce. He addressed the consequences for a wife dismissed by her husband and the person who marries her, giving an exception involving sexual immorality. The act of writing a notice does not exhaust the question of responsibility.", "He turned from marriage to the reliability of words. His hearers knew the duty to keep oaths made to the Lord. Jesus told them to avoid oaths that appealed to heaven, earth, Jerusalem or even one's own head. A person's yes or no should be dependable. The passage ends by putting truthfulness into the simplest words people use with one another."], "lesson": "Faithfulness reaches into the desires we entertain and the words others must rely on. Jesus calls for decisive action against habits that make unfaithfulness easier.", "question": "Where would a truthful yes or no be better than a promise dressed up to sound convincing?"},
  {"id": 91011, "title": "When an Enemy Needs Your Prayer", "subtitle": "Love patterned after the Father", "text": "Matthew 5:38–48", "chapters": [[39, 5]], "start": 38, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus recalled the rule about an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth. He then gave his hearers a series of situations in which they might feel entitled to answer in kind. He spoke of a person struck on the cheek, of a lawsuit over a garment and of someone compelled to go a mile. In each picture, he asked the hearer to consider a response beyond returning the injury or doing the minimum demanded.", "He went on to speak of giving to someone who asks and of receiving a person who would borrow. These are ordinary occasions when another person's claim can feel like an interruption. The situations are different, but each places a choice in the hands of the person hearing Jesus' words.", "Jesus then spoke of loving enemies and praying for persecutors. He pointed to the Father, who makes his sun rise on evil and good people and sends rain on just and unjust alike. The illustration locates the example for love in what the Father gives every day to people whose conduct differs.", "He asked what was distinctive about loving only those who love us or greeting only those who belong to our own circle. Even people his hearers looked down on could do as much. The passage closes with the Father's perfection as the measure. Jesus has moved from a single blow or demand to the kind of love his followers are to practise towards people who oppose them."], "lesson": "Jesus calls disciples to act beyond the circle of people who repay kindness. Prayer for an enemy places that person's welfare before God and tests our desire for revenge.", "question": "Whom could you pray for by asking God for a specific good in that person's life?"},
  {"id": 91012, "title": "What the Father Sees in Secret", "subtitle": "Giving, prayer and fasting", "text": "Matthew 6:1–18", "chapters": [[39, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus asked his disciples to consider who they were trying to please when they gave, prayed or fasted. A person could make a show of giving and receive public praise for it. Jesus described an act of giving carried out so quietly that one hand did not know what the other was doing. The Father who sees in secret would see the gift.", "He raised the same question about prayer. Some chose visible places where others could see them. Jesus directed his hearers to go into a room and shut the door. He also warned against piling up words under the assumption that numbers would make God listen. Their Father knew what they needed before they asked.", "Then he gave them a prayer. It begins with the Father's name, his kingdom and his will. It asks for daily bread, forgiveness as the people praying forgive others, and deliverance from temptation and evil. After the prayer Jesus returned to forgiveness, giving it direct weight in how his hearers dealt with people who had wronged them.", "When he turned to fasting, he described people making their faces look miserable so everyone would notice. His followers were to wash and care for their appearance as they usually would. Their fast could then remain a matter between them and the Father who sees in secret. The three instructions leave plenty for a disciple to do where there may be no audience at all."], "lesson": "The prayer Jesus gave orders our desires around the Father before naming everyday needs. A private practice of mercy or prayer can expose how much we depend on being noticed.", "question": "How might the order of the Lord's Prayer change what you ask for today?"},
  {"id": 91013, "title": "Treasure and Tomorrow", "subtitle": "Trusting the Father for daily needs", "text": "Matthew 6:19–34", "chapters": [[39, 6]], "start": 19, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus spoke about two places in which treasure could be stored. On earth it could be damaged by moth or rust, or taken by thieves. Treasure in heaven was beyond those losses. He joined the choice of treasure to the direction of the heart. He then spoke of the eye as a lamp for the body and warned about what happens when the light within is darkness.", "He gave his hearers another picture: one servant trying to serve two masters. A divided attachment would show itself in how the servant loved and held to one while despising the other. Jesus applied it plainly to the service of God and the service of money.", "The subject of money led into the needs that make people anxious. Jesus spoke of food, drink and clothing. The birds did not sow, reap or store food in barns, yet their heavenly Father fed them. The lilies did not labour or spin, yet Solomon in his glory was not clothed like one of them. The Father who clothed grass that lasted so briefly knew his people's needs.", "Jesus told his hearers to seek first God's kingdom and righteousness. He brought the teaching back to the day they had in front of them. Tomorrow would have its own concerns, and today had enough trouble of its own. The passage moves from where people store their treasure to what they give first place when real needs come to mind."], "lesson": "Jesus connects anxiety about provision with what has first claim on the heart. Trust allows us to attend to today's duties while placing tomorrow before the Father.", "question": "What would seeking God's kingdom first change about one decision concerning money or time?"},
  {"id": 91014, "title": "The Speck and the Beam", "subtitle": "Honest self-examination and generous prayer", "text": "Matthew 7:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus began with a warning about judging. The judgement and measure his hearers applied to others would be applied to them. Then he pictured someone noticing a speck in a brother's eye while failing to notice a beam in his own. The person with the beam even offered to remove the speck. Jesus told him to deal with the beam first so that he could see clearly enough to help his brother.", "Jesus gave another warning about discernment. He pictured giving what was holy to dogs or throwing pearls before pigs. What was given could be trampled and the giver attacked. The image adds a different kind of care to the warning about judgement. The person offering help still has to pay attention to the situation.", "Jesus then told his hearers to ask, seek and knock. He pictured a child asking a parent for bread or a fish. A parent would not answer by handing over a stone or a serpent. If parents who fell short in other ways knew how to give good gifts, their Father in heaven would give good things to those who asked him.", "He drew the instruction towards daily conduct by telling them to do for others what they wanted others to do for them. He connected this with the law and the prophets. The listener has been asked to examine his own approach to correction, trust the Father's generosity and put himself in the place of another person."], "lesson": "Correction should begin with honest attention to our own conduct. The Father's generosity also shapes how we approach the needs of others.", "question": "What would removing the beam first change about a conversation you need to have?"},
  {"id": 91015, "title": "Two Builders in the Storm", "subtitle": "Hearing that becomes obedience", "text": "Matthew 7:13–29", "chapters": [[39, 7]], "start": 13, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus brought the sermon towards its close, he spoke of two gates. The broad road was easy to enter and many went down it, but it ended in destruction. The narrow way led to life. He warned that false prophets could arrive looking like sheep while being dangerous within. Fruit would show what kind of tree stood before the hearer.", "Jesus then spoke of people who would call him Lord and point to impressive deeds done in his name. He said that entering the kingdom belonged to those who did his Father's will. A name spoken publicly and deeds cited in one's own defence could leave the question of obedience unanswered.", "He set two builders before the crowd. A wise man heard his sayings and acted on them, like a man building on rock. Rain came, streams rose and winds beat against the house. It stood because it had been founded on rock. A foolish man heard the same words and failed to act, like a builder who set his house on sand. The same forces beat on it, and its fall was great.", "When Jesus finished speaking, the crowds were astonished at the way he taught. Matthew says they heard authority different from that of their scribes. The people had heard the same sermon together. The picture Jesus left them with concerned what each person would go on to do with what he had heard."], "lesson": "The storm exposes a difference that was already present in how the builders chose their foundations. Jesus links a sound foundation to hearing his words and doing them.", "question": "Which instruction from the Sermon on the Mount have you heard often enough to put into practice now?"},
  {"id": 91016, "title": "A Man Reaches Out, a Soldier Asks", "subtitle": "The authority and compassion of Jesus", "text": "Matthew 8:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus came down from the mountain, large crowds followed him. A man with leprosy approached and knelt before him. The man did not question whether Jesus had the power to cleanse him. He put his request in terms of whether Jesus was willing. Jesus stretched out his hand, touched him and said he was willing. The man was cleansed at once. Jesus told him to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses had commanded.", "In Capernaum a centurion approached Jesus about a servant who lay at home paralysed and in great distress. Jesus said he would come and heal him. The officer said he was unworthy to have Jesus under his roof. He understood what an order could do: one soldier went when told to go and another came when told to come. He trusted Jesus to speak the word from where he stood.", "Jesus marvelled and told those following him that he had not found such faith in Israel. He spoke of people coming from east and west to sit with Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and warned about presuming on a place at the table. Then he sent the centurion on his way. The servant was healed that hour.", "In Peter's house Jesus saw Peter's mother-in-law sick with fever. He touched her hand, the fever left and she rose and served him. In the evening people brought more sufferers. Matthew connects his care for them with Isaiah's words about bearing infirmities and sicknesses."], "lesson": "The man with leprosy appealed to Jesus' willingness. The centurion relied on the authority of his word. Both brought a real need before him.", "question": "What did the centurion understand about authority that shaped his request?"},
  {"id": 91017, "title": "The Cost of Following and the Storm", "subtitle": "Discipleship when safety is uncertain", "text": "Matthew 8:18–27", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 18, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus saw the crowds around him, he gave orders to cross to the other side of the lake. Before he set off, a scribe offered to follow him wherever he went. Jesus answered by speaking of foxes with holes and birds with nests, while the Son of Man had nowhere to lay his head. His reply gave the man a picture of what travelling with him could involve.", "Another disciple asked for leave to go first and bury his father. Jesus told him to follow and leave the dead to bury their own dead. Matthew does not say how either man answered. The next people he describes are the disciples who followed Jesus into the boat.", "A violent storm arose on the lake. Waves covered the boat, and Jesus was asleep. The disciples came to wake him and cried for the Lord to save them because they were perishing. He asked why they were so afraid and spoke of their little faith. Then he stood and rebuked the winds and the sea.", "A great calm followed. The people in the boat marvelled and asked what sort of man this was, since even the winds and sea obeyed him. Their question carries the account beyond relief at reaching calm water. They had set out with the man who spoke of having nowhere to lay his head, and they were discovering an authority they had not expected."], "lesson": "The call to follow Jesus is given before the storm arrives. In the boat the disciples discover how much more they have to learn about the one they follow.", "question": "How do Jesus' words before the crossing prepare you to read what happened on the lake?"},
  {"id": 91018, "title": "Two Men Among the Tombs", "subtitle": "When deliverance unsettles a town", "text": "Matthew 8:28–34", "chapters": [[39, 8]], "start": 28, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus reached the other side of the lake, two men came out of the tombs to meet him. They were so fierce that people could not pass along that road. Matthew brings the crossing straight into this encounter. The men cried out to Jesus as the Son of God and asked whether he had come to torment them before the appointed time.", "Some distance away a herd of pigs was feeding. The demons begged Jesus to send them into the pigs if he drove them out. He told them to go. They left the men and entered the herd. The whole herd rushed down the steep bank into the sea and died in the water. The men who tended the pigs fled to the town.", "In the town, the herdsmen reported what had happened, including what had happened to the men who had been possessed. Matthew keeps both parts of their report together. The herd's loss was plain, and the power that had held the two men had been driven out. People in the town received news of both before coming out to meet Jesus.", "The whole town came out. When the people saw Jesus, they begged him to leave their district. Matthew ends there without telling us what the two men said afterwards or giving a reason for the townspeople's request. Their appeal to Jesus to go is the last word in the scene."], "lesson": "A community can witness a powerful act and still want distance from Jesus. The town's response invites us to examine what we are anxious to protect when his authority disturbs familiar arrangements.", "question": "What happened to the two men, and what did the townspeople ask Jesus to do?"},
  {"id": 91019, "title": "Forgiveness, Healing and a Place at the Table", "subtitle": "Mercy for those who need it", "text": "Matthew 9:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus crossed back by boat and came to his own town. People brought him a paralysed man lying on a bed. Seeing their faith, Jesus addressed the man as a son and told him to take courage because his sins were forgiven. Some scribes said to themselves that Jesus was blaspheming. He knew their thoughts and asked why they were thinking evil in their hearts.", "Jesus asked which was easier to say: that the man's sins were forgiven, or that he should rise and walk. Then he gave the people present something they could see. To show that the Son of Man had authority on earth to forgive sins, he told the man to rise, take up his bed and go home. The man rose and went home. The crowds marvelled and glorified God.", "As Jesus went on from there, he saw Matthew sitting at the tax booth. Jesus told him to follow, and Matthew rose and followed him. Later, as Jesus sat at a meal in a house, many tax collectors and people regarded as sinners came and ate with him and his disciples.", "The Pharisees asked the disciples why their teacher ate with such people. Jesus heard and answered with the picture of a physician. It was the sick who needed one. He told his hearers to go and learn what God meant by desiring mercy and spoke of his calling to sinners. The man on the bed and the people at the table each give the reader a place to consider the mercy Jesus came to exercise."], "lesson": "Forgiveness addresses a need the people bringing the man may have overlooked. At Matthew's table Jesus shows why the company of sinners fits his mission of mercy.", "question": "How do Jesus' words to the paralysed man help explain why he ate at Matthew's table?"},
  {"id": 91020, "title": "A Question About Fasting", "subtitle": "Recognising the time and the occasion", "text": "Matthew 9:14–17", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 14, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["John's disciples came to Jesus with a question about fasting. They fasted, and so did the Pharisees. Why did Jesus' disciples go about their days without keeping the same practice? Matthew has just described Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners. The question now concerns another difference that people could see in the conduct of those around him.", "Jesus asked them to think of a wedding. As long as the bridegroom was with the wedding party, it would be out of place for his companions to mourn. The days would come when he was taken away from them. Then they would fast. Jesus gave fasting a place in the days to come and explained why the present moment called for a different response.", "He gave another example from a familiar task. A patch of new cloth sewn onto an old garment could pull away and make the tear worse. Then he spoke of new wine in old wineskins. The skins would burst, the wine would spill and the skins would be ruined. New wine belonged in new skins so that both could be preserved.", "John's disciples had asked why one company followed a practice that another did not. Jesus answered by placing himself in the picture. They needed to consider who was present and what that presence meant before deciding what his disciples should be doing."], "lesson": "Jesus explains a religious practice in light of his presence and the occasion. The question encourages us to consider whether our habits express a living response to him.", "question": "How does the bridegroom image explain why the disciples' conduct differed?"},
  {"id": 91021, "title": "Interrupted on the Way to a Child", "subtitle": "Faith when hope seems gone", "text": "Matthew 9:18–26", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 18, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["While Jesus was speaking, a ruler came and knelt before him. His daughter had just died. He asked Jesus to come and lay his hand on her, believing that she would live. Jesus got up and followed him, and his disciples went with him.", "On the way, a woman came up behind Jesus. She had suffered from bleeding for twelve years. She told herself that if she could touch his garment she would be made well. She reached for its edge. Jesus turned, saw her and told her to take courage. He addressed her as daughter and said that her faith had made her well. Matthew says she was healed from that hour.", "Jesus continued to the ruler's house. Inside were musicians and a crowd making a commotion. He told the people to go out and said the girl was sleeping. They laughed at him. After the crowd had been put out, he went in, took her by the hand and the girl rose. News of what had happened spread through the district.", "The two encounters share a journey. The ruler had come openly to ask Jesus to touch his child. The woman had approached from behind and touched the edge of his garment. Jesus stopped to speak to her and continued to the child, entering the house despite the response of the people who had gathered there."], "lesson": "Jesus had time to address the woman personally even on the way to the ruler's house. The account gives attention to two sufferers whom others might have passed over or given up for dead.", "question": "What do Jesus' words to the woman tell you about the way he received her?"},
  {"id": 91022, "title": "Opening Eyes and Seeing the Harvest", "subtitle": "Compassion that asks for workers", "text": "Matthew 9:27–38", "chapters": [[39, 9]], "start": 27, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus went on from the ruler's house, two blind men followed him and called for mercy, addressing him as the Son of David. They came to him inside a house. He asked whether they believed he could do what they were asking. They said they did. He touched their eyes, spoke to them about their faith, and their eyes were opened.", "Jesus warned them sternly that no one should know what had happened. They went out and spread the report through the district. As they were leaving, others brought a man who could not speak and was afflicted by a demon. When the demon was driven out, the man spoke. The crowds were amazed and said they had never seen anything like it in Israel. The Pharisees gave a very different account, attributing his work to the ruler of demons.", "Matthew then follows Jesus through cities and villages. He taught in the synagogues, proclaimed the good news of the kingdom and healed the sick. When he saw the crowds, he had compassion because they were distressed and scattered, like sheep without a shepherd.", "Jesus turned to his disciples and described a harvest too plentiful for the few workers available. He told them to ask the Lord of the harvest to send workers into it. The crowd's condition had moved him to compassion, and he gave the disciples a way to begin sharing in his concern."], "lesson": "Jesus sees more than a crowd. His compassion notices its condition and gives the disciples something specific to pray for.", "question": "Who in your community comes to mind when you read about sheep without a shepherd?"},
  {"id": 91023, "title": "The Twelve Are Sent Out", "subtitle": "Giving freely what you have received", "text": "Matthew 10:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus had just told the disciples to pray for workers. Now he called twelve disciples and gave them authority over unclean spirits and diseases. Matthew names them, beginning with Simon Peter and his brother Andrew, and James and John, the sons of Zebedee. The list includes Matthew the tax collector, Simon the Canaanite and Judas Iscariot, whom Matthew identifies as the one who would betray him.", "Jesus sent the Twelve out with instructions for this journey. They were to go to the lost sheep of Israel. As they went, they were to announce that the kingdom of heaven was near. The work included healing the sick, cleansing people with leprosy, raising the dead and driving out demons. They were to give freely because they had received freely.", "They were to travel without taking gold, silver or spare clothing as provisions. Jesus said a worker was worthy of his food. On arriving in a town or village, they should look for a suitable household and remain there until they left. The household's welcome gave them a place from which to carry out the work.", "Jesus also prepared them for refusal. If a house or town would not receive them or listen to them, they were to leave and shake the dust from their feet. He took that refusal seriously, comparing the coming judgement with Sodom and Gomorrah. The instructions give the disciples something to do both when a door opens and when it stays shut."], "lesson": "Their authority was a gift and their service was to bear the same character. The instructions join generosity with dependence on God and on the hospitality of others.", "question": "How would receiving freely affect the way the disciples treated people who could give them nothing?"},
  {"id": 91024, "title": "Speaking When Opposition Comes", "subtitle": "Courage under the Father's care", "text": "Matthew 10:16–33", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told the Twelve that he was sending them out like sheep among wolves. He called for the wisdom of serpents and the innocence of doves. He warned them that people would hand them over to councils and bring them before governors and kings. Those hearings would give them an occasion to bear witness.", "When they were handed over, they need not be anxious beforehand about what to say. In that hour the Spirit of their Father would speak through them. Jesus also warned of opposition closer to home: relatives would turn against one another and his followers would be hated on account of his name. He called them to endure, telling them to move to another city when persecuted in one.", "A disciple could expect to be treated as his teacher had been treated. If people had spoken against the head of the house, they could speak in the same way of those who belonged to him. Even so, Jesus told his messengers to speak openly what they had learned from him. There was a limit to what people who could kill the body could do.", "He brought their attention to the Father's care. Sparrows sold for very little, yet their Father knew what happened to them. Even the hairs on the disciples' heads were numbered. Jesus ended by addressing whether they would acknowledge him before others. The people he had warned about hearings and hostility could carry that assurance into the moment when they had to speak."], "lesson": "Jesus prepares his followers for costly witness and assures them of the Father's knowledge of each one. His teaching gives courage a firmer basis than the hope that people will approve.", "question": "What does the sparrow illustration contribute to Jesus' instructions about public witness?"},
  {"id": 91025, "title": "A Cup of Cold Water", "subtitle": "Allegiance and welcome", "text": "Matthew 10:34–42", "chapters": [[39, 10]], "start": 34, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus finished speaking to the Twelve about their mission, he warned of divisions that could enter even a household. A person might find relatives opposed to following him. He said that even the love owed to parents or children could not take first place over allegiance to him. He spoke of taking up a cross and of losing one's life for his sake and finding it.", "Then he turned to a second group of people in the same mission: those who would receive his messengers. A person who welcomed them welcomed Jesus, and a person who welcomed Jesus welcomed the one who had sent him. What appeared to be an ordinary reception of travelling people could carry that significance.", "Jesus spoke of someone receiving a prophet because he was a prophet, and of someone receiving a righteous person because he was righteous. Each welcome would have its reward. He came at last to a small thing a person could do even with very little to offer. Someone who gave one of the little ones a cup of cold water because he was a disciple would not lose that reward.", "The instruction ends with water in a cup. Earlier in the speech, the apostles had been told to go out and rely on hospitality. Here Jesus gives meaning to a simple act of the person on the other side of the door."], "lesson": "The passage places demanding loyalty and ordinary hospitality in the same mission. Help offered quietly to a disciple can express real allegiance to Jesus.", "question": "What kind of welcome is within your reach even if you have little to give?"},
  {"id": 91026, "title": "John Sends a Question From Prison", "subtitle": "Taking uncertainty to Jesus", "text": "Matthew 11:1–19", "chapters": [[39, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After giving instructions to the Twelve, Jesus went on to teach and preach in their towns. John the Baptist was in prison. Hearing about the works of Christ, he sent disciples to ask Jesus whether he was the one who was to come or whether they should look for another.", "Jesus told the messengers to report what they heard and saw. Blind people were receiving sight. The lame walked, people with leprosy were cleansed, the deaf heard, the dead were raised and good news was preached to the poor. He added a blessing for the person who did not stumble on account of him. The messengers had something concrete to take back into the prison.", "When they left, Jesus spoke to the crowd about John. He asked what they had gone into the wilderness to see. John was no reed wavering in the wind and no man dressed for a palace. He was a prophet and more than a prophet, the messenger sent to prepare the way. Jesus spoke of John's place in what God had been doing, telling those with ears to hear to listen.", "Then he compared the generation to children in the marketplace who complained that others would neither dance to a tune nor mourn at a lament. John had come with an austere manner of life and been accused of having a demon. The Son of Man came eating and drinking and was criticised for his company. Jesus let the hearers examine why they had found reasons to dismiss both."], "lesson": "John's question went directly to Jesus. Jesus answered by directing attention to his works, while exposing the habit of finding an excuse to reject every messenger.", "question": "What evidence did Jesus tell John's disciples to carry back to him?"},
  {"id": 91027, "title": "An Invitation to the Weary", "subtitle": "Repentance and rest", "text": "Matthew 11:20–30", "chapters": [[39, 11]], "start": 20, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus began to rebuke towns where many of his mighty works had been done because they had not repented. He named Chorazin and Bethsaida. If Tyre and Sidon had seen what those towns had seen, he said, they would have repented long ago in sackcloth and ashes. He also addressed Capernaum, comparing its accountability with that of Sodom. The works he had done had given these towns a reason to change their conduct.", "At that time Jesus thanked his Father, Lord of heaven and earth, for revealing things to little children that were hidden from the wise and understanding. He acknowledged the Father's good pleasure in this. What he had said to the towns makes the thanks worth hearing carefully. A place could have seen much and still fail to receive what was being shown to it.", "Jesus said all things had been given to him by his Father. He spoke of the Father knowing the Son and the Son knowing the Father, and of the Son revealing the Father to others. Then he turned to people carrying heavy burdens and called them to come to him for rest.", "He asked them to take his yoke and learn from him, describing himself as meek and lowly in heart. The rest he promised would be found under his teaching. His invitation names both the burdens people already carry and the person from whom they can learn a different way to bear their lives."], "lesson": "The invitation to rest comes from the same Jesus who calls the towns to repent. Rest is found in coming under the teaching of a gentle Lord.", "question": "What does Jesus ask weary people to do after they come to him?"},
  {"id": 91028, "title": "Two Sabbath Disputes", "subtitle": "Mercy and doing good", "text": "Matthew 12:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus and his disciples were passing through grainfields. The disciples were hungry and began to pluck grain and eat. When some Pharisees saw, they accused the disciples of doing what was unlawful on the Sabbath. Jesus answered by recalling David, who was hungry with his companions and ate the consecrated bread ordinarily reserved for priests.", "He also asked whether they had read about the priests who worked in the temple on the Sabbath and were held guiltless. He spoke of someone greater than the temple and appealed to God's desire for mercy. If his critics understood that, he said, they would not have condemned the guiltless. He declared the Son of Man Lord of the Sabbath.", "Jesus went into their synagogue. There he found a man with a withered hand. The people questioning him asked whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath, looking for a charge to bring against him. Jesus asked what any of them would do if a sheep fell into a pit that day. They would take hold of it and lift it out. A person was worth more than a sheep.", "Jesus said it was lawful to do good on the Sabbath and told the man to stretch out his hand. He did, and it was restored like the other. The Pharisees went out and consulted about how to destroy Jesus. Matthew gives the reader two uses of that day to consider: restoring a man's hand and taking counsel against the one who restored it."], "lesson": "Jesus makes the suffering person visible in a dispute about rules. His questions call us to examine whether our judgements reflect God's mercy.", "question": "How does the example of the sheep challenge the way the critics looked at the man?"},
  {"id": 91029, "title": "A Healing and an Accusation", "subtitle": "Words reveal the heart", "text": "Matthew 12:15–37", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 15, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Knowing of the opposition, Jesus withdrew. People followed him, and he healed them and told them to keep his identity quiet. Matthew saw in this the servant Isaiah described, gentle in the streets and careful with people like bruised reeds. The prophet's words also look beyond Israel to the nations placing hope in his name.", "Then a man who was blind and unable to speak was brought to Jesus. The man was afflicted by a demon, and after Jesus healed him he could both see and speak. The people wondered whether Jesus was the Son of David. The Pharisees heard and claimed he drove out demons by the ruler of demons.", "Jesus answered that a kingdom divided against itself would come to ruin. If Satan cast out Satan, how could his kingdom stand? If Jesus drove out demons by the Spirit of God, then God's kingdom had come upon them. He pictured someone entering a strong man's house only after binding the man who guarded it. He also warned about speaking against the Holy Spirit, giving grave weight to what the Pharisees were saying.", "He turned to a tree and its fruit. Words come from what fills the heart, as fruit comes from a tree. People would answer for even their careless words. The scene began with a man receiving speech, and it closes with Jesus asking the people who already had speech to consider what their words revealed."], "lesson": "The crowd and the Pharisees saw the same healing and spoke about it differently. Jesus warns us to take seriously the heart from which our judgements and accusations come.", "question": "What did the Pharisees say about the healing, and how did Jesus answer their explanation?"},
  {"id": 91030, "title": "A Sign and a Family", "subtitle": "Responding to the light already given", "text": "Matthew 12:38–50", "chapters": [[39, 12]], "start": 38, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Some scribes and Pharisees told Jesus they wanted to see a sign from him. He answered by speaking of Jonah. As Jonah was three days and nights in the great fish, the Son of Man would be in the heart of the earth. The people of Nineveh would stand as witnesses against the present generation because they had repented at Jonah's preaching. Jesus said that someone greater than Jonah was there.", "He also recalled the queen of the South, who came a great distance to hear Solomon's wisdom. His hearers had someone greater than Solomon before them. In both examples, Jesus asked people looking for a new sign to consider how others had responded to the opportunity already given them.", "Jesus then described an unclean spirit leaving a person and later returning to find its former home empty, swept and put in order. It brought back seven spirits worse than itself, and the person's final condition was worse than the first. He applied that picture to the generation hearing him.", "While Jesus was still speaking, his mother and brothers stood outside wanting to speak to him. When someone brought the message, he asked who his mother and brothers were. Pointing towards his disciples, he said that whoever did his Father's will was his brother, sister and mother. The passage closes by putting the response to God's will into the language of belonging."], "lesson": "The passage asks what we do with the light we have already received. Jesus describes a close relationship with him in terms of doing his Father's will.", "question": "How does Nineveh's response challenge the people asking for another sign?"},
  {"id": 91031, "title": "The Sower and Four Soils", "subtitle": "How the word takes root", "text": "Matthew 13:1–23", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["That day Jesus went out of the house and sat beside the sea. A crowd gathered so large that he got into a boat and sat there while the people stood on the shore. He began telling them about a man who went out to sow and scattered seed across ground where it would meet different conditions.", "Some seed fell on the path and birds ate it. Some fell where the soil was shallow over rock. It came up quickly, but when the sun rose it withered for lack of root. Other seed fell among thorns, which grew up and choked it. Seed that fell on good ground bore fruit in different amounts. Jesus called on those with ears to hear.", "The disciples asked why he spoke to the crowd in parables. Jesus answered about what had been given to the disciples to understand. He described people who could hear and see without taking in what was before them. Then he blessed the disciples' eyes and ears for what they were seeing and hearing.", "He explained the soils. The word could be snatched away when someone failed to understand. It could be received eagerly and abandoned under pressure, or choked by worries and the deceitfulness of riches. The good ground pictured people who hear, understand and bear fruit. The parable gives the hearer time to ask what happens after the first moment of hearing."], "lesson": "The seed meets different conditions. Jesus gives us a way to name what can stop the word from bearing fruit after our first interest in it.", "question": "Which obstacle in Jesus' explanation deserves particular attention in your own life?"},
  {"id": 91032, "title": "Wheat, Weeds and Quiet Growth", "subtitle": "Patience while God brings the harvest", "text": "Matthew 13:24–43", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 24, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who sowed good seed in a field. While people slept, an enemy sowed weeds among the wheat and left. As the crop came up, the servants noticed the weeds and asked where they had come from. Hearing that an enemy had done it, they offered to pull them up. The owner warned that they might uproot the wheat at the same time. He told them to let both grow until harvest.", "Jesus gave the crowd two more pictures of the kingdom. A mustard seed was small when sown and grew large enough for birds to lodge in its branches. A woman hid leaven in flour until it worked through the whole amount. Both images ask the hearer to pay attention to a beginning that could easily be overlooked.", "When Jesus left the crowd and went into the house, the disciples asked him to explain the weeds. He named the Son of Man as the sower and the world as the field. The good seed stood for children of the kingdom and the weeds for children of the wicked one. The enemy was the devil, the harvest the end of the age and the reapers angels.", "He described the judgement at harvest and the righteous shining in their Father's kingdom. The servants' question had been about what to pull out as soon as they saw it. Jesus gave the final separating a time and agents of its own."], "lesson": "The farmer's restraint teaches patience with a world in which good and evil are still mingled. The mustard seed and leaven encourage faith when the kingdom's work seems small.", "question": "What work does the farmer reserve for harvest, and what does that teach about judgement?"},
  {"id": 91033, "title": "Hidden Treasure and a Familiar Town", "subtitle": "Recognising what stands before you", "text": "Matthew 13:44–58", "chapters": [[39, 13]], "start": 44, "end": 58, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to treasure hidden in a field. A man found it, hid it again and in his joy sold what he had to buy the field. A merchant looking for fine pearls found one of great value and also sold all he had to buy it. One man came upon treasure in the ground. The other knew what he was looking for and recognised the pearl when he found it.", "Then Jesus spoke of a net cast into the sea that gathered fish of every kind. When it was full, people drew it ashore, kept the good fish and threw the others away. He applied it to the separation at the end of the age. When the disciples said they understood, Jesus compared a teacher instructed in the kingdom to a householder who could bring out both new and old things from his treasure.", "After finishing these parables, Jesus went to his own country and taught in the synagogue. People were astonished at his wisdom and mighty works. They asked whether he was the carpenter's son and named his mother and brothers. His sisters, they said, were still among them. What they already knew about his family made it hard for them to receive what they were hearing.", "They took offence at him. Jesus spoke of a prophet being without honour in his own country and house. Matthew says he did few mighty works there because of their unbelief."], "lesson": "The people in the parables recognised value and acted decisively. Jesus' neighbours let their familiarity with his family shape how they received him.", "question": "What difference do you see between the buyers in the parables and the people in Jesus' hometown?"},
  {"id": 91034, "title": "The Death of John the Baptist", "subtitle": "Courage when truth is costly", "text": "Matthew 14:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When reports about Jesus reached Herod, he said to his servants that this was John the Baptist risen from the dead. Matthew then goes back to explain why Herod spoke of John that way. Herod had arrested and bound John because John told him it was unlawful for him to have Herodias, the wife of his brother. Herod wanted to kill him but feared the crowd, who regarded John as a prophet.", "At Herod's birthday celebration, Herodias's daughter danced before the company and pleased him. He swore to give her whatever she asked. Prompted by her mother, she requested John's head on a dish. The king was grieved, but his oath and the people sitting at the meal mattered to him. He commanded that her request be granted.", "John was killed in prison. What had been requested was brought to the girl, and she took it to her mother. Herod's promise, spoken during a celebration, had been carried out against a prisoner who had challenged his conduct. The company before whom he made the promise had helped shape what he was prepared to do.", "John's disciples came for his body, buried him and went to tell Jesus. Matthew shows the outcome first in Herod's fearful explanation of the reports about Jesus, then at the close in the grief and duties of the disciples who had to bury their teacher."], "lesson": "Herod allowed public embarrassment to outweigh what he knew he was about to do. John's costly witness makes the weakness behind Herod's display of authority plain.", "question": "Which pressures influenced Herod, and where do similar pressures arise in ordinary decisions?"},
  {"id": 91035, "title": "Bread for the Crowd", "subtitle": "Compassion when resources look small", "text": "Matthew 14:13–21", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 13, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus heard what had happened to John, he withdrew by boat to a deserted place apart. The people heard and followed him on foot from the towns. When he came out and saw the large crowd, he had compassion and healed their sick. The place he had chosen for withdrawal became a place where many people received his attention.", "Evening came. The disciples pointed out that the place was remote and the hour late. They suggested sending people into the villages to buy their own food. Jesus told them the people did not need to leave and asked the disciples to give them something to eat. They answered that they had only five loaves and two fish. Jesus told them to bring the food to him.", "He had the crowd sit on the grass. Taking the loaves and fish, he looked up to heaven, blessed the food and broke the loaves. He gave them to the disciples, and the disciples gave them to the crowd. The men who had first looked to the surrounding villages for provision were now passing food among the people themselves.", "Everybody ate and was satisfied. The disciples collected twelve baskets of what remained. Matthew gives the number as about five thousand men, besides women and children. The five loaves and two fish had looked insufficient when the disciples counted them. At the end of the account there was enough to feed the crowd and enough remaining to gather."], "lesson": "The disciples first saw a problem to send elsewhere. Jesus asked them to bring what they had and made them participants in caring for the crowd.", "question": "What changed when the disciples brought their limited food to Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91036, "title": "Peter Steps Out of the Boat", "subtitle": "Calling on Jesus when fear rises", "text": "Matthew 14:22–36", "chapters": [[39, 14]], "start": 22, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the crowd had eaten, Jesus sent the disciples into the boat to go ahead of him. He dismissed the people and went up the mountain by himself to pray. By evening he was still there alone. The boat was out on the lake, battered by the waves because the wind was against it.", "Late in the night Jesus came towards the boat, walking on the sea. The disciples were terrified and cried out, supposing it was a spirit. Jesus spoke to them at once. He told them to take courage, identified himself and told them not to be afraid. Peter asked Jesus to command him to come if it was really him. Jesus told him to come, and Peter climbed out of the boat and walked towards him on the water.", "When Peter noticed the strength of the wind, he became afraid and began to sink. He cried out to the Lord to save him. Jesus immediately stretched out his hand and caught him, then asked why he had doubted. When they got into the boat the wind ceased. Those in the boat worshipped Jesus and confessed that he truly was God's Son.", "They crossed to Gennesaret. When the people there recognised Jesus, they sent word through the district and brought the sick to him. They asked to touch even the edge of his garment, and those who did were made well. Matthew ends the crossing with recognition and renewed activity on the other shore."], "lesson": "Peter had enough trust to step out and enough need to cry for help. The account draws our attention to the hand Jesus extended and the confession the disciples made.", "question": "What did Peter do when he began to sink, and what did the others come to say about Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91037, "title": "Clean Hands and an Unclean Heart", "subtitle": "God's command above custom", "text": "Matthew 15:1–20", "chapters": [[39, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Scribes and Pharisees came from Jerusalem and asked why Jesus' disciples broke the elders' tradition by eating bread without washing their hands. Jesus responded with a question about a command from God. Honour was due to father and mother, yet their tradition permitted someone to declare what a parent might have received from him a gift devoted to God. The declaration could then excuse the person from giving the parent help.", "Jesus said they had set aside God's command for the sake of their tradition. He cited Isaiah's words about people honouring God with their lips while their hearts were far from him and treating human commands as doctrine. He then called the crowd and spoke directly to the question of what defiles a person.", "He said that what goes into the mouth does not defile a person. What comes out of the mouth does. The disciples told him the Pharisees were offended. He answered with the image of a plant his Father had not planted and of blind guides leading the blind. Peter asked him to explain the saying.", "Jesus distinguished what happens to food from what comes out of the heart. From the heart come evil thoughts, murder, adultery, sexual immorality, stealing, false testimony and slander. These are the things he said defile a person. The original question had been about hands at a meal. His answer took the disciples back to how a person treats parents and to what comes from within."], "lesson": "A religious custom becomes harmful when it makes room for neglect of a clear duty. Jesus points to the heart as the place where the deeper problem must be addressed.", "question": "Which duty did Jesus say the tradition could undermine, and how did it happen?"},
  {"id": 91038, "title": "A Persistent Mother and Another Hungry Crowd", "subtitle": "Mercy beyond familiar borders", "text": "Matthew 15:21–39", "chapters": [[39, 15]], "start": 21, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus withdrew to the region of Tyre and Sidon. A Canaanite woman cried out to him as the Son of David and begged for mercy because her daughter was severely afflicted by a demon. He did not answer her at first. The disciples asked him to send her away. Jesus spoke of being sent to the lost sheep of Israel. The woman came nearer, bowed before him and asked him to help her.", "Jesus used the image of children's bread that should not be thrown to the dogs. The woman answered that even the dogs ate crumbs falling from their masters' table. Jesus commended her great faith and told her that what she desired would be done. Matthew says her daughter was healed from that hour.", "Jesus went on beside the Sea of Galilee and sat on a mountain. People brought him many who were sick or disabled, and he healed them. When the crowd saw people who could not speak beginning to speak, the lame walking and the blind seeing, they glorified the God of Israel.", "After three days with the crowd, Jesus told his disciples he had compassion because the people had nothing to eat and might faint along the way. The disciples asked where they could find enough bread. They had seven loaves and a few fish. Jesus gave thanks, broke the food and gave it to the disciples to distribute. About four thousand men ate, besides women and children, and seven baskets remained. Then he dismissed the crowd and crossed by boat."], "lesson": "The woman keeps her request before Jesus and takes hold of the possibility of mercy in his own illustration. His answer gives weight to the faith of someone the disciples wanted sent away.", "question": "How did the woman answer Jesus' illustration, and what did he commend in her?"},
  {"id": 91039, "title": "Reading the Weather and Missing the Moment", "subtitle": "Discernment about teaching", "text": "Matthew 16:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Pharisees and Sadducees came together and asked Jesus to show them a sign from heaven. Matthew says they meant to test him. Jesus answered with a skill they already used. At evening they could look at the redness of the sky and expect fair weather. In the morning they could see a red and threatening sky and expect a storm. They could read the weather, yet they failed to discern the signs of the times. He gave them the sign of Jonah as his answer and left them.", "When the disciples reached the other side, they had forgotten to take bread. Jesus warned them to beware of the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees. The disciples discussed the warning among themselves and decided it must concern the bread they had left behind.", "Jesus knew what they were saying and asked why they were so occupied with having no bread. He called them people of little faith and directed their memory to the two crowds he had fed. How many baskets had they collected after the five loaves fed the five thousand? How many after the seven loaves fed the four thousand? They had seen how those two shortages ended.", "They then understood what Jesus meant. He had been warning them about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Remembering what he had done about bread helped them attend to what he was now saying about something else."], "lesson": "Concern about immediate provisions can make it harder to hear a warning on another matter. Jesus calls the disciples to remember what they had already seen him do.", "question": "How did the memory of the feedings help the disciples understand what Jesus meant by leaven?"},
  {"id": 91040, "title": "Peter's Confession and Jesus' Rebuke", "subtitle": "The Christ and the way of the cross", "text": "Matthew 16:13–28", "chapters": [[39, 16]], "start": 13, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus came to the district of Caesarea Philippi and asked his disciples what people were saying about the Son of Man. The answers included John the Baptist, Elijah, Jeremiah and one of the prophets. Then he put the question directly to them. Simon Peter answered that Jesus was the Christ, the Son of the living God.", "Jesus called Simon blessed and said his Father in heaven had revealed this to him. He spoke about Peter, the rock and the church he would build, and said the gates of death would not prevail against it. He spoke of the keys of the kingdom and of binding and loosing. Then he charged the disciples to tell no one that he was the Christ.", "From that time Jesus began telling them what lay ahead. He must go to Jerusalem, suffer at the hands of the elders, chief priests and scribes, be killed and be raised on the third day. Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him. Jesus told him to get behind him and called him a stumbling block, because his concern was with human things rather than the things of God.", "Jesus then spoke to anyone who would come after him. Following him meant denying oneself and taking up a cross. He asked what a person would gain by acquiring the whole world at the cost of his soul. Peter's answer had named Jesus rightly. His objection showed how much he still had to hear about the road Jesus would take."], "lesson": "Peter spoke truly about Jesus' identity and still resisted what Jesus said about his mission. Understanding who Jesus is requires listening when he describes the road he will take.", "question": "What does Peter accept in his confession, and what does he resist a few verses later?"},
  {"id": 91041, "title": "The Voice on the Mountain", "subtitle": "Listening to the beloved Son", "text": "Matthew 17:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Six days later Jesus took Peter, James and John up a high mountain apart from the others. His appearance changed before them. His face shone like the sun and his garments became bright as light. The disciples saw Moses and Elijah speaking with him.", "Peter said it was good for them to be there. He offered to make three shelters, one for Jesus, one for Moses and one for Elijah. While he was still speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them. A voice from the cloud called Jesus the beloved Son in whom he was well pleased and told them to listen to him. The disciples fell on their faces in fear.", "Jesus came to them, touched them and told them to get up and not be afraid. When they lifted their eyes, they saw Jesus alone. On the way down he told them to speak of the vision to no one until the Son of Man had risen from the dead. What they had seen on the mountain would have to be held alongside what he had been telling them about suffering and rising.", "The disciples asked why the scribes said Elijah must come first. Jesus said Elijah had already come, and people had failed to recognise him and had done to him what they pleased. He said the Son of Man would suffer in a similar way. The disciples understood that he was speaking about John the Baptist."], "lesson": "Peter had a proposal ready while the scene was still unfolding. The Father's instruction places listening to Jesus first.", "question": "What would it mean to obey the voice from the cloud as you read what follows?"},
  {"id": 91042, "title": "A Suffering Boy and a Tax Question", "subtitle": "Dependence and consideration", "text": "Matthew 17:14–27", "chapters": [[39, 17]], "start": 14, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When Jesus and the three disciples returned to the crowd, a man came and knelt before him. He asked Jesus to have mercy on his son, who suffered severely and often fell into fire or water. He had brought the boy to the disciples, but they had been unable to heal him. Jesus lamented the unbelief of the generation and asked for the boy to be brought to him. He rebuked the demon, and the boy was healed from that hour.", "The disciples came privately to ask why they had failed. Jesus spoke about their little faith. He pictured faith as small as a mustard seed and spoke of its power to move a mountain. Later, while they were in Galilee, he told them again that the Son of Man would be handed over and killed and would rise on the third day. They were deeply grieved.", "When they came to Capernaum, the collectors of the temple tax asked Peter whether his teacher paid it. Peter said he did. Back in the house, Jesus asked him whether earthly kings collected taxes from their own sons or from others. Peter answered that they collected from others. Jesus said the sons were free.", "He then made provision for the payment so they would not give offence. Peter was to catch a fish and find a coin in its mouth. It would cover the tax for Jesus and Peter. The passage gives Peter a reason for the payment as well as the means of making it."], "lesson": "The healing exposes the disciples' need for faith. The tax conversation shows Jesus considering the effect that exercising a freedom would have on others.", "question": "Why did Jesus choose to pay the tax after explaining that the sons were free?"},
  {"id": 91043, "title": "A Child in the Middle", "subtitle": "The Father's concern for little ones", "text": "Matthew 18:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The disciples came to Jesus with a question about who was greatest in the kingdom of heaven. Jesus called a child and set the child among them. Before naming anyone as greatest, he spoke about the change needed to enter the kingdom at all. They had to turn and become like children. Whoever humbled himself like that child was greatest, and whoever welcomed such a child in Jesus' name welcomed Jesus.", "He then turned to the danger of causing one of the little ones who believed in him to stumble. He used severe pictures to show how seriously he took the harm. He spoke of a millstone and the sea, and of removing even a hand, foot or eye if it led a person into sin. The desire to be important gave the disciples no excuse for overlooking the effect of their own conduct.", "Jesus told them not to despise any of the little ones. He spoke of their angels seeing his Father's face. Then he asked them to picture a man with a hundred sheep. If one wandered, the man would leave the others and go to look for the one that had strayed.", "If he found it, he would rejoice over that sheep. Jesus connected the search with his Father's will that none of the little ones should perish. The disciples' question about rank had led them to a child in their midst and then to a shepherd looking for one sheep."], "lesson": "Jesus answers ambition by giving attention to someone the disciples might overlook. The searching shepherd makes the value of one vulnerable person plain.", "question": "Whose welfare does Jesus direct the disciples to consider when they want to discuss rank?"},
  {"id": 91044, "title": "A Private Conversation and an Unpaid Debt", "subtitle": "Seeking restoration and giving forgiveness", "text": "Matthew 18:15–35", "chapters": [[39, 18]], "start": 15, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus gave his disciples a way to speak to a brother who had sinned against them. The first conversation should be private. If the brother listened, the relationship had been recovered. If he did not listen, one or two others should go along so the matter could be established by witnesses. Only after that would it come before the congregation. Jesus also spoke of agreement in prayer and his presence when people gathered in his name.", "Peter then asked how often he ought to forgive a brother who sinned against him. Would seven times be enough? Jesus answered with a much greater number and told of a king who began settling accounts with his servants. One owed him ten thousand talents. As he could not pay, the king ordered that he be sold with his household and possessions. The man pleaded for patience. The king had compassion and cancelled the debt.", "That servant found a fellow servant who owed him a hundred denarii. He took hold of him and demanded payment. The fellow servant pleaded for patience in much the same way, but the first servant had him put in prison. Other servants saw and brought the matter to the king.", "The king summoned the servant and asked why he had failed to give the mercy he had received. He handed him over for punishment until the debt should be paid. Jesus brought the account back to his disciples and the forgiveness they were called to give from the heart."], "lesson": "The first step aims to regain a brother. The parable asks whether a person who has received mercy is passing it on in the way he deals with others.", "question": "How did the first servant's treatment of his fellow servant contradict the mercy he had received?"},
  {"id": 91045, "title": "A Question About Marriage and a Welcome for Children", "subtitle": "Faithfulness and care for families", "text": "Matthew 19:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus left Galilee and travelled to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan. Crowds followed him, and he healed people there. Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked whether a man could put away his wife for any reason. Jesus took them back to creation, when God made male and female and said the two would become one flesh. He told them to take seriously what God had joined.", "They asked why Moses had directed that a certificate be given when a wife was put away. Jesus said Moses had permitted it because of the hardness of their hearts. He contrasted that permission with what was intended from the beginning and spoke of the gravity of divorce and remarriage. His disciples said that, if the obligations were so demanding, it might be better not to marry.", "Jesus answered that people did not all receive the same course in life. He spoke of different reasons a person might remain unmarried, including a choice for the sake of the kingdom of heaven. He left the saying with those able to receive it.", "Then people brought children for Jesus to lay his hands on and pray for them. The disciples rebuked those who brought them. Jesus told them to let the children come and said the kingdom of heaven belonged to such as these. He laid his hands on them before going on his way."], "lesson": "Jesus brings God's purpose and human hardness of heart into the discussion of marriage. His welcome of children reminds disciples to make room for those who depend on their care.", "question": "What does Jesus give attention to that the disciples were ready to dismiss?"},
  {"id": 91046, "title": "The Man Who Went Away Sad", "subtitle": "When possessions hold the heart", "text": "Matthew 19:16–30", "chapters": [[39, 19]], "start": 16, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["A young man came to Jesus asking what good thing he must do to have eternal life. Jesus directed him towards the commandments. When he asked which ones, Jesus named commands concerning murder, adultery, stealing, false testimony and honour towards parents. He included loving one's neighbour as oneself. The man said he had kept these from his youth and asked what he still lacked.", "Jesus told him that if he wished to be perfect, he should sell his possessions, give to the poor, and come and follow him. He spoke of treasure in heaven. The young man went away sorrowful because he had great possessions. The question he had brought to Jesus had become a decision about what he would carry into the life he was asking for.", "Jesus told the disciples how hard it was for a rich person to enter the kingdom of heaven. He used the picture of a camel passing through the eye of a needle. The disciples were astonished and asked who could be saved. Jesus looked at them and said that what was impossible for people was possible with God.", "Peter pointed out that they had left everything and followed him. Jesus spoke of what his followers would receive in the renewal and of the inheritance awaiting those who had left things for his name. He ended by saying that many who were first would be last, and the last first. The next story would give that reversal a place in a vineyard."], "lesson": "The man's sorrow reveals how a possession can become something we cannot imagine surrendering. Jesus turns the disciples from confidence in human ability to dependence on God.", "question": "Where does the man's response change, and what does that change reveal?"},
  {"id": 91047, "title": "The Workers Hired at the Last Hour", "subtitle": "Generosity and comparison", "text": "Matthew 20:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to a householder who went out early to hire workers for his vineyard. He agreed with the first men on a denarius for the day and sent them to work. Later in the morning he saw others standing in the marketplace. He sent them to the vineyard and promised to give what was right. He did the same around noon and again in the afternoon.", "Near the end of the day he found still more men standing there. He asked why they had been idle all day. They answered that no one had hired them. He sent them into his vineyard too. Their time among the vines would be much shorter than that of the men hired first.", "At evening the owner told his steward to call the workers and pay them, beginning with the last hired. The men who had come near the end each received a denarius. Those hired first expected more when they saw this. When they received the same sum, they complained that the others had worked only one hour while they had borne the heat and labour of the day.", "The owner answered one of them that he had paid what they agreed. He could choose to give the last man the same amount, and asked why his generosity provoked resentment. Jesus ended by returning to the reversal he had spoken of before: the last would be first and the first last."], "lesson": "Comparison turned an agreed wage into a grievance. The owner's question invites us to welcome generosity even when someone else receives its benefit.", "question": "What had the first workers been promised, and what made them feel wronged?"},
  {"id": 91048, "title": "A Request for Places and a Cry for Mercy", "subtitle": "Greatness takes the form of service", "text": "Matthew 20:17–34", "chapters": [[39, 20]], "start": 17, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus went up to Jerusalem, he took the Twelve aside on the road. He told them the Son of Man would be handed over to the chief priests and scribes, condemned and delivered to the Gentiles. He spoke of mockery, scourging and crucifixion, and said he would rise on the third day.", "Then the mother of the sons of Zebedee came with her sons and asked that they sit at Jesus' right and left in his kingdom. Jesus asked if they could drink his cup. They said they could. He told them they would share in his cup, while the places they requested were for those for whom his Father had prepared them. The other ten were indignant with the brothers.", "Jesus called them together. He described how rulers of the Gentiles exercised authority, then told his followers that greatness among them took the form of service. The Son of Man himself had come to serve and give his life as a ransom for many. His words about what would happen at Jerusalem now had a reason attached to them.", "As they left Jericho, two blind men by the roadside heard Jesus was passing and called for mercy. The crowd tried to silence them. They called louder. Jesus stopped, asked what they wanted and, moved with compassion, touched their eyes. They received sight and followed him. Along the same road where the disciples had heard about service, Jesus had stopped to listen to people the crowd wanted to quiet."], "lesson": "The disciples' dispute about rank is followed by Jesus attending to people a crowd wanted silenced. His conduct shows the kind of greatness he had just described.", "question": "How does Jesus' response to the two men illustrate his teaching about service?"},
  {"id": 91049, "title": "The King Arrives on a Donkey", "subtitle": "Recognising the purpose of worship", "text": "Matthew 21:1–17", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["As Jesus approached Jerusalem, he sent two disciples into a village near the Mount of Olives. They would find a donkey tied there and a colt with her. They were to untie and bring them, saying if challenged that the Lord needed them. They followed his instructions. Matthew connects Jesus' entry with the prophet's picture of a meek king coming to his people riding on a donkey.", "The disciples put garments on the animals, and Jesus rode towards the city. Many spread their garments on the road, while others cut branches from trees and laid them down. The people ahead and behind cried Hosanna to the Son of David. When Jerusalem was stirred and people asked who this was, the crowd identified him as Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee.", "Jesus went into the temple and drove out those buying and selling. He overturned the tables of the money changers and the seats of people selling doves. He spoke of God's house as a house of prayer and said they had made it a den of thieves. Blind and lame people came to him there, and he healed them.", "The chief priests and scribes saw the things he did and heard children crying Hosanna in the temple. They objected. Jesus answered from Scripture about praise coming from the mouths of children. He then left the city and lodged at Bethany."], "lesson": "The entry and the temple visit reveal what matters to this king. He defends the purpose of prayer and receives people who come to him in need.", "question": "What actions and voices does Matthew bring together in the temple after Jesus enters Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 91050, "title": "The Fig Tree and Two Sons", "subtitle": "Fruit in conduct", "text": "Matthew 21:18–32", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 18, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Returning to the city in the morning, Jesus was hungry. He approached a fig tree by the road and found only leaves. When he told it that it would bear no fruit again, it withered. The disciples wondered how it had happened so suddenly. Jesus answered with teaching about faith and prayer and spoke of what faith could ask of God.", "In the temple, chief priests and elders wanted to know what authority he had and who gave it to him. Jesus asked them a question in return. Did John's baptism come from heaven or from people? They reasoned that if they said it was from heaven, Jesus would ask why they had not believed John. If they said it was from people, they feared the crowd, which regarded John as a prophet. They answered that they did not know.", "Jesus then told them about two sons. Their father asked each to work in the vineyard. The first refused and later changed his mind and went. The second said he would go and did not. Jesus asked which son had done his father's will. They gave the answer themselves: the first.", "Jesus applied it to their response to John. Tax collectors and prostitutes had believed him, while those questioning Jesus still had not changed their minds after seeing that response. The parable gave the leaders a clear example of the change they had refused to make."], "lesson": "The leaders' calculation stands beside a parable that makes obedience concrete. A sincere change of mind can be seen in what a person goes on to do.", "question": "Which son did his father's will, and how does Jesus apply the answer to John's ministry?"},
  {"id": 91051, "title": "The Tenants and the Son", "subtitle": "Answering to the owner", "text": "Matthew 21:33–46", "chapters": [[39, 21]], "start": 33, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a householder who planted a vineyard, set a hedge around it, dug a winepress and built a tower. He leased it to tenants and went away. At harvest he sent servants to receive his fruit. The tenants seized the servants, beating one, killing another and stoning another. The owner sent more servants, and they treated them in the same way.", "Finally the owner sent his son, expecting the tenants to respect him. They recognised him as the heir and saw a chance to take the inheritance for themselves. They seized him, threw him out of the vineyard and killed him. Their conduct made plain what they thought the owner's rights were worth when he was away.", "Jesus asked what the owner would do when he came. His hearers said the owner would judge the tenants severely and lease the vineyard to others who would give him the fruit in season. Jesus then recalled the words of Scripture about the stone rejected by builders becoming the cornerstone, something the Lord had done.", "Jesus spoke of the kingdom being given to people who would produce its fruit. The chief priests and Pharisees understood that he was speaking about them. They wanted to seize him, but held back because the crowds regarded him as a prophet. The men who had answered his question about the vineyard now recognised where he had directed it."], "lesson": "The tenants treated something entrusted to them as their possession. The story presses anyone entrusted with responsibility to consider what fruit belongs to God.", "question": "What do the tenants' responses to the servants and the son reveal about how they viewed the vineyard?"},
  {"id": 91052, "title": "The Wedding Feast", "subtitle": "Taking the invitation seriously", "text": "Matthew 22:1–14", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a king who prepared a wedding feast for his son. When he sent servants to call those already invited, they would not come. He sent others with a fuller announcement. The meal was ready, the animals had been prepared and the guests were to come to the wedding.", "Some made light of the summons and went away, one to his farm and another to his business. Others seized the servants, mistreated them and killed them. The king was angry and judged those responsible. Then he told his servants that the wedding was ready and the first guests had shown themselves unworthy. They were to go to the roads and invite whomever they found.", "The servants gathered people, both bad and good, and the hall filled with guests. When the king came to see them, he noticed a man without a wedding garment. He addressed the man and asked how he had come in without one. The man had no answer. The king ordered his attendants to remove him from the feast.", "Jesus closed by saying that many are called and few chosen. The story carries the reader from people who dismissed the invitation to a full hall with guests drawn from the roads. Even there, the king's question makes the response of a person who has accepted the invitation a matter to consider."], "lesson": "The invitation reaches widely, even beyond the doors first visited. The king's inspection keeps the hearer from treating an invitation as a reason for indifference.", "question": "What different responses to the king do you find in those invited and in the guest questioned inside?"},
  {"id": 91053, "title": "A Coin and a Question About Resurrection", "subtitle": "God's claim and God's power", "text": "Matthew 22:15–33", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 15, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The Pharisees considered how to trap Jesus in what he said. They sent their disciples with the Herodians. They began with praise for his truthfulness and then asked whether it was lawful to pay tribute to Caesar. Jesus recognised what they were doing and asked them to show him the coin used for the tax.", "They brought him a denarius. He asked whose image and inscription it carried. They answered that they were Caesar's. He told them to give Caesar what belonged to Caesar and God what belonged to God. His hearers marvelled, left him and went away.", "That same day Sadducees, who said there was no resurrection, brought him another question. They described a woman who had been married in turn to seven brothers after each previous husband died, without leaving children. Then the woman died. They asked whose wife she would be in the resurrection. Their question assumed that the arrangements of this life would carry over in the same way.", "Jesus said they had failed to understand both the Scriptures and God's power. He explained that marriage did not continue in that way in the resurrection. Then he appealed to God's words about being the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and said God was the God of the living. The crowd was astonished at his teaching. Both questions had been framed as traps, and both answers had brought God's authority into view."], "lesson": "Each question tried to confine the answer to its questioner's assumptions. Jesus answered by directing attention to God's claim on people and his power over death.", "question": "Why did Jesus ask about the coin's image before replying to the question about tax?"},
  {"id": 91054, "title": "The Greatest Commandments and David's Lord", "subtitle": "Love at the centre of obedience", "text": "Matthew 22:34–46", "chapters": [[39, 22]], "start": 34, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["When the Pharisees heard that Jesus had silenced the Sadducees, they gathered. One of them, a lawyer, asked him which commandment was greatest in the law. Matthew says he asked to test him. Jesus answered by calling for love of the Lord one's God with all the heart, soul and mind. He identified it as the first and greatest commandment.", "He then named a second like it: loving one's neighbour as oneself. He said the law and the prophets hung on these two. The lawyer had asked for one commandment to be named. Jesus gave him the first and showed how closely it was joined to the conduct owed to another person.", "With the Pharisees still gathered, Jesus asked them a question of his own. Whose son was the Christ? They answered that he was David's son. Jesus asked why David, speaking in the Spirit, called him Lord. He cited David's words about the Lord giving that person a place at his right hand until his enemies were put beneath his feet.", "If David called him Lord, Jesus asked, how was he his son? No one was able to answer, and Matthew says they did not dare question him further from that day. The exchange that began with a question about reading the law ends with a question about reading what David said about the Christ."], "lesson": "Love for God and neighbour holds the commands together. Jesus' own question invites the hearers to give closer attention to the identity of the one giving that answer.", "question": "How would the two commands guide you through a decision where your duty to a neighbour is easy to overlook?"},
  {"id": 91055, "title": "The Seats of Honour", "subtitle": "Serving without seeking rank", "text": "Matthew 23:1–12", "chapters": [[39, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus turned from the exchanges with his questioners to speak to the crowds and his disciples. He said the scribes and Pharisees sat in Moses' seat. His hearers should pay attention to what they told them to observe, while recognising that their conduct could fail to match their teaching. Jesus pictured them binding heavy burdens and laying them on other people's shoulders without lifting a finger to move them.", "He spoke about the desire to be seen. Some made the cases holding Scripture on their bodies conspicuously broad or made the fringes of their garments wide. They sought the places of honour at feasts and the best seats in synagogues. They enjoyed greetings in the marketplaces and being called Rabbi. Each setting gave people a chance to recognise their importance.", "Jesus then addressed his own disciples. He directed them away from titles that placed them above one another and reminded them that they were brothers with one teacher and one Father in heaven. They were to give Christ his place as their guide. A community hearing this teaching would have to consider how it spoke of those who taught and how those teachers carried themselves.", "Jesus gave them a different way to recognise greatness. The greatest among them would be their servant. He ended by saying that whoever exalted himself would be humbled and whoever humbled himself would be exalted. The teaching begins with burdens carried by ordinary people and ends by asking what those with standing will do for them."], "lesson": "Teaching puts an obligation on the one who teaches. Jesus directs attention to quiet service when titles and public honour begin to matter more.", "question": "Where might a person make a duty heavier for others while declining to help with it?"},
  {"id": 91056, "title": "The Cup, the Tomb and the City", "subtitle": "Justice, mercy and faithfulness", "text": "Matthew 23:13–39", "chapters": [[39, 23]], "start": 13, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus pronounced a series of woes against scribes and Pharisees. He spoke of shutting the kingdom of heaven in people's faces and travelling far to gain a convert without giving him a better way to live. He exposed distinctions they drew between swearing by the temple and its gold, or by the altar and the gift on it. He directed attention back to what made those things holy.", "He spoke of tithes on small herbs alongside larger duties they had neglected: judgement, mercy and faithfulness. He pictured people straining out a tiny gnat and swallowing a camel. Then he compared their outward and inward lives to a cup cleaned outside while greed and excess remained within, and to a tomb made beautiful outside while holding what was unclean.", "Jesus addressed those who decorated the tombs of prophets and said they would never have shared in killing them. He warned that messengers would still be persecuted and killed. The treatment of the people he would send would show whether the honour paid to earlier prophets had changed anything.", "The speech ends in lament. Jesus addressed Jerusalem as the city that killed prophets and stoned those sent to it. He spoke of longing to gather its children like a hen sheltering her brood under her wings, and of their refusal. He spoke of their house being left desolate. The last picture gives the warning a voice of grief."], "lesson": "Outward care can coexist with a neglected inner life. Jesus names justice, mercy and faithfulness plainly, and his lament shows grief over the people he rebukes.", "question": "Which weightier matter did Jesus name that calls for greater attention in your conduct?"},
  {"id": 91057, "title": "The Stones and the Warnings", "subtitle": "Steadiness in troubled times", "text": "Matthew 24:1–28", "chapters": [[39, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus left the temple and the disciples drew his attention to its buildings. He told them a day was coming when one stone would not be left on another. Later he sat on the Mount of Olives, and the disciples approached him privately. They asked when these things would happen and what sign would mark his coming and the end of the age.", "Jesus began by warning them against being deceived. Many would come claiming to be the Christ. There would be wars, reports of wars, famines and earthquakes, yet these would be the beginning of sorrows. His followers would face hatred and betrayal. False prophets would mislead people, and as wrongdoing increased, the love of many would grow cold. He spoke of endurance and of the gospel of the kingdom going out to all nations.", "He then spoke of the desolating sight described by Daniel and the urgency of leaving when it appeared. He pictured a person on a roof with no time to fetch things from the house and someone in a field with no time to return for a garment. He asked his hearers to pray about the circumstances of their flight.", "Finally he warned about reports that the Christ was in the wilderness or in a private room. False signs could make those reports persuasive. He compared the coming of the Son of Man to lightning that crosses the sky. The disciples had asked how to recognise the time. His answer gave them reasons to be discerning under pressure."], "lesson": "Jesus prepares the disciples for pressure and deception. His warnings direct them to endure and keep their love alive when fear makes dramatic claims easy to believe.", "question": "Which dangers in the passage arise within people themselves, as well as around them?"},
  {"id": 91058, "title": "The Unannounced Hour", "subtitle": "Readiness in ordinary responsibilities", "text": "Matthew 24:29–51", "chapters": [[39, 24]], "start": 29, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus went on to describe signs in the heavens after the distress and the coming of the Son of Man with power and glory. He spoke of angels gathering his elect. Then he pointed to a fig tree. When its branches become tender and put out leaves, people recognise that summer is near. His words would remain even though heaven and earth passed away.", "Jesus said the day and hour were unknown. He recalled Noah's time, when people carried on eating, drinking and marrying until the flood came. He pictured people side by side at work and yet met by different outcomes. The usual tasks of the day could give little warning of what was about to interrupt them.", "A householder who knew the hour a thief would come would keep watch. Jesus called his disciples to be ready because the Son of Man would come at an hour they did not expect. He then pictured a servant appointed to give the people of a household their food in season. The master would find that servant blessed if he was still doing the work when he returned.", "Another servant told himself that the master was delayed and used the time to mistreat his fellow servants and indulge himself. His master returned when he did not expect him. Jesus gives the reader two ways of living in the interval: carrying out the responsibility at hand or using the delay as permission to neglect it."], "lesson": "Jesus gives readiness an ordinary shape by describing work that a faithful servant does while waiting. Uncertainty about the hour gives present duties greater weight.", "question": "What is the faithful servant doing when the master returns?"},
  {"id": 91059, "title": "The Lamps at Midnight", "subtitle": "Preparing before the call comes", "text": "Matthew 25:1–13", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus said the kingdom of heaven would be like ten young women who took lamps and went out to meet a bridegroom. Five were wise and five foolish. All took lamps. The wise also took oil in vessels. The difference in what they carried would matter later, when there was little time to put it right.", "The bridegroom took longer than they expected. All of them grew drowsy and slept. At midnight someone cried that the bridegroom was coming and that they should go out to meet him. All ten got up and put their lamps in order. The foolish saw that their lamps were going out and asked the others for some oil.", "The wise answered that there might not be enough for all of them. They told the others to go and buy some. While they were away, the bridegroom arrived. Those who were ready went in with him to the wedding and the door was shut.", "Later the other women came and asked for the door to be opened. The bridegroom answered that he did not know them. Jesus concluded by telling his hearers to watch because they did not know the day or hour. All ten had expected the same bridegroom and all had slept during the delay. Their different outcomes came from what they had brought while there was still time to prepare."], "lesson": "The delay exposed a difference in preparation. Jesus' call to watch asks hearers to take responsibility while there is still time to act.", "question": "What did the delay reveal that was already true when the women set out?"},
  {"id": 91060, "title": "The Servants and the Entrusted Money", "subtitle": "Faithfulness with what is in your hands", "text": "Matthew 25:14–30", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 14, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who was going on a journey. He called his servants and entrusted his possessions to them. He gave one five talents, another two and another one, to each according to his ability. Then he left. The two servants who had received more put what they had to work and each gained an equal amount in addition. The third dug a hole and hid his master's money.", "After a long time the master returned to settle accounts. The man with five talents brought five more. The master called him a good and faithful servant and invited him to share his joy. The man with two brought two more and received the same welcome. Their responsibilities had differed, but the master commended each for being faithful with what he had received.", "The last servant came with the single talent. He described his master as a hard man and said he had been afraid. That was why he had hidden the money. He handed it back. The master asked why, given what the servant claimed to know about him, he had not at least entrusted the money to bankers so it could gain interest.", "The master took the talent from him and gave it to the servant with ten. The unprofitable servant was put outside. The account follows what each servant did during the master's absence and what those choices looked like once he returned."], "lesson": "The first two received unequal amounts and the same commendation. The parable turns attention to faithful use of opportunity and exposes the inactivity that fear can excuse.", "question": "What opportunity or responsibility are you tempted to bury because the result is uncertain?"},
  {"id": 91061, "title": "The King Remembers the Least", "subtitle": "Mercy in concrete acts", "text": "Matthew 25:31–46", "chapters": [[39, 25]], "start": 31, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus described the Son of Man coming in glory with the angels and sitting on his throne. The nations would be gathered before him, and he would separate people as a shepherd separates sheep from goats. The king would invite the people on his right to inherit the kingdom prepared for them. He would remember being hungry, thirsty, a stranger, in need of clothing, sick and in prison, and receiving their care.", "Those people would ask when they had seen him in any of those conditions. The king would answer that what they had done to one of the least of his brothers, they had done to him. They had been able to see the need before them and act on it without knowing the significance the king would give their acts.", "He would then address those on his left. He named the same conditions and said he had received no food, drink, welcome, clothing or visit from them. They too would ask when they had seen him in need and failed to serve him. His answer would put the people whose needs they had passed by at the centre of the judgement.", "Jesus ended by speaking of punishment and eternal life. The two groups hear the same list of ordinary needs. Each asks when it met the king. His answer gives weight to what happened when one person had occasion to care for another."], "lesson": "The judgement brings ordinary acts of care into view. Jesus gives weight to needs that can be overlooked because the person in front of us seems unimportant.", "question": "Which act of care named by the king could you offer to someone this week?"},
  {"id": 91062, "title": "An Anointing in Bethany and a Bargain", "subtitle": "Two ways of valuing Jesus", "text": "Matthew 26:1–16", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After Jesus finished teaching, he told the disciples that Passover was two days away and that the Son of Man would be handed over to be crucified. The chief priests, scribes and elders gathered at the house of Caiaphas, the high priest. They considered how to seize Jesus by stealth and kill him. They wanted to avoid doing it during the feast for fear of a disturbance among the people.", "In Bethany Jesus was at the house of Simon the leper. A woman came with an alabaster jar of very costly ointment and poured it on his head while he reclined at the table. The disciples were indignant and called it waste. They said the ointment could have been sold for a large sum and the money given to the poor.", "Jesus asked why they were troubling the woman. He said she had done a good work for him. They would always have poor people with them, while his own time with them was limited. He connected what she had done to his burial and said her act would be remembered wherever the gospel was preached.", "Then Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, went to the chief priests and asked what they would give him for handing Jesus over. They agreed on thirty pieces of silver. From then on he looked for an opportunity. Matthew moves directly from a gift whose cost the disciples questioned to the sum one of them accepted for betrayal."], "lesson": "Matthew places a costly gift alongside a price accepted for betrayal. The two scenes ask what our choices reveal about the value we give Jesus.", "question": "How does reading the bargain immediately after the anointing sharpen your understanding of both events?"},
  {"id": 91063, "title": "The Passover Table and Peter's Promise", "subtitle": "The covenant and human weakness", "text": "Matthew 26:17–35", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 17, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["On the first day of the feast of unleavened bread, the disciples asked Jesus where they should prepare the Passover. He sent them into the city to a particular man and told them what to say about keeping the feast at his house. They went and prepared it. When evening came, Jesus sat at the table with the Twelve.", "As they ate, Jesus said that one of them would betray him. They were deeply troubled and began asking one by one whether it was them. He spoke of the one who dipped with him in the dish. The Son of Man would go as Scripture said, but Jesus pronounced woe upon the betrayer. Judas asked whether he was the one, and Jesus answered him.", "While they were eating, Jesus took bread, blessed and broke it and gave it to them to eat, speaking of his body. Then he took a cup, gave thanks and gave it to them. He spoke of his covenant blood poured out for many for the forgiveness of sins. He said he would drink the fruit of the vine with them again in his Father's kingdom.", "They sang a hymn and went out to the Mount of Olives. Jesus told them they would fall away that night, recalling the words about the shepherd being struck and the sheep scattered. He also spoke of going before them to Galilee after he was raised. Peter insisted he would remain loyal. Jesus said Peter would deny him three times before the rooster crowed. Peter and the others protested."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the cup in a room where betrayal and weakness are already present. Peter's promise invites us to pray for the faithfulness we are quick to claim.", "question": "What does the passage reveal about Jesus' purpose and Peter's confidence in himself?"},
  {"id": 91064, "title": "Prayer and Arrest in Gethsemane", "subtitle": "Obedience in anguish", "text": "Matthew 26:36–56", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 36, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Jesus came with the disciples to Gethsemane and asked them to sit while he went to pray. He took Peter and the two sons of Zebedee farther with him and began to be deeply distressed. He told them his soul was sorrowful to the point of death and asked them to stay and watch with him. Then he went a little farther, fell on his face and asked his Father whether the cup might pass, yielding the matter to his Father's will.", "When he returned, he found the three asleep. He asked Peter whether they could watch with him for one hour and told them to watch and pray against temptation. He went away to pray again, now saying that if the cup could not pass without his drinking it, the Father's will should be done. He found them asleep again and went away a third time to pray.", "By the time he returned, Judas was approaching with a crowd carrying swords and clubs. Judas had arranged a kiss as the sign identifying Jesus. He greeted him and kissed him, and the crowd seized him. One of the people with Jesus drew a sword and struck the high priest's servant, cutting off his ear.", "Jesus told him to put away the sword. He spoke of the help he could ask of his Father and of the Scriptures being fulfilled through what was happening. He reminded the crowd that he had taught openly in the temple. As the arrest went ahead, the disciples left him and fled."], "lesson": "Jesus brings his distress before the Father and yields to him. The disciples' sleep gives urgency to his instruction to watch and pray before the trial reaches them.", "question": "What does Jesus pray for himself, and what does he urge the disciples to pray about?"},
  {"id": 91065, "title": "Inside the Hearing and Outside by the Fire", "subtitle": "Truth under pressure", "text": "Matthew 26:57–75", "chapters": [[39, 26]], "start": 57, "end": 75, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["Those who had seized Jesus took him to Caiaphas, where the scribes and elders had gathered. Peter followed at a distance and sat with the servants in the courtyard to see what would happen. Inside, the chief priests and council sought testimony against Jesus to put him to death. Many false witnesses came, and finally two spoke about his words concerning the temple.", "The high priest asked Jesus what answer he had to the witnesses. Jesus remained silent. Then the high priest put him under oath and asked whether he was the Christ, the Son of God. Jesus answered and spoke of the Son of Man sitting at the right hand of power and coming with the clouds. The high priest tore his clothes. The council declared him deserving of death, and people spat on and struck him.", "Outside, a servant girl told Peter that he had been with Jesus of Galilee. Peter denied it before the people there. He went towards the entrance, and another girl said to those nearby that he had been with Jesus of Nazareth. This time he denied it with an oath. Later others said his way of speaking gave him away. Peter began to curse and swear that he did not know the man.", "The rooster crowed. Peter remembered how Jesus had told him he would deny him three times before it crowed. He went out and wept bitterly. Matthew leaves the courtyard with Peter remembering words he had been sure would never come true."], "lesson": "Matthew places Jesus' answer alongside Peter's denials. Peter's tears begin with remembering the word he had dismissed earlier that night.", "question": "What brought Peter to recognise what he had done?"},
  {"id": 91066, "title": "Silver Returned and a Prisoner Released", "subtitle": "Responsibility under pressure", "text": "Matthew 27:1–26", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["In the morning the chief priests and elders agreed to put Jesus to death. They bound him and delivered him to Pilate the governor. When Judas saw that Jesus had been condemned, he brought back the thirty pieces of silver and confessed that he had betrayed innocent blood. The chief priests and elders told him that was his concern. Judas threw the money down in the temple and left. He died by suicide.", "The priests decided the silver could not go into the treasury because it was the price of blood. They used it to buy the potter's field as a burial place for strangers. Matthew relates this to words spoken through the prophet Jeremiah.", "Before Pilate, Jesus was asked whether he was the King of the Jews. He answered that question, then made no reply to the accusations made against him. Pilate was amazed. At the feast the governor customarily released a prisoner of the crowd's choice. He offered them Barabbas or Jesus. While he sat on the judgement seat, his wife sent him a warning based on a troubling dream.", "The chief priests and elders persuaded the crowds to ask for Barabbas and demand Jesus' death. Pilate asked what wrong Jesus had done, but the cries grew louder. Seeing a disturbance forming, he washed his hands in front of the crowd and declared himself innocent of the blood. Then he released Barabbas, had Jesus scourged and handed him over to be crucified."], "lesson": "The priests' reply to Judas and Pilate's gesture both attempt to put responsibility elsewhere. The sequence invites us to face what our decisions actually set in motion.", "question": "What does Pilate do after washing his hands, and why does the order of those actions matter?"},
  {"id": 91067, "title": "The Mocked King on the Cross", "subtitle": "Faithfulness through humiliation", "text": "Matthew 27:27–44", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 27, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The governor's soldiers took Jesus inside and gathered their company around him. They put a scarlet robe on him, made a crown of thorns and placed a reed in his right hand. Kneeling before him, they mocked him as King of the Jews. They spat on him and struck him. When they had finished, they put his own clothes back on him and led him away.", "On the way they compelled Simon, a man from Cyrene, to carry the cross. At Golgotha, a place whose name Matthew explains as the place of a skull, they offered Jesus a drink that he tasted and refused. They crucified him and cast lots for his clothes. Above his head they set the charge calling him Jesus the King of the Jews. Two others were crucified with him, one on either side.", "People passing by shook their heads and challenged him to save himself. They recalled words about the temple and told him to come down if he was God's Son. Chief priests, scribes and elders also mocked him. They said he had saved others but could not save himself, and demanded that he come down if they were to believe him. They spoke of his trust in God as another reason to taunt him.", "Even the men crucified beside him joined in. The title the soldiers had used in mockery now stood above his head in writing, and different people below were using it to say what they thought he must do."], "lesson": "The mockers treated escape as the proof of who Jesus was. Matthew has already shown Jesus choosing the path that would bring him to this place.", "question": "How do the mockers' challenges sound in light of what Jesus prayed in Gethsemane?"},
  {"id": 91068, "title": "The Torn Curtain and the Sealed Tomb", "subtitle": "Witness in the hour of grief", "text": "Matthew 27:45–66", "chapters": [[39, 27]], "start": 45, "end": 66, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["From noon until mid-afternoon darkness lay over the land. Jesus cried out to God in words some standing there thought called for Elijah. Someone brought him sour wine, and others waited to see whether Elijah would come. Jesus cried out again and died. Matthew describes the temple curtain tearing from top to bottom, an earthquake, rocks splitting and graves opening. He also records that after the resurrection people came out of those graves and appeared in the city.", "The centurion and those watching with him were frightened by what they saw and called Jesus God's Son. Women who had followed him from Galilee and served him were watching from a distance. Matthew names Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and the mother of the sons of Zebedee.", "At evening Joseph of Arimathea, a wealthy man who had become Jesus' disciple, asked Pilate for the body. Pilate ordered it released. Joseph wrapped it in clean linen and laid it in his own new tomb, which had been cut in rock. He rolled a large stone to the entrance and went away. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary remained sitting opposite the tomb.", "The next day the chief priests and Pharisees asked Pilate to secure the tomb. They recalled Jesus' words about rising after three days and feared the disciples might take the body. Pilate told them to make it as secure as they could. They sealed the stone and set a guard."], "lesson": "Joseph and the women remain present after public disgrace and death. The passage gives quiet acts of care and witness a place beside events of great public consequence.", "question": "Who remained close enough to witness the burial, and what did Joseph do?"},
  {"id": 91069, "title": "The Women at the Tomb", "subtitle": "Fear, joy and truthful witness", "text": "Matthew 28:1–15", "chapters": [[39, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["After the Sabbath, as the first day of the week was dawning, Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to see the tomb. There was an earthquake. An angel of the Lord came down, rolled back the stone and sat on it. The guards were terrified and became like dead men. The angel addressed the women and told them not to be afraid.", "He knew they were seeking Jesus who had been crucified. Jesus was not there, he said, because he had risen as he had said. The women were invited to see where he had lain. Then they were told to hurry to the disciples with the news and say that Jesus would go ahead of them to Galilee, where they would see him.", "The women left the tomb with fear and great joy and ran to tell the disciples. Jesus met them on the way. They came to him, took hold of his feet and worshipped him. He also told them not to be afraid and sent them to tell his brothers to go to Galilee.", "Meanwhile some of the guards went into the city and told the chief priests what had happened. The leaders consulted and gave them a large sum of money to say the disciples stole the body while they slept. They promised to deal with the governor if necessary. The guards did as they were told. Matthew follows the news along those two roads, to the disciples and to the leaders."], "lesson": "The women carried their message with fear and great joy. The payment to the guards shows the pressure that can surround the telling of what one has witnessed.", "question": "What did the women see and hear before Jesus sent them on their way?"},
  {"id": 91070, "title": "The Last Meeting on the Mountain", "subtitle": "Making disciples with Jesus' presence", "text": "Matthew 28:16–20", "chapters": [[39, 28]], "start": 16, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Matthew", "story": ["The eleven disciples went into Galilee and came to the mountain Jesus had appointed. They had been sent there with a promise that they would see him. When they saw him, they worshipped him. Matthew also records that some doubted. This last scene includes the different responses of people who had come to the same place.", "Jesus came to them and spoke first about his authority. All authority in heaven and on earth had been given to him. That was the basis on which he sent them out. They were to go and make disciples of all nations. The work was to reach people beyond the circle that had stood with him in Galilee.", "He told them to baptise in the name of the Father, the Son and the Holy Spirit. He also told them to teach people to observe all he had commanded. Earlier in the Gospel these disciples had heard his teaching, seen his acts and at times been corrected by him. Now they were being given responsibility to teach others to practise what he had taught.", "Jesus ended with a promise that he would be with them always, to the end of the age. Matthew began by naming Jesus Emmanuel, God with us. The Gospel closes with Jesus assuring the people he sends out that his presence will go with them as they do the work he gave them."], "lesson": "Jesus joins his authority to a command and a promise. Teaching people to practise what he commanded takes sustained attention, and the work rests on his continuing presence.", "question": "Which parts of Jesus' commission require a relationship that continues after a person first hears the message?"},
  {"id": 91071, "title": "The Road, the River and the Wilderness", "subtitle": "The beginning of the good news", "text": "Mark 1:1–13", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Mark opens by calling what follows the beginning of the good news of Jesus Christ, the Son of God. He recalls the prophetic promise of a messenger who would prepare the Lord's way. Then he takes the reader to the wilderness, where John called people to repent and be baptised for the forgiveness of sins. People came out from Judea and Jerusalem, confessed their sins and were baptised in the Jordan.", "John wore camel's hair with a leather belt and ate locusts and wild honey. Mark gives those details and then turns to what John said about the person coming after him. Someone mightier was on the way. John did not regard himself as worthy even to stoop and untie his sandals. He baptised with water, but the coming one would baptise with the Holy Spirit.", "Jesus came from Nazareth in Galilee and was baptised by John in the Jordan. Coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens opening and the Spirit descending like a dove. A voice from heaven addressed him as the beloved Son in whom the Father was well pleased.", "The Spirit immediately drove him into the wilderness, where he remained forty days and was tempted by Satan. Mark mentions the wild animals and the angels who ministered to him. He gives no account here of the tempter's words. He carries the reader directly from the Father's declaration at the river to the Son's testing in the wilderness."], "lesson": "The Father's declaration precedes the wilderness. A place of testing can come directly after a clear word of assurance.", "question": "What does Mark tell you about Jesus before he records his first public words?"},
  {"id": 91072, "title": "From the Nets to the Doorway", "subtitle": "Authority that reaches ordinary homes", "text": "Mark 1:14–34", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 14, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After John was put in prison, Jesus came to Galilee proclaiming God's good news. He said that the time had been fulfilled and God's kingdom had drawn near. People were to repent and believe. Beside the sea he saw Simon and Andrew casting a net and called them to follow him. They left their nets. Farther on, James and John left the boat where they had been mending nets with their father and the hired men.", "They went to Capernaum. On the Sabbath Jesus taught in the synagogue, where people were astonished because he spoke with authority. A man with an unclean spirit cried out and called him the Holy One of God. Jesus ordered the spirit to be silent and come out. It left the man, and the people began asking one another what kind of teaching they had heard.", "Jesus and the four men went from the synagogue to the home of Simon and Andrew. Simon's mother-in-law was lying sick with a fever. They told Jesus about her, and he took her by the hand and lifted her up. The fever left her, and she began serving them.", "At sunset people brought those who were ill or afflicted by demons. Mark describes the town gathered at the door. Jesus healed many and would not allow the demons to speak. The men called from the shore had seen him teach in public and then attend to the needs of a household."], "lesson": "Mark follows Jesus from public teaching to a sickbed in a home. His authority has a personal reach, and the healed woman answers with service.", "question": "What does Jesus do for Simon's mother-in-law, and what is her first recorded response?"},
  {"id": 91073, "title": "The Place of Prayer and the Man Outside", "subtitle": "Compassion guided by purpose", "text": "Mark 1:35–45", "chapters": [[40, 1]], "start": 35, "end": 45, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Before daylight Jesus got up and went out to a solitary place. He prayed there. Simon and his companions went looking for him, and when they found him they said everyone was seeking him. The night before, the town had brought its sick to the door. Now the disciples reported that people still wanted him.", "Jesus said they should go on to the nearby towns so he could preach there as well. He named preaching as the purpose for which he had come out. He travelled through Galilee, preaching in the synagogues and driving out demons. His answer to Simon took them beyond the place where people already knew to seek him.", "A man with leprosy came, knelt and asked to be made clean if Jesus was willing. Jesus had compassion, stretched out his hand and touched him. He said he was willing. The leprosy left him immediately. Jesus charged him to say nothing about it and sent him to show himself to the priest and offer what Moses had commanded.", "The man went out and spread the news freely. As a result Jesus could no longer go openly into a town. He stayed in places outside, and people came to him from every direction. Mark begins the passage with Jesus withdrawing to pray. It ends with him outside the towns again, this time with people knowing where to find him."], "lesson": "The search of the crowd did not settle where Jesus would go. Prayer and the purpose for which he came help us read his decision to move on.", "question": "How did Jesus respond when told that everyone was looking for him?"},
  {"id": 91074, "title": "Through the Roof and Around the Table", "subtitle": "Forgiveness and fellowship", "text": "Mark 2:1–17", "chapters": [[40, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus returned to Capernaum, word spread that he was in a house. So many people came that there was no room, even at the door, while he spoke to them. Four men carrying a paralysed man could not bring him through. They opened the roof above Jesus and lowered the man on his bed into the room.", "Jesus saw their faith and addressed the man as a son. He said his sins were forgiven. Some scribes sitting there reasoned inwardly that this was blasphemy, since only God can forgive sins. Jesus knew what they were thinking and asked which was easier to say: that the man's sins were forgiven, or that he should rise and walk. To show his authority to forgive on earth, he told the man to rise, take up his bed and go home.", "The man got up at once and went out in front of them all. The people were amazed and glorified God. Later Jesus went out beside the sea and taught there. Passing a tax booth, he saw Levi son of Alphaeus and called him to follow. Levi stood up and went with him.", "At a meal, many tax collectors and sinners sat with Jesus and his disciples. The scribes and Pharisees questioned the disciples about his company. Jesus heard and answered with the picture of a physician whose work is with the sick. The people who had been waiting to see what his authority meant now had another occasion to see whom he had come to help."], "lesson": "The four men make their faith visible through steady trouble on another person's behalf. Jesus brings the man's need of forgiveness into view before he sends him home walking.", "question": "What do the four men recognise about the need to bring their friend to Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91075, "title": "A Wedding and a Walk Through Grain", "subtitle": "Receiving God's gifts in their purpose", "text": "Mark 2:18–28", "chapters": [[40, 2]], "start": 18, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["John's disciples and the Pharisees were fasting. People asked Jesus why his own disciples were doing differently. Jesus asked them to picture a wedding. As long as the bridegroom was with the wedding guests, they could not fast. The time would come when he was taken away, and then they would fast. His answer gave them a reason to consider the occasion before comparing what the two groups were doing.", "He spoke of someone sewing a patch of new cloth onto an old garment. The new material could pull away from the old and make a worse tear. He gave the same listeners a second picture. New wine put into old skins could burst them, with both wine and skin lost. Fresh skins make room for the new wine.", "On a Sabbath his disciples passed through grainfields and began plucking the heads of grain. The Pharisees asked why they were doing something they considered unlawful on the Sabbath. Jesus asked them whether they had read about David when he and his companions were hungry. David went into the house of God, ate the bread normally reserved for priests and gave some to his companions.", "Jesus said that the Sabbath was made for people and that the Son of Man was Lord of the Sabbath. The first question had been about when it was fitting to fast. The second gave Jesus occasion to speak about the need of hungry people and the purpose of a day God had given them."], "lesson": "Jesus draws attention to the occasion for a practice and the purpose for which God gave it. Those questions can expose needless severity in our treatment of people.", "question": "How does Jesus use David's need to answer the objection over the grain?"},
  {"id": 91076, "title": "A Hand Restored and Twelve Appointed", "subtitle": "Being with Jesus before being sent", "text": "Mark 3:1–19", "chapters": [[40, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus entered a synagogue where a man had a withered hand. Some people watched to see whether Jesus would heal on the Sabbath, hoping to accuse him. Jesus told the man to stand where they could all see him. He then asked whether it was lawful on the Sabbath to do good or harm, to save a life or kill. They remained silent.", "Jesus looked around at them with anger and grieved over their hardness of heart. He told the man to stretch out his hand. The man did, and it was restored. The Pharisees went out and began consulting with the Herodians about how to destroy Jesus. Mark places their plans alongside the healing that had brought the man's need into the open.", "Jesus withdrew to the sea with his disciples. People came from Galilee, Jerusalem and other regions when they heard what he had done. He asked for a boat to be kept ready because the crowd pressed towards him. People with diseases reached to touch him. Unclean spirits cried out that he was God's Son, and he told them not to make him known.", "Then Jesus went up a mountain and called those he wanted. He appointed twelve to be with him and to be sent out to preach and exercise authority over demons. Mark names them, including Simon, whom Jesus called Peter, and James and John, whom he called sons of thunder. The work for which they were appointed began with being with him."], "lesson": "Jesus puts a man's need in the centre of the room. When he appoints the Twelve, he makes being with him part of their calling before their public work.", "question": "What two purposes does Mark name when Jesus appoints the Twelve?"},
  {"id": 91077, "title": "Voices Outside the House", "subtitle": "The family that does God's will", "text": "Mark 3:20–35", "chapters": [[40, 3]], "start": 20, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus came into a house, the crowd gathered again, so that he and the disciples could not even eat. People close to him heard what was happening and went out to take charge of him, saying he was beside himself. Scribes who had come from Jerusalem offered a different account. They said he had Beelzebub and drove out demons by the ruler of demons.", "Jesus called them and answered in pictures. How could Satan drive out Satan? A kingdom divided against itself could not stand, and the same was true of a house. A person entering a strong man's house to take his goods must first bind the strong man. Jesus' question required the scribes to reckon with what would happen if their own explanation were true.", "Jesus then spoke about forgiveness and gave a serious warning concerning blasphemy against the Holy Spirit. Mark tells the reader why the warning came in this exchange: they had been saying Jesus had an unclean spirit. Their words concerned the source of something they had seen him do.", "His mother and brothers arrived and stood outside, sending word in for him. The crowd sitting around him told him they were looking for him. Jesus asked who his mother and brothers were, then looked at the people around him. He named whoever did God's will as his brother, sister and mother. The house that had been too crowded for a meal became the setting for that description of his family."], "lesson": "Outsiders were busy explaining Jesus, while people around him were listening to him. The family he names is marked by a response to God's will.", "question": "How does Jesus' picture of a divided house answer the scribes' charge?"},
  {"id": 91078, "title": "The Seed and the Lamp", "subtitle": "Attending to what you hear", "text": "Mark 4:1–25", "chapters": [[40, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus began teaching beside the sea, and the crowd grew so large that he sat in a boat while the people remained on shore. He told them to listen. A sower went out scattering seed. Some fell on the path and the birds ate it. Some fell on shallow, rocky ground and sprang up quickly, then withered in the sun because it had no root. Some fell among thorns and was choked. Seed in good soil grew and yielded a crop.", "Afterwards, when Jesus was with those around him and the Twelve, they asked about the parables. He spoke of the mystery of God's kingdom and recalled the distinction between seeing or hearing and actually taking in what was said. Then he explained the sower's work. The seed was the word.", "The seed on the path pictured people from whom Satan quickly took away what they heard. Rocky ground pictured those who received it gladly until trouble or persecution came. Cares, riches and other desires choked the word in others. In good soil people heard, received and bore fruit. Several people could hear the same words, yet what followed could differ.", "Jesus went on to speak about a lamp, which belongs on a stand, and about things hidden that would be made known. He told them to pay attention to what they heard. The passage begins with a call to listen and returns to it after explaining what can happen to the word."], "lesson": "The parable gives us more to examine than our first response to the word. It asks whether we are making room for it to grow after that first hearing.", "question": "What do the thorns represent, and where could they be choking what you have heard?"},
  {"id": 91079, "title": "A Growing Field and a Quiet Sea", "subtitle": "Faith when you cannot control events", "text": "Mark 4:26–41", "chapters": [[40, 4]], "start": 26, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus compared God's kingdom to a person scattering seed on the ground. He went about the ordinary cycle of sleeping and rising while the seed grew, though he could not explain how. The earth produced first the blade, then the head and then the grain. When the crop was ready, the farmer brought in the harvest. Jesus also spoke of a small mustard seed growing large enough for birds to shelter under its branches.", "That evening Jesus told the disciples to cross to the other side of the lake. They left the crowd and took him in the boat where he had been teaching. Other boats went with them. A great storm arose, and waves beat into the boat until it was filling. Jesus was at the stern asleep on a cushion.", "The disciples woke him and asked whether he cared that they were perishing. He got up and rebuked the wind and told the sea to be still. The wind stopped, and there was a great calm. Then he asked the disciples why they were so afraid and why they had so little faith.", "They were greatly afraid and asked one another who he was, since the wind and sea obeyed him. Earlier they had heard about seed growing while a farmer slept. Now they had watched Jesus rise from sleep and do something they could not do for themselves."], "lesson": "The farmer cannot account for every stage of the growth, and the disciples cannot command the storm. The two scenes ask where trust belongs when events are beyond our control.", "question": "What did the disciples' question assume about Jesus while he was asleep?"},
  {"id": 91080, "title": "The Man Who Wanted to Go With Jesus", "subtitle": "Witness starts at home", "text": "Mark 5:1–20", "chapters": [[40, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After the crossing, Jesus stepped out of the boat and a man came from among the tombs to meet him. The man lived there and could no longer be held even with chains. He had broken the restraints people put on him. Night and day he cried out and injured himself with stones. When he saw Jesus, he ran and fell before him. Jesus commanded the unclean spirit to come out.", "Asked its name, the spirit answered Legion, because there were many. The spirits begged Jesus to let them enter a herd of pigs feeding nearby. He allowed it. The herd rushed down the steep bank into the water and drowned. The herdsmen fled and told people in the town and countryside what had happened.", "Those who came out saw the man sitting down, clothed and in his right mind. They also heard the account of what had happened to him and to the pigs. They were afraid and asked Jesus to leave their district. When Jesus got into the boat, the man who had been delivered asked to go with him.", "Jesus gave him a different place to serve. He sent him home to his own people to tell them what the Lord had done for him and how he had shown him mercy. The man went through the Decapolis telling what Jesus had done. People marvelled at the report carried by the man who had once lived among the tombs."], "lesson": "The restored man received a task close to home. Telling people who knew his former condition gave them a concrete account of mercy.", "question": "What did Jesus ask the man to tell his own people?"},
  {"id": 91081, "title": "Two Daughters in Need", "subtitle": "Jesus receives the hidden sufferer", "text": "Mark 5:21–43", "chapters": [[40, 5]], "start": 21, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When Jesus crossed the lake again, a crowd gathered around him. Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, fell at his feet and pleaded for him to come. His little daughter was near death. Jesus went with him, and the crowd pressed around them on the way.", "Among the people was a woman who had been bleeding for twelve years. She had endured much under several physicians and spent all she had, yet her condition had grown worse. Hearing about Jesus, she reached through the crowd and touched his garment. The bleeding stopped. Jesus stopped too and asked who had touched him. His disciples pointed to the crowd pressing on him, but Jesus looked around. The woman came trembling and told him the whole truth. He called her daughter and sent her away in peace.", "While Jesus was still speaking, people arrived from Jairus's house to say the girl had died. They asked why he should trouble the teacher further. Jesus told Jairus not to be afraid, but to believe, and went on with Peter, James and John. At the house people were weeping loudly. When Jesus spoke of the child as sleeping, they laughed at him.", "Jesus took the girl's parents and the three disciples into the room. Taking her by the hand, he told her to rise. She got up and walked. Jesus gave strict instructions about making the event known and told those there to give her something to eat. The account ends with the people in the room astonished and with an ordinary need for them to attend to."], "lesson": "Jesus addresses one sufferer in a crowd and remembers an ordinary need after raising another. Care can include attention to details others consider too small to notice.", "question": "What did Jesus ask people to do for the girl after she rose?"},
  {"id": 91082, "title": "The Hometown and the Roads Beyond", "subtitle": "Witness amid rejection", "text": "Mark 6:1–13", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus returned to his own country with the disciples. When the Sabbath came, he taught in the synagogue. Many who heard him were astonished and asked where he had received this wisdom and how he did such works. They knew him as the carpenter, Mary's son, and they named his brothers and spoke of his sisters living among them. What they knew of his household shaped the way they heard him, and they took offence.", "Jesus said that a prophet could be without honour in his own country, among his relatives and in his own house. Mark says Jesus laid hands on a few sick people and healed them there, and marvelled at their unbelief. He continued teaching in the villages around them.", "He called the Twelve and began sending them out in pairs. He gave them authority over unclean spirits and instructed them to take a staff but no bread, bag or money. They could wear sandals and were to travel without a spare tunic. When a household received them, they were to stay there until they left that place.", "If people would neither receive nor hear them, they were to leave and shake the dust from their feet as a testimony. The disciples went out and preached repentance, drove out demons and anointed and healed many sick people. The chapter moves from a town where Jesus met offence into villages where others were given a chance to hear."], "lesson": "Rejection in a familiar place did not end the work. Jesus trained the Twelve to minister together and to handle refusals without getting stuck in them.", "question": "What did Jesus instruct the Twelve to do when a place would not receive them?"},
  {"id": 91083, "title": "Herod's Banquet and John's Death", "subtitle": "The cost of pleasing an audience", "text": "Mark 6:14–29", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 14, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Reports about Jesus reached Herod because his name had become widely known. People offered different explanations. Some thought Elijah had come, and others thought Jesus was a prophet like those of earlier times. Herod believed John the Baptist, whom he had beheaded, had risen. Mark then explains what had happened to John.", "Herod had seized and imprisoned him because John told him it was unlawful to have Herodias, the wife of his brother Philip. Herodias wanted to kill John, but could not do so. Herod feared John, knowing him to be a just and holy man, and kept him safe. Even though John's words concerned his own conduct, Herod listened to him gladly.", "An opportunity came at Herod's birthday banquet, where he entertained his officials and leading men of Galilee. Herodias's daughter danced and pleased those at the table. Herod swore to give her whatever she asked, up to half his kingdom. She went out to consult her mother, then returned and asked for John's head on a dish.", "Herod was deeply sorry, yet he gave the order because of his oaths and the guests. John was killed in prison, and what the girl requested was brought to her. She took it to her mother. John's disciples heard, came for his body and laid it in a tomb. The man Herod had listened to was dead because Herod placed another set of voices first."], "lesson": "Listening to a truthful person can coexist with refusing to act on what he says. Herod's decision shows what can happen when approval in a room matters more than conscience.", "question": "How did Herod's opinion of John differ from what he finally chose to do?"},
  {"id": 91084, "title": "Bread for the Crowd and Jesus on the Water", "subtitle": "Remembering the help already given", "text": "Mark 6:30–56", "chapters": [[40, 6]], "start": 30, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["The apostles returned and told Jesus what they had done and taught. There were so many people coming and going that they had no time to eat. Jesus invited them to come apart and rest. They went by boat to a secluded place, but people recognised them, ran there on foot from the towns and arrived ahead of them.", "Jesus saw the crowd and had compassion because they were like sheep without a shepherd. He taught them. Late in the day the disciples wanted to send people out to buy food. Jesus told the disciples to feed them. When they found five loaves and two fish, he had the people sit in groups on the grass, blessed and broke the food and gave it to the disciples to distribute. All ate, and twelve baskets of pieces were gathered.", "Jesus sent the disciples away in the boat while he dismissed the crowd, then went to the mountain to pray. That night he saw them struggling against the wind. He came to them walking on the sea. They thought they saw a spirit and cried out. He told them who he was and spoke courage to them. When he got into the boat the wind stopped.", "Mark connects their astonishment with their failure to understand the loaves. After landing at Gennesaret, people recognised Jesus and brought the sick to him. They asked to touch the edge of his garment, and those who touched it were made well."], "lesson": "A fresh fear can hide the significance of help we have just received. Mark invites readers to hold the feeding and the crossing together as they ask who Jesus is.", "question": "What does Mark say the disciples had failed to understand before the crossing?"},
  {"id": 91085, "title": "The Gift That Excused Neglect", "subtitle": "Looking beneath religious appearances", "text": "Mark 7:1–23", "chapters": [[40, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Pharisees and scribes from Jerusalem gathered around Jesus and saw some disciples eating with unwashed hands. Mark explains the washing customs that lay behind their question. They asked why the disciples did not walk according to the tradition of the elders. Jesus answered by recalling Isaiah's words about people who honoured God with their lips while their hearts were far from him.", "He gave them a concrete example. Moses commanded people to honour father and mother. But someone could declare that the help a parent would have received from him was Corban, meaning a gift devoted to God. In practice the tradition could leave the parent without help. Jesus said that in this way they set aside God's word through what they had handed down.", "Then he called the crowd and told them to listen and understand. Something entering a person from outside could not defile in the way that what came out of a person could. When he entered the house, the disciples asked him to explain.", "Jesus distinguished where food goes from what comes out of the heart. He named such things as evil thoughts, stealing, murder, deceit, envy, slander and pride. These proceed from within and defile. The first question had been about what the disciples did with their hands before eating. Jesus' answer gave the hearers reason to examine what they allowed their hearts to produce."], "lesson": "A religious claim can become a polished excuse for neglecting someone. Jesus makes both the neglected duty and the motives of the heart visible.", "question": "Which command did Jesus say could be set aside through the tradition about gifts?"},
  {"id": 91086, "title": "A Mother's Reply and an Opened Ear", "subtitle": "Mercy in unfamiliar places", "text": "Mark 7:24–37", "chapters": [[40, 7]], "start": 24, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went to the region of Tyre and Sidon and entered a house. He wanted to keep his presence quiet, but word reached a woman whose daughter had an unclean spirit. The woman was a Greek, Syrophoenician by birth. She came, fell at his feet and begged him to drive the spirit out of her child.", "Jesus spoke about letting the children eat first and said their bread should not be thrown to the dogs. The woman answered from within the picture he had given her: even the dogs under the table ate the children's crumbs. Jesus told her to go because of what she had said. When she arrived home, she found the girl lying on the bed and the spirit gone.", "Jesus travelled on towards the Sea of Galilee through the region of the Decapolis. People brought a man who was deaf and had difficulty speaking. They asked Jesus to lay his hand on him. Jesus took the man away from the crowd, touched his ears and tongue, looked towards heaven and sighed. He told him to be opened. The man's ears opened and he began to speak clearly.", "Jesus charged the people to keep it quiet, but the more he told them, the more widely they spoke about it. They marvelled that he made the deaf hear and those who could not speak speak plainly. In one house a mother received an answer about a child she could not bring there. Beside the lake a man received attention away from the people who brought him."], "lesson": "Jesus hears the mother's reply and deals personally with the man brought by others. People beyond familiar religious boundaries receive his attention.", "question": "What do you learn from the way Jesus handled the man privately before the healing became widely known?"},
  {"id": 91087, "title": "Another Meal and Sight in Stages", "subtitle": "Learning to see what is before you", "text": "Mark 8:1–26", "chapters": [[40, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["A large crowd had been with Jesus for three days and had nothing to eat. He told the disciples he had compassion. Some people had come from far away and might faint if sent home hungry. The disciples asked where bread could be found in that place. Jesus took seven loaves and a few small fish, gave thanks and had them distribute the food. About four thousand ate, and seven baskets were gathered.", "After the crossing, Pharisees came to question Jesus and demanded a sign from heaven to test him. He sighed deeply and asked why that generation wanted a sign. Then he left them and crossed again. The disciples had forgotten bread and had only one loaf in the boat. When Jesus warned them about the leaven of the Pharisees and of Herod, they thought he was speaking about their supplies.", "He asked why they were reasoning about having no bread. He made them remember the numbers of baskets after both feedings. They could answer with twelve and seven. Then he asked if they still did not understand.", "At Bethsaida people brought a blind man to Jesus. He took him by the hand and led him outside the village. After Jesus touched his eyes, the man could see people, but they looked like walking trees. Jesus laid his hands on him again, and he saw clearly. Jesus sent him home, charging him to keep away from the village."], "lesson": "Mark places questions about seeing beside a healing in stages. Honest attention to what we only partly understand can make room for further instruction.", "question": "What do the disciples remember about the baskets, and what do they still need to understand?"},
  {"id": 91088, "title": "The Confession on the Road", "subtitle": "The identity of Jesus and the cost of following", "text": "Mark 8:27–38", "chapters": [[40, 8]], "start": 27, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["As Jesus and the disciples walked towards the villages around Caesarea Philippi, he asked what people said about him. They reported several answers: John the Baptist, Elijah or one of the prophets. Jesus then asked what the disciples themselves said. Peter answered that he was the Christ. Jesus told them to tell no one about him.", "Then he began teaching them plainly that the Son of Man must suffer many things. The elders, chief priests and scribes would reject him. He would be killed and would rise after three days. Peter took Jesus aside and began to rebuke him. Jesus turned, saw the other disciples and rebuked Peter in their hearing. Peter had set his mind on human concerns instead of God's.", "Jesus called the crowd and the disciples to hear what following him meant. Anyone who would come after him must deny himself, take up his cross and follow. A person who tried to save his life would lose it. One who lost it for Jesus and for the gospel would save it.", "Jesus asked what it would profit someone to gain the whole world and lose his soul, or what he could give in exchange for it. He spoke of people being ashamed of him and his words, and of the Son of Man coming in his Father's glory. Peter had given the right name on the road. Jesus now explained what allegiance to the Christ would cost."], "lesson": "Peter accepts a title and objects to what Jesus says it will involve. Jesus teaches disciples to examine what they are protecting when his way calls for surrender.", "question": "What words of Jesus provoked Peter's objection?"},
  {"id": 91089, "title": "Glory on the Mountain and Need Below", "subtitle": "Prayer when faith is weak", "text": "Mark 9:1–29", "chapters": [[40, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus told his hearers that some standing there would see God's kingdom come with power before they died. Six days later he took Peter, James and John up a high mountain apart from the others. His appearance changed, and his garments became brilliantly white. Elijah and Moses appeared and spoke with him. Peter suggested making three shelters. Mark says he did not know what to say because they were afraid.", "A cloud overshadowed them and a voice called Jesus the beloved Son and told them to hear him. When they looked around, they saw Jesus alone. On the descent Jesus told them to keep the event to themselves until the Son of Man had risen. They discussed what rising from the dead could mean and asked about Elijah. Jesus spoke about Elijah having come and about the suffering of the Son of Man.", "Below, scribes were disputing with the disciples. A father explained that his boy was tormented by a spirit and that the disciples had been unable to drive it out. He brought the boy to Jesus and asked for help. When Jesus spoke of believing, the father cried out that he believed and asked for help with his unbelief.", "Jesus commanded the spirit to leave. The boy lay so still people said he was dead. Jesus took him by the hand and lifted him up. Later the disciples privately asked why they had failed. Jesus directed them to prayer."], "lesson": "The father's plea gives words to imperfect faith. He brings his uncertainty to Jesus along with his son's need.", "question": "How does the father's plea hold belief and need together?"},
  {"id": 91090, "title": "The Argument They Kept Quiet", "subtitle": "Welcome, humility and care", "text": "Mark 9:30–50", "chapters": [[40, 9]], "start": 30, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus and the disciples passed through Galilee without seeking notice. He was teaching them that the Son of Man would be handed over, killed and raised. They did not understand and were afraid to ask. When they reached a house in Capernaum, Jesus asked what they had been discussing along the road. They kept silent because they had argued about who was greatest.", "He sat down, called the Twelve and told them that anyone who wanted to be first should be last and servant of all. He set a child among them and took the child in his arms. Whoever received such a child in his name received him, and whoever received him received the one who sent him.", "John said they had tried to stop someone who was driving out demons in Jesus' name because he did not follow their group. Jesus told them not to stop him. He spoke of someone giving his followers even a cup of water on account of belonging to Christ, and of the seriousness of causing one of the little ones to stumble.", "Jesus used severe images of giving up a hand, foot or eye to impress on them the danger of what led into sin. He ended by speaking of salt and telling them to be at peace with one another. The group that had been silent about its argument was given something concrete to practise together."], "lesson": "The disciples' argument kept their attention on position. Jesus gave them practical matters to consider, including the welcome they offered and the harm they might cause.", "question": "What did Jesus place before the disciples to give their argument a different direction?"},
  {"id": 91091, "title": "Children at the Door and a Man With Wealth", "subtitle": "Receiving the kingdom with open hands", "text": "Mark 10:1–31", "chapters": [[40, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went to the region of Judea beyond the Jordan, and crowds gathered for him to teach. Pharisees asked whether a man could put away his wife, trying to test him. Jesus asked what Moses commanded. They referred to the certificate of divorce. He said that permission had been written because of hardness of heart. He went back to creation, when God made male and female and joined two into one flesh. In the house he addressed his disciples again about divorce and remarriage.", "People brought children for Jesus to touch, and the disciples rebuked them. Jesus was indignant and told them to let the children come. The kingdom belonged to such as these, he said, and must be received like a child. He took the children in his arms, laid his hands on them and blessed them.", "As he was setting out, a man ran up, knelt and asked what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus named commandments, and the man said he had kept them since youth. Jesus looked at him with love. He told him to sell what he had, give to the poor and follow him. The man went away sorrowful because he owned much.", "Jesus spoke about how hard it was for the wealthy to enter God's kingdom, using the image of a camel and a needle's eye. When the disciples asked who could be saved, he pointed them to what was possible with God. Peter spoke of what they had left. Jesus promised provision with persecutions now, and eternal life in the age to come."], "lesson": "Mark records Jesus' love for the man who walked away. It makes the hard instruction personal and shows that a demand can come from love.", "question": "What does Mark say about the way Jesus looked at the man before giving the instruction?"},
  {"id": 91092, "title": "Two Requests on the Road", "subtitle": "Serving and seeing", "text": "Mark 10:32–52", "chapters": [[40, 10]], "start": 32, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["On the road to Jerusalem Jesus was walking ahead. Those following were afraid, and he took the Twelve aside to tell them what awaited him. He spoke of being handed over, condemned and killed, and of rising again. James and John then approached him with something they wanted him to do. Asked what it was, they requested places beside him in his glory.", "Jesus spoke of the cup he would drink and the baptism he would undergo. The brothers said they were able to share them. When the other ten grew indignant, Jesus gathered the disciples and spoke about how rulers exercise power. Among his followers, greatness would take the form of service. He gave his own mission as the pattern, saying he came to serve and give his life as a ransom for many.", "As they left Jericho, Bartimaeus sat begging by the road. Hearing that Jesus was passing, he cried out to the Son of David for mercy. People rebuked him, but he cried louder. Jesus stopped and told them to call him. The people now encouraged the man to rise. Bartimaeus threw aside his garment and came to Jesus.", "Jesus asked him what he wanted, as he had asked James and John earlier on the road. Bartimaeus asked to see. Jesus told him his faith had made him well. He received his sight and followed Jesus along the way, the same road on which Jesus had been teaching his disciples what following him would involve."], "lesson": "Jesus asks what people want in both scenes. The answers reveal different desires as he goes towards Jerusalem.", "question": "What do James and John ask for, and what does Bartimaeus ask for when Jesus stops?"},
  {"id": 91093, "title": "Branches, a Temple and a Withered Tree", "subtitle": "The fruit of prayer and forgiveness", "text": "Mark 11:1–26", "chapters": [[40, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Near Bethphage and Bethany, Jesus sent two disciples to a village where they would find a colt tied that no one had ridden. They were to bring it and say the Lord needed it if anyone asked. They found the colt, answered the people standing there and brought it to Jesus. He rode towards Jerusalem while people spread garments and branches on the road and cried Hosanna. He entered the temple, looked around and went back to Bethany because it was late.", "Returning the next day, Jesus was hungry. He went to a fig tree with leaves and found no fruit. Mark notes it was not the season for figs. The disciples heard Jesus say that no one would eat fruit from it again. Then they went into Jerusalem.", "In the temple Jesus drove out those buying and selling and overturned the money changers' tables and the sellers' seats. He taught that God's house was to be a house of prayer for all nations and said they had made it a den of thieves. The chief priests and scribes heard and looked for a way to destroy him. He went out of the city at evening.", "Next morning the disciples saw the fig tree withered from the roots. Peter remembered Jesus' words. Jesus told them to have faith in God and spoke about prayer. He also told them to forgive when standing to pray, bringing the lesson back to their own conduct."], "lesson": "Mark places the temple visit between the two encounters with the tree. The sequence invites examination of the fruit of worship, including a willingness to forgive.", "question": "Which instruction about forgiveness accompanies Jesus' teaching about faith and prayer?"},
  {"id": 91094, "title": "An Answer Kept Back", "subtitle": "Being honest about authority", "text": "Mark 11:27–33", "chapters": [[40, 11]], "start": 27, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus and the disciples came again to Jerusalem. As he walked in the temple, the chief priests, scribes and elders approached him. They asked what authority he had to do these things and who had given it to him. Mark has just described his actions in the temple and the leaders looking for a way to destroy him. Now they challenged him to account for what he had done.", "Jesus said he would ask them a question and answer theirs if they answered his. He brought them back to John's baptism. Had it come from heaven or from people? He asked them to say plainly which they believed.", "The leaders discussed it among themselves. If they said it came from heaven, Jesus could ask why they had not believed John. If they said it came from people, they had reason to fear the crowd, because people considered John a prophet. Their discussion centred on how each answer would be received. Mark records no conclusion among them about the source of John's authority.", "They told Jesus they did not know. He replied that he would not tell them by what authority he acted. The question they had brought remained unanswered after they had declined to give an answer of their own."], "lesson": "Concern for position governed their discussion. When an honest answer carries a cost, the passage asks us to notice the temptation to retreat into convenient uncertainty.", "question": "What were the leaders considering when they decided how to answer?"},
  {"id": 91095, "title": "The Vineyard, the Coin and the Living God", "subtitle": "Answering to God", "text": "Mark 12:1–27", "chapters": [[40, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus told of a man who planted a vineyard, prepared it for making wine, built a tower and entrusted it to tenants. When he sent servants to receive some of the fruit, the tenants beat or killed them. At last he sent his beloved son. They recognised the heir and killed him to try to take the inheritance. Jesus asked what the owner would do and spoke of judgement and of the rejected stone becoming the cornerstone. The leaders knew the parable concerned them.", "They sent Pharisees and Herodians to trap him with a question about paying tribute to Caesar. Jesus asked for a coin and asked whose image and inscription it bore. When they said Caesar's, he told them to give Caesar what was Caesar's and God what was God's. They marvelled at him.", "Then Sadducees, who denied resurrection, told of one woman who had been married in succession to seven brothers, each of whom died without leaving children. They asked whose wife she would be in the resurrection. Jesus said they had misunderstood Scripture and God's power. Marriage would not carry over in the way they supposed.", "He took them to the account of God speaking to Moses at the bush and naming himself the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He spoke of God as the God of the living. Each exchange in the passage returns to a claim that the questioners had treated too lightly."], "lesson": "The passages move from responsibility for what is entrusted to us towards God's claim and power. Jesus reads each question against what the questioners have missed about God.", "question": "How does the owner's claim in the parable help you consider what belongs to God?"},
  {"id": 91096, "title": "The Listening Scribe and the Widow's Coins", "subtitle": "Wholehearted devotion", "text": "Mark 12:28–44", "chapters": [[40, 12]], "start": 28, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["A scribe heard Jesus answer the others well and asked which commandment came first. Jesus began with Israel's confession that the Lord is one and called for love of God with heart, soul, mind and strength. He gave a second command, to love one's neighbour as oneself. The scribe agreed and said that such love mattered more than offerings and sacrifices. Jesus saw that he had answered thoughtfully and told him he was near God's kingdom.", "While teaching in the temple, Jesus asked how the scribes could say the Christ was David's son when David, speaking by the Holy Spirit, called him Lord. He also warned people about scribes who sought public greetings and places of honour, while devouring widows' houses and making long prayers for appearance.", "Jesus then sat opposite the treasury and watched people putting in their money. Many wealthy people put in much. A poor widow came and put in two very small coins. Jesus called the disciples to notice what she had done. He said she had put in more than all the others.", "The others had given from what they had in abundance. She had put in all she had to live on. Mark gives the widow's two coins the last word in a passage about what people say matters most and what Jesus regards as significant in the temple."], "lesson": "The attentive scribe and the widow each receive an individual response from Jesus. He considers the substance of a person's devotion, which a public display or the amount of a gift may conceal.", "question": "What did Jesus notice about the widow's gift that an observer counting coins could miss?"},
  {"id": 91097, "title": "Across From the Temple", "subtitle": "Discernment under pressure", "text": "Mark 13:1–23", "chapters": [[40, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["As Jesus left the temple, one disciple drew his attention to its great stones and buildings. Jesus said a day was coming when one stone would not be left on another. Sitting opposite the temple on the Mount of Olives, Peter, James, John and Andrew asked him privately when it would happen and how they would recognise the time.", "Jesus warned against being misled by people claiming his name. Wars and reports of wars would come, along with earthquakes and famines. These would be beginnings. He told the disciples they would be brought before councils and rulers and would have opportunities to bear witness. In those moments the Holy Spirit would give them what to say. Even families would be divided over their loyalty to him.", "When the desolating sight appeared, those in Judea were to flee. Jesus used the examples of someone on a roof who could not afford to go down for possessions and someone in a field who should not return for a garment. He spoke of people for whom flight would be especially hard and told them to pray that it would not happen in winter.", "He then warned that false Christs and false prophets would appear with convincing signs. His disciples should be alert. They had asked how they would recognise what lay ahead. He supplied warnings they could remember when rumours, fear and oppression made judgement harder."], "lesson": "Jesus answers the question about timing by preparing his followers for discernment and endurance. His promise concerning the Spirit addresses a real moment of vulnerability.", "question": "What instructions does Jesus give for a time when fear and false claims are widespread?"},
  {"id": 91098, "title": "The Doorkeeper's Watch", "subtitle": "Faithfulness while waiting", "text": "Mark 13:24–37", "chapters": [[40, 13]], "start": 24, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus went on speaking about what would follow the distress. The sun would be darkened and the moon would cease to give light. The powers in heaven would be shaken. Then people would see the Son of Man coming in clouds with power and glory. He would send angels to gather his chosen people from the ends of earth and heaven.", "Jesus asked the disciples to learn from a fig tree. Tender branches and new leaves tell people that summer is near. He connected this with recognising what was near when they saw the things he had described. Heaven and earth would pass away, but his words would remain.", "Then he spoke about what they could not calculate. The day or hour was known to the Father. They were to watch. He compared the situation to a man going away from his house. The man gave authority and a particular task to each servant and instructed the doorkeeper to watch.", "The master might come in the evening, at midnight, when the rooster crowed or in the morning. Jesus warned against being found asleep. The last instruction is addressed to all who hear it. Each servant has work to attend to during the wait, and the doorkeeper has a watch to keep even though he cannot schedule the return."], "lesson": "Each servant has work while the master is away. Jesus joins expectancy to attention to the duty already assigned.", "question": "What has the master already given each servant to do before he returns?"},
  {"id": 91099, "title": "The Costly Gift and the Shared Cup", "subtitle": "Devotion in a room of weakness", "text": "Mark 14:1–25", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Passover was approaching, and the chief priests and scribes looked for a way to seize Jesus without stirring up the people. At Bethany, in the house of Simon the leper, a woman came while Jesus reclined at the table. She broke a jar of costly perfume and poured it on his head. Some protested that it could have been sold and the money given to the poor, and they rebuked her.", "Jesus told them to leave her alone. She had done a good work, he said. They could still help the poor, but his time with them was short. She had done what she could and had anointed his body beforehand for burial. Wherever the gospel was proclaimed, her act would be remembered. Judas then went to the leaders to arrange to hand Jesus over. They promised him money, and he began looking for an opportunity.", "When it was time to prepare the Passover, Jesus gave two disciples directions. A man carrying a water jar would lead them to a house. They found the upstairs room furnished and ready and made the preparations there.", "At the meal Jesus said one of the Twelve eating with him would betray him. They were sorrowful and asked in turn if it was them. Jesus took bread, blessed it and gave it to them, speaking of his body. He gave thanks over the cup and spoke of his covenant blood poured out for many. He said he would drink again in God's kingdom."], "lesson": "The anointing is remembered for what the woman did within her reach. At the meal Jesus gives his disciples a way to remember what he is doing for them.", "question": "Why does Jesus link the woman's gift with his burial?"},
  {"id": 91100, "title": "The Sleeping Friends and the Arrest", "subtitle": "Watching and yielding to the Father", "text": "Mark 14:26–52", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 26, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["After singing a hymn, Jesus and the disciples went to the Mount of Olives. He said they would all fall away and recalled the words about the shepherd being struck and the sheep scattered. He promised to go before them to Galilee after he was raised. Peter insisted he would stay faithful, and Jesus told him he would deny him three times before the rooster crowed twice. Peter repeated his promise, and the others said the same.", "At Gethsemane Jesus took Peter, James and John with him. Deeply distressed, he asked them to stay awake. Going farther, he fell to the ground and prayed to his Father that the cup might pass, yielding to his Father's will. He returned to find them asleep. He told Peter to watch and pray because the spirit was willing and the flesh weak.", "Jesus went to pray again and again found them sleeping. When he came back the third time, he told them the hour had come. Judas approached with people sent by the chief priests, scribes and elders. He gave them the agreed sign by greeting Jesus and kissing him. The group laid hands on Jesus. Someone drew a sword and cut off the high priest's servant's ear.", "Jesus reminded them he had taught openly in the temple and spoke of the Scriptures being fulfilled. The disciples left him and fled. Mark also records a young man who escaped when seized by leaving behind the linen cloth he wore."], "lesson": "Jesus had warned the disciples about their weakness before the crowd arrived. The passage shows the need to bring that weakness into prayer while there is time.", "question": "What did Jesus warn Peter about the spirit and the flesh when he found him asleep?"},
  {"id": 91101, "title": "Peter Below in the Courtyard", "subtitle": "Remembering the word after failure", "text": "Mark 14:53–72", "chapters": [[40, 14]], "start": 53, "end": 72, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Jesus was led to the high priest, where the chief priests, elders and scribes gathered. Peter followed at a distance into the courtyard and sat with the servants by the fire. Inside, the council sought testimony that would justify putting Jesus to death. Witnesses came forward, but their accounts did not agree. Even the witnesses who spoke about the temple differed from one another.", "The high priest stood and asked Jesus to answer the accusations. Jesus remained silent. When asked directly whether he was the Christ, the Son of the Blessed, he answered and spoke of the Son of Man seated at the right hand of power and coming with the clouds. The high priest tore his clothes and the council condemned Jesus as deserving death. Some spat on him, covered his face and struck him.", "Below in the courtyard, a servant girl saw Peter warming himself and said he had been with Jesus of Nazareth. Peter denied it and moved out towards the porch. When the girl spoke to others about him, he denied it again. Later those standing nearby insisted he was one of the followers, pointing to his being a Galilean. Peter began to curse and swear that he did not know the man they were talking about.", "The rooster crowed a second time. Peter remembered that Jesus had told him he would deny him three times before it crowed twice. Remembering the words he had earlier contradicted, he broke down and wept."], "lesson": "Remembering Jesus' words allowed Peter to recognise his failure. A word we once resisted can become the beginning of honest repentance.", "question": "What detail brought Jesus' warning back to Peter's mind?"},
  {"id": 91102, "title": "The Rejected Prisoner and the Mock Crown", "subtitle": "Recognising the power of a crowd", "text": "Mark 15:1–20", "chapters": [[40, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["Early in the morning the leaders consulted, bound Jesus and took him to Pilate. Pilate asked whether he was the King of the Jews. Jesus answered him, then remained silent when the chief priests brought further charges. Pilate marvelled that he gave no reply.", "At the feast Pilate usually released a prisoner the people requested. Barabbas was in prison with men who had committed murder during an uprising. When the crowd asked for the customary release, Pilate offered to release the King of the Jews. Mark says he knew the chief priests had handed Jesus over out of envy. The priests stirred the crowd to ask for Barabbas instead.", "Pilate asked what he should do with Jesus. They shouted for him to be crucified. He asked what wrong Jesus had done, and they cried out more loudly. Pilate wanted to satisfy the crowd. He released Barabbas, had Jesus scourged and handed him over to be crucified.", "The soldiers took Jesus inside and called together their company. They dressed him in purple, crowned him with thorns and saluted him in mockery as King of the Jews. They struck him, spat on him and knelt before him. Then they removed the purple robe, put his own clothes on him and led him away. The question with which Pilate began had become a title for the soldiers to mock."], "lesson": "Mark gives a reason for Pilate's decision: he wanted to satisfy the crowd. His choice asks what happens when a person uses authority chiefly to preserve approval.", "question": "What did Pilate know about the chief priests' motives, and how did he still decide?"},
  {"id": 91103, "title": "The Centurion, the Women and the Tomb", "subtitle": "Witness and courage at the cross", "text": "Mark 15:21–47", "chapters": [[40, 15]], "start": 21, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["The soldiers compelled Simon of Cyrene, the father of Alexander and Rufus, to carry Jesus' cross. They brought Jesus to Golgotha, offered him wine mixed with myrrh, which he refused, and crucified him. They cast lots for his clothes. The charge above him called him King of the Jews, and two men were crucified beside him.", "Passers-by mocked Jesus and told him to come down. The chief priests and scribes spoke of him saving others and taunted him about saving himself. Those crucified alongside him also reviled him. Darkness lay over the land from noon until the middle of the afternoon. Jesus cried out, and some people thought he was calling Elijah. After another loud cry, Jesus died.", "The temple curtain tore from top to bottom. A centurion who faced Jesus saw how he died and called him God's Son. Women watched from a distance, including Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Joses, and Salome. Mark remembers that they had followed and served him in Galilee.", "As evening approached before the Sabbath, Joseph of Arimathea went courageously to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. Pilate checked with the centurion that Jesus was dead before releasing it. Joseph wrapped the body in linen, laid it in a tomb cut from rock and rolled a stone across the entrance. The two Marys saw where he was laid."], "lesson": "People in different places bear witness to what happened. Joseph's request took courage, while the women attended closely enough to know where to return.", "question": "Which details does Mark give about the people who saw Jesus die or saw where he was buried?"},
  {"id": 91104, "title": "The Stone Already Rolled Away", "subtitle": "Hope spoken to failing disciples", "text": "Mark 16:1–8", "chapters": [[40, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["When the Sabbath was past, Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James and Salome bought spices so that they could anoint Jesus' body. They went to the tomb early on the first day of the week, as the sun was rising. They had a practical question on the way. The stone across the entrance was large, and they wondered who would roll it away for them.", "When they looked up, they saw that it had already been rolled back. They entered the tomb and saw a young man sitting on the right side, dressed in white. They were alarmed. He told them he knew they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth, who had been crucified. He had risen and was no longer there. The young man directed them to the place where Jesus had been laid.", "He then gave them something to do. They were to go and tell the disciples and Peter that Jesus was going ahead of them into Galilee. There they would see him, as he had told them. Naming Peter gives the reader a reason to remember the courtyard where he had denied Jesus.", "The women went out quickly and fled from the tomb, trembling and astonished. Mark says they said nothing to anyone as they fled because they were afraid. They had come bearing spices for the dead. They left with a message about where the disciples would see him alive."], "lesson": "Peter is named when the message is sent to the disciples. Readers who remember his denial can hear the personal care in that small addition.", "question": "What difference does the mention of Peter make after the account of his denial?"},
  {"id": 91105, "title": "The News Carried Outward", "subtitle": "From unbelief to witness", "text": "Mark 16:9–20", "chapters": [[40, 16]], "start": 9, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Mark", "story": ["In the closing verses printed in the King James Version, the risen Jesus appears first to Mary Magdalene, from whom he had cast out seven demons. She goes to the people who had been with him and finds them mourning and weeping. They hear her report that he is alive and that she has seen him, but they do not believe her.", "Jesus then appears in another form to two of them as they walk into the country. They go back and give their report, and the others do not believe them either. Afterwards Jesus appears to the eleven while they are eating. He reproaches their unbelief and hardness of heart because they had not believed the people who had seen him risen.", "He sends them out into the world to proclaim the gospel to all creation and speaks of the response of belief and baptism. The passage describes signs that would accompany believers. The people Jesus sends now have work to do with the message they themselves had been slow to receive.", "After speaking to them, Jesus is received into heaven and sits at God's right hand. They go out and preach everywhere. The account pictures the Lord working with them and confirming the word. It has moved from the grief of people unable to believe another person's report to those same followers going out to bear witness."], "lesson": "The people commissioned to speak had themselves struggled to believe. Their earlier failure gives context to the patient work of bearing witness to others.", "question": "How do the disciples' first reactions compare with what they do at the close of the passage?"},
  {"id": 91106, "title": "Zechariah Comes Out Silent", "subtitle": "A promise in the years of waiting", "text": "Luke 1:1–25", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke tells Theophilus that others have already undertaken to write accounts of these events. The accounts draw on those who saw what happened and served the word from the beginning. Luke has traced the events carefully and wants Theophilus to know the certainty of what he has been taught. The first people he puts before him are an elderly priest and his wife.", "Zechariah and Elisabeth lived in the days of Herod. Both are described as righteous before God and faithful in keeping his commands. They had no child, and both were old. When Zechariah's division was on duty, the lot fell to him to enter the sanctuary and offer incense. The people waited and prayed outside.", "An angel appeared by the altar, and Zechariah was afraid. The angel told him his prayer had been heard. Elisabeth would bear a son named John. The child would bring joy, turn many to the Lord and prepare people for his coming. Zechariah asked how he could know this, pointing to his and Elisabeth's age. The angel identified himself as Gabriel and told him he would be unable to speak until the promise came to pass because he had not believed.", "Outside, the people wondered why he was taking so long. When he emerged, he could only gesture, and they realised he had seen a vision. After completing his service he went home. Elisabeth conceived and kept herself apart for five months. She spoke of the Lord seeing her and taking away the reproach she had borne among her people."], "lesson": "Luke presents the couple as faithful before their prayer is answered. Elisabeth's years of waiting deserve attention alongside the joy that follows.", "question": "How does Luke describe Zechariah and Elisabeth before Gabriel brings the news?"},
  {"id": 91107, "title": "Mary Goes to See Elisabeth", "subtitle": "Believing God's word in uncertain circumstances", "text": "Luke 1:26–56", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 26, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Gabriel was sent to Nazareth in the sixth month of Elisabeth's pregnancy. He came to Mary, who was pledged to Joseph, a man of David's house. His greeting troubled her. He told her she had found favour with God and would have a son named Jesus. The child would be called the Son of the Highest and would reign on David's throne without end.", "Mary asked how this could happen since she had not been with a man. Gabriel spoke of the Holy Spirit coming upon her and the power of the Highest overshadowing her. He also told her that Elisabeth, who had been called barren, was already in her sixth month. Mary placed herself at the Lord's service and accepted that it should happen according to his word.", "Mary went quickly to the hill country and greeted Elisabeth. At the sound of her greeting, Elisabeth's child leaped. Elisabeth was filled with the Holy Spirit and blessed Mary and the child she carried. She wondered at being visited by the mother of her Lord and called Mary blessed for believing what had been spoken to her.", "Mary answered in praise. She spoke of God seeing her low estate, bringing down the powerful, lifting the lowly, filling the hungry and remembering mercy towards Israel. Her words reached back to God's promise to Abraham and his descendants. Mary stayed with Elisabeth about three months before returning home. The private announcement in Nazareth had become an occasion for two women to speak of what God was doing."], "lesson": "Mary's praise places her own experience inside God's mercy to generations. Elisabeth's welcome gives her a place to share what has happened.", "question": "What parts of Mary's song reach beyond her own circumstances?"},
  {"id": 91108, "title": "The Name Written on the Tablet", "subtitle": "Mercy remembered and a way prepared", "text": "Luke 1:57–80", "chapters": [[41, 1]], "start": 57, "end": 80, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The time came for Elisabeth to give birth, and she had a son. Her neighbours and relatives heard how the Lord had shown her mercy, and they rejoiced with her. On the eighth day they came to circumcise the child. They expected to name him Zechariah after his father, but Elisabeth said his name was to be John. They objected that no one in her family was called by that name.", "They made signs to the child's father to ask what he wanted him called. Zechariah asked for a writing tablet and wrote that his name was John. Everyone marvelled. At once Zechariah could speak again, and he used his first words to bless God. The neighbours were filled with awe, and word of these events spread through the hill country. People wondered what this child would become.", "Filled with the Holy Spirit, Zechariah spoke of God visiting and redeeming his people. He recalled the promises to David and Abraham and the covenant by which God would deliver people to serve him without fear, in holiness and righteousness. The promise concerning his son belonged to a purpose older and wider than the family's joy at his birth.", "Then he addressed the child. John would go before the Lord to prepare his ways and give people knowledge of salvation through forgiveness. Zechariah pictured light dawning upon people in darkness and guiding their feet into peace. Luke ends by telling how the child grew strong in spirit and lived in the wilderness until his public appearance to Israel."], "lesson": "The name marks obedience to what was spoken before the child was born. Zechariah uses his restored speech to bless God and to describe a calling greater than family expectations.", "question": "What does Zechariah say John will do for the people?"},
  {"id": 91109, "title": "The Night the Shepherds Went to Bethlehem", "subtitle": "Good news carried by ordinary witnesses", "text": "Luke 2:1–21", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A decree for registration went out from Caesar Augustus. People travelled to their own towns. Joseph went from Nazareth in Galilee to Bethlehem in Judea because he belonged to David's family. Mary went with him, and while they were there the time came for her to give birth. She wrapped her firstborn son and laid him in a manger because there was no room for them in the lodging place.", "Shepherds were staying in the fields nearby, keeping watch at night. An angel appeared and the glory of the Lord shone around them. They were frightened. The angel told them of good news and great joy: a Saviour, Christ the Lord, had been born in David's town. The sign they were given was a baby wrapped and lying in a manger. A multitude from heaven praised God for what had happened.", "When the angels had gone, the shepherds decided to go to Bethlehem and see what the Lord had made known. They went quickly and found Mary, Joseph and the child lying where the angel had said. They made known what they had been told about him. The people who heard were amazed, while Mary kept these things and pondered them in her heart.", "The shepherds returned to their work glorifying and praising God for what they had heard and seen. Eight days later the child was circumcised and named Jesus, the name given by the angel before he was conceived."], "lesson": "The shepherds act on a sign they can go and check. Mary shows another faithful response by keeping and pondering what she hears.", "question": "What did the shepherds do between hearing the news and telling it to others?"},
  {"id": 91110, "title": "Simeon and Anna See the Child", "subtitle": "Waiting with hope and attention", "text": "Luke 2:22–40", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 22, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["When the days of purification were completed, Mary and Joseph brought Jesus to Jerusalem to present him to the Lord as the law required. Luke records the offering they brought: a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons. They came to carry out an ordinary duty, and there they met people who had been waiting for something God had promised.", "Simeon was righteous and devout and was waiting for Israel's consolation. The Holy Spirit had told him he would see the Lord's Christ before he died. Led by the Spirit, he came to the temple. When the parents brought Jesus in, Simeon took the child in his arms and blessed God. He said he could depart in peace because he had seen God's salvation, prepared before all peoples, a light for the Gentiles and glory for Israel.", "Jesus' parents marvelled. Simeon blessed them and spoke to Mary of a child appointed for the fall and rising of many and for a sign people would speak against. He told her that a sword would pierce her own soul. The words spoken over the child gave her reason to prepare for grief as well as to give thanks.", "Anna, a prophetess and widow who served in the temple with fasting and prayer, came up at that hour. She thanked God and spoke about the child to people waiting for redemption in Jerusalem. After doing what the law required, the family returned to Nazareth, where the child grew in strength and wisdom under God's grace."], "lesson": "Years of prayer have attuned Simeon and Anna to what God is doing. Simeon's words put joy and coming sorrow before Mary together.", "question": "What had Simeon and Anna been waiting for, and how did each respond?"},
  {"id": 91111, "title": "Three Days Searching for Jesus", "subtitle": "Growing in wisdom and obedience", "text": "Luke 2:41–52", "chapters": [[41, 2]], "start": 41, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus' parents went to Jerusalem every year for Passover. When he was twelve, they went up as usual. After the feast they began the journey home, but Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem without their knowing it. Thinking he was with the travelling company, they went a day's journey and looked for him among relatives and acquaintances. When they did not find him, they went back to Jerusalem searching for him.", "After three days they found him in the temple, sitting among the teachers. He was listening and asking questions. Those who heard him were astonished at his understanding and answers. Mary and Joseph were also astonished when they saw him, but they had been living through a different part of the story as they looked for him.", "Mary asked why he had treated them in this way and told him how anxiously she and his father had searched. Jesus asked why they had been looking for him and whether they did not know that he must be concerned with his Father's affairs. Luke says they did not understand his words.", "Jesus went back with them to Nazareth and lived in obedience to them. Mary kept these things in her heart. Luke describes Jesus growing in wisdom and stature and in favour with God and people. The account leaves Mary with something she can remember before she can fully explain it, and it leaves Jesus growing within the household to which he returned."], "lesson": "Luke records both Jesus' awareness of his Father and his obedience in the household. Mary keeps what she cannot yet explain and allows time for understanding.", "question": "What does Luke say Jesus did after the conversation at the temple?"},
  {"id": 91112, "title": "What Shall We Do?", "subtitle": "Repentance in daily dealings", "text": "Luke 3:1–20", "chapters": [[41, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke names the rulers and high priests of the time before moving to the wilderness, where God's word came to John son of Zechariah. John went through the country around the Jordan preaching a baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins. Luke connects his work with Isaiah's voice calling for the Lord's way to be prepared, with rough ground made smooth so that all people might see God's salvation.", "John warned the crowds who came to be baptised. They could not rely on descent from Abraham to escape judgement. They should produce fruit in keeping with repentance. He pictured an axe already laid at the root of trees that failed to bear good fruit. People asked him what they should do.", "His answers addressed the circumstances of the people who asked. Whoever had two coats should share with someone who had none, and whoever had food should do likewise. Tax collectors should take only what was appointed. Soldiers should avoid extortion and false accusation and be content with their wages. The fruit John asked for could be seen in the way people used what they had and treated people in their power.", "People wondered whether John was the Christ. He spoke of someone mightier who would baptise with the Holy Spirit and fire and clear his threshing floor. John continued exhorting the people. He also reproved Herod for his conduct, including his dealings with Herodias. Luke records that Herod added to his wrongs by shutting John in prison."], "lesson": "John answers each group in terms of the power and goods already in its hands. Repentance reaches how people earn, speak, share and exercise authority.", "question": "What would a practical answer like John's sound like in your own work?"},
  {"id": 91113, "title": "The Son at Prayer", "subtitle": "Jesus and the human family", "text": "Luke 3:21–38", "chapters": [[41, 3]], "start": 21, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke has just described people coming to John in response to his preaching. Now he turns to Jesus at the river. Jesus also was baptised, and Luke gives particular attention to what happened while he was praying. Heaven opened, and the Holy Spirit descended upon him in bodily form like a dove. A voice from heaven addressed Jesus as the beloved Son in whom the Father was well pleased.", "Luke then tells the reader that Jesus was about thirty years old when he began. People supposed him to be Joseph's son. The account turns from the voice at the baptism to a record of generations, working backwards from Joseph. Luke moves through a succession of names to Zerubbabel and Shealtiel and farther back through Nathan to David.", "David is followed in the backward record by Jesse and earlier generations, including Boaz and Obed, then Judah, Jacob, Isaac and Abraham. Luke continues beyond Abraham through Shem and Noah and through the generations before the flood. The line reaches Seth and Adam, whom the record calls the son of God.", "The passage gives the reader two statements about sonship to hear carefully. At the baptism the Father speaks directly to Jesus. In the genealogy Luke follows Jesus' human family line as it was understood, past the kings and patriarchs of Israel to the beginning of humanity. The next account will take Jesus from the Jordan into the wilderness to face a challenge to the words spoken there."], "lesson": "Luke places a genealogy reaching to Adam beside the Father's declaration. The two passages give the reader a wide horizon for the ministry about to begin.", "question": "How does Luke's decision to trace the line to Adam widen the opening of the story?"},
  {"id": 91114, "title": "Three Tests in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Obedience in hunger and weakness", "text": "Luke 4:1–13", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus returned from the Jordan full of the Holy Spirit and was led in the wilderness. For forty days he was tempted by the devil. He ate nothing during those days, and when they ended he was hungry. The first suggestion came at the point of that hunger. If he was God's Son, the devil said, he could command a stone to become bread. Jesus answered from Scripture about the food a person needs to live.", "The devil then took Jesus up and showed him the kingdoms of the world in a moment of time. He offered their power and glory in return for worship. Jesus answered that Scripture directs people to worship the Lord their God and serve him alone. The offer of authority came with a demand Jesus refused to meet.", "Next the devil brought him to Jerusalem and placed him on a high point of the temple. He again challenged him to act if he was God's Son, this time by throwing himself down. To support the proposal he quoted words about angels being given charge over him and bearing him up. Jesus answered with the command that a person must not put the Lord his God to the test.", "Having completed the temptations, the devil departed for a time. Luke has placed the test of trust at the temple last. The promise of protection had been presented as a reason to force a display of it. Jesus' answer keeps trust joined to obedience."], "lesson": "Jesus meets both plain temptation and a temptation dressed in biblical words. His answers show how obedience holds Scripture together.", "question": "What is the difference between trusting God's promise and demanding proof of it?"},
  {"id": 91115, "title": "The Reading at Nazareth", "subtitle": "Receiving grace beyond familiar boundaries", "text": "Luke 4:14–30", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 14, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus returned to Galilee in the power of the Spirit. Reports about him spread and he taught in the synagogues. At Nazareth, where he had been brought up, he went to the synagogue on the Sabbath as he usually did. He stood to read and was handed the book of Isaiah. He read about the Spirit of the Lord anointing him to bring good news to the poor, freedom to the oppressed and sight to the blind, and to announce the Lord's acceptable year.", "He closed the book, returned it to the attendant and sat down. With everyone's eyes on him, he said that the Scripture had been fulfilled that day in their hearing. People wondered at his gracious words and asked whether he was Joseph's son.", "Jesus spoke of the proverb about a physician healing himself and of the expectation that he would do in his hometown what they had heard about Capernaum. He recalled two occasions when prophets helped people outside Israel. Elijah was sent to a widow at Sarepta during the famine. In Elisha's time, Naaman the Syrian was cleansed although many in Israel had leprosy.", "The listeners became furious. They drove Jesus out of the town and brought him to the brow of the hill intending to throw him down. He passed through their midst and went away. The people who had begun by marvelling at his gracious words rejected the examples he gave of where God's grace had gone."], "lesson": "Jesus' neighbours are willing to hear gracious words until he speaks of grace reaching outsiders. Their reaction tests how we respond when God's kindness crosses our boundaries.", "question": "Why did the examples of the widow and Naaman matter to Jesus' hearers?"},
  {"id": 91116, "title": "An Open Door and Another Town", "subtitle": "Compassion and a clear purpose", "text": "Luke 4:31–44", "chapters": [[41, 4]], "start": 31, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went down to Capernaum and taught on the Sabbath. The people were astonished at the authority of his word. A man in the synagogue who had an unclean spirit cried out and called him the Holy One of God. Jesus told the spirit to be silent and come out. It threw the man down in their midst and came out without harming him. Those who saw it spoke to one another about the authority with which Jesus commanded, and news spread through the region.", "Jesus left the synagogue and entered Simon's house. Simon's mother-in-law had a high fever, and people appealed to him on her behalf. He stood over her and rebuked the fever. It left her, and she got up at once and served them. The authority people had heard in the synagogue reached into an ordinary household and relieved someone whose need had been brought to him.", "At sunset people came bringing friends and relatives with many kinds of sickness. Jesus laid his hands on each of them and healed them. Demons came out of many, crying that he was the Christ, the Son of God. He rebuked them and would not let them speak.", "At daybreak Jesus went out to a deserted place. When the people found him, they tried to keep him from leaving. He explained that he must preach God's kingdom to other towns too because he had been sent for this purpose. Luke follows him on to the synagogues where that work continued."], "lesson": "Jesus gives personal attention to sufferers while keeping his larger mission before him. Real need in one place does not cancel the call to serve elsewhere.", "question": "What reason does Jesus give when the people want him to stay?"},
  {"id": 91117, "title": "Nets That Nearly Break", "subtitle": "A call that reaches past unworthiness", "text": "Luke 5:1–16", "chapters": [[41, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["People pressed around Jesus beside the lake of Gennesaret to hear God's word. Two boats were at the shore, and the fishermen were washing their nets. Jesus got into Simon's boat and asked him to put out a little from the land. He sat there and taught the people. When he finished, he told Simon to put out into deeper water and let down the nets.", "Simon explained that they had worked all night and caught nothing, but he said he would lower the net at Jesus' word. They caught so many fish that the net began to break. They signalled their partners in the other boat. Both boats filled until they began to sink. Simon Peter fell at Jesus' knees and asked him to depart, saying he was a sinful man. James and John, who were his partners, shared his astonishment.", "Jesus told Simon not to fear and said that from then on he would catch people. When they brought the boats to land, they left everything and followed him. The catch at the end of the night's work became the occasion for a different calling.", "In one of the towns a man covered with leprosy fell on his face before Jesus. He said that if Jesus was willing, he could make him clean. Jesus stretched out his hand, touched him and cleansed him. He sent the man to the priest with the offering Moses required. The report spread, crowds came for teaching and healing, and Jesus withdrew into deserted places to pray."], "lesson": "Simon and the man with leprosy each approach Jesus conscious of a barrier. Jesus answers one with a calling and the other with a touch.", "question": "Why does Simon's response to the catch go beyond gratitude for fish?"},
  {"id": 91118, "title": "A Man Lowered and a Tax Collector Called", "subtitle": "Forgiveness and a place at the table", "text": "Luke 5:17–39", "chapters": [[41, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was teaching, with Pharisees and teachers of the law present from Galilee, Judea and Jerusalem. Some men carried a paralysed man on a bed and tried to bring him in. They could not get through the crowd, so they went onto the roof and lowered him through the tiles into the middle of the room. Jesus saw their faith and told the man his sins were forgiven.", "The scribes and Pharisees asked inwardly who could forgive sins except God. Jesus knew what they were thinking and asked which was easier to say, that the man's sins were forgiven or that he should get up and walk. To show his authority to forgive, he told him to rise, take his bed and go home. He did so in front of them and went away glorifying God. The crowd was amazed.", "Jesus went out and saw a tax collector named Levi sitting at the booth. He called him to follow. Levi left everything, rose and followed him, then made a great feast for him at his house. When people complained about Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners, he answered with a physician's work among the sick and said he had come to call sinners to repentance.", "A question then arose about fasting. Jesus spoke of a bridegroom, a new patch and wineskins. The people at the feast were hearing how his presence shaped the practices they were accustomed to judging."], "lesson": "Jesus' choice to eat with people others shun belongs to his call to repentance. The healing and the feast both give visible form to his concern for people in need.", "question": "How does Jesus' picture of a physician explain his presence at Levi's table?"},
  {"id": 91119, "title": "A Sabbath Question and a Night of Prayer", "subtitle": "Mercy and the calling of the Twelve", "text": "Luke 6:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus and the disciples passed through grainfields. The disciples plucked heads of grain, rubbed them in their hands and ate. Pharisees asked why they were doing what they considered unlawful. Jesus recalled David eating the bread reserved for priests when he and his men were hungry. He declared the Son of Man Lord of the Sabbath.", "On another Sabbath Jesus taught in a synagogue where a man had a withered right hand. Scribes and Pharisees watched to see if Jesus would heal him so they could accuse him. Knowing their thoughts, Jesus had the man stand in the middle. He asked whether the Sabbath allowed doing good or harm, saving a life or destroying it. He told the man to stretch out his hand, and it was restored. His opponents were furious and discussed what they might do to him.", "In those days Jesus went out to a mountain and spent the whole night praying to God. When day came, he called his disciples and chose twelve of them, naming them apostles. Luke records their names, including Judas Iscariot, who became the betrayer.", "Jesus came down with them and stood on a level place. A large gathering of disciples and people from as far as Jerusalem and the coast of Tyre and Sidon had come to hear him and be healed. People reached out to touch him. The night of prayer and choice of the Twelve were followed by a public gathering full of need."], "lesson": "Luke places a night of prayer before a consequential choice. Public ministry and the calling of fellow workers arise alongside time spent before God.", "question": "What does Jesus do before choosing the Twelve, and how might that shape your own decisions?"},
  {"id": 91120, "title": "The Level Place and Two Foundations", "subtitle": "Mercy that reaches enemies", "text": "Luke 6:20–49", "chapters": [[41, 6]], "start": 20, "end": 49, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus looked at his disciples and spoke of people who were poor, hungry, weeping or rejected for the Son of Man's sake. He told them what they could hope for. He also spoke woes to people already satisfied with riches, food, laughter and public approval. The conditions people could see in the present did not tell the whole story of where their lives were going.", "He told those listening to love their enemies, bless people who cursed them and pray for those who mistreated them. He spoke of giving and lending, and asked what was distinctive about doing good only to those who could do good in return. He directed his hearers to the mercy of their Father and called them to be merciful. He addressed judging, forgiving and giving.", "Then Jesus spoke of a blind person leading another, and of someone trying to remove a speck from a brother's eye while overlooking a beam in his own. He used fruit to show how a tree could be known and speech to show what the heart was full of. He asked why people called him Lord while disregarding what he said.", "He described two houses. A person who heard and obeyed dug deep and laid a foundation on rock. A flood could not shake the house. The person who heard and failed to act built without a foundation, and the flood brought it down."], "lesson": "The risk Jesus describes is hearing strong teaching and leaving ordinary conduct untouched. Mercy towards a person who cannot repay us is one place obedience becomes visible.", "question": "What would doing one instruction from this sermon look like before the week ends?"},
  {"id": 91121, "title": "A Servant and a Widow's Son", "subtitle": "Authority exercised with compassion", "text": "Luke 7:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["After Jesus finished speaking, he entered Capernaum. A centurion's servant, whom the officer valued, was gravely ill. The centurion sent Jewish elders to ask Jesus to come and heal him. The elders pleaded for him, saying he loved their people and had built their synagogue. Jesus went with them.", "Before he reached the house, the officer sent friends with a message. He did not think himself worthy to have Jesus under his roof or to approach him in person. He knew how an order could be obeyed at a distance, because he himself served under authority and gave orders to soldiers. He trusted that Jesus could speak the word and heal his servant. Jesus marvelled and said he had found no such faith in Israel. The messengers returned to find the servant well.", "Jesus then went to Nain. At the town gate he met a funeral procession. The man who had died was the only son of a widow, and a large company from the town was with her. Jesus saw her, had compassion and told her not to weep.", "He touched the bier and the men carrying it stood still. He told the young man to rise. The man sat up and began to speak, and Jesus gave him back to his mother. Fear came over the people, and they glorified God for visiting his people. News of the event spread."], "lesson": "In Nain Jesus notices a grieving person before anyone asks him for help. The centurion's confidence in his authority is followed by an account of the compassion with which he uses it.", "question": "What about the widow's situation does Luke take care to tell you?"},
  {"id": 91122, "title": "John's Messengers Bring a Question", "subtitle": "Taking questions to Jesus", "text": "Luke 7:18–35", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 18, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["John's disciples reported these things to him. He called two of them and sent them to Jesus with a question. Was he the one who was to come, or should they look for someone else? They reached Jesus and repeated the question. Luke says that at that hour Jesus healed many people, delivered those troubled by evil spirits and gave sight to blind people.", "He told the messengers to return and tell John what they had seen and heard. The blind saw, the lame walked, people with leprosy were cleansed, the deaf heard, the dead were raised and good news was preached to the poor. He added a blessing for the person who did not stumble on account of him.", "After they left, Jesus spoke to the crowds about John. He asked what they had gone into the wilderness to see. John was more than a prophet, the messenger appointed to prepare the way. Luke notes that people who had received John's baptism acknowledged God's justice, while Pharisees and lawyers who had refused it rejected God's counsel for themselves.", "Jesus compared the generation to children complaining in the marketplace when others would neither dance nor mourn. John had lived austerely and been accused of having a demon. The Son of Man ate and drank and was called a glutton and a friend of sinners. Opposite ways of living had given people two excuses for withholding a response."], "lesson": "Jesus answers a serious question with evidence. His picture of children also exposes the habit of rejecting a messenger whatever he does.", "question": "What did Jesus give John's messengers to tell him on returning?"},
  {"id": 91123, "title": "Tears at Simon's Table", "subtitle": "Love that grows from forgiveness", "text": "Luke 7:36–50", "chapters": [[41, 7]], "start": 36, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Simon, a Pharisee, invited Jesus to eat with him. As Jesus reclined at the meal, a woman known in the city as a sinner entered with a jar of ointment. She stood behind him at his feet, weeping. Her tears wet his feet. She wiped them with her hair, kissed them and anointed them. Simon watched and reasoned to himself that a prophet ought to know who was touching him.", "Jesus told Simon he had something to say. Two people owed money to the same lender. One owed five hundred denarii and the other fifty. Neither could pay, and the lender cancelled both debts. Jesus asked which of the two would love him more. Simon supposed it would be the one whose larger debt had been cancelled, and Jesus said he had judged rightly.", "Jesus turned towards the woman and asked Simon whether he saw her. Simon had given him no water for his feet, while she had washed them with tears. He had given him no kiss of greeting, while she kept kissing his feet. He had not anointed Jesus' head with oil, while she had anointed his feet with ointment. Jesus drew a connection between forgiveness and the love made visible in her actions.", "Then he told the woman her sins were forgiven. The other guests wondered who he was to forgive sins. Jesus told her that her faith had saved her and sent her away in peace. Her last words from the table were addressed to her directly, after others had been speaking or thinking about her."], "lesson": "Simon classifies the woman by her past. Jesus makes her actions visible and asks Simon to consider the love that gratitude for mercy can produce.", "question": "Which acts of welcome does Jesus name when he turns Simon's attention to the woman?"},
  {"id": 91124, "title": "Women on the Road, Seed in the Soil", "subtitle": "Receiving the word and doing it", "text": "Luke 8:1–21", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went through towns and villages announcing the good news of God's kingdom. The Twelve went with him. Luke also names women who had been healed and supported the company from their resources: Mary Magdalene, Joanna the wife of Chuza, Susanna and many others. Their care belongs to the account of how the travelling ministry went forward.", "When people gathered from many towns, Jesus told them of a sower whose seed fell on a path, on rocky ground, among thorns and on good soil. The first seed was trampled and eaten by birds. The shallow ground could not hold moisture, and the thorns choked what grew among them. The good soil brought a crop. Jesus called on those with ears to hear.", "His disciples asked what the parable meant. He said the seed was God's word. Some people heard and lost it. Others received it with joy and fell away when tested. Cares, riches and pleasures kept some from bearing mature fruit. The good soil pictured people who held the word in an honest and good heart and bore fruit with patience.", "He spoke next of placing a lamp where people could see and called for care in how they heard. When his mother and brothers could not reach him through the crowd, he said his family were those who heard God's word and did it. The passage ends by joining hearing to doing once more."], "lesson": "Luke names people whose service made the travelling ministry possible. The teaching that follows calls for a response with the patience to bear fruit.", "question": "What does Jesus say marks the soil that brings fruit to maturity?"},
  {"id": 91125, "title": "Across the Storm to the Tombs", "subtitle": "Faith and testimony", "text": "Luke 8:22–39", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 22, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus got into a boat with his disciples and told them to cross the lake. While they sailed he fell asleep. A storm came down upon the water, the boat began to fill and they were in danger. They woke him, crying that they were perishing. Jesus rebuked the wind and waves, and they stopped. He asked where their faith was. Afraid and astonished, they asked one another who he was, since winds and water obeyed him.", "When they landed opposite Galilee, a man from the town met Jesus. He had long been afflicted by demons, wore no clothes and lived among the tombs. People had kept him bound and watched, but he broke away and was driven into deserted places. As Jesus commanded the spirit to come out, he asked its name. The answer was Legion, because many demons had entered him.", "The demons begged to go into pigs feeding nearby. When Jesus allowed it, the herd rushed down the steep bank into the lake and drowned. The people who came after hearing the herdsmen's report found the man sitting at Jesus' feet, clothed and in his right mind. Fear seized them, and they asked Jesus to leave.", "The man asked to go with him. Jesus sent him home to tell what God had done for him. He went through the town telling what Jesus had done. The people who knew his former condition now had his own account of what had changed."], "lesson": "The restored man's neighbours have known his former condition. Jesus gives him a ministry among those who can see the change for themselves.", "question": "Why might the man's testimony matter particularly in his own home and town?"},
  {"id": 91126, "title": "The Woman Who Touched His Garment", "subtitle": "Care for a person hidden by the crowd", "text": "Luke 8:40–56", "chapters": [[41, 8]], "start": 40, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["When Jesus returned, the crowd welcomed him because they had been waiting for him. Jairus, a ruler of the synagogue, fell at his feet and pleaded with him to come to his house. His only daughter was about twelve and was dying. As Jesus went with him, the crowd pressed closely around him.", "A woman in the crowd had been bleeding for twelve years. She had found no one able to heal her. She came from behind and touched the edge of Jesus' garment, and the bleeding stopped. Jesus asked who had touched him. When people denied it, Peter pointed out that the crowd was pressing around him. Jesus said he knew that power had gone out from him. The woman came trembling, fell before him and told everyone why she had touched him and how she had been healed at once. He called her daughter and sent her away in peace.", "While Jesus was still speaking, someone came from Jairus's house and said the child had died. Jesus told Jairus to be unafraid and believe. At the house he admitted only Peter, John, James and the girl's parents. People were weeping, and they laughed when he spoke of her as sleeping.", "Jesus took the girl by the hand and told her to rise. Her spirit returned and she got up. He directed that she be given food. The parents were astonished, and Jesus told them to keep what happened to themselves."], "lesson": "Jesus gives both sufferers personal attention. The woman's public account allows the crowd to hear the peace he speaks to someone who could have gone away unnoticed.", "question": "What does the woman do after she realises that she cannot remain hidden?"},
  {"id": 91127, "title": "Sending the Twelve and Feeding the Crowd", "subtitle": "Serving from what God provides", "text": "Luke 9:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus called the Twelve together and gave them authority over demons and diseases. He sent them to proclaim God's kingdom and heal the sick. They were to travel without spare supplies, stay in the house that received them and shake the dust from their feet when a town refused them. They went through the villages preaching and healing.", "Reports of what was happening reached Herod the tetrarch. Some said John had risen, others that Elijah had appeared or that one of the old prophets had risen. Herod knew he had beheaded John. He asked who this could be and wanted to see Jesus. Luke leaves that question with Herod and turns back to the apostles.", "They returned and told Jesus what they had done. He took them away towards Bethsaida, but the crowds found out and followed. Jesus welcomed them, taught them about the kingdom and healed those in need. As the day wore away, the Twelve wanted to send the people into surrounding villages for lodging and food. Jesus told the disciples to feed them.", "They counted five loaves and two fish. At Jesus' direction they seated the people in groups of about fifty. He looked towards heaven, blessed and broke the food and gave it to the disciples to set before the crowd. Everyone ate and was satisfied, and twelve baskets of pieces remained. The apostles who had been sent out without provisions now distributed a meal in a deserted place."], "lesson": "The disciples return from ministry and find another occasion to serve. Jesus welcomes the people who have interrupted their retreat and gives the disciples a part in feeding them.", "question": "How does Jesus respond to the crowd before there is any talk of bread?"},
  {"id": 91128, "title": "Peter's Answer and the Voice From the Cloud", "subtitle": "Hearing Jesus about the road ahead", "text": "Luke 9:18–36", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 18, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was praying apart from the crowd, with his disciples nearby. He asked who the crowds said he was. They reported that some said John the Baptist, others Elijah or an old prophet risen again. Then he asked the disciples for their own answer. Peter said he was the Christ of God.", "Jesus charged them to keep it to themselves and told them what the Son of Man would face. The elders, chief priests and scribes would reject him, he would be killed and he would rise on the third day. He spoke to all about denying themselves, taking up a cross daily and following him. He asked what a person would gain by winning the world and losing himself.", "About eight days after these sayings, Jesus took Peter, John and James up a mountain to pray. While he prayed, his appearance changed and his clothing became bright. Moses and Elijah appeared in glory and spoke with him about the departure he was to accomplish at Jerusalem. The disciples, heavy with sleep, became fully awake and saw his glory.", "As the men were leaving, Peter suggested three shelters without knowing what he was saying. A cloud overshadowed them, and a voice directed them to hear Jesus, God's beloved Son. Then they saw Jesus alone. They kept silent about what they had seen. Luke has let the disciples hear about Jerusalem twice, once from Jesus himself and once in the conversation on the mountain."], "lesson": "The conversation on the mountain concerns the road Jesus has already described. Glory and the costly work awaiting him belong together in Luke's account.", "question": "What subjects connect Jesus' first prediction with the conversation on the mountain?"},
  {"id": 91129, "title": "From the Mountain Towards Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Humility and a settled purpose", "text": "Luke 9:37–62", "chapters": [[41, 9]], "start": 37, "end": 62, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The following day, when they came down from the mountain, a crowd met Jesus. A father cried out for help for his only child. A spirit caused the boy great suffering, and the disciples had been unable to drive it out. As the boy came towards Jesus, the spirit threw him down. Jesus rebuked it, healed the boy and gave him back to his father.", "People marvelled, but Jesus told the disciples to listen carefully: the Son of Man would be handed over. They did not understand and were afraid to ask. When an argument arose about who was greatest, Jesus set a child beside him and spoke about receiving the child in his name. John then told him the disciples had tried to stop someone acting in Jesus' name. Jesus told them not to stop him.", "As the time drew near for Jesus to be taken up, he set his face towards Jerusalem. Messengers entered a Samaritan village, but the people would not receive him because he was headed for Jerusalem. James and John asked about calling down fire. Jesus rebuked them, and they went to another village.", "On the road, several people spoke of following him or were called to follow. Jesus spoke about having nowhere to lay his head, the urgency of proclaiming God's kingdom and the danger of looking back after putting a hand to the plough. The journey to Jerusalem had begun, and his words pressed the question of what it meant to go with him."], "lesson": "Several scenes show the disciples using rank, group identity or power to settle questions Jesus handles differently. His settled direction towards Jerusalem gives shape to his replies about following.", "question": "What responses does Jesus correct as the disciples move from the mountain to the road?"},
  {"id": 91130, "title": "The Messengers Return Rejoicing", "subtitle": "Joy rooted in belonging to God", "text": "Luke 10:1–24", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus appointed another group of messengers and sent them in pairs to the places he intended to visit. He told them the harvest was plentiful and the workers few, and asked them to pray for workers. They were going out like lambs among wolves. They were to travel simply, speak peace upon entering a house and remain with those who received them, accepting what was set before them.", "In a town that welcomed them they were to care for the sick and announce that God's kingdom had come near. Where people refused them, they were to tell them plainly and leave. Jesus spoke of the seriousness of such refusal and of how receiving or rejecting the messengers concerned the one who had sent them.", "The messengers returned with joy because even demons had been subject to them in Jesus' name. He spoke of Satan falling and of authority given to them. Then he directed their joy towards a different fact: their names were written in heaven.", "Jesus rejoiced and thanked the Father for revealing things to little children that were hidden from the wise and understanding. He spoke of the Son making the Father known. Turning privately to the disciples, he called their eyes blessed for seeing what prophets and kings had longed to see. The people who set out as workers came back with reason to give thanks for what they themselves had received."], "lesson": "The messengers have good reason to rejoice in what they have seen. Jesus directs them to a more lasting ground for joy than results they can report.", "question": "What reason for rejoicing does Jesus place above the success of their mission?"},
  {"id": 91131, "title": "The Good Samaritan", "subtitle": "Becoming a neighbour through mercy", "text": "Luke 10:25–37", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 25, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A lawyer stood up to test Jesus by asking what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus asked what was written in the law and how he read it. The lawyer answered that a person should love God with heart, soul, strength and mind, and love his neighbour as himself. Jesus told him to do this and live. Wanting to justify himself, the lawyer asked who his neighbour was.", "Jesus told him about a man travelling from Jerusalem down to Jericho. Robbers stripped him, wounded him and left him half dead. A priest came down the road and passed on the other side when he saw him. A Levite also came to the place, looked at the man and passed by. The man remained where the robbers had left him.", "Then a Samaritan came along the road. He saw the wounded man and had compassion. He bandaged the wounds, pouring on oil and wine, put the man on his own animal and took him to an inn. There he cared for him. The next day he paid the innkeeper and asked him to continue the care, promising to repay any additional expense on his return.", "Jesus asked which of the three had proved a neighbour to the man who fell among robbers. The lawyer answered that it was the one who showed him mercy. Jesus told him to go and do likewise. The conversation that began by asking where the duty ended closed with a concrete example of what mercy required."], "lesson": "The Samaritan gives the injured man time, transport and money. Jesus directs the lawyer's attention to what it means to act as a neighbour when need is already in front of him.", "question": "Which of the Samaritan's actions required him to give up something of his own?"},
  {"id": 91132, "title": "At Martha's House", "subtitle": "Making room to listen to Jesus", "text": "Luke 10:38–42", "chapters": [[41, 10]], "start": 38, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["As Jesus travelled on, he entered a village, and a woman named Martha welcomed him into her house. Luke has just finished telling of a wounded traveller for whom practical care could not wait. Now he brings the reader into another home and another kind of need. Here the person who has been welcomed has something to say.", "Martha's sister Mary sat at Jesus' feet and listened to his words. Martha was occupied and distracted by much serving. Luke does not give a list of her tasks. He tells us how they affected her and how she brought the matter to Jesus.", "Martha asked whether he cared that Mary had left her to serve alone. She wanted him to tell her sister to help. Jesus addressed Martha by name twice. He said she was anxious and troubled about many things, and told her that Mary had chosen the good part, which would not be taken away from her.", "The account ends with his answer. Martha's welcome has given Jesus a place in the house. Mary's place at his feet shows the attention that a guest's words can require. The question Martha asked gives the reader a way to notice how helpful work can go on until the person being served is scarcely heard."], "lesson": "Martha's welcome is real, and so is the strain she feels. Jesus names the distraction that can crowd out listening even when the work began as hospitality.", "question": "What was Mary doing when Martha asked Jesus to intervene?"},
  {"id": 91133, "title": "Teach Us to Pray", "subtitle": "Asking the Father with confidence", "text": "Luke 11:1–13", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus had been praying in a certain place. When he finished, one disciple asked him to teach them to pray, as John had taught his disciples. Jesus gave them a prayer directed to the Father. It asked for his name to be honoured and his kingdom to come. It brought daily bread, forgiveness and the need for deliverance from temptation into the same prayer. Those asking forgiveness were to remember the debts of people they themselves forgave.", "Jesus then asked them to imagine going to a friend at midnight to ask for three loaves. A traveller had arrived, and the host had nothing to give him. From inside, the friend said the door was shut and his children were already in bed. Jesus said the man would get up and give what was needed because the request kept being made.", "He urged his hearers to ask, seek and knock. The needs people carry into prayer could be spoken plainly and brought again. He then pictured a child asking a parent for food. A parent would understand the difference between a fish and a serpent or between an egg and a scorpion.", "If people who fell short knew how to give good gifts to their children, Jesus said, their heavenly Father would give the Holy Spirit to those who asked him. The passage begins with disciples watching someone pray and ends by giving them a reason to trust the one to whom they pray."], "lesson": "Jesus gives his disciples words for both large hopes and daily needs. The assurance of the Father's goodness gives persistence in prayer a personal basis.", "question": "How does the midnight request prepare you for what Jesus says about the Father?"},
  {"id": 91134, "title": "The Sign and the Lamp", "subtitle": "Taking care how you receive light", "text": "Luke 11:14–36", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 14, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus drove out a demon that had kept a man from speaking. When the man spoke, the crowd marvelled. Some people said Jesus drove out demons by Beelzebub, their ruler. Others wanted a sign from heaven. Jesus knew their thoughts and asked how a kingdom divided against itself could stand. If he drove out demons by the finger of God, then God's kingdom had already come upon them.", "He pictured a strong man guarding his house until someone stronger overpowered him and took away his armour. He also spoke of a spirit that left a person and later returned with seven others, finding its former place swept and put in order. When a woman in the crowd called his mother blessed, Jesus spoke of the blessing of hearing God's word and keeping it.", "As the crowd thickened, Jesus answered the demand for a sign. He called attention to Jonah and to the Ninevites who repented when they heard him. He recalled the queen who travelled to hear Solomon's wisdom. Both examples asked his hearers what they had done with the person already before them.", "He ended with a lamp that belongs where people can see its light. Speaking of the eye as the lamp of the body, he told them to see that the light in them was not darkness. The passage begins with people disputing what they saw and ends with an instruction to attend to how they see."], "lesson": "A demand for another sign can keep a person from facing the evidence already present. Jesus calls attention to the condition of those who are looking.", "question": "What does Jesus say Nineveh did when it had heard Jonah?"},
  {"id": 91135, "title": "The Meal That Exposed Hidden Burdens", "subtitle": "Justice and love alongside religious care", "text": "Luke 11:37–54", "chapters": [[41, 11]], "start": 37, "end": 54, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A Pharisee invited Jesus to eat with him. When Jesus sat down without first washing, the host was surprised. Jesus spoke of people cleaning the outside of a cup and dish while leaving greed and wickedness within. The one who made the outside had also made the inside. The host had noticed a custom at the table, and Jesus directed his attention to matters at work beneath it.", "Jesus spoke of careful tithes on mint, rue and herbs alongside neglect of justice and the love of God. His hearers could attend to the one without leaving the other undone. He mentioned prominent seats and public greetings, then pictured unmarked graves that people walked over without knowing what lay beneath them.", "A lawyer objected that these words insulted his group too. Jesus replied that they placed burdens on people which they would not touch with a finger themselves. He recalled the prophets and messengers who had been killed or persecuted, and spoke of people building their tombs while sharing in the disposition of those who killed them.", "Jesus then charged the lawyers with taking away the key of knowledge. They had not entered, and they obstructed those who were entering. As he left, scribes and Pharisees began pressing him on many subjects, looking for something he might say that they could use against him. The invitation had become an occasion for examining what teachers were doing to the people they were meant to help."], "lesson": "A person can take care over visible duties and leave a neighbour under an unnecessary burden. Jesus connects knowledge and authority with responsibility for what they do to others.", "question": "Which matters did Jesus say were neglected while people paid attention to small tithes?"},
  {"id": 91136, "title": "Barns, Ravens and an Inheritance", "subtitle": "Trust when possessions promise security", "text": "Luke 12:1–34", "chapters": [[41, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A great crowd gathered, pressing upon one another. Jesus began by warning his disciples about the leaven of the Pharisees, which he called hypocrisy. What was concealed would come to light. He told his friends where to place their fear and reminded them that God knew even what became of sparrows. He also spoke of acknowledging him openly and of the Spirit helping them when they had to answer before authorities.", "Someone in the crowd asked him to tell a brother to divide an inheritance. Jesus declined to act as their judge and warned against covetousness. He told of a rich man whose land produced so much that he planned to pull down his barns and build larger ones. He expected to live at ease from what he stored. God told him that his life would be required that night. The man could make plans for his produce without knowing who would use it.", "Jesus turned to the disciples and spoke about food and clothing. Ravens did not sow or store food in barns, yet God fed them. Lilies did not labour or spin, yet Solomon was not clothed like one of them. Their Father knew what they needed.", "He told them to seek God's kingdom and reminded them of the Father's pleasure in giving it to them. He spoke of giving to people in need and of treasure safe from thieves and decay. Where their treasure was, there their hearts would be."], "lesson": "The man in the parable makes plans that centre on his own stores. Jesus gives his disciples a different way to reckon with provision, generosity and the future.", "question": "What did the rich man leave out of the conversation he had with himself?"},
  {"id": 91137, "title": "The Lamps and the Changing Weather", "subtitle": "Faithfulness while there is time", "text": "Luke 12:35–59", "chapters": [[41, 12]], "start": 35, "end": 59, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus pictured servants keeping their lamps lit while waiting for their master to come back from a wedding. Those he found watching would be blessed. Jesus gave the picture an unexpected turn: the master would seat those servants and serve them himself. He also spoke of a householder who would watch if he knew when a thief was coming. The Son of Man would come at an hour people did not expect.", "Peter asked whether the parable was for the disciples or everyone. Jesus described a steward charged with giving the household food at the proper time. A faithful steward would be found doing that work. Another might use the master's delay as permission to mistreat servants and indulge himself. The return would show what each had made of his responsibility. More was required of those who had been entrusted with more.", "Jesus spoke of division that would arise even among members of the same family. His coming would force decisions people in the same household might make differently. He spoke of the distress he himself faced as he approached what he called a baptism.", "Then he turned to the crowd. They could read signs of rain or heat in the weather, yet failed to judge the time they themselves were living in. He pictured two people on their way to court and urged settlement before they reached the judge. The road still gave them time to act."], "lesson": "The faithful steward uses his authority to care for people during the master's absence. Jesus' urgency concerns decisions a hearer can still make today.", "question": "What does the faithful steward give the household, and when does he give it?"},
  {"id": 91138, "title": "A Fig Tree Granted Time and a Woman Set Free", "subtitle": "Repentance and mercy without delay", "text": "Luke 13:1–17", "chapters": [[41, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Some people told Jesus about Galileans whom Pilate had killed while they were offering sacrifices. Jesus asked whether those Galileans were worse sinners than others because they had suffered in that way. He gave another example from Jerusalem, where a tower in Siloam fell and killed eighteen people. In both cases he turned the hearers from judging the victims to considering their own need to repent.", "He told of a man who came looking for fruit on a fig tree in his vineyard and found none. After three years he wanted it cut down. The gardener asked for another year to dig around it and manure it, with a decision to follow if it still bore no fruit. The extra time had a purpose.", "On a Sabbath Jesus was teaching in a synagogue. He saw a woman who had been bent over for eighteen years. He called her, told her she was released from her infirmity and laid his hands on her. She stood upright and glorified God. The synagogue ruler objected to healing on the Sabbath and told people to come on other days.", "Jesus asked whether people untied an ox or donkey to take it to water on the Sabbath. He called the woman a daughter of Abraham whom Satan had bound for eighteen years and asked why she should remain bound that day. His opponents were ashamed, and the crowd rejoiced at what he had done."], "lesson": "More time in the parable comes with attention to bearing fruit. In the synagogue Jesus uses the time at hand to relieve a person's suffering.", "question": "How do the gardener and Jesus each respond to a need that has lasted a long time?"},
  {"id": 91139, "title": "A Narrow Door and a Lament", "subtitle": "Responding while the door is open", "text": "Luke 13:18–35", "chapters": [[41, 13]], "start": 18, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus asked what God's kingdom was like. He compared it to a mustard seed a man put in his garden. It grew until birds lodged in its branches. He compared it also to leaven a woman worked into flour until it had spread through the whole amount. In both pictures the effect became visible after an inconspicuous beginning.", "On his way towards Jerusalem, someone asked whether only a few would be saved. Jesus urged the hearers to strive to enter through the narrow door. He pictured a householder closing it while people outside knocked and appealed to having eaten in his company and heard him teach in their streets. He spoke of people arriving from every direction to sit in God's kingdom and of first and last places being reversed.", "Later some Pharisees warned him that Herod wanted to kill him. Jesus called Herod a fox and spoke of continuing his work of deliverance and healing. He said he must go on towards Jerusalem and spoke of the city in connection with the deaths of prophets.", "Then he addressed Jerusalem with grief. He had longed to gather its children as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, and they had refused. He told them their house was left desolate. The passage closes with the longing of the person whose invitation had been resisted."], "lesson": "Jesus answers speculation with a call to respond. His lament gives us a view of the compassion behind the urgency of his words.", "question": "How does Jesus redirect the question about who will be saved?"},
  {"id": 91140, "title": "Places at the Table and an Open Invitation", "subtitle": "Hospitality that expects no repayment", "text": "Luke 14:1–24", "chapters": [[41, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["On a Sabbath Jesus went to eat in the house of a leading Pharisee. People watched him closely. A man suffering from swelling stood before him. Jesus asked the lawyers and Pharisees whether it was lawful to heal on the Sabbath. They kept silent. He healed the man and sent him away, then asked what they would do if an animal fell into a well on that day. They could not answer.", "Seeing the guests choose places of honour, Jesus described a person who took the best place and had to move when someone more distinguished arrived. He advised taking the lower place and letting the host decide whether to move the guest higher. Then he told the host to invite people who could not repay him, including the poor and people with disabilities.", "A guest spoke of the blessing of eating in God's kingdom. Jesus told of a man who prepared a great supper and sent word when it was ready. The people invited offered excuses. One had bought land, another had bought oxen, and another had married. Each had something else to attend to.", "The host told his servant to bring in people from the streets and lanes. There was still room, so he sent him farther out to bring in more. The meal where people had been choosing the best seats became the setting for a story about a host determined to fill his house with people others might pass by."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks to the people choosing seats and to the people choosing guests. His teaching gives hospitality a purpose beyond rank and repayment.", "question": "Who would receive an invitation if you followed Jesus' advice to the host?"},
  {"id": 91141, "title": "Before You Build the Tower", "subtitle": "Counting the cost of discipleship", "text": "Luke 14:25–35", "chapters": [[41, 14]], "start": 25, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Large crowds were travelling with Jesus. He turned to them and spoke about the terms of following him. He named father, mother, spouse, children, brothers and sisters, and even a person's own life. His words put every close attachment under the claim of discipleship. He spoke of carrying a cross and coming after him.", "He asked them to picture a man who intended to build a tower. The builder would sit down first and reckon whether he had enough to finish it. If he laid the foundation and could go no further, the people who saw it would mock him. His beginning would remain visible while the work he had undertaken stayed unfinished.", "Jesus gave another example, a king considering whether his ten thousand men could meet an approaching army of twenty thousand. If he could not, he would send a delegation to seek terms of peace while the other king was still far away. In both pictures the time to consider what lay ahead came before the decisive step.", "Jesus applied the pictures to what a disciple must be willing to give up. He spoke of salt that had lost its usefulness and could no longer season anything. He closed by calling on those with ears to hear. The crowd had already been walking with him, and he was asking what each person was prepared to do as the journey continued."], "lesson": "Jesus respects his hearers enough to tell them what following will demand. Counting the cost gives substance to the commitment they are considering.", "question": "Why does Jesus speak of the builder before saying what a disciple must be ready to give up?"},
  {"id": 91142, "title": "The Sheep and the Coin", "subtitle": "Joy in finding the lost", "text": "Luke 15:1–10", "chapters": [[41, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Tax collectors and others known as sinners drew near to hear Jesus. Pharisees and scribes complained that he received such people and ate with them. Jesus answered by asking his hearers to consider what they would do if they had a hundred sheep and lost one. A person would leave the ninety-nine where they were and search for the missing sheep until he found it.", "When he found it, he would lay it on his shoulders with joy. At home he would call his friends and neighbours and ask them to rejoice with him. Jesus said there would be joy in heaven over one sinner who repented. A person whom the hearers might regard as an unwelcome guest had a place in that account of joy.", "Jesus then asked about a woman with ten silver coins who lost one. She would light a lamp, sweep the house and search carefully until she found it. Finding it, she would call her friends and neighbours to share her joy. The work of searching takes place inside the house in this picture, with a lamp and a broom.", "Jesus again connected the joy with one person who repented, this time naming joy in the presence of God's angels. Both stories end with the finder inviting others to celebrate. The people who had complained about Jesus receiving sinners were given two pictures of how they might respond when someone lost was found."], "lesson": "Both searchers take the initiative. Jesus uses their joy to challenge people who resented his welcome of those who needed repentance.", "question": "What do the shepherd and the woman both do as soon as they find what was lost?"},
  {"id": 91143, "title": "The Father Goes Out to Both Sons", "subtitle": "Repentance, welcome and resentment", "text": "Luke 15:11–32", "chapters": [[41, 15]], "start": 11, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A father's younger son asked for his share of the estate. The father divided his property between the two sons, and the younger gathered what he had and went to a far country. He wasted it there. When a famine came, he found work feeding pigs and was hungry enough to want their food. No one gave him any. He remembered how well his father's hired servants were fed and resolved to go home, confess his sin and ask to be taken on as a servant.", "While the son was still a long way off, his father saw him and had compassion. He ran to him, embraced him and kissed him. The son began his confession. The father called for the best robe, a ring and shoes, and told the servants to prepare a feast. The son he spoke of as dead was alive again. The one who had been lost had been found.", "The older son was in the field. Coming near the house, he heard music and dancing and asked a servant what had happened. When he heard, he grew angry and refused to go in. His father went out and pleaded with him. The older son recounted his years of service and complained that he had never been given even a young goat for a feast with his friends.", "The father told him that he was always with him and that what remained was his. He returned to the reason for the celebration: the one who had come back was his brother. Jesus leaves the two sons and the father's words before the hearer without saying whether the older son went inside."], "lesson": "The father approaches both sons, each at a different distance from the feast. The older son's words help us notice the resentment that can grow alongside outward service.", "question": "What does the father say to the older son about why the feast is necessary?"},
  {"id": 91144, "title": "The Steward Facing Dismissal", "subtitle": "Stewardship with the future in view", "text": "Luke 16:1–18", "chapters": [[41, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told his disciples about a rich man whose steward was accused of wasting his possessions. The man called the steward and asked him to give account, telling him that he could no longer hold the position. The steward considered his prospects. He felt unable to dig and ashamed to beg. He wanted people to receive him into their houses once he lost his post.", "He called his master's debtors in one by one. He asked the first what he owed, then told him to write a smaller amount for the oil. He did the same with another man's debt in wheat. The master commended the dishonest steward for acting shrewdly. The steward had recognised that the position he held would not last and acted with his future in mind.", "Jesus spoke of using worldly wealth with an eye to what endures. He made faithfulness in small things a test of faithfulness in greater things and asked who would entrust true riches to someone unfaithful with what belonged to another. He said that a servant could not serve both God and money.", "The Pharisees, who loved money, mocked him. Jesus said they justified themselves before people, but God knew their hearts. He spoke of the law and prophets in relation to the proclamation of the kingdom, affirmed that the law was not lightly set aside and ended with a saying about marriage and divorce."], "lesson": "The steward acts decisively because he knows an accounting is coming. Jesus connects that foresight with faithful conduct in the small responsibilities we already have.", "question": "Which qualities does Jesus demand of his hearers after describing the steward's shrewdness?"},
  {"id": 91145, "title": "The Man at the Gate", "subtitle": "Listening before the opportunity passes", "text": "Luke 16:19–31", "chapters": [[41, 16]], "start": 19, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus described a rich man who dressed in purple and fine linen and feasted splendidly every day. A poor man named Lazarus was laid at his gate, covered with sores and longing to eat the crumbs that fell from the rich man's table. Dogs came and licked his sores. The gate put the two lives close to one another while their circumstances remained far apart.", "Lazarus died and was carried by angels to Abraham's side. The rich man also died and was buried. In torment he looked up and saw Abraham with Lazarus far away. He called for Abraham to send Lazarus to dip the tip of his finger in water and cool his tongue.", "Abraham told him to remember what each had received in life, and said that a great gulf now lay between them which neither could cross. The rich man asked instead for Lazarus to warn his five brothers so they would not come to the same place. Even in this request he imagined Lazarus as someone who could be sent to serve his household.", "Abraham answered that the brothers had Moses and the prophets and should listen to them. The man insisted they would repent if someone went to them from the dead. Abraham said that if they would not hear Moses and the prophets, they would not be persuaded even if someone rose from the dead."], "lesson": "The gate places the rich man's responsibility near enough to see each day. Abraham's reply presses the living reader to act on the instruction already given.", "question": "What opportunity did the rich man have while Lazarus still lay at his gate?"},
  {"id": 91146, "title": "The One Who Returned", "subtitle": "Mercy received with gratitude", "text": "Luke 17:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus warned his disciples that occasions for sin would come and gave a severe warning to anyone who caused one of the little ones to stumble. He told them to rebuke a brother who sinned and forgive him when he repented, even repeatedly. When the apostles asked for more faith, Jesus gave the picture of faith like a mustard seed. He also spoke of a servant who carried out his duties without treating them as a claim to special thanks.", "On the way to Jerusalem, Jesus passed along the region of Samaria and Galilee. As he entered a village, ten men with leprosy stood at a distance and called to him as Master, asking for mercy. When he saw them, he told them to go and show themselves to the priests. They went, and as they went they were cleansed.", "One of them saw that he had been healed, turned back and glorified God loudly. He fell at Jesus' feet and thanked him. Luke identifies him as a Samaritan. All ten had obeyed the instruction to go. This one returned to acknowledge what he had received.", "Jesus asked where the other nine were and why only this foreigner had come back to give glory to God. He told the man to rise and go, and spoke of the faith by which he had been made well. Luke brings the man back within speaking distance of the person to whom he had first called from far off."], "lesson": "All ten act on Jesus' instruction. One also turns back to acknowledge the mercy received. Gratitude gives us a way to remember who helped us.", "question": "What does the Samaritan do that brings him back to Jesus after the healing?"},
  {"id": 91147, "title": "The Kingdom and the Days of Noah", "subtitle": "Readiness in ordinary life", "text": "Luke 17:20–37", "chapters": [[41, 17]], "start": 20, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Pharisees asked Jesus when God's kingdom would come. He said it did not come in a way that allowed people to point to it here or there. The kingdom was in their midst. Jesus then spoke to his disciples about a time when they would long to see one of the days of the Son of Man.", "He warned them not to follow people who said he was here or there. The day of the Son of Man would be like lightning shining across the sky. First he must suffer and be rejected by that generation. Jesus then turned to the days of Noah, when people were eating, drinking and marrying until Noah entered the ark and the flood came.", "He recalled the days of Lot too. People ate, drank, bought, sold, planted and built. On the day Lot left Sodom, judgement came suddenly. Jesus said the revealing of the Son of Man would come in that way. He told his listeners to remember Lot's wife, and spoke of someone on a roof or in a field needing to leave without turning back for possessions.", "He spoke of people together when one was taken and another left. The disciples asked where, and Jesus replied with an image of birds gathering. The teaching returns the hearer to ordinary places and tasks and asks what readiness would mean in the midst of them."], "lesson": "Daily routines can occupy the whole field of attention. Jesus gives ordinary life a wider horizon by calling people to remember where their deepest loyalties lie.", "question": "Which everyday activities does Jesus name, and what do they reveal about the suddenness of what followed?"},
  {"id": 91148, "title": "The Widow Who Kept Coming", "subtitle": "Prayer without losing heart", "text": "Luke 18:1–8", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Luke tells why Jesus gave this parable before he tells the story. His hearers should pray and should not lose heart. The person he puts at the centre of it is a widow who has a case against an adversary. The judge in her town fears neither God nor people. She keeps coming to him, asking him to give her justice.", "For a time he will not act. Then he considers the matter privately. He acknowledges that he still does not fear God or respect people. What changes his decision is the widow's repeated visits. He decides to give her justice because she keeps troubling him. The widow wins a hearing from someone who gives her little reason to expect it.", "Jesus asks his hearers to take notice of what that judge said. Then he turns from the judge's motives to God's care for his chosen people who cry to him day and night. He asks whether God will fail to give them justice and assures them that he will act. The parable gives people facing a long wait reason to keep bringing their case to him.", "Jesus ends with a question about whether the Son of Man will find faith on earth when he comes. Luke has already told the reader what kind of conduct the story is meant to encourage. The question puts that conduct before the people who must keep praying during the interval."], "lesson": "Jesus introduces the story as instruction to pray and persevere. The widow's persistence gives shape to a kind of faith that continues through delay.", "question": "What does the judge admit about his motives, and how does Jesus contrast his response with God's care?"},
  {"id": 91149, "title": "Two Prayers and the Children", "subtitle": "Coming to God without a claim to rank", "text": "Luke 18:9–17", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 9, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told a parable to people who trusted in their own righteousness and looked down on others. Two men went up to the temple to pray. One was a Pharisee and the other a tax collector. The Pharisee stood and thanked God that he was unlike people he regarded as unjust, including the tax collector. He spoke of fasting twice a week and giving tithes from what he received.", "The tax collector stood far off. He would not lift his eyes to heaven. He beat his breast and asked God for mercy as a sinner. His short prayer placed his own condition before God. Jesus said this man went down to his house justified, and spoke of the proud being humbled and the humble being exalted.", "Then people brought even their infants for Jesus to touch. When the disciples saw, they rebuked the people bringing them. Jesus called the children to him and told the disciples to let them come. God's kingdom belonged to such as these. He said that a person must receive it like a child in order to enter it.", "The two scenes give the reader different places to see what it means to come before God. In the temple one man has a record to speak about and another asks for mercy. The children come in the arms of others and are welcomed by Jesus."], "lesson": "The tax collector brings a need before God. Jesus' welcome of the children gives dependence a visible form and challenges those who think they must establish their standing first.", "question": "What is each man relying on as he prays?"},
  {"id": 91150, "title": "Two Men Asking Jesus a Question", "subtitle": "Seeing what stands in the way", "text": "Luke 18:18–43", "chapters": [[41, 18]], "start": 18, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A ruler asked Jesus what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus spoke of God's goodness and named commandments concerning adultery, murder, stealing, false testimony and honouring parents. The ruler said he had kept these from his youth. Jesus told him he still lacked one thing. He should sell what he had, distribute it to the poor and follow him. The man became very sorrowful because he was very rich.", "Jesus spoke about the difficulty wealth could bring in entering God's kingdom, using the image of a camel and a needle's eye. When people asked who could be saved, he said what was impossible with people was possible with God. Peter spoke of what the disciples had left, and Jesus answered with a promise about the life of those who leave things for God's kingdom.", "Jesus took the Twelve aside and told them what awaited him in Jerusalem. The prophets' words would be fulfilled. He would be mistreated and killed and would rise. Luke says the disciples did not understand what he was telling them.", "Near Jericho a blind man heard a crowd passing and asked what it meant. Told it was Jesus, he cried out to the Son of David for mercy. People tried to hush him, but he cried louder. Jesus stopped and asked what he wanted. The man asked to see. He received his sight and followed Jesus, glorifying God. The people who saw it praised God as well."], "lesson": "The ruler becomes very sad after hearing what following would cost. The healed man follows and praises God. Their responses prompt reflection on what we do after Jesus answers.", "question": "How does the blind man's response differ from that of the ruler?"},
  {"id": 91151, "title": "Jesus Stops Under Zacchaeus's Tree", "subtitle": "Repentance that makes restitution", "text": "Luke 19:1–10", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was passing through Jericho. Zacchaeus, a wealthy chief tax collector, wanted to see who he was, but the crowd stood in his way and he was short. He ran ahead and climbed a sycamore tree along the road Jesus would take. From there he could look down as the people came past.", "When Jesus reached the place, he looked up and called Zacchaeus by name. He told him to come down quickly because he must stay at his house that day. Zacchaeus came down at once and received him with joy. The people who saw it began to complain. In their eyes Jesus had gone to be the guest of a sinner.", "Zacchaeus stood and spoke to the Lord about his possessions and his dealings with people. He said he would give half his goods to the poor and restore fourfold if he had taken anything by false accusation. Luke records the amounts. Zacchaeus's words concern things that could be returned, shared and counted. The people who had complained could hear that his response involved the way he used his wealth and the debts of justice that wrongdoing leaves behind.", "Jesus announced that salvation had come to that house and called Zacchaeus a son of Abraham. He then explained the purpose behind the visit: the Son of Man had come to seek and save the lost. Zacchaeus had climbed to get a look at Jesus. By the end of the encounter it was Jesus who had sought him out, spoken his name and entered his house."], "lesson": "Jesus' welcome comes before the public promise of restitution. Zacchaeus then names ways to repair wrongdoing that the people affected can actually experience.", "question": "What does Zacchaeus say he will give to the poor and restore to those he wronged?"},
  {"id": 91152, "title": "The Nobleman and the Money Entrusted", "subtitle": "Using what you have while the king is away", "text": "Luke 19:11–27", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 11, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus was near Jerusalem, and people who heard him supposed God's kingdom would appear immediately. He told them about a nobleman who travelled to a distant country to receive a kingdom and return. Before leaving, he called ten servants, gave them money and told them to put it to work until he came back. Some of his citizens hated him and sent word that they did not want him to reign over them.", "When he returned as king, he called the servants to learn what each had gained. One said the sum entrusted to him had earned ten more. The king commended him for being faithful in a small matter and gave him authority over ten cities. Another reported a gain of five and received authority over five cities.", "Another servant brought back the money he had wrapped in a cloth. He said he had been afraid because he regarded the nobleman as a severe man. The king asked why the servant's own account of him had not at least led him to put the money in a bank so that it could earn interest.", "He ordered the money taken from that servant and given to the one who had ten. He also ordered judgement on the citizens who had rejected his rule. The parable made room for an interval before the king's return, during which his servants already had something to do and his opponents had made their position clear."], "lesson": "Jesus places responsibility inside the waiting period. The servants' work takes place before they can see the nobleman return and settle accounts.", "question": "What does the master ask the servants to do with the time and resources they have?"},
  {"id": 91153, "title": "Praise on the Road and Tears for the City", "subtitle": "Recognising the time of visitation", "text": "Luke 19:28–48", "chapters": [[41, 19]], "start": 28, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus went on towards Jerusalem. Near Bethphage and Bethany by the Mount of Olives, he sent two disciples to fetch a colt no one had ridden. When its owners asked why they were untying it, they said the Lord needed it. They brought it to Jesus, put their garments on it and set him on it. As he rode, people spread their clothes on the road.", "As he came down the Mount of Olives, the multitude of disciples began praising God for the mighty works they had seen. They blessed the king coming in the Lord's name and spoke of peace in heaven. Some Pharisees asked Jesus to rebuke his disciples. He answered that if they kept quiet, the stones would cry out.", "When the city came into view, Jesus wept over it. He spoke of the things that would have brought it peace and said they were now hidden from its eyes. He described enemies surrounding it and the ruin ahead because it had failed to recognise the time of its visitation.", "Jesus went into the temple and drove out those trading there. He recalled its purpose as a house of prayer and said it had been made a den of thieves. He taught there daily. The leaders looked for a way to destroy him, but could find none for the time being, because the people listened attentively to him."], "lesson": "The praise of the disciples and Jesus' grief belong to the same arrival. His tears reveal how deeply he cares about a city that fails to receive what he brings.", "question": "What does Jesus see about Jerusalem that the rejoicing crowd may have missed?"},
  {"id": 91154, "title": "Who Gave You Authority?", "subtitle": "Giving God what belongs to God", "text": "Luke 20:1–26", "chapters": [[41, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While Jesus taught and proclaimed good news in the temple, chief priests and others asked what authority he had. He asked them in return whether John's baptism came from heaven or from people. They weighed the consequences of answering either way and declared they did not know. Jesus then declined to tell them by what authority he acted.", "He told the people about a man who planted a vineyard, leased it to tenants and went away. The tenants beat or wounded the servants he sent for fruit and sent them away empty. At last the owner sent his beloved son. The tenants recognised him as the heir, threw him out and killed him. Jesus spoke of the owner's coming judgement and asked about the stone rejected by builders that became the cornerstone. The leaders knew the parable concerned them.", "They watched him and sent spies pretending to be sincere, hoping to bring his words to the governor. The spies praised his teaching and asked whether it was lawful to pay tribute to Caesar. Jesus saw what they intended and asked to see a coin. He asked whose image and inscription it carried.", "When they said it was Caesar's, he told them to give Caesar what belonged to Caesar and God what belonged to God. They could not catch him in his words before the people. They marvelled at his answer and kept quiet."], "lesson": "The tenants treat an entrusted vineyard as if it were theirs to seize. The question about the coin gives the hearer another opportunity to consider what belongs to God.", "question": "What do the tenants refuse to acknowledge when the owner sends for fruit?"},
  {"id": 91155, "title": "The Living God and Honour in Public", "subtitle": "Reading Scripture with humility", "text": "Luke 20:27–47", "chapters": [[41, 20]], "start": 27, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Sadducees, who said there was no resurrection, came to Jesus with a question. They quoted the law about a man marrying his brother's widow if the brother died childless. Then they imagined seven brothers who married one woman in succession and each died without children. When the woman died too, whose wife would she be in the resurrection?", "Jesus said that people in this age marry, but those counted worthy of the resurrection do not live under the same conditions. They cannot die any more. He appealed to what Moses wrote about the bush, where the Lord called himself the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He spoke of God as the God of the living, for all live to him. Some scribes approved of his answer, and people no longer dared question him.", "Jesus then asked how people could say Christ was David's son when David himself, in the Psalms, called him Lord. He spoke of the place at God's right hand until the enemies were made a footstool. The people who had brought a question about reading Moses now heard a question about reading David.", "With the people listening, Jesus warned his disciples about scribes who liked long robes, greetings and seats of honour. He also said they devoured widows' houses and made long prayers for appearance. Their conduct towards vulnerable people mattered more than the respect the crowds were accustomed to giving them."], "lesson": "Jesus gives Scripture careful attention and condemns the use of a religious appearance to hide harm. Humility in interpretation should be joined to honesty in conduct.", "question": "What activity did Jesus warn could be used to cover harm to widows?"},
  {"id": 91156, "title": "Two Small Coins and Great Stones", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when appearances change", "text": "Luke 21:1–19", "chapters": [[41, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus looked up and saw rich people putting gifts into the temple treasury. He also saw a poor widow put in two small coins. He told his hearers she had put in more than all the others. They had given from what they had to spare. She had given all she had to live on.", "Some spoke admiringly of the temple's fine stones and gifts. Jesus told them the days would come when the stones would be thrown down. They asked when it would happen and what sign would come first. He warned against being deceived by people claiming his name or saying the time had arrived. Wars and disturbances should not make them panic.", "Jesus spoke of conflicts between nations, earthquakes, famines and fearful signs. He also told the disciples that, before these things, they would be seized and brought before authorities. That would become an occasion for testimony. He urged them not to decide beforehand how to defend themselves, promising words and wisdom their opponents could not withstand.", "They might be betrayed by relatives or friends and hated on account of his name. Jesus gave them reason to endure. The discussion of the temple's future gives the disciples a way to think about their own conduct if the structures and protections around them begin to fail."], "lesson": "The widow's small gift receives Jesus' notice beside a building that appears permanent. His teaching gives lasting weight to faithfulness in moments others may discount.", "question": "What does Jesus see in the widow's gift that a person admiring the temple might overlook?"},
  {"id": 91157, "title": "Jerusalem Surrounded and the Call to Watch", "subtitle": "Prayer when hearts grow heavy", "text": "Luke 21:20–38", "chapters": [[41, 21]], "start": 20, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Jesus told his hearers that when they saw Jerusalem surrounded by armies, they would know its desolation was near. People in Judea should flee to the mountains, and people inside the city should leave. He spoke of days of distress in which some would die and others be taken captive. Jerusalem would be trampled until the times of the Gentiles were fulfilled.", "He spoke next of signs in the sun, moon and stars, of distress among nations and of people fainting from fear. The Son of Man would be seen coming in a cloud with power and great glory. Jesus told his disciples to lift their heads when these things began because their redemption was drawing near.", "He pointed them to fig trees and other trees. New growth was enough to tell people that summer was near. In the same way, they were to recognise when God's kingdom was near. Jesus said that heaven and earth would pass away, but his words would remain.", "Then he directed his hearers to attend to their own hearts. Excess, drunkenness and the cares of life could weigh them down and leave them unready. They should watch and pray. Luke ends with Jesus teaching in the temple by day and spending the nights at the Mount of Olives. The people came early in the morning to hear him."], "lesson": "The trouble Jesus describes is public, but he identifies a danger within the hearer as well. Prayer and alertness keep cares from occupying the heart entirely.", "question": "Which things does Jesus say can weigh down a person's heart?"},
  {"id": 91158, "title": "The Meal Prepared in Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Receiving and remembering Jesus' gift", "text": "Luke 22:1–23", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Passover drew near. The chief priests and scribes looked for a way to kill Jesus, though they feared the people. Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, went to consult them and the officers about handing him over. They agreed to give him money, and he looked for a chance to betray him away from the crowd.", "When the day came for preparing the Passover, Jesus sent Peter and John. They asked where they should prepare it. He told them to enter the city and follow a man carrying a water jar. At the house he entered, they were to ask for the guest room. They found the furnished upper room as Jesus had said and prepared the meal.", "Jesus sat down with the apostles and told them he had earnestly desired to eat this Passover with them before suffering. He spoke of the kingdom to come. He took bread, gave thanks and broke it, and gave it to them as his body given for them, calling them to remember him. After supper he spoke of the cup as the new covenant in his blood poured out for them.", "Then he said the hand of the one betraying him was with him at the table. The Son of Man would go as determined, but he pronounced woe on the one who handed him over. The apostles began asking among themselves who could be about to do such a thing."], "lesson": "Luke places a meal of remembrance in a setting where betrayal is already under way. Jesus speaks of giving himself while those around him are questioning what one of them might do.", "question": "What instructions did Peter and John follow before the meal where Jesus spoke of remembering him?"},
  {"id": 91159, "title": "A Servant at the Table and Prayer on the Mount", "subtitle": "Praying through weakness", "text": "Luke 22:24–46", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 24, "end": 46, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["A dispute arose among the apostles about who was greatest. Jesus described kings who exercised authority and were called benefactors. Among his followers, the one who led should be like one who served. He asked them to consider what they saw at a table and spoke of himself being among them as one who served. He also told them they had remained with him in his trials.", "Then he spoke directly to Simon. He said Satan had asked to sift them like wheat and that he had prayed for Simon's faith. When Simon turned back, he was to strengthen his brothers. Peter said he was ready to go to prison and death with him. Jesus told him he would deny knowing him three times before the rooster crowed. He then spoke to the disciples about conditions changing and told them that Scripture concerning him would be fulfilled.", "They went to the Mount of Olives as usual. Jesus told them to pray against temptation and withdrew a short distance. On his knees he asked his Father to take away the cup if he was willing, and yielded to the Father's will. Luke describes an angel strengthening him as he prayed in anguish.", "Jesus found the disciples sleeping from sorrow. He asked why they slept and told them to rise and pray. The warning he had given on arrival was the last thing he said to them there before the arrest began."], "lesson": "Jesus sees the weakness behind Peter's confident words. He also looks ahead to Peter's return and gives him the work of strengthening others.", "question": "What does Jesus ask Peter to do after he has turned back?"},
  {"id": 91160, "title": "A Healed Ear and a Remembered Word", "subtitle": "Mercy and truth at the point of failure", "text": "Luke 22:47–71", "chapters": [[41, 22]], "start": 47, "end": 71, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While Jesus was still speaking to his disciples, a crowd approached, led by Judas, one of the Twelve. Judas came near to kiss him. Jesus asked whether he was betraying the Son of Man with a kiss. The people around Jesus asked if they should strike with the sword. One struck the high priest's servant and cut off his right ear. Jesus stopped them, touched the man's ear and healed him.", "Jesus addressed the chief priests, officers and elders who had come. Why had they come with weapons as if against a robber, when they had seen him daily in the temple? He spoke of their hour and the power of darkness. They took him to the high priest's house. Peter followed at a distance and sat near a fire in the courtyard.", "A servant girl recognised Peter. He denied knowing Jesus. Two others challenged him in turn, and he denied it again. While he was still speaking the third time, the rooster crowed. Jesus turned and looked at him. Peter remembered what Jesus had said and went outside and wept bitterly.", "The men holding Jesus mocked and struck him. At daybreak the council gathered and questioned him about being the Christ. Jesus spoke of the Son of Man sitting at God's right hand. They asked whether he was God's Son, and he answered. The council said it needed no further testimony."], "lesson": "Jesus heals someone on the side arresting him. Peter receives a look that brings the truth of Jesus' words home to him.", "question": "Which moments in this passage show Jesus giving attention to people around him while he himself is under arrest?"},
  {"id": 91161, "title": "Between Pilate and Herod", "subtitle": "Responsibility for an unjust decision", "text": "Luke 23:1–25", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["The council brought Jesus to Pilate. They accused him of misleading the nation, forbidding tribute to Caesar and claiming to be Christ a king. Pilate asked if he was King of the Jews. Jesus answered him, and Pilate told the leaders and crowd he found no fault in him. They pressed the charge and said his teaching had stirred people from Galilee to Jerusalem.", "When Pilate heard he was a Galilean, he sent him to Herod, who was also in Jerusalem. Herod was glad to see Jesus because he had heard of him and hoped to see a miracle. He questioned him at length, but Jesus gave him no answer. The leaders accused him fiercely. Herod and his soldiers mocked him, dressed him in a splendid robe and sent him back. Luke notes that Pilate and Herod became friends that day.", "Pilate called the leaders and people together. He said that neither he nor Herod had found Jesus guilty of anything deserving death. He proposed punishing and releasing him. The crowd called for Barabbas instead, a man imprisoned for insurrection and murder.", "Pilate addressed them again, wanting to release Jesus. They kept demanding crucifixion. A third time he asked what evil Jesus had done. Their voices prevailed. Pilate released the prisoner they requested and delivered Jesus to their will."], "lesson": "Pilate's own stated finding makes his eventual decision harder to excuse. The sequence asks how far a person will carry a truthful judgement when the people around him insist otherwise.", "question": "What does Pilate say he found, and what does he finally do?"},
  {"id": 91162, "title": "The Road to the Cross and the Man Beside Jesus", "subtitle": "A plea for mercy at the last hour", "text": "Luke 23:26–43", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 26, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["As Jesus was led away, those taking him seized Simon of Cyrene, who was coming in from the country, and put the cross on him to carry behind Jesus. A large crowd followed, including women who wept for him. Jesus turned and spoke to them as daughters of Jerusalem. He told them to weep for themselves and their children, and spoke of hard days ahead.", "Two criminals were led out with him. At the place called Calvary they crucified Jesus with one on either side. Jesus prayed to the Father for forgiveness for the people doing this. Those there divided his clothes by lot. Rulers mocked him for saving others and challenged him to save himself. Soldiers also mocked him. A notice above him named him King of the Jews.", "One criminal joined the taunts and demanded that Jesus save himself and them. The other rebuked him. He acknowledged that the two of them were receiving what their actions deserved and said Jesus had done nothing wrong. Then he asked Jesus to remember him when he came into his kingdom.", "Jesus answered that the man would be with him in paradise that day. Luke leaves both men's voices in the account. They were in the same condition beside Jesus and addressed him differently, and the second received an answer from the one he had asked to remember him."], "lesson": "The second man brings no record of service to commend himself. He acknowledges the truth about his condition and addresses Jesus with a plain request.", "question": "How does the second criminal describe himself and Jesus before making his request?"},
  {"id": 91163, "title": "The Last Prayer and the Borrowed Tomb", "subtitle": "Faithful care in grief", "text": "Luke 23:44–56", "chapters": [[41, 23]], "start": 44, "end": 56, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["About noon darkness came over the land and lasted until the middle of the afternoon. The temple curtain tore. Jesus cried aloud to the Father and committed his spirit into his hands. Then he died. A centurion watching what happened glorified God and called Jesus righteous. People who had gathered to watch went home beating their breasts.", "Those who knew Jesus and the women who had followed from Galilee stood at a distance and saw these things. Luke then introduces Joseph of Arimathea, a good and just man who belonged to the council but had not agreed with its decision or action. He was waiting for God's kingdom.", "Joseph went to Pilate and asked for Jesus' body. He took it down, wrapped it in linen and laid it in a tomb cut from rock where no one had yet been laid. It was the day of preparation, and the Sabbath was drawing near. The burial had to take place within the time available.", "The women who had come with Jesus from Galilee followed and saw the tomb and how his body was laid. They returned and prepared spices and ointments. Then they rested on the Sabbath according to the commandment. The passage ends with the place of burial known to the women and with their preparations waiting for the next day on which they could come."], "lesson": "Joseph uses what opportunity remains to care for Jesus' body. The women attend to the burial carefully enough to return to the place.", "question": "What does Luke tell you about Joseph's earlier decision and his action after Jesus dies?"},
  {"id": 91164, "title": "The Women Remember His Words", "subtitle": "Hope remembered amid confusion", "text": "Luke 24:1–12", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Early on the first day of the week, the women brought the spices they had prepared to the tomb. They found the stone rolled away. When they went in, they did not find the body of the Lord Jesus and did not know what to make of it. Two men in shining garments stood beside them. The women were afraid and bowed their faces to the ground.", "The men asked why they were looking for the living among the dead. Jesus had risen. They reminded the women what he had told them in Galilee: the Son of Man would be handed over, crucified and rise on the third day. The women remembered his words. They had come to a place they knew and now had to understand it in light of something they had heard before.", "They returned and told the eleven and the rest what had happened. Luke names Mary Magdalene, Joanna and Mary the mother of James, with the other women who brought the news. Their words seemed like nonsense to the apostles, who did not believe them.", "Peter got up and ran to the tomb. He stooped to look and saw the linen cloths by themselves. Then he went away wondering at what had happened. The women's report had been dismissed, yet Peter's visit left him with something he had now seen for himself."], "lesson": "The messengers draw the women's attention to words they have already heard. Those words allow them to understand the empty place before others believe their report.", "question": "Which part of Jesus' earlier teaching do the women remember at the tomb?"},
  {"id": 91165, "title": "A Stranger on the Road to Emmaus", "subtitle": "Reading disappointment in the light of Scripture", "text": "Luke 24:13–35", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 13, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["Two disciples were walking from Jerusalem to Emmaus that day, talking over the things that had happened. Jesus drew near and went with them, but they were kept from recognising him. He asked what they were discussing. Cleopas wondered whether the stranger was the only visitor to Jerusalem who had not heard. When Jesus asked what things, they told him about his own ministry, his condemnation and his death. They had hoped he would redeem Israel. They had also heard the women's report about the empty tomb and the angels, but their companions had not seen him.", "Jesus answered by taking them through Moses and the prophets. He showed them how the Christ's suffering and entrance into glory belonged together. They were nearing the village and he appeared ready to go farther. They urged him to stay because evening was coming. He entered and sat down with them.", "At the table he took bread, blessed and broke it, and gave it to them. Their eyes were opened and they recognised him, and he vanished from their sight. They remembered how their hearts had burned while he spoke on the road and opened the Scriptures to them.", "They got up that same hour and went back to Jerusalem. There they found the eleven and their companions gathered and heard that the Lord had risen and appeared to Simon. The two added what had happened on their road and how they had recognised Jesus in the breaking of the bread."], "lesson": "Jesus listens to their account of disappointment, then teaches them to read events through Scripture. Their return to Jerusalem shows what changes when the story they thought had ended opens again.", "question": "What is different in the two disciples' understanding by the time they return to Jerusalem?"},
  {"id": 91166, "title": "An Opened Understanding and the Parting Blessing", "subtitle": "Witness grounded in what Jesus has done", "text": "Luke 24:36–53", "chapters": [[41, 24]], "start": 36, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "Luke", "story": ["While the disciples were talking about what had happened, Jesus stood among them and spoke peace. They were frightened and thought they were seeing a spirit. He asked why doubts had arisen and invited them to see his hands and feet and touch him. While they still marvelled in their joy, he asked whether they had food. They gave him a piece of broiled fish, and he ate before them.", "Then he took their attention back to the words he had spoken while he was with them. The things written about him in the law of Moses, the prophets and the psalms had to be fulfilled. He opened their understanding to the Scriptures and spoke of the Christ suffering and rising on the third day.", "He told them that repentance and forgiveness of sins would be proclaimed in his name to all nations, beginning at Jerusalem. They were witnesses to these things. He spoke of sending what his Father had promised, and told them to remain in the city until they received power from on high. They had a message and a place to begin, and still had to wait.", "He led them out towards Bethany, lifted his hands and blessed them. While blessing them, he parted from them and was carried up into heaven. They worshipped him and returned to Jerusalem with great joy, spending their time in the temple praising and blessing God."], "lesson": "Jesus grounds their witness in what they have seen and in what Scripture explains. He also calls them to wait for what the Father has promised before beginning their work.", "question": "What does Jesus tell the disciples to proclaim, and what does he tell them to wait for?"},
  {"id": 91167, "title": "The Word Comes to Dwell Among Us", "subtitle": "Receiving the One who makes the Father known", "text": "John 1:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["John opens before the first scene on earth. In the beginning the Word already was. The Word was with God and was God. All things came into being through him. John speaks of life in the Word and of that life as the light of men. The light shines in darkness. The conflict between light and darkness is already present before John names the people who will see it.", "A man named John was sent by God to bear witness to the light so that people might believe through him. He himself was a witness. The true light was coming into the world. The world was made through him, yet it failed to recognise him. He came to his own, and his own did not receive him. To those who did receive him and believe in his name, he gave the right to become children of God.", "The Word became flesh and dwelt among us. John speaks as one who has seen his glory, full of grace and truth. The witness John cries out that the one coming after him ranks before him because he was before him.", "From his fullness people have received grace. John names Moses and the law, then names Jesus Christ in connection with grace and truth. He ends the opening by speaking of the Son making the unseen God known. The person about whom witnesses will speak is already the one through whom the world came into being."], "lesson": "The opening puts God's initiative before any human response. Receiving Jesus begins with recognising who has come and what he makes known.", "question": "What does the opening tell you about the Word before it describes his coming into the world?"},
  {"id": 91168, "title": "A Witness at the Jordan", "subtitle": "Directing attention to Jesus", "text": "John 1:19–34", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 19, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Priests and Levites came from Jerusalem to ask John who he was. He answered plainly that he was not the Christ. They asked whether he was Elijah or the prophet they expected, and he answered no. They needed something to report to those who had sent them. John described himself in Isaiah's words as a voice in the wilderness calling for the Lord's way to be made straight.", "Some sent from the Pharisees asked why he was baptising if he made none of those claims. John said he baptised with water. Among them stood someone they did not know, someone coming after him whose sandal he was unworthy to untie. John gave them an answer about his work by pointing to the person who gave it its purpose.", "The next day John saw Jesus coming towards him and called him the Lamb of God who takes away the sin of the world. He identified Jesus as the one about whom he had spoken. His baptism with water had served to make this person known to Israel.", "John testified that he saw the Spirit descend like a dove and remain on Jesus. The one who sent him to baptise had told him what to look for: the man on whom the Spirit remained would baptise with the Holy Spirit. John said he had seen and bore witness that this was the Son of God."], "lesson": "John speaks plainly about the limits of his own role. His testimony gives the people a person to look towards beyond the man baptising them.", "question": "What does John say about Jesus that explains why his own work points beyond himself?"},
  {"id": 91169, "title": "Come and See", "subtitle": "The first steps of discipleship", "text": "John 1:35–51", "chapters": [[42, 1]], "start": 35, "end": 51, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["John was standing with two disciples when he saw Jesus walking by. He called him the Lamb of God. The two heard him and followed Jesus. Jesus turned, saw them coming and asked what they were seeking. They called him Teacher and asked where he was staying. He invited them to come and see. They went with him and stayed that day.", "One of the two was Andrew, Simon Peter's brother. Andrew found Simon and told him they had found the Messiah. He brought his brother to Jesus, who looked at him and gave him the name Cephas, which John explains as Peter. The invitation to see where Jesus stayed had already reached a household beyond the original two.", "The next day Jesus went towards Galilee. He found Philip and called him to follow. Philip found Nathanael and said they had found the person spoken of by Moses and the prophets, Jesus of Nazareth. Nathanael questioned whether anything good could come from Nazareth. Philip invited him to come and see.", "Jesus said he had seen Nathanael under the fig tree before Philip called him. Nathanael answered by calling him the Son of God and King of Israel. Jesus promised him greater things, speaking of heaven opened and angels ascending and descending upon the Son of Man. The chapter ends with the promise of more to see."], "lesson": "Philip answers a doubt with an invitation to meet Jesus. The first followers discover more by coming near, listening and bringing others.", "question": "How does Philip respond to Nathanael's objection about Nazareth?"},
  {"id": 91170, "title": "The Jars at the Wedding", "subtitle": "Obedience before the outcome is visible", "text": "John 2:1–12", "chapters": [[42, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["A wedding took place at Cana in Galilee. Jesus' mother was there, and Jesus and his disciples were invited. When the wine ran out, his mother told him about it. Jesus answered that his hour had not yet come. His mother turned to the servants and told them to do whatever he said.", "Six stone jars stood there for the Jewish practice of purification. They were large, and Jesus told the servants to fill them with water. They filled them to the brim. He then told them to draw some out and take it to the steward of the feast. They carried it to him as directed.", "The steward tasted the water that had become wine. He did not know where it came from, although the servants who had drawn the water knew. He called the bridegroom and remarked on what had happened from his point of view. Usually the good wine was served first. In this case the bridegroom had kept the good wine until now.", "John calls this the beginning of Jesus' signs. The steward could recognise what was in the cup without knowing how it came to be there. The servants knew what they had been asked to do. The disciples saw something more in what had happened: Jesus had shown his glory, and they believed in him. After the wedding Jesus went to Capernaum with his mother, brothers and disciples, and they stayed there a few days."], "lesson": "The servants fill the jars before knowing what the steward will taste. John directs the reader past the immediate relief at the wedding to the glory the disciples begin to see.", "question": "Who knew where the wine came from, and what does John say the disciples did in response?"},
  {"id": 91171, "title": "The Temple at Passover", "subtitle": "Worship and the sign Jesus gives", "text": "John 2:13–25", "chapters": [[42, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Passover was near, and Jesus went up to Jerusalem. In the temple he found people selling cattle, sheep and doves, and money changers seated at their tables. He made a whip of cords and drove them out, along with the sheep and cattle. He scattered the money and overturned the tables. He told the people selling doves to remove them and spoke of his Father's house, which they had made a place of trade.", "His disciples remembered the Scripture about zeal for God's house. The people who challenged him wanted to see a sign to show what authority he had to act. Jesus answered by speaking of destroying the temple and of his raising it in three days. His hearers thought he was speaking of the building and replied that it had taken forty-six years to build.", "John tells the reader that Jesus meant the temple of his body. Later, when Jesus had been raised, his disciples remembered what he had said. They believed both the Scripture and his words. The narrator has carried the reader forward to show how the answer was understood after the event.", "During the feast many believed in his name when they saw the signs he did. Jesus did not entrust himself to them, because he knew people and did not need anyone to tell him what was in them."], "lesson": "Understanding sometimes arrives when an earlier saying is read in light of later events. John tells us the disciples remembered both Scripture and Jesus' words.", "question": "Why did Jesus' hearers and his disciples understand the temple saying differently?"},
  {"id": 91172, "title": "Nicodemus Comes at Night", "subtitle": "New birth and coming into the light", "text": "John 3:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Nicodemus, a Pharisee and a ruler of the Jews, came to Jesus at night. He addressed him as a teacher from God because of the signs he had seen. Jesus went straight to the need to be born again to see God's kingdom. Nicodemus asked how a grown person could enter his mother's womb and be born a second time.", "Jesus spoke of being born of water and the Spirit. What is born of flesh is flesh, and what is born of the Spirit is spirit. He compared the Spirit's work to the wind. A person hears it without knowing where it comes from or where it goes. Nicodemus asked how these things could happen. Jesus questioned how a teacher of Israel could fail to understand and spoke of testimony that his hearers did not receive.", "The conversation then reached back to Moses lifting up the serpent in the wilderness. Jesus spoke of the Son of Man being lifted up so that believers might have eternal life. The passage goes on to speak of God's love for the world and the giving of his Son so that people might be saved through him.", "John's account ends this exchange with a contrast between loving darkness and coming to the light. Those who do truth come into the light so their deeds can be seen for what they are before God. The man who came with a judgement about Jesus' signs had been asked to consider a change that reached his whole life."], "lesson": "Nicodemus brings knowledge and recognised standing to the conversation. Jesus directs him to life the Spirit gives and to a response that allows deeds to come into the light.", "question": "What makes Nicodemus's first understanding of birth inadequate for what Jesus is saying?"},
  {"id": 91173, "title": "The Friend of the Bridegroom", "subtitle": "Joy when another takes the greater place", "text": "John 3:22–36", "chapters": [[42, 3]], "start": 22, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus and his disciples went into the Judean countryside, where they stayed and baptised. John was also baptising at Aenon near Salim because there was plenty of water there. People kept coming to him. John tells the reader this was before the Baptist was put in prison. For a time both ministries were drawing people.", "A dispute arose about purification. John's disciples came to him with particular news: the man John had testified about across the Jordan was baptising, and people were going to him. John replied that a person could receive only what had been given him from heaven. He reminded them that they themselves had heard him deny that he was the Christ and say that he had been sent ahead of him.", "John pictured a wedding. The bride belonged to the bridegroom. The bridegroom's friend stood nearby and rejoiced when he heard his voice. John described his own joy as complete. He said Jesus must increase and he himself must decrease. The news his disciples brought as a concern was news he could receive with joy.", "The passage goes on to speak of the one who comes from above and bears witness to what he has seen and heard. The Father loves the Son and has given all things into his hand. The conclusion places eternal life with believing in the Son and takes seriously the consequence of refusing him."], "lesson": "John's joy depends on the bridegroom being heard. His disciples' concern gives him a chance to explain success in terms of the work he was given.", "question": "What makes John rejoice in the very news that concerns his followers?"},
  {"id": 91174, "title": "A Conversation Beside Jacob's Well", "subtitle": "Living water and truthful worship", "text": "John 4:1–26", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus left Judea for Galilee and travelled through Samaria. Near Sychar he stopped at Jacob's well, tired from the journey. His disciples went into the town to buy food. A Samaritan woman arrived to draw water. Jesus asked her for a drink. She was surprised: a Jewish man was asking a Samaritan woman to give him water.", "Jesus told her that if she knew God's gift and who was asking her, she would ask him and he would give her living water. She looked at the deep well and asked how he would draw it without a vessel. She brought up Jacob, who had given them the well. Jesus answered that people who drank from this well would thirst again. The water he gave would become in a person a spring rising to eternal life. She asked him for it.", "Jesus told her to call her husband. When she said she had none, he acknowledged the truth of her answer and said she had had five husbands and the man she now had was not her husband. She recognised him as a prophet and asked about the different places where Samaritans and Jews worshipped.", "Jesus spoke of a time when worship of the Father would be bound neither to their mountain nor to Jerusalem. He said that the Father sought worshippers in spirit and in truth. The woman spoke of the Messiah who would come and explain these things. Jesus identified himself to her as the person she was waiting for. The conversation had begun with his request for a drink. He ended it by telling her who was speaking to her."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks about worship to someone whose life and community have made the meeting unusual. He brings truth about her circumstances and the offer of living water into the same conversation.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the question about the place of worship?"},
  {"id": 91175, "title": "The Woman Leaves Her Water Pot", "subtitle": "Witness and a harvest already waiting", "text": "John 4:27–42", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 27, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The disciples came back from buying food and were surprised to find Jesus speaking with the Samaritan woman. Although they wondered about it, none of them asked why he was talking with her. The woman left her water pot and went into the town. She told people to come and see a man who had told her what she had done, and asked whether he might be the Christ. They left the town and came towards him.", "In the meantime the disciples urged Jesus to eat. He said he had food they knew nothing about, and they wondered whether someone had brought him a meal. He explained that his food was doing the will of the one who sent him and finishing his work. Then he asked them to lift their eyes and look at fields ready for harvest. He spoke of people sowing and reaping and of work the disciples were entering after others had laboured.", "Many Samaritans believed because of the woman's testimony. When they reached Jesus, they asked him to stay. He stayed two days, and many more believed because of what he said himself.", "They told the woman that their conviction now rested on what they had heard for themselves. They spoke of Jesus as the Saviour of the world. The woman had gone from the well with a question and enough confidence to invite people to come. The town's hearing continued after the visit that her testimony had made possible."], "lesson": "The woman gives her neighbours a reason to come. Their own hearing deepens what began with her report.", "question": "What two stages does John describe in the townspeople's response?"},
  {"id": 91176, "title": "The Father Who Went Home on a Word", "subtitle": "Trust before confirmation arrives", "text": "John 4:43–54", "chapters": [[42, 4]], "start": 43, "end": 54, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After two days Jesus left Samaria for Galilee. The Galileans received him, having seen what he did at the feast in Jerusalem. He returned to Cana, where he had made the water wine. A royal official from Capernaum had a son who was ill. On hearing that Jesus had come into Galilee, he went and asked him to come down and heal his child, who was near death.", "Jesus spoke about people needing to see signs and wonders before they would believe. The father brought the request back to his son. He asked Jesus to come before the boy died. Jesus answered that the man should go because his son lived. The man believed what Jesus told him and set out without having Jesus accompany him to the house.", "While he was on the way down, his servants met him with the news that his son was alive. He asked when the boy had begun to recover. They told him that the fever left the previous day at the seventh hour. The father recognised that it was the same hour in which Jesus had said his son lived.", "The man believed, and so did his household. John calls this the second sign Jesus did after coming from Judea into Galilee. He lets the reader follow the father from his request for Jesus to come with him, to the journey made on Jesus' word, to the moment he could connect that word with news from home."], "lesson": "The father must decide how to act while he is still away from his son. The journey home gives his trust a practical form before he learns the hour of the healing.", "question": "At what point did the father begin to act on what Jesus told him?"},
  {"id": 91177, "title": "The Man Who Carried His Bed", "subtitle": "Receiving help after long waiting", "text": "John 5:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus went up to Jerusalem for a feast. Near the sheep gate was a pool with five covered porches, where many people with illnesses or disabilities lay. One man had been ill for thirty-eight years. Jesus saw him lying there and knew he had been in that condition a long time. He asked whether he wanted to be made well.", "The man explained the obstacle as he understood it. He had no one to put him in the pool when the water was stirred, and someone else arrived ahead of him. Jesus told him to get up, take up his bed and walk. The man was immediately made well, took his bed and walked. It was the Sabbath.", "The authorities told him it was unlawful to carry his bed that day. He explained that the one who healed him had told him to carry it. They asked who gave the instruction, but he did not know. Jesus had withdrawn into the crowd. Later Jesus found him in the temple and spoke to him about the conduct of his life now that he was well.", "The man told the authorities that it was Jesus who had healed him. They opposed Jesus because he did these things on the Sabbath. Jesus answered that his Father was working and that he was working too. They took this as a claim about his relation to God, and the dispute grew more serious."], "lesson": "A man who has described years of frustrated hope rises at Jesus' word. The dispute that follows shows how easily a healed person can become secondary to an argument.", "question": "What does the man say about his situation before Jesus tells him to rise?"},
  {"id": 91178, "title": "The Witnesses Jesus Names", "subtitle": "Reading Scripture with willingness to come", "text": "John 5:19–47", "chapters": [[42, 5]], "start": 19, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus answered the people questioning him by speaking about the Son's relation to the Father. The Son does what he sees the Father doing. The Father loves him and shows him his works. As the Father raises the dead and gives life, the Son gives life. The Father has also entrusted judgement to him so that people honour the Son as they honour the Father.", "Jesus spoke about the person who hears his word and believes the one who sent him. Such a person has passed from death to life. He spoke of the dead hearing his voice and of a future hour when those in the graves would come out. He said his judgement was just because he sought the will of the Father who sent him.", "Then Jesus set out the witnesses. John bore witness to the truth, and people had been willing for a time to rejoice in his light. The works the Father gave Jesus to do were a greater witness. He also spoke of the Father's own testimony and said his hearers did not have God's word abiding in them, because they did not believe the one he sent.", "Jesus directed them to the Scriptures, which testified about him. They searched them expecting to find life, yet refused to come to him. He asked how they could believe while looking for honour from one another. Finally he named Moses, in whom they hoped, and said Moses had written about him."], "lesson": "Searching Scripture calls for willingness to respond to what it reveals. Jesus connects the hearers' reception of him with the glory they seek from one another.", "question": "Which witnesses does Jesus name, and what response does he find missing?"},
  {"id": 91179, "title": "The Boy's Lunch and the Boat at Night", "subtitle": "Jesus knows the need before the disciples do", "text": "John 6:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus crossed the Sea of Galilee, and a large crowd followed because people had seen the signs he did for the sick. He went up a mountain and sat with his disciples. Passover was near. Seeing the crowd approach, he asked Philip where they could buy bread for them. John says Jesus knew what he would do and asked to test him.", "Philip reckoned that two hundred denarii would not buy enough for everyone to have even a little. Andrew pointed out a boy with five barley loaves and two small fish and asked what they could do with so little. Jesus told the people to sit down. He gave thanks and distributed the food until they had as much as they wanted. At his direction the disciples gathered twelve baskets of fragments.", "People who saw the sign spoke of Jesus as the prophet who was to come. When he realised they meant to take him and make him king, he withdrew alone to the mountain. The crowd had reached a conclusion about the power that had fed them, and Jesus would not let them turn it into that kind of rule.", "At evening the disciples set out towards Capernaum. It grew dark, the wind blew and the sea rose. They saw Jesus walking towards the boat and were afraid. He spoke to them, told them who he was and said not to fear. They received him, and the boat reached the land they were going to."], "lesson": "Philip's estimate and Andrew's small find are both honest. Jesus has a purpose beyond their calculations and declines the kingship the crowd tries to impose.", "question": "What reasons for fear or concern appear in the feeding and in the crossing?"},
  {"id": 91180, "title": "Bread That Gives Life", "subtitle": "Seeking Jesus beyond a full stomach", "text": "John 6:22–40", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 22, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The next day people on the far shore remembered seeing the disciples depart without Jesus. Boats had come from Tiberias near the place where they had eaten. When they could find neither Jesus nor the disciples, they crossed to Capernaum to look for him. They found him on the other side and asked when he had come there.", "Jesus told them why they were seeking him. They had eaten the loaves and been satisfied. He urged them to work for food that endures to eternal life, which the Son of Man would give. They asked what they should do to perform God's works. He directed them to believe in the one God sent.", "They asked for a sign and recalled their fathers eating manna in the wilderness. Jesus said the true bread from heaven came from his Father and gave life to the world. The people asked for that bread always. He then called himself the bread of life and spoke of the person who comes to him never hungering and the person who believes never thirsting.", "Jesus said he had come to do his Father's will. He would receive those who came and lose none of what the Father gave him. He spoke of believers having eternal life and being raised on the last day. The conversation had begun with people looking for another meal and had reached what Jesus was promising to give them."], "lesson": "The crowd has received real food and wants more. Jesus uses that need to speak about a deeper hunger and about the life he gives.", "question": "What reason does Jesus give for the crowd's search, and where does he direct it?"},
  {"id": 91181, "title": "When Many Turned Back", "subtitle": "Staying when Jesus' words are hard", "text": "John 6:41–71", "chapters": [[42, 6]], "start": 41, "end": 71, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Some of Jesus' hearers grumbled because he said he was bread that had come down from heaven. They knew him as the son of Joseph and knew his parents. How could he speak that way about where he came from? Jesus told them to stop murmuring. He spoke of the Father drawing people to him and of people hearing and learning from the Father.", "He returned to the manna their fathers ate and to the fact that those people died. He said the bread he would give for the life of the world was his flesh. This led to an argument. In the synagogue at Capernaum he went on speaking of eating his flesh and drinking his blood, of abiding in him and living through him.", "Many disciples called it a hard saying and asked who could hear it. Jesus asked whether it offended them and spoke of the Spirit giving life. He said his words were spirit and life and recognised that some did not believe. Many who had followed him went back and no longer walked with him.", "Jesus asked the Twelve if they also wanted to go away. Peter answered by asking to whom they would go. Jesus had the words of eternal life. Peter spoke of their faith in him, and Jesus answered with a warning concerning one of the Twelve. John names Judas as the one who would betray him."], "lesson": "Peter's reply is a confession about where he has found life. John leaves the departure of many and the future betrayal in view, so that remaining cannot be reduced to standing with the crowd.", "question": "What ground does Peter give for staying with Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91182, "title": "A Journey Made Quietly", "subtitle": "Judging with care", "text": "John 7:1–24", "chapters": [[42, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus stayed in Galilee because opponents in Judea sought to kill him. As the Feast of Tabernacles approached, his brothers urged him to go and show his works openly. They argued that a person seeking public notice would not act in secret. John says his brothers did not believe in him. Jesus answered that his time had not yet come and stayed behind when they left.", "After they had gone, he went up quietly. At the feast people looked for him and discussed him among themselves. Some called him good while others accused him of misleading the people. Fear kept them from speaking openly. About the middle of the feast, Jesus entered the temple and taught. People wondered how he knew what he taught without having studied as they expected.", "Jesus said his teaching came from the one who sent him. A person willing to do God's will would be in a position to consider where the teaching came from. He spoke about seeking the honour of the sender and then challenged people who claimed to have Moses' law while seeking to kill him.", "He brought them back to a work he had done on the Sabbath. They themselves circumcised on the Sabbath when the law required it. Why be angry with him for making a whole person well? He called them to judge fairly instead of stopping at appearances."], "lesson": "Jesus gives little weight to the demand to establish himself by publicity. His instruction about judgement asks listeners to consider the substance of the work they have seen.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the objection about making a man well on the Sabbath?"},
  {"id": 91183, "title": "The Offer to the Thirsty at the Feast", "subtitle": "Hearing before judging", "text": "John 7:25–53", "chapters": [[42, 7]], "start": 25, "end": 53, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Some people in Jerusalem asked whether Jesus was the man the leaders wanted to kill. They saw him speaking openly and wondered why no one stopped him. Others argued about where the Christ would come from. Jesus taught in the temple, saying he had been sent by one whom they did not know. People tried to seize him, but his hour had not come. Many believed and asked whether the Christ would do more signs than Jesus had done.", "The leaders heard what the crowd was saying and sent officers to take him. Jesus spoke of being with them a little longer and then going to the one who sent him. They wondered where he would go that they could not find him.", "On the last great day of the feast, Jesus called those who thirsted to come to him and drink. John explains his words about living water in connection with the Spirit whom believers would receive. The crowd again divided over who Jesus was and where he came from.", "The officers returned without him. Asked why, they said that no one had ever spoken as he did. The Pharisees dismissed the response of the crowd. Nicodemus asked whether their law judged a man before hearing him and learning what he had done. His question drew a rebuke in return, and the gathering broke up."], "lesson": "Nicodemus asks a modest but consequential question about fairness. The passage shows people reaching conclusions while others pause because they have listened.", "question": "What principle does Nicodemus appeal to when the leaders have already reached a judgement?"},
  {"id": 91184, "title": "The Accusers Leave One by One", "subtitle": "Mercy that calls for change", "text": "John 8:1–11", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["In the account printed in the King James Version, Jesus went to the Mount of Olives and returned early to the temple. People came to him, and he sat down to teach. Scribes and Pharisees brought a woman accused of adultery and placed her in the middle of the gathering. They said she had been caught in the act and put a question to Jesus about the penalty in Moses' law.", "John says they were testing him so that they might have something to accuse him of. Jesus stooped and wrote on the ground with his finger. The passage leaves the words he wrote unreported. As the questioners kept asking, Jesus straightened and told the one who was without sin to throw the first stone. Then he stooped and wrote again.", "Those who heard began leaving one by one, starting with the older ones. The woman remained in the middle, and Jesus asked where her accusers had gone and whether anyone had condemned her. She answered that no one had.", "Jesus told her that neither did he condemn her and told her to go and sin no more. The crowd had heard the question posed over her head. The last exchange was with the woman herself, and the instruction she received concerned how she would live after she left."], "lesson": "Jesus brings the accusers' own conduct into view and gives the woman a direct word about her future. The call to change is spoken after the threatening crowd has gone.", "question": "What happens to the accusers after Jesus answers, and what does he say once they have left?"},
  {"id": 91185, "title": "A Light and Two Witnesses", "subtitle": "Listening to the One sent by the Father", "text": "John 8:12–30", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus spoke again and called himself the light of the world. Whoever followed him would have the light of life. The Pharisees said his testimony could not stand because he gave it about himself. Jesus answered that he knew where he came from and where he was going. They were judging without knowing those things. He appealed to the testimony of the Father who sent him alongside his own.", "They asked where his Father was. He told them that if they knew him, they would know his Father too. John locates the exchange in the temple treasury and says no one seized Jesus because his hour had not yet come.", "Jesus spoke again about going where his hearers could not come. They misunderstood and asked what he meant. He spoke of their belonging to this world and of his coming from above. He warned about dying in their sins and put the question of believing who he was before them. When they asked who he was, he spoke of what he had been telling them and of the truth of the one who sent him.", "Jesus said that when the Son of Man was lifted up, they would know who he was and that he spoke what his Father taught him. John ends this part of the exchange by saying many believed as he spoke."], "lesson": "Jesus grounds his testimony in his relation to the Father. His hearers must attend to who is speaking before they can understand what it means to follow his light.", "question": "How does Jesus answer the objection that he is testifying about himself alone?"},
  {"id": 91186, "title": "The Claim to Be Abraham's Children", "subtitle": "Freedom that begins with truth", "text": "John 8:31–59", "chapters": [[42, 8]], "start": 31, "end": 59, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told people who had believed him that if they continued in his word, they were truly his disciples. They would know the truth, and the truth would make them free. They appealed to being descended from Abraham and objected to the suggestion that they needed freedom. Jesus spoke of a person who commits sin being its servant. He said that if the Son made them free, they would be free indeed.", "He acknowledged their descent from Abraham and then asked them to consider whether their conduct resembled Abraham's. They sought to kill someone who had told them the truth he heard from God. The discussion went deeper as they claimed God as their Father. Jesus asked why, if that were so, they did not love the one who had come from God.", "Their replies became hostile. They called him a Samaritan and accused him of having a demon. Jesus answered that he honoured his Father and spoke of someone keeping his word and never seeing death. They returned to Abraham and the prophets, who had died, and asked who Jesus made himself out to be.", "He said Abraham had rejoiced to see his day. When they objected that Jesus was not yet fifty, he spoke of himself existing before Abraham. They took up stones, and Jesus went out of the temple. The words about freedom had led to a confrontation over whether they could receive the person offering it."], "lesson": "Jesus puts conduct and willingness to hear beside claims of ancestry. Freedom in this conversation involves receiving a truth the hearers find uncomfortable.", "question": "Why do the hearers object when Jesus speaks to them about becoming free?"},
  {"id": 91187, "title": "A Man Washes at Siloam", "subtitle": "Seeing a person before assigning blame", "text": "John 9:1–23", "chapters": [[42, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["As Jesus passed by, he saw a man who had been blind from birth. The disciples asked whether the man or his parents had sinned for him to be born blind. Jesus turned their attention from assigning blame to the works of God that would be made visible in him. He spoke of doing the work of the one who sent him and called himself the light of the world.", "Jesus made clay, put it on the man's eyes and told him to wash in the pool of Siloam. John explains that the name means Sent. The man went, washed and came back seeing. Neighbours who remembered him begging disputed whether he was the same person. He said that he was.", "They asked how his eyes had been opened. He described what Jesus had done and said, but did not know where Jesus was. They brought him to the Pharisees. It was the Sabbath when Jesus made the clay. Some said a man who did that could not be from God. Others asked how a sinner could do such signs. The man called Jesus a prophet.", "The authorities called his parents to establish whether he had really been born blind. They confirmed that he was their son and had been born blind. Afraid of being put out of the synagogue, they said that he was old enough to answer for himself."], "lesson": "The disciples begin with a question about blame. Jesus addresses the man himself and gives him something to do. The parents' caution shows what public testimony could cost.", "question": "What does the healed man know from experience that the others are disputing?"},
  {"id": 91188, "title": "The Man Cast Out and Found Again", "subtitle": "Truthful witness under pressure", "text": "John 9:24–41", "chapters": [[42, 9]], "start": 24, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The authorities called the healed man again. They urged him to give glory to God and told him they knew Jesus was a sinner. He said he did not know whether Jesus was a sinner, but he did know that he had been blind and now could see. When they asked again how it happened, he said he had already told them. He asked whether they also wanted to become Jesus' disciples.", "They insulted him and said they were disciples of Moses. They knew God had spoken to Moses, they said, but did not know where Jesus came from. The man found it astonishing that they did not know this when Jesus had opened his eyes. He reasoned from what had happened and said that if Jesus were not from God he could do nothing. They answered that he was born in sins and threw him out.", "Jesus heard that he had been cast out and went to find him. He asked whether he believed in the Son of God. The man asked who that was so he might believe. Jesus told him that he had seen him and that he was the one speaking with him. The man believed and worshipped.", "Jesus spoke of judgement in which those who could not see would see. Some Pharisees asked whether they too were blind. He addressed the responsibility of those who claimed that they could see."], "lesson": "The man speaks from what he can honestly say, even when he is pressured to withdraw it. Jesus seeks him out after the leaders have rejected him.", "question": "What does Jesus do once he hears that the man has been cast out?"},
  {"id": 91189, "title": "The Shepherd Who Knows His Sheep", "subtitle": "Recognising a voice through care", "text": "John 10:1–21", "chapters": [[42, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus described a sheepfold and the difference between people who entered by its door and people who climbed in elsewhere. The shepherd entered through the door. The keeper opened for him, and the sheep heard his voice. He called his own sheep by name and led them out. They followed a voice they knew and would flee from a stranger's voice.", "His hearers did not understand the picture, so he spoke again. He called himself the door of the sheep and spoke of entering, being saved and finding pasture. He contrasted the thief who comes to steal and destroy with his own coming to give life abundantly.", "He then called himself the good shepherd, who gives his life for the sheep. A hired man sees a wolf coming and leaves, because the sheep are not his. Jesus spoke of knowing his sheep and being known by them, and of bringing other sheep so there would be one flock and one shepherd.", "Jesus said he laid down his life willingly and had authority to take it up again. His words divided the hearers. Some said he had a demon and was mad. Others objected that such words were unlike those of someone possessed. They asked whether a demon could open blind eyes. The healing from the previous chapter remained part of what the people had to consider."], "lesson": "The shepherd's authority is visible in his knowledge of the sheep and his willingness to bear danger for them. Jesus extends this care beyond the fold already in view.", "question": "What does the shepherd do when faced with the danger that makes the hireling leave?"},
  {"id": 91190, "title": "A Question on Solomon's Porch", "subtitle": "Trusting the Shepherd's hold", "text": "John 10:22–42", "chapters": [[42, 10]], "start": 22, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["It was winter at the feast of dedication in Jerusalem. Jesus was walking in the temple on Solomon's porch when people gathered around him. They wanted him to say plainly whether he was the Christ. Jesus answered that he had told them and that the works he did in his Father's name testified about him.", "He spoke again about sheep. His own heard his voice, he knew them and they followed him. He gave them eternal life and spoke of their safety in his hand and in the Father's hand. Then he said he and the Father were one. Some of those listening took up stones.", "Jesus asked which of the good works he had shown them from the Father made them want to stone him. They replied that the issue was blasphemy, because they heard him making himself God. He answered from Scripture and returned to the works. Even if they refused his words, he asked them to consider what the works showed about his relation to the Father. They tried to seize him, and he escaped.", "He went across the Jordan to the place where John had first baptised. People came to him there. John had done no sign, they said, yet what he said about Jesus had been true. Many believed. The passage ends where an earlier witness could still be remembered and compared with what they had now seen."], "lesson": "Jesus' promise of safety belongs to the relationship of hearing and following. Beyond Jordan people remember John's testimony and weigh it against what they now see.", "question": "What two actions does Jesus describe on the part of the sheep, and what does he promise them?"},
  {"id": 91191, "title": "The Message From Bethany", "subtitle": "Hope spoken to a grieving sister", "text": "John 11:1–27", "chapters": [[42, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Lazarus of Bethany, the brother of Mary and Martha, became ill. His sisters sent Jesus word that the man he loved was sick. Jesus spoke of God's glory in what would happen. John says Jesus loved Martha, her sister and Lazarus, and also tells us that after hearing the news he stayed two more days where he was.", "When Jesus said they should return to Judea, the disciples remembered that people there had tried to stone him. He spoke about walking in the light and then told them Lazarus was asleep and that he was going to wake him. They took this to mean ordinary sleep. Jesus told them plainly Lazarus had died. Thomas said to the others that they should go too, even if it meant dying with him.", "By the time Jesus arrived, Lazarus had been in the tomb four days. Many people had come to comfort the sisters. Martha heard that Jesus was coming and went to meet him. She told him that if he had been there her brother would not have died, and spoke of trusting what God would give him. Jesus told her her brother would rise.", "Martha understood that as a reference to the last day. Jesus spoke of himself as the resurrection and the life and asked whether she believed. She confessed that he was the Christ, the Son of God who was to come into the world."], "lesson": "Martha speaks her disappointment openly and also listens to Jesus' promise. Her confession is made before she sees anything change at the tomb.", "question": "What does Martha believe about Jesus before Lazarus comes out of the tomb?"},
  {"id": 91192, "title": "Jesus Weeps and Lazarus Comes Out", "subtitle": "Compassion and life at the tomb", "text": "John 11:28–57", "chapters": [[42, 11]], "start": 28, "end": 57, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Martha went to her sister Mary and told her privately that the Teacher was calling for her. Mary got up quickly. The people who had been comforting her thought she was going to the grave and followed. When she reached Jesus, she fell at his feet and told him what Martha had already said: if he had been there, her brother would not have died.", "Jesus saw Mary and the people with her weeping. He was deeply moved and asked where Lazarus had been laid. They invited him to come and see. Jesus wept. Some saw in his tears how much he loved Lazarus. Others wondered why the man who had opened blind eyes had allowed this man to die.", "At the tomb Jesus asked them to remove the stone. Martha objected because her brother had been dead four days. Jesus reminded her of what he had said about seeing God's glory. They took the stone away. He thanked the Father aloud so the people standing there might believe the Father had sent him. Then he called Lazarus by name. Lazarus came out with his hands and feet wrapped and his face covered. Jesus told the people to unbind him and let him go.", "Many who saw believed. Others reported what Jesus had done to the Pharisees. The chief priests and Pharisees convened a council and spoke of what Rome might do if people continued believing in him. From that day they planned his death. Jesus withdrew, and as Passover drew near people wondered whether he would come to the feast."], "lesson": "Jesus' tears have their place beside his authority to call Lazarus. The leaders' reaction shows that evidence can become threatening when people fear what they will lose.", "question": "What two very different responses follow the raising of Lazarus?"},
  {"id": 91193, "title": "Perfume at Supper and Branches on the Road", "subtitle": "Honouring Jesus before the crowds change", "text": "John 12:1–19", "chapters": [[42, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Six days before Passover, Jesus came back to Bethany. The last time John had taken us there, Lazarus had walked out of a tomb with his hands and feet still wrapped. Now he was reclining at supper with Jesus while Martha served. Mary took a pound of costly perfume made from pure nard, poured it on Jesus' feet and wiped them with her hair. Soon everyone in the house could smell what she had done.", "Judas Iscariot put a price on the gift. The perfume could have brought three hundred denarii, he said, and the money could have gone to the poor. John tells us what the others could not see: Judas kept the common purse and used to take from it. Jesus told him to leave Mary alone and spoke of the day of his burial. Lazarus was sitting there alive, and already Jesus was speaking of his own grave.", "News of the supper brought people out from Jerusalem. They wanted to see Jesus and the man he had raised. The chief priests began planning to kill Lazarus too, because people were going over to Jesus on account of him. The man sitting at supper had become a witness by the simple fact that he was alive.", "The next day people took palm branches and went to meet Jesus, acclaiming him as Israel's king. He rode in on a young donkey, as Scripture had said Zion's king would do. Only after Jesus was glorified did the disciples put that Scripture together with what they had seen. For now the crowd kept growing, and the Pharisees could only complain to one another that the whole world seemed to have gone after him."], "lesson": "Mary's gift receives meaning from the approaching burial. The crowds see a public welcome, while John also records what leaders are planning in response to Lazarus.", "question": "What different motives for coming to Bethany or reacting to Lazarus does John describe?"},
  {"id": 91194, "title": "The Grain That Falls Into the Ground", "subtitle": "Fruit through surrender", "text": "John 12:20–50", "chapters": [[42, 12]], "start": 20, "end": 50, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Among the people who had come to worship at the feast were some Greeks. They approached Philip, who was from Bethsaida, and asked to see Jesus. Philip told Andrew, and the two took the request to Jesus. He answered by saying that his hour had come. Then he spoke of something small enough to hold between two fingers. A grain of wheat remains a single grain until it falls into the earth and dies. Only then does it bear much fruit.", "Jesus put his servants into the same picture. They were to follow him, and the Father would honour whoever served him. Then Jesus spoke openly about his own distress. He had come for this hour and asked the Father to glorify his name. A voice answered from heaven. Some in the crowd heard thunder, and others thought an angel had spoken. Jesus said the voice had come for their sake. When he was lifted up, he would draw people to himself. John says this pointed to the kind of death he would die.", "The crowd wondered how the Son of Man could be lifted up if the Christ was to remain forever. Jesus urged them to walk while they had the light. Then he went away and hid himself. John brings in Isaiah to account for the unbelief that had persisted through so many signs. Even among the rulers some believed, yet they feared being put out of the synagogue and kept their belief to themselves.", "Jesus' public words leave a choice before them. To believe him is to believe the One who sent him. He came as light and spoke what the Father had given him, with eternal life in view. The word people heard and refused would still be there to judge them on the last day."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks about fruit when his own death draws near. John also names public approval as something that can restrain a person's confession.", "question": "Why is the picture of the grain appropriate when Jesus says his hour has arrived?"},
  {"id": 91195, "title": "The Towel and the Basin", "subtitle": "Serving from a secure place", "text": "John 13:1–20", "chapters": [[42, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["At supper, before the Passover, Jesus knew his hour had come to leave the world and go to the Father. John says he loved his own to the end. The betrayal was already taking shape. Jesus rose from the meal, took off his outer garment and wrapped a towel around himself. He poured water into a basin and began to wash and dry the disciples' feet.", "When he came to Simon Peter, Peter questioned whether the Lord would wash his feet. Jesus said Peter did not understand what he was doing now but would understand later. Peter refused. Jesus replied that if he did not wash him, Peter had no part with him. Peter then asked for his hands and head as well. Jesus answered with the picture of a person already bathed who needed only his feet washed. He added that they were clean, though not all of them, because he knew who would betray him.", "After washing their feet, Jesus put on his garment and returned to the table. He asked whether they understood what he had done. They were right to call him Teacher and Lord. Because he had washed their feet, they ought to wash one another's feet. He had given them an example to do as he had done.", "Jesus spoke of a servant and the one who sends him. He said the blessing lay in doing what they now knew. He also spoke of the coming betrayal and of receiving the people he would send."], "lesson": "Jesus knows where he comes from and where he is going when he kneels to serve. His example puts a concrete duty before people who call him Lord.", "question": "How does Jesus use the titles Teacher and Lord when he explains his action?"},
  {"id": 91196, "title": "Into the Night", "subtitle": "Love when loyalty is tested", "text": "John 13:21–38", "chapters": [[42, 13]], "start": 21, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After washing the disciples' feet, Jesus sat among them again. He became troubled and said that one of them would betray him. The disciples looked from face to face, unsure whom he meant. Peter motioned to the disciple reclining close to Jesus, and that disciple leaned back to ask. Jesus answered by telling him to watch who received the piece of bread he was about to dip. He dipped it and gave it to Judas Iscariot.", "John says that after Judas took the bread, Satan entered him. Jesus told him to do quickly what he was doing. The others heard the instruction without catching its meaning. Judas carried the common purse, so they supposed Jesus was sending him to buy something for the feast or give money to the poor. Judas received the bread and went out at once. By then it was night.", "Once Judas had gone, Jesus spoke of the Son of Man being glorified and of God being glorified in him. He told the disciples he would be with them only a little longer. Where he was going they could not come. With that separation ahead, he gave them a command for the life they would share after he left. They were to love one another as he had loved them. That love would show other people whose disciples they were.", "Peter was still thinking about where Jesus meant to go. Jesus told him that he would follow later. Peter wanted to follow now and offered to lay down his life. Jesus took up those very words and asked whether Peter would do so. Before the rooster crowed, Jesus said, Peter would deny him three times."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the command to love in a room marked by betrayal and impending failure. The test of that love will come when loyalty costs more than the disciples expect.", "question": "What does Jesus say will identify his disciples to other people?"},
  {"id": 91197, "title": "A Place in the Father's House", "subtitle": "Trust when the road ahead is unclear", "text": "John 14:1–14", "chapters": [[42, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Peter had just promised to lay down his life, and Jesus had told him what he would do before morning. The rest of the disciples had heard that Jesus was going somewhere they could not yet follow. In that room Jesus told them to trust God and trust him. He spoke of his Father's house, where there was room for them. He was going to prepare a place and would come back to take them to himself. The promise was that they would be where he was.", "Thomas stopped at the difficulty Jesus' words raised for him. If they did not know where Jesus was going, how could they know the way? Jesus answered by speaking of himself as the way, the truth and the life. Anyone coming to the Father comes through him. He told them that in knowing him they were coming to know the Father whom they longed to see.", "Philip asked Jesus to show them the Father. Jesus asked how Philip could still speak that way after spending so much time with him. The Father was in him, and he was in the Father. The words the disciples had heard and the works they had seen belonged together. Even if they struggled with what Jesus said about his relation to the Father, they could look at what he had done.", "Jesus then spoke of work for the people who would remain. The one who believed in him would do the works he did, and greater works, because he was going to the Father. He promised to answer what they asked in his name so that the Father would be glorified in the Son. Their questions had begun with how to follow him. Jesus left them with work to do and a name in which to pray."], "lesson": "Thomas and Philip ask for different kinds of clarity. Jesus answers by drawing them back to the person with whom they have been living and learning.", "question": "How does Jesus answer Philip's request to see the Father?"},
  {"id": 91198, "title": "The Promise of the Comforter", "subtitle": "Peace and obedience in Jesus' absence", "text": "John 14:15–31", "chapters": [[42, 14]], "start": 15, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus had told the disciples that he was going to the Father. Now he spoke about the help they would have in his absence. Their love for him would take shape in keeping his commands, and he would ask the Father to give them another Comforter who would stay with them. He called him the Spirit of truth. The world could not receive him because it neither saw nor knew him. The disciples already knew him, and Jesus said that he would be in them.", "Jesus promised that he would come to them and that they would live because he lived. Then Judas, whom John distinguishes from Judas Iscariot, asked why Jesus would make himself known to his disciples and keep that from the world. Jesus spoke of someone who loves him and keeps his word. The Father would love that person, and the Father and Son would make their home there. What had begun with talk of a place in the Father's house now came close to where a disciple lived.", "Jesus was telling them these things while he was still with them. Afterward, the Father would send the Holy Spirit in his name. The Spirit would teach them and bring back to mind what Jesus had said. Words they could hear in the room that night would come back to them when they needed them in days they had yet to face.", "Jesus left them his peace and called them away from fear. He told them beforehand about his going so that they could believe when it happened. The ruler of this world was coming and had no claim on him. Jesus would do what his Father commanded, so the world could see that he loved the Father. Then he called the others to get up and go with him."], "lesson": "The Spirit's work includes bringing Jesus' words back to mind. His promise of peace sits beside the call to keep those words through what is coming.", "question": "What does Jesus say the Comforter will teach or bring to remembrance?"},
  {"id": 91199, "title": "The Vine and Its Branches", "subtitle": "Fruit that comes from abiding", "text": "John 15:1–17", "chapters": [[42, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus spoke of himself as the true vine and of his Father as the one who tended it. A grower sees which branches are bearing grapes and knows where to cut. Jesus said that the Father removes the branches that bear no fruit and prunes the fruitful ones so they can bear more. He told the disciples they were already clean through the word he had spoken to them. Now they were to remain in him.", "A branch lying on the ground might still have leaves, yet it cannot keep growing grapes once severed from the vine. Jesus put the disciples in the place of those branches. They could bear much fruit by remaining in him, and apart from him they could do nothing. He joined the promise of answered prayer to his words remaining in them. Their fruit would bring glory to the Father and show that they were his disciples.", "Then Jesus spoke about love. The Father had loved him, and he had loved these disciples. They were to remain in this love by keeping his commands, just as he kept his Father's commands. He wanted them to share his joy and have it in full. The command he put before them was to love one another as he had loved them. Soon they would see what he meant when he spoke of giving one's life for one's friends.", "Jesus called them friends because he had shared what he heard from the Father. He reminded them that he had chosen them and given them work to do. They were to go and bear fruit that would last. Before he moved on to what they might expect from the world, he gave the command again to love one another."], "lesson": "A branch receives what sustains its fruit before it produces anything. Jesus gives abiding a concrete setting through his words, obedience and love for others.", "question": "What actions does Jesus connect with remaining in his love?"},
  {"id": 91200, "title": "When the World Hates You", "subtitle": "Witness in the face of opposition", "text": "John 15:18–27", "chapters": [[42, 15]], "start": 18, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus had just told the disciples to love one another. Now he told them how some people would treat them outside that circle. The world's hatred had reached him first, and they could expect it to reach them. If they belonged to the world, it would love them as its own. Jesus had chosen them out of it, and their attachment to him would carry a cost they were soon to feel.", "He asked them to remember something he had already said, that a servant ranks below his master. People who persecuted him would persecute them. Those who kept his word would keep theirs also. The disciples would encounter both responses. Jesus traced the hostility back to people's failure to know the One who sent him. It would come upon the disciples because they carried his name.", "Jesus brought the matter back to what people had actually heard and seen. He had spoken to them and done works among them that no one else had done. After seeing those works, some still hated both him and his Father. Jesus said this fulfilled what was written in their law about being hated without cause. There was a record of what people had been given the chance to receive.", "The disciples would have help when it was their turn to speak. Jesus would send the Comforter from the Father, the Spirit of truth, who would bear witness about him. The disciples would bear witness too. They had been with Jesus from the beginning. They could tell what they had heard and seen for themselves, even when the people listening had already made up their minds."], "lesson": "Opposition is something Jesus prepares his disciples for in advance. He joins their memory of what they have seen to the witness of the Spirit.", "question": "Why does Jesus say these disciples are able to bear witness about him?"},
  {"id": 91201, "title": "The Work of the Spirit of Truth", "subtitle": "Help after Jesus goes", "text": "John 16:1–15", "chapters": [[42, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told the disciples why he had spoken so plainly about what lay ahead. He wanted to keep them from falling away when it happened. They would be put out of synagogues. A time would come when people who killed them would think they were doing God a service. Jesus said that these people had known neither the Father nor him. When the hour came, he wanted the disciples to remember that he had told them.", "While Jesus was with them, he had not needed to say these things from the beginning. Now he was going back to the One who sent him. The disciples had heard about the dangers and the departure, and sorrow filled their hearts. Jesus told them there was an advantage in his leaving that they could hardly see at the time. If he went, he would send the Comforter to them.", "Jesus described the Spirit's work in terms of sin, righteousness and judgment. The Spirit would show the world its sin in refusing to believe in Jesus. He would show it righteousness because Jesus was going to the Father and the disciples would see him no more. He would show it judgment because the ruler of this world had already been judged.", "There were things Jesus could still tell the disciples that night, but he knew they could not bear them yet. The Spirit of truth would guide them into all the truth. He would speak what he heard, show them what was to come and glorify Jesus by making known what belonged to him. The disciples would still be taught when their teacher was beyond their sight."], "lesson": "The disciples see the loss before them. Jesus also describes help they will receive, including guidance for things they cannot yet bear to hear.", "question": "What does Jesus say the Spirit will do for the disciples that they need after his departure?"},
  {"id": 91202, "title": "A Little While", "subtitle": "Joy after grief and peace in trouble", "text": "John 16:16–33", "chapters": [[42, 16]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus told the disciples that in a little while they would lose sight of him and that a little while later they would see him again. The words passed among them as questions. What did he mean by a little while? How did that fit with his going to the Father? Jesus could tell that they wanted to ask him. He took up the question himself and told them some of what those two intervals would hold.", "They would weep and lament while the world rejoiced. Then the very sorrow they felt would turn to joy. Jesus asked them to think of a woman in labour. The hour brings real pain, and when the child is born that pain gives way to the joy of another life in the world. The disciples would see Jesus again, their hearts would rejoice, and nobody would be able to take that joy from them.", "Jesus spoke of a day when they would ask the Father in his name. The Father loved them because they loved Jesus and believed he had come from God. Jesus put his journey plainly before them. He had come from the Father into the world and was leaving the world to return to the Father. The disciples said they could now understand him and that they believed.", "Jesus asked whether they believed now. The time had come when they would scatter, each to his own place, and leave him alone. The Father would still be with him. Jesus had told them these things so they could have peace in him. They would have trouble in the world, and he gave them reason to take courage. He had overcome it."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the disciples language for grief that will feel final while it lasts. He also names their coming failure before speaking peace to them.", "question": "What does Jesus say will happen to their sorrow, and what does he tell them about coming trouble?"},
  {"id": 91203, "title": "Jesus Prays for Those Given to Him", "subtitle": "Knowing the Father and being kept in his name", "text": "John 17:1–12", "chapters": [[42, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus finished speaking to the disciples and lifted his eyes to heaven. He told the Father that the hour had come and asked to be glorified so that he might glorify the Father. The Father had given him authority over all people, and he would give eternal life to those the Father had entrusted to him. Jesus spoke of that life as knowing the only true God and knowing Jesus Christ, whom God had sent.", "He looked back over his work among them as work he had brought to completion. Then he asked the Father to glorify him in his presence with the glory he had shared with him before the world existed. The disciples listening to him had known him on the roads and in the houses of their own country. Now they heard him speak of his life with the Father before any of those places existed.", "Jesus turned to the people who had been with him. He had made the Father's name known to them and given them the words the Father gave him. They had received those words and come to believe that the Father sent him. Jesus prayed for them as people who belonged to the Father. He was coming to the Father, and they would remain in the world.", "He asked the Holy Father to keep them in his name so that they could be one. While Jesus had been with them, he had kept them and guarded them. The one destined for destruction had been lost, in fulfilment of Scripture. Judas had gone out into the night. As Jesus was about to go from the others too, he entrusted them to his Father's care."], "lesson": "Jesus speaks of eternal life in terms of knowing God. The prayer gives the disciples' security a place in the Father's care as Jesus' departure approaches.", "question": "How does Jesus describe eternal life as he begins to pray?"},
  {"id": 91204, "title": "A Prayer for Those Who Will Believe", "subtitle": "Unity, truth and a shared witness", "text": "John 17:13–26", "chapters": [[42, 17]], "start": 13, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus kept praying where the disciples could hear him. He wanted them to have his joy in full. He had given them the Father's word, and they had already been marked out by it. The world hated them because they did not belong to it. Jesus asked that the Father keep them from the evil one while they stayed where he had put them. The disciples still had work to do there.", "He asked the Father to make them holy through the truth and said that the Father's word is truth. The Father had sent him into the world, and he had sent the disciples into it in turn. Jesus spoke of dedicating himself for their sake so that they too could be made holy in truth. His prayer joined the work awaiting him that night to the work he was giving them.", "Then he prayed for people who had yet to hear their words. Others would believe through the testimony of those gathered there. Jesus asked that they all be one, as the Father was in him and he was in the Father. Their life together would help the world believe that the Father had sent him. He wanted the world to know that the Father loved them as he loved his Son.", "Jesus asked that those given to him would be with him and see the glory the Father had given him. He spoke of the Father's love for him before the world began. The world had failed to know the Father, and Jesus had made him known to the people who were his. He would go on making the Father's name known, so that this same love would be in them and he would be in them."], "lesson": "Jesus includes later hearers in his prayer. The unity he seeks belongs with truth and mission, because it bears witness to the Father who sent him.", "question": "How does Jesus connect the oneness of believers with what the world may come to know?"},
  {"id": 91205, "title": "The Garden, the Door and the Fire", "subtitle": "Jesus steps forward while Peter draws back", "text": "John 18:1–27", "chapters": [[42, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus crossed the Kidron valley with his disciples and went into a garden. Judas knew where to find him because Jesus had often met his disciples there. He came with soldiers and officers from the chief priests and Pharisees, carrying lanterns, torches and weapons. Jesus knew what was coming and went out to meet them. When they said they were looking for Jesus of Nazareth, he identified himself. They drew back and fell to the ground.", "Jesus asked again whom they were looking for. Once more he identified himself and asked them to let the others go. Peter drew a sword and struck off the right ear of the high priest's servant. John gives the servant's name as Malchus. Jesus told Peter to put the sword away. The Father had given him a cup to drink, and he would drink it. The officers bound him and took him first to Annas, the father-in-law of Caiaphas.", "Peter followed with another disciple who was known to the high priest. That disciple got Peter through the gate, where the servant keeping the door asked whether he belonged to Jesus. Peter denied it. Then he stood with the servants and officers at a charcoal fire they had made because it was cold.", "Inside, Jesus was questioned about his disciples and teaching. He told his questioner to ask those who had heard him speak openly. An officer struck him, and Annas sent him bound to Caiaphas. By the fire Peter denied Jesus again. A relative of Malchus then asked whether he had seen Peter in the garden. Peter denied it a third time, and at once a rooster crowed."], "lesson": "Jesus acts to protect his followers and speaks openly when questioned. John's movement between Jesus and Peter shows two responses under pressure.", "question": "What does Jesus ask for the disciples at the arrest, and what does Peter say about belonging to him?"},
  {"id": 91206, "title": "Pilate Asks About Truth", "subtitle": "Listening when a claim challenges your authority", "text": "John 18:28–40", "chapters": [[42, 18]], "start": 28, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Early in the morning Jesus was taken from Caiaphas to the governor's headquarters. The men who brought him stayed outside so they could eat the Passover without becoming ceremonially unclean. Pilate went out and asked what charge they were bringing. They replied that they would hardly have handed Jesus over if he were innocent. Pilate told them to judge him by their own law. They answered that they had no authority to put anyone to death. John connects this with what Jesus had said about the way he would die.", "Pilate went inside again and called Jesus. He asked whether he was the king of the Jews. Jesus asked where the question had come from. Pilate said that Jesus' own nation and its chief priests had handed him over. He wanted to know what Jesus had done.", "Jesus told him that his kingdom was not from this world. If it were, his servants would have fought to prevent his arrest. Pilate fastened on the word kingdom and asked whether Jesus was a king after all. Jesus spoke of what he had come into the world to do. He had come to bear witness to the truth, and those who belonged to the truth listened to his voice.", "Pilate asked what truth was and went outside again. He said he found no fault in Jesus. Then he brought up the custom of releasing a prisoner at Passover and offered them the man he had just questioned as their king. The people called for Barabbas instead. John tells us that Barabbas was a robber."], "lesson": "Pilate asks a striking question and leaves before John records any answer from Jesus. The scene asks whether our questions leave room to hear what might demand a decision.", "question": "How does Jesus explain the character of his kingdom to Pilate?"},
  {"id": 91207, "title": "The Crown and the Appeal to Caesar", "subtitle": "Authority and accountability", "text": "John 19:1–16", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Pilate had Jesus flogged. The soldiers twisted thorns into a crown and set it on his head. They put a purple robe on him, came up to salute him as king and struck him. Pilate went outside and told the people again that he found no fault in Jesus. Then Jesus came out in the crown and robe. Pilate called on them to look at the man standing before them.", "The chief priests and officers called for crucifixion. Pilate told them to take Jesus themselves, repeating that he found no fault in him. They answered that by their law Jesus ought to die because he had made himself the Son of God. On hearing this, Pilate grew more afraid. He went indoors again and asked Jesus where he came from. Jesus gave him no answer.", "Pilate reminded Jesus that he had the power to release him or crucify him. Jesus replied that Pilate could have no power over him unless it had been given from above. He also said that the one who handed him over bore the greater sin. Pilate continued trying to release him, and those outside turned their charge toward Caesar. If Pilate released a man who called himself king, they said, he was showing himself to be no friend of Caesar.", "Pilate brought Jesus out and sat on the judgment seat at the Stone Pavement, called Gabbatha. It was the day of preparation for Passover. Pilate presented Jesus as their king. The chief priests declared that Caesar was their only king, and Pilate handed Jesus over to be crucified."], "lesson": "Pilate's authority is real enough for his decision to matter. Jesus' words place that authority under a higher accountability.", "question": "What different pressures can you see in Pilate's actions and questions?"},
  {"id": 91208, "title": "The Written Charge and the Finished Work", "subtitle": "Care and faithfulness at the cross", "text": "John 19:17–30", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 17, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Jesus went out carrying the cross to the Place of the Skull, called Golgotha. There the soldiers crucified him between two others. Pilate had an inscription placed over him naming Jesus of Nazareth as king of the Jews. The place was near the city and many could read the charge, which was written in Hebrew, Greek and Latin. The chief priests wanted Pilate to change it to say Jesus had claimed to be king. Pilate let the wording stand.", "The soldiers divided Jesus' clothing into four shares, one for each of them. They came to his tunic and found that it was woven in one piece. They agreed to leave it whole and cast lots for it. John sees in their action the fulfilment of Scripture about dividing someone's garments and casting lots for his clothing.", "Jesus' mother stood near the cross with other women, among them Mary Magdalene. Jesus saw his mother and the disciple he loved standing nearby. He addressed her first and gave her the disciple as a son. Then he gave the disciple Mary as a mother. From that hour the disciple took her into his own home. Even here, Jesus made provision for the people who would go home without him.", "Jesus knew that his work was now complete. To fulfil Scripture he said he was thirsty. There was a jar of sour wine nearby, and they put a sponge soaked in it on hyssop and raised it to his mouth. When he had received it, he said the work was finished. Then he bowed his head and gave up his spirit."], "lesson": "Jesus gives attention to his mother's future while in suffering himself. John records that personal act in the same scene as the public inscription and Jesus' last words.", "question": "What responsibility does Jesus give to the disciple standing near his mother?"},
  {"id": 91209, "title": "A Witness and a Burial Before Sunset", "subtitle": "Courage when public favour has gone", "text": "John 19:31–42", "chapters": [[42, 19]], "start": 31, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["The next day was a Sabbath of special importance. The leaders asked Pilate to have the legs of the crucified men broken and their bodies removed before the Sabbath began. The soldiers broke the legs of the men on either side of Jesus. When they reached him, they found he was already dead. They left his legs untouched, and one soldier pierced his side with a spear. Blood and water came out.", "At this point John pauses over the testimony of the person who saw it. His witness is given so that others may believe. John draws attention to two things that had happened in front of that witness. Jesus' bones had been left whole, and his side had been pierced. He connects both details with Scripture.", "Joseph of Arimathea had followed Jesus secretly because he was afraid. Now he went to Pilate and asked for permission to take away Jesus' body. Pilate agreed. Nicodemus came too, carrying about a hundred pounds of myrrh and aloes. John reminds us that Nicodemus was the man who had first gone to Jesus at night. The two men wrapped the body in linen with the spices, following the Jewish burial custom.", "There was a garden near the place of execution and a new tomb in it where nobody had been laid. The day of preparation was drawing to its close, and the tomb was close at hand. Joseph and Nicodemus laid Jesus there. When John takes us back to the tomb, someone else will come looking for him."], "lesson": "Joseph and Nicodemus use their opportunity to care for Jesus after his death. Their actions bring a measure of public courage into lives John earlier describes as cautious.", "question": "How does Nicodemus's action here compare with the circumstances of his first visit in John 3?"},
  {"id": 91210, "title": "Mary Hears Her Name", "subtitle": "Recognition in the place of grief", "text": "John 20:1–18", "chapters": [[42, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Mary Magdalene went to the tomb early on the first day of the week while it was still dark. She saw the stone had been removed and ran to Peter and the disciple Jesus loved. She told them the Lord had been taken out of the tomb and that they did not know where he had been laid. Both men ran there. The other disciple arrived first, but Peter went in ahead of him.", "They saw the linen cloths and the cloth that had covered Jesus' head, lying apart. The other disciple entered and believed. John says they did not yet understand the Scripture that Jesus must rise from the dead. The men went back to their homes.", "Mary remained outside weeping. Looking into the tomb, she saw two angels sitting where the body had been. They asked why she wept, and she told them she did not know where her Lord had been taken. She turned and saw Jesus standing there, but did not recognise him. Thinking he was the gardener, she asked whether he had carried the body away and where he had put it.", "Jesus said her name. She turned and called him Teacher. He sent her to his brethren with a message about his going to his Father and theirs. Mary went to the disciples and told them she had seen the Lord and that he had spoken to her. Her morning had begun with a report about an empty place and ended with testimony to a person she had met."], "lesson": "Mary comes looking for a body and leaves with a message from Jesus. His speaking her name marks the moment recognition comes.", "question": "What does Mary assume at first, and what changes her understanding?"},
  {"id": 91211, "title": "Closed Doors and Thomas's Confession", "subtitle": "Peace for fearful disciples and faith for readers", "text": "John 20:19–31", "chapters": [[42, 20]], "start": 19, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["That evening the disciples were together behind locked doors. Mary Magdalene had brought them her news, and the fear of those outside was still real. Jesus came and stood among them. He greeted them with peace and showed them his hands and side. The wounds belonged to the man they had lost, and they were glad to see him standing there.", "Jesus spoke peace again and told them that he was sending them as the Father had sent him. He breathed on them and told them to receive the Holy Spirit. He gave them responsibility concerning the forgiveness and retention of sins. They were still in the room where they had shut themselves in, and Jesus was already speaking of sending them out.", "Thomas, one of the Twelve, had been absent. When the others told him they had seen the Lord, he said what it would take for him to believe. He wanted to see the nail marks and put his finger in them, and put his hand into Jesus' side. Eight days later the disciples were indoors again. This time Thomas was with them. Jesus stood among them, greeted them with peace and invited Thomas to examine the wounds as he had asked.", "Thomas answered by calling Jesus his Lord and his God. Jesus spoke of the blessing of those who would believe without seeing him. John then turns to the people reading the book. Jesus did many other signs in the disciples' presence. John chose the ones he recorded so readers might believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God, and have life in his name."], "lesson": "Jesus takes Thomas's stated difficulty seriously and calls him to believe. John then turns towards readers who will encounter the signs through testimony.", "question": "How does John's stated purpose connect Thomas's encounter to your reading of the Gospel?"},
  {"id": 91212, "title": "Breakfast by the Lake", "subtitle": "Care after a night of empty nets", "text": "John 21:1–14", "chapters": [[42, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["Seven disciples were together by the Sea of Tiberias. Peter said he was going fishing, and the others went with him. They put out in the boat and worked through the night without a catch. At daybreak Jesus was standing on the shore. From the boat they could see a man there, but they did not know it was him. He called to ask if they had caught anything. They had to tell him they had nothing.", "Jesus told them to cast the net on the right side of the boat. When they did, there were so many fish they could hardly draw it in. The disciple Jesus loved recognised him first and told Peter it was the Lord. Peter pulled on his outer garment and threw himself into the water. The others brought the boat in, dragging the net behind it. They were only about two hundred cubits from shore.", "When they landed, they found a charcoal fire with fish already on it, and bread. Jesus told them to bring some of the fish they had caught. Peter hauled the net ashore. There were a hundred and fifty-three large fish in it, and the net had held. A little earlier, they had called back to the shore to say they had nothing.", "Jesus invited them to eat and gave them bread and fish. Nobody ventured to ask who he was. They knew now. John counts this as the third time Jesus showed himself to the disciples after his resurrection. The meal by the fire came before the conversation Jesus was about to have with Peter."], "lesson": "The risen Jesus meets the disciples in familiar work and provides a meal. John gives time to this quiet welcome before recording what Jesus will ask of Peter.", "question": "What details show that Jesus had prepared for the disciples before they came ashore?"},
  {"id": 91213, "title": "Peter Is Asked to Feed the Sheep", "subtitle": "Love expressed through patient responsibility", "text": "John 21:15–25", "chapters": [[42, 21]], "start": 15, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "John", "story": ["After the disciples had eaten beside the lake, Jesus turned to Simon Peter. He asked him whether he loved him more than these. Peter answered by appealing to what Jesus knew: Jesus knew that he loved him. Jesus told him to feed his lambs. A second time Jesus asked about his love, and Peter answered much as before. Jesus gave him the care of his sheep.", "Jesus asked a third time. Peter was grieved at being asked again and replied that the Lord knew all things and knew that he loved him. Jesus again gave him the responsibility of feeding his sheep. The three questions gave Peter room to answer. Each answer was followed by work that required care for others.", "Jesus then spoke of a time when Peter would grow old, stretch out his hands and be taken where he would not wish to go. John explains that this indicated the manner of death by which Peter would glorify God. Jesus told him to follow him.", "Peter turned and saw the disciple Jesus loved following. He asked what would happen to him. Jesus answered that the other disciple's future did not alter Peter's own call to follow. A report spread that the other disciple would not die. John corrects the report by recalling the conditional way Jesus had spoken. The Gospel closes by affirming the disciple's testimony and acknowledging that much more could be written about what Jesus did."], "lesson": "Jesus gives Peter a practical expression for his love: care for the people entrusted to him. When Peter asks about someone else's path, Jesus puts his own responsibility before him again.", "question": "What task follows each question about Peter's love?"},
  {"id": 91214, "title": "From Jerusalem to the Ends of the Earth", "subtitle": "Waiting before setting out", "text": "Acts 1:1–11", "chapters": [[43, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Luke addresses Theophilus again as he begins the second part of his account. His first book told what Jesus began to do and teach up to the day he was taken into heaven. Now Luke goes back over the days between the resurrection and that departure. Jesus showed himself alive to the apostles repeatedly over forty days. They had seen him suffer, and now they had many proofs that he was alive. During those days he spoke to them about the kingdom of God.", "Jesus gave them an instruction that required staying where they were. They were to remain in Jerusalem and wait for what the Father had promised. John had baptised with water. In a few days they would be baptised with the Holy Spirit. The city where Jesus had been crucified was the place from which their work would begin.", "When they came together, the apostles asked whether he would now restore the kingdom to Israel. Jesus told them that the Father had kept the times and seasons under his own authority. Then he spoke of what they would be given. The Holy Spirit would come upon them with power, and they would bear witness to Jesus in Jerusalem, across Judea and Samaria, and to the ends of the earth.", "While they watched, Jesus was lifted up and a cloud took him from sight. The apostles kept looking into heaven. Two men in white stood beside them and asked why they were still looking up. This same Jesus, they said, would return in the way they had seen him go. The apostles had a promise about his return and an instruction about where they were to wait for what came next."], "lesson": "Jesus gives the disciples a responsibility they can prepare for and leaves the timetable they cannot control with the Father.", "question": "What does Jesus tell the disciples to expect, and what work does he give them?"},
  {"id": 91215, "title": "A Place Among the Twelve", "subtitle": "Prayer when a decision must be made", "text": "Acts 1:12–26", "chapters": [[43, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The apostles came back to Jerusalem from the Mount of Olives. It was a short walk, about the distance allowed on a Sabbath. They went to the upper room where they were staying. Luke names the eleven who were there. The women were with them, as were Mary the mother of Jesus and his brothers. They gave themselves together to prayer while they waited for what Jesus had promised.", "At a gathering of about a hundred and twenty, Peter stood up and spoke about Judas, who had guided the people who arrested Jesus. Judas had been counted among them and had shared in their ministry. Luke records the violent end associated with the field called Aceldama, the Field of Blood. Peter turned to the Psalms, finding there both a word about Judas's place being left desolate and a reason for someone else to take up his office.", "The person chosen had to be able to join the apostles as a witness to Jesus' resurrection. Peter said he should have been with them throughout Jesus' ministry, beginning with John's baptism and continuing to the day Jesus was taken up. The people put forward two men who had been there, Joseph called Barsabas, who was also known as Justus, and Matthias.", "The group prayed to the Lord, who knew what was in every heart, and asked him to show which man he had chosen for the work. They cast lots, and the lot fell to Matthias. He was counted with the eleven apostles. Judas's place among the Twelve was filled as they prepared for the testimony they would soon have to give."], "lesson": "The community takes care over who can bear witness, then acknowledges the limits of what people can see of another person's heart.", "question": "What qualifications does Peter name for the person who will take Judas's place?"},
  {"id": 91216, "title": "The Day the Crowd Heard Its Own Languages", "subtitle": "The Spirit and the good news", "text": "Acts 2:1–21", "chapters": [[43, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the day of Pentecost came, the believers were together in one place. Suddenly a sound like a rushing wind came from heaven and filled the house where they were sitting. What looked like divided tongues of fire appeared and rested on them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and began speaking in other languages as the Spirit enabled them.", "Jerusalem was home at that time to devout Jews from many countries. When a crowd gathered, people were bewildered because they heard the speakers in the languages of the places where they had been born. They recognised the speakers as Galileans and wondered how they could hear their own languages. Luke names people from places as far apart as Mesopotamia, Egypt and Rome, all hearing about God's mighty works.", "Some asked one another what it meant. Others mocked and said the speakers had been drinking. Peter stood with the eleven and raised his voice. He told the crowd that it was still early in the morning and that the people were not drunk, as some supposed. Then he turned to the prophet Joel to explain what the crowd had witnessed.", "Joel had spoken of God pouring out his Spirit. Sons and daughters would prophesy, young people would see visions and old people would dream. Servants would be included. Joel's words ended with the promise that whoever called on the Lord's name would be saved. Peter put that promise before people who were already hearing the wonders of God in their own languages."], "lesson": "The Spirit enables the gathered people to hear rather than leaving them to cross the language barrier alone. Peter gives an explanation grounded in Scripture.", "question": "What did the hearers recognise about the languages in which the disciples spoke?"},
  {"id": 91217, "title": "Peter Finishes Speaking and the People Respond", "subtitle": "Repentance and a shared life", "text": "Acts 2:22–47", "chapters": [[43, 2]], "start": 22, "end": 47, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter spoke to the people about Jesus of Nazareth. They knew the works God had done through him. Jesus had been handed over and crucified, Peter said, but God raised him. He read David's words about hope beyond the grave and distinguished David, whose tomb was known to them, from the one whose resurrection David foresaw. Peter said he and the others were witnesses. He connected what the crowd now saw and heard with Jesus receiving the promised Spirit from the Father and pouring him out.", "Peter ended by telling the people that God had made the Jesus they crucified both Lord and Christ. They were cut to the heart and asked Peter and the other apostles what they should do. Peter called them to repent and be baptised in Jesus Christ's name for the forgiveness of sins. He spoke of the gift of the Spirit and said the promise reached their children and people far away whom the Lord would call.", "Those who received his message were baptised. About three thousand were added that day. Luke then follows them past the public event. They continued in the apostles' teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread and prayers.", "People shared their possessions to meet needs. They met in the temple and broke bread in homes, eating with gladness and simplicity of heart. As they praised God and received favour among the people, the Lord added to the church. Luke gives the reader a picture of what continued after the day's excitement."], "lesson": "Luke follows the public response with the daily practices that gave it substance. Repentance had consequences for the people's time, possessions and relationships.", "question": "Which habits continued after the people heard Peter and were baptised?"},
  {"id": 91218, "title": "A Man Stands at the Beautiful Gate", "subtitle": "Mercy that directs attention to Jesus", "text": "Acts 3:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter and John were going to the temple at the afternoon hour of prayer. Near the entrance called the Beautiful Gate, a man who had been unable to walk from birth was being carried to his usual place. People laid him there every day so he could ask worshippers for alms. When Peter and John approached, he asked them too. Peter told him to look at them, and the man looked up expecting money.", "Peter had no silver or gold to give him. He called on the name of Jesus Christ of Nazareth and told the man to walk. Then he took him by the right hand and lifted him. Strength came into the man's feet and ankles. He stood, walked into the temple with them and began leaping and praising God. People recognised the man they were accustomed to seeing at the gate and came running to the porch called Solomon's.", "As the man held on to Peter and John, Peter asked the crowd why they were staring at them as if their own power or holiness had made him walk. The God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob had glorified Jesus, whom the people had rejected and handed over. They had asked for a murderer and killed the author of life. God had raised him, and the apostles were witnesses. Peter said the man stood before them sound through faith in Jesus' name.", "Peter acknowledged that the people and their rulers had acted in ignorance. He urged them to repent so their sins could be wiped away. He drew together the promises spoken by Moses and the prophets and the blessing God had promised Abraham for all the families of the earth. God had sent his servant to them first, Peter said, to bless them by turning each of them from wrongdoing."], "lesson": "Peter attends to the man and then to the crowd's misunderstanding. The power he exercises is an occasion to direct attention towards Jesus.", "question": "How does Peter answer the crowd's assumption about his power or holiness?"},
  {"id": 91219, "title": "A Council Sees the Man Standing", "subtitle": "Speaking with courage about what you know", "text": "Acts 4:1–22", "chapters": [[43, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Peter and John were still speaking when the priests, the captain of the temple and the Sadducees came up to them. They objected to the apostles teaching the people and proclaiming resurrection through Jesus. Since it was already evening, they put the two men in custody until the next day. The message had already reached many hearers. Luke puts the number of men who believed at about five thousand.", "The next day the rulers, elders and scribes assembled, with Annas, Caiaphas and others of the high priest's family. They set the apostles in the middle and asked by what power or name they had acted. Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said the man had been made well through Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they had crucified and God had raised. The stone they had rejected had become the cornerstone. Peter told them that salvation was found in Jesus' name.", "The council saw how freely Peter and John spoke and recognised that they had been with Jesus. The healed man was there too, standing beside them. The council sent the three outside and talked over what they could do. A sign had happened that people throughout Jerusalem knew about. They decided to threaten the apostles and forbid them to speak or teach in Jesus' name.", "Peter and John asked the rulers to judge whether it was right before God to obey them in place of God. They had to speak about what they had seen and heard. After further threats the council released them. The people were praising God for what had happened to the man, who was more than forty years old."], "lesson": "Their answer rests on a testimony they can give honestly. The man standing with them makes the subject of the proceedings visible.", "question": "What evidence does Luke name that the council could see for itself?"},
  {"id": 91220, "title": "The Prayer for Boldness", "subtitle": "Courage and care within the church", "text": "Acts 4:23–37", "chapters": [[43, 4]], "start": 23, "end": 37, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When Peter and John were released, they went back to their own people and told them what the chief priests and elders had said. The believers lifted their voices together in prayer. They began with God as maker of heaven, earth and sea, then recalled the words of David about rulers joining forces against the Lord and his anointed. The opposition they had just heard about had a history they recognised in Scripture.", "They named Herod and Pontius Pilate, the Gentiles and the people of Israel who had gathered against Jesus in that city. They acknowledged that the events had taken place within what God had determined beforehand. Now they asked the Lord to consider the threats and give his servants boldness to speak. They asked him to stretch out his hand to heal and do signs through the name of Jesus.", "When they finished praying, the place where they were gathered shook. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke God's word boldly. Luke then takes the reader from that shared prayer to the way the believers were living together. They were of one heart and soul, and people made what they owned available to one another. The apostles spoke with great power about the resurrection, and great grace was upon them all.", "Those who owned lands or houses sold them and brought the proceeds to the apostles to distribute according to need. Luke names one of them. Joseph was a Levite from Cyprus whom the apostles called Barnabas, meaning son of encouragement. He sold a field and brought the money to the apostles. The gift belonged to the same life Luke had just described, sustained by prayer and attentive to the people who would otherwise go without."], "lesson": "The community asks for courage to carry on with its work. Luke places that prayer beside generosity that answers needs within the same community.", "question": "What do the believers ask God to give them after hearing about the threats?"},
  {"id": 91221, "title": "Ananias and Sapphira's Agreement", "subtitle": "Honesty before God", "text": "Acts 5:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Luke has just told how Barnabas sold a field and brought the money to the apostles. Now Ananias and his wife Sapphira sold a property of their own. They agreed to keep some of the money back. Ananias brought the rest and laid it before the apostles. Peter asked him why he had allowed Satan to fill his heart so that he lied to the Holy Spirit about the proceeds.", "Peter reminded Ananias that the property had been his before the sale and that the money was still in his power afterward. He could decide what to do with it. The lie he had chosen to tell was a lie to God. When Ananias heard Peter's words, he fell down and died. Young men wrapped his body, carried him out and buried him. The news brought great fear to those who heard it.", "About three hours later Sapphira came in, unaware of what had happened. Peter asked whether the amount Ananias had brought was what they had received for the property. She said it was. Peter asked how they had agreed to put the Spirit of the Lord to the test. The men who had buried Ananias were at the door. Sapphira fell at Peter's feet and died, and they carried her out and buried her beside her husband.", "Fear came over the whole church and everyone who heard the account. Luke goes on to describe signs performed through the apostles and believers gathering at Solomon's Porch. More men and women came to believe. People brought the sick into the streets and laid them on beds and mats in the hope that even Peter's shadow might fall on them. Crowds came from towns around Jerusalem, bringing the sick and those troubled by unclean spirits, and they were healed."], "lesson": "Peter's question makes clear that retaining money was possible. The public appearance of a gift became an occasion for dishonesty about what had been given.", "question": "What freedom did Peter say Ananias had over the property and the proceeds?"},
  {"id": 91222, "title": "The Open Prison and the Unfinished Work", "subtitle": "Obedience after a warning", "text": "Acts 5:17–42", "chapters": [[43, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 42, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The high priest and the Sadducees with him were filled with jealousy. They arrested the apostles and put them in the public prison. During the night an angel of the Lord opened the prison doors and led them out. He directed them to go to the temple and tell the people the words of this life. At daybreak they went to the temple and began teaching.", "The council met and sent for the prisoners. The officers found the doors shut and the guards at their posts, but the prison was empty. Then someone reported that the men they had locked up were teaching in the temple. The captain and officers brought them in without force. They feared that the people might stone them.", "The high priest reminded the apostles that they had been forbidden to teach in Jesus' name. Peter and the others said they must obey God. God had raised Jesus, whom the rulers had hung on a tree, and exalted him as Prince and Saviour to give Israel repentance and forgiveness. The apostles were witnesses, and so was the Holy Spirit. The council was furious and wanted to kill them.", "Gamaliel, a respected teacher of the law, had the apostles sent outside. He recalled how the followers of Theudas and Judas the Galilean had dispersed after their leaders died. He advised the council to leave the apostles alone. If their work came from men, it would fail. If it came from God, the council could find itself fighting God. They took his advice, though they beat the apostles and ordered them to stop speaking in Jesus' name. The apostles left rejoicing that they had suffered for him. They went on teaching about Jesus daily in the temple and in homes."], "lesson": "The opened prison leads straight back to the work that caused their arrest. Their courage is measured by what they continue doing after the council releases them.", "question": "Where do the apostles teach after receiving orders to stop?"},
  {"id": 91223, "title": "The Widows Who Were Overlooked", "subtitle": "Fair service and shared responsibility", "text": "Acts 6:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The number of disciples was growing, and a complaint came from the Greek-speaking Jews against the Hebrew-speaking Jews. Their widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution. The Twelve called the disciples together. The work of serving at the tables needed people who could give it proper attention, and the apostles needed to keep their attention on prayer and the ministry of the word.", "The Twelve asked the group to choose seven men of good reputation, full of the Holy Spirit and wisdom, whom they could put in charge. The proposal pleased the gathering. They chose Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas and Nicolas, a convert to Judaism from Antioch. They brought the men before the apostles, who prayed and laid their hands on them. Luke records that the word of God spread, the number of disciples in Jerusalem grew greatly and many priests accepted the faith.", "Then Luke stays with Stephen. Full of grace and power, he did wonders and signs among the people. Men from the synagogue associated with the Freedmen and from groups connected with Cyrene, Alexandria, Cilicia and Asia argued with him. They could not withstand the wisdom and the Spirit with which he spoke. They persuaded others to accuse him of speaking against Moses and against God.", "The people, elders and scribes were stirred up. Stephen was seized and brought before the council. False witnesses said he spoke against the holy place and the law. They accused him of saying Jesus of Nazareth would destroy the place and change the customs Moses had handed down. The people sitting in the council fixed their eyes on Stephen. Luke says his face looked like the face of an angel."], "lesson": "The complaint exposes a specific gap in care. The community answers it by giving capable people responsibility and recognising their work publicly.", "question": "What change made it possible to respond to the widows' needs while the apostles continued their work?"},
  {"id": 91224, "title": "Stephen Begins With Abraham and Joseph", "subtitle": "God's presence in unfamiliar places", "text": "Acts 7:1–29", "chapters": [[43, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The high priest asked Stephen whether the charges were true. Stephen began his answer far from Jerusalem. The God of glory had appeared to Abraham in Mesopotamia, before Abraham lived in Haran, and called him to leave his country and relatives. God moved him on to the land where Stephen's hearers now lived. Abraham received no piece of it to call his own at the time, yet God promised the land to his descendants before he even had a child. God also told him they would live as strangers and suffer oppression for four hundred years before coming out to worship him.", "Stephen traced the family from Abraham through Isaac, Jacob and the twelve patriarchs. Joseph's brothers grew jealous and sold him into Egypt. God was with Joseph there, brought him through his troubles and gave him favour and wisdom before Pharaoh. When famine came, the brothers went to Egypt for food. On the second visit Joseph made himself known. He sent for Jacob and the whole family, seventy-five people in Stephen's account. Jacob and the patriarchs died in Egypt, and Stephen speaks of their burial in the ancestral tomb at Shechem.", "As the time of the promise drew near, the people multiplied in Egypt. A new king who had no knowledge of Joseph dealt cruelly with them. Their infants were put out so they would not survive. Moses was born in that period. He was kept for three months in his father's house, then Pharaoh's daughter took him in and raised him as her son. He was educated in the wisdom of Egypt.", "At forty, Moses went to see his own people and defended an Israelite by striking down the Egyptian who was mistreating him. The next day he tried to reconcile two Israelites who were quarrelling. The man doing wrong pushed Moses away and asked who had made him ruler and judge. He knew what Moses had done the previous day. Moses fled to Midian, where he lived as a stranger and had two sons."], "lesson": "Stephen recalls God working through people living far from the places later associated with worship. His history also shows how quickly a brother's help can be refused.", "question": "Where does Stephen say God was with Joseph, and what had brought Joseph there?"},
  {"id": 91225, "title": "The Burning Bush and Stephen's Last Prayer", "subtitle": "Faithful witness and forgiveness", "text": "Acts 7:30–60", "chapters": [[43, 7]], "start": 30, "end": 60, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Forty years passed before Moses saw the flame in the bush in the wilderness near Mount Sinai. As he drew close, he heard the voice of the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob. He was told to take off his sandals because the ground was holy. God had seen the suffering of his people and heard them groan. He would send Moses back to Egypt, the very man whom one of his own people had refused as ruler and judge.", "Moses led them out with signs in Egypt, at the Red Sea and in the wilderness. He spoke of the prophet God would raise up from among their people. The people refused to obey Moses, turned back toward Egypt in their hearts and made a calf to worship. Stephen recalled the tent of testimony they carried in the wilderness, which came into the land with Joshua. David sought a dwelling for God and Solomon built the house. Stephen quoted the prophet to remind his hearers that the Most High could never be contained in a house built by human hands.", "Stephen then spoke directly to the council. They resisted the Holy Spirit as their fathers had done. Their fathers persecuted the prophets who foretold the coming of the Righteous One. Now they themselves had betrayed and killed him. They had received the law, and Stephen charged them with failing to keep it.", "The hearers were enraged. Stephen, full of the Holy Spirit, looked up and saw Jesus standing at God's right hand. When he told them what he saw, they covered their ears and rushed at him. They drove him out and stoned him, leaving their coats with a young man named Saul. Stephen asked Jesus to receive his spirit. Then he knelt and asked the Lord to forgive those killing him. After that prayer he died."], "lesson": "Stephen ends a severe speech with a prayer for his attackers. The two actions show that naming wrongdoing and praying for those responsible can belong together.", "question": "What two requests does Stephen make as he is dying?"},
  {"id": 91226, "title": "Scattered People Carry the Word", "subtitle": "A gift beyond purchase", "text": "Acts 8:1–25", "chapters": [[43, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul approved of Stephen's death. That day persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem, and believers were scattered through Judea and Samaria while the apostles remained in the city. Devout men buried Stephen and mourned him deeply. Saul went from house to house, dragging away men and women and sending them to prison. The people forced to leave Jerusalem carried the message with them.", "Philip went to a city in Samaria and proclaimed Christ. The crowds listened as they heard him and saw what happened among them. Unclean spirits came out of people, and many who had been paralysed or unable to walk were healed. Joy spread through the city. For a long time the people had been impressed by a man named Simon, who practised sorcery and presented himself as someone great. Now men and women believed Philip's message about God's kingdom and Jesus Christ and were baptised. Simon was baptised too and followed Philip, amazed at the signs.", "Word reached Jerusalem that Samaria had received God's word, so the apostles sent Peter and John. The two prayed for the believers and laid their hands on them, and they received the Holy Spirit. Simon saw what happened and offered the apostles money. He wanted the power to give the Holy Spirit to anyone on whom he laid his hands.", "Peter told Simon that God's gift could never be bought. His heart was wrong before God, and he needed to turn from his wickedness and pray for forgiveness. Simon asked them to pray that the things Peter had spoken of would not happen to him. Peter and John bore witness and preached in many Samaritan villages on their way back to Jerusalem."], "lesson": "Persecution sends the message beyond Jerusalem. Simon's response shows how easily a person can see God's gift through the habits of an earlier life.", "question": "What does Simon ask to buy, and why does Peter reject the offer?"},
  {"id": 91227, "title": "Philip Meets a Reader on the Road", "subtitle": "Beginning with the question someone is asking", "text": "Acts 8:26–40", "chapters": [[43, 8]], "start": 26, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["An angel of the Lord told Philip to go south to the road from Jerusalem to Gaza. Philip went and saw an Ethiopian official returning from worship in Jerusalem. The man was responsible for the treasury of Candace, queen of the Ethiopians. He was sitting in his chariot reading Isaiah as he travelled.", "The Spirit told Philip to go near and join the chariot. Philip ran alongside and heard the man reading. He asked whether he understood what he was reading. The official answered that he needed someone to guide him and invited Philip to sit with him. The passage described someone led like a sheep to slaughter, silent before the one who shears it. The official asked whether the prophet was speaking about himself or about someone else.", "Philip began with that passage and told him the good news about Jesus. As they went along, they came to water. The official pointed it out and asked what prevented him from being baptised. In the passage as printed in the King James Version, their exchange includes his confession of faith in Jesus Christ as the Son of God. He ordered the chariot to stop, and both men went down into the water. Philip baptised him.", "When they came up, the Spirit took Philip away. The official did not see him again and went on his way rejoicing. Philip was found at Azotus and preached in the towns as he travelled towards Caesarea."], "lesson": "Philip asks a question before giving an explanation. He begins where the man is reading and responds to the question the man actually brings.", "question": "Which question opens the conversation, and which Scripture is the man reading?"},
  {"id": 91228, "title": "Saul Is Led by the Hand", "subtitle": "Grace that changes a persecutor's direction", "text": "Acts 9:1–22", "chapters": [[43, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul was still threatening the followers of Jesus. He obtained letters to the synagogues in Damascus so that he could arrest people of the Way and bring them bound to Jerusalem. As he approached Damascus, a light from heaven shone around him. He fell to the ground and heard a voice asking why he was persecuting him. Saul asked who was speaking. The answer was Jesus, the one he was persecuting.", "Saul rose and opened his eyes but could not see. His companions led him by the hand into Damascus. For three days he was without sight and ate and drank nothing. The man who had set out with authority to take others away had to be led into the city himself.", "In Damascus the Lord called a disciple named Ananias and gave him the street and house where he would find Saul. Ananias had heard of the harm Saul had done in Jerusalem and knew why he had come. The Lord told him to go and explained that Saul was chosen to carry his name and would suffer for it. Ananias went into the house, laid his hands on Saul and addressed him as a brother.", "Something like scales fell from Saul's eyes. He could see, rose and was baptised, then took food and regained strength. He spent time with the disciples in Damascus and began preaching that Jesus was the Son of God. Those who heard were astonished to hear that message from the man who had come to arrest people for calling on Jesus' name."], "lesson": "Ananias names a reasonable fear and then obeys. His greeting gives a new relationship practical expression before Saul has built a different reputation.", "question": "What did Ananias know about Saul, and what did he choose to do?"},
  {"id": 91229, "title": "A Basket, a Friend and a Room of Mourners", "subtitle": "The value of help that can be seen", "text": "Acts 9:23–43", "chapters": [[43, 9]], "start": 23, "end": 43, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Saul had been preaching in Damascus that Jesus was the Christ. After many days, some of his opponents plotted to kill him and watched the city gates day and night. Saul learned of the plan. His disciples found another way through the wall. During the night they lowered him in a basket, beyond the reach of the people waiting at the gates.", "In Jerusalem, Saul tried to join the disciples. They knew his reputation and were afraid that his claim to be a disciple was false. Barnabas took him to the apostles and told them how Saul had seen the Lord on the road and spoken boldly in Jesus' name in Damascus. Saul went about with the believers in Jerusalem and spoke openly there too. When the Greek-speaking Jews he disputed with tried to kill him, the believers took him down to Caesarea and sent him on to Tarsus. Luke records a time of peace and growth for the churches across Judea, Galilee and Samaria.", "Peter travelled among the believers and came to Lydda. There he found Aeneas, who had been paralysed and confined to bed for eight years. Peter told him that Jesus Christ healed him and that he should get up and make his bed. Aeneas rose at once. People in Lydda and the plain of Sharon saw him and turned to the Lord.", "In nearby Joppa, a disciple named Tabitha, also called Dorcas, fell ill and died. The widows stood weeping beside Peter when he arrived, showing him garments she had made. Peter sent everyone out, knelt and prayed. He called Tabitha to get up, and she opened her eyes. He gave her his hand and presented her alive to the believers and widows. As news spread and many believed, Peter stayed in Joppa with Simon, a tanner."], "lesson": "The basket, Barnabas's testimony and the garments make care visible. Luke gives attention to service which would otherwise remain in the background.", "question": "What did the widows bring to show Peter about Dorcas's life?"},
  {"id": 91230, "title": "Two Households Receive Instructions", "subtitle": "Letting God correct a settled distinction", "text": "Acts 10:1–23", "chapters": [[43, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Cornelius lived in Caesarea and commanded a hundred soldiers in the Italian regiment. He and his household feared God. He gave generously to the people and prayed regularly. One afternoon he saw an angel come in and call him by name. Frightened, Cornelius asked what it meant. The angel said his prayers and gifts had come before God and told him to send to Joppa for Simon Peter, who was lodging with Simon the tanner by the sea.", "Cornelius called two servants and a devout soldier, told them what had happened and sent them off. The next day, as they approached Joppa, Peter went up on the roof to pray at noon. He grew hungry, and while food was being prepared he fell into a trance. He saw something like a large sheet lowered by its four corners, with animals, creeping things and birds inside.", "A voice told Peter to kill and eat. He protested that he had never eaten anything common or unclean. The voice told him to stop calling unclean what God had made clean. This happened three times and the sheet was taken up. Peter was still wondering what the vision meant when the men from Caesarea reached the gate and asked for him.", "The Spirit told Peter that three men were looking for him and that he should go with them without hesitation. Downstairs, the men explained who Cornelius was and how an angel had told him to send for Peter. Peter brought them in and gave them lodging. The next day he set out with them, accompanied by some of the believers from Joppa."], "lesson": "Peter has to act on the direction before he fully understands his vision. The visitors at the door turn an unfamiliar lesson into a concrete choice.", "question": "How do Cornelius's messengers help Peter begin to see what the vision concerns?"},
  {"id": 91231, "title": "Peter Enters Cornelius's House", "subtitle": "Receiving people whom God receives", "text": "Acts 10:24–48", "chapters": [[43, 10]], "start": 24, "end": 48, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Cornelius was expecting Peter and had called together his relatives and close friends. When Peter came in, Cornelius fell at his feet. Peter raised him and reminded him that he too was a man. Inside, Peter found many people waiting. He explained that God had shown him he should call no person common or unclean. That was why he had come when they sent for him. He asked Cornelius what had led him to do so.", "Cornelius told the people what had happened as he prayed. The man in shining clothing had spoken of his prayers and gifts and told him where to find Peter. He had sent immediately. Now everyone was there in God's presence to hear what Peter had been commanded to say. Peter began by acknowledging what this visit had taught him. God shows no partiality. In every nation he receives those who fear him and do what is right.", "Peter spoke about Jesus of Nazareth, whom God anointed with the Holy Spirit and power. Jesus went about doing good and healing people, and Peter and the other witnesses had seen his work. People had put him to death on a tree, and God had raised him on the third day. Peter spoke as someone who had eaten and drunk with Jesus after he rose. The prophets bore witness that those who believed in him would receive forgiveness through his name.", "While Peter was still speaking, the Holy Spirit fell on those listening. The Jewish believers who had come with him were astonished to hear Gentiles speaking in tongues and praising God. Peter asked who could refuse water to people who had received the Holy Spirit as they had. He ordered them to be baptised, and they asked him to stay a few days."], "lesson": "Peter's understanding changes through meeting people and seeing what God does among them. He lets that evidence govern his welcome.", "question": "What reason does Peter give for baptising these hearers?"},
  {"id": 91232, "title": "Peter Tells the Whole Story", "subtitle": "Listening before drawing a boundary", "text": "Acts 11:1–18", "chapters": [[43, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The news that Gentiles had received God's word reached the apostles and believers in Judea. When Peter went up to Jerusalem, some of the Jewish believers challenged him for going into a house of uncircumcised men and eating with them. Peter began at the beginning and told them what had happened in order. The people who had heard what he did were about to hear what had led him there.", "He described praying in Joppa, seeing the sheet descend and looking closely at the animals and birds on it. He told them what the voice had commanded and how he had answered. Three times he had been told to stop calling unclean what God had cleansed. As soon as the vision ended, the three men from Caesarea arrived. The Spirit directed him to go with them without making a distinction. Six believers went with him and entered the house.", "Peter told them what the man of the house had reported. An angel had instructed him to send for Peter, who would bring a message by which he and his household would be saved. As Peter began to speak, the Holy Spirit fell on them as on the disciples at the beginning. Peter remembered Jesus' words about John's baptism with water and the baptism with the Holy Spirit.", "God had given these people the same gift he had given the disciples who believed in Jesus Christ. Peter asked what right he had to stand in God's way. The objections ceased. The people who had questioned him now glorified God, acknowledging that he had granted the Gentiles repentance leading to life."], "lesson": "Peter answers the objection with the sequence of what happened. The hearers allow that account to change their judgement.", "question": "What event in Peter's account persuaded the hearers that God was at work?"},
  {"id": 91233, "title": "The Church That Grew in Antioch", "subtitle": "Encouragement that grows into practical help", "text": "Acts 11:19–30", "chapters": [[43, 11]], "start": 19, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When persecution followed Stephen's death, believers travelled as far as Phoenicia, Cyprus and Antioch. At first the people who scattered took the message to Jews alone. Some men from Cyprus and Cyrene came to Antioch and also preached the Lord Jesus to Greek-speaking hearers there. The hand of the Lord was with them. Many believed and turned to him, and word of this reached the church in Jerusalem.", "The church sent Barnabas to Antioch. When he arrived, he saw what God's grace had done and was glad. He urged them all to hold to the Lord with a settled purpose. Luke calls Barnabas a good man, full of the Holy Spirit and faith, and many more people were brought to the Lord. There was work enough for another teacher, so Barnabas went to Tarsus to look for Saul.", "When Barnabas found him, he brought him back to Antioch. For a whole year the two met with the church and taught many people. Luke pauses over a name that would travel beyond this city. It was in Antioch that the disciples were first called Christians.", "In those days prophets came down from Jerusalem, and one of them, Agabus, foretold by the Spirit that a severe famine would spread over the inhabited world. Luke says it came in the days of Claudius. The disciples in Antioch decided each to give according to what he could afford, so the believers in Judea would have help. They sent the relief to the elders through Barnabas and Saul. The people who had welcomed teachers from Jerusalem were now sending help to believers in that region."], "lesson": "Barnabas sees what has begun and brings help to strengthen it. The people who receive teaching in Antioch become people who send relief elsewhere.", "question": "How does Antioch respond to the prospect of hardship in Judea?"},
  {"id": 91234, "title": "Peter at the Gate While the Church Prays", "subtitle": "Making room for an unexpected answer", "text": "Acts 12:1–19", "chapters": [[43, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["King Herod laid hands on members of the church and killed James, the brother of John, with the sword. When he saw that this pleased some of the people, he had Peter arrested during the Feast of Unleavened Bread. Four squads of soldiers took turns guarding him. Herod meant to bring him out after Passover. While Peter was held in prison, the church prayed earnestly for him.", "The night before Herod was to bring him out, Peter slept between two soldiers, bound with two chains, with guards at the door. An angel appeared and light shone in the cell. He struck Peter on the side to wake him and told him to get up quickly. The chains fell away. Peter put on his sandals and cloak and followed, thinking he was seeing a vision. They passed two guard posts. The iron gate to the city opened of itself, and the angel left him after they had gone along one street.", "Peter came to himself and went to the house of Mary, the mother of John Mark, where many people were praying. A servant named Rhoda answered his knock. She recognised Peter's voice and was so glad that she ran inside to tell the others without opening the gate. They thought she was out of her mind. When she insisted, they suggested it was his angel. Peter kept knocking until they opened it and saw him.", "Peter motioned for quiet, told them how the Lord had brought him out and asked them to tell James and the other believers. Then he left for another place. In the morning the soldiers could not account for his disappearance. Herod searched for him, questioned the guards and ordered them executed. Then he went down to Caesarea."], "lesson": "Luke leaves James's death and Peter's deliverance in the same account. The house scene also shows people praying in earnest and struggling to believe a report of deliverance.", "question": "What did Rhoda recognise before the others were willing to believe her?"},
  {"id": 91235, "title": "Herod Receives the Crowd's Praise", "subtitle": "The danger of taking God's honour", "text": "Acts 12:20–25", "chapters": [[43, 12]], "start": 20, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Herod was in Caesarea after his search for Peter. Luke now turns to a dispute that had made the king very angry with the people of Tyre and Sidon. The two cities acted together and came to seek peace. They depended on the king's territory for food, so they had reason to settle the quarrel. They secured the help of Blastus, who was in charge of the king's chamber, and made their request.", "On an appointed day Herod put on his royal garments and sat on his throne to address them. Luke leaves the contents of the speech unrecorded. He tells us what the crowd called out when they heard it. They acclaimed what they were hearing as the voice of a god. The king they had come to appease was sitting before them in his royal robes as the acclamation went up.", "Luke says that immediately an angel of the Lord struck Herod because he had failed to give glory to God. Herod was eaten by worms and died. The king who had ordered the death of James and put Peter in prison came to his own end in Caesarea. After recording that death, Luke tells us that the word of God continued to grow and spread.", "Barnabas and Saul had gone to deliver the help the believers in Antioch had gathered for Judea. They completed that service and returned, taking John Mark with them. We have already heard his name in connection with the house where people prayed for Peter. He now goes with the two men who will soon be sent out from Antioch."], "lesson": "The crowd's dependence gives its praise a setting worth noticing. Herod accepts what should be given to God, and Luke contrasts his end with the spread of the word.", "question": "What reason does Luke give for Herod's judgement?"},
  {"id": 91236, "title": "Sent From Antioch to Cyprus", "subtitle": "Service begun in prayer", "text": "Acts 13:1–12", "chapters": [[43, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The church in Antioch had several prophets and teachers. Luke names Barnabas, Simeon called Niger, Lucius of Cyrene, Manaen, who had been brought up with Herod the tetrarch, and Saul. While they worshipped the Lord and fasted, the Holy Spirit told them to set apart Barnabas and Saul for the work to which he had called them. They fasted and prayed, laid their hands on the two men and sent them off.", "Sent out by the Holy Spirit, Barnabas and Saul went down to Seleucia and sailed from there to Cyprus. At Salamis they proclaimed God's word in the Jewish synagogues. John Mark went with them as their assistant. They crossed the island as far as Paphos, where they met a Jewish sorcerer and false prophet called Bar-Jesus. He was with Sergius Paulus, the Roman governor, whom Luke describes as a discerning man.", "The governor called for Barnabas and Saul because he wanted to hear God's word. The sorcerer, also called Elymas, opposed them and tried to turn him away from the faith. Here Luke tells us that Saul was also called Paul. Filled with the Holy Spirit, Paul looked steadily at Elymas and charged him with twisting the straight ways of the Lord. He told him that the Lord's hand was against him and that for a time he would be blind.", "Mist and darkness fell on Elymas at once. He went about looking for someone to lead him by the hand. Having seen what happened, the governor believed. Luke says he was astonished at the teaching of the Lord, the very teaching he had called the visitors to hear."], "lesson": "The journey begins with worship and shared discernment. Opposition does not prevent the governor from hearing the teaching he asked for.", "question": "What was the church doing when the call to set apart Barnabas and Saul came?"},
  {"id": 91237, "title": "Paul Speaks in Pisidian Antioch", "subtitle": "Hearing the promise and answering it", "text": "Acts 13:13–52", "chapters": [[43, 13]], "start": 13, "end": 52, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul and his companions sailed from Paphos to Perga in Pamphylia. John Mark left them there and returned to Jerusalem. Paul and Barnabas continued inland to Pisidian Antioch. On the Sabbath they sat in the synagogue. After the reading from the Law and the Prophets, the synagogue rulers invited them to speak if they had a word of encouragement. Paul stood and motioned for the people to listen.", "He began with the history they knew. God had chosen Israel's ancestors, brought the people out of Egypt, borne with them in the wilderness and given them their land. Paul spoke of the judges, Saul the king and David. From David's descendants God had brought Israel the promised Saviour, Jesus. John had prepared the way by preaching repentance and had pointed to the one who came after him. Paul said the people of Jerusalem and their rulers had fulfilled the prophets' words in condemning Jesus. They laid him in a tomb, and God raised him.", "Jesus was seen for many days by people who had travelled with him from Galilee. Paul announced that God had fulfilled his promise to their ancestors. He drew on the Psalms to show that Jesus, unlike David, rose without seeing decay. Through Jesus, he said, forgiveness was being proclaimed and everyone who believed was justified in ways the law of Moses could never provide. He ended with a warning from the prophets and was asked to speak again the following Sabbath.", "Almost the whole city gathered the next week. Some who saw the crowd grew jealous and contradicted Paul. He and Barnabas said they had brought the message to their own people first and would now turn to the Gentiles. Many Gentiles rejoiced, and the word spread through the region. When opponents stirred up influential people and drove the two men out, they shook the dust from their feet and went to Iconium. The disciples were filled with joy and with the Holy Spirit."], "lesson": "Paul gives his hearers a history in which the promise has reached them. Luke records both the joy of those who receive it and the resistance of those who see others responding.", "question": "What does Paul announce that his hearers can receive through Jesus?"},
  {"id": 91238, "title": "From Applause to Stones and Back to the Churches", "subtitle": "Steadiness when a crowd changes its mind", "text": "Acts 14:1–28", "chapters": [[43, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["In Iconium, Paul and Barnabas spoke in the synagogue and many Jews and Greeks believed. Other hearers stirred up opposition, and the city divided over them. The two stayed a long time and spoke boldly, and the Lord confirmed the message of his grace through signs. When they learned that people on the opposing side planned to mistreat and stone them, they fled to Lystra and Derbe and continued preaching there.", "At Lystra a man who had never walked listened to Paul. Paul saw that he had faith to be healed and called out for him to stand. The man sprang up and walked. The crowd cried out in the Lycaonian language that gods had come down to them. They called Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes, since Paul did most of the speaking. The priest of Zeus brought oxen and garlands to the gates to offer a sacrifice with the crowd.", "When Paul and Barnabas heard what was happening, they tore their clothes and rushed in among the people. They told them they were fellow human beings and urged them to turn to the living God. He made heaven and earth and had given them rain, crops and food. Even with those words the two had trouble stopping the sacrifice. Later opponents arrived from Antioch and Iconium and won the crowd over. Paul was stoned and dragged out of the city for dead. When the disciples gathered around him, he got up and went back into the city.", "The next day he and Barnabas went to Derbe and made many disciples. On their return they went back through Lystra, Iconium and Antioch to strengthen the believers. They warned them that entering God's kingdom would involve hardship and appointed elders in each church with prayer and fasting. They passed through Pisidia, preached at Perga and sailed from Attalia. At the Antioch from which they had been sent, they gathered the church and reported what God had done, including how he had opened a door of faith to the Gentiles."], "lesson": "Public praise and public hostility can arise close together. The return visit shows where the missionaries place their responsibility even after rejection.", "question": "What do Paul and Barnabas do for the young churches when they pass through again?"},
  {"id": 91239, "title": "The Dispute Brought to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Listening to testimony and Scripture", "text": "Acts 15:1–21", "chapters": [[43, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Some men came down from Judea to Antioch and taught that Gentile believers had to be circumcised according to the custom of Moses to be saved. Paul and Barnabas disputed with them at length. The church sent the two men and others to Jerusalem to put the question before the apostles and elders. On the journey they told believers in Phoenicia and Samaria how Gentiles had turned to God, which brought great joy to those who heard.", "In Jerusalem the church, apostles and elders welcomed them. Paul and Barnabas reported what God had done through them. Some believers who belonged to the party of the Pharisees rose and insisted that the Gentiles should be circumcised and told to keep the law of Moses. The apostles and elders gathered to consider it. After much discussion, Peter recalled how God had chosen him to bring the good news to Gentile hearers.", "God knew their hearts and had given them the Holy Spirit as he had given him to the Jewish believers. He had cleansed their hearts by faith. Peter asked why they would put a burden on these disciples that their own ancestors had been unable to bear. They believed that both groups would be saved through the grace of the Lord Jesus. The gathering fell silent and listened to Barnabas and Paul tell of the signs God had done among the Gentiles.", "When they finished, James took up Peter's account and showed how it agreed with the prophets. He recalled the promise of David's fallen dwelling being rebuilt so that others, including Gentiles called by God's name, might seek the Lord. James proposed sending instructions to abstain from what was polluted by idols, sexual immorality, meat from strangled animals and blood. He pointed out that Moses was read every Sabbath in the synagogues."], "lesson": "The gathering hears experience, attends to Scripture and considers a burden people are being asked to bear. Its judgement follows a public exchange.", "question": "What did Peter say God had already done among the Gentile believers?"},
  {"id": 91240, "title": "A Letter Delivered and Two Friends Part Ways", "subtitle": "Careful words when agreement is hard", "text": "Acts 15:22–41", "chapters": [[43, 15]], "start": 22, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The apostles and elders, with the whole church in Jerusalem, chose Judas Barsabas and Silas to go to Antioch with Paul and Barnabas. They carried a letter to the Gentile believers in Antioch, Syria and Cilicia. The letter said that the people who had troubled them had gone out without instructions from Jerusalem. Judas and Silas would confirm in person what the church had decided to write.", "The letter said that it had seemed good to the Holy Spirit and to them to lay only these requirements on the believers. They should abstain from food offered to idols, blood, meat from strangled animals and sexual immorality. When the messengers gathered the believers in Antioch and delivered the letter, the people rejoiced at its encouragement. Judas and Silas, who were prophets, spoke at length to strengthen them. After some time the messengers were dismissed in peace. Paul and Barnabas remained in Antioch, teaching and preaching with many others.", "After a while Paul suggested going back to the towns where they had preached to see how the believers were doing. Barnabas wanted to take John Mark. Paul thought they should leave him behind because he had withdrawn in Pamphylia and had gone no farther with them in the work. The disagreement grew sharp enough that the two men parted company.", "Barnabas took Mark and sailed to Cyprus. Paul chose Silas and left after the believers commended him to the Lord's grace. He travelled through Syria and Cilicia, strengthening the churches. The agreement carried from Jerusalem had brought relief to Antioch. Now the two men who had carried it together were following different routes."], "lesson": "The letter shows the care needed to convey a difficult decision fairly. The personal dispute also shows that devoted workers can part without the work ending.", "question": "How did the Jerusalem church make sure Antioch received its decision and encouragement clearly?"},
  {"id": 91241, "title": "Turned Towards Macedonia", "subtitle": "Guidance and a door opened in Philippi", "text": "Acts 16:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul came to Derbe and Lystra, the towns he had visited with Barnabas. At Lystra he met Timothy, the son of a Jewish believer and a Greek father. The believers in Lystra and Iconium spoke well of Timothy, and Paul wanted him to come along. He circumcised him because the Jews in those places knew his father was Greek. As the group travelled, they delivered the decisions the apostles and elders had made in Jerusalem. The churches grew stronger in faith and increased in number daily.", "The travellers passed through Phrygia and Galatia after the Holy Spirit kept them from speaking the word in Asia. Near Mysia they tried to go into Bithynia and were prevented again. They passed by Mysia and came down to Troas. During the night Paul saw a man from Macedonia in a vision, standing and pleading for him to come over and help them. Luke now speaks of what we did. The group concluded that God had called them to preach there and sought passage at once.", "They sailed from Troas by way of Samothrace and Neapolis and came to Philippi, a Roman colony in Macedonia. On the Sabbath they went outside the city gate to a riverside place where they expected to find people praying. They sat and spoke to the women who had gathered there.", "One of the women was Lydia, a dealer in purple cloth from Thyatira who worshipped God. As she listened, the Lord opened her heart to attend to Paul's words. She and her household were baptised. Then she urged the travellers to stay at her house if they judged her faithful to the Lord. She prevailed on them, and a journey that had begun with a call for help brought them into a home opened to them."], "lesson": "The group's direction changes through closed routes and a new invitation. The first meeting Luke describes takes place beside a river among people already gathered to pray.", "question": "How does Lydia put hospitality into practice after hearing the message?"},
  {"id": 91242, "title": "Songs at Midnight", "subtitle": "Mercy when the prison door opens", "text": "Acts 16:16–40", "chapters": [[43, 16]], "start": 16, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["A slave girl with a spirit of divination followed Paul and his companions in Philippi for many days. Her owners earned money through her predictions. She kept calling out that the men were servants of the Most High God who were showing the way of salvation. After hearing her repeatedly, Paul commanded the spirit to come out in Jesus' name. When the owners saw that their hope of profit was gone, they took Paul and Silas before the magistrates. The men were beaten and put into the inner prison, their feet secured in stocks.", "At midnight they prayed and sang, and the other prisoners listened. An earthquake shook the foundations. The doors opened and the prisoners' restraints came loose. The jailer woke, saw the open doors and assumed the prisoners had escaped. He drew his sword, but Paul cried out to stop him, saying that everyone was still there.", "The jailer called for a light, came in trembling and brought Paul and Silas out. He asked what he must do to be saved. They told him to believe in the Lord Jesus and spoke the word to his household. That same night he washed their wounds. He and his household were baptised, and he set food before the men he had been guarding.", "In the morning the magistrates sent word to release them. Paul pointed out that they had been beaten publicly without trial although they were Roman citizens. The magistrates came in person and asked them to leave the city. Before leaving, Paul and Silas visited Lydia's house and encouraged the believers gathered there."], "lesson": "Paul pays attention to the jailer's danger when escape seems possible. The man who was guarding them then uses his freedom to care for their wounds.", "question": "What do Paul and the jailer each do to help someone in danger or pain?"},
  {"id": 91243, "title": "Two Towns Hear the Scriptures Opened", "subtitle": "Examining a message carefully", "text": "Acts 17:1–15", "chapters": [[43, 17]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul and Silas passed through Amphipolis and Apollonia and came to Thessalonica. Paul went to the synagogue, as was his custom. Over three Sabbaths he reasoned from the Scriptures, explaining why the Christ had to suffer and rise from the dead. He told them that the Jesus he proclaimed was this Christ. Some of the Jews were persuaded, along with many Greeks who worshipped God and a number of prominent women.", "Others grew jealous. They gathered men from the marketplace, formed a mob and set the city in an uproar. They went to Jason's house looking for Paul and Silas. When they could not find them, they dragged Jason and some of the believers before the city officials. They said the visitors had caused trouble everywhere and that Jason had welcomed them. The charge that most disturbed the city was that these people proclaimed another king, Jesus, in defiance of Caesar's decrees. The officials took security from Jason and the others and let them go.", "The believers sent Paul and Silas away by night to Berea. There the two went to the synagogue. The people received the message eagerly and examined the Scriptures every day to see whether Paul's claims held up. Luke commends the way they listened and looked for themselves. Many believed, including prominent Greek women and men.", "When people from Thessalonica heard that Paul was preaching in Berea too, they came and stirred up the crowds there. The believers sent Paul away toward the sea while Silas and Timothy remained. His escorts took him as far as Athens and left carrying his request that the two join him as soon as they could."], "lesson": "The Bereans combine readiness to hear with steady examination. Their example gives both openness and care a place in studying a new claim.", "question": "What did the Bereans do daily after receiving Paul's message?"},
  {"id": 91244, "title": "An Altar in Athens", "subtitle": "Speaking to people from where they are", "text": "Acts 17:16–34", "chapters": [[43, 17]], "start": 16, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["While Paul waited in Athens, he was troubled by how full the city was of idols. He reasoned in the synagogue with Jews and others who worshipped God. In the marketplace he spoke daily with whoever happened to be there. Epicurean and Stoic philosophers joined the discussion. Some dismissed him as a collector of scraps of talk, and others thought he was introducing foreign gods because he preached Jesus and the resurrection.", "They brought him to the Areopagus and asked what his new teaching meant. Paul began with something he had seen among the city's objects of worship, an altar dedicated to an unknown god. He told them he would speak about the One they worshipped without knowing. The God who made the world and everything in it is Lord of heaven and earth. He gives people life and breath and has no need of anything human hands can supply.", "Paul said that God had made every nation from one human origin and determined their times and boundaries so they might seek him. God was near to each one. Paul quoted their own poets to help them follow his point about being God's offspring. If that was so, an image made from gold, silver or stone by human skill was an inadequate likeness for God.", "God now called people everywhere to repent. Paul said a day had been set when God would judge the world justly through the man he had appointed. He had given assurance of this by raising him from the dead. Some mocked when they heard of resurrection. Others wanted to hear Paul again. A few joined him and believed, including Dionysius of the Areopagus and a woman named Damaris."], "lesson": "Paul notices what his hearers already recognise and moves towards what they have yet to hear. The resurrection remains central even when it divides the audience.", "question": "How does the altar give Paul a starting point for what he says about God?"},
  {"id": 91245, "title": "A Workshop and a Long Stay in Corinth", "subtitle": "Courage for steady work", "text": "Acts 18:1–17", "chapters": [[43, 18]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul left Athens for Corinth. There he met Aquila, a Jew from Pontus, and his wife Priscilla. They had recently come from Italy because Claudius had ordered Jews to leave Rome. They shared Paul's trade as tentmakers, so he stayed and worked with them. On Sabbaths he went to the synagogue and tried to persuade both Jews and Greeks. When Silas and Timothy arrived from Macedonia, Paul devoted himself to testifying that Jesus was the Christ.", "Some of his hearers fiercely opposed him. Paul shook out his clothes and said that from then on he would go to the Gentiles. He moved to the house of a worshipper of God named Justus, right next door to the synagogue. Crispus, the synagogue ruler, believed in the Lord with his whole household. Many Corinthians who heard the message believed and were baptised.", "One night the Lord told Paul in a vision to keep speaking without fear. He was with Paul, and nobody would attack him to do him harm, for he had many people in the city. Paul stayed there a year and six months, teaching the word of God among them. What began with work in a shared trade became a long stay among people learning to follow Christ.", "When Gallio was governor of Achaia, Paul's opponents brought him before the judgment seat. They accused him of persuading people to worship God against the law. Before Paul could answer, Gallio said he would have heard a case involving a crime. Questions about words, names and their own law were for them to settle. He drove them away from the judgment seat. Sosthenes, the synagogue ruler, was beaten in front of it, and Gallio took no notice."], "lesson": "The assurance Paul receives leads to a long period of ordinary teaching. His work in Corinth also has a place for a trade and the companionship of fellow workers.", "question": "What does the Lord tell Paul to keep doing, and how long does Paul remain?"},
  {"id": 91246, "title": "Priscilla and Aquila Hear Apollos", "subtitle": "Correction given with care", "text": "Acts 18:18–28", "chapters": [[43, 18]], "start": 18, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul stayed in Corinth a good while longer before taking leave of the believers and sailing for Syria. Priscilla and Aquila went with him. At Cenchreae Paul had his hair cut because he was under a vow. When they reached Ephesus, Paul went to the synagogue and reasoned with the Jews there. They asked him to stay longer, but he took leave and said he would return if God willed. Priscilla and Aquila remained in Ephesus.", "Paul sailed to Caesarea, went up and greeted the church and then went down to Antioch. After spending some time there, he set out again. He went from place to place in Galatia and Phrygia, strengthening the disciples. While Paul was away, another teacher arrived in Ephesus.", "Apollos was a Jew from Alexandria, an eloquent man with a strong knowledge of the Scriptures. He had been instructed in the way of the Lord. With fervour he spoke and taught accurately what he knew about Jesus, though he knew only John's baptism. Apollos began to speak boldly in the synagogue. Priscilla and Aquila heard him, took him aside and explained the way of God to him more fully.", "When Apollos wanted to cross over to Achaia, the believers encouraged him and wrote to the disciples there asking them to welcome him. On arrival he was a great help to those who had believed through grace. He answered his opponents in public and showed from the Scriptures that Jesus was the Christ. The Scriptures he had brought to Ephesus were still at the heart of what he had to say in Achaia."], "lesson": "Priscilla and Aquila give Apollos the instruction he needs in a setting where he can hear it. His existing gifts then serve more fully.", "question": "How does the couple approach Apollos when they recognise a gap in his understanding?"},
  {"id": 91247, "title": "A Name and a Changed Way of Life", "subtitle": "Repentance with a real cost", "text": "Acts 19:1–20", "chapters": [[43, 19]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["While Apollos was in Corinth, Paul travelled through the inland regions and came to Ephesus. There he met about twelve disciples and asked whether they had received the Holy Spirit when they believed. They had not even heard of the Holy Spirit and knew only John's baptism. Paul explained that John had baptised people who repented and directed them to believe in the one coming after him, Jesus. They were baptised in the name of the Lord Jesus. When Paul laid his hands on them, the Holy Spirit came upon them and they spoke in tongues and prophesied.", "For three months Paul spoke boldly in the synagogue about God's kingdom. When some hardened against the message and spoke against the Way in public, he took the disciples with him and taught daily in the hall of Tyrannus. This continued for two years, and word of the Lord reached Jews and Greeks throughout the province of Asia. God worked extraordinary miracles through Paul. Even cloths that had touched him were carried to the sick, and illnesses and evil spirits left them.", "Some travelling Jewish exorcists tried using the name of the Jesus Paul preached. Seven sons of Sceva were doing this when an evil spirit answered that it knew Jesus and knew about Paul and asked who they were. The man overpowered them, and they fled the house wounded and stripped of their clothes. The account became widely known in Ephesus, and people held Jesus' name in honour.", "Many believers came forward and confessed what they had done. People who had practised magic gathered their books and burned them publicly. The books were valued at fifty thousand pieces of silver. Luke ends the account by saying that the word of the Lord grew and prevailed."], "lesson": "Luke distinguishes a formula borrowed for power from a response that reshapes conduct. The cost of giving up the books makes the change visible.", "question": "What did the people give up when they openly acknowledged their former practices?"},
  {"id": 91248, "title": "The Crowd in the Theatre", "subtitle": "Recognising the interests behind a protest", "text": "Acts 19:21–41", "chapters": [[43, 19]], "start": 21, "end": 41, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul decided to go through Macedonia and Achaia on his way to Jerusalem. After Jerusalem he meant to see Rome. He sent Timothy and Erastus ahead to Macedonia and stayed in Asia for a time. While he was still in Ephesus, a serious disturbance broke out over the Way. Demetrius, a silversmith, made silver shrines of Artemis, also known as Diana. The work brought substantial business to craftsmen in the city.", "Demetrius gathered the workers and reminded them that their income came from this trade. Paul had persuaded people across Ephesus and much of Asia that things made by human hands were no gods. Demetrius warned them that their trade might fall into disrepute and that the great temple of Artemis might lose its standing. The men grew angry and began shouting praise to Artemis. The commotion spread, and people rushed into the theatre, dragging along Gaius and Aristarchus, two Macedonians travelling with Paul.", "Paul wanted to go in among the crowd. The disciples held him back, and some provincial officials who were his friends sent word urging him to stay out. Inside, people shouted different things. Most had no idea why they were there. Some put Alexander forward, but when the crowd realised he was Jewish, they shouted in praise of Artemis for about two hours.", "The city clerk finally quieted them. He said the men they had dragged in had neither robbed temples nor spoken against their goddess. Demetrius and the craftsmen could take any charge to the courts. Other matters could be handled in a lawful assembly. As things stood, the city risked being called to account for a riot it could give no reason for. Then he dismissed the crowd."], "lesson": "The protest begins with a particular financial interest and becomes a gathering many cannot explain. Luke gives a reason to examine what moves us before joining a crowd.", "question": "What concerns did Demetrius raise before the crowd began to assemble?"},
  {"id": 91249, "title": "The Young Man at the Window", "subtitle": "Care in an interrupted gathering", "text": "Acts 20:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 20]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the uproar in Ephesus had ended, Paul called the disciples together, encouraged them and left for Macedonia. He went through those districts speaking to the believers before coming to Greece, where he spent three months. He was about to sail for Syria when he learned of a plot against him, so he went back through Macedonia. Several companions went ahead to Troas. Luke says the rest sailed from Philippi after the Feast of Unleavened Bread, reached Troas in five days and stayed a week.", "On the first day of the week they gathered to break bread. Paul was leaving the next day and kept speaking until midnight. Many lamps were burning in the upstairs room. A young man named Eutychus was sitting in the window. As Paul went on speaking, Eutychus sank into a deep sleep and fell from the third storey. When people lifted him, he was dead.", "Paul went downstairs, bent over Eutychus and took him in his arms. He told the people to stop being alarmed because the young man was alive. Then Paul went back upstairs, broke bread and ate. He talked with the group until daybreak and left as planned. They took Eutychus away alive and were greatly comforted.", "Paul's companions went ahead by ship to Assos, where Paul, who had gone on foot, joined them. They sailed by way of Mitylene, Chios and Samos to Miletus. Paul decided to pass Ephesus without stopping. He was trying, if possible, to reach Jerusalem by Pentecost."], "lesson": "The gathering stops for a person in danger. Luke remembers the community's comfort alongside the teaching and the journey.", "question": "How does the gathering respond after Eutychus falls?"},
  {"id": 91250, "title": "Paul Says Farewell at Miletus", "subtitle": "Shepherding with humility and vigilance", "text": "Acts 20:17–38", "chapters": [[43, 20]], "start": 17, "end": 38, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["From Miletus Paul sent to Ephesus for the elders of the church. When they came, he asked them to remember how he had lived among them from his first day in Asia. They had seen him serve the Lord humbly, with tears and through danger. He had taught them publicly and from house to house. To Jews and Greeks alike he had urged repentance toward God and faith in the Lord Jesus.", "Now he was bound in spirit for Jerusalem, uncertain what would happen there. The Holy Spirit had warned him in city after city that imprisonment and hardship awaited him. Paul was intent on finishing the work the Lord Jesus had given him, bearing witness to the gospel of God's grace. He told the elders that they would see his face no more. He could say that he had held back none of God's counsel from them.", "Paul told the elders to watch over themselves and the flock in their care. The Holy Spirit had made them overseers to shepherd the church. After his departure, fierce wolves would come in, and even men from their own group would twist the truth to draw disciples after themselves. He asked them to remember how, for three years, he had warned them night and day with tears. Now he entrusted them to God and the word of his grace.", "Paul reminded them that his hands had supplied what he and his companions needed. They too should labour to help the weak, remembering that Jesus called giving more blessed than receiving. When he finished, he knelt and prayed with them. They wept, embraced and kissed him, grieved especially at his saying they would see him no more. Then they went with him to the ship."], "lesson": "Paul puts responsibilities before the elders which his own conduct has illustrated. Their oversight should attend to threats and to people who need support.", "question": "Which patterns from Paul's own work does he ask the elders to remember?"},
  {"id": 91251, "title": "Warnings on the Way to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Courage with consideration for others", "text": "Acts 21:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 21]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["After the farewell at Miletus, the travellers sailed by way of Cos, Rhodes and Patara. They found a ship for Phoenicia, passed Cyprus and landed at Tyre, where the ship was unloading. They found the disciples and stayed seven days. The disciples, speaking through the Spirit, urged Paul against going to Jerusalem. When it was time to leave, the believers came out with their wives and children. They all knelt on the shore and prayed before the travellers boarded.", "They went on through Ptolemais to Caesarea and stayed with Philip the evangelist, one of the Seven. His four unmarried daughters prophesied. During the visit a prophet named Agabus came from Judea. He took Paul's belt, bound his own feet and hands and said the Holy Spirit had shown him that the owner would be bound in Jerusalem and handed over to the Gentiles. Paul's companions and the local believers pleaded with him to stay away.", "Paul asked why their weeping was breaking his heart. He was ready to be bound in Jerusalem and even to die there for the name of the Lord Jesus. When they could no longer persuade him, they left the outcome to the Lord's will. They went up to Jerusalem, where the believers received them gladly. The next day Paul met James and the elders and told them in detail what God had done among the Gentiles through his ministry.", "The elders glorified God and told Paul that many Jewish believers had heard reports that he taught Jews living among the Gentiles to abandon Moses. They advised him to join four men in their purification and pay their expenses, so people could see how he lived. They also reaffirmed what had been decided about Gentile believers. Paul took the men, purified himself with them and went into the temple to give notice of when the days of purification would end."], "lesson": "Paul faces personal danger and is also willing to take steps that address the concerns of other believers. His resolve leaves room for hearing their counsel.", "question": "What different concerns do Paul's companions and the Jerusalem elders bring to him?"},
  {"id": 91252, "title": "From the Temple Steps Paul Tells His Story", "subtitle": "Witness in the language people understand", "text": "Acts 21:27–22:21", "chapters": [[43, 21], [43, 22]], "start": 27, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When the seven days of purification were almost over, Jews from Asia saw Paul in the temple. They stirred up the crowd by accusing him of teaching against their people, their law and the temple, and of bringing Greeks inside. Luke explains that they had seen Trophimus the Ephesian with Paul in the city and assumed Paul had brought him into the temple. The crowd dragged Paul out, and the gates were shut behind him.", "They were trying to kill him when the Roman commander heard that Jerusalem was in an uproar. Soldiers ran down, and the crowd stopped beating Paul. The commander had him bound with two chains and asked who he was and what he had done. People shouted different answers, so he ordered Paul taken into the barracks. On the steps the soldiers had to carry him because the crowd pressed against them shouting for his death.", "Paul spoke to the commander in Greek and corrected the impression that he was an Egyptian who had led a revolt. Paul said he was a Jew from Tarsus and asked to speak to the people. The commander agreed. Paul motioned from the steps, the crowd quieted, and he addressed them in their own Hebrew speech. He said he had been brought up in Jerusalem, trained under Gamaliel and zealous for God. He had persecuted the Way and set out for Damascus with authority to bring its followers back in chains.", "Near Damascus a great light struck him down, and Jesus identified himself as the one Paul was persecuting. Blinded, Paul was led into the city, where Ananias came, restored his sight and told him he would be a witness. Ananias urged him to be baptised and call on the Lord. Paul then told of praying later in the temple in Jerusalem. Jesus had warned him to leave the city and told him he would send him far away to the Gentiles."], "lesson": "Paul opens with ground his hearers know and explains how his own life changed. He lets the costly part of his calling remain part of the account.", "question": "Which facts about his earlier life does Paul give before he tells what happened near Damascus?"},
  {"id": 91253, "title": "An Unanswered Question About Citizenship", "subtitle": "Using a lawful protection truthfully", "text": "Acts 22:22–30", "chapters": [[43, 22]], "start": 22, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The crowd listened until Paul said that Jesus had sent him to the Gentiles. Then they raised their voices and cried that he should be put to death. People threw off their cloaks and tossed dust into the air. The Roman commander, who had already heard conflicting shouts about Paul on the steps, ordered him taken inside. He meant to find out why the crowd was shouting this way by having Paul questioned under the lash.", "As they stretched Paul out with straps, he spoke to the centurion standing nearby. He asked whether it was lawful to flog a Roman citizen who had never been condemned. The centurion went straight to the commander and told him to consider what he was about to do. Paul was a Roman citizen, and the question he had asked could not be brushed aside.", "The commander came to Paul and asked him directly whether he was a Roman. Paul said he was. The commander said he had paid a large sum for his own citizenship, and Paul replied that he had been born a citizen. Those about to question him withdrew immediately. The commander was afraid when he realised whom he had bound.", "The commander still wanted to learn the charge against Paul. The next day he released him from his bonds and ordered the chief priests and the whole council to assemble. He brought Paul down and set him before them. The planned flogging had ended with a question from the prisoner. Now the people who had accused him would have to speak in a setting where the commander could hear them."], "lesson": "Paul's willingness to suffer leaves room for a truthful appeal to a legal protection. His question gives the officers a chance to stop what they are about to do.", "question": "What fact changes the course of the examination?"},
  {"id": 91254, "title": "The Council Divides Over Resurrection", "subtitle": "Courage for the next place of witness", "text": "Acts 23:1–11", "chapters": [[43, 23]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Paul looked steadily at the council and said he had lived before God with a good conscience to that day. The high priest Ananias ordered those standing near him to strike him on the mouth. Paul called Ananias a whitewashed wall and asked how a man sitting to judge him by the law could order him struck in breach of it. The people standing nearby asked whether he would speak that way to God's high priest.", "Paul said he had not known that Ananias was the high priest. He acknowledged the command in Scripture against speaking evil of a ruler of the people. Then he took account of the men before him. Some were Sadducees and others Pharisees. Paul called out that he was a Pharisee, a son of Pharisees, and that he was on trial for the hope of the resurrection of the dead.", "The council divided at once. The Sadducees denied resurrection, angels and spirits, while the Pharisees acknowledged them. Some scribes on the Pharisees' side stood up and said they found no wrong in Paul. They raised the possibility that a spirit or an angel had spoken to him. The dispute grew so violent that the Roman commander feared Paul would be torn apart. He ordered soldiers to take him from among them and bring him back into the barracks.", "The following night the Lord stood by Paul and told him to take courage. He had borne witness in Jerusalem, and he must bear witness in Rome as well. Paul was still in the barracks where the soldiers had brought him. By morning others would be making plans for his life, and he already had the Lord's word about where his testimony would go."], "lesson": "The Lord names a future place of witness while Paul is still in custody. The encouragement gives him a direction before he can see how he will get there.", "question": "What does the Lord say about Paul's witness in Jerusalem and in Rome?"},
  {"id": 91255, "title": "A Young Man Hears of the Plot", "subtitle": "The quiet value of reporting danger", "text": "Acts 23:12–35", "chapters": [[43, 23]], "start": 12, "end": 35, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["The next morning more than forty men bound themselves by an oath to eat and drink nothing until they had killed Paul. They told the chief priests and elders and asked them to arrange for the commander to bring Paul down again. The request would appear to be for a closer inquiry. The men meant to kill him before he reached the council.", "Paul's sister's son heard about the ambush and went into the barracks to tell him. Paul called a centurion and asked him to take the young man to the commander. The commander took him by the hand, drew him aside and asked privately what he had to report. After hearing the plan, he sent the young man away with instructions to tell nobody that he had reported it.", "The commander called two centurions and ordered an escort of two hundred soldiers, seventy horsemen and two hundred spearmen to be ready by nine that evening. He provided mounts for Paul and sent him to Felix, the governor. In his letter the commander, Claudius Lysias, said the dispute concerned questions of the Jewish law and that he had found no charge deserving death or imprisonment. He explained that he had sent Paul to Felix after learning of the plot and had directed Paul's accusers to make their case there.", "The soldiers took Paul by night as far as Antipatris. The next day the horsemen went on to Caesarea while the rest returned to the barracks. Felix read the letter and asked where Paul was from. When he learned it was Cilicia, he said he would hear Paul once the accusers arrived. Then he ordered Paul kept under guard in Herod's headquarters."], "lesson": "The nephew cannot organise an escort himself. He does what is within reach by bringing information to someone able to act on it.", "question": "What sequence brings the nephew's warning to the commander?"},
  {"id": 91256, "title": "Felix Listens and Delays", "subtitle": "Hearing truth that asks for a response", "text": "Acts 24:1–27", "chapters": [[43, 24]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Five days later the high priest Ananias came to Caesarea with some elders and an advocate named Tertullus. Tertullus praised Felix for the peace he said the governor had brought. Then he charged Paul with stirring up trouble among Jews throughout the world, leading the sect of the Nazarenes and attempting to profane the temple. The others joined in affirming his account.", "At Felix's signal Paul answered. He had been in Jerusalem only twelve days since going there to worship, and nobody had found him arguing in the temple or stirring up a crowd. He acknowledged that he worshipped the God of his ancestors according to the Way. He believed what was written in the Law and the Prophets and hoped for the resurrection of the righteous and the unrighteous. He tried to keep a clear conscience before God and people.", "Paul explained that he had come to bring gifts for his people and present offerings. Some Jews from Asia had found him in the temple after he had purified himself, with no crowd around him. Paul said that those men ought to be present if they had a charge. He asked whether the people who were there could name any wrongdoing beyond his declaring before the council that he was on trial over the resurrection. Felix put off his decision until Lysias might come down. He kept Paul in custody with some freedom and let his friends attend to him.", "Later Felix came with his wife Drusilla, who was Jewish, and heard Paul speak about faith in Christ. When Paul spoke about righteousness, self-control and judgment to come, Felix grew afraid and sent him away, saying he would call again when he found a convenient time. He did send for him often, hoping Paul would offer money. Two years passed, and Felix left him in prison when Festus succeeded him, wishing to gain favour with Paul's accusers."], "lesson": "Felix's alarm shows that the words reached him. His repeated delays show how an opportunity to act can be lost while a person waits for convenience.", "question": "What does Felix do when Paul's teaching alarms him?"},
  {"id": 91257, "title": "Paul Appeals to Caesar", "subtitle": "Making a clear appeal under pressure", "text": "Acts 25:1–27", "chapters": [[43, 25]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Three days after taking up his post, Festus went from Caesarea to Jerusalem. The chief priests and leading men brought their case against Paul and urged Festus to have him transferred. They planned to ambush him on the way. Festus said Paul was being held in Caesarea and invited their leaders to go down and bring any charges there. When Festus returned to Caesarea, he took his seat in court the next day and had Paul brought in.", "Paul's accusers surrounded him and brought serious charges they could not prove. Paul said he had committed no offence against the Jewish law, the temple or Caesar. Festus, wanting to do the accusers a favour, asked Paul whether he would go to Jerusalem for trial. Paul answered that he was already before Caesar's tribunal, where he ought to be tried. If he had committed a crime deserving death, he would accept the penalty. Since the charges had no foundation, nobody could hand him over. He appealed to Caesar, and Festus accepted after consulting his advisers.", "Some days later King Agrippa and Bernice came to greet Festus. Festus described a prisoner left by Felix and charges that had surprised him. The dispute concerned the accusers' religion and a man named Jesus, who had died and whom Paul said was alive. Festus had been at a loss over how to inquire into such matters. Paul had appealed, and Festus was holding him for the emperor. Agrippa asked to hear him.", "The next day Agrippa and Bernice entered the audience hall with great ceremony, accompanied by officers and prominent men of Caesarea. Festus had Paul brought in. He told the gathering that he had found nothing in Paul's conduct deserving death. The appeal required him to send the prisoner to the emperor, and he had no clear charge to write. He hoped the hearing would give him something definite to report."], "lesson": "Paul gives a direct answer about the proper place for his hearing. The appeal provides a route forward when the proposed transfer exposes him to danger.", "question": "What prompts Paul to appeal, and what problem does Festus still face after accepting it?"},
  {"id": 91258, "title": "Paul Speaks Before Agrippa", "subtitle": "Telling the truth about a changed life", "text": "Acts 26:1–32", "chapters": [[43, 26]], "start": 1, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Agrippa gave Paul permission to speak, and Paul stretched out his hand to begin. He was glad to answer before someone familiar with Jewish customs and disputes. He had lived as a Pharisee and now stood on trial over the promise God had made to their ancestors. The twelve tribes served God earnestly in hope of that promise. Paul asked why anyone there thought it incredible that God raises the dead.", "Paul knew what it was to oppose the name of Jesus of Nazareth. He had put believers in prison, supported their execution and pursued them even to foreign cities. He was on such a journey to Damascus when a light brighter than the sun shone around him and his companions at midday. They fell to the ground. Jesus spoke to Paul and told him he was being appointed a servant and witness. He would be sent to open people's eyes, so they could turn from darkness to light and receive forgiveness through faith in Jesus.", "Paul said he obeyed the vision. In Damascus, Jerusalem, Judea and among the Gentiles, he called people to repent, turn to God and show their repentance by what they did. This was what he had been doing when he was seized in the temple. God had helped him to that day. His testimony agreed with Moses and the prophets about the Christ suffering, rising from the dead and bringing light to Israel and the Gentiles.", "Festus interrupted and cried that Paul's learning had driven him mad. Paul answered respectfully and appealed to Agrippa's knowledge of the prophets. Agrippa asked whether Paul expected to persuade him so quickly. Paul said he prayed that everyone listening might become as he was, apart from his chains. After the hearing the officials agreed that Paul had done nothing deserving death or imprisonment. Agrippa told Festus he could have been released if he had not appealed to Caesar."], "lesson": "Paul describes what he once did without excusing it. His account connects the direction of his present life to the word he received.", "question": "What did Paul begin preaching after the encounter he describes to Agrippa?"},
  {"id": 91259, "title": "A Ship Driven Out of Sight of Land", "subtitle": "Hope spoken in danger", "text": "Acts 27:1–26", "chapters": [[43, 27]], "start": 1, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["When it was decided that they would sail for Italy, Paul and other prisoners were put in the charge of Julius, a centurion of the Augustan cohort. Luke records the journey as one he made himself, and Aristarchus from Thessalonica was with them too. At Sidon, Julius kindly allowed Paul to visit friends who could care for him. Adverse winds took the ship under the shelter of Cyprus. At Myra, Julius transferred the party to an Alexandrian ship bound for Italy.", "They made slow progress and reached Fair Havens on Crete with difficulty. The season was already dangerous for sailing. Paul warned that going on would put the ship, cargo and lives at risk. Julius gave more weight to the pilot and shipowner. Most on board wanted to reach Phoenix, a more suitable harbour where they could spend the winter. When a gentle south wind blew, they weighed anchor and sailed close to Crete.", "A violent wind swept down from the island and drove the ship away. In the shelter of a small island the crew managed with difficulty to secure the boat and bind the ship with ropes. Fearing the sandbanks of Syrtis, they lowered their gear and let the vessel drift. They threw cargo overboard and later threw out the ship's tackle. Days passed without a sight of the sun or stars, and hope of survival faded.", "Paul stood among them after they had gone a long time without food. He reminded them of his warning and urged them to take heart. An angel of the God he served had stood by him that night and told him he must appear before Caesar. God had granted him the lives of all those sailing with him. The ship would be lost, and they would run aground on an island. Paul told them he trusted God to do what he had said."], "lesson": "Paul gives the crew a specific reason for hope while the conditions remain dangerous. His assurance concerns the people on the ship as well as his own future.", "question": "What does Paul tell the people will be lost, and what does he say will be spared?"},
  {"id": 91260, "title": "Bread Before the Shipwreck", "subtitle": "Encouragement joined to practical care", "text": "Acts 27:27–44", "chapters": [[43, 27]], "start": 27, "end": 44, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["On the fourteenth night at sea, the sailors sensed that land was near. Their soundings showed the water was getting shallower. Fearing rocks, they dropped four anchors from the stern and waited for daylight. Some tried to lower the boat under the pretence of setting anchors from the bow. Paul warned the centurion that the men needed to stay aboard, so the soldiers cut the ropes and let the boat go.", "As dawn approached, Paul urged everyone to eat. They had been waiting anxiously without a proper meal. He reminded them that their lives would be spared, took bread, thanked God in front of them and began to eat. The others took courage and ate too. Luke gives their number as 276. Afterwards they lightened the ship by throwing the wheat into the sea.", "At daylight they did not recognise the coast, but they saw a bay with a beach and tried to run the ship ashore. It stuck fast at the bow while the force of the waves broke up the stern. The soldiers proposed killing the prisoners to prevent their escape.", "The centurion wanted to save Paul and stopped that plan. He ordered those who could swim to go first. The rest followed on planks and pieces of the ship. Everyone reached land safely. The promise Paul had spoken in the storm was fulfilled through a series of ordinary decisions, from taking food to giving the prisoners a chance to reach shore."], "lesson": "Paul's confidence in God's promise sits alongside attention to food and to keeping the sailors aboard. The centurion's decision also protects people who cannot protect themselves.", "question": "What practical actions help bring everyone safely to shore?"},
  {"id": 91261, "title": "A Fire on Malta and Friends on the Road", "subtitle": "Receiving care after danger", "text": "Acts 28:1–16", "chapters": [[43, 28]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Once everyone was safely ashore, the travellers learned that the island was Malta. The people there showed them unusual kindness. It was raining and cold, so they lit a fire and welcomed them around it. Paul gathered an armful of sticks. As he laid them on the fire, a viper came out because of the heat and fastened to his hand. The islanders saw it hanging there and took it as a sign that justice had caught up with a murderer who had escaped the sea.", "Paul shook the snake into the fire and suffered no harm. The islanders watched, expecting him to swell up or fall down dead. After waiting a long time and seeing that nothing happened, they changed their minds and called him a god. Soon Luke turns from what people said about Paul to what they did for the travellers.", "Publius, the leading man of the island, welcomed them and cared for them for three days. His father was ill with fever and dysentery. Paul visited him, prayed, laid his hands on him and healed him. Others on the island who were sick came and were cured. The people honoured the travellers and supplied what they needed when they were ready to leave.", "Three months later they sailed in an Alexandrian ship that had spent the winter on the island. They travelled through Syracuse and Rhegium to Puteoli, where fellow believers invited them to stay a week. Others came from Rome to meet them at the Forum of Appius and the Three Taverns. When Paul saw them, he thanked God and took courage. In Rome he was allowed to live on his own with a soldier to guard him."], "lesson": "Hospitality comes first from strangers and later from fellow believers. Luke lets Paul receive help as well as give it.", "question": "What different forms of help do the islanders and the believers offer?"},
  {"id": 91262, "title": "A Door Open in Rome", "subtitle": "Serving within present limits", "text": "Acts 28:17–31", "chapters": [[43, 28]], "start": 17, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Acts", "story": ["Three days after reaching Rome, Paul called together the local Jewish leaders. He told them that he had done nothing against his people or their ancestral customs, yet he had been handed over to the Romans in Jerusalem. The Romans had found no reason to put him to death and wanted to release him. When his accusers objected, he felt compelled to appeal to Caesar. He had brought no charge against his own people. He wanted them to know that he wore his chain because of the hope of Israel.", "The leaders said they had received no letters from Judea about Paul and heard no evil report about him from anyone who had arrived. They wanted to hear what he thought, since they knew that the movement he belonged to was spoken against everywhere. They arranged a day, and more people came to the place where he was staying.", "From morning until evening Paul explained the kingdom of God and tried to persuade them about Jesus from the Law of Moses and the Prophets. Some were persuaded and others refused to believe. As they began to leave in disagreement, Paul recalled Isaiah's words about people who hear without understanding and see without perceiving. Then he told them that God's salvation had been sent to the Gentiles and that they would listen.", "Paul stayed two whole years in his rented lodging. He welcomed everyone who came to see him. The guard and the chain had a place in his life there, and so did the visitors who came to hear him. Luke leaves Paul in that lodging, proclaiming God's kingdom and teaching about the Lord Jesus Christ openly and without hindrance."], "lesson": "Paul uses the place available to him and receives the people who come. Luke ends with the word being taught freely even while Paul's circumstances are restricted.", "question": "What can Paul still do from his rented lodging, and who can come to hear him?"},
  {"id": 91263, "title": "Paul Explains Why He Wants to Come to Rome", "subtitle": "A gospel meant for every hearer", "text": "Romans 1:1–17", "chapters": [[44, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul introduces himself as a servant of Jesus Christ, called to be an apostle and set apart for God's gospel. This good news had been promised beforehand through the prophets in the Scriptures. It concerns God's Son, descended from David according to the flesh and declared Son of God in power through his resurrection from the dead. Through Jesus, Paul says, he received grace and his commission to call people among all nations to the obedience that comes from faith. The believers in Rome belong among those called to Jesus Christ.", "Paul addresses them as people loved by God and called to be saints. He wishes them grace and peace from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. Before he tells them why he is writing, he thanks God through Jesus for them. Their faith is being spoken of widely. Paul remembers them continually in prayer and asks that, by God's will, a way may finally open for him to visit them.", "He longs to see them in person and share a spiritual gift that will strengthen them. He goes on to put the visit in terms of what he hopes to receive as well. Their faith and his would encourage each other. He has intended to come many times and has been prevented. He still hopes for fruit from his work among them, as among other Gentiles. He feels a duty to Greeks and those outside Greek culture, to the wise and the unwise.", "That debt helps explain his eagerness to preach in Rome. He is unashamed of the gospel because it is God's power for salvation to everyone who believes, to the Jew first and also to the Greek. In it God's righteousness is revealed in connection with faith. Paul gives the thought a line from Scripture that will carry into the rest of the letter. The righteous will live by faith."], "lesson": "Paul writes with a calling to teach and an expectation of receiving encouragement. The people he hopes to visit already have faith from which he expects to benefit.", "question": "What does Paul expect to give and receive if he visits Rome?"},
  {"id": 91264, "title": "When Gratitude Gives Way to Idols", "subtitle": "Honouring the Creator", "text": "Romans 1:18–32", "chapters": [[44, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has announced the good news as God's power for salvation. Now he shows why people need it. God's wrath is revealed against ungodliness and wrongdoing, Paul says, because people hold down the truth by the way they live. God has made something of himself plain in the things he has made. His eternal power and divine nature can be seen through creation. Paul says people are therefore without excuse.", "The refusal begins with worship and thanksgiving. Although people knew God, they gave him neither the honour nor the thanks due to him. Their reasoning became empty and their hearts darkened. Claiming wisdom, they traded the glory of the immortal God for images of mortal people, birds, animals and creeping things. Paul describes this as exchanging God's truth for a lie and serving created things in place of their Creator.", "Paul repeatedly says that God gave them over to the course they chose. He speaks first of desires through which they dishonoured their bodies. He then describes women exchanging natural relations and men abandoning relations with women and burning in desire for one another. Finally, he says that because people saw no reason to retain the knowledge of God, they were given over to a corrupt mind and conduct that followed from it.", "The final list brings Paul's argument into life with other people. He names envy, murder, quarrelling, deceit, gossip, pride, disobedience to parents and a lack of mercy, among other kinds of wrongdoing. People know God's judgment against such conduct and persist in it, Paul says. They also approve when others do the same. The passage that begins with what people can see of their Creator ends with what they have come to accept among themselves."], "lesson": "Paul begins the account with a refusal to honour and thank God. Gratitude helps keep the gifts of creation in relation to the one who gives them.", "question": "Which changes in conduct does Paul trace back to a change in worship?"},
  {"id": 91265, "title": "The Person Who Judges Is Also Accountable", "subtitle": "Receiving kindness as a call to repent", "text": "Romans 2:1–16", "chapters": [[44, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul turns from the people he has described to the reader who condemns them. The person passing judgment can recognise wrongdoing clearly enough to name it. If he practises the same things, that judgment also tells against him. God's judgment is according to truth, Paul says. Someone who sees another person's guilt and goes on in the same conduct has no reason to expect to escape it.", "Paul asks whether such a person takes God's kindness, patience and willingness to wait lightly. That kindness has a purpose. It leads people toward repentance. A stubborn heart that refuses to repent is storing up wrath for the day when God's righteous judgment will be revealed. God will repay each person according to what that person has done.", "Paul sets out the two outcomes. To those who patiently continue doing good and seek glory, honour and immortality, God will give eternal life. Wrath and trouble await those who reject the truth and obey unrighteousness. Paul names both Jews and Gentiles in speaking of trouble for wrongdoing and of glory, honour and peace for doing good. God judges without favouritism.", "Those who sin without the law will be judged accordingly, and those who sin under the law will be judged by it. Hearing the law does not of itself make a person righteous before God. Gentiles who do what the law requires show its work written on their hearts. Their consciences bear witness, and their thoughts accuse or defend them. Paul brings even these inward matters into view as he mentions the day God will judge people's secrets through Jesus Christ."], "lesson": "God's patience gives time to repent. It is easy to interpret that time as approval when we are occupied with pointing out another person's faults.", "question": "Where is Paul asking his reader to turn attention after condemning someone else?"},
  {"id": 91266, "title": "The Teacher Under His Own Teaching", "subtitle": "Integrity that reaches the heart", "text": "Romans 2:17–29", "chapters": [[44, 2]], "start": 17, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now addresses the reader who calls himself a Jew, relies on the law and takes pride in his relationship to God. Such a person has been taught to recognise what God approves. He considers himself a guide to the blind and a light to people in darkness, able to teach the foolish and instruct the young. In the law he has a form of knowledge and truth he can pass on.", "Paul turns these claims into questions. Does the one teaching others also teach himself? If he preaches against stealing, does he steal? If he forbids adultery, does he commit it? If he detests idols, does he rob temples? A person can boast in the law while dishonouring God by breaking it. Paul reminds his reader of what Scripture says about God's name being blasphemed among the Gentiles because of such conduct.", "Then Paul takes up circumcision, the outward sign of belonging to the covenant people. It has value if a person keeps the law. Breaking the law makes the sign count as uncircumcision. Paul asks what follows when an uncircumcised person carries out what the law requires. His obedience exposes the disobedience of the person who possesses the written law and bears the sign.", "Paul ends with what God sees inwardly. The circumcision he describes takes place in the heart, by the Spirit, and reaches where a written code and a mark on the body cannot reach. The person who lives this way receives praise from God. The argument has travelled from the honour the teacher claims in public to the life God sees beneath that claim."], "lesson": "The responsibility to teach gives the teacher an additional reason to examine his own life. Public recognition cannot substitute for God's judgement of the heart.", "question": "Which of Paul's questions would help you examine a duty you urge on other people?"},
  {"id": 91267, "title": "Every Mouth Comes to Silence", "subtitle": "Honesty before God", "text": "Romans 3:1–20", "chapters": [[44, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul anticipates what his reader might ask after hearing what he has said about the law and circumcision. What advantage, then, does the Jew have? Paul answers that there is much advantage. To begin with, the Jews were entrusted with the words of God. Some had been unfaithful. Their unfaithfulness could never make God unfaithful. Paul appeals to Scripture to affirm that God is true and will be vindicated when his words are judged.", "A further objection follows. If people's unrighteousness makes God's righteousness more evident, would God be unjust to judge them? Paul rejects the argument, for on those terms God could scarcely judge the world. He also rejects the claim that people should do evil so that good may result. He says some had slanderously attributed such teaching to him, and he calls their condemnation deserved.", "Paul then returns to the question of whether having these privileges puts Jews in a better position before God's judgment. His charge has already included Jews and Gentiles alike under sin. He gathers several lines from Scripture to describe that condition. Nobody is righteous. No one seeks God. Their speech carries deceit and bitterness. Their paths are marked by violence and trouble, and they do not know the way of peace. There is no fear of God before their eyes.", "Paul says the law speaks to those under it. Its words leave every mouth silent and the whole world accountable before God. Nobody will be justified before him by works of the law. Through the law comes knowledge of sin. The Scripture entrusted to this people has brought the argument to a point where every reader needs to hear what God will do."], "lesson": "A genuine privilege can make accountability deeper. The Scripture Paul cites gives people words for their condition before it gives them grounds for hope.", "question": "What does Paul say the law makes known when people stand accountable before God?"},
  {"id": 91268, "title": "The Righteousness God Makes Known", "subtitle": "Grace gives no room for boasting", "text": "Romans 3:21–31", "chapters": [[44, 3]], "start": 21, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has brought the whole world before God with no defence to offer. Now he says God's righteousness has been made known apart from works of the law. The Law and the Prophets themselves bear witness to it. This righteousness comes through faith in Jesus Christ to all who believe. Paul gives the reason he can speak to them together. All have sinned and fall short of God's glory.", "Those who are justified receive it as a gift of grace through the redemption that is in Christ Jesus. God put Christ forward as an atoning sacrifice through his blood, to be received by faith. In his patience God had passed over sins committed beforehand. Paul says that what God has now done shows his righteousness. He is just, and he justifies the one who has faith in Jesus.", "Paul then asks what has become of boasting. It has been shut out. An arrangement based on what people achieve would leave them something to boast about, but faith gives them no such ground. His conclusion is that a person is justified by faith apart from works of the law. The argument concerns people who have spent their lives under the law as well as those who have lived outside it.", "God is the God of Gentiles as well as of Jews. Since there is one God, he justifies the circumcised by faith and the uncircumcised through faith. Paul anticipates one last question. Does this faith overthrow the law? He says it upholds it. He will now go back to Abraham, whose place in Scripture lets him show how long this truth has been there."], "lesson": "The same grace meets people with different histories. Boasting loses its ground when acceptance depends on what God gives through Christ.", "question": "How does the conclusion about boasting follow from the way Paul describes justification?"},
  {"id": 91269, "title": "Abraham Before the Sign", "subtitle": "Trust in the God who justifies", "text": "Romans 4:1–12", "chapters": [[44, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul takes up Abraham, the ancestor whose story his Jewish readers know well. What did Abraham find? If he had been justified by works, he would have a ground for boasting, Paul says, though he would have none before God. Paul asks the reader to look at what Scripture actually says. Abraham believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness.", "Paul asks his readers to think about the difference between wages and a gift. If someone works for a wage, the payment is counted as something owed. In Abraham's case, the righteousness was counted through faith. Paul speaks here of the person who trusts the God who justifies the ungodly. He then draws in David, who speaks of the blessedness of the person to whom God counts righteousness apart from works. David describes a person whose wrongdoing is forgiven, whose sins are covered and whose sin the Lord does not count against him.", "Paul asks who can share this blessing. Is it for the circumcised alone? He looks at the order of events in Abraham's life. Abraham's faith was counted to him as righteousness while he was still uncircumcised. Circumcision came later as a sign and seal of the righteousness he already had by faith.", "That order gives Abraham a place in the lives of people who have never borne his outward sign. He is the father of uncircumcised people who believe, so that righteousness may be counted to them also. He is also the father of circumcised people who follow in the footsteps of the faith he had before his circumcision. Paul makes the timing of the sign part of his answer about who may belong to this family of faith."], "lesson": "Paul pays attention to the timing of the sign. That sequence gives people beyond Abraham's physical descendants a place in the story of faith.", "question": "What happened before Abraham received the sign, and why is the order important to Paul?"},
  {"id": 91270, "title": "The Promise Faced by an Old Man", "subtitle": "Hope grounded in the power of God", "text": "Romans 4:13–25", "chapters": [[44, 4]], "start": 13, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now considers the promise that Abraham and his descendants would inherit the world. It came through the righteousness of faith, apart from the law. If the inheritance depended on keeping the law, faith would be emptied and the promise would lose its force. The law brings wrath where it is broken. Paul says the promise rests on faith so that it depends on grace and is sure to all Abraham's offspring, including those who share his faith without having his people's law.", "Scripture speaks of Abraham as father of many nations. Paul points to the God in whom Abraham believed, the God who gives life to the dead and calls into being things that do not exist. Abraham trusted him when the ordinary grounds for expecting a child had disappeared. He was about a hundred years old and knew the condition of his own body. He also knew that Sarah's womb was past bearing children.", "Abraham went on trusting what God had spoken. He grew strong in faith, gave glory to God and was fully persuaded that God could do what he had promised. This is why his faith was counted to him as righteousness. Paul has brought the reader close enough to Abraham's circumstances to see what gave that faith its footing.", "The words about righteousness being counted were written for later readers too. Righteousness will be counted to those who believe in the God who raised Jesus our Lord from the dead. Jesus was handed over for our trespasses and raised for our justification. At the close of the passage, the God who brings life out of death is the ground of hope for Paul's readers as he was for Abraham."], "lesson": "Abraham's trust takes the facts of his condition seriously. Its ground is what God is able to do.", "question": "What two human limitations does Paul name when he describes Abraham's faith?"},
  {"id": 91271, "title": "Peace After Justification", "subtitle": "Hope in the midst of trouble", "text": "Romans 5:1–11", "chapters": [[44, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul turns to what follows when a person has been justified by faith. Believers have peace with God through the Lord Jesus Christ. Through him they have gained access by faith into the grace in which they stand, and they rejoice in the hope of sharing God's glory. Paul speaks of this as a place already given to them and a hope for which they still wait.", "He has room in that hope for trouble. Suffering produces endurance, endurance produces tested character, and character produces hope. This hope will hold because God's love has been poured into their hearts through the Holy Spirit he has given them. Paul then directs their attention to what that love has already done for them.", "Christ died for the ungodly while they were still weak. Paul asks his readers to consider how seldom someone dies for a righteous person, though someone might dare to die for a good person. God shows his love in that Christ died for us while we were still sinners. Paul puts the death of Christ at the point in the story where people had nothing in their own condition to commend them.", "Now that they have been justified by Christ's blood, Paul says, they can trust that they will be saved from wrath through him. If God reconciled them to himself through his Son's death while they were enemies, how much more will they be saved by his life now that they are reconciled? They rejoice in God through Jesus Christ, through whom they have received reconciliation. What Christ has already done gives Paul his reason for confidence about what lies ahead."], "lesson": "Christian hope in this passage begins with reconciliation God has given. It makes room to speak honestly about suffering while trusting the love that met people in their weakness.", "question": "How does Christ's death for sinners support Paul's confidence about what lies ahead?"},
  {"id": 91272, "title": "Adam and Christ", "subtitle": "Grace that abounds over sin", "text": "Romans 5:12–21", "chapters": [[44, 5]], "start": 12, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul now goes back to Adam to put the work of Christ in view over the length of human history. Sin came into the world through one man, and death came through sin. Death spread to all because all sinned. Sin was in the world before the law was given. Death reigned from Adam to Moses, even over people whose sin differed from Adam's breaking of a direct command. Paul calls Adam a figure of the one who was to come.", "The effect of Adam's trespass helps Paul show the reach of Christ's gift. Many died through one man's trespass. God's grace and the gift that comes by the grace of Jesus Christ have abounded to many. A single trespass brought judgment and condemnation. The gift comes after many trespasses and brings justification. Paul draws attention to how much the gift has answered.", "Death reigned through the one man. Paul says those who receive abundant grace and the gift of righteousness will reign in life through Jesus Christ. He brings the two accounts together again. One trespass brought condemnation for all, and one righteous act brought justification and life. Through one man's disobedience many were made sinners. Through the obedience of the one, many will be made righteous.", "Paul says the law entered so that the trespass would increase. As sin increased, grace abounded all the more. Sin had reigned in death. Grace now reigns through righteousness leading to eternal life through Jesus Christ our Lord. This leaves the reader with a question Paul will take up next. What does this abundance of grace mean for the way believers live now?"], "lesson": "Paul places humanity's condition inside a larger account of what Christ has done. The gift is described as greater than the ruin it answers.", "question": "What changes when Paul moves from what entered through Adam to what comes through Christ?"},
  {"id": 91273, "title": "Buried and Raised With Christ", "subtitle": "Living in the newness of life", "text": "Romans 6:1–14", "chapters": [[44, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul anticipates a question about his words on abundant grace. Should believers continue in sin so that grace will increase? He rejects the thought and asks how those who died to sin can go on living in it. To explain what he means by dying, he brings his readers back to their baptism. Those baptised into Christ Jesus were baptised into his death. They were buried with him so that, as Christ was raised through the Father's glory, they too might walk in newness of life.", "If they have been united with Christ in a death like his, Paul says, they will share in his resurrection. Their old self was crucified with him so that the body ruled by sin would lose its power and they would no longer serve sin as slaves. The person who has died has been freed from sin's claim. Paul expects those who died with Christ to live with him.", "Christ, having risen, will never die again. Death no longer rules over him. He died to sin once for all and now lives to God. Paul asks his readers to reckon themselves in the light of what is true of Christ. They are to count themselves dead to sin and alive to God in Christ Jesus. What they think about their life in him bears on what they do with it.", "Paul therefore tells them to refuse sin's rule over their mortal bodies and its demands on their conduct. They are to present themselves to God as people brought from death to life and offer their bodily powers as instruments of righteousness. Sin will no longer rule them, Paul says, because they live under grace. He has brought the question about grace down to what they will do with their hands, their speech and everything else they can offer to God."], "lesson": "Grace gives believers reason to change whom they obey. Paul asks them to offer their actual capacities to God in the life they now live.", "question": "What would presenting one part of your daily life as an instrument of righteousness involve?"},
  {"id": 91274, "title": "The Master You Obey", "subtitle": "Freedom that bears the fruit of holiness", "text": "Romans 6:15–23", "chapters": [[44, 6]], "start": 15, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul raises a second question about grace. Since believers are under grace rather than under the law, may they sin? He rejects the idea. He asks his readers to think about what it means to present themselves to someone for obedience. They become servants of the one they obey. Sin leads to death, and obedience leads to righteousness. The freedom he has been describing has a direction that can be seen in daily conduct.", "Paul thanks God as he looks back on what has changed for his readers. They once served sin. Then they obeyed from the heart the teaching to which they had been entrusted. Having been freed from sin, they became servants of righteousness. Paul says he is speaking in ordinary human terms because of their weakness. The familiar picture of service helps them see what their allegiance means.", "They once placed their bodies at the service of impurity and lawlessness, and lawlessness led to more of the same. Now they are to put themselves at the service of righteousness, which leads to holiness. Paul asks them to look back at the fruit of the things they are now ashamed of. They were free from the service of righteousness at the time, but the end of those things was death.", "Now they have been freed from sin and have become servants of God. Their fruit is holiness, and the end is eternal life. Paul brings the two courses to their conclusion in a sentence. Sin pays its wages in death. God gives eternal life as a gift through Jesus Christ our Lord."], "lesson": "Paul asks his hearers to compare where two kinds of obedience lead. Choices that look isolated can form habits that direct an entire life.", "question": "What fruit and conclusion does Paul associate with service to God?"},
  {"id": 91275, "title": "The Command That Reveals Coveting", "subtitle": "Recognising what the law brings to light", "text": "Romans 7:1–13", "chapters": [[44, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["At the end of the previous passage, Paul described people who had been freed from sin and now served God. He returns to the question of the law and asks his readers to think about something a law can only do while a person lives. A married woman is bound by law to her husband while he is alive. If she belongs to another man during that time, the law calls her an adulteress. If her husband dies, the bond ends, and she is free to marry again. The point of the example is that death changes the reach of an existing claim.", "In the case of Paul's readers, it is they who have died to the law through the body of Christ. They now belong to the one who was raised from the dead, so that they may bear fruit for God. Paul looks back on the former life and says sinful passions, aroused through the law, were at work in their bodies and bore fruit for death. Having died to what held them, they now serve in the new way of the Spirit. The change reaches beyond how they understand a written command. It concerns the person to whom they belong and the life their service produces.", "This raises an awkward question. If the law could stir up sinful passions, was the law itself sinful? Paul rejects the idea. He takes a command that reaches into the thoughts people may keep to themselves. He says that he would not have recognised coveting if the law had not forbidden it. Sin took the command as an opportunity and produced desires of every kind. Paul describes himself as once alive apart from the law. When the command came, sin sprang to life and he died.", "Even the command that held out life proved to bring death in his case. Paul says sin seized the opportunity, deceived him and killed him through the command. The law remains holy, and the command is holy, righteous and good. Did something good itself bring him death? Paul puts the responsibility on sin, which used something good to produce death. The command exposes sin in its true character. The example began with a rule whose claim ends at death and comes to rest on another question altogether, what can bring a person under sin into a life fruitful for God?"], "lesson": "A good command can uncover a desire we had allowed to pass unnoticed. Paul distinguishes what the command shows from the sin that takes advantage of it.", "question": "Which command does Paul use to explain how he came to recognise coveting?"},
  {"id": 91276, "title": "The Good I Want to Do", "subtitle": "Taking inward conflict seriously", "text": "Romans 7:14–25", "chapters": [[44, 7]], "start": 14, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has already called the command holy and good. He now says the law is spiritual, then puts his own condition beside it. In the first person he describes someone of flesh, sold under sin, who finds his actions hard to understand. He does the very thing he hates, while the thing he wants to do remains undone. There is a kind of agreement with the law even in that confession. By hating the wrong he does, he acknowledges that the law which forbids it is good.", "Paul draws attention to sin dwelling within him. He says he finds no good thing dwelling in his flesh. The desire to do right is present, yet he cannot carry it through. He returns to the same experience from another direction. The good he wants stays out of reach, and the evil he wants to leave alone is what he actually does. His repeated description makes the gap plain. A person can see the goodness of the command, sincerely desire to obey it and still discover another power at work in his conduct.", "Paul uses the word law again for the pattern he keeps finding. When he wants to do good, evil is close at hand. In his inward being he delights in God's law. In his bodily members he sees another law waging war against the law of his mind and taking him captive to sin. The image changes from a person whose actions puzzle him to a prisoner who can see what holds him. The agreement of his mind with God's law has not ended the struggle he describes.", "At that point he cries out over his wretched condition and asks who will deliver him from this body of death. He answers with thanksgiving to God through Jesus Christ our Lord. The closing sentence holds both parts of his experience in view. With his mind he serves God's law, and with his flesh he serves the law of sin. Paul has followed the trouble all the way from the command he approves to the captivity he cannot break by wishing. His thanks directs the reader toward the deliverance he will take up as he begins to speak about life in the Spirit."], "lesson": "Honest confession can name the gap between conviction and conduct. Paul's cry places hope of deliverance with God through Jesus Christ.", "question": "What tension is expressed between what the speaker approves and what he actually does?"},
  {"id": 91277, "title": "The Spirit and the Cry of Adoption", "subtitle": "Living as children who call on the Father", "text": "Romans 8:1–17", "chapters": [[44, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has just cried out for deliverance and given thanks through Jesus Christ. Now he tells the people who are in Christ Jesus that there is no condemnation for them. The law of the Spirit of life in Christ has set them free from the law of sin and death. The law could not overcome the weakness of the flesh. God acted by sending his own Son in the likeness of sinful flesh and for sin, and judged sin in the flesh. Paul's purpose in following that thought is practical. The righteous requirement of the law is to be fulfilled in people who walk according to the Spirit.", "Paul then asks his readers to consider what occupies their minds. People living according to the flesh set their minds on what the flesh wants, and people living according to the Spirit set their minds on what the Spirit desires. One course ends in death, while the mind set on the Spirit has life and peace. The mind ruled by the flesh is hostile to God and cannot submit to his law. Paul speaks that plainly before turning to the people in whom God's Spirit dwells. Anyone who lacks Christ's Spirit does not belong to him.", "Paul sets the body's mortality beside the life that comes through righteousness. The God who raised Jesus from the dead will give life even to their mortal bodies through his Spirit who dwells in them. That promise bears on how they live before their bodies are raised. They owe the flesh no obedience. If they live by it they will die. If by the Spirit they put to death the deeds of the body, they will live. Paul says those led by God's Spirit are God's children.", "He then brings them from the fear of slavery to the language of a household. They have received the Spirit of adoption, by whom they cry to God as Abba, Father. The Spirit bears witness with their own spirit that they are God's children. Children are also heirs, heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ. Paul includes suffering with Christ in the life of those who will share his glory. The believer who calls on God as Father has a place in that family even while the suffering is still real."], "lesson": "The Spirit leads believers into a family relationship with obligations and assurance. They can address God as Father even in circumstances that carry a cost.", "question": "How does adoption change the way believers approach God according to Paul?"},
  {"id": 91278, "title": "Creation Groans and Love Holds", "subtitle": "Hope when words are difficult", "text": "Romans 8:18–39", "chapters": [[44, 8]], "start": 18, "end": 39, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has just spoken of suffering with Christ and sharing his glory. Now he weighs those two things. The sufferings of the present time are small beside the glory to come. His view widens from the believer to creation itself. Creation has been subjected to frustration and waits for God's children to be revealed, in hope of sharing their freedom from decay. Paul likens its groaning to the pains of childbirth. Believers who already have the firstfruits of the Spirit groan too, waiting for adoption in its fullness, the redemption of their bodies. They wait patiently for what they cannot yet see.", "The Spirit helps them in their weakness. At times they do not know how to pray as they should, and the Spirit himself intercedes with groans beyond speech. God searches hearts and knows the mind of the Spirit, whose intercession for believers accords with God's will. Paul goes on to speak of all things working together for the good of those who love God and are called according to his purpose. They are to be conformed to the image of his Son, the firstborn among many brothers and sisters. Paul traces God's work from foreknowledge and purpose through calling and justification to glory.", "He then puts several questions to his readers. If God is for them, who can stand against them? God gave up his own Son for them. They can trust him to give what they need with him. Who can make a charge stick against the people God has chosen, when God himself justifies them? Who can condemn them? Christ died, was raised and is at God's right hand interceding for them. Paul has given them two pictures of intercession in the same passage, one in their weakness and one at God's right hand.", "He asks whether trouble, distress, persecution, hunger, want, danger or the sword can separate them from Christ's love. Scripture itself speaks of God's people facing death. Even in such circumstances, Paul says, they conquer through the one who loved them. He ends with what he is persuaded of. Neither death nor life, spiritual powers, the present or the future, height or depth, nor anything else in creation can separate them from God's love in Christ Jesus their Lord."], "lesson": "Paul gives groaning and assurance space in the same argument. Weakness in prayer is met by the Spirit's help and by confidence in God's love.", "question": "Where does Paul direct a believer who is too weak to know how to pray?"},
  {"id": 91279, "title": "Paul Grieves for Israel", "subtitle": "Mercy and the faithfulness of God", "text": "Romans 9:1–29", "chapters": [[44, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["After speaking of the love from which nothing can separate believers, Paul tells the truth about a grief he cannot put aside. He says his conscience bears witness in the Holy Spirit. He carries great sorrow for his own people, the Israelites, and says he could wish himself accursed and cut off from Christ for their sake. He names what God has given them. The adoption and the glory, the covenants, the law, the worship and the promises are theirs. The patriarchs belong to them, and from their people came the Christ according to the flesh.", "Has God's word failed? Paul answers by returning to the family stories in Scripture. He distinguishes between physical descent from Israel and belonging to Israel in the sense his argument now requires. Abraham had more than one son, and God said that his offspring would be named through Isaac. Paul recalls the promise that Sarah would have a son. Then he turns to Rebekah, whose two children had the same father, Isaac. Before the twins were born or had done good or evil, she was told that the elder would serve the younger. Paul connects this with God's purpose in calling and with what Scripture later says about Jacob and Esau.", "Paul knows the objection this raises. Is there injustice with God? He cites God's words to Moses about showing mercy and compassion as he chooses. Mercy depends on God, beyond any human willing or effort. Paul brings in Pharaoh as another example. Scripture says God raised him up so that his power might be shown and his name proclaimed throughout the earth. Paul says God has mercy on whom he wills and hardens whom he wills.", "The objection grows sharper. Why does God still find fault? Paul asks the questioner to consider the relation of clay to its potter. A potter can make vessels for different purposes out of the same lump. Paul speaks of God's patience in bearing vessels of wrath and of the riches of his glory made known to vessels of mercy. Those called include Gentiles as well as Jews. Hosea speaks of people once called 'not my people' becoming God's people. Isaiah speaks of a remnant of Israel being saved and of the Lord leaving survivors. Paul has answered the question of God's faithfulness by going back to the very Scriptures entrusted to the people for whom he grieves."], "lesson": "Paul's argument begins with grief for real people. His appeal to mercy calls for humility when considering God's freedom and the limits of human claims.", "question": "What privileges does Paul remember before asking how God's promise stands?"},
  {"id": 91280, "title": "The Message Comes Near", "subtitle": "Righteousness received by faith and proclaimed", "text": "Romans 9:30–10:21", "chapters": [[44, 9], [44, 10]], "start": 30, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul brings the argument back to a difference he wants his readers to see. Gentiles who had never pursued righteousness attained it through faith. Israel pursued a law of righteousness and failed to reach its goal, because they pursued it as though it depended on works. Paul says they stumbled over a stone, and brings together words from Isaiah about the stone set in Zion and the security of the one who believes. Then he speaks of his own prayer to God that Israel might be saved. He acknowledges their zeal for God and says it lacks knowledge. Seeking to establish their own righteousness, they failed to submit to God's righteousness. Christ is the end of the law for righteousness to everyone who believes.", "Paul sets the righteousness described in the law beside the way the righteousness of faith speaks. He draws on Moses' language about going up or going down to obtain the word. The hearer has no need to ascend to bring Christ down or descend to bring him up from the dead. The word is already near, in the mouth and in the heart. Paul identifies it as the message of faith he proclaims. The person who confesses Jesus as Lord and believes in the heart that God raised him from the dead will be saved. The same Lord is Lord of all, rich toward everyone who calls on him.", "Then Paul asks how someone comes to call on the Lord. Calling follows believing. Believing depends on hearing, and hearing depends on someone proclaiming the message. A preacher needs to be sent. Isaiah's words about the welcome given to messengers of good news belong here, and so does Isaiah's question about who believed what they heard. The message goes out, and hearers give different answers. Faith comes through hearing the word about Christ.", "Paul asks whether Israel had heard and whether they had understood. He turns to the Scriptures again. Moses spoke of Israel being provoked by people who were no nation. Isaiah spoke of God being found by people who had never sought him. Of Israel he speaks of God stretching out his hands all day toward a disobedient and contrary people. Paul's prayer at the beginning of the passage gives these words their setting. He is speaking about people he longs to see call on the Lord."], "lesson": "Paul's concern for his people becomes prayer and an argument for proclamation. The sequence makes the work of bringing the message to others a practical responsibility.", "question": "What links does Paul name between someone being sent and a hearer calling on the Lord?"},
  {"id": 91281, "title": "Unexpected Branches on an Olive Tree", "subtitle": "Humility after being welcomed", "text": "Romans 11:1–24", "chapters": [[44, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul asks whether God has rejected his people. His own life gives the first answer. He too is an Israelite, descended from Abraham and from the tribe of Benjamin. He reminds his readers of Elijah, who complained to God that Israel had killed the prophets and torn down the altars. Elijah thought he alone had survived and that his life was in danger. God told him that he had kept seven thousand people who had never bowed to Baal. Paul sees a remnant in his own time too, chosen by grace. Grace would lose its meaning if the choice depended on works.", "Israel, Paul says, failed to obtain what it was seeking. The chosen obtained it, and the others were hardened. He cites Scripture about unseeing eyes, unhearing ears and a table that becomes a snare. Then he asks whether they stumbled so as to fall beyond recovery. Through their trespass salvation has come to the Gentiles, which in turn is meant to move Israel to jealousy. If their loss has brought riches to the world, Paul asks his readers to consider what their fullness would mean. He takes his ministry to the Gentiles seriously in part because he hopes to stir his own people and save some of them.", "Paul pictures an olive tree. If the root is holy, the branches are holy. Some of the branches have been broken off, and a shoot from a wild olive has been grafted among the others and now shares the nourishment of the root. Paul tells his Gentile readers to think carefully about any claim to superiority. The root supports the grafted branch. If the branch replies that others were removed to make room for it, Paul points back to what happened. They were broken off through unbelief, and the grafted branch stands by faith. That is reason for reverence.", "Paul asks them to consider God's kindness and severity. God has been kind to them, and they are to continue in that kindness. The warning against pride matters because God can cut off the grafted branches too. And if those who were broken off do not continue in unbelief, God is able to graft them in again. If a wild branch could be grafted into a cultivated tree, Paul says, how much more readily could the natural branches be grafted back into their own olive tree?"], "lesson": "The image gives people included through faith a reason for gratitude and reverence. It also keeps open a possibility they may be tempted to dismiss in others.", "question": "How does Paul's reminder about the root answer the temptation to boast?"},
  {"id": 91282, "title": "The Argument Ends in Praise", "subtitle": "A place for humility before God's wisdom", "text": "Romans 11:25–36", "chapters": [[44, 11]], "start": 25, "end": 36, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul tells his Gentile readers that he wants them to understand a mystery so they will have less reason to think themselves wise. A hardening has come upon Israel in part until the fullness of the Gentiles comes in. In this way, Paul says, all Israel will be saved. He draws on Scripture about a deliverer coming from Zion, turning ungodliness away from Jacob, and God keeping his covenant by taking away sins. Paul has just spoken about branches that God can graft back into their own tree. Here he states the hope in terms of God's promise.", "Paul keeps two facts in view at once. With regard to the gospel, these people are enemies for the Gentiles' sake. With regard to election, they are beloved for the sake of their ancestors. God's gifts and calling are irrevocable. The same people who have opposed the gospel are still the people about whom Paul can speak of God's love and calling. He asks the Gentiles to remember that they too have a history of disobedience.", "They once disobeyed God and have now received mercy through Israel's disobedience. Israel is now disobedient and can also receive mercy in connection with the mercy shown to the Gentiles. Paul says God has shut all up in disobedience in order to have mercy on all. Neither group, then, can take its own record of obedience as the reason it has been shown mercy. The question of how one group came to receive the message has brought both groups back to the same need.", "At this point Paul's words turn to praise. He speaks of the depth of God's riches, wisdom and knowledge. God's judgments are beyond searching out, and his ways are beyond tracing. Paul asks who has known God's mind, who has served as his adviser, or who has first given him something that God now owes back. All things come from God, hold together through him and come to their end in him. Paul gives him glory forever. The next thing he will ask of his readers begins with the mercies he has spent these chapters tracing."], "lesson": "Paul has reasoned carefully and still ends in worship. The passage shows the place of humility after patient study of a difficult subject.", "question": "What reasons for humility does Paul give before he turns to praise?"},
  {"id": 91283, "title": "A Living Sacrifice in Ordinary Relationships", "subtitle": "Love that takes visible form", "text": "Romans 12:1–21", "chapters": [[44, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has spent much of the letter tracing the mercy of God. He now appeals to his readers on the strength of that mercy. He asks them to present their bodies as a living sacrifice, holy and acceptable to God. They are to resist being shaped by the present age and be transformed by the renewing of their minds. A mind being renewed can discern what God wants, what is good and pleasing and complete. The next thing Paul asks of that mind is that it judge its own importance soberly, according to the measure of faith God has given.", "Paul pictures a body with many members. The members have different work to do, and in Christ they belong to one another. Gifts differ according to the grace each has received. Paul tells the person who prophesies to do so according to faith, the person who serves to give attention to serving, and the teacher to teach. Encouragement has its own work. Those who give should give generously, those who lead should do it diligently, and those who show mercy should do it cheerfully. The picture of a living sacrifice has become a picture of people doing the work their neighbours need.", "Love must be sincere. Paul asks them to hate evil and hold to what is good, to care for each other with family affection and to take the lead in giving honour. They are to serve the Lord with zeal, rejoice in hope, endure trouble and keep praying. They should help meet the needs of fellow believers and make a habit of welcoming strangers. Rejoicing with those who rejoice and weeping with those who weep require attention to what is happening in another person's life. Paul asks them to live in harmony and associate with people of low position.", "The harder instructions concern those who harm them. Bless people who persecute you, Paul says, and repay nobody evil for evil. As far as it depends on them, they are to live at peace with everyone. They must leave vengeance to God. Anyone whose enemy is hungry can give him food. Anyone whose enemy is thirsty can give him a drink. Paul ends with a choice his readers can carry into a quarrel or an injury. They can overcome evil with good."], "lesson": "The offering of the body becomes visible in the way people use gifts and respond to others. In Paul's examples, even hostility becomes an occasion to do good.", "question": "Which of Paul's instructions gives you a way to act towards someone with whom you are in conflict?"},
  {"id": 91284, "title": "Duties to Authorities and to Neighbours", "subtitle": "Paying what is owed and walking in the light", "text": "Romans 13:1–14", "chapters": [[44, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has just asked his readers to live at peace with others as far as it depends on them. He now turns to the authorities under whom they live. He tells them to submit to governing authorities because authority has its place under God. Resisting that authority brings judgment. Paul describes the office in terms of the work it is meant to do. The ruler serves God for the good of the person who does right and bears the sword to punish wrongdoing. A person should therefore submit for conscience's sake as well as from concern about punishment.", "The duties that follow are ordinary enough to recognise. Paul says this is why the readers pay taxes. The authorities are attending to their work as public servants. Each person should receive what is owed, whether taxes, customs duties, respect or honour. Paul moves directly from these obligations to a debt that remains whenever one person deals with another. They should keep owing one another love. The person who loves a neighbour has fulfilled the law.", "Paul reminds them of commands against adultery, murder, stealing and coveting. Their common direction is expressed in the command to love one's neighbour as oneself. Love does no harm to a neighbour. That is how it fulfils the law. A reader can think about each command from the side of the person who would suffer if it were broken. The neighbour is the one whose marriage, life or possessions are treated with care when love governs what a person does.", "Paul urges them to act with an awareness of the time. The hour has come to wake from sleep, for salvation is nearer now than when they first believed. The night is nearly over and the day is near. He asks them to cast away the works of darkness and put on the armour of light. They are to walk honourably as in the daytime, leaving behind drunken revelry, sexual immorality, quarrelling and jealousy. Paul closes by telling them to put on the Lord Jesus Christ and make no provision for the desires of the flesh. The approaching day gives urgency to the choices they can make while it is still dark."], "lesson": "Paul gives particular duties their place and then describes love as a debt that keeps making claims on us. The approaching day gives urgency to daily conduct.", "question": "How does Paul connect love of neighbour with the commands he names?"},
  {"id": 91285, "title": "Different Tables Within One Fellowship", "subtitle": "Making room for conscience", "text": "Romans 14:1–23", "chapters": [[44, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul asks the believers to welcome a person whose faith is weak without pulling him into quarrels about disputed matters. He puts a meal before them. One person believes he may eat anything. Another eats only vegetables. The person who eats must take care not to despise the one who abstains, and the one who abstains must take care not to judge the person who eats. God has welcomed him. Each is another master's servant. It is before his own master that he stands or falls, and the Lord is able to make him stand.", "The same issue comes up over days. One person regards a day as special, while another regards all days alike. Paul asks each to be fully convinced in his own mind. The person who observes a day does it for the Lord. The person who eats gives thanks, and the one who abstains also gives thanks. They live and die to the Lord because they belong to him. Christ died and lived again to be Lord of the dead and the living. Each person will give an account to God. That prospect should curb both contempt and judgment among people sitting together.", "Paul asks them to make a different kind of judgment, a decision to put no obstacle in another believer's way. He is persuaded in the Lord Jesus that food is clean in itself. For someone who considers it unclean, however, it is unclean to him. A believer who distresses another by what he eats has stopped walking in love. Paul reminds the reader that Christ died for the person across the table. God's kingdom has to do with righteousness, peace and joy in the Holy Spirit. Those are the things that should direct a shared life.", "They should pursue what makes for peace and builds up one another. Paul says it is good to forego meat, wine or anything else that causes another to stumble. A person with a settled conviction can keep it before God. A person who eats while doubting acts against conscience, and Paul says that whatever does not come from faith is sin. Knowing that one is free to eat gives a person a choice about what to do with that freedom at a particular table."], "lesson": "Paul gives weight to both conscience and the effect of freedom on another person. A choice can be permitted and still be unhelpful in a particular company.", "question": "Why might a believer freely give up something at a shared meal?"},
  {"id": 91286, "title": "The Strong Bear With the Weak", "subtitle": "Welcome patterned on Christ", "text": "Romans 15:1–13", "chapters": [[44, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul has asked believers to think about the person beside them at the table. Now he sets a duty before those who count themselves strong. They ought to bear with the failings of the weak and refrain from making their own satisfaction the rule for everyone. Each should seek the good of the neighbour in a way that builds that person up. Paul places himself among the strong as he writes. He speaks of their strength as something that makes them better able to carry a burden with someone else.", "He gives Christ as the pattern. Christ did not live to please himself. Paul uses a line from the Psalms about bearing the insults aimed at God. He then pauses to explain why a line written long before can guide this fellowship. The Scriptures were written for their instruction. The endurance and encouragement they receive through them make room for hope. Paul asks the God who gives endurance and encouragement to grant them harmony with one another according to Christ Jesus. Then, with one mind and one voice, they can glorify the God and Father of their Lord.", "He brings the instruction back to a word he used at the start of the discussion. They should welcome each other as Christ welcomed them, to the glory of God. Christ became a servant to the circumcised to show God's truthfulness and confirm the promises made to the ancestors. The Gentiles, for their part, give glory to God for his mercy. Paul lets the readers hear several strands of Scripture together. One voice praises God among the nations. Another calls the nations to rejoice with God's people. The Psalms call all peoples to praise the Lord. Isaiah speaks of the root of Jesse rising to rule the Gentiles, in whom the nations will hope.", "Paul ends this part of the letter with a prayer that the God of hope will fill his readers with all joy and peace as they believe, so that they may abound in hope through the power of the Holy Spirit. He has taken a disagreement about meals and days all the way to a gathering of people who can praise God together. The welcome he asks of them follows from the welcome both have already received."], "lesson": "Welcome is work someone has to do, especially where strengths and convictions differ. Paul gives it a common purpose in the glory of God.", "question": "How does Paul's instruction to the strong follow from what he says about Christ?"},
  {"id": 91287, "title": "A Collection, a Journey and a Request for Prayer", "subtitle": "Serving across distances", "text": "Romans 15:14–33", "chapters": [[44, 15]], "start": 14, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul tells the believers in Rome that he is confident they are full of goodness, well supplied with knowledge and able to instruct one another. He has written rather boldly on some points to remind them, because God gave him grace to serve Christ among the Gentiles. He describes his work with the language of priestly service. He ministers God's gospel so that the Gentiles may become an acceptable offering, made holy by the Holy Spirit.", "Paul speaks of what Christ accomplished through him by word and deed, through signs and wonders and the power of the Spirit. From Jerusalem around to Illyricum he has carried the gospel of Christ. He made it his ambition to preach where Christ had yet to be named. He wanted to reach people who had never heard, as Scripture had said, and to avoid building on another person's foundation. This work had often kept him from coming to Rome. Now he sees no further place for that work in those regions and has long wanted to visit on his way to Spain. He hopes to spend time with the believers and receive their help for the journey.", "Before any of that he must go to Jerusalem. The believers in Macedonia and Achaia have gladly made a contribution for the poor among the believers there. Paul says they also owe a debt. Gentile believers have shared in the spiritual blessings that came through the Jewish people. They can now serve them with material help. When he has safely delivered the gift, he intends to go to Spain by way of Rome. He expects to come with the fullness of Christ's blessing.", "Paul asks his readers, through Jesus Christ and the love of the Spirit, to struggle alongside him in prayer. They can ask that he be delivered from opponents in Judea and that the believers in Jerusalem receive his service favourably. If God wills it, he hopes to come to Rome with joy and find refreshment among them. Paul still has a gift to deliver, dangers to face and a journey he cannot yet make. The readers can share in that work from where they are. His closing wish is that the God of peace be with them all."], "lesson": "Paul sees practical support as part of fellowship across communities. He also asks believers who may never travel with him to share in the work through prayer.", "question": "What work takes Paul to Jerusalem before his hoped-for visit to Rome?"},
  {"id": 91288, "title": "The People Who Carried the Work", "subtitle": "Recognising service by name", "text": "Romans 16:1–27", "chapters": [[44, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "Romans", "story": ["Paul commends Phoebe to the Roman believers. She serves the church at Cenchreae and has helped many people, Paul among them. He asks the believers to receive her in the Lord in a manner worthy of their calling and to help her in whatever business she needs from them. He sends greetings to Prisca and Aquila, his fellow workers in Christ. They risked their lives for him. Paul thanks them, and says the Gentile churches have reason to thank them too. He also greets the church that meets in their house.", "The greetings move through households and groups of friends. Epaenetus was among the first to come to Christ in his region. Mary has worked hard. Andronicus and Junia, Paul's relatives and former fellow prisoners, were in Christ before him. He names Ampliatus, Urbanus, Stachys and Apelles, and sends greetings to people connected with the households of Aristobulus and Narcissus. He remembers his relative Herodion, the work of Tryphaena, Tryphosa and Persis, and Rufus and his mother, who has been a mother to Paul as well. He greets the believers with Asyncritus, Phlegon, Hermas, Patrobas and Hermes, and the saints with Philologus, Julia, Nereus and his sister, and Olympas. Then he asks them to greet each other with a holy kiss.", "Paul also asks them to watch for people who cause divisions and put obstacles in the way of the teaching they received. Such people serve their own appetites and deceive by smooth speech. Paul rejoices that the Romans' obedience is widely known. He wants them to be wise about what is good and innocent about what is evil. The God of peace will soon crush Satan under their feet.", "Greetings come from Timothy and others with Paul. Tertius, who put the letter in writing, adds his own greeting. Gaius is host to Paul and the whole church, and Erastus, the city treasurer, and Quartus also greet them. Paul closes by giving glory to the God who can strengthen them through the gospel of Jesus Christ. A mystery long kept secret has now been made known through the prophetic writings to all nations for the obedience of faith. The great purpose announced at the beginning of the letter is still in view as it ends among people Paul knows by name."], "lesson": "Paul remembers people by name and sometimes tells what their work cost. The greetings put the earlier teaching about one body into the life of particular households and workers.", "question": "Which acts of service are made visible in the greetings Paul sends?"},
  {"id": 91289, "title": "A Household Reports a Division", "subtitle": "The church belongs to Christ", "text": "1 Corinthians 1:1–17", "chapters": [[45, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul writes as an apostle called by the will of God, and he names Sosthenes as a brother alongside him. He addresses the church of God in Corinth as people set apart in Christ Jesus and called to be holy. Their calling joins them to all those who call on the name of Jesus Christ in other places. The first thing Paul wishes them is grace and peace from God their Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. Before he asks the Corinthians to change anything, he reminds them who has called them and how widely that calling is shared.", "Paul thanks God for the grace the church has already received. In Christ they have been enriched in speech and knowledge, and the testimony about Christ has been confirmed among them. They lack no spiritual gift as they wait for the Lord to be revealed. Paul is confident that Christ will sustain them to the end so they will stand blameless on his day. He grounds that confidence in the faithfulness of God, who called them into fellowship with his Son. Their gifts and their future both give him reason to thank God.", "Then Paul appeals to them in the name of that same Lord. He asks them to agree with one another and put an end to the divisions among them. People from Chloe's household have told him about their quarrels. Some claim Paul as their leader, others Apollos or Cephas, and others say they belong to Christ. Paul asks whether Christ has been divided, whether Paul was crucified for them and whether they were baptised in Paul's name. Each question takes them back to something they received together.", "Paul remembers baptising Crispus and Gaius and then adds the household of Stephanas. Beyond those he cannot recall whether he baptised anyone else. He is grateful that nobody can use his baptising them to say they were baptised in his name. Christ sent him to preach the gospel. Paul says he was to do so without resting the message on eloquence, lest the cross of Christ be emptied of its power. He has answered their quarrel about names by asking them to remember whose name they called on and whose death brought them together."], "lesson": "Paul can give thanks for a congregation and still name a damaging habit plainly. He asks where the church's allegiance belongs before addressing its factions.", "question": "Which of Paul's questions exposes the weakness of building a party around a leader?"},
  {"id": 91290, "title": "The Word of the Cross", "subtitle": "Where boasting comes to an end", "text": "1 Corinthians 1:18–31", "chapters": [[45, 1]], "start": 18, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul has just spoken of the danger of emptying the cross of its power by relying on eloquence. Now he considers what people hear when someone speaks about the cross. To those who are perishing the message sounds foolish. To those being saved it is God's power. Paul recalls the Scripture in which God promises to bring human wisdom to nothing. Then he asks where the wise person, the scribe and the debater of this age stand. What can their recognised skill accomplish when it comes to knowing God?", "In God's wisdom, Paul says, the world failed to know God through its wisdom. God was pleased to save those who believe through the message that people judged foolish. Paul names two expectations his readers would recognise. Jews ask for signs, while Greeks look for wisdom. What Paul and his fellow workers actually proclaim is Christ crucified. This comes as a stumbling block to some and as folly to others. To those God calls, Jews and Greeks alike, Christ is God's power and God's wisdom. What people call God's foolishness is wiser than they are, and what they call his weakness is stronger than they are.", "Paul then asks the Corinthians to look around at the people who received that call. Few were regarded as wise by human standards. Few had influence, and few were from notable families. God chose what the world counted foolish to shame the wise and what it counted weak to shame the strong. He chose what the world held low and despised, things people hardly counted at all, to bring to nothing the things they thought mattered. Their own congregation provides evidence of what Paul has been saying about the way God works.", "The result is that nobody has grounds to boast before God. It is because of God that they are in Christ Jesus. Christ has become wisdom for them from God, as well as their righteousness, holiness and redemption. Paul finishes with the scriptural instruction that anyone who boasts should boast in the Lord. The Corinthians have been measuring themselves by names and distinctions that people in their city value. Paul brings them back to the one in whom their whole standing before God is found."], "lesson": "Paul connects the message of the cross with the kind of people God calls. Together they question the church's confidence in social status and persuasive display.", "question": "Why does Paul ask the Corinthians to consider who they were when they were called?"},
  {"id": 91291, "title": "What the Spirit Makes Known", "subtitle": "Depending on God in teaching", "text": "1 Corinthians 2:1–16", "chapters": [[45, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul asks the Corinthians to remember his first visit. He did not arrive with an impressive display of speech or wisdom. He had resolved to centre his teaching among them on Jesus Christ and his crucifixion. Paul recalls being with them in weakness, fear and much trembling. His message did not depend on persuasive arguments devised to display the speaker's skill. It came with a demonstration of the Spirit and power. He wanted the faith of the people listening to rest on God's power, where it could stand after he had gone.", "Paul does speak wisdom among the mature. He describes it as God's wisdom hidden in a mystery, which God determined before the ages for their glory. The rulers of this age did not understand it. Had they understood, they would never have crucified the Lord of glory. Paul draws on Scripture to speak of things no eye has seen, no ear has heard and no human heart has imagined, which God has prepared for those who love him. God has now revealed these things through his Spirit.", "The Spirit searches everything, even the depths of God. To help his readers grasp what that means, Paul starts with the inner life they know. A person's own spirit knows what goes on within that person. In a similar way it is God's Spirit who knows what belongs to God. The believers have received the Spirit from God so that they may understand the things God has freely given them. Paul speaks about these things in words taught by the Spirit, fitting spiritual words to spiritual truths.", "Someone who receives only what natural understanding can take in regards the things of God's Spirit as foolish and cannot recognise them for what they are. They are discerned spiritually. The spiritual person can discern these things and is not adequately judged by someone without that understanding. Paul takes his readers back to a question from Scripture about who has known the Lord's mind well enough to instruct him. Then he says that we have the mind of Christ. That is the answer to the question of where their knowledge comes from, and it gives them reason for humility about what they have received."], "lesson": "Paul cares about what supports faith after a speaker leaves. The Spirit's work gives teachers and hearers a reason to approach spiritual matters with humility.", "question": "What did Paul want their faith to rest on?"},
  {"id": 91292, "title": "The Field and the Building", "subtitle": "Working together under God", "text": "1 Corinthians 3:1–23", "chapters": [[45, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul returns to the way he found the Corinthians able to receive his teaching. He had to speak to them as infants in Christ and give them milk because solid food was more than they could take. He says they are still unready, and he names what makes that plain. Jealousy and quarrelling continue among them. One person follows Paul and another follows Apollos. Their treatment of the two men shows how much their thinking is still shaped by ordinary rivalry.", "What is Apollos, Paul asks, and what is Paul? They are servants through whom the Corinthians came to believe, each doing what the Lord assigned him. Paul planted and Apollos watered. God gave the growth. The person planting and the person watering belong to the same work. Each will receive according to his own labour. Paul calls the Corinthians God's field and God's building. The workmen have real duties, and the field belongs to the one who gives it life.", "Paul then considers the building. By the grace God gave him he laid a foundation as a skilled builder. Someone else is building on it, and each person must take care how he builds. The foundation is Jesus Christ. People may build with gold, silver and precious stones or with wood, hay and straw. The coming day will show the quality of the work as fire tests it. Work that survives brings a reward. The person whose work burns suffers loss, though he himself is saved as one passing through fire. Paul then reminds the whole congregation that together they are God's temple and that God's Spirit dwells among them. God takes seriously what anyone does to destroy that temple.", "Paul returns to the danger of thinking oneself wise by the standards of the age. He cites Scripture about God catching the wise in their craftiness and knowing how empty their thoughts can be. Then he asks the Corinthians to stop boasting about human leaders. Paul, Apollos and Cephas are all theirs, along with the world, life and death, the present and what is to come. They belong to Christ, and Christ belongs to God. The Corinthians began by trying to decide which teacher they belonged to. Paul turns their attention to the one to whom the whole church belongs."], "lesson": "The field image gives distinct tasks a place in one work. The building image asks people to consider the quality of what they add to the foundation.", "question": "What was within Paul's and Apollos's responsibility, and what belonged to God?"},
  {"id": 91293, "title": "A Steward Answers to His Master", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when opinions differ", "text": "1 Corinthians 4:1–21", "chapters": [[45, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul has told the Corinthians that their teachers are there for their good and that they belong to Christ. He now tells them how to regard those teachers. They are servants of Christ and stewards of the mysteries of God. What matters in a steward is faithfulness to the one who entrusted him with the work. Paul takes the verdict of a human court lightly in comparison with the Lord's. Even his own clear conscience cannot acquit him. The Lord is his judge. When the Lord comes, he will bring hidden things to light and disclose the purposes of people's hearts. Then each will receive whatever praise is due from God.", "Paul says he has applied these things to himself and Apollos for their benefit. They are to learn from the example to stay within what is written and stop taking pride in one teacher over another. He asks what they have that they did not receive. If they received it, what ground do they have for boasting as though they had made it theirs? He speaks about their satisfaction with a sharp irony. They act as if they are already rich and reigning, and Paul says he wishes they really were reigning so the apostles might reign with them.", "The apostles' actual circumstances make the irony plain. Paul pictures them at the end of a procession, like men condemned to die and made a spectacle to the world. The Corinthians consider themselves wise, strong and honoured. The apostles go hungry and thirsty, are poorly clothed and have no settled home. They work with their own hands. When insulted they bless, when persecuted they endure, and when slandered they answer kindly. They are treated as refuse.", "Paul writes to warn the Corinthians as his beloved children. They might have many guardians in Christ, but through the gospel he became their father. He urges them to imitate him and has sent Timothy to remind them of his way of life in Christ, the same way he teaches in every church. Some have grown arrogant as if Paul would never come again. If the Lord wills, he says, he will come soon and find out what power lies behind their talk. God's kingdom is a matter of power. He ends by asking whether they want him to come with a rod or with love and a spirit of gentleness."], "lesson": "Paul tells the church to hold its verdicts with restraint. He also gives an example people can examine in his life and in Timothy's account of it.", "question": "What does Paul say the Lord will bring to light when he comes?"},
  {"id": 91294, "title": "A Case the Church Had Tolerated", "subtitle": "Facing harm within the fellowship", "text": "1 Corinthians 5:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["A report has reached Paul about sexual immorality in the church. A man is living with his father's wife, conduct Paul says is not tolerated even among the Gentiles. He is disturbed by the act and by the congregation's response to it. The Corinthians have remained proud when they should have mourned and removed the man from their fellowship. Paul has just asked how they wish him to come to them. Here they can see the kind of matter that requires them to take his warning seriously.", "Paul is away from Corinth, yet he says he is present in spirit and has already passed judgment on the case. When they assemble in the name of the Lord Jesus, they are to act with the Lord's power. Paul speaks of handing the man over to Satan for the destruction of the flesh, so that his spirit may be saved on the day of the Lord. The action he calls for is severe. He gives it an aim beyond the immediate separation, the man's salvation on the Lord's day.", "To show why the congregation's response matters, Paul asks them to think about dough. A little leaven works through the whole batch. They are to clear out the old leaven and be a fresh batch, in keeping with what they already are. Christ, their Passover sacrifice, has been offered. The fitting way to keep the feast is with the unleavened bread of sincerity and truth. Malice and wickedness belong to the leaven that must go. Paul has moved from a single reported act to the habits the church allows to spread through its common life.", "Paul clarifies an earlier instruction about keeping company with sexually immoral people. Avoiding every immoral, greedy or dishonest person in the world would require leaving the world. He is speaking about someone who bears the name of brother and persists in sexual immorality, greed, idolatry, abusive speech, drunkenness or swindling. Paul tells them to withhold even the fellowship of a meal from such a person. God judges those outside. The church has responsibility for those within, and Paul ends by telling the congregation to remove the wrongdoer from among them."], "lesson": "The leaven illustration makes the congregation's response part of the issue. Paul treats shared responsibility and holiness as matters that require action.", "question": "How does Paul explain the scope of the judgement he asks the church to make?"},
  {"id": 91295, "title": "Disputes and the Use of the Body", "subtitle": "Belonging to Christ in everyday decisions", "text": "1 Corinthians 6:1–20", "chapters": [[45, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul turns to disputes between believers. He asks how one of them dares take a case against another before the unrighteous instead of bringing it to the saints. The saints will judge the world, he says, and even angels. Surely there should be someone among them able to settle ordinary affairs between brothers. Instead one believer brings another before unbelievers. The existence of these lawsuits is already a defeat. Paul asks why they would not rather suffer a wrong or bear a loss. As it stands, they are wronging and defrauding their own brothers and sisters.", "Paul warns that the unrighteous will not inherit God's kingdom. His examples include sexual immorality, idolatry, adultery, theft, greed, drunkenness, abusive speech and fraud. Some of the Corinthians had lived in these ways. Paul reminds them that they were washed, made holy and justified in the name of the Lord Jesus Christ and by God's Spirit. He brings those words into an argument about what they do with one another now. Their former conduct gives them a way to recognise the things from which they were delivered.", "Then Paul answers a claim about freedom. Even where something is permissible, it may be unhelpful, and Paul refuses to come under its power. Food is for the stomach and the stomach for food, and God will bring both to an end. The body is for the Lord, and the Lord for the body. God raised the Lord and will raise believers by his power. Paul asks them to consider what it means that their bodies are members of Christ. Joining oneself to a prostitute makes the two one body, as Scripture says. The person joined to the Lord is one spirit with him.", "Paul tells them to flee sexual immorality. He says this sin is committed against one's own body, and then asks whether they know what that body has become. It is a temple of the Holy Spirit, whom they have received from God. They belong to God because they were bought at a price. That is why they are to glorify God in their bodies. The chapter began with people claiming what was owed to them in a dispute. It ends with a reminder of the price already paid for them and the obligation that follows."], "lesson": "The chapter puts the rights people claim alongside the obligations of belonging to Christ. Paul asks whether a choice serves God's glory in the body and in the fellowship.", "question": "How does belonging to Christ change Paul's answer to the claim that something is permissible?"},
  {"id": 91296, "title": "Households and Their Different Callings", "subtitle": "Faithfulness in present responsibilities", "text": "1 Corinthians 7:1–24", "chapters": [[45, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["The Corinthians had written to Paul about relations between men and women. He begins his answer with married people and speaks to husband and wife in the same terms. Each owes the other the affection that belongs to marriage, and each has a claim on the other. If they set aside their ordinary relations for a time of prayer, it should be something they agree to together, with a time to come together again. Paul knows that self-control varies from person to person. He wishes others could live as he does, yet he recognises that one person receives one gift from God and another receives a different gift.", "He then speaks to the unmarried and to widows. Remaining single, as he has, is a good course for those who can do it. Those who find that the struggle with desire overwhelms them should marry. When he addresses couples already married, he appeals to the Lord's command against separation and divorce. He tells a wife who has separated to remain unmarried or be reconciled to her husband, and tells a husband to keep his marriage. A new conviction about what is spiritually desirable gives neither spouse permission to cast off the other.", "A different question arises where one spouse believes and the other does not. Paul gives his own counsel for this case. If the unbelieving husband or wife is willing to stay, the believer should remain in the marriage. Paul speaks of the unbelieving spouse as sanctified in relation to the believer, and of their children as holy. He also leaves their ultimate salvation open. How can either spouse know whether the other will be saved through them? If the unbelieving spouse chooses to leave, Paul says to let the separation happen. God has called believers to peace.", "Paul extends the principle to other conditions in which people first heard God's call. The circumcised and the uncircumcised need not change their outward status. Keeping God's commandments is the matter before them both. A slave called in the Lord is the Lord's freed person, and a free person called by Christ becomes Christ's servant. Paul speaks to the person who can gain freedom and reminds everyone that they were bought with a price. The household and the wider world may assign very different places to people. Each can begin to live with God in the place where the call reached them."], "lesson": "Paul applies the call of God to concrete situations. Faith can be practised within existing obligations while room remains for changes the passage permits.", "question": "What does Paul say about a believing spouse whose unbelieving spouse is willing to remain?"},
  {"id": 91297, "title": "Choices in a Time of Distress", "subtitle": "Making decisions with devotion in view", "text": "1 Corinthians 7:25–40", "chapters": [[45, 7]], "start": 25, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["When Paul turns to those who have never married, he says plainly that he has no command from the Lord on their particular question. He gives a judgement as someone whom the Lord has shown mercy and made trustworthy. The present distress weighs in his advice. A person bound to a wife should stay with her, and a person free from that bond need not seek a wife. Anyone who marries has committed no sin. Paul expects some who marry to face trouble in ordinary life, and he would spare them that trouble where he can. These sentences explain both the seriousness of his counsel and the room he leaves for a choice.", "His thoughts then turn to the shortness of the time. The married, the grieving and the joyful all have present concerns that can absorb their attention. A person may buy something and feel settled by what it adds to the household. Another may be so occupied with sorrow that tomorrow seems impossible to imagine. Paul names these conditions together and asks his readers to hold them in view of the passing form of this world. They still have marriages to care for, griefs to bear and purchases to make. Each of those things belongs within a life whose horizon reaches beyond the present order.", "Paul explains why he values singleness in these circumstances. An unmarried man can give his attention to the things of the Lord, while a married man also has responsibilities to his wife. The same distinction applies to a woman devoted to the Lord who has no husband and a wife whose life includes caring for her husband. Paul says that he gives this advice for their benefit. He wants them to attend to the Lord with as little distraction as possible, and he takes care to leave them freedom to act.", "He considers a case where someone believes marriage is the proper course and says to let it take place. A settled decision against marriage can also be honoured when it is made freely and carried through with self-control. Then he speaks of a widow. She is bound while her husband lives, and after his death she is free to marry whom she wishes, only in the Lord. Paul thinks she will be happier if she remains as she is. He gives that preference as his judgement, confident that he too has the Spirit of God."], "lesson": "Paul explains what makes his counsel fitting to the situation. His concern is that people can attend to the Lord while facing the responsibilities of the choice they make.", "question": "What reason does Paul give for the advice he offers in this part of the chapter?"},
  {"id": 91298, "title": "A Meal That Could Wound a Conscience", "subtitle": "Love governs what knowledge permits", "text": "1 Corinthians 8:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["The Corinthians bring Paul a question about food that has been offered to idols. Some of them possess an answer they consider sufficient. An idol has no real standing as a god, and there is one God. Paul shares that conviction. He speaks of one God, the Father, from whom all things come and for whom believers live, and of one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom all things came and through whom they live. Before he draws out what follows for their meals, he asks how they are using this knowledge. A person can grow proud of having the right answer. Love builds up the people around that person.", "Some members of the fellowship still associate the food with the worship in which it was offered. Habit and memory are part of what they bring to a meal. When they eat that food as something belonging to an idol, their conscience is troubled and defiled. Paul makes clear that food itself gives no one a better standing before God. Those who leave it alone lose nothing before him, and those who eat gain nothing before him. That should make it possible for a person with freedom about food to pause and consider the person whose experience is different.", "Paul gives the Corinthians a scene they can picture. Someone with knowledge sits down to eat in an idol's temple. A person with a weak conscience sees the believer there and takes courage from the example. The spectator goes ahead and eats although the act still means participation in idol worship to that person. The confident believer may imagine that the example has helped a brother or sister gain freedom. Paul sees a person drawn into doing what conscience still condemns. A meal has become the occasion for the ruin of someone for whom Christ died.", "That last description governs Paul's answer. The person at risk belongs to Christ, who gave his life for that person. When believers wound the weak conscience of a brother or sister, they sin against Christ himself. Paul applies the answer first to his own table. If food causes his brother to stumble, he says he will give up eating meat rather than cause the fall. The question that began with the status of an idol has reached the duty of the person whose example another believer is likely to follow."], "lesson": "Knowledge can describe what a person is free to do. Love also considers what someone else is likely to learn from watching that choice.", "question": "Why does Paul give weight to the conscience of the person who sees another believer eating?"},
  {"id": 91299, "title": "A Right Paul Chooses to Lay Aside", "subtitle": "Serving the gospel ahead of advantage", "text": "1 Corinthians 9:1–18", "chapters": [[45, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul asks the Corinthians to think about what they already know of his work among them. He has seen Jesus their Lord, and the congregation itself is evidence of his apostleship. Others may question his claim, yet the Corinthians have seen the fruit of his labour first-hand. As he answers those who examine him, he asks whether he and his fellow workers have the right to food and drink and to travel with a believing wife, as other apostles, the Lord's brothers and Cephas do. He asks why he and Barnabas should be treated as the only ones obliged to earn their own living while they carry out this work.", "The first examples come from everyday occupations. A soldier does not pay the costs of serving in the army. Someone who plants a vineyard can expect to eat its fruit. A shepherd drinks milk from the flock he tends. Then Paul goes to the law of Moses. An ox treading grain is to be allowed to eat as it works. Paul draws from that command a principle for the ploughman and the thresher, who both labour in hope of sharing in the harvest. If he and his fellow workers have sown spiritual things among the Corinthians, a share in their material goods is a reasonable return.", "Paul takes the argument further. Other workers already receive support from this congregation. Those who serve at the temple eat from what is brought there, and those who attend the altar share in the offerings. The Lord likewise directed those who proclaim the gospel to live from that work. Paul piles up the examples so the Corinthians can see the right clearly. Then he tells them what he has done with it. He and his companions have endured the cost themselves so that they would place no obstacle in the way of the gospel of Christ.", "Paul has no intention of turning this letter into a request for payment. He feels under necessity to preach, and he says that woe awaits him if he fails to do it. The work was entrusted to him as a stewardship. What reward, then, can he speak of as his own? He can give the gospel freely as he preaches it and leave unused the right that belongs to him in this work. His choice has meaning because the right is real and others may properly exercise it."], "lesson": "Paul can recognise a genuine right and still decide that its exercise would hinder his work in a particular setting. The reason for restraint is the good of the people hearing the gospel.", "question": "What right is Paul defending, and why does he choose to set it aside?"},
  {"id": 91300, "title": "The Runner and the Finish", "subtitle": "Discipline for the sake of others", "text": "1 Corinthians 9:19–27", "chapters": [[45, 9]], "start": 19, "end": 27, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul has just explained why he can preach without taking the support due to him. He now describes the wider choice that lies beneath it. As a free man he owes no one the service of a slave, yet he has made himself a servant to all so that he may win more people. Among Jews he lives as a Jew. When he is with people under the law, he can order his conduct as they do so that his own habits do not get in the way of their hearing. He makes this choice even though he does not count himself under the law in the same way.", "He can also live among people who are outside the law of Moses without insisting that their customs become Jewish before he speaks with them. Paul marks the boundary of that freedom. He is answerable to God and under the law of Christ. Then he names another group whom it would be easy to leave behind. With the weak he becomes weak, so that he may win the weak. The congregation has already heard what can happen when someone confident about food uses that freedom in front of a person with a troubled conscience. Paul is willing to feel the limits another person feels if that is what it takes to serve them.", "He gives the reason for these changes in his way of living. He does them for the sake of the gospel and hopes to share in its blessings. The responsibility is his as well as theirs. To show how seriously he takes it, Paul asks his readers to picture runners in a race. All run, and one receives the prize. He wants them to run with the purpose of obtaining it. Competitors practise self-control in all things for a crown that will perish. Paul thinks of a crown that will last and of the training appropriate to that hope.", "The picture changes from a runner to a boxer. Paul gives each movement an aim, like a fighter who strikes with a particular object in view. He disciplines his own body and brings it under control. After calling others to run, he wants to be fit to finish the course himself. The freedom to change his habits for the sake of his hearers and the discipline to govern his own conduct are part of the same work. Both have their place in the life of a person who wants others to receive the gospel he preaches."], "lesson": "Paul's flexibility has a purpose and boundaries. He exercises restraint in his own conduct so that his life does not contradict the message he gives.", "question": "What does the athlete image add to Paul's explanation of becoming a servant to many?"},
  {"id": 91301, "title": "Lessons From Israel in the Wilderness", "subtitle": "Receiving privileges with humility", "text": "1 Corinthians 10:1–22", "chapters": [[45, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul takes the Corinthians back to the days when Israel left Egypt. The whole company went beneath the cloud and passed through the sea. Paul describes them as baptised into Moses in the cloud and in the sea. They ate the same spiritual food and drank the same spiritual drink. He speaks of a spiritual rock that accompanied them and identifies that rock as Christ. As he tells it, the people shared in God's provision from the beginning of their journey. Yet the wilderness became the place where most of them fell. God had carried them through the sea, and their conduct on the other side still mattered to him.", "Paul remembers what happened when the people craved evil things. He brings to mind the feast around the idol, where the people sat down to eat and drink and rose to play. He recalls the immorality that brought a great loss of life in a single day, the testing that brought destruction by serpents, and the grumbling that ended in judgment. These events were written down for people living long after the wilderness, including the congregation now hearing his letter. Paul wants the person who feels sure of standing to watch their footing. He also tells the person who feels the pull of temptation that God is faithful. The testing they face is common to human life. God will provide a way through it so that they can endure.", "The immediate instruction is to flee idolatry. Paul asks the Corinthians to judge for themselves what takes place when they bless the cup and break the bread. The cup is a sharing in Christ's blood, and the bread is a sharing in Christ's body. They are many people, yet they are one body because they all share in the one bread. Israel's sacrifices give him another example. Those who eat the offerings share in the altar. In both cases the meal joins the people who eat to something beyond the food in their hands.", "Paul has already agreed that an idol has no standing as a god. He presses his readers to consider what happens in the worship practised around it. The sacrifices offered there are offered to demons, and he wants the Corinthians to have no fellowship with them. They cannot drink the Lord's cup and the cup of demons or share both tables. He closes by asking whether they mean to provoke the Lord to jealousy and whether they think they are stronger than he is. The question brings the congregation back to the wilderness, where a people with every reason to trust God still had to choose whom they would serve."], "lesson": "Shared privileges gave Israel real gifts and still left room for disobedience. Paul asks believers to learn from the old events before overestimating their own strength.", "question": "How does Paul place God's faithfulness beside the warning against confidence in oneself?"},
  {"id": 91302, "title": "At Someone Else's Table", "subtitle": "Seeking another person's good", "text": "1 Corinthians 10:23–33", "chapters": [[45, 10]], "start": 23, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul has just told the Corinthians to flee idolatry. He now follows them into settings where the decision about food can seem less clear. One of them goes to the market for meat. Another is invited to eat in the home of someone who does not believe. What should each do when the food might once have been offered in sacrifice? Paul begins with a familiar claim about what is lawful and asks what the use of that freedom will accomplish. Some choices contribute little to a neighbour's good. He wants each person to look for what will build up the other person at the table.", "At the market, Paul says, buy and eat without questioning the seller for the sake of conscience. He recalls the words of the psalm that the earth and all its fullness belong to the Lord. The buyer can receive food in that confidence. The same freedom applies to an invitation from an unbelieving neighbour. If a believer wants to go, the believer may eat what the host sets down without asking where it came from. Paul leaves room for the simple kindness of accepting a meal. The guest need not make the host account for everything on the table.", "Then someone speaks up and says the meat was offered in sacrifice. The food on the plate may be the very same food the guest was ready to eat a moment earlier. The remark gives the act of eating a significance for someone present. Paul says to leave the food alone for the sake of the person who pointed it out and for conscience. He specifies that he means the other person's conscience. His questions about freedom and thanksgiving keep the distinction in view. The believer can give thanks for food and still leave a particular helping untouched because of what eating it would communicate in that company.", "Paul brings the discussion back to God. Eating, drinking and anything else they do should be done to his glory. He names Jews, Greeks and the church of God as people the Corinthians should take care to avoid causing to stumble. He applies the same concern to his own conduct. He seeks the good of many so that they may be saved. That gives the guest at the meal a reason to pay attention when someone speaks. The act of putting down a piece of food can express the same purpose as the act of receiving it with thanks on another day."], "lesson": "The same food can be received in one circumstance and declined in another. Paul directs his readers to attend to the people present and the meaning the action has for them.", "question": "What new information at a meal changes the decision Paul gives?"},
  {"id": 91303, "title": "Honour in Prayer and Prophecy", "subtitle": "Considering others in public worship", "text": "1 Corinthians 11:1–16", "chapters": [[45, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul begins by commending the Corinthians for remembering him and holding to the instructions he passed on. His earlier appeal to follow his example sets the direction for the discussion, because he follows Christ. Now he turns to something worshippers do in plain sight when they pray or prophesy. He sets out a series of relationships, saying that Christ is the head of every man, the man is the head of the woman, and God is the head of Christ. The word head then carries his argument from those relationships to what people wear on their heads while taking part in worship. What they do outwardly can express honour or bring dishonour.", "Paul speaks first of a man praying or prophesying with his head covered and then of a woman doing either with her head uncovered. He says that each dishonours their head in those circumstances. When he compares an uncovered woman with one whose hair has been shaved, he presses the Corinthians to consider what the appearance would communicate. If the one would be felt as shameful, why treat the other as a matter of indifference? His example assumes that both men and women pray or prophesy. His immediate concern is the way they do so and the honour their conduct shows in the gathering.", "Paul goes back to creation to explain his reasoning. He calls man the image and glory of God and woman the glory of man, then recalls that woman came from man and was created for man. He says a woman ought to have authority on her head because of the angels, giving no further explanation of that brief reference. He then makes an important turn within the same argument. In the Lord, woman and man depend on one another. Woman came from man at creation, and every man since then has come into the world through a woman. Both owe their existence to God. That is where Paul places the final emphasis as he brings these relationships together.", "At the end Paul asks the Corinthians to judge whether it is fitting for a woman to pray to God uncovered. He appeals to what they recognise as fitting about the length of a man's hair and the glory of a woman's long hair, which he describes as given to her for a covering. He closes by addressing anyone inclined to dispute the matter and appeals to the practice of the churches of God. Taken together, his examples ask the congregation to pay attention to what their conduct says when they come before God, even in a detail someone might ordinarily overlook."], "lesson": "Public worship calls for reverence toward God and care for the people with whom we worship. Read Paul's instructions about honour alongside his reminder that men and women depend on one another in the Lord and that all things come from God.", "question": "How do verses 11–12 shape the way you understand the honour Paul asks for earlier in the passage?"},
  {"id": 91304, "title": "When One Eats and Another Goes Hungry", "subtitle": "Recognising the body at the Lord's Supper", "text": "1 Corinthians 11:17–34", "chapters": [[45, 11]], "start": 17, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Having praised the Corinthians at the beginning of the chapter, Paul comes to a matter for which he has no praise. The believers gather, yet their meetings make things worse. He has heard that there are divisions among them and says he partly believes it. The way they eat makes the division plain. Each goes ahead with their own supper. One person goes hungry while another has had enough to become drunk. Paul asks whether they have houses in which to eat and drink. Why should the gathering of God's church be the place where those who have little are made ashamed of it? A meal can carry the name of the Lord's Supper while the conduct around the table gives Paul reason to say that this is scarcely what they have come together to eat.", "He brings them back to the night when Jesus was betrayed. Paul passes on what he received from the Lord. Jesus took bread, gave thanks and broke it. He gave it to His disciples in remembrance of Himself, speaking of His body given for them. After supper He took the cup and spoke of the new covenant in His blood, telling them to drink in remembrance of Him. Every time the church eats the bread and drinks the cup, Paul says, it proclaims the Lord's death until He comes. That proclamation gives everyone at the table the same reason for being there. The person who arrived hungry comes to remember the same Lord as the person who brought plenty.", "Paul warns against eating the bread or drinking the cup in an unworthy manner, for doing so makes a person answerable concerning the body and blood of the Lord. He tells each person to examine themselves and then to eat and drink. He connects their failure to discern the body with judgment and says that many among them are weak and sick and some have died. He explains the Lord's judgment as discipline, so that they will escape condemnation with the world. His words give the congregation reason to take seriously what they are doing to one another while handling the signs of Christ's death.", "Paul finishes with a direction they can put into practice at the very next gathering. When they assemble to eat, they are to wait for one another. Anyone who is simply hungry can eat at home, so the gathering does not bring judgment on them. The other matters can wait until Paul comes. This part can change as soon as they next sit down together. Those who have been accustomed to going ahead can look around the table and make room for those they have been leaving behind."], "lesson": "Remembering Christ's death should change how we receive one another. Examine your own conduct before the Lord's Supper, including whether your habits leave someone overlooked or ashamed.", "question": "How would obeying Paul's instruction to wait for one another change what the whole congregation experiences at the table?"},
  {"id": 91305, "title": "Many Members in One Body", "subtitle": "Giving honour to the overlooked", "text": "1 Corinthians 12:1–31", "chapters": [[45, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul turns from the way the Corinthians share the Lord’s Supper to the spiritual gifts at work among them. He reminds them of the days when they were led away to idols that could give them no answer. Now he gives them a way to judge what they hear. No one speaking by the Spirit of God calls Jesus accursed, and the confession that Jesus is Lord comes by the Holy Ghost. From there Paul names three kinds of variety. There are different gifts, different ways to serve, and different works. Through them all he speaks of the same Spirit, the same Lord, and the same God. What the Spirit gives to each person is meant for the good of all.", "One believer receives a word of wisdom, another a word of knowledge, and another faith. Paul also names gifts of healing, miracles, prophecy, discernment of spirits, different kinds of tongues and their interpretation. The same Spirit works through these different gifts and gives to each as He wills. Paul then asks his readers to consider their own bodies. A body has many parts, yet it is one body. Jews and Gentiles, enslaved and free, have all been baptised by one Spirit into one body and made to drink of one Spirit. The distinctions that divided people in Corinth could never give one member a greater claim on the life they now shared in Christ.", "Paul lets the parts of a body speak. Suppose the foot says it has no place because it is different from the hand, or the ear says the same because it is different from the eye. A foot remains part of the body, and so does an ear. If the whole body were an eye, who would hear? If it were all ear, where would the sense of smell be? God has placed each member where He chose. Then Paul turns the conversation round. The eye has no grounds for telling the hand that it is unnecessary, and the head depends on the feet. The parts that seem weaker are indispensable. God has joined the body so that those who lack honour receive greater honour and all its members care for one another. When one suffers, the others suffer with it. When one is honoured, the others rejoice with it.", "Paul now tells the Corinthians directly that they are Christ’s body and each has a place in it. God has appointed apostles, prophets and teachers, and has also given miracles, healings, help, administration and different kinds of tongues. Paul asks a string of questions. Are all apostles? Are all teachers? Do all speak in tongues or interpret? The body he has just described already supplies the answer. He urges them to desire the greater gifts and promises to show them a more excellent way. The next chapter takes up that promise by showing what becomes of even the most impressive gifts when love is absent."], "lesson": "God gives each member a place in Christ’s body and gives spiritual gifts for the good of all. Give practical honour to the people whose service is easily overlooked. Share in their troubles and rejoice when they are honoured.", "question": "Think of someone whose contribution your fellowship could easily overlook. What would it look like this week to give that person the care and honour Paul describes?"},
  {"id": 91306, "title": "The More Excellent Way", "subtitle": "Love gives gifts their purpose", "text": "1 Corinthians 13:1–13", "chapters": [[45, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul has just described a body whose members need one another. Now he takes the gifts that impressed the Corinthians and asks what they amount to when love is absent. Imagine someone speaking in every human language and even in the tongues of angels. Without love, Paul says, that person has become a noisy instrument. Imagine someone able to prophesy, understand mysteries and possess extraordinary knowledge. Add faith powerful enough to move mountains. Paul puts himself inside the example and says that without love he is nothing. Then he goes further. A person might give away everything to feed the poor and surrender their own body to be burned. Even such costly acts bring that person no profit when love is absent. The scale of an action cannot by itself reveal the heart from which it comes.", "Paul next describes what love does among people who must live and serve together. It bears patiently with others and acts kindly toward them. It has no need to envy another person's gifts or advertise its own achievements. It gives up boasting and pride. Love treats people with respect, refuses to make its own advantage the governing concern and resists the impulse to answer every irritation with anger. It does not keep counting wrongs against another person. Nor does it find pleasure when someone falls into wrongdoing. It rejoices with the truth. These descriptions reach into the ordinary exchanges of a congregation, including what happens when one member receives the attention another wanted, or when a disagreement gives someone an opportunity to humiliate a neighbour.", "Paul continues with love's willingness to bear, believe, hope and endure. The love he describes remains faithful through difficulty. His earlier insistence that it rejoices in truth also belongs alongside these words. Then he looks beyond the present use of spiritual gifts. Prophecy, tongues and knowledge have their appointed limits. What believers know now is partial, and what they prophesy is partial. When completeness comes, the partial gives way. Paul recalls how he once spoke, understood and reasoned as a child. Growing into adulthood meant leaving those childish ways behind. He uses that familiar change to help the Corinthians consider the difference between their present understanding and the fullness still ahead.", "A second picture brings the argument closer. For now, Paul says, people see an imperfect reflection in a mirror. Then they will see face to face. His present knowledge is partial, but he looks toward knowing fully, even as he is fully known. Someone conscious of those limits has reason to be humble when exercising a gift or correcting another believer. Paul ends with faith, hope and love abiding, and names love as the greatest. The congregation that has been weighing one person's gift against another's now has a searching measure for its shared life. Whatever a person can say, understand, give or accomplish, love must shape the way that person serves."], "lesson": "Spiritual gifts and costly service call for a heart governed by love. Examine the patience, kindness, humility and truthfulness with which you treat the people nearest to you, including those whose gifts differ from yours.", "question": "Which description of love exposes something in the way you treated someone recently, and what practical change does that require?"},
  {"id": 91307, "title": "Words People Can Understand", "subtitle": "Using gifts to build up hearers", "text": "1 Corinthians 14:1–25", "chapters": [[45, 14]], "start": 1, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["After describing love, Paul tells the Corinthians to pursue it while desiring spiritual gifts. The purpose of a gathered church must govern the way those gifts are used. Someone speaking in a tongue may be speaking mysteries to God without being understood by the congregation. Prophecy, by contrast, brings strengthening, encouragement and comfort to those who hear. Paul does not forbid tongues or deny their value. He asks whether interpretation allows the church to receive benefit. The gift is not being measured as a private badge of importance. Its use in the assembly must be considered from the position of the people who need to understand.", "Paul turns to familiar sounds. A flute or harp must produce distinct notes if anyone is to recognise the music. A trumpet must give a clear signal if soldiers are to prepare for battle. Speech also needs meaning that its hearers can grasp. Otherwise people become strangers to one another despite occupying the same room. A person eager for spiritual gifts should therefore seek to build up the church. Someone speaking in a tongue should pray for interpretation. Paul will pray and sing with the spirit and also with understanding. Spiritual earnestness and intelligibility belong together because the gathering involves people who should be able to respond.", "He pictures a listener trying to say Amen to thanksgiving that cannot be understood. The thanksgiving may be sincere, but the other person remains unable to share in it knowingly. Paul thanks God that he speaks in tongues abundantly, then says that in church he would rather speak five understood words that teach others than ten thousand in a tongue. The comparison comes from someone who values the gift, which gives his correction particular force. He calls for mature understanding joined to innocence concerning evil. An impressive experience should not leave believers childish about the purpose for which they have gathered or careless about the neighbour listening beside them.", "Paul recalls the scriptural warning about foreign speech coming to a people who still would not listen. He describes tongues as a sign concerning unbelievers, then shows why the church must not confuse that sign with an automatically helpful impression. If visitors find everyone speaking in tongues, they may conclude that the congregation is mad. If understandable prophetic speech exposes the heart, the visitor may be convicted, fall before God and recognise His presence among them. The contrast directs the Corinthians away from a desire merely to astonish. The aim is that God's truth should reach people in a way they can receive, understand and answer. Love seeks the hearer's good, even when that requires the speaker to surrender a more impressive appearance."], "lesson": "Use spiritual gifts with love for the people receiving them. Clear, truthful speech helps others respond to God rather than remain spectators.", "question": "Do I give more attention to how impressive my contribution sounds or to whether another person can understand and benefit?"},
  {"id": 91308, "title": "Room for Others to Speak", "subtitle": "Order that serves the congregation", "text": "1 Corinthians 14:26–40", "chapters": [[45, 14]], "start": 26, "end": 40, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul imagines the Corinthians gathering with different contributions. One brings a psalm, another teaching, another a revelation, tongue or interpretation. The variety could serve the church, but it could also become a contest for attention. His governing instruction is that everything should build others up. Having something to offer does not mean having a right to speak without restraint. The assembly belongs to God and includes people who need to learn, discern and receive comfort. Paul therefore moves from affirming their contributions to regulating their use. Love must shape not only what each person intends but also when that person speaks and when they yield.", "For tongues, Paul allows two speakers or at most three, speaking in turn with interpretation. Without an interpreter, the person is to remain silent in the congregation and speak to themselves and God. Prophets likewise are to speak two or three, while others weigh what is said. A claim to spiritual inspiration is not exempt from examination. If another receives a revelation, the first speaker is to make room. The purpose is shared learning and encouragement. Paul says the spirits of prophets are subject to prophets, rejecting the idea that spiritual activity necessarily removes responsible self-control. God is the God of peace rather than confusion, and conduct within His church should reflect that character.", "Paul also addresses women, directing silence and submission in the churches and telling those wishing to enquire to ask their husbands at home. This instruction must be faced rather than omitted because it raises difficult questions. Earlier in the same letter, Paul discussed women praying and prophesying, so these words should also be read alongside that passage rather than isolated from it. Here the immediate argument concerns ordered speech and conduct in the gathered church. The text does not tell every circumstance behind the instruction, and explanations of its exact scope differ. What is clear is that Paul requires the Corinthians to receive apostolic direction rather than make their own assembly the independent source or sole owner of God's word.", "He challenges anyone claiming to be prophetic or spiritual to acknowledge that his written directions carry the Lord's authority. A claim to special insight cannot justify refusing the teaching meant to govern the whole congregation. His conclusion retains both desire and restraint: they should desire prophecy and must not forbid tongues, while doing everything decently and in order. The chapter does not end by eliminating spiritual gifts. It ends by bringing their exercise under love, discernment and peace. Every participant is asked to consider other people, submit to what God has given and accept that faithful service sometimes includes waiting. Order protects the opportunity for a contribution to be heard and tested instead of lost within competing voices."], "lesson": "Spiritual service calls for self-control, humility and willingness to be tested. Making room for others can serve the church as faithfully as speaking.", "question": "Which instruction here challenges the way I claim attention, respond to correction or make room for someone else's contribution?"},
  {"id": 91309, "title": "The Gospel Paul Received and Passed On", "subtitle": "Faith rests on a risen Christ", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:1–19", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 1, "end": 19, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul turns to the gospel he preached, which the Corinthians received and in which they stand. He reminds them that their salvation is connected with holding to that message rather than abandoning it. What he delivered was something he had himself received: Christ died for their sins according to the Scriptures, was buried and rose on the third day according to the Scriptures. The burial belongs within the testimony to a real death, and the resurrection belongs within the same announced saving work. Paul is not offering a new idea to satisfy curiosity. He is recalling the foundation of the faith they already profess.", "He lists witnesses to the risen Christ. Cephas saw Him, then the Twelve, then more than five hundred believers at once, most of whom were still alive when Paul wrote. James and all the apostles also received appearances. Finally, Christ appeared to Paul, whose calling he describes as unusually late. The account joins a received message to people who bore witness to it. The resurrection was not presented as a helpful feeling created inside the congregation. Paul announced that the crucified Christ had been raised and had been seen. The Corinthians' response to that claim mattered because it stood at the centre of the gospel, not at the edge of an optional discussion.", "Paul remembers his own unworthiness. He had persecuted God's church and did not deserve the name apostle. Grace made him what he was, and that grace did not remain fruitless. He laboured intensely, while insisting that the grace of God with him explained the labour. Humility did not mean denying the work done, and effort did not mean claiming independence from the One who enabled it. Whether the Corinthians had heard Paul or the other apostles, this was the message preached and believed. The witnesses differed in their histories and responsibilities, but the risen Christ stood at the centre of their common proclamation.", "Some in Corinth nevertheless said that there was no resurrection of the dead. Paul follows that claim to its consequences. If the dead cannot be raised, Christ has not been raised either. Then preaching is empty, faith is empty and the apostles have falsely testified about God. More seriously, believers remain in their sins, and those who died in Christ have perished. Hope limited to this present life would make their costly allegiance pitiable. Paul refuses to let the Corinthians keep the comfort of the gospel while removing its living Lord. Their disagreement about future resurrection reaches into the truth of Christ's resurrection and the reality of salvation. The question must be answered because the whole message depends upon it."], "lesson": "The resurrection belongs to the heart of the gospel. God's grace brings both humble gratitude and a life changed through faithful service.", "question": "How does Christ's resurrection affect what I believe about forgiveness, death and the purpose of my present service?"},
  {"id": 91310, "title": "The Firstfruits and the Last Enemy", "subtitle": "Hope that changes present conduct", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:20–34", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 20, "end": 34, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Having shown the consequences of denying resurrection, Paul states the truth on which Christian hope rests. Christ has been raised and is the firstfruits of those who have died. The first portion of a harvest points towards what follows. Death came through a man, and resurrection also comes through a man. Adam and Christ stand at the centre of the comparison: humanity shares the death associated with Adam, while those who belong to Christ will share the life secured through Him. Their resurrection follows His, at His coming. Paul's hope is therefore ordered around the risen Lord rather than a vague confidence that human life somehow continues unchanged.", "He looks forward to the end, when Christ hands the kingdom to God the Father after bringing opposing rule, authority and power to nothing. Christ reigns until every enemy is under His feet. The last enemy to be destroyed is death. Death is therefore neither treated as harmless nor granted the final word. Paul also distinguishes the Father who subjects all things from the things subjected. When all is brought under Christ, the Son Himself is subject to the One who subjected everything to Him, so that God may be all in all. The passage directs the reader towards the complete establishment of God's rule, where no hostile power retains an independent claim over His people.", "Paul then returns to conduct in the present. He asks about those baptised for the dead and what their action would mean if the dead were never raised. This brief reference does not explain the practice fully, and the passage should not be made to say more about it than Paul provides. He uses it as part of his argument against denying resurrection rather than setting out instructions for a new ceremony. His own life supplies another question. Why does he face danger repeatedly if there is no future resurrection? The daily risks of his ministry would make no sense if death ended every hope and the only aim were to preserve a comfortable life.", "He recalls the struggle associated with Ephesus and confronts the alternative outlook of eating and drinking because tomorrow brings death. That conclusion follows naturally if resurrection is removed, but it does not fit the gospel he has preached. He warns that corrupt company damages good conduct and calls the Corinthians to wake up, stop sinning and recognise the shame of ignorance about God among them. Future hope is therefore brought directly into present choices. What they believe about the risen Christ affects the teachers they follow, the risks they accept and the way they use each day. Resurrection does not invite carelessness about this life. It gives faithful endurance and righteous conduct a reason that death cannot finally overturn."], "lesson": "The defeat of death gives present obedience lasting meaning. Guard the influences that weaken this hope and encourage careless living.", "question": "What choice would change if I took more seriously Paul's claim that death is an enemy Christ will finally destroy?"},
  {"id": 91311, "title": "A Seed Goes Into the Ground", "subtitle": "The hope of a changed body", "text": "1 Corinthians 15:35–58", "chapters": [[45, 15]], "start": 35, "end": 58, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul anticipates questions about how the dead are raised and what sort of body they receive. He points to seed placed in the ground. What is sown does not emerge in exactly the form first buried, yet God gives it the body suited to it. Wheat and other seeds make the point familiar. Creation itself contains different kinds of flesh and different splendours among earthly and heavenly bodies. Sun, moon and stars do not all display the same glory. The Corinthians should therefore not assume that their present experience sets the limit of what God can provide. The Creator already shows a variety beyond one fixed bodily form.", "Paul applies the picture to resurrection. What is sown perishable is raised imperishable, what is sown in dishonour is raised in glory, and what is sown in weakness is raised in power. A natural body gives way to a spiritual body. His contrast does not simply replace a body with a bodiless existence. He continues speaking of a body, transformed for the life God gives. Adam became a living being, while the last Adam gives life. The earthly comes first, and the heavenly follows. Believers who have borne the image of the earthly man will also bear the image of the heavenly. The future belongs to Christ's power rather than to the limitations of mortal life.", "Flesh and blood in their present perishable condition cannot inherit God's kingdom. Paul then announces a mystery: not all believers will die, but all will be changed. At the final trumpet, in an instant, the dead will be raised imperishable and the living transformed. The perishable must put on imperishability and the mortal immortality. His language joins continuity of the person with a decisive change in condition. The hope is not that fragile bodies will simply struggle on forever. God will fit His people for a life over which decay and death no longer hold their present power. The transformation belongs to His promise, not a human technique for escaping mortality.", "Then the scriptural word about death swallowed up in victory will be fulfilled. Paul challenges death's victory and sting, identifies sin as its sting and the law as sin's strength, and thanks God for victory through Jesus Christ. He ends with an instruction directed towards ordinary work: be steadfast, unmovable and abundant in the Lord's service, knowing that such labour is not empty. The great future does not make present faithfulness insignificant. It assures believers that work done in the Lord is not finally swallowed by the grave. The chapter that began with the received gospel closes by giving tired hands and uncertain hearts a reason to continue serving the risen Christ."], "lesson": "Resurrection hope supports steady work in the Lord. Present weakness does not determine the future God has promised through Christ.", "question": "Which responsibility needs renewed perseverance because my labour in the Lord is not in vain?"},
  {"id": 91312, "title": "Setting Something Aside and Greeting Fellow Workers", "subtitle": "Preparing generosity in advance", "text": "1 Corinthians 16:1–24", "chapters": [[45, 16]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul closes with arrangements that bring the letter's teaching into shared daily life. He directs the Corinthians to prepare a collection for the believers, as he had instructed the Galatian churches. Each person should set something aside on the first day of the week according to their prosperity. This would avoid a hurried collection at his arrival. The church would approve trustworthy people to carry its gift to Jerusalem, with letters, and Paul might travel with them. Generosity was to be willing and proportionate, while its handling required the confidence of the congregation. A spiritual purpose did not remove the need to plan or account for the money entrusted.", "His travel plans also show the mixture of intention and dependence within ministry. He expected to pass through Macedonia and hoped to remain with Corinth long enough for more than a passing visit, perhaps through winter, if the Lord permitted. For the present he would remain at Ephesus until Pentecost because a great opportunity had opened, even though many opposed him. Opportunity did not mean the absence of resistance. He asked the Corinthians to receive Timothy without fear or contempt because Timothy did the Lord's work. Apollos had been strongly encouraged to visit but was unwilling to come at that time and would go when opportunity allowed. Paul's concern did not become a claim to control every worker's movements.", "The church was to stay alert, stand firm in faith, act courageously and be strong, while doing everything in love. That final qualification recalls the measure already given in chapter thirteen. Strength without love would fail the very purpose of service. Paul pointed to Stephanas's household, among the first believers in Achaia, who had devoted themselves to serving others. Such workers deserved recognition and willing cooperation. Stephanas, Fortunatus and Achaicus had refreshed Paul's spirit and supplied what was missing through the Corinthians' absence. The names keep ministry personal. A congregation's life is carried by people who travel, welcome, give, encourage and do tasks that are easily taken for granted.", "Greetings came from the churches of Asia and from Aquila and Priscilla with the church meeting in their home. Believers were to greet one another in holiness and affection. Paul added his own handwritten greeting, a solemn warning concerning those without love for the Lord and an appeal associated with the Lord's coming. He closed with the grace of Jesus Christ and his love for them all in Christ. The letter had contained severe correction, but it ended in personal affection rather than withdrawal. The troubled church was still called to live as Christ's people, receiving grace and showing love through dependable giving, respectful welcome and faithful work together."], "lesson": "Love takes practical form through planned generosity, welcome and respect for fellow workers. Correction should remain joined to a sincere desire for another person's good.", "question": "Which person or shared responsibility needs a practical expression of my love this week?"},
  {"id": 91313, "title": "Comfort Received and Passed On", "subtitle": "Learning dependence in trouble", "text": "2 Corinthians 1:1–11", "chapters": [[46, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul and Timothy greet the church in Corinth and the believers throughout Achaia with grace and peace from God. Paul then blesses the Father of mercies and God of all comfort. He does not begin by pretending that faithful ministry has made suffering unusual or easy. God comforts His servants within affliction so that they can comfort others with what they themselves have received. Comfort is therefore more than a private relief kept within one person's story. It becomes something passed into the lives of people whose trouble may require patient presence, truthful encouragement and a testimony that God's mercy has met real weakness.", "Paul connects the abundant sufferings associated with Christ's service to the abundant consolation received through Him. Whether the workers suffer or receive comfort, their experience is directed towards the Corinthians' good and endurance. He holds a firm hope for them because sharing suffering does not exclude sharing consolation. The relationship between apostle and church is not built on one side possessing strength while the other merely receives instruction. Their lives are joined through a Lord who sustains His people. Paul can speak openly about affliction because the gospel does not require him to maintain an appearance of being beyond it.", "He then recalls the distress that came upon them in Asia. The pressure exceeded their strength so greatly that they despaired of life. The letter does not give enough detail here to reconstruct the event, and the severity should not be reduced by inventing an easy resolution. Paul describes the experience as carrying a sentence of death within themselves. It taught them to rely on the God who raises the dead rather than on their own capacity. His faith did not consist in claiming that he had never felt overwhelmed. It lay in learning where trust must rest when human ability had reached its limit.", "God had delivered them, and Paul continued hoping in His deliverance. The Corinthians also had a part through prayer. As many prayed and God's gift was received, many could join in thanksgiving. Distance from the suffering did not make the congregation useless to those facing it. Their intercession belonged within the shared work, just as their later gratitude would belong within its answer. The opening passage brings mercy, weakness, comfort and prayer together. Paul is preparing a troubled relationship for honest conversation by showing that God's strength does not depend on hiding the need of His servants. What they receive from Him equips them to become a source of help to others."], "lesson": "Receive God's comfort without concealing the weakness in which you need it. Prayer and compassionate support let received mercy become help for others.", "question": "Whom could I comfort through patient care grounded in help I have received from God?"},
  {"id": 91314, "title": "A Changed Visit and a Call to Forgive", "subtitle": "Correction that seeks restoration", "text": "2 Corinthians 1:12–2:11", "chapters": [[46, 1], [46, 2]], "start": 12, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul appeals to the testimony of a clear conscience. His conduct towards the Corinthians has been marked by sincerity and God's grace rather than manipulative calculation. He had planned to visit them on his way to Macedonia and again on his return, but the changed arrangements had raised questions about his reliability. He asks whether he treated his promises lightly, saying yes and no according to convenience. His answer directs them towards the faithful God proclaimed through Jesus Christ. God's promises find their fulfilment in Christ, and God establishes both workers and congregation in Him, giving the Spirit as His pledge. Their relationship rests on something firmer than suspicion about an itinerary.", "Paul then explains that he delayed coming in order to spare them another painful confrontation. He was not claiming dominion over their faith. He wanted to work for their joy, since they stood by faith. Another visit in sorrow would wound the very people from whom he hoped to receive gladness. Instead, he had written with anguish and many tears. His purpose was to show the depth of his love, not make them suffer for its own sake. The explanation places difficult correction within a desire for restored fellowship. Firmness was meant to serve the people rather than demonstrate the apostle's ability to distress them.", "He turns to a person whose wrongdoing had caused grief. The precise identity is not established in this passage, so the reader need not force every detail into a reconstructed biography. The discipline administered by the majority had now been sufficient. Paul calls for forgiveness and comfort so that the person will not be overwhelmed by excessive sorrow. They must confirm their love towards him. A congregation can fail through refusing necessary correction, but it can also fail through continuing rejection after correction has served its purpose. Restoration needs a visible response that assures the person the community's aim is recovery rather than permanent disgrace.", "Paul joins their forgiveness and explains that he acts for their sake before Christ. He does not want Satan to gain an advantage, recognising the enemy's designs. In this setting, spiritual vigilance includes resisting a destructive refusal to restore. The passage gives no reason to separate concern about spiritual opposition from the treatment of an actual person. A changed travel plan, a painful letter and congregational discipline are all judged by the good they should serve. Paul's authority finds its purpose in helping believers stand and rejoice in Christ. His appeal asks the church to finish the work of correction through mercy, so that truth and love remain together in its life."], "lesson": "Correction should seek repentance and restoration. When its purpose has been served, continued humiliation can become another wrong.", "question": "Is there someone whose sincere return needs to be met with forgiveness and a clear confirmation of love?"},
  {"id": 91315, "title": "An Open Door and a Letter on Hearts", "subtitle": "God makes people sufficient for service", "text": "2 Corinthians 2:12–3:6", "chapters": [[46, 2], [46, 3]], "start": 12, "end": 6, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul recalls arriving in Troas to preach Christ's gospel and finding an opportunity opened by the Lord. Yet he had no rest because Titus was not there. His concern about the relationship with Corinth remained unresolved, so he left and went to Macedonia. The account lets the reader see a servant carrying emotional strain while genuine opportunities stand before him. An open door had not made concern for a fellow worker or a troubled church disappear. Paul does not hide that tension behind a polished account of uninterrupted success. His thanksgiving that follows arises within the actual life he is describing.", "He thanks God for leading His servants in Christ's triumph and spreading through them the fragrance of the knowledge of Christ. The image gives God the initiative and makes the workers bearers of something received. Their witness stands before both those being saved and those perishing, with profoundly different responses. Paul asks who is sufficient for such a responsibility. Proclaiming Christ is not simply a matter of making an attractive impression. The message concerns life and death and is spoken before God. The weight of that calling makes any easy confidence in personality or technique inadequate.", "Paul distinguishes his ministry from those who corrupt or trade upon God's word. He speaks sincerely, as one commissioned by God and accountable before Him in Christ. He then asks whether he needs to begin commending himself again or seek letters of recommendation to and from the Corinthians. They themselves are his letter, carried in his heart and open for others to read. More deeply, they are a letter of Christ, served through the workers and written by the Spirit of the living God. Their changed lives bear witness in a way that a document alone cannot produce. The work has reached human hearts rather than remaining ink on a page.", "This confidence comes through Christ towards God. Paul does not claim that the workers are sufficient in themselves or that every fruitful thought originates in their own ability. God supplies their sufficiency and makes them servants of the new covenant. The contrast between letter and Spirit concerns a ministry that exposes death and the Spirit who gives life. It is not permission to dismiss the written Scriptures or replace careful understanding with whatever feels inspired. Paul's own letter is being written to teach truth. He locates the life-giving work in God rather than in an impressive credential, preserving both the seriousness of service and the humility of those entrusted with it. Their dependence is the proper answer to the question of who can carry such a calling."], "lesson": "Serve sincerely before God and depend on His enabling. The lives helped through faithful service matter more than an impressive claim about yourself.", "question": "Where do I need to replace anxiety about my reputation with renewed dependence on God's help?"},
  {"id": 91316, "title": "The Veil Lifted", "subtitle": "Transformation in the Lord's presence", "text": "2 Corinthians 3:7–18", "chapters": [[46, 3]], "start": 7, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul continues comparing the ministry associated with the stone tablets and the ministry of the Spirit. The law came with glory, visible in Moses' shining face, though that glory was passing. Paul does not call the earlier revelation worthless. Its glory becomes the point from which he argues that the ministry bringing righteousness through the Spirit has a greater and enduring glory. What condemns cannot by itself supply the life it shows people to need. The new covenant hope concerns God's work through Christ, and its surpassing glory gives His servants reason for confidence in the message they have received.", "That hope leads Paul to speak openly and boldly. He recalls Moses placing a veil over his face and applies the image to a failure to perceive the fulfilment reached in Christ. He describes a veil remaining when the old covenant is read without that recognition. The argument concerns understanding and turning to the Lord. It must not be reduced to a claim that possessing an ancestry makes someone less human or undeserving of love. Paul's concern is how the promise is understood in relation to Christ, the One through whom he says the veil is removed. The needed opening is a work of grace, not an achievement that gives a believer grounds for pride.", "When a person turns to the Lord, the veil is taken away. Paul joins this turning to the work of the Spirit and the liberty found where the Spirit of the Lord is present. Freedom here belongs within access to God and the transformation he is describing. It is not an unrestricted permission to ignore moral commands or refuse accountability. The Spirit's life frees people for the relationship and obedience that condemnation alone could not create. A person whose face is unveiled can attend to the Lord rather than remain trapped in an effort to establish worth through an outward appearance of religious achievement.", "The closing picture embraces all believers beholding or reflecting the Lord's glory with unveiled faces. They are being changed into His likeness from one degree of glory to another through the Spirit. Paul describes an ongoing transformation, keeping attention on both its source and its direction. Spiritual experience is to issue in the likeness of the Lord, not simply a story of having seen something unusual. Patience, truthfulness, holiness and love belong within the life shaped by Him. The passage leaves the reader with a reason to turn, behold and depend. God's glory is not only an object admired at a distance. Through Christ and by the Spirit, His people are being brought into a changed life that displays the Lord they seek."], "lesson": "The Spirit's freedom leads into transformation in Christ's likeness. Look for His work in character and obedience as well as understanding.", "question": "What part of my character needs to be brought more fully under the transforming work of the Lord?"},
  {"id": 91317, "title": "Treasure in Earthen Vessels", "subtitle": "Light and hope carried in weakness", "text": "2 Corinthians 4:1–18", "chapters": [[46, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Because ministry has come through mercy, Paul refuses to lose heart. He has renounced secret shame, cunning and dishonest handling of God's word. Instead, he sets truth openly before people's consciences in God's sight. The gospel may still be veiled to unbelief, and Paul describes the god of this age blinding minds against the light of Christ's glory. His answer is to proclaim Jesus Christ as Lord and himself as a servant for Jesus' sake. The worker must not become the centre of the message. The God who commanded light out of darkness has shone into hearts to make His glory known in Christ.", "Paul calls this knowledge a treasure carried in earthen vessels. The container is fragile so that the surpassing power will be recognised as God's rather than the worker's possession. He describes pressure without final crushing, perplexity without total despair, persecution without abandonment and being struck down without destruction. These are not claims that trouble leaves no marks. He and his companions bear suffering associated with Jesus in their bodies so that Jesus' life may also be displayed. Their mortality remains visible. The gospel's power is therefore not proved by pretending that its servants are incapable of pain, uncertainty or exhaustion.", "Death is at work in the workers' costly service while life reaches the Corinthians. Paul speaks in faith because the God who raised Jesus will also raise His servants and bring them with the church into His presence. This shared future gives their labour a purpose larger than preserving their present comfort. The grace reaching more people will multiply thanksgiving to God's glory. The direction of the ministry remains outward towards the church and upward in gratitude. Paul is not asking the congregation to admire suffering as though pain were valuable by itself. He is explaining why faithful service can continue when the messenger pays a real cost.", "Although the outer person is wasting away, the inner person is renewed daily. Paul compares present affliction with an eternal weight of glory beyond comparison. Calling it light and momentary is a statement about that contrast, not a denial of the severe distress he has already described. He fixes attention on what endures rather than limiting hope to what can presently be seen. The passage gives tired servants a way to understand their weakness without making it their final identity. The jar remains breakable, but the treasure does not derive its value from the jar's strength. Renewed courage comes through God's mercy, the risen Christ and the promise that the work's deepest meaning is not exhausted by present appearances."], "lesson": "Weakness does not make faithful service empty. Handle God's word honestly and let His mercy and resurrection promise sustain you through real limits.", "question": "Where do I need to stop hiding weakness and ask for the help that allows faithful service to continue?"},
  {"id": 91318, "title": "A Tent, a Home and a Message of Reconciliation", "subtitle": "Living for the One who died and rose", "text": "2 Corinthians 5:1–21", "chapters": [[46, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul compares present bodily life to an earthly tent and speaks of an enduring dwelling provided by God. He knows the burden and groaning of mortal life, yet his desire is for mortality to be swallowed up by life, not for human existence simply to disappear. God has prepared His people for this future and given the Spirit as a pledge. The hope rests on God's provision rather than the tent's durability. Faith can therefore look beyond what is visible while still taking the present body seriously. Its frailty points towards need, but God's promise points towards the life He intends to give.", "Paul is confident about being with the Lord and makes pleasing Him his aim, whether present or absent. All must appear before Christ's judgment seat and answer for what has been done in the body. Future hope is therefore joined to present accountability. He persuades people in reverence before the Lord, while rejecting a contest based on outward appearance. Christ's love constrains him. Since Christ died for all, those who live should no longer live for themselves but for the One who died and was raised for them. The gospel changes both the basis of life and the purpose for which it is used.", "That change also affects the way Paul regards people. Old judgments based merely on outward standing no longer supply the governing measure. Anyone in Christ belongs to a new creation, with the old passing and the new coming into being. This renewal originates in God, who reconciles people to Himself through Christ and entrusts the message of reconciliation to His servants. The starting point is God's gracious action towards those who need forgiveness. The messenger cannot claim to have created the peace being offered. The task is to announce faithfully what God has done and invite people to receive the relationship made possible through His Son.", "Paul describes himself and his fellow workers as Christ's ambassadors, through whom God makes His appeal. They plead that people be reconciled to God. The sinless Christ was made sin for us so that in Him we might become God's righteousness. Paul gives the heart of the exchange without reducing it to a demand that people first make themselves worthy of approach. The message is costly grace grounded in Christ's work. It then calls those reconciled to live differently. The passage has moved from a frail tent and future dwelling to a present ministry of appeal. A life destined for God's presence is to be spent honouring the Lord and carrying His invitation to people whose worth can no longer be judged by appearances alone."], "lesson": "Christ's love gives a new purpose to life. Receive reconciliation with gratitude and let it shape your treatment of others and your witness to them.", "question": "What would change in one relationship if I acted from Christ's reconciling love rather than self-interest?"},
  {"id": 91319, "title": "An Open Heart and a Call to Holiness", "subtitle": "Integrity under hardship and pressure", "text": "2 Corinthians 6:1–7:1", "chapters": [[46, 6], [46, 7]], "start": 1, "end": 1, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul urges the Corinthians not to receive God's grace without a real response. The accepted time and day of salvation call for attention now. His own ministry seeks to avoid placing needless obstacles before the message. He describes endurance through affliction, need, distress, beatings, imprisonment, unrest, labour, sleeplessness and hunger. The list removes any expectation that God's service always presents an easy public life. Yet hardship alone is not his credential. Beside it he places purity, understanding, patience, kindness, the Holy Spirit, genuine love, truthful speech and God's power. The character of the servant matters within the pressure, because suffering does not excuse deceit or cruelty.", "He then describes apparent contradictions. Honour and dishonour, good report and slander accompany a ministry that some call deceptive though it is true. The workers seem unknown yet are known, seem dying yet live, bear sorrow yet rejoice, possess little yet enrich many. Their condition cannot be measured adequately through appearances. The gospel's riches are not identical with a comfortable treasury, and joy does not require the absence of grief. Paul is preparing the Corinthians to recognise a ministry they might otherwise dismiss for failing to resemble a display of worldly success. Its life is being given for others under God's care.", "His appeal becomes personal. He has spoken openly, and his heart is enlarged towards them. The restriction lies in their own affections, and he asks them, as his children, to open their hearts in return. That welcome is joined to a serious warning against being unequally yoked with unbelief. Paul asks what partnership righteousness can have with lawlessness, light with darkness or God's temple with idols. The issue is binding participation that compromises allegiance to God. It should be read alongside the letter's wider biblical setting, rather than used to forbid ordinary kindness or cancel the earlier instruction to remain with an unbelieving spouse willing to live together.", "God's people are His living temple, among whom He promises to dwell and walk. The call to come out from uncleanness stands beside His promise to receive them as sons and daughters. The next chapter's opening draws the response: cleanse every defilement of body and spirit and pursue holiness in reverence for God. Separation is therefore about fidelity to the Father who draws near, not a licence for contempt towards neighbours. The passage holds open-hearted relationships and moral clarity together. Paul's concern is that grace should produce a people who love sincerely, welcome truth and refuse loyalties that would hollow out the worship they profess. God's promises give both the invitation and the reason for their obedient response."], "lesson": "Grace calls for a present response in character and allegiance. Keep a generous heart towards people while refusing commitments that require disobedience to God.", "question": "Is there a relationship or arrangement in which I need both a more loving attitude and a clearer obedience to God?"},
  {"id": 91320, "title": "Titus Brings News From Corinth", "subtitle": "Sorrow that leads to a changed response", "text": "2 Corinthians 7:2–16", "chapters": [[46, 7]], "start": 2, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul again asks the Corinthians to make room for him. He has wronged, corrupted and defrauded no one, and his words are not intended to condemn them. They remain in his heart through life and death. He speaks with confidence and joy even amid affliction. He then returns to the troubled period in Macedonia. There was no bodily rest, with conflict outside and fear within. God, who comforts the downcast, comforted him through Titus's arrival. The comfort included the man himself and the report he carried. The Corinthians longed for Paul, mourned what had happened and showed renewed concern for him. A damaged relationship was beginning to heal.", "Paul acknowledges the mixed feelings surrounding his earlier letter. Knowing it had caused sorrow had brought regret, though he now rejoiced in its result. His pleasure was not that the congregation had been hurt. Their sorrow had led to repentance. He distinguishes grief directed towards God, producing a saving change without regret, from worldly sorrow leading towards death. Pain by itself is not the measure of a faithful response. A person may feel deeply and still cling to the wrongdoing or collapse into hopelessness. The sorrow Paul welcomes opens into obedience, showing that the correction has reached more than the surface of their emotions.", "He names the earnestness their response produced: concern, a desire to clear the matter, indignation at the wrong, reverence, longing, zeal and readiness for justice. Their actions showed a determination to address the situation. Paul explains that his letter was not merely about one offender or one injured person. It was meant to make their responsibility and earnest concern clear before God. A congregation's response to wrongdoing affects its whole life. Their renewed care comforted the workers, and Titus himself had been refreshed by the way all received him. His experience confirmed the confidence Paul had expressed about them.", "Paul had spoken truthfully to the Corinthians and spoken well of them to Titus, and their welcome had not left him ashamed. Titus remembered their obedience and reverent reception with growing affection. Paul closes this section rejoicing that he can again have confidence in them. The passage leaves room for both the difficulty of correction and the gladness of a response that receives it well. Neither the apostle's authority nor the congregation's feelings are the final object. The aim is a people brought back into truthful, loving conduct before God. Titus's arrival becomes a concrete example of comfort passing between persons: God strengthens a weary servant through another worker carrying news of a church willing to repent."], "lesson": "Judge sorrow by the response it produces. Godly repentance moves towards truthful action, repaired relationships and renewed willingness to obey.", "question": "What practical change would show that my sorrow over a wrong has become repentance?"},
  {"id": 91321, "title": "The Macedonians Ask to Take Part", "subtitle": "Giving willingly and handling gifts honestly", "text": "2 Corinthians 8:1–24", "chapters": [[46, 8]], "start": 1, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul describes God's grace among Macedonia's churches. Severe affliction and deep poverty had not prevented abundant joy or generous giving. The believers gave willingly and pleaded for the privilege of sharing relief for the saints. Before their material gift, they had given themselves to the Lord and to the work according to God's will. Their generosity was therefore a response of devotion rather than a purchase of favour. Paul tells the story to encourage Corinth, where Titus had already begun the collection. A church rich in faith, speech, knowledge, diligence and affection should also grow in this grace of practical care.", "Paul does not present the appeal as a command forcing an amount. Others' earnestness offers an opportunity to show the sincerity of Corinth's love. The central example is Jesus Christ, who became poor for their sake so that they might receive His riches. His self-giving sets the character of the appeal. The Corinthians had begun both desiring and acting a year earlier. Now willingness needed completion from what they actually possessed. An accepted gift is measured according to what a person has, not what the person lacks. The passage therefore calls for generosity without granting a collector permission to demand imaginary resources or treat poverty as disobedience.", "Paul explains that he does not intend relief for others to become oppression for the givers. Their present abundance can meet another community's need, just as another community may later supply theirs. He recalls the scriptural pattern in which the person gathering much had no excess and the person gathering little had no lack. The purpose is mutual provision within God's people. Giving joins believers whose situations differ and helps them recognise shared dependence. The Macedonians' extraordinary willingness is honoured, while Paul's instruction concerning available means protects the appeal from becoming a rule that everyone must reproduce the same sacrifice regardless of circumstance.", "Titus shares Paul's earnest concern and goes willingly. Other approved brothers accompany him, including one chosen by the churches to travel with the gift and another repeatedly proved diligent. Paul deliberately arranges matters honourably before both God and people, avoiding blame in the handling of a substantial collection. Trustworthiness does not make visible accountability unnecessary. It welcomes arrangements that reassure the congregation and protect the work from suspicion. He asks Corinth to receive these messengers and demonstrate its love. The chapter joins a generous heart to careful administration, with neither replacing the other. A gift offered for God's glory should be received and delivered in a way that also honours those who entrust it."], "lesson": "Let generosity grow from devotion to Christ and complete good intentions according to your means. Handle shared resources with clear and trustworthy accountability.", "question": "What willing commitment should I now complete, and what arrangement would help it be carried out honestly?"},
  {"id": 91322, "title": "A Gift Ready Before the Visitors Arrive", "subtitle": "Cheerful generosity and thanksgiving", "text": "2 Corinthians 9:1–15", "chapters": [[46, 9]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul knows the Corinthians' readiness to help and has spoken of it to Macedonia. Their enthusiasm has encouraged others. Yet a promised gift still needs preparation. He sends brothers ahead so that his confidence will not prove empty if visitors find the collection unfinished. The aim is to have the offering ready as a blessing, freely given rather than squeezed out at the last moment. Preparation protects willingness. A person who intends generosity may need time to make the promise practical, while a sudden public demand can turn the act into embarrassment or pressure. Paul wants the gift's manner to agree with its purpose.", "He uses the image of sowing. Sparse sowing produces a sparse harvest, while generous sowing produces generously. Each person should give according to a decision made in the heart, without resentment or compulsion, because God loves a cheerful giver. The image is not a contract guaranteeing personal wealth in return for payment. Paul describes grace making believers sufficient to abound in good work. The One who supplies seed and bread can also increase the fruit of righteousness. The purpose of receiving from God remains connected to the good others will receive, not a promise that the giver will escape every material difficulty.", "The service will meet the saints' needs and produce abundant thanksgiving to God. Recipients will recognise a practical expression of obedience to Christ's gospel and glorify God for the generous fellowship shown to them and others. They will also pray for the givers with affection because of God's grace at work among them. The gift therefore moves in more than one direction. Material help reaches one community, while gratitude, prayer and strengthened fellowship reach beyond it. What began as a collection becomes an expression of a shared life under Christ. The administration matters because its result is meant to nourish both bodies and relationships.", "Paul ends by thanking God for His indescribable gift. The conclusion returns the reader to the generosity that makes human giving possible. The church is not the original source of grace, and the people receiving help are not merely an occasion for the donors to feel important. Both stand before God as recipients. The chapter encourages a thoughtful examination of our own practice: whether we prepare, whether we give freely and whether our methods allow the act to bless rather than shame. Cheerfulness need not mean a careless lack of planning. A settled decision carried out dependably can express profound joy. Giving in that manner directs attention towards the Lord whose provision has become practical help through His people."], "lesson": "Prepare generosity so that it remains willing and dependable. Seek the good of recipients and the thanksgiving due to God rather than personal display or promised profit.", "question": "What practical preparation would help my giving become a blessing freely offered rather than a hurried response to pressure?"},
  {"id": 91323, "title": "Letters, Presence and the Limits of Boasting", "subtitle": "Letting the Lord decide what is commendable", "text": "2 Corinthians 10:1–18", "chapters": [[46, 10]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul appeals through Christ's meekness and gentleness to people who accuse him of being timid in person and bold only when absent. He asks that his coming should not require the firmness he is prepared to use against those misjudging his ministry. Though he lives in an ordinary human body, the conflict is not carried out through merely human weapons. Its power comes from God. Paul describes the overthrow of arguments and proud claims raised against the knowledge of God, bringing thought into obedience to Christ. The image concerns the truth of the gospel confronting resistant reasoning and disobedience. It gives no licence to coerce a person's conscience through physical force.", "He challenges the Corinthians' reliance on outward appearance. Anyone confident of belonging to Christ should recognise that Paul also belongs to Him. The authority he has received is for building the church rather than destroying it. His letters are not intended as empty threats, and his presence will not contradict their seriousness. The criticism about weak appearance and unimpressive speech has mistaken a servant's outward manner for the limits of God's commission. Paul does not answer by promising to become more theatrical. He insists on consistency between what he writes and what he will actually do for the congregation's good.", "He then questions those who commend themselves by comparing themselves within their own circle. A group can construct a flattering measure that never submits its claims to God's judgment. Paul refuses to boast beyond the sphere God has assigned, which genuinely includes Corinth because he reached them with Christ's gospel. He does not need to take credit for another worker's labour to establish his relationship with them. His hope is that their growing faith will enlarge the opportunity to carry the message further. The direction is towards service still needed, not the accumulation of borrowed achievements that make the worker appear greater.", "Anyone who boasts should boast in the Lord. Approval belongs to the one He commends, not merely the person praising himself. This conclusion places both critics and apostle under a standard higher than reputation. The church needs discernment about authority, but discernment must attend to truth, entrusted work and its purpose. Neither a forceful appearance nor a circle of admirers can supply God's approval. The passage also gives workers a safeguard against pride: recognise the boundary of what has been given, honour the labour of others and use influence to build people up. Paul's defence concerns his calling, yet its goal remains a faithful congregation and an expanding witness rather than victory in a competition for status."], "lesson": "Measure service by truth and God's entrusted responsibility. Influence should build others up, and genuine approval cannot be manufactured through comparison or self-praise.", "question": "Am I claiming more than my actual responsibility, or measuring myself only by people likely to admire me?"},
  {"id": 91324, "title": "A Different Message and Impressive Messengers", "subtitle": "Discernment when a speaker looks convincing", "text": "2 Corinthians 11:1–15", "chapters": [[46, 11]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul asks the Corinthians to bear with what he calls a little foolishness, meaning the reluctant self-defence the situation has drawn from him. His jealousy concerns their faithfulness to Christ. He pictures the congregation promised to one husband and fears deception like that through which the serpent misled Eve. The danger is a corruption of sincere devotion. He names its substance: another Jesus, a different spirit and a different gospel from what they received. The congregation's willingness to tolerate such a change disturbs him. A teacher's apparent confidence cannot make the message safe when it moves the people away from the Christ proclaimed to them.", "Paul is not inferior to the impressive figures against whom his ministry is being measured. He admits that he may lack their polished manner of speech, but not the knowledge made clear among the Corinthians. He asks whether preaching the gospel to them without charge was somehow an offence. Other churches had supplied what he needed, including brothers who came from Macedonia, so that he would not burden Corinth. His striking language about taking support from others exposes the cost of that arrangement. The congregation had received ministry without bearing all its expense, and should not turn that generosity into a reason for distrusting the worker.", "He intends to continue the practice in Achaia. Its reason is not lack of love, as God knows. He wants to remove an opportunity from rivals seeking to place their claims on the same footing as his. The dispute concerns more than a preferred financial arrangement. It involves the use of appearances to draw the congregation's allegiance and the distortion of a gospel whose servant has deliberately given himself for them. Paul's own affection and refusal to exploit them supply part of the evidence they should consider. They must examine what a ministry actually seeks and produces rather than judge its worth by how much it charges or how impressive its representative appears.", "He identifies the opponents as false apostles and deceptive workers presenting themselves as Christ's apostles. Such disguise should not seem impossible, because Satan can appear as an angel of light and his servants as ministers of righteousness. The warning does not mean that everyone with a different style or opinion must be labelled satanic. It concerns the deception Paul has specified in a changed Christ, spirit and gospel. The final measure will include the workers' deeds. Discernment therefore joins careful attention to the message with examination of conduct and purpose. The church's loyalty belongs to Christ, and no attractive messenger has the right to redirect it towards another gospel while retaining the language of Christian service."], "lesson": "Test a ministry by the Christ it proclaims, the gospel it teaches and the conduct it produces. Appearance and polish cannot replace faithfulness.", "question": "Which claim or practice should I examine more carefully before accepting a teacher's authority?"},
  {"id": 91325, "title": "The Experiences Paul Would Rather Mention", "subtitle": "Strength measured by care in hardship", "text": "2 Corinthians 11:16–33", "chapters": [[46, 11]], "start": 16, "end": 33, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul again calls the coming comparison foolish, but asks the Corinthians to hear him because they have tolerated so much boasting from others. His sharp irony exposes their claim to wisdom while accepting domination, exploitation, arrogance and even humiliation. They have treated harsh behaviour as though it proved superior authority. If such conduct is called strength, Paul admits he has been weak by that measure. He then answers the claims about ancestry. He too is a Hebrew, an Israelite and a descendant of Abraham. The rivals do not possess an identity that automatically excludes him from the service they claim.", "When he turns to ministry, however, his list moves away from prestige. He names greater labour, imprisonment, repeated beatings and exposure to death. Five times he received thirty-nine lashes. Three times he was beaten with rods, once stoned and three times shipwrecked, including a night and day in the sea. The details describe a costly history without allowing the reader to reconstruct every event from the account of Acts alone. Paul has endured dangers from rivers, robbers, fellow Jews, Gentiles, cities, wilderness, sea and false brothers. The life in which he served Christ repeatedly placed him where comfort and safety could not be taken for granted.", "Weariness, sleeplessness, hunger, thirst, lack of food, cold and inadequate clothing added bodily need to public opposition. Beyond these outward pressures, he carried daily concern for the churches. Another believer's weakness affected him, and another's stumbling troubled him deeply. That inward burden gives the catalogue its pastoral centre. Paul is not collecting injuries as trophies to prove that he deserves unlimited obedience. He is showing a life spent for congregations whose well-being remains personal to him. The servant's strength cannot be measured only through his capacity to impose. It is also displayed through a willingness to bear cost and remain attentive to those needing help.", "If he must boast, he will speak of what reveals weakness. Calling God as witness to his truthfulness, he ends with an escape from Damascus. The governor under King Aretas guarded the city to arrest him, but he was lowered in a basket through an opening in the wall and escaped. The ending offers little material for a heroic public display. A hunted man depended on others and an unglamorous means of rescue. It fits Paul's wider argument precisely. God's service does not require the worker to appear invulnerable. The church that has mistaken domination for authority is asked to recognise faithfulness in a servant who suffers, cares and receives help rather than always looking triumphant."], "lesson": "Do not confuse domination with spiritual strength. Costly care, truthful endurance and willingness to receive help can reveal faithful service more clearly than impressive claims.", "question": "Whose quiet endurance or care have I undervalued because it lacks an impressive outward appearance?"},
  {"id": 91326, "title": "The Thorn and the Answer Paul Receives", "subtitle": "Receiving grace in weakness", "text": "2 Corinthians 12:1–21", "chapters": [[46, 12]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul reluctantly turns to visions and revelations. He describes a man in Christ caught up fourteen years earlier to the third heaven and paradise. Whether in the body or outside it, he does not know, and repeats that God knows. The man heard words he was not permitted to repeat. Paul allows the extraordinary experience to remain bounded by what can truthfully be said. He will not use it to make people think more of him than his observable life and message warrant. The passage therefore reports revelation without supplying readers a method for reproducing it or permission to invent details about the heavenly experience.", "The abundance of revelation could have led to pride. Paul describes a thorn in the flesh, a messenger of Satan troubling him, that kept him from being exalted. The exact condition is not identified here. Three times he asked the Lord to remove it. The answer was that God's grace was sufficient and His strength perfected in weakness. Paul therefore became willing to boast in weakness so that Christ's power might rest upon him. The answer did not accuse him of failing to pray sincerely. It supplied a promise by which he could continue serving within a difficulty he had wanted taken away.", "He returns to Corinth's judgment of his ministry. Signs of an apostle had appeared among them with perseverance, signs, wonders and mighty works. He had not burdened them financially and now prepared to come again, seeking them rather than their possessions. Like a parent providing for children, he would gladly spend and be spent, even if increased love met diminished affection. He also challenges suspicion that an apparently free ministry concealed exploitation through others. Titus and the accompanying brother had not taken advantage of them. The same spirit and conduct marked their service. His defence keeps returning to the people's good rather than the worker's enrichment.", "All these explanations are spoken before God in Christ for their building up. Paul fears that on arrival he may find quarrelling, jealousy, anger, slander, arrogance and disorder, together with sexual sin that has not been repented of. Such a discovery would bring grief rather than satisfaction that he had proved himself right. The chapter moves from an extraordinary revelation through ongoing weakness to ordinary congregational conduct. A spiritual experience does not make those daily matters insignificant. Christ's sufficient grace sustains His servant in confronting them with costly love. The power displayed in weakness is meant to serve a holy, restored people, not simply provide Paul an unusual story with which to win admiration."], "lesson": "God's sufficient grace can sustain faithful service within an unresolved difficulty. Let spiritual experience lead to humility, integrity and care for others.", "question": "What weakness needs to be brought to Christ's sufficient grace while I continue the duty He has given me?"},
  {"id": 91327, "title": "Before the Third Visit", "subtitle": "Examining yourself and seeking restoration", "text": "2 Corinthians 13:1–14", "chapters": [[46, 13]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Corinthians", "story": ["Paul prepares the Corinthians for his third visit. Matters will be established through two or three witnesses, and those continuing in earlier sin must not assume that correction will always be delayed. They have demanded proof that Christ speaks through Paul. He answers by directing them towards Christ's power already at work among them. The Lord was crucified in weakness but lives through God's power. The workers also share weakness in Him and will act through that power. Outward frailty therefore does not make apostolic responsibility empty. Yet the coming firmness will have to serve truth rather than a desire to win a personal contest.", "The demand for proof turns back upon the congregation. They should examine themselves to see whether they are in the faith and recognise Christ's presence among them. Paul's words call for honest testing rather than endless self-torment or an excuse for accusing neighbours. The people questioning a messenger must also consider their own response to the message. Persistent refusal to repent cannot be hidden behind a debate about his appearance or style. He hopes they will recognise the genuineness of his ministry, but the next part of his prayer shows that his reputation is not the highest good he seeks.", "He prays that they will do no evil. He would rather they act rightly even if he appears unapproved, than need a confrontation that publicly establishes his strength. Nothing can rightly be done against the truth, only for it. If he seems weak while the church becomes strong, he is glad. His desire is their restoration and maturity. He writes before arriving so that severe action will not be necessary. The authority the Lord gave him is intended for building up rather than tearing down. The passage gives a clear measure for spiritual leadership: the worker's vindication must remain subordinate to the people's truthful, holy growth.", "The final appeal calls them towards restoration, encouragement, agreement and peace, with the God of love and peace among them. A holy greeting expresses fellowship, and the saints send their greetings. Paul closes with the grace of Jesus Christ, the love of God and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit. This blessing embraces the whole troubled congregation he has corrected so earnestly. The letter does not end in a withdrawal of affection, nor does affection make the call to repent unnecessary. Grace, love and shared life with God supply the setting for a renewed community. Paul wants his coming to meet people who have already answered the truth, so that the visit can strengthen a fellowship learning to live faithfully in the Lord's presence."], "lesson": "Examine your own response to Christ as honestly as you examine others. Use influence for restoration and desire another person's good above personal vindication.", "question": "Where should I seek a person's restoration instead of an opportunity to prove that I was right?"},
  {"id": 91328, "title": "An Opening That Goes Straight to the Trouble", "subtitle": "Holding to the gospel received", "text": "Galatians 1:1–10", "chapters": [[47, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul opens by identifying his apostleship as coming through Jesus Christ and God the Father, who raised Him from the dead. The brothers with him join the greeting to Galatia's churches. Grace and peace come from the Father and the Lord Jesus, who gave Himself for sins to deliver His people from the present evil age according to God's will. Even before the dispute is named, the greeting establishes the source and substance of the message. Salvation begins with God's purpose and Christ's self-giving. The churches' future cannot be secured by treating that gift as an incomplete beginning requiring another basis for acceptance.", "Paul then moves directly to astonishment. The Galatians are quickly turning from the One who called them in Christ's grace towards a different gospel. He immediately explains that there is no genuine alternative gospel. People are troubling the churches and distorting Christ's message. The issue is therefore more serious than an unfamiliar expression or a disagreement over personal preference. What is being offered changes the good news itself and draws people from the grace through which they were called. The opening urgency asks the congregation to recognise that the identity and purpose of the message matter more than the confidence with which a new teacher delivers it.", "Paul places every messenger under the same test. Even if he and his companions, or an angel from heaven, announce a gospel contrary to the one already preached, that messenger stands under condemnation. He repeats the warning concerning anyone who teaches a different gospel from what they received. His own reputation does not give him permission to replace the message later. Nor would a claim to supernatural appearance make a distorted teaching true. The fixed point is the gospel of Christ, not the ability of a speaker to sound authoritative or offer an experience beyond the congregation's ordinary knowledge. The severity of the warning reflects what is at stake in abandoning that foundation.", "Paul asks whether he is seeking human approval or God's, and whether his purpose is to please people. If pleasing people governed his service, he would not be Christ's servant. That statement does not make harshness a virtue or release a teacher from love. It explains why he cannot soften the issue into something harmless simply to avoid offence. The churches need a truthful warning because grace is being obscured. The passage calls readers to examine both the teaching they receive and the desire for approval within themselves. A faithful messenger remains accountable to Christ, and a discerning hearer refuses to make prestige or apparent spiritual power the final measure of the gospel."], "lesson": "Test every message by the gospel of Christ, including messages from admired teachers. Seek God's approval without allowing the desire to please people to alter truth.", "question": "What claim am I accepting mainly because of the speaker's reputation rather than its agreement with the gospel?"},
  {"id": 91329, "title": "The Persecutor Becomes a Preacher", "subtitle": "Grace that changes an established direction", "text": "Galatians 1:11–24", "chapters": [[47, 1]], "start": 11, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul explains that the gospel he preached did not originate as a human invention or a lesson received from another teacher. It came through the revelation of Jesus Christ. This claim matters within the letter because opponents have raised questions about the basis of his authority and message. He does not answer only with a title. He begins recounting the direction his life had taken before grace interrupted it. The Galatians had heard about his former conduct, his fierce persecution of God's church and his efforts to destroy it. His earlier zeal was substantial, but it had been turned against the people he would later serve.", "He had advanced beyond many contemporaries in commitment to ancestral traditions. The account leaves no basis for assuming that intensity alone makes a course faithful. A determined person can defend a wrong direction with great energy. Paul then describes God's initiative: the One who set him apart from birth called him by grace and revealed His Son in him so that he might preach among the Gentiles. The turning point is God's mercy and commission rather than Paul's discovery that his previous achievements had earned a better assignment. He recognises purpose reaching back before his own awareness, while freely naming the harm he actually caused.", "After this revelation, he did not immediately seek human confirmation or go to Jerusalem to the earlier apostles. He went to Arabia and returned to Damascus. Three years later he visited Peter in Jerusalem and stayed fifteen days. Of the other apostles, he saw only James, the Lord's brother. Paul solemnly calls God as witness to the truth of this account. The sequence supports his argument about the message's source without making fellowship with other apostles unimportant. He is distinguishing the origin of his gospel from later meetings, not claiming that an isolated teacher never needs to answer questions or recognise other servants.", "He then went to Syria and Cilicia. The churches in Judea did not know him personally, but they heard that the former persecutor now preached the faith he had once attacked. They glorified God because of him. The change became a reason for worship rather than merely admiration of an unusual personality. The man whose name had once brought fear was now associated with the message he had tried to suppress. His past was not hidden, and his present service did not need to deny it. Grace had given an established zeal a new direction, and those hearing the report recognised that the deepest explanation lay in the God who called him. Paul places that mercy beneath his continuing defence of the gospel."], "lesson": "Grace can change a life without requiring its past harm to be concealed. Let a changed direction bring glory to God rather than pride in yourself.", "question": "Where should gratitude for God's mercy replace either boasting about my past or despair that it can ever change?"},
  {"id": 91330, "title": "Titus Goes With Paul to Jerusalem", "subtitle": "Fellowship without adding a burden", "text": "Galatians 2:1–10", "chapters": [[47, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul recounts a later visit to Jerusalem with Barnabas, taking Titus as well. He went in response to revelation and set before the recognised leaders the gospel he preached among Gentiles. A private conversation addressed the relationship between his work and theirs, so that the mission would not be frustrated by a divided understanding. Titus's presence gave the issue a personal form. He was Greek, yet he was not compelled to undergo circumcision. The discussion was not only about the wording of an agreement. It concerned whether an actual Gentile believer would have to accept an added requirement as a condition of belonging in Christ.", "Paul describes false brothers who had entered to investigate the freedom believers possessed in Jesus and bring them into bondage. He refused to yield to their demand, even temporarily, so that the truth of the gospel would remain with the churches. The firmness served people beyond the room, including Galatia's believers. A concession made for an apparently quiet settlement could establish a burden later imposed upon many. Paul therefore distinguishes legitimate conversation among fellow workers from submission to a requirement that changes the basis of fellowship. Peace cannot be secured faithfully by giving away the freedom the gospel itself supplies.", "The recognised leaders added nothing to his message. Paul acknowledges their standing while insisting that God does not judge through outward status. They recognised that he had been entrusted with the gospel to the uncircumcised, as Peter had received responsibility among the circumcised. The same God was effective in both missions. James, Peter and John perceived the grace given to Paul and offered him and Barnabas the right hand of fellowship. Different spheres of labour did not require different ways of salvation. The agreement affirmed shared allegiance to Christ while recognising the responsibilities through which the one message would reach different peoples.", "They asked that the poor be remembered, something Paul was already eager to do. The practical concern belongs within the same account as the defence of gospel freedom. Refusing an added ritual condition did not mean refusing the needs of other believers. The churches were to recognise one another through both a common message and generous care. Titus leaves the passage as a person whose inclusion did not depend on becoming ethnically Jewish, while the poor remain a responsibility no theological agreement should overlook. Paul has shown that the fellowship of Jerusalem's leaders confirmed rather than corrected the gospel he proclaimed. Their handshake and request for practical compassion direct the reader towards unity grounded in grace and made visible through service."], "lesson": "Defend the freedom of the gospel while seeking genuine fellowship. Shared faith should include practical care for people in need.", "question": "Am I adding an unnecessary condition to another believer's acceptance, or neglecting a practical duty of care we should share?"},
  {"id": 91331, "title": "Peter Leaves the Table", "subtitle": "Living consistently with justification by faith", "text": "Galatians 2:11–21", "chapters": [[47, 2]], "start": 11, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["At Antioch, Peter shared meals with Gentile believers. When certain people associated with James arrived, he withdrew and separated himself because he feared those insisting upon circumcision. Other Jewish believers followed, and even Barnabas was drawn into the same behaviour. The account exposes the influence of a leader's conduct. Peter's withdrawal did not remain a private preference about where to sit. It communicated something about who could be received freely and placed pressure upon Gentiles to adopt Jewish identity in order to share fellowship without reserve. Fear of one group was shaping the treatment of another.", "Paul opposed him openly because the conduct was not in line with the gospel's truth. He asked how Peter, who had lived freely among Gentiles, could now compel Gentiles to live as Jews. The correction addressed a public inconsistency with public effects. It was not a claim that Peter had never served faithfully or that differences should always be handled through confrontation before an audience. Here an influential action had compromised the basis of shared life. Paul's concern was that what the gospel announced about acceptance should not be contradicted by what believers practised at their tables.", "He explains that justification comes through faith in Jesus Christ rather than works of the law. Even Jewish believers have trusted Christ for that reason, knowing that no human being is justified by such works. Seeking acceptance in Christ does not make Him a servant of sin. Rebuilding the old basis after leaving it would reveal inconsistency in the one doing so. Paul speaks of dying to the law through the law so that he might live to God. His purpose is not a life beyond obedience. It is a life whose standing before God no longer rests upon an attempt to secure righteousness through law observance.", "The argument becomes intensely personal. Paul has been crucified with Christ, yet he lives because Christ lives in him. The life he now lives bodily is lived by faith in the Son of God who loved him and gave Himself for him. He will not set aside God's grace, because if righteousness came through the law, Christ's death would have been unnecessary. The shared table at Antioch therefore reaches into the meaning of the cross. A barrier erected through fear can deny in practice what a person confesses in words. The passage calls believers to examine the social consequences of their doctrine. Those accepted through Christ's self-giving should not require another person to purchase a place through an identity or achievement that cannot provide their own justification."], "lesson": "Let conduct towards other believers agree with the grace you profess. Fear of a group's opinion must not create barriers Christ's gospel removes.", "question": "Where might my treatment of someone contradict my confession that acceptance before God rests on Christ?"},
  {"id": 91332, "title": "The Question About Receiving the Spirit", "subtitle": "Beginning and continuing by faith", "text": "Galatians 3:1–14", "chapters": [[47, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul addresses the Galatians sharply because the crucified Christ had been clearly presented to them, yet they are turning from the truth. He asks how they received the Spirit: through works of the law or through hearing with faith. Their own beginning supplies a question they cannot evade. If the Spirit gave life, how can human effort under the proposed requirement now bring that life to completion? He also asks whether what they have experienced has been empty and points to God's continuing provision of the Spirit and mighty works. The issue concerns the source on which the whole Christian life depends, not merely the memory of an earlier moment.", "Paul turns to Abraham, who believed God and was counted righteous. Those who share faith are therefore Abraham's children in the sense of the promise he is discussing. Scripture anticipated the blessing of the nations when God told Abraham that all peoples would be blessed in him. Gentile believers are not an afterthought requiring a new basis for admission. Their reception through faith stands within God's long-announced purpose. The example takes the Galatians away from pride in a newly imposed achievement and towards trust in the God who gives. Abraham's significance lies in believing God's word rather than supplying a ground for self-congratulation.", "Reliance on works of the law as the basis of justification brings the problem of its curse. The law requires continuing in all that is written, not selecting a visible mark while ignoring the whole demand. Paul cites the righteous living by faith and contrasts that basis with securing life through doing everything the law commands. The difficulty is not that God's commands are evil. Human sin leaves those seeking justification through perfect compliance unable to establish the righteousness they need. Adding a ritual requirement therefore cannot solve the deeper condition from which the gospel promises rescue. It risks moving their confidence away from the One who has already acted for them.", "Christ redeemed from the law's curse by bearing the curse on their behalf, as Paul explains through the scriptural words concerning one hanged upon a tree. The cross is central to the blessing reaching Gentiles and to receiving the promised Spirit through faith. God's promise and Christ's costly work belong together. The Galatians are not being asked to treat obedience as unimportant. They are being called to understand where acceptance and new life come from, so that obedience can grow from trust rather than an attempt to replace grace with personal qualification. The passage leaves them with both a remembered experience and a scriptural history directing them back to the same crucified Lord."], "lesson": "Continue depending on the grace through which new life began. Obedience should grow from faith in Christ rather than an attempt to earn what His cross provides.", "question": "What practice am I tempted to treat as the basis of acceptance with God instead of a response to His grace?"},
  {"id": 91333, "title": "The Promise, the Law and the Heirs", "subtitle": "Belonging to Christ across familiar boundaries", "text": "Galatians 3:15–29", "chapters": [[47, 3]], "start": 15, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul uses an ordinary agreement to explain the stability of God's promise. Once a covenant is confirmed, someone arriving later cannot simply annul it or add a new condition. God's promises were spoken to Abraham and his offspring, and Paul identifies their fulfilment in Christ. The law given four hundred thirty years later did not cancel the earlier promise. If inheritance depended upon law, it would no longer be received as promise. God's gift to Abraham was given through His word. The timing therefore matters: the later arrangement must be understood within the purpose already announced rather than used to overturn it.", "Paul anticipates the question of why the law was given. It was added because of transgressions until the promised offspring came. He describes its mediation through angels and a mediator while affirming God's unity. He refuses the conclusion that the law is opposed to God's promises. If law could have supplied life, righteousness would have come through it. Instead, Scripture confines all under sin so that the promise may be given to those believing in Jesus Christ. The law's role reveals the condition of those who need the promise. It does not become an independent source of saving life competing with the One through whom the promise reaches fulfilment.", "He pictures life before faith's revealed fulfilment as being guarded under the law, held for what was to come. The law served as a guardian leading towards Christ so that justification might come through faith. With that fulfilment, believers no longer stand under the guardian in the earlier way. The image helps explain a change in covenantal position without suggesting that God's truth was a mistake. What served a preparatory role is being understood in relation to its goal. The Galatians should not mistake the earlier supervision for a permanent condition they must newly accept in order to qualify for an inheritance already opened in Christ.", "They are all God's children through faith in Christ Jesus. Those baptised into Him have put on Christ, and familiar divisions cannot determine a superior or inferior standing within that shared belonging. Jew and Greek, slave and free, male and female are one in Him. The statement does not pretend that every social condition has already disappeared from daily life. It denies those conditions the right to cancel another believer's place among the heirs. Belonging to Christ makes them Abraham's offspring according to the promise. Paul's argument about chronology has reached the actual composition of the congregation. People with different histories can receive one inheritance because its ground lies in Christ rather than a privilege one group possesses over another."], "lesson": "God's promise reaches its fulfilment in Christ. Honour the shared standing of believers whose background, sex or social position differs from your own.", "question": "What distinction am I allowing to weaken the welcome or dignity I should give another believer?"},
  {"id": 91334, "title": "An Heir Learns to Call God Father", "subtitle": "Receiving sonship and caring for others", "text": "Galatians 4:1–20", "chapters": [[47, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul develops the picture of an heir who is still a child. Although the estate will belong to him, his daily position resembles that of a servant while guardians manage his life until the father's appointed time. Paul applies the image to an earlier condition of bondage. When the fullness of time came, God sent His Son, born of a woman and under the law, to redeem those under it and give adoption. The change rests upon God's sending and Christ's work. The heir does not reach the new position by persuading the household to recognise a greatness achieved independently of the father's decision.", "Because believers are sons, God sends the Spirit of His Son into their hearts, crying to the Father. They are no longer slaves but children and heirs through God. Paul then asks why those once enslaved to beings that are not truly gods would turn back towards weak and impoverished elements. They know God, or more deeply, are known by Him. Their new relationship should govern the way they regard religious observances. In this dispute, their attention to days, months, seasons and years signals a return towards bondage, and Paul fears for the result of his labour. Practices cannot replace the belonging already given through Christ.", "His appeal becomes personal. He asks them to identify with him as he has with them, recalling their first reception of the gospel during his bodily weakness. They had not despised him because of that condition but welcomed him with extraordinary kindness. Their affection had once been so strong that he describes them as willing to give their own eyes. The words convey their former devotion without identifying his illness more precisely than the passage permits. Now he asks whether speaking truth has made him their enemy. Their changed reception of him reveals how deeply the new influence has affected a relationship once marked by trust and generosity.", "The rival teachers court the Galatians eagerly but with a harmful purpose, seeking to shut them away so that they become dependent on those teachers' attention. Zeal can be good when directed towards what is good, and faithfulness should continue whether Paul is physically present or not. He addresses them as children for whom he again suffers labour pains until Christ is formed in them. His goal is therefore larger than restoring his popularity. He longs to be present and speak in a different tone because he is perplexed about them. The passage places the Father's gift of adoption beside a servant's costly concern that adopted children should grow in the likeness of the Son rather than return to servitude."], "lesson": "Remember the relationship God gives through Christ. Receive truthful care even when it challenges an influence that feels flattering or important.", "question": "Am I allowing a person's approval or a religious practice to replace the confidence of belonging to the Father through Christ?"},
  {"id": 91335, "title": "Hagar and Sarah in Paul's Argument", "subtitle": "Understanding how Paul uses the promise", "text": "Galatians 4:21–31", "chapters": [[47, 4]], "start": 21, "end": 31, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul addresses those who want to be under the law by asking whether they actually hear what it says. He recalls Abraham's two sons, one born to a slave woman and the other to a free woman. Their births form the starting point for his comparison: one according to ordinary human means and the other through promise. The Genesis history contains the lives of real people, and Paul now explicitly gives it an allegorical use in his argument. Recognising that move helps the reader follow his purpose without assuming that every detail of the earlier narrative must receive a new hidden meaning.", "He associates Hagar with Sinai and a covenant producing bondage, corresponding to the Jerusalem of his own day. By contrast, the Jerusalem above is free and is the mother of those belonging to the promise. He supports the picture with Isaiah's call for the barren woman to rejoice because her children will be many. The point concerns the source and character of the inheritance. What appears unproductive or unable to secure its future through ordinary advantage can receive what God promises. Paul's use of the image belongs to the dispute over whether the Galatians must accept an added legal condition to stand fully among God's people.", "He calls the believers children of promise like Isaac. He also recalls conflict between the one born according to the flesh and the one associated with the Spirit, relating it to the pressure now experienced. The scriptural direction concerning the slave woman and her son is used to deny that slavery and freedom can provide the same basis of inheritance. This is a forceful argument about covenantal standing, not a command for Christians to mistreat people descended from a particular family or living in a particular nation. Its application must remain tied to the freedom and promise Paul is defending throughout the letter.", "He concludes that believers are children of the free woman rather than the slave woman. The passage asks the Galatians to understand their position from God's promise in Christ instead of treating bondage as a new step towards spiritual maturity. It also asks careful readers to distinguish Paul's stated interpretation from unrestrained speculation of their own. He identifies the comparison, develops it towards one purpose and draws a conclusion consistent with the gospel already explained. Freedom is being defended so that the churches can remain faithful to the One who called them by grace. The story of the two sons therefore serves a present pastoral concern: the people who have received a promised inheritance must not allow fear or pressure to persuade them that they now need a different parentage to secure it."], "lesson": "Read Paul's comparison within his argument about promise and freedom. Confidence in God's gift should lead to gratitude rather than hostility or spiritual pride.", "question": "How does Paul's conclusion challenge the belief that another form of bondage would make my standing in Christ more secure?"},
  {"id": 91336, "title": "Freedom Put to Work Through Love", "subtitle": "Serving without returning to bondage", "text": "Galatians 5:1–15", "chapters": [[47, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul calls the Galatians to stand firmly in the freedom Christ has given and refuse a renewed yoke of slavery. His warning concerning circumcision addresses the use being urged upon them as a requirement for justification. Accepting it on that basis makes a person accountable to keep the whole law and shifts confidence away from Christ. Those seeking justification through the law are abandoning the ground of grace. The issue is not a claim that a bodily condition by itself decides a person's worth. Paul makes clear that in Christ neither circumcision nor its absence supplies the decisive standing. What matters is faith expressing itself through love.", "Through the Spirit, believers wait by faith for the hope of righteousness. Paul remembers that they had been running well and asks who interrupted their obedience to the truth. The persuasion drawing them away did not come from the One who called them. A little leaven affects the whole batch, so a seemingly limited addition can change the message's character. He retains confidence in them through the Lord while warning that the person troubling them will face judgment. The disturbance cannot be treated as harmless simply because the demanded sign appears outwardly small. It changes what people are being told to rely upon for their acceptance.", "Paul rejects any suggestion that he still preaches circumcision as the necessary condition. If he did, the offence of the cross and the persecution connected with it would cease. His severe wish that the agitators would cut themselves off expresses the depth of his opposition to the harm they are doing. The language should not be turned into permission for violence against teachers or hearers. He is insisting that a gospel centred on Christ's sufficient work cannot be combined with an imposed requirement that supplies a different basis. The Galatians need to see why his resistance is stronger than a disagreement over custom.", "Freedom, however, is not an opportunity for self-indulgence. Paul immediately directs it towards serving one another in love. The command to love a neighbour as oneself gathers the law's moral concern into their shared life. If they keep biting and devouring one another, they risk consuming the community they claim to defend. This movement prevents the defence of grace from becoming an excuse for careless conduct. Those freed in Christ are released for a loving life under Him. Their liberty should appear in the way they use time, speech and strength for another person's good. Faith working through love becomes the practical answer both to legal pressure and to the selfishness that might misuse freedom."], "lesson": "Stand in Christ's grace and use freedom to serve. Refusing a false basis of acceptance must be joined to love that seeks a neighbour's good.", "question": "Where can I use a freedom or opportunity to help someone rather than merely secure my own comfort?"},
  {"id": 91337, "title": "What Grows When the Spirit Leads", "subtitle": "Recognising character by its fruit", "text": "Galatians 5:16–26", "chapters": [[47, 5]], "start": 16, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul tells the churches to walk by the Spirit so that the desires of the flesh will not govern their conduct. The flesh and Spirit oppose one another, making the conflict within moral life real. A believer's hope is not a claim that every desire has become automatically good. It is the Spirit's guidance and power for a different way of living. Those led by Him are not under the law in the earlier condition Paul has described. Yet this freedom does not erase the distinction between faithful and destructive actions. The next lists make their effects on people and communities plain.", "The works of the flesh include sexual wrongdoing, impurity, idolatry, sorcery, hatred, jealousy, anger, selfish rivalries, divisions, envy, drunkenness and similar conduct. Some sins are publicly dramatic, while others can hide inside a congregation defending its own importance. Paul places them within the same warning. People who persist in such conduct will not inherit God's kingdom. His earlier defence of justification by faith therefore cannot honestly be read as indifference towards how people live. Grace brings a life that must resist the very actions capable of destroying relationships, corrupting worship and making the community unsafe for its vulnerable members.", "The fruit of the Spirit is love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. These qualities describe life formed by God's Spirit rather than a display designed only to impress. They can be observed in ordinary exchanges: receiving correction, responding to irritation, keeping a promise or helping someone who cannot repay. Paul says there is no law against such fruit. God's moral purpose is not offended by a life growing in those qualities. The contrast also supplies a measure for claims of spiritual maturity. Vocabulary, reputation and a striking experience do not replace the character through which the Spirit's work becomes visible.", "Those belonging to Christ have crucified the flesh with its passions and desires. Since believers live by the Spirit, they are to keep in step with Him. Paul concludes by warning against empty glory, provoking others and envying one another. The ending returns the teaching directly to the relationships of the Galatian churches. A person can discuss freedom and still use a disagreement to advertise superiority. Walking in the Spirit reaches that temptation as well as more easily recognised wrongdoing. The passage calls for a settled allegiance expressed through repeated choices. Its fruit is not produced by merely admiring the list, but through dependence on the Spirit and a willingness to put away conduct that contradicts the Lord to whom the people now belong."], "lesson": "Look for the Spirit's work in character and relationships. Depend on Him while deliberately refusing the conduct that harms people and contradicts Christ.", "question": "Which aspect of the Spirit's fruit needs to become more visible in the way I handle disagreement?"},
  {"id": 91338, "title": "Bearing Burdens and Sowing for the Future", "subtitle": "Gentleness, endurance and the cross", "text": "Galatians 6:1–18", "chapters": [[47, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Galatians", "story": ["Paul brings life in the Spirit into the care of a person caught in wrongdoing. Those who are spiritual should restore the person gently, watching themselves because they too may be tempted. Restoration is not an occasion for proving superiority. Believers are to carry one another's burdens and fulfil Christ's law. At the same time, each should examine their own work rather than build an identity through comparison. Paul also speaks of each carrying their own load. Mutual help does not remove personal accountability, and accountability does not cancel the duty to assist. The congregation needs both truths if correction and care are to remain faithful.", "Those taught the word should share good things with their teachers. Paul then warns that God cannot be mocked: what a person sows will be reaped. Sowing to the flesh produces corruption, while sowing to the Spirit leads towards eternal life. The image concerns the direction and outcome of life, not a commercial promise that a payment must return a larger payment. Choices have a harvest beyond the convenience of their immediate moment. He therefore urges the churches not to become weary in doing good. In due time they will reap if they continue, so present effort should not be abandoned merely because its fruit is not yet obvious.", "Whenever opportunity comes, they should do good to all, with particular care for the household of faith. Paul then draws attention to the large letters written with his own hand and returns to the dispute. Those pressuring them towards circumcision want an outward display and wish to avoid persecution for Christ's cross. They do not themselves keep the whole law but want to boast over the Galatians' bodies. The demand has exposed a desire for visible success and safety rather than the good of the people receiving it. Paul's closing explanation again asks the churches to consider what a teacher is seeking through the requirement being imposed.", "For himself, Paul will boast only in the cross of Jesus Christ, through which the world has been crucified to him and he to the world. Neither circumcision nor uncircumcision supplies the decisive measure, but new creation. Peace and mercy accompany those walking by this rule. He bears in his body the marks associated with belonging to Jesus and asks that the troubling cease. The letter ends by invoking Christ's grace upon their spirit and addressing them as brothers and sisters. Firm correction has not removed their place in his affection. The gospel he defends is meant to produce gentle restoration, shared burdens and enduring goodness, with the cross remaining the ground of confidence for the whole renewed community."], "lesson": "Help others gently, accept responsibility for your own conduct and keep doing good. Let the cross rather than outward success supply your reason for confidence.", "question": "What act of patient goodness or gentle restoration should I continue even without an immediate visible result?"},
  {"id": 91339, "title": "Blessed in Christ", "subtitle": "Grace gives God's people a home and a future", "text": "Ephesians 1:1–14", "chapters": [[48, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul greets the saints and faithful believers with grace and peace from God the Father and Jesus Christ. He then blesses God for every spiritual blessing given in Christ. The opening draws attention towards what God has done before asking how the people should live. They are chosen in Christ before the world's foundation to be holy and blameless in love. Adoption belongs to God's gracious purpose, bringing them into the relationship of children through His Son. The language invites worship and gratitude. Their place before Him begins with His generosity rather than an achievement by which they could make Him owe them acceptance.", "In Christ they have redemption through His blood and forgiveness according to the riches of grace. The price and the generosity belong together. Forgiveness is not a claim that wrongdoing never mattered, but a gift grounded in Christ's self-giving. God also makes known His purpose to bring all things in heaven and earth together in Christ at the fullness of time. The mystery here is something revealed through the gospel, drawing the reader towards the central place of the Son. God's purpose reaches beyond the private experience of an individual while still including the person who needs mercy and a secure belonging.", "Paul speaks of an inheritance within the purpose of the God who works all things according to His will. Those who first hoped in Christ are to exist for the praise of His glory. He then includes the hearers directly: they heard the word of truth, the gospel of salvation, believed in Christ and were sealed with the promised Holy Spirit. Their participation is described through hearing and faith, with God's own gift marking their belonging. The Spirit is the pledge of the inheritance until redemption's completion. The future is therefore approached through a promise already accompanied by the presence of the One who will fulfil it.", "The passage repeatedly returns to praise. Election, adoption, redemption, revelation and the Spirit's pledge do not form a list designed to make one believer feel superior to another. They name gifts that establish a people in Christ and call them to holiness and gratitude. Paul will later address their relationships, work and conduct, but those duties rest upon this foundation. Remembering what has been received can free a person from the restless need to prove worth through comparison. It also gives a reason to pursue the holy life for which God has called them. The opening places both identity and hope within the Father's gracious purpose, the Son's redeeming work and the Spirit's promised presence."], "lesson": "Begin obedience with gratitude for God's gifts in Christ. Belonging, forgiveness and hope should lead to praise and a life shaped by love.", "question": "Which gift in this passage most directly challenges the way I have been trying to prove my worth?"},
  {"id": 91340, "title": "A Prayer for Opened Eyes", "subtitle": "Knowing the hope of God's calling", "text": "Ephesians 1:15–23", "chapters": [[48, 1]], "start": 15, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul has heard of the believers' faith in Jesus and love towards the saints. He gives thanks continually and remembers them in prayer. Their existing faith is a reason to pray for further understanding rather than assume they have no need to grow. He asks the Father of glory to give wisdom and revelation in the knowledge of Him. The purpose is a deeper grasp of the God they already trust. Learning is therefore joined to relationship and worship, with prayer recognising that correct information alone does not guarantee a heart able to receive and live by its meaning.", "Paul asks that the eyes of their hearts be enlightened to know the hope of God's calling, the riches of His glorious inheritance among the saints and His surpassing power towards believers. Each request directs attention to something grounded in God's action. Their future is not determined solely by the pressures visible around them. Their place among His people has a value beyond a community's present outward standing. The power available for faithful life is not merely the confidence produced through personal determination. Paul's prayer seeks an understanding capable of steadying them because it sees these realities in relation to the Lord who called them.", "He identifies that power through what God did in raising Christ from death and seating Him at His right hand in the heavenly places. The risen Lord stands above every principality, power, might, dominion and named authority in the present age and the age to come. These words are meant to magnify Christ, not leave the church absorbed in a speculative ranking of every unseen being. Whatever the precise reach of a particular authority, none occupies His place. The resurrection and exaltation give the prayer a concrete centre. Paul is not asking them to trust an undefined spiritual force, but the God whose mighty work is displayed in Jesus Christ.", "Everything is placed beneath Christ's feet, and He is given as head over all things to the church, His body. The congregation's life therefore belongs under the authority and care of the One whose reign exceeds the powers that might threaten it. This does not promise that believers will avoid every hardship; Paul himself writes as a servant familiar with suffering. It provides the larger truth within which hardship is faced. The passage invites a searching response from readers who already believe: ask to understand the hope, value and strength of the calling more fully. A clearer view of Christ's supremacy can change the way fear, comparison and discouragement govern daily decisions."], "lesson": "Pray for understanding that reaches the heart and conduct. Christ's resurrection and supremacy give believers reason for hope beyond present appearances.", "question": "Which fear or discouragement should I reconsider in light of the Lord Paul describes here?"},
  {"id": 91341, "title": "From Death to a Life of Good Works", "subtitle": "Grace gives rise to obedience", "text": "Ephesians 2:1–10", "chapters": [[48, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul recalls a former life described as death in trespasses and sins. The people once followed the course of the world and the spiritual power associated with disobedience. He includes himself and others within the same human condition, living according to the desires of flesh and mind. The account does not leave one group standing above another as naturally deserving rescue. Sin involves outward patterns, inward desire and hostile influence, with no part supplying an excuse that removes personal need. The seriousness of the description prepares the reader to recognise the size of the mercy that follows.", "God, rich in mercy and acting from His great love, made them alive with Christ even while they were dead in sin. Paul interrupts the account to emphasise that salvation is by grace. God also raised them with Christ and gave them a place with Him in the heavenly realm. Their new standing is grounded in union with the risen Lord, rather than a claim that their bodies have already left the earth or that suffering has ceased. God's kindness will display the exceeding riches of His grace through the ages. The story of rescue is meant to show what He gives to people unable to establish their own claim upon Him.", "Paul states the basis clearly: salvation is by grace through faith, God's gift rather than the product of works that could create grounds for boasting. A person receiving it cannot rightly turn that reception into contempt for someone else. The sentence does not make faith a new performance through which God is obliged to save. It places the whole rescue within His generosity and calls the hearer to trust what He has done in Christ. The absence of a basis for boasting is therefore practical. It changes how believers remember their past, speak of their present and welcome others who need the same mercy.", "The passage ends with good works. Those saved are God's workmanship, created in Christ for the works He has prepared as their path. Good conduct has not disappeared from the argument. Its place is after and within the gift of new life, as the purpose grace equips people to pursue. The sequence guards against two distortions: trying to earn salvation through achievement and treating salvation as permission to remain indifferent to obedience. A life once shaped by disobedience is now called to walk differently. Paul wants the people to see that humility and active goodness grow from the same source. The God who makes them alive also gives their renewed life a direction in which mercy can become visible through what they do."], "lesson": "Receive salvation as God's gift and let gratitude become practical obedience. Good works express the life grace has given rather than a price paid for it.", "question": "What good work is available to me today as a grateful response to God's mercy?"},
  {"id": 91342, "title": "Those Who Were Far Off Come Near", "subtitle": "Peace that makes room for others", "text": "Ephesians 2:11–22", "chapters": [[48, 2]], "start": 11, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul asks the Gentile believers to remember their former position. They were described through uncircumcision, excluded from Israel's commonwealth, strangers to the covenants of promise and without hope or God in the world. The memory is not intended to keep them permanently ashamed. It makes the change in Christ clear. Those once far away have been brought near through His blood. Their welcome is not a grudging exception granted by a group whose standing remains untouched. The peace Christ makes reaches the relationship of the divided peoples as well as the need each has before God.", "Christ Himself is their peace. Through His bodily self-giving He has broken the dividing barrier and dealt with the hostility expressed through the law's separating ordinances. His purpose is to create one new humanity from the two and reconcile both to God through the cross. Paul's language moves beyond the idea of one community merely allowing another to stand nearby. Both are brought into a common life in the same Lord. The cross removes the ground on which either might treat hostility as the final truth of their relationship. Peace must be understood through the costly work of the One who made it.", "The message of peace comes to those far and near. Through Christ, both have access in one Spirit to the Father. This is a profound statement about belonging. A Gentile believer does not need a separate route of lesser value, and an inherited religious privilege does not grant another person a superior Father. Paul therefore calls them fellow citizens with the saints and members of God's household. The images place people inside the shared home rather than leaving them at its threshold. Their different histories remain real, but those histories cannot be used to deny the relationship Christ has opened.", "Paul adds the picture of a building founded on apostles and prophets, with Jesus Christ as its cornerstone. In Him the whole structure is joined and grows into a holy temple, and the believers are being built together as God's dwelling through the Spirit. Each image turns the change of standing towards a shared responsibility. Citizens live together, households care for their members and stones belong within one structure. A congregation may confess peace while maintaining social habits that make some people feel permanently foreign. This passage calls those habits into question. The work of Christ should shape the welcome, dignity and cooperation through which the church makes its shared access to the Father visible in ordinary life."], "lesson": "Let peace with God shape welcome towards people whose background differs from yours. Those brought near through Christ belong within the household, with its dignity and shared responsibilities.", "question": "Who might still be treated as an outsider in a community where Christ has made them family?"},
  {"id": 91343, "title": "Paul Names the Mystery", "subtitle": "Sharing the promise across former boundaries", "text": "Ephesians 3:1–13", "chapters": [[48, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul describes himself as Christ Jesus' prisoner for the sake of the Gentiles. The circumstances of his service have not cancelled the grace entrusted to him. He explains that the mystery made known through revelation can be understood as they read what he has written. Mystery here does not mean knowledge reserved forever for a few spiritual specialists. It names a purpose once hidden and now disclosed through the Spirit to the apostles and prophets. Paul wants the congregation to understand it, because it concerns their own place in the life God is bringing into being.", "He gives the content plainly: Gentiles are fellow heirs, members of the same body and sharers in the promise in Christ through the gospel. The repeated language of sharing leaves little room for a permanent lower rank. Their welcome rests in Christ rather than the possession of the same ancestry. Paul became a servant of this message through God's gracious power. He calls himself less than the least of the saints while acknowledging a real commission. Humility does not mean denying the work entrusted to him. It means remembering that the opportunity comes from grace and should be used for the good of those whom the message includes.", "His task is to proclaim Christ's unsearchable riches among the nations and make the administration of the mystery clear. The purpose was hidden in the Creator and now becomes visible through the church. Even principalities and powers in the heavenly realm are to know God's manifold wisdom through this shared people. The point is larger than individual religious experience. A church in which formerly divided people belong to one body bears witness to the wisdom of God's eternal purpose in Christ. That significance should lead to a careful practice of unity, not pride in possessing a vocabulary of invisible powers.", "In Christ believers have bold and confident access through faith. Paul therefore asks them not to lose heart over the suffering he bears for their sake, which serves their honour. A messenger's imprisonment might look like evidence that the work has failed. He directs the congregation to judge it through the purpose being fulfilled and the relationship opened to them. The passage does not glorify suffering for its own sake or make the worker's pain a demand for admiration. It places costly service within the revelation of grace. The people receiving the message should take courage, understand what God has given and live together in a way consistent with being fellow heirs. Paul's explanation of mystery reaches its proper result when shared belonging becomes an understood and practised reality."], "lesson": "God's revealed purpose calls formerly divided people into shared belonging. Let that truth strengthen unity and encourage those serving at personal cost.", "question": "What practical action could make our shared inheritance in Christ more visible to someone who has felt excluded?"},
  {"id": 91344, "title": "Rooted and Grounded in Love", "subtitle": "Praying for strength in the inner person", "text": "Ephesians 3:14–21", "chapters": [[48, 3]], "start": 14, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul bows his knees before the Father, from whom the family in heaven and earth receives its name. His explanation of God's purpose now becomes prayer for the people included in it. He asks according to the riches of God's glory that they be strengthened with power through the Spirit in the inner person. The request recognises that receiving truth intellectually does not remove the need for divine help to live within it. A congregation facing pressure, difference and uncertainty needs strength reaching inward, where trust, fear, desire and purpose shape the actions others will eventually see.", "He prays that Christ may dwell in their hearts through faith and that they be rooted and grounded in love. The images suggest stability drawn from a foundation and nourishment. Love is not treated as an attractive addition after more important spiritual achievements. It is the setting in which the believers are to become established. Paul asks for power connected with receiving Christ and growing in this love, rather than power chiefly displayed through personal prestige. The people whose place in God's household has just been explained need hearts that can actually inhabit the relationship and extend its care to one another.", "Together with all the saints, they are to grasp breadth, length, height and depth and know Christ's love surpassing knowledge. The language acknowledges something greater than the mind can finally contain while still asking for a genuine understanding of it. The phrase about all the saints matters. Growth in this knowledge is not a private ascent that makes other believers dispensable. A shared life of worship, instruction, service and forgiveness helps display dimensions no isolated individual possesses fully. The goal is fullness from God, a life increasingly formed by what He gives rather than governed by the scarcity and rivalry of self-protection.", "Paul concludes with praise to the God able to do beyond what people ask or imagine, according to His power at work among them. Glory belongs to Him in the church and in Christ Jesus through every generation. The praise should remain connected to the prayer rather than become a promise that every personal ambition must be fulfilled. The power Paul has described strengthens hearts, establishes love and makes God's wisdom visible through His people. That work is already more profound than a favourable outward change alone. The passage teaches believers what to ask for themselves and one another: the Spirit's strengthening, Christ's settled presence and an understanding of love deep enough to transform the way they live. Prayer becomes a practical means of seeking the character and confidence the gospel calls them to receive."], "lesson": "Pray for inward strength and a deeper knowledge of Christ's love. Spiritual growth should make us more established in love and more connected to God's people.", "question": "Whom could I pray for using Paul's requests rather than only asking for an easier situation?"},
  {"id": 91345, "title": "One Body Learning to Grow", "subtitle": "Unity and maturity through shared service", "text": "Ephesians 4:1–16", "chapters": [[48, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul, writing as the Lord's prisoner, calls the believers to live in a manner worthy of their calling. The first qualities he names are humility, gentleness, patience and bearing with one another in love. They are to work diligently to preserve the Spirit's unity through peace. The order challenges a view of maturity centred on being the most impressive person in the gathering. Faithfulness begins in the treatment of people whose needs, habits and weaknesses may differ from our own. Unity is a gift to be guarded through conduct, not an excuse to demand that others submit to our preferences.", "He names the shared foundations: one body, one Spirit, one hope, one Lord, one faith, one baptism and one God and Father. Within that unity, grace is given according to Christ's gift. Paul speaks of the victorious Lord who descended and ascended and gives gifts to His people. The passage joins the exalted Christ to service within His body rather than turning His exaltation into a pattern of personal superiority. Differences in gift are real, but they belong within a relationship already defined by the one Lord. No member can use a received gift to claim independence from the body or replace its Head.", "Apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers are given to equip the saints for service and build up Christ's body. The aim is a growing unity of faith and knowledge of the Son, leading towards maturity measured by Christ's fullness. Teaching should help believers become stable rather than remain children swept about by every new doctrine and the schemes of deceptive people. The test of leadership therefore includes what it enables others to become and do. A ministry that leaves everyone permanently dependent on the leader's unexamined claims has missed the direction of the equipping Paul describes.", "Speaking the truth in love, the body grows into Christ, its Head. Each joint supplies something needed, and each part's effective work contributes to growth in love. The picture refuses both isolated self-sufficiency and passive spectatorship. Members need one another, and their varied service matters to the health of the whole. Truth should not be separated from care, while care should not become a refusal to tell the truth. Paul's account brings the grand unity of God's purpose into the practical exchanges of a congregation. A patient correction, a reliable act of service or the willingness to learn can help the body grow. Its maturity is expressed through people becoming more faithful to Christ and more able to serve one another under His direction."], "lesson": "Use gifts to equip others and strengthen the whole body. Humility, truthful speech and love are essential measures of maturity.", "question": "Does my contribution help other people grow in faithful service, or mainly draw attention to my own importance?"},
  {"id": 91346, "title": "Putting Off the Old Way", "subtitle": "Practising truth and kindness", "text": "Ephesians 4:17–32", "chapters": [[48, 4]], "start": 17, "end": 32, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul urges the believers to stop living according to the empty thinking and hardened hearts associated with their former life. Darkened understanding and insensitivity had led towards impurity and greed. Learning Christ calls them into a different way. They are to put off the old person shaped by deceitful desires, be renewed in their thinking and put on the new person created in righteousness and true holiness. The change reaches more than outward appearance. It concerns the patterns through which desire, thought and conduct have been joined, and it requires a renewed allegiance to the truth found in Jesus.", "Paul then makes the instruction concrete. Lying must give way to truthful speech because believers are members of one another. Another person's need for truth matters within the shared body. Anger must not be allowed to become sin or remain cultivated without resolution. Giving the devil no opportunity belongs within that practical concern, rather than serving as a substitute for addressing the actual resentment. A person who has stolen must stop, work honestly and have something to share with someone in need. The positive aim reaches beyond avoiding punishment or building a better reputation. Honest labour becomes a means of generosity.", "Harmful speech should be replaced by words fitted to build people up according to their need and give grace to the hearers. The question is therefore larger than whether a statement can be defended as technically accurate. Its purpose, timing and effect deserve attention. Paul also warns against grieving the Holy Spirit, who seals them for redemption. Their conduct takes place within a living relationship with God. The Spirit's presence is not merely an item of doctrine remembered in a service. It gives seriousness to the way believers use their mouths, manage anger and treat the people entrusted to their care.", "Bitterness, rage, angry shouting, slander and malice are to be put away. In their place Paul calls for kindness, tenderheartedness and forgiveness shaped by God's forgiveness in Christ. The passage offers no room for a spirituality that celebrates unseen power while leaving habitual cruelty untouched. New life becomes visible through repeated choices in ordinary relationships. Forgiveness also does not require pretending a wrong never occurred; its source is the God who deals truthfully with sin and grants mercy. The whole section gives a practical pattern for change: recognise the harmful habit, put it away and take up the good conduct that serves its neighbour. Renewal becomes a daily task of living consistently with the Christ the people have learned."], "lesson": "Replace harmful habits with positive obedience that serves others. New life in Christ should be visible in speech, work, anger and forgiveness.", "question": "Which habit should I put away, and what specific good action does Paul place in its stead?"},
  {"id": 91347, "title": "Walking in Love, Light and Wisdom", "subtitle": "Making good use of the time", "text": "Ephesians 5:1–21", "chapters": [[48, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["As beloved children, believers are called to imitate God and walk in love. Christ's self-giving supplies the pattern: He loved and gave Himself as an offering to God. Love therefore has a costly direction towards another person's good. It cannot be reduced to a feeling that leaves conduct unchanged. Paul immediately addresses sexual immorality, impurity and greed as incompatible with a holy people. Speech also matters, with degrading or foolish talk giving way to thanksgiving. The warning is serious because practices such as greed can become a form of idolatry, placing desire where allegiance to God belongs.", "The church must not be deceived by empty assurances that persistent disobedience carries no consequence. Those once characterised by darkness are now light in the Lord and should live accordingly. Goodness, righteousness and truth mark that life. Paul tells them to discern what pleases the Lord and refuse participation in unfruitful deeds, exposing them instead. This does not require fascination with hidden wrongdoing or public retelling of every degrading detail. Light makes the truth visible and calls sleepers to awaken to Christ. The purpose is an honest, holy response rather than the satisfaction of knowing another person's shame.", "Walking wisely includes careful use of time because the days are evil. The believers need understanding of the Lord's will rather than drifting according to whatever opportunity or pressure appears. Paul contrasts drunkenness, which leads into disorder, with being filled by the Spirit. The direction concerns what governs a person's life. A momentary religious appearance cannot replace a pattern of sobriety, wisdom and responsive obedience. The people whose lives have been received as light are to consider their steps, recognise the available good and act while the opportunity remains. Time is treated as a responsibility before God, not merely a possession to spend without thought.", "Paul describes Spirit-filled shared life through psalms, hymns, spiritual songs, heartfelt melody to the Lord, thanksgiving to the Father in Christ's name and submission to one another in reverence for Him. The presence of the Spirit reaches both worship and relationships. It produces a congregation able to speak truth and praise, receive God's gifts gratefully and consider other people rather than demand constant recognition. This conclusion joins the earlier calls to love, light and wisdom. A song should belong within the same life as honest conduct, restrained desires and thoughtful service. The passage therefore gives readers practical ways to examine spiritual health through ordinary habits: what they celebrate, how they use the day and whether reverence for Christ shapes the way they share life with others."], "lesson": "Walk as someone loved by Christ and brought into His light. Let the Spirit shape gratitude, wise use of time and considerate relationships.", "question": "What change in my speech, time or attention would make my daily life more consistent with the Lord I worship?"},
  {"id": 91348, "title": "Faithfulness Within the Household", "subtitle": "Answering to Christ in close relationships", "text": "Ephesians 5:22–6:9", "chapters": [[48, 5], [48, 6]], "start": 22, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul brings the letter's teaching into household relationships, following the call to mutual submission in reverence for Christ. Wives are addressed concerning submission to their own husbands, while Christ's relationship with the church supplies the governing picture. Husbands receive a demanding command to love as Christ loved and gave Himself for the church. The model is self-giving care directed towards another's holiness and good. The passage must not become permission for intimidation or violence. A husband who uses it to justify harm has abandoned the sacrificial love that is explicitly commanded of him. Authority is placed under the Lord whose life defines its faithful use.", "Husbands are to love their wives as their own bodies, nourishing and cherishing them as Christ cares for the church. Paul recalls the two becoming one flesh and calls the relationship to reflect the mystery of Christ and His people. Love and respect are therefore matters of continuing conduct. A person's devotion cannot be measured only in public worship while a spouse experiences neglect or cruelty at home. The letter's earlier calls to truthful speech, kindness and forgiveness remain relevant within this closest relationship. The household is not an exception to the character Christ forms among His people.", "Children are called to obey parents in the Lord and honour father and mother. Fathers are told not to provoke their children but bring them up in the Lord's training and instruction. Responsibility reaches those with greater power as well as those under it. Discipline should serve growth rather than become a release for adult anger. Paul then speaks to enslaved people within the ancient household order. They are to serve sincerely before Christ rather than act only when watched or merely seek human approval. Their work is seen by a Lord who recognises good whether a person is enslaved or free.", "Masters are commanded to act with the same accountability, give up threats and remember their common Master in heaven, who shows no partiality. Addressing people within slavery does not make another human being an object without dignity or provide a timeless justification for owning them. The passage places even a socially powerful master beneath Christ's judgment and denies him a privileged moral standing. Across marriage, parenting and labour, Paul's concern is that allegiance to the Lord should transform the way power and responsibility are exercised. Faithfulness includes the person ordinarily overlooked within a household, while the one able to demand most is called to answer most carefully for the care given. Every relationship is brought before the same Lord."], "lesson": "Let Christ's care govern close relationships and the use of authority. Those with greater power must practise love, restraint and responsibility before the same impartial Lord.", "question": "What duty towards someone affected by my authority needs more of Christ's self-giving care?"},
  {"id": 91349, "title": "Standing Firm and Praying for Others", "subtitle": "Strength supplied by the Lord", "text": "Ephesians 6:10–24", "chapters": [[48, 6]], "start": 10, "end": 24, "kind": "story", "category": "Ephesians", "story": ["Paul closes by calling believers to strength in the Lord and His mighty power. They are to put on God's whole armour so that they can stand against the devil's schemes. The struggle includes hostile spiritual powers rather than being reducible to human enemies. Naming those powers directs the church towards dependence on God, not permission to treat an opponent, neighbour or relative as something less than human. The command repeatedly emphasises standing. Their task is faithful endurance under the Lord whose supremacy the opening prayer has already proclaimed. The passage should be read within that established assurance rather than used to replace it with fear.", "The armour includes truth as a belt, righteousness as a breastplate and readiness from the gospel of peace as footwear. Faith serves as a shield against the enemy's fiery attacks, salvation as a helmet and God's word as the Spirit's sword. The images give concrete expression to the gifts and conduct developed throughout the letter. Truth belongs with truthful speech, righteousness with the new life, and peace with reconciliation through Christ. The believer is not being told to purchase sacred objects or master a hidden ritual. God's provision is received through faith and lived through allegiance to the gospel.", "Prayer accompanies the whole instruction. The people are to pray in the Spirit at all times, watch with perseverance and intercede for all the saints. Paul includes himself among those needing their support. He asks for words and boldness to make the gospel known, even while serving as an ambassador in chains. His imprisonment has not made the armour ineffective or the calling worthless. The request shows what standing can require in a difficult circumstance: courage to speak faithfully rather than a guarantee that no suffering will occur. The church's prayers join the apostle's witness, making resistance a shared responsibility rather than an isolated display of personal strength.", "Tychicus, a beloved brother and faithful servant, will tell them how Paul is doing and encourage their hearts. The closing returns from the image of battle to the care of actual people through truthful news and trusted companionship. Paul blesses the believers with peace, love with faith and grace for all who love Jesus sincerely. The end gathers the letter's practical direction into its opening gifts. A people saved by grace and made one in Christ are to stand in truth, live lovingly and support one another in prayer. Their hope concerns the Lord who leads His body, and their spiritual strength should make them more faithful in the relationships and responsibilities through which His gospel is heard and seen."], "lesson": "Stand through the truth and strength God supplies, and pray persistently for others. Spiritual vigilance should deepen faithful witness, peace and mutual care.", "question": "Whom should I support in prayer for courage to speak or act faithfully within a difficult responsibility?"},
  {"id": 91350, "title": "A Prisoner Gives Thanks for His Partners", "subtitle": "Praying for love with discernment", "text": "Philippians 1:1–11", "chapters": [[49, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul and Timothy greet the believers at Philippi as servants of Jesus Christ. The greeting includes the church's overseers and deacons, placing recognised responsibilities within the fellowship of all God's people. Grace and peace come from God the Father and the Lord Jesus Christ. Paul then remembers the Philippians with gratitude and joy. His imprisonment has not erased the history they share. From the first day until the present, they have participated in the gospel. Their fellowship has involved a common commitment that continues even when distance and chains make ordinary meetings impossible. Remembering them becomes an occasion for prayer rather than merely a longing for easier circumstances.", "Paul is confident that God, who began a good work among them, will bring it to completion at the day of Jesus Christ. This confidence looks towards God's faithful purpose and the church's final hope. It does not promise that every present plan will proceed without difficulty. Paul can speak with affection because the believers have shared in grace with him, both in his chains and in the defence and confirmation of the gospel. They have continued to identify with a servant whose circumstances could have made such loyalty costly. His gratitude has a concrete history behind it.", "He calls God as witness to the depth of his longing for them in the affection of Christ Jesus. That love takes the form of a particular request. Their love should abound increasingly in knowledge and discernment. Paul does not assume that sincere feeling automatically produces a wise response. Love must learn to recognise what is excellent and make sound moral judgements. The believer may need to distinguish a helpful act from a harmful indulgence, a true teaching from attractive error, or a lasting good from an immediate preference. Growing love remains willing to learn because it seeks the actual good of another person.", "The prayer looks towards sincerity, freedom from stumbling and a life filled with the fruit of righteousness through Jesus Christ. Its final purpose is God's glory and praise. The passage joins affection, discernment, moral growth and dependence on Christ. Paul has not stopped at thanking the church for past loyalty. He asks for the continuing work that will prepare them for the day of Christ. Readers are invited into the same pattern of grateful prayer, remembering specific evidence of fellowship and asking God to make love wiser and more fruitful. The confidence that God continues his work gives a reason to pray for that growth with hope."], "lesson": "Love grows well when it learns truth and exercises discernment. Gratitude for faithful fellowship should lead to prayer for further growth through Christ.", "question": "For whom could you pray that love would grow together with knowledge and sound judgement?"},
  {"id": 91351, "title": "Chains Open a Door for the Gospel", "subtitle": "Courage and purpose under restrictions", "text": "Philippians 1:12–30", "chapters": [[49, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul wants the Philippians to understand what his circumstances have actually produced. His imprisonment has contributed to the spread of the gospel. Those around him, including the imperial guard or palace circle referred to in the passage, have learned that his chains are connected with Christ. Other believers have gained courage to speak the word more freely. The apostle does not call confinement pleasant or suggest that suffering should be sought for its own sake. He shows that the restriction placed upon him has not placed the same restriction upon the message he serves. God has continued to work within circumstances Paul would not have chosen.", "The motives of the preachers are mixed. Some proclaim Christ from love and recognise Paul's task of defending the gospel. Others act from envy and rivalry, imagining that their activity will add distress to his imprisonment. Paul neither approves their envy nor makes his own reputation the centre of the account. He rejoices that Christ is being proclaimed. This response concerns preaching whose message is Christ despite the speakers' wrong motives. It must not be stretched into approval of any false teaching simply because a religious name appears within it. Paul can expose a corrupt motive while refusing to let rivalry extinguish joy in the spread of the true message.", "He expects help through the church's prayers and the provision of the Spirit of Jesus Christ. His earnest hope is that Christ will be honoured in his body, whether through life or death. To live means Christ and fruitful labour, while death means being with Christ. His reflection expresses the tension of a prisoner facing an uncertain outcome. He desires to be with the Lord, yet recognises the church's need for his continued service. It is not an invitation to seek death. Love for the believers gives present life a clear purpose, their progress and joy in faith.", "Paul turns from his own future to their conduct. Whether he visits or remains absent, they must live in a manner worthy of the gospel, stand together and contend for its faith with one spirit and purpose. Opponents should not frighten them into abandoning that course. Believing in Christ can include suffering for his sake, and their conflict connects them with what they have seen and heard in Paul. The chapter therefore joins courage with unity and service. The church's response to pressure should show the character of the message it confesses, while Paul's restricted life remains fruitful through prayer, witness and concern for others."], "lesson": "Circumstances can restrict a servant without stopping God's work. Courage under pressure should deepen unity and service rather than feed rivalry.", "question": "What faithful service remains possible within a restriction you cannot presently change?"},
  {"id": 91352, "title": "Christ Takes the Place of a Servant", "subtitle": "Humility that gives others attention", "text": "Philippians 2:1–11", "chapters": [[49, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul appeals to what the believers already share in Christ. Encouragement, the comfort of love, fellowship in the Spirit and tender mercy should shape their life together. He asks them to complete his joy through a common love and purpose. This unity is tested by the motives brought into ordinary relationships. Rivalry and empty self-importance must give way to humility. Each person is to consider others with honour and attend to their interests as well as his own. The appeal does not require denying another person's wrongdoing or pretending that every opinion is equally sound. It challenges the habit of making one's own standing the measure of every encounter.", "The mind Paul commends is seen in Christ Jesus. Christ, who was in the form of God and equal with God, did not pursue advantage through grasping self-assertion. He made himself of no reputation, took the form of a servant and came in human likeness. The movement is one of willing humility from the highest dignity. His becoming a servant does not make him a sinner or reduce him to an ordinary person with no divine identity. The passage asks readers to recognise who it is that stoops. The greatness of the one who serves gives the example its searching force.", "Found in human form, Christ humbled himself in obedience to death, even death on a cross. The cross is not an attractive symbol of inconvenience within this account. It names the shameful and costly death he accepted. His obedience reaches the point at which serving the Father's purpose costs his life. Paul places this before a church tempted by competition over honour. The pattern asks the believers to consider whether their own insistence on recognition can survive a serious look at the Lord they confess. Humility is made visible in concrete obedience and costly concern, not merely in speaking modestly about oneself.", "God highly exalted Christ and gave him the name above every name. Every knee will bow and every tongue confess that Jesus Christ is Lord, to the glory of God the Father. The scope reaches heaven, earth and the realm below, declaring the universal acknowledgement due to him. This exaltation belongs first to Christ's unique identity and work. It is not a bargain promising a higher human rank to everyone who performs a humble task. The passage returns the reader to shared life with a renewed measure of greatness. Those who confess this Lord should learn from his mind, using their freedom, gifts and position to serve rather than treating fellowship as a contest for importance."], "lesson": "The humility of Christ gives believers a pattern for the use of dignity and responsibility. Confessing him as Lord should change how we regard another person's needs.", "question": "Where could you lay aside the demand for recognition and give practical attention to someone else's good?"},
  {"id": 91353, "title": "Two Fellow Workers Worth Receiving", "subtitle": "Obedience and honour in ordinary service", "text": "Philippians 2:12–30", "chapters": [[49, 2]], "start": 12, "end": 30, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul asks the Philippians to continue in obedience during his absence. Their faith must not depend on an apostle watching them. They are to work out their salvation with reverent seriousness because God is at work within them, shaping both desire and action according to his good purpose. This is active response to divine grace, not the purchase of salvation through enough effort. They should act without grumbling and hostile argument, holding to the word of life and shining within a crooked generation. Their conduct matters to Paul's hope that his labour will be seen as fruitful at the day of Christ. Even if his life is poured out in their service, he can rejoice with them.", "The next paragraphs show such service in people the church knows. Paul hopes to send Timothy so that news of their condition will encourage him. Timothy has a genuine concern for them and a tested history of working with Paul in the gospel. He is compared to a son serving alongside his father. Others are absorbed in their own interests, but Timothy seeks what belongs to Jesus Christ. His suitability rests on proven character rather than impressive claims. Paul plans to send him when the outcome of his own circumstances becomes clearer and also hopes, in the Lord, to visit personally.", "Epaphroditus is to return sooner. He is Paul's brother, fellow worker and fellow soldier, as well as the church's messenger and the one who has attended to Paul's needs. He has been longing for the Philippians and is distressed because they heard that he was ill. His illness was serious enough to bring him near death. Paul describes his recovery as God's mercy towards both the sick worker and himself. He openly acknowledges that a death would have added sorrow to sorrow. Trust in God has not made affection, fear for a friend or grief unreal.", "Paul sends Epaphroditus so the church can rejoice at seeing him and his own anxiety can be eased. They must receive him gladly and honour people like him, because he risked his life in Christ's work to provide the service they could not personally give from a distance. His return must not be mistaken for failure. The passage joins God's inward work to ordinary, costly expressions of obedience, truthful news, care for the sick and honour for faithful helpers. Timothy and Epaphroditus make the teaching concrete. The church is being shown the sort of people whose character deserves its gratitude and whose example can guide its own service."], "lesson": "God's work within believers bears fruit in willing obedience and sincere care. Faithful helpers deserve honour, including when illness interrupts their work.", "question": "Who has served faithfully in a quiet or costly way, and how could you receive or encourage that person?"},
  {"id": 91354, "title": "What Paul Once Counted as Gain", "subtitle": "Knowing Christ and pressing forward", "text": "Philippians 3:1–21", "chapters": [[49, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul calls the believers to rejoice in the Lord and repeats a warning for their protection. He opposes teachers who place confidence in circumcision and human religious standing. Those who worship by God's Spirit and glory in Christ Jesus must not rest their hope in such credentials. Paul can speak as someone who once had an impressive list of them. He was circumcised on the eighth day, belonged to Israel and the tribe of Benjamin, was a Hebrew of Hebrews and a Pharisee in relation to the law. His zeal had even driven him to persecute the church. Outwardly, his record under that former standard had appeared blameless.", "His encounter with Christ changed the value of everything he had counted as gain. He now considers it loss beside knowing Christ Jesus his Lord. He wants to be found in Christ with righteousness received from God through faith, rather than a righteousness used as grounds for self-confidence. This change is deeper than exchanging one impressive religious identity for another. The centre has moved from what he can present about himself to the Lord in whom he trusts. His desire is to know Christ, the power of his resurrection and fellowship in his sufferings, being conformed to his death and looking towards resurrection.", "Paul does not claim already to have reached the final goal. He presses on because Christ Jesus has taken hold of him. Forgetting what lies behind and reaching towards what lies ahead describes a runner's concentrated direction. It does not require erasing memory, denying responsibility for past wrong or ignoring lessons already learned. Paul has just remembered his own past in order to explain grace. The point is that former gain and previous progress cannot become the place where he settles. Mature believers should share this forward-looking purpose and continue walking faithfully according to the truth they have received.", "The church is urged to observe and imitate people who live according to this example. Paul speaks with tears about others whose conduct makes them enemies of Christ's cross, governed by appetite and earthly concerns and boasting in what should bring shame. His grief guards the warning against a spirit of personal triumph. Believers have their citizenship in heaven and await the Lord Jesus Christ from there. He will transform their lowly bodies to be like his glorious body through the power by which he subjects all things to himself. The promised future gives direction to present conduct. Knowing Christ leads to faithful perseverance, humble confidence and a hope that reaches beyond the achievements the world can measure."], "lesson": "Knowing Christ changes the value of former achievements and gives life a continuing direction. Confidence rests in him while obedience presses towards the promised future.", "question": "What former ground of pride or present comfort could distract you from continuing to know and follow Christ?"},
  {"id": 91355, "title": "A Plea for Two Fellow Workers", "subtitle": "Reconciliation, prayer and disciplined thought", "text": "Philippians 4:1–9", "chapters": [[49, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul addresses the Philippians with affection as his joy and crown and urges them to stand firm in the Lord. He then names Euodias and Syntyche and appeals to each to be of the same mind in the Lord. The disagreement matters enough to address openly, but the letter does not disclose its subject. Readers should not invent one or assume that either woman has ceased to be a believer. Paul asks a trusted fellow worker to help them, remembering that both have laboured beside him in the gospel with Clement and others whose names are in the book of life. Their previous service remains part of how he regards them.", "The request for help gives the church a responsibility beyond choosing a side or repeating the quarrel. Reconciliation may need patient assistance from someone who cares about both people and the work they share. Paul then calls the whole fellowship to rejoice in the Lord and let its gentleness be known. The Lord is near. This confidence should shape how believers respond under pressure. Joy does not require pretending a disagreement is insignificant, and gentleness does not require avoiding the truthful conversation needed to resolve it. Both qualities can govern the way the difficult matter is approached.", "The believers are invited to bring every concern to God through prayer and supplication with thanksgiving. Anxiety need not become a burden held in isolation or a force directing every decision. God's peace, surpassing human understanding, will guard hearts and minds in Christ Jesus. The image suggests protection at the very place where fear and conflict can unsettle judgement. The promise should not be used to shame someone whose distress is persistent or to forbid seeking wise help. It gives a reason to pray honestly, repeatedly and gratefully, bringing real needs before the God whose care reaches beyond what the sufferer can presently explain.", "Paul also directs attention towards what is true, honourable, just, pure, lovely and commendable, including what displays moral excellence and deserves praise. Thinking is treated as a responsibility because what is repeatedly welcomed can shape the heart's response. The believers must then practise what they have learned, received, heard and seen in Paul. The God of peace will be with them. The passage joins reconciliation, joy, prayer, careful attention and obedient action. None of these is left as an isolated slogan. They belong to a shared life in which named workers can receive help, troubled minds can seek God and the church can learn to make peace through the truth it actually practises."], "lesson": "Peace grows through prayer, truthful thought and practical obedience. Helping people reconcile should preserve their dignity and remember their shared service.", "question": "What concern should you bring to God, and what practical step towards peace should follow that prayer?"},
  {"id": 91356, "title": "The Gift That Reached Paul Again", "subtitle": "Contentment and generosity", "text": "Philippians 4:10–23", "chapters": [[49, 4]], "start": 10, "end": 23, "kind": "story", "category": "Philippians", "story": ["Paul rejoices that the Philippians have again found an opportunity to express their care for him. He is careful about how he describes the interval. Their concern had continued, though the opportunity to act had been lacking. His gratitude therefore avoids turning a delayed gift into an accusation. He also explains that he has learned contentment in differing conditions. He knows what it is to be brought low and what it is to have plenty, to be hungry and to be well fed, to lack and to abound. This learning has taken place through actual changes of circumstance, not through a life insulated from need.", "His declaration that he can do all things through Christ who strengthens him belongs within this account. Christ enables him to endure and remain faithful in abundance and deprivation. The statement should not be detached from its setting and made a guarantee of unlimited achievement, wealth or victory in every personal ambition. Paul's strength is shown partly in receiving enough for today without making comfort his master, and partly in facing want without abandoning the Lord. Contentment does not make hunger good or remove the value of practical assistance. He immediately tells the Philippians that they have done well to share in his affliction.", "He remembers their earlier giving when he left Macedonia, including repeated help while he was in Thessalonica. Their partnership has a history. Paul is not chiefly seeking another payment, but the fruit that grows from their generosity before God. He has received what they sent through Epaphroditus and describes it as an offering with a pleasing fragrance, acceptable to God. Ordinary material support has become an act of worship through the love and faithfulness expressed in it. The messenger, the church and the prisoner are joined in service by a gift that meets a real need.", "Paul trusts that God will supply their need according to his riches in glory in Christ Jesus, and he gives glory to God the Father. This assurance comes within grateful fellowship and reliance on divine care. It is not a sales promise that a donation will compel God to return a larger sum. The letter closes with greetings to every believer, greetings from those with Paul, and a special mention of believers belonging to Caesar's household. Even near imperial power, the gospel has gathered people into Christ's fellowship. The final blessing of grace gathers the letter's concerns together, joy, endurance, unity, generous care and a life sustained by the Lord whose strength is sufficient through changing circumstances."], "lesson": "Christ's strength enables faithfulness in both need and plenty. Generosity meets real needs and expresses fellowship without turning giving into a demand for material reward.", "question": "How can you practise contentment while also responding generously to a real need you can help meet?"},
  {"id": 91357, "title": "News Brought by Epaphras", "subtitle": "A growing life that gives thanks", "text": "Colossians 1:1–14", "chapters": [[50, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 14, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul and Timothy greet the Colossians. Paul gives thanks for their faith and love and recalls how they learned the gospel from Epaphras. He prays that they will know God's will, bear fruit, endure patiently and thank the Father who rescued them from darkness and brought them into his Son's kingdom."], "lesson": "The prayer joins knowledge to a way of walking. Paul expects understanding to show up in fruitful work, endurance and gratitude.", "question": "What does Paul pray their knowledge of God's will will produce?"},
  {"id": 91358, "title": "Christ Holds All Things Together", "subtitle": "Remaining grounded in reconciliation", "text": "Colossians 1:15–29", "chapters": [[50, 1]], "start": 15, "end": 29, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul describes Christ as the image of the invisible God, the one through whom all things were created and the head of the church. Through his death God reconciles those once alienated. Paul calls them to remain grounded in faith. He describes his own work of making known Christ among them and presenting people mature in him."], "lesson": "The greatness of Christ gives weight to the peace he makes. Paul's labour seeks a settled maturity in the people who have received that peace.", "question": "How does Paul's description of Christ's place in creation and in the church shape what he says about reconciliation?"},
  {"id": 91359, "title": "Rooted in the One They Received", "subtitle": "Living from Christ's sufficiency", "text": "Colossians 2:1–15", "chapters": [[50, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul wants believers to be knit together in love and attain a settled knowledge of Christ. He urges them to walk in the one they received and warns against deceptive teaching. Fullness dwells in Christ. Paul speaks of believers being buried and raised with him, forgiven and released from the record that stood against them."], "lesson": "Paul measures new teaching against what believers already have in Christ. Their roots are to grow deeper in him while their gratitude grows.", "question": "What reasons does Paul give for remaining rooted in Christ?"},
  {"id": 91360, "title": "Shadows and the Life Hidden With Christ", "subtitle": "Choosing the direction of your attention", "text": "Colossians 2:16–3:4", "chapters": [[50, 2], [50, 3]], "start": 16, "end": 4, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul tells believers not to let others judge them over food, drink or religious days. He warns against practices that look wise while drawing people away from the head. Having died and risen with Christ, they are to seek what is above. Their life is hidden with Christ and will be revealed when he appears."], "lesson": "Paul answers pressure from religious regulations with a clearer account of where believers' lives are held. That belonging gives shape to what they seek.", "question": "What does Paul say is hidden now, and what will happen when Christ appears?"},
  {"id": 91361, "title": "Getting Dressed for a Changed Life", "subtitle": "Love binds the new habits together", "text": "Colossians 3:5–17", "chapters": [[50, 3]], "start": 5, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul names destructive desires and practices to put away, including greed, anger and lying. He then describes what to put on as God's beloved people: compassion, humility, patience, forgiveness and love. He calls them to let Christ's peace rule, let his word dwell among them and do everything with thanksgiving."], "lesson": "Paul's clothing image helps people name practical habits to take up each day. Forgiveness and truthful speech give the change a form other people can recognise.", "question": "Which instruction in this passage would change the way you respond to an irritation at home or at work?"},
  {"id": 91362, "title": "The Lord of the Home and the Open Door", "subtitle": "Doing your work with accountability and grace", "text": "Colossians 3:18–4:6", "chapters": [[50, 3], [50, 4]], "start": 18, "end": 6, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Paul addresses relationships between spouses, parents and children, and servants and masters. Those in power have duties, and everyone ultimately answers to the Lord. He asks the church to continue in prayer and to pray for a door for the word. He urges wisdom towards outsiders and speech seasoned with grace."], "lesson": "The Lord's authority reaches the way people carry out everyday duties and the way they speak outside the fellowship. Prayer draws both concerns into their shared life.", "question": "What does Paul request for his own witness, and what way of speaking does he ask of them?"},
  {"id": 91363, "title": "Letters and Greetings Travelling Together", "subtitle": "Remembering people whose work is easily missed", "text": "Colossians 4:7–18", "chapters": [[50, 4]], "start": 7, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "Colossians", "story": ["Tychicus and Onesimus will bring news. Paul names fellow prisoners, workers, hosts and messengers, and says Epaphras wrestles in prayer for the believers' maturity. He asks that letters be exchanged between churches and tells Archippus to attend to his ministry. Paul closes by asking them to remember his chains."], "lesson": "The list shows how letters, hospitality, prayer and personal news sustain churches at a distance. Paul's brief instruction to Archippus places a particular responsibility in his hands.", "question": "What does Epaphras pray for, and what does Paul ask Archippus to attend to?"},
  {"id": 91364, "title": "From Idols to the Living God", "subtitle": "A changed life becomes a public witness", "text": "1 Thessalonians 1:1–10", "chapters": [[51, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul, Silvanus and Timothy remember the Thessalonians' work of faith, labour of love and endurance of hope. The church received the word amid affliction with joy from the Spirit. Its faith became known in other places. People report how the believers turned from idols to serve God and wait for Jesus."], "lesson": "Paul describes conversion through a new service and a new hope. Both became visible beyond the congregation itself.", "question": "What changes did people report about the Thessalonians after they received the message?"},
  {"id": 91365, "title": "The Manner in Which They Came", "subtitle": "Gentle care and a life worth imitating", "text": "1 Thessalonians 2:1–12", "chapters": [[51, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul recalls coming after mistreatment at Philippi and speaking with boldness. He denies using flattery or seeking advantage. He compares his care to a nursing mother and to a father encouraging his children. He reminds them that he worked to avoid burdening them and shared his life as well as the gospel."], "lesson": "The images of family give Paul's bold preaching a personal setting. His conduct lets the hearers examine how he cared for them while he spoke.", "question": "What details show how Paul sought to avoid taking advantage of those he taught?"},
  {"id": 91366, "title": "Timothy Returns With Good News", "subtitle": "Strengthening faith during separation", "text": "1 Thessalonians 2:13–3:13", "chapters": [[51, 2], [51, 3]], "start": 13, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul gives thanks that the church received the message as God's word despite suffering. Unable to return, he sends Timothy to strengthen the believers and learn how they are faring. Timothy brings news of their faith and love. Paul is comforted and prays that they will increase in love and be established in holiness."], "lesson": "The separation leads to a practical act of care. Timothy's report then gives Paul fresh grounds for gratitude and prayer.", "question": "Why did Paul send Timothy, and what about his report encouraged him?"},
  {"id": 91367, "title": "Holiness and a Quiet, Responsible Life", "subtitle": "Respecting others in conduct and work", "text": "1 Thessalonians 4:1–12", "chapters": [[51, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul urges the believers to grow in the way they live before God. He speaks about sexual holiness and warns against wronging another person. He commends their love for fellow believers and calls them to grow in it. He urges them to live quietly, attend to their responsibilities and work with their hands."], "lesson": "Paul joins holiness to the treatment of other people. Daily work and respect for others help give their witness credibility outside the church.", "question": "Which ordinary habits does Paul name when he speaks of their conduct towards outsiders?"},
  {"id": 91368, "title": "Grieving With Hope", "subtitle": "Comfort grounded in resurrection", "text": "1 Thessalonians 4:13–18", "chapters": [[51, 4]], "start": 13, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul addresses concern for believers who have died. Because Jesus died and rose, those who have died in him are included in the hope of his coming. He says the dead in Christ will rise, and those alive will be gathered with them to meet the Lord. He tells the church to comfort one another with these words."], "lesson": "Paul takes grief seriously and gives it a source of hope. The teaching is given to a community that can speak comfort to people who mourn.", "question": "What assurance does Paul give about the place of believers who have already died?"},
  {"id": 91369, "title": "Children of the Day", "subtitle": "Readiness through faith, love and hope", "text": "1 Thessalonians 5:1–11", "chapters": [[51, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul says the day of the Lord comes unexpectedly, like a thief at night. He calls the believers children of light and urges alertness and sobriety. Faith and love are pictured as a breastplate and hope of salvation as a helmet. He directs them to continue encouraging and building one another up."], "lesson": "Readiness has a communal shape. Paul gives faith, love and hope work to do while people wait for a day whose timing they cannot control.", "question": "How does Paul's description of readiness affect the way believers should treat one another?"},
  {"id": 91370, "title": "Helping People According to Their Need", "subtitle": "Patient care, prayer and discernment", "text": "1 Thessalonians 5:12–28", "chapters": [[51, 5]], "start": 12, "end": 28, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks the church to respect those who work among them and be at peace. He distinguishes between warning the unruly, encouraging the fainthearted and supporting the weak. He calls for patience with everyone. He ends with instructions about prayer, thanksgiving and weighing what is heard, and prays for God to sanctify them wholly."], "lesson": "Patient care pays attention to what a person actually needs. Paul's final prayer puts the church's growth in the hands of the God who calls them.", "question": "How do the different instructions in verse 14 help you respond wisely to people in different situations?"},
  {"id": 91371, "title": "Faith Growing Under Pressure", "subtitle": "Endurance while justice remains with God", "text": "2 Thessalonians 1:1–12", "chapters": [[52, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul, Silvanus and Timothy give thanks for the growth of the church's faith and love during persecution. They speak of God's righteous judgement and of relief when Jesus is revealed. Paul prays that God will fulfil the believers' good purposes and work of faith so that Jesus is glorified in them."], "lesson": "Paul notices growth that suffering has not erased. The promise of just judgement supports his prayer that the believers' present work may continue.", "question": "What qualities does Paul see growing in the church while it faces persecution?"},
  {"id": 91372, "title": "A Report That Shook the Church", "subtitle": "Discernment when a claim sounds urgent", "text": "2 Thessalonians 2:1–12", "chapters": [[52, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 12, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks the believers not to be shaken by claims, even a letter presented as coming from him, that the day of the Lord has arrived. He recalls what he taught about rebellion and the disclosure of the lawless one. He describes deceptive signs and the eventual defeat of the lawless one by the Lord Jesus."], "lesson": "An urgent claim can borrow the authority of a trusted messenger. Paul asks the church to remember what it received and to love the truth while weighing the claim.", "question": "What kinds of messages did Paul say might have unsettled the believers?"},
  {"id": 91373, "title": "Stand Firm in What You Received", "subtitle": "Encouragement for settled obedience", "text": "2 Thessalonians 2:13–17", "chapters": [[52, 2]], "start": 13, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul gives thanks for the believers' calling through the gospel and speaks of sanctification by the Spirit and belief of the truth. He urges the church to hold to what it received by word or letter. He asks Jesus and the Father to comfort their hearts and establish their words and works."], "lesson": "Paul's response to confusion includes encouragement as well as correction. A settled heart is meant to show itself in dependable speech and work.", "question": "What two areas does Paul ask God to establish after encouraging them to stand firm?"},
  {"id": 91374, "title": "Praying for the Word and Working Quietly", "subtitle": "Perseverance with responsible care", "text": "2 Thessalonians 3:1–18", "chapters": [[52, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Thessalonians", "story": ["Paul asks for prayer that the message will spread. He reminds the church of the example he set by working and addresses those who refuse to work and interfere in others' affairs. He urges them to work quietly. When correction is necessary, Paul tells the church to admonish the person as a brother. He closes with peace and grace."], "lesson": "Paul's instruction concerns refusal to work. Correction addresses the conduct while maintaining a relationship in which a person can hear and respond.", "question": "What distinction does Paul make between warning someone and treating that person as an enemy?"},
  {"id": 91375, "title": "A Charge to Stay in Ephesus", "subtitle": "Teaching that produces love", "text": "1 Timothy 1:1–11", "chapters": [[53, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 11, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul reminds Timothy why he asked him to remain in Ephesus. Some people were teaching differently and becoming absorbed in stories and genealogies that generated disputes. Paul says the aim of the charge is love from a pure heart, a good conscience and sincere faith. He explains that the law has a proper use in addressing conduct contrary to sound teaching."], "lesson": "A teacher can ask what a discussion is producing. Paul gives Timothy a measure that reaches the heart and the conduct of the people who listen.", "question": "What three things does Paul connect with the love his charge is meant to produce?"},
  {"id": 91376, "title": "Mercy Given to a Former Persecutor", "subtitle": "Remembering grace while defending the faith", "text": "1 Timothy 1:12–20", "chapters": [[53, 1]], "start": 12, "end": 20, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul thanks Christ for strengthening and appointing him to serve. He recalls speaking against Christ and persecuting believers before receiving mercy. His life becomes an example of Christ's patience. After giving glory to God, Paul charges Timothy to hold faith and a good conscience and names two men whose course shows what can happen when these are put aside."], "lesson": "Paul speaks of receiving mercy before instructing Timothy about faithfulness. The truth he guards is the same truth that gave him a future after his former conduct.", "question": "What does Paul say his own experience shows to people who will later believe?"},
  {"id": 91377, "title": "Prayer for People in Authority", "subtitle": "Looking beyond familiar circles in prayer", "text": "1 Timothy 2:1–7", "chapters": [[53, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks that petitions, prayers and thanksgivings be made for all people, including kings and others in authority. He connects that prayer with the possibility of a quiet and godly life. He says God desires people to be saved and to know the truth, and points to Christ Jesus as the one mediator who gave himself as a ransom."], "lesson": "Paul gives the congregation a wide field for prayer. A person in authority can be included even when the congregation's influence over that person's decisions is small.", "question": "How does Paul's description of Christ's work help explain the breadth of the prayer he requests?"},
  {"id": 91378, "title": "Prayer, Modesty and Learning", "subtitle": "Conduct fitted to worship", "text": "1 Timothy 2:8–15", "chapters": [[53, 2]], "start": 8, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul directs men to pray without anger or quarrelling and urges women to give attention to modesty and good works. He calls for a woman to learn quietly and says he does not permit a woman to teach or exercise authority over a man. In making his case he refers to Adam and Eve. The passage closes with a reference to childbearing and continuance in faith, love, holiness and self-control."], "lesson": "Paul treats the manner of prayer, dress and learning as matters that shape gathered worship. The closing virtues deserve attention alongside the disputed details of the passage.", "question": "Which instructions in this passage address what worshippers bring into the gathering through their conduct?"},
  {"id": 91379, "title": "The Character of Those Who Serve", "subtitle": "Trustworthy conduct in God's household", "text": "1 Timothy 3:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul describes the qualifications for overseers and deacons. He gives weight to reputation, self-control, hospitality, the management of one's household and faithfulness in service. He explains that he is writing so Timothy will know how people should conduct themselves in God's household. The chapter closes with a confession concerning Christ and the mystery of godliness."], "lesson": "Paul asks the church to look across a person's life when entrusting responsibility. A visible gift needs the support of habits that other people can trust.", "question": "Which qualifications would be recognisable to people who know a prospective leader outside a church meeting?"},
  {"id": 91380, "title": "Give Attention to Reading and Teaching", "subtitle": "Training for godliness in public and private", "text": "1 Timothy 4:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul warns that some will turn towards teachings that forbid marriage and certain foods. He says God's gifts are to be received with thanksgiving. He tells Timothy to train himself for godliness, set an example in speech and life, devote himself to Scripture reading and teaching, and attend to the gift he has received. Timothy is to watch his life and his teaching together."], "lesson": "Paul gives a young leader several ways to become credible through conduct. Steady attention to Scripture and to his own life belongs to his care of others.", "question": "What two things does Paul tell Timothy to pay close attention to at the end of the chapter?"},
  {"id": 91381, "title": "Care for Widows and Respect Within the Church", "subtitle": "Making help dependable and responsible", "text": "1 Timothy 5:1–16", "chapters": [[53, 5]], "start": 1, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks Timothy to approach older and younger people as family and to honour widows in real need. He distinguishes those who have relatives able to care for them from those left alone. He gives instructions for enrolling widows and asks families to shoulder responsibilities they can carry, so the church can assist those without support."], "lesson": "Care becomes dependable when people identify the needs and duties that are actually present. Paul gives a reason for asking households to take responsibility where they can.", "question": "How does Paul's instruction to households affect what the church is able to do for widows who are alone?"},
  {"id": 91382, "title": "Honour, Evidence and Impartial Judgement", "subtitle": "Careful decisions about leaders", "text": "1 Timothy 5:17–25", "chapters": [[53, 5]], "start": 17, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul calls for honour for elders who lead well, especially those who labour in teaching. He requires witnesses before receiving a charge against an elder and asks Timothy to act without partiality. He warns against laying hands on someone hastily. After personal advice about Timothy's health, he observes that conduct sometimes becomes clear only with time."], "lesson": "Receiving an accusation carefully and treating people impartially are both necessary. The warning against haste gives Timothy time to learn what a first impression cannot show.", "question": "What safeguards does Paul place around receiving accusations and entrusting responsibility?"},
  {"id": 91383, "title": "When Godliness Is Treated as Gain", "subtitle": "Contentment that guards the heart", "text": "1 Timothy 6:1–10", "chapters": [[53, 6]], "start": 1, "end": 10, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul addresses servants in relation to their masters and then warns about teachers whose arguments produce quarrels and whose interest in godliness is financial. He speaks of the gain of godliness with contentment and reminds readers what people bring into life and take from it. The desire to be rich can lead people into many troubles."], "lesson": "The passage asks what someone hopes to gain through religion. Contentment gives a person a basis for refusing a profitable course that damages faith and relationships.", "question": "What desires and outcomes does Paul connect with the pursuit of riches?"},
  {"id": 91384, "title": "The Good Confession and the Open Hand", "subtitle": "Keeping what has been entrusted", "text": "1 Timothy 6:11–21", "chapters": [[53, 6]], "start": 11, "end": 21, "kind": "story", "category": "1 Timothy", "story": ["Paul tells Timothy to pursue righteousness, godliness, faith, love, endurance and gentleness. He recalls Timothy's confession and charges him before God and Christ to keep the command. The wealthy are to place hope in God, be rich in good works and share. Paul closes by telling Timothy to guard what has been entrusted to him."], "lesson": "Paul gives Timothy qualities to pursue and the wealthy a way to put their resources to work. Both forms of faithfulness are visible in choices made over time.", "question": "What does Paul tell people with wealth to do with it, and where are they to place their hope?"},
  {"id": 91385, "title": "Paul Remembers Timothy's Tears", "subtitle": "Courage drawn from grace and fellowship", "text": "2 Timothy 1:1–18", "chapters": [[54, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 18, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul remembers Timothy's tears and the sincere faith he saw in Timothy's grandmother Lois and mother Eunice. He asks Timothy to stir up his gift and to share in suffering for the gospel according to God's power. Paul tells him to guard what was entrusted through the Holy Spirit. He remembers Onesiphorus, who sought him out in Rome and refreshed him."], "lesson": "Paul encourages Timothy by naming people who passed on faith and people who assisted a prisoner. Their acts give courage a human shape alongside God's promise of power.", "question": "What did Onesiphorus do that showed he was willing to be associated with Paul?"},
  {"id": 91386, "title": "Passing the Work to Faithful People", "subtitle": "Endurance for a trust that goes beyond you", "text": "2 Timothy 2:1–13", "chapters": [[54, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 13, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul tells Timothy to be strong in grace and entrust what he has heard to faithful people able to teach others. He uses images of a soldier, an athlete and a farmer to speak of discipline and patience. He directs Timothy to remember the risen Jesus, and says that although he himself is bound, God's word is free."], "lesson": "Timothy receives something he is expected to pass on. The images remind him that work spanning generations takes concentration, honest practice and patience.", "question": "What qualities should Timothy look for in the people to whom he passes on the teaching?"},
  {"id": 91387, "title": "A Workman Who Handles the Word Carefully", "subtitle": "Gentleness when correction is needed", "text": "2 Timothy 2:14–26", "chapters": [[54, 2]], "start": 14, "end": 26, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul warns about quarrels over words and tells Timothy to handle the word of truth accurately. He names teaching about the resurrection that has upset some believers. Using vessels in a house as an image, he calls Timothy to a clean life. The Lord's servant must teach patiently and correct opponents gently in hope that they will repent."], "lesson": "Accuracy and gentleness belong together in Paul's instructions. The teacher's purpose is that a person may come to recognise truth and change.", "question": "What does Paul say the Lord's servant should do instead of entering a quarrel?"},
  {"id": 91388, "title": "What Timothy Has Known Since Childhood", "subtitle": "Scripture equips a faithful life", "text": "2 Timothy 3:1–17", "chapters": [[54, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 17, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul describes difficult times when people may keep an outward form of godliness while denying its power. He reminds Timothy of the conduct and suffering he has witnessed in Paul. Timothy is to continue in what he learned and remember the sacred writings he has known since childhood. Scripture teaches, reproves, corrects and trains so that God's servant is equipped for good work."], "lesson": "Paul points Timothy to trusted teaching, lived example and Scripture. He describes what Scripture does to equip a person for the conduct God requires.", "question": "Which of the four uses of Scripture named here speaks most directly to a need in your life?"},
  {"id": 91389, "title": "Preach the Word and Finish the Course", "subtitle": "Faithfulness when hearers prefer something else", "text": "2 Timothy 4:1–8", "chapters": [[54, 4]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul solemnly charges Timothy to preach, correct and encourage with patience. People will gather teachers who say what they want to hear. Timothy is to remain watchful, endure hardship and fulfil his ministry. Paul speaks of his own departure as near and looks back on a fight fought, a course finished and faith kept."], "lesson": "Paul puts the immediate duty of teaching alongside a whole life of service. Timothy can measure success by fulfilling the work entrusted to him through changes in the audience.", "question": "What does Paul ask Timothy to keep doing when people turn towards messages they prefer?"},
  {"id": 91390, "title": "Come Before Winter", "subtitle": "Remembering the needs of a faithful worker", "text": "2 Timothy 4:9–22", "chapters": [[54, 4]], "start": 9, "end": 22, "kind": "story", "category": "2 Timothy", "story": ["Paul asks Timothy to come soon. He tells him who has left and who remains, asks him to bring Mark and requests a cloak, books and parchments. He recalls facing his first defence without human support and says the Lord stood with him. He sends greetings, asks Timothy to come before winter and closes with grace."], "lesson": "The letter leaves Paul with ordinary needs and difficult memories alongside confidence in the Lord. The request for Mark also makes room for a worker to be useful after earlier trouble.", "question": "What concrete acts of help does Paul ask Timothy to bring with him?"},
  {"id": 91391, "title": "The Work Left to Titus in Crete", "subtitle": "Choosing people whose lives support their teaching", "text": "Titus 1:1–9", "chapters": [[55, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 9, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul greets Titus and reminds him why he left him in Crete: to put unfinished matters in order and appoint elders in each town. He describes their household conduct, self-control, hospitality and love of what is good. They must hold firmly to the trustworthy message so they can encourage with sound teaching and answer those who oppose it."], "lesson": "Paul links the authority to correct others with qualities visible in a person's daily life. The same person must be able to welcome, teach and exercise restraint.", "question": "Which qualities concern an elder's conduct, and which concern the handling of teaching?"},
  {"id": 91392, "title": "When Teaching Upsets Households", "subtitle": "Correction with the hope of sound faith", "text": "Titus 1:10–16", "chapters": [[55, 1]], "start": 10, "end": 16, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul says some people in Crete are misleading households for gain. He quotes a severe local saying and calls on Titus to rebuke the people concerned so they may become sound in faith. He warns about fables and human commands and observes that people can profess to know God while denying him by their deeds."], "lesson": "The stated aim of correction is soundness in faith. Paul judges a profession by what a person actually does and notices who is affected when teaching goes wrong.", "question": "What harm was spreading through the households, and what result did Paul seek through correction?"},
  {"id": 91393, "title": "Grace Teaches People How to Live", "subtitle": "Making sound teaching visible", "text": "Titus 2:1–15", "chapters": [[55, 2]], "start": 1, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul tells Titus to teach what fits sound doctrine and addresses older and younger people in different responsibilities. Titus must model good work and sound speech. Paul asks servants to show trustworthiness. He then explains that God's grace has appeared, training people to live with self-control and godliness as they await Christ and become eager for good works."], "lesson": "Paul describes grace as a teacher of daily conduct. The ordinary duties listed earlier are places where people can see what that teaching produces.", "question": "What does Paul say grace has appeared to teach people to leave behind and to practise?"},
  {"id": 91394, "title": "What God Did When We Were Foolish", "subtitle": "Mercy becomes a reason for gentleness", "text": "Titus 3:1–8", "chapters": [[55, 3]], "start": 1, "end": 8, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul asks believers to be ready for good work, considerate in public life and gentle towards others. He reminds them of their own former foolishness and hostility. God saved them through his mercy, renewal by the Holy Spirit and grace through Jesus Christ. Paul asks those who believe God to be careful to devote themselves to good works."], "lesson": "Remembering what mercy found in us can change how we speak to others. Paul places gentleness beside a clear account of the grace believers received.", "question": "How does remembering what believers once were support Paul's instruction about treating others?"},
  {"id": 91395, "title": "Leaving Unfruitful Disputes and Meeting Real Needs", "subtitle": "Giving useful work your attention", "text": "Titus 3:9–15", "chapters": [[55, 3]], "start": 9, "end": 15, "kind": "story", "category": "Titus", "story": ["Paul tells Titus to avoid quarrels and arguments that accomplish little. He gives instructions for dealing with a divisive person after repeated warnings. Then he turns to travel plans and asks Titus to help Zenas and Apollos on their way. Believers should learn to maintain good works that meet pressing needs."], "lesson": "The closing turns quickly from unproductive arguments to named people with practical needs. Paul gives Titus a choice about where to spend time and effort.", "question": "What help does Paul want Titus to give Zenas and Apollos for their journey?"},
  {"id": 91396, "title": "Paul Begins With the Good He Has Heard", "subtitle": "Recognising love before making an appeal", "text": "Philemon 1:1–7", "chapters": [[56, 1]], "start": 1, "end": 7, "kind": "story", "category": "Philemon", "story": ["Paul greets Philemon, Apphia, Archippus and the church meeting in the house. He gives thanks for Philemon's love and faith and prays that their fellowship will become effective as they recognise the good they have in Christ. He says the hearts of the saints have been refreshed through Philemon."], "lesson": "Paul names evidence of grace before he raises a difficult matter. His thanksgiving is specific enough to show that his appeal takes Philemon's earlier conduct seriously.", "question": "What does Paul say Philemon has already done for fellow believers?"},
  {"id": 91397, "title": "Receive Onesimus as You Would Receive Me", "subtitle": "Love changes how a person is welcomed", "text": "Philemon 1:8–25", "chapters": [[56, 1]], "start": 8, "end": 25, "kind": "story", "category": "Philemon", "story": ["From imprisonment Paul appeals for Onesimus, who has become dear to him. He sends him back and says he wanted Philemon's good deed to be willing. He asks Philemon to receive Onesimus as more than a slave, as a beloved brother, and to welcome him as he would Paul. If there is wrong or debt, Paul offers to take it on himself. He closes with greetings and plans for a possible visit."], "lesson": "Paul puts his relationship with both men to work in the appeal. Calling Onesimus a brother gives Philemon a concrete way to reconsider how he will receive him.", "question": "What does Paul ask Philemon to do if Onesimus owes him anything, and what kind of welcome does he request?"}
];
